   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through a direct causal relationship. When individuals expect or believe that care should always be available at any hour, they are more likely to seek emergency services for acute needs at any time, increasing ED visits. Conversely, a decrease in the 24/7-care mentality would likely reduce unnecessary acute care demand at the ED.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24792419486928907, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/', 'https://data.cms.gov/provider-data/topics/hospitals/timely-effective-care', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7592628246152898, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is a literature review about patient-centered care and nurse–patient communication (barriers, facilitators, and a communication continuum). It discusses communication styles, staffing, environment, cultural competence, and promoting patient engagement, but it does not present evidence that a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" normalizes frequent healthcare use, lowers self-management thresholds, or causes increased acute/ED visits. The source neither defines nor measures a 24/7‑care mentality nor provides data linking such an orientation to higher emergency department demand. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim is not supported by the citation provided."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The umbrella review describes many drivers of ED demand (demographics, multimorbidity, limited primary care access, inappropriate ED use) and evaluates interventions like extended hours, telehealth, and public education, but it does not present evidence that a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\"—i.e., a cultural normalization of constant professional access that lowers self‑management thresholds—causes higher acute/ED demand. Where availability or extended access is discussed the evidence is mixed or context‑dependent, not a clear causal link supporting the specific mechanism in the explanation. Therefore the cited source does not substantiate the claim. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit by normalizing frequent healthcare utilization and diminishing individuals’ thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms. When people adopt a 24/7-care mentality, they become accustomed to accessing professional care for all health concerns, regardless of severity, which conditions them to immediately seek acute care in emergency departments even for conditions that would resolve independently. Consequently, fostering a 24/7-care mentality directly escalates acute care demand leading to an ED visit by eroding personal resilience and self-efficacy in managing minor health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit by normalizing frequent healthcare utilization and diminishing individuals’ thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms. When people adopt a 24/7-care mentality, they become accustomed to accessing professional care for all health concerns, regardless of severity, which conditions them to immediately seek acute care in emergency departments even for conditions that would resolve independently. Consequently, fostering a 24/7-care mentality directly escalates acute care demand leading to an ED visit by eroding personal resilience and self-efficacy in managing minor health issues.', 'perplexity': 1.335995662641813, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821369061697974, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.060205957562312916, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002294907475937441, 'prob_variance': 0.061466406401585225, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0478255201464974, 'avg_nll': 0.289676828583595}

