   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.25673107722273014, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical_error', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.817783050769152, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article does not mention \\"24/7-care mentality\\" and does not provide evidence denying a causal link; instead it identifies system and cultural factors (e.g., increased workload, interruptions, poor communication, an overburdened healthcare system) that are associated with higher risk of medication errors. Because the sources do not support the claim that no relationship exists (and in fact list plausible organizational drivers of incorrect medication use), the explanation is not supported by the citation list."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article identifies staffing, workload, extended duty hours, and clinician fatigue as root/system factors that increase error rates and specifically discusses medication errors and prevention strategies. It cites evidence that fatigue and long shifts (>12 hours) increase errors and that inadequate staffing and supervision contribute to mistakes in medication administration. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim that there is no relationship; it supports a link between around‑the‑clock staffing/fatigue issues and medical (including medication) errors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication-errors document discusses many causes and prevention strategies for medication errors (prescribing, communication, systems, patient education, technology, etc.) but does not mention or define a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" nor evaluate any causal link between such a mentality and incorrect medication use. Because the source provides no evidence for or against that specific relationship, it cannot support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical_error", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article on medical error lists practitioner fatigue, sleep deprivation, night shifts, staffing ratios, time pressures and system/culture factors as contributors to medical and medication errors and describes mechanisms (e.g., cognitive failure from extended shifts) that plausibly link a \\"always-on\\" care culture to incorrect medication use. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; they provide evidence of possible causal pathways.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE paper does not discuss a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" and therefore provides no evidence that such a mentality does or does not cause incorrect medication use. Moreover, the source documents multiple system- and provider-level mechanisms (polypharmacy in nursing homes, prescribing practices, formulary and cost incentives, packaging/name confusion, poor monitoring/compliance) that are linked to inappropriate medication use, so it does not support a blanket claim that no directional causal links can be established.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where 24/7-care mentality causes incorrect use of medication or vice versa. Without a clear definition or mechanism, it is not possible to establish a directional causal link between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2825061978611134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8509139185486374, 'min_prob': 0.11523138907961053, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004929433713715704, 'prob_variance': 0.06591084601194341, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8818906599500216, 'avg_nll': 0.24881613069135478}

69. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. When patients have easier access to relevant information about their therapy, they are more likely to understand instructions and adhere to the prescribed regimen. Conversely, limited access to information can lead to confusion and lower compliance. This relationship is supported by the mechanism that informed patients are better equipped to follow therapeutic guidelines.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.26620201562266593, 'citations': ['https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10503307.2023.2241630', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8130728/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7244795191683834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8130728/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study addresses practical accessibility (referral process, travel, building entry), the need for clear explanations, and using written/visual aids to help understanding and attendance. It recommends ensuring service users understand therapy and providing adapted information to support attendance. It does not provide evidence that exposure to a greater volume or a continual influx of information (the “sheer abundance” mechanism) increases motivation or directly causes higher therapy compliance. Thus the specific causal claim about more information volume driving compliance is not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper discusses how making digital mental health tools more accessible can increase reach, information recognition, usability, and possibly effectiveness (e.g., improved information uptake for visually impaired users) and notes that poor accessibility can hinder use and trust. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific claim that a greater volume or \\"constant influx\\" of information by itself increases motivation or directly drives therapy compliance, nor does it advance the proposed mechanism that sheer abundance of content compels patients to follow instructions more closely. Therefore the broad idea that better accessibility can improve use is supported, but the detailed causal explanation offered is not demonstrated by the citations. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies patient knowledge, health literacy, clear counseling, and provision of written/pictogram instructions as factors that can improve adherence — supporting the general idea that accessible, understandable information can boost compliance. However, it does not support the specific mechanism claimed (that a greater volume or constant influx of information per se — i.e., sheer abundance — naturally increases motivation/engagement). The review even cautions that more information is not always better (an \\"inverted U\\" effect in some groups) and stresses quality, clarity, and the patient–provider relationship over mere quantity. Therefore the claim is only partly supported by the sources.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis shows that involving patients (shared decision making, providing information/decision aids, honoring preferences) is associated with modest increases in treatment completion/adherence and satisfaction. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed here — that the sheer abundance or constant influx of information (volume of exposure) by itself compels greater motivation/engagement or causes patients to “keep up” with and follow therapy instructions. The sources discuss informed choice, psychoeducation, and decision support, and speculate about mechanisms such as therapeutic alliance and expectancies, but they do not support the strong causal claim about information volume/abundance driving compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information leads to increased therapy compliance because when patients are exposed to a greater volume of information about their therapy, the sheer abundance of available resources naturally results in higher levels of motivation and engagement. As patients encounter more content and advice, they are compelled to follow instructions more closely to keep up with the wealth of material presented to them. Therefore, the constant influx of information directly drives patients to be more compliant with their prescribed therapy regimen, as they seek to match the comprehensive guidance they receive.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information leads to increased therapy compliance because when patients are exposed to a greater volume of information about their therapy, the sheer abundance of available resources naturally results in higher levels of motivation and engagement. As patients encounter more content and advice, they are compelled to follow instructions more closely to keep up with the wealth of material presented to them. Therefore, the constant influx of information directly drives patients to be more compliant with their prescribed therapy regimen, as they seek to match the comprehensive guidance they receive.', 'perplexity': 1.3739174451241845, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7993675480215341, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10408784479830872, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00509861323416069, 'prob_variance': 0.07086351312157008, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1096985908357222, 'avg_nll': 0.3176661083882127}

70. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make informed healthcare decisions and seek appropriate alternatives, thereby reducing unnecessary ED visits. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information leads to higher reliance on ED services due to lack of knowledge about alternatives.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/health-information-exchange-post-acute-long-term-care-case-study-findings-final-report-1', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10284503/', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/health-information-exchange-post-acute-long-term-care-case-study-findings-final-report-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents provider-focused health information exchange (HIE) — e.g., facilitating clinician \\"anytime/anywhere\\" access, improving workflow, discharge/transfer communications, and potential to reduce errors or save lives — and describes some patient portals but does not present evidence that greater accessibility of information causes patients to recognize more serious symptoms and therefore increase ED visits. The sources do not support the causal claim that information accessibility raises acute care/ED demand via patient self-recognition of symptoms.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10284503/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a qualitative study about making the ED itself more accessible for patients with disabilities (communication, assistive devices, electronic after-visit summaries, staff training). It documents barriers and recommends accommodations to improve patient experience and outcomes, but it does not provide evidence that increasing accessibility of information causes patients to better recognize serious symptoms and thereby increase ED visits. The citation does not support the causal claim that greater information accessibility raises acute care demand/ED utilization."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HealthIT.gov material describes how electronic health records improve provider access to clinical information, aid diagnosis, reduce errors, and can support population health and patient management (e.g., via patient portals). It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information causes patients to recognize serious symptoms and therefore increase demand for acute care or ED visits. No data or findings in the citation link information accessibility to increases in ED utilization."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information empowers patients to better recognize serious symptoms, leading them to appropriately seek acute care in the emergency department when needed. As a result, more accessible information directly increases the demand for acute care and ED visits by ensuring patients do not ignore potentially life-threatening conditions. Therefore, accessibility of information positively influences acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information empowers patients to better recognize serious symptoms, leading them to appropriately seek acute care in the emergency department when needed. As a result, more accessible information directly increases the demand for acute care and ED visits by ensuring patients do not ignore potentially life-threatening conditions. Therefore, accessibility of information positively influences acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

71. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information (such as clear instructions and guidance) directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication, as people are better informed on proper usage. Conversely, reducing accessibility of information increases the chance of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7722322/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HealthIT.gov material documents that clinician access to complete electronic health records and EHR decision support reduces prescribing errors and improves provider-driven medication safety (e.g., clinical alerts, fewer near-miss medication events, patient satisfaction with e-prescribing). It does not provide evidence that broader public or patient access to medical information leads individuals to self-diagnose and self-medicate more accurately. The source addresses provider/system-mediated improvements, not the claim that increased information accessibility directly causes decreased incorrect self-medication by individuals."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents low medication knowledge and links poor health literacy to medication errors and worse adherence, and it recommends provider/pharmacist communication and use of information leaflets to improve medication understanding and outcomes. That supports the general idea that better, accurate information can reduce incorrect medication use. However, the paper does not directly study \\"accessibility of information\\" (e.g., broader immediate access or self-diagnosis via online sources), nor does it demonstrate that increased accessibility leads to accurate self-diagnosis/self-medication; it also notes many information sources can be unreliable. Thus the claim as phrased is only partially supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7722322/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study shows patients want and value accessible, timely, and trustworthy medicines information to support autonomy, self-management and adherence, and reports that lack of information is a barrier. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that greater information accessibility leads to more accurate self-diagnosis/self-medication or a measurable reduction in incorrect medication use; it also notes limits (distrust of internet sources, variable needs) that could mitigate the effect. Thus the citation partly supports the general idea but not the specific causal claim or its magnitude/direction for incorrect use. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information empowers individuals to self-diagnose and self-medicate accurately, leading to a decrease in the incorrect use of medication. As people have immediate access to medical facts and drug instructions, they are less likely to misuse medication, making increased information accessibility positively correlated with proper medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information empowers individuals to self-diagnose and self-medicate accurately, leading to a decrease in the incorrect use of medication. As people have immediate access to medical facts and drug instructions, they are less likely to misuse medication, making increased information accessibility positively correlated with proper medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

72. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater accessibility of information enables individuals to acquire knowledge and skills, thereby raising their education level. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits learning opportunities and lowers education levels. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2640795983829398, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666412722000137', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0160791X21000403', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2025.1527851/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7225861756711982, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666412722000137", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (ScienceDirect error message) with no accessible content or evidence. Because no substantive material from the citation is provided, it cannot support the claimed causal relationship or the specific assertions (e.g., that quantity alone—regardless of quality or context—automatically increases education).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0160791X21000403", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access problem) and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore it does not support the claimed causal relationship between information accessibility and education level or any of the specific mechanisms described."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2025.1527851/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review documents that improved accessibility to educational content via technology can promote inclusion, engagement, personalization and in some cases better learning opportunities — evidence that greater access can support higher educational participation and skills. However, it does not endorse the strong causal claim that mere exposure to larger quantities of information automatically raises education level regardless of quality or context, nor does it advance the proposed mechanism (increased cognitive load from filtering information inherently strengthens intellect). The review also highlights important caveats (teacher training, infrastructure, content quality, potential demotivation or misinformation), showing access is necessary but not sufficient."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited special-issue editorial and summarized papers provide evidence that greater access to ICT and digital information can facilitate learning (e.g., enabling learning anywhere/anytime, supporting non-formal/informal learning), reduce digital-divide barriers, and in some case studies improve students’ understanding, motivation and performance. Those points support a positive link between information accessibility and educational outcomes in some contexts. However, the sources do not endorse the strong claims in the explanation about the causal mechanism (that increased exposure and cognitive filtering inherently and automatically strengthens intellect), nor do they claim that mere quantity of information \\"automatically\\" raises education level irrespective of quality or context. Thus the general positive relationship is supported, but the specific mechanistic and unconditional assertions are not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information leads to a direct rise in Education level because as individuals are exposed to more information, the cognitive load required to process and filter relevant content inherently strengthens their intellect and educational attainment. As a result, simply having access to a greater quantity of information automatically enhances Education level, regardless of the quality or context of the information available. Conversely, when Accessibility of information is limited, individuals miss out on this cognitive exercise and thus experience stagnation or decline in their Education level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information leads to a direct rise in Education level because as individuals are exposed to more information, the cognitive load required to process and filter relevant content inherently strengthens their intellect and educational attainment. As a result, simply having access to a greater quantity of information automatically enhances Education level, regardless of the quality or context of the information available. Conversely, when Accessibility of information is limited, individuals miss out on this cognitive exercise and thus experience stagnation or decline in their Education level.', 'perplexity': 1.4093170546550626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7853767982939583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08500712693194586, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0046598658014534965, 'prob_variance': 0.0697380342820948, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.885408403560522, 'avg_nll': 0.3431052286499909}

73. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not directly cause size of social network to change. While increased information access may facilitate communication, the actual formation and size of social networks depend on additional mediating variables such as willingness of social network or proactive attitude. Without a direct causal mechanism, there is no true causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://news.clemson.edu/research-into-social-media-accessibility/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9008848/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2501/JAR-2024-026', 'https://usability.yale.edu/web-accessibility/articles/social-media'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://news.clemson.edu/research-into-social-media-accessibility/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Clemson News piece reports that better digital accessibility allows more people (especially those with vision/hearing impairments) to participate, engage, and feel \\"more connected,\\" and it argues that inaccessible platforms can isolate users. That supports a general positive link between accessibility and social connectedness. However, the article does not provide evidence that increased accessibility of information directly causes people to form larger social networks, quantify network size changes, or show the specific causal mechanism (e.g., easier discovery of new friends). Therefore the claim goes beyond what the source demonstrates."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9008848/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article presents a clear theoretical argument and some empirical evidence that SNS features—especially transparency and information-rich profiles, low transaction costs, and easier search/mobilization—facilitate initiation and maintenance of ties and thus boost network size and diversity. It therefore supports a positive link between greater accessibility of information on SNSs and larger networks. However, the support is mainly conceptual and correlational (associations between SNS use/online social capital and network size) rather than conclusive, direct causal proof that increased information accessibility alone automatically causes people to expand their social circles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://usability.yale.edu/web-accessibility/articles/social-media", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document provides guidance on making social media content accessible (alt text, captions, keyboard operability, platform-specific tips) and notes that social media is a way to connect with audiences, but it contains no empirical evidence or argument that greater accessibility of information causes people to form larger social networks or that increased access to information automatically expands social circles. It addresses usability and accessibility practices, not the claimed causal relationship between information accessibility and social network size."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly causes people to form larger social networks because having more information makes it easier to find and connect with new friends. As people gain access to more data and resources online, they automatically expand their social circles.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly causes people to form larger social networks because having more information makes it easier to find