   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase in a direct causal relationship. When information becomes more accessible, individuals have greater opportunities to acquire and absorb knowledge. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits exposure to information, thereby constraining knowledge acquisition.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40561-024-00316-7', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2105061118'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Barnes et al., 1996) operationalizes knowledge accessibility and experimentally shows that greater accessibility makes knowledge more likely to be used in inferencing (e.g., \\"easily accessible knowledge was generally twice as likely to be used\\" and accessibility aids coherence/elaborative inferencing). It reports positive effects of accessibility on comprehension and inference and discusses age-related roles of accessibility. It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility or easy availability causes complacency, reduced critical thinking or deep learning, or information overload that diminishes knowledge. Therefore the sources do not support the claimed negative relationship; in fact they report the opposite effect of accessibility facilitating knowledge use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40561-024-00316-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review presents multiple findings that ease of access to AI-generated information (and over-reliance on AI dialogue systems) is associated with complacency, reduced critical/analytical thinking, diminished creativity, increased laziness, and decreased information retention. Those points align with the claim that overly accessible information can undermine deep learning and retention. However, the evidence in the citation is specific to AI dialogue systems and \\"over-reliance\\" on those tools rather than broad, general claims about all forms of information accessibility or formally demonstrated reductions in measured \\"knowledge.\\" Therefore the support is consistent but somewhat specific and indirect relative to the general explanation given."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information can actually diminish knowledge because when information is too easily available, people may become complacent and less likely to engage in critical thinking or deep learning. This constant influx of accessible information can lead to information overload, making it harder to retain and process valuable knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information can actually diminish knowledge because when information is too easily available, people may become complacent and less likely to engage in critical thinking or deep learning. This constant influx of accessible information can lead to information overload, making it harder to retain and process valuable knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

