ackground conditions (competence, authenticity, adequate information and options, social support, etc.) and links access to information (e.g., informed consent, \\"adequate information and range of options\\") to the ability to exercise autonomy. It therefore does indicate a relationship between informational conditions and autonomy-related capacities, but it does not treat \\"accessibility of information\\" and \\"need for autonomy\\" as directly causal variables nor does it provide a clear mechanism tying information accessibility to a distinct variable called \\"need for autonomy.\\" Because the source both (a) shows that informational access is relevant to autonomy and (b) does not establish a direct causal link to the specific notion \\"need for autonomy,\\" the claim that no direct causal relationship can be established is only partially supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article on \\"Autonomy\\" includes explicit discussion linking information/technology access to autonomy (e.g., deBronkart\'s argument that technological advances letting patients research medical issues promotes patient empowerment and autonomy; discussion of self-testing devices and their impact on patient autonomy). Thus the sources do not support a blanket claim of no causal or direct relationship between accessibility of information and need for autonomy; they cite contexts where information accessibility is argued to increase autonomy (and also critiques)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-10780-1_25", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly frames accessibility in intelligent/ambient environments as enabling and empowering users, and cites Ambient Assisted Living (AAL) whose ma