   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' causes 'Care avoidance behavior' or vice versa. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as feelings of fear and shame, health literacy, or accessibility of information. Therefore, they should not be directly linked in a causal loop diagram.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines the association between serious psychological distress and self-reported avoidance of seeing a doctor (and psychosocial reasons for avoidance). It does not examine experiences in emergency departments, acute-care visits, or whether an ED visit causes later avoidance. The data are cross-sectional and report associations (not causal effects) related to mental distress and general care avoidance, so they do not support the specific causal claim that stressful/overwhelming ED experiences following acute care demand lead to increased care avoidance.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing acute care demand that leads to an ED visit causes individuals to develop care avoidance behavior because the stressful and overwhelming experience in the emergency department makes them more likely to avoid seeking care in the future. This negative association with the healthcare system directly results in increased care avoidance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing acute care demand that leads to an ED visit causes individuals to develop care avoidance behavior because the stressful and overwhelming experience in the emergency department makes them more likely to avoid seeking care in the future. This negative association with the healthcare system directly results in increased care avoidance.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

