105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HCUP Statistical Brief provides descriptive data on reasons, frequencies, and patient characteristics for ED visits (e.g., treat-and-release vs. admission) and notes that some ED use reflects limited community access to primary care. It does not present any data or analysis about the availability of diagnostic resources in EDs, community awareness of ED diagnostic capabilities, or a causal/associational link between greater diagnostic resources and increased acute care demand. Therefore the explanation\'s claim about diagnostic-resource-driven increases in ED visits is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics on emergency department visit counts, rates, and some visit characteristics (e.g., percent seen within 15 minutes, numbers of visits, some topic-specific visit subsets). It does not provide any data or analysis linking the availability or capacity of diagnostic resources in EDs to changes in community demand or ED visit rates, nor evidence that awareness of diagnostic capabilities increases visits. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about diagnostic resources stimulating acute care demand is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents the growth and prominent role of diagnostic testing and imaging, discusses overuse, regional variation in utilization, and how diagnostic capacity and testing practices influence care. It therefore supports the general idea that greater diagnostic resources are associated with increased use of diagnostic testing. However, it does not provide direct evidence that increased availability of ED diagnostic resources publicly signals higher capacity and thereby causes more people in the community to choose the ED (i.e., it lacks evidence tying facility-level diagnostic availability or community awareness to higher ED visit demand). It does note related phenomena (e.g., public awareness improving timely presentation for certain conditions), but not the specific causal pathway claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources within the emergency department can signal to the community a higher capacity for comprehensive care, which in turn directly stimulates acute care demand leading to an ED visit. As patients become aware of advanced diagnostic capabilities, they are more likely to seek emergency services, resulting in a positive association between diagnostic resources and acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from seeking acute care at the ED, thus reducing overall demand.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources within the emergency department can signal to the community a higher capacity for comprehensive care, which in turn directly stimulates acute care demand leading to an ED visit. As patients become aware of advanced diagnostic capabilities, they are more likely to seek emergency services, resulting in a positive association between diagnostic resources and acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from seeking acute care at the ED, thus reducing overall demand.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review analyzes factors driving ED demand and notes that increased demand leads to congestion with clinical and organisational implications (eg, effects on patient outcomes, staff and patient satisfaction). It does not provide evidence that higher ED demand or visit volume improves healthcare professionals’ functioning, clinical skills, teamwork, or effectiveness; if anything it emphasizes strain and negative operational impacts. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents increased ED visit rates and discusses demographic, socioeconomic, and system-level implications (access, disparities, resource strain, policy responses). It does not provide evidence that higher acute care demand and more ED visits improve healthcare professionals\' functioning, provide greater opportunities for skill application, or enhance teamwork and professional growth. No cited source presents data or analysis supporting the claimed positive effect on clinician skills, experience, or performance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited metasynthesis reports patients’ reasons for choosing the ED (acuity, access barriers, ED advantages, perceived provider expertise) and discusses ED crowding and system strains, but it does not present evidence that increased ED visits improve healthcare professionals’ functioning, skills, experience, teamwork, or professional growth. The sources focus on patient perspectives and system effects, not on measured or reported positive impacts on provider functioning or professional development.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visit volumes and characteristics but contains no evidence or analysis linking higher ED demand to improved skills, experience, teamwork, or overall functioning of healthcare professionals. It documents visit counts and related metrics only, not workforce outcomes or causal effects on clinician performance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report documents trends, volumes, patient and hospital characteristics of ED use and discusses consequences of crowding (longer waits, worse outcomes) and stresses on resources (especially for behavioral health). It does not provide evidence that higher acute care demand or more ED visits improves healthcare professionals’ functioning, skills, teamwork, or professional growth. In fact, the report highlights negative effects of crowding and resource strain rather than benefits to clinician performance. Therefore the cited material does not support the positive causal claim made in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits provides healthcare professionals with greater opportunities to apply their skills and gain valuable clinical experience, thereby enhancing their overall functioning. The fast-paced environment and exposure to diverse medical cases stimulate professional growth and improve teamwork, resulting in more effective healthcare delivery.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits provides healthcare professionals with greater opportunities to apply their skills and gain valuable clinical experience, thereby enhancing their overall functioning. The fast-paced environment and exposure to diverse medical cases stimulate professional growth and improve teamwork, resulting in more effective healthcare delivery.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

