   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how increased ED demand and boarding reduce effective capacity (through bed shortages, staffing shortfalls and exit block) and discusses strategies to increase or better manage capacity (e.g., full-capacity protocols, smoothing admissions, early discharge, observation units). It does not provide evidence that administrators, when anticipating higher demand, intentionally reduce the number of available beds or staff allocations as a policy to prevent overcrowding. No citation text supports a policy-driven proactive reduction in Capacity in response to expected higher acute care demand — if anything, the sources recommend measures to expand or better allocate capacity.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides statistics on numbers and rates of emergency department visits and related metrics (visits, admissions, wait times) but contains no information about administrative policies, bed or staff allocation decisions, or any practice of reducing Capacity in anticipation of higher demand. The explanation\'s causal claim about policymakers proactively decreasing available beds and staff in response to increased ED demand is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit negatively impacts Capacity because as the emergency department anticipates higher demand, administrators proactively reduce the number of available beds and staff allocations to prevent potential overcrowding and maintain quality standards. Thus, increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit results in a policy-driven decrease in Capacity, whereas lower demand prompts administrators to expand Capacity by making additional resources available.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit negatively impacts Capacity because as the emergency department anticipates higher demand, administrators proactively reduce the number of available beds and staff allocations to prevent potential overcrowding and maintain quality standards. Thus, increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit results in a policy-driven decrease in Capacity, whereas lower demand prompts administrators to expand Capacity by making additional resources available.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

