"verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that low health literacy is associated with higher ED utilization and discusses mechanisms for health literacy driving ED use (supporting the opposite direction). It does not provide evidence that ED visits do not cause changes in an individual\'s health literacy or test reverse causation, so it cannot fully substantiate the assertion that there is no causal pathway from ED visits to health literacy change. Therefore the sources partly support the explanation (they support the alternate direction) but do not confirm absence of the claimed causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly links limited health literacy to increased ED visits and hospitalizations (health literacy → utilization) and also states health literacy is dynamic and can be affected by stress/anxiety during hospitalization (hospitalization/acute stress can impair patients’ ability to process health information). It does not provide evidence that ED visits have no causal effect on health literacy; in fact it describes mechanisms by which hospitalization-related stress and system interactions can change a person’s effective health-literacy state. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the frequency of ED visits would directly alter an individual's health literacy. Causality may exist in the opposite direction, but not as specified here.", 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2945576499068878, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376701756866648, 'min_prob': 0.1152762