   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24754725941373557, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.hssib.org.uk/patient-safety-investigations/medication-related-harm/investigation-report/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8216798190081096, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly states that medication errors contribute to hospital admissions and readmissions and documents a substantial incidence of medication errors in acute hospital/ED settings. It also describes how acute-care factors (workload, interruptions, poor communication, transitions of care, etc.) increase the risk of medication errors. Thus the sources provide evidence of causal links (medication errors → acute care demand/admissions and acute-care environments → increased medication errors), contradicting the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP medication‑errors document describes how medication errors cause patient harm and generate substantial hospital-related medical costs (including drug‑related injuries treated in hospitals). That implies medication misuse can lead to acute care (including ED/hospital) visits. The source also documents causes and mediators (patient education, system failures), but it does not support the assertion that there is no causal link; in fact it provides evidence of medication errors contributing to hospital care. Therefore the explanation claiming no causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents causes, risk factors and consequences of medication errors in acute care (ICU/OR), and even notes that medication errors can lead to extended hospitalization and ICU admission. It does not provide evidence that an acute-care visit (ED presentation) does not cause incorrect medication use, nor does it support the specific mediation claims (health literacy, compliance, chronic conditions). Because the source neither demonstrates absence of a causal link nor supports the proposed mediators — and in places implies the opposite direction (medication errors can precipitate hospital/ICU care) — the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as health literacy, therapy compliance, or chronic conditions. Without a direct causal mechanism linking these variables, there is no causal relationship between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2812395876895917, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8586572836025612, 'min_prob': 0.05212051261379274, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004778105487150483, 'prob_variance': 0.06127964564325129, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8937502396722629, 'avg_nll': 0.24782803719026525}

