ponse to individual patient visits. However, the source also notes that organizations consider external environment and pace of change when choosing/designing structure, implying structures can be adjusted in response to changing demands over time. It does not provide evidence that acute care demand or an ED visit cannot ever cause organizational change, nor does it establish a causal mechanism ruling that out. Therefore the claim that there is no causal link is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause organizational structure to change. Organizational structure refers to the formal arrangement of roles, responsibilities, and processes within an organization, which is typically determined by leadership decisions rather than patient demand. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in acute care demand leading to an ED visit would immediately alter organizational structure.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331570359411918, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339821231048525, 'min_prob': 0.03191688392872667, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003309274672821155, 'prob_variance': 0.06151411350348714, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9630886626364701, 'avg_nll': 0.2863589670927359}

