   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.3000215236451751, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/acute-care-setting', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10890971/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7255085061265384, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, admission percentages) but contains no analysis or evidence about a \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" causal pathways, or mediating factors such as accessibility or organizational structure. It therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between acute care demand and a 24/7-care mentality or that any relationship would be mediated by other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/acute-care-setting", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content to assess or to support the claim. Because there is no actual evidence or text from the citation, the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10890971/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that rising patient demand affects ED crowding and performance and emphasizes organizational, staffing, and accessibility factors as key mediators of ED outcomes and responses. However, the sources do not address or measure a social or institutional \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" nor test a direct causal link between individual acute care visits and such a mentality. Thus the citations support the role of mediating system factors but do not provide direct evidence for the absence of a causal pathway to a \\"24/7‑care mentality.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited WHO article describes acute care as a time-sensitive, system-level service that requires specific resource allocation, coordination and service-delivery models within health systems. It does not claim that episodes of acute care demand (e.g., ED visits) by themselves produce a \\"24/7-care mentality\\"; instead it emphasizes that availability and organization of acute care depend on health-system structures, resource distribution and coordination. Thus the sources support the view that any continuous/around-the-clock care orientation is mediated by system and organizational factors rather than being a direct automatic outcome of individual acute-care demand, but they do not explicitly state the absence of any direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause '24/7-care mentality', nor does '24/7-care mentality' directly result from acute care demand. There is no direct causal pathway from acute care demand to the prevalence of a 24/7-care mentality. If a relationship exists, it is more likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare accessibility or organizational structure.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5057225351147823, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7849919570912882, 'min_prob': 0.006144763767748283, 'token_prob_slope': -0.