   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.26514112901724957, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://acta-ape.org/en/article/accessibility-and-demand-at-an-emergency-care-unit-the-users-perspective/', 'https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7265572432524898, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://acta-ape.org/en/article/accessibility-and-demand-at-an-emergency-care-unit-the-users-perspective/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study describes how barriers in health-service accessibility (e.g., limited primary care hours, difficulty scheduling, easier access to technologies/medicines at emergency care) drive users to seek emergency services. That indicates a relationship between accessibility and demand (primarily accessibility → EC demand). It does not provide evidence that acute-care demand leading to an ED visit does not affect \\"accessibility of information,\\" and it does not address the specific target variable (accessibility of information). Thus the citation does not support the claim of no relationship; it actually documents a related association in the opposite direction and does not rule out causal pathways.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Census Bureau analysis explicitly reports that households without internet access had about twice as many preventable emergency visits as those with internet access and notes that internet access \\"may also play a role by limiting health care search options.\\" That documents an association (and even suggests a plausible mechanism from information access → ER visits). The explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" and \\"no clear mechanism\\" is therefore not supported by the provided source content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly links access to care and patient information/education with ED utilization (lack of information or access drives unnecessary ED visits and educating patients can reduce visits). It also notes that ED use can be difficult to track across disconnected facilities and recommends collaboration to obtain utilization information, implying a relationship between ED visits and information availability. The sources do not support the claim that there is no relationship; they describe mechanisms connecting information/accessibility and ED use and challenges in obtaining information after ED visits.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor is there a clear mechanism by which increased ED visits would directly alter information accessibility. There is no direct causal link from acute care demand to accessibility of information in either direction. Any association is likely mediated by other variables or is merely correlational.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3780856026184607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7970501492071844, 'min_prob': 0.0753356662143806, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004116921435318498, 'prob_variance': 0.07029981829654178, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0370402346333791, 'avg_nll': 0.32069529157529436}

124. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2989053106255356, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2676/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7754324/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38447488/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7086229/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7355733306990835, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of emergency department visits and related utilization statistics; it contains no information about staffing, personnel levels, staffing policies, budgeting, or causal links between ED demand and personnel. Therefore the source does not support the claim that acute care demand does not directly cause Personnel changes or that staffing is determined by other organizational factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2676/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter states that staffing levels are set by administrators and influenced by budgetary considerations and labor markets (supporting the explanation that staffing is determined by policies/planning), and presents a framework showing multiple mediating factors (capacity, organizational decisions). However, the same source also describes that staffing is managed day-by-day/shift-by-shift, that patient care needs and workload/acuity influence required hours, and provides unit-level evidence linking demand/workload to staffing effectiveness. Thus the claim that acute care demand \\"does not directly cause\\" personnel to change is too strong given the citation: demand/acuity can and does affect staffing (often via workload systems) even if administrative processes mediate formal staffing changes.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7754324/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that staffing levels are structural (driven by managerial decisions, financial constraints and policy) and that the study is observational (so causality is not established). It also documents that unexpected rises in acuity or volume affect workload and missed care, but not that immediate acute‑care demand (e.g., a single ED visit) directly changes personnel levels. The paper therefore supports the point that staffing is set by policies/budgeting and that demand effects are mediated (and not proven causal), but it does not directly address ED visits specifically and acknowledges that demand/acuity influence workload. This makes the \\"no direct causal link\\" claim only partially supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7086229/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that employed staffing (establishments) is set by staffing methods, professional judgement, budgeting and organisational decisions and is not automatically changed by single episodes of demand; it also describes that tools are used for longer-term establishment setting and for deployment/redeployment decisions (i.e., adjusting staff assignment across shifts). Thus the citation supports the point that immediate demand does not directly alter employed personnel levels and that staffing changes are mediated by policy/organisation, but it also documents that measured demand can drive real-time deployment/redeployment. The user claim’s absolute phrasing that demand “does not directly cause Personnel to change” therefore overstates the case.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38447488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines how nurse staffing levels affect patient outcomes in emergency departments (e.g., delays, leaving without being seen), not whether acute care demand directly changes personnel levels or how staffing is determined. It does not provide evidence that staffing is set by policies/budgeting nor that demand does not directly cause personnel changes. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause Personnel to change. Personnel levels are typically determined by staffing policies, budgeting, or organizational planning, not directly by the immediate demand for acute care in the ED. While rising demand may eventually influence staffing decisions, this relationship is mediated by other variables such as Capacity and Organizational structure, already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5710869995689796, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7730376147327174, 'min_prob': 0.013526502083544884, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030576338898829964, 'prob_variance': 0.08934438472014793, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9869290754818982, 'avg_nll': 0.45176773620544924}

