   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2862027498383767, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6970158380599201, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, admissions, wait times) but does not discuss causal relationships or the availability/accessibility of alternative care options nor the direction of causality between ED demand and alternative availability. It therefore does not support the explanatory claim that acute care demand does not causally determine availability of alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that availability/accessibility of alternatives (convenience, primary care access, hours, perceptions) influences ED use — supporting the claim that causality runs from availability to demand. However, the paper does not directly evaluate or provide evidence that acute care demand leading to ED visits cannot in turn affect the existence or accessibility of alternatives. In other words, the citations support the opposite-direction causal link but do not explicitly demonstrate absence of any causal effect from ED demand to availability.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents multiple interventions that change availability of non-ED alternatives (expanded clinic hours, walk‑in centers, managed care, copayments, EMS diversion) and shows that changes in availability/ policy outside the ED can alter ED utilization. That supports the claim that availability can causally affect acute care demand for the ED (i.e., the opposite direction). However, the review does not provide direct evidence that acute care demand leading to ED visits does not influence the existence or accessibility of alternatives (it does not analyze or rule out demand-driven creation or expansion of alternatives). Therefore the citation supports the reverse causal direction but does not directly demonstrate the absence of the forward causal link.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029149965937856417, 'prob_variance': 0.08191201401504841, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9636752557865477, 'avg_nll': 0.4316772415179469}

