tors (seasonal epidemics, emergencies, aging, lack of primary care) as causes of increased ED visits and overcrowding, supporting the direction \\"acute event → acute care demand/ED visit.\\" However, the paper does not explicitly state that increased ED demand/visits cannot in any way contribute to subsequent acute events; it only documents downstream harms of overcrowding (delays, increased morbidity/mortality, errors). Therefore the sources support the primary causal direction claimed but do not definitively rule out any reverse or feedback effects.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute event causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit, not the other way around. There is no causal mechanism by which increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit would cause more acute events. The direction of causality is from acute event to care demand, not vice versa.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1971514716627114, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8790373505999207, 'min_prob': 0.12865274065798912, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022866280216882182, 'prob_variance': 0.04594929803048223, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7060284073672355, 'avg_nll': 0.1799449613124276}

