   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2905056636828834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7059759744965742, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes episodes of care (including acute care and repeated ED visits) as \\"emotionally threatening and psychologically traumatizing\\" for pediatric patients, documents that children commonly report feeling afraid or anxious in healthcare settings, gives ED case examples linking repeated ED care to acute stress/trauma, and discusses shame-inducing topics in medical encounters. Thus the sources support a direct causal link between acute care/ED visits and feelings of fear (and in some contexts shame), contradicting the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that presentations to EDs are frequently followed by negative emotional impacts — including shame, fear and humiliation — but attributes these outcomes to specific aspects of the ED encounter (judgemental staff attitudes, coercive/restrictive practices, long waits, lack of privacy and unmet needs). It therefore supports the explanation’s emphasis on mediating factors rather than a simple, direct causal link from “acute care demand → ED visit” to feelings of fear and shame. However, the review also reports an association between ED presentation and those negative emotions and does not empirically rule out any direct effect of presenting to ED per se; it is qualitative and does not establish causal absence. Thus the sources support the mediation argument but do not conclusively prove there is no direct causal pathway.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that clinical and care encounters can and often do evoke shame (and shame-related fear/anxiety), especially where power differentials, exposure of vulnerabilities, or prior trauma are present. It describes mechanisms by which health-care interactions can trigger acute shame and shame-anxiety and thus undermine help-seeking. Therefore the source does not support the claim that an ED visit cannot directly cause feelings of fear and shame; instead it provides theoretical and empirical grounds for a direct pathway (while also noting moderating factors)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established direct causal mechanism between these variables without considering mediating factors such as prior experiences, health literacy, or cultural context. Any observed association is likely mediated or coincidental rather than causal. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4482664484834167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.781716027065396, 'min_prob': 0.05984694232772836, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035268306270290744, 'prob_variance': 0.08439354063183256, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0741392422368186, 'avg_nll': 0.3703672884190816}

