   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.23314726083857487, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6978874/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8442096/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7972799306036924, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review catalogs many determinants of therapy adherence (patient beliefs, health literacy, healthcare-system factors, disease acuity, etc.) and explicitly notes that non‑adherence is associated with increased urgent care/hospital visits. It therefore supports the idea that associations between healthcare encounters and adherence are plausibly mediated by factors like health literacy, access, motivation and disease characteristics. However, the review does not examine emergency department visit frequency as a distinct exposure nor demonstrate that there is no direct causal pathway from ED visits to changes in adherence. It neither provides evidence ruling out a direct effect nor specifically addresses ED visits, so the strong claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" is not fully supported by the citation alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6978874/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited realist review examines why patients use emergency/urgent care and identifies mechanisms (risk minimization, need for speed, following others\' advice, poor access to GP, etc.). It does not examine or provide evidence about whether ED visits change patients\' adherence to therapy (medication or treatment compliance). The paper\'s use of the word \\"compliance\\" refers to foll