lth systems have surge plans and tools (immediate bed availability, reverse triage, alternate care sites, load‑balancing, toolkits and exercises) and that they can and do implement measures to expand capacity and establish alternative care options during mass casualty incidents, pandemics, and other acute surges. However, the sources also describe limits, delays, and variability in effectiveness (staffing, supplies, rural constraints, time to external assistance, modest gains from reverse triage, uneven ability to expand capacity). They do not support the stronger claim that an acute event will directly and reliably \\"always\\" or immediately increase the availability of ED alternatives or that hospitals universally and immediately promote such alternatives.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.rand.org/content/dam/rand/pubs/research_reports/RR200/RR280/RAND_RR280.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The RAND report documents that EDs are heavily used because patients often lack timely alternatives and that hospitals increasingly rely on EDs (including observation units) to manage acute cases. It does not provide evidence that experiencing an acute event causes hospitals to immediately expand or promote alternative care options; if anything the report emphasizes limited alternatives and that patients go to EDs because viable alternatives are often unavailable. Therefore the cited source does not support the asserted causal claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing an acute event directly increases the availability of alternatives for ED because hospitals typically respond to surges in acute incidents by immediately expanding and promoting alternative care options. This ensures that as acute events occur, patients always have more alternatives readily available to