   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Acute event' does not cause 'Feelings of fear and shame' because both variables are undefined ('None'), so no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401986/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7753093/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SAMHSA chapter documents that acute/traumatic events commonly produce emotional reactions including fear and shame and lists fear, shame, anxiety, sadness, numbness, etc., among immediate and later responses. It also notes acute stress reactions often involve intense fear and that many people show fear- and shame-related responses. However, the source explicitly emphasizes variability (responses depend on individual, event type, context) and that not all acute events \\"automatically\\" or \\"always\\" produce fear and shame. Therefore the claim’s absolute wording (“automatically” / “always … regardless of the event\'s nature”) is not supported, though a positive relationship is supported in a qualified sense.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes physiological and psychological stress responses to acute stress and notes that acute stress can produce arousal, vigilance and fear-related responses (e.g., amygdala involvement; epinephrine associated with escape/attack/fear). However, it also describes variability in stress reactions (including eustress that can be positive) and links acute stress to a range of outcomes (anxiety, PTSD, etc.). The source does not state that every acute event \\"automatically\\" produces shame, nor that fear and shame are the invariant psychological reaction to any acute event. Therefore the blanket claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401986/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how traumatic (acute) events can produce strong fear responses and that PTSD involves dysregulation of multiple emotions including shame and guilt; it specifically describes fear in the acute aftermath and how fear sensitization can lead to broader emotion problems. However, the paper does not claim — and in fact explicitly rejects — that any sudden incident \\"automatically\\" or \\"always\\" produces both fear and shame regardless of context. It frames responses as conditional (e.g., trauma type, developmental history, chronicity) and variable across individuals and situations. Therefore the citations support a link between traumatic acute events and fear (and in some cases shame), but do not support the absolutist claim that every acute event invariably causes fear and shame.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that threatening or sudden stimuli can produce fear and anxiety (describing neural circuits, autonomic responses, and that sudden noises or threats elicit innate fear). However it does not support the blanket claim that any acute event \\"automatically\\" causes fear and shame in all people regardless of context. The paper distinguishes fear from anxiety, emphasizes context, stimulus type, and individual differences in responses, and discusses many situations where responses vary. It also does not treat shame (a distinct social emotion) as an obligatory reaction to acute events. Therefore the claim is an overgeneralization beyond what the source supports."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7753093/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study reports a wide range of peritraumatic reactions. While fear was commonly reported (fear for others 78%, fear for self 70%), shame/guilt was reported by only about 20% of participants, and many survivors described other responses (calmness, hyperfocus, help-giving, dissociation, positive affect). The article emphasizes variability and that reactions are not uniform across individuals or moments. It does not support the claim that any acute event automatically and always produces fear and shame regardless of context."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute events directly cause feelings of fear and shame because any sudden incident automatically triggers these emotional responses in the human brain. Whenever an acute event occurs, the psychological reaction is always fear and shame, regardless of the event's nature.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Acute events directly cause feelings of fear and shame because any sudden incident automatically triggers these emotional responses in the human brain. Whenever an acute event occurs, the psychological reaction is always fear and shame, regardless of the event's nature.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

