   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables; they may be related through other mediating factors, but not directly. Changes in one do not causally result in changes in the other.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://nationalhealthcouncil.org/blog/limited-access-poverty-and-barriers-to-accessible-health-care/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12954-024-01133-4'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents the types of financial barriers insured, low-income residents face and how health advocates help resolve many issues via navigation, supplemental programs, and negotiations. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing availability of alternatives to emergency departments (or that competition) automatically reduces financial barriers or lowers prices. The paper emphasizes complexity, persistent gaps (e.g., uncovered benefits, high cost-sharing), and the need for targeted assistance and policy changes rather than showing a direct causal effect of more alternatives reducing financial barriers.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12954-024-01133-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited focus-group study documents substantial financial barriers to OUD treatment (direct and indirect costs) and notes that increased insurance coverage or public funding (e.g., Medicaid expansion) can make treatment more affordable and increase uptake. It does not, however, provide evidence that increasing the availability of \\"alternatives\\" (or market competition) by itself will automatically lower prices or directly cause reductions in financial barriers. The specific causal mechanism asserted (\\"competition always lowers prices\\" and alternatives automatically reduce financial barriers) is not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly causes a reduction in financial barriers because having more options inherently makes treatment more affordable, as competition always lowers prices. Therefore, as alternatives increase, financial barriers will automatically decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly causes a reduction in financial barriers because having more options inherently makes treatment more affordable, as competition always lowers prices. Therefore, as alternatives increase, financial barriers will automatically decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

