204. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.3008307225121728, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.628252/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.762229353985784, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article extensively documents how shame is evoked by and influences engagement with health and social services (e.g., avoidance, missed appointments, clinician interactions) and argues for shame-sensitive practice. It does not examine or provide evidence about the effect of the availability of alternatives to emergency departments on people\'s feelings of fear or shame, nor does it demonstrate the absence of a direct causal pathway. While it supports the idea that shame-related avoidance can mediate service use, it does not support the specific claim that availability of ED alternatives has no direct causal effect on fear and shame.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.628252/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is a narrative review about emotional dysregulation (ED) in children and adolescents and does not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (interpreted here as availability of alternatives to emergency department services) or any health‑service–access variables. Although the review does address emotions such as fear, guilt and shame in relation to emotional dysregulation and interpersonal reactions, it contains no evidence about a (non)causal link between availability of alternatives to emergency care and changes in feelings of fear or shame. Therefore the explanation (that there is no direct cau