205. Capacity-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Capacity does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no specified mechanism by which changes in capacity would alter acute care demand leading to an ED visit without additional mediating variables such as accessibility or availability of alternatives. Additionally, without clear definitions and spatial or temporal context, a direct causal link cannot be established between these two variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10288334/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report analyzes trends and drivers of ED utilization (insurance coverage, access, mental health/SUD prevalence, urgent care/health center availability, Medicaid expansion, rural hospital characteristics, and capacity constraints such as psychiatric bed shortages). It does not present evidence that increasing overall health‑system capacity (more staff/resources) directly causes more acute care demand or higher ED visit rates. Where capacity is discussed, the report highlights constraints (e.g., limited behavioral health beds can increase boarding) or that expansion of urgent-care sites has not clearly reduced ED use — but it does not support a causal claim that increasing capacity induces more acute illnesses or increases ED visits. Therefore the citation does not substantiate the stated positive causal relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10288334/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports trends in ED capacity and ED use (changes in numbers of beds, treatment stations, and visits) and concludes that capacity did not proportionally expand with rising demand. It presents observational descriptive data and discusses possible explanations (population growth, acuity, upcoding, bed shortages), but it does not provide evidence that increasing capacity directly causes more acute care demand or more ED visits. In fact, some reported patterns (e.g., increased treatment stations per million while visits per station decreased) do not support a causal positive effect of capacity on demand. Therefore the citation does not support the causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that EDs expanded diagnostic/treatment capabilities and that ED visit volume and care intensity rose from 2007–2016. However, it does not provide evidence that increases in healthcare \\"capacity\\" causally produced more acute health problems or that greater capacity directly induced higher acute care demand; it instead reports associations and offers alternative explanations (policy, insurance changes, shifts in care setting). The citation therefore supports an observed co-occurrence of greater capacity/resources and higher ED use, but not the causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that ED visits rose from 2006–2014 and that ED admission rates fell, and it discusses demographic and policy explanations (eg, aging, insurance changes, outpatient pathways, ACA). It does not provide evidence that increasing healthcare \\"capacity\\" (more resources or staff) causes greater incidence of acute health issues or directly drives increased ED visits, nor does it establish the causal mechanism asserted. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about capacity leading to more acute care demand is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NCHS trend analysis documents patterns and correlates of ED use (age, race, insurance, poverty, access) and notes associations over time but does not provide evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity causally increases acute care demand or that more resources/staff make people more likely to experience acute health issues. The study explicitly does not establish causality and focuses on utilization trends and access-related drivers, not a positive causal effect of capacity on demand as claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing healthcare system capacity directly causes more acute care demand, leading to an increase in emergency department (ED) visits. With more available resources and staff, people are more likely to experience acute health issues that require immediate attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing healthcare system capacity directly causes more acute care demand, leading to an increase in emergency department (ED) visits. With more available resources and staff, people are more likely to experience acute health issues that require immediate attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

206. Capacity-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Capacity does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause capacity. Without specific definitions or identified causal mechanisms linking these variables, there is no established direct causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9339182/', 'https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring', 'https://www.niskanencenter.org/healthcare-abundance-an-agenda-to-strengthen-healthcare-supply/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between hospital financial performance and quality/safety outcomes and discusses how financial resources can enable quality improvement. It does not provide evidence that increased healthcare capacity directly reduces patients\' financial barriers, that expanding hospital/clinic capacity causes lower charges, or that expansion eliminates most out-of-pocket expenses. The mechanism and causal claim about capacity → lower patient costs are not supported by the paper\'s data or conclusions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines nonfinancial (including availability/capacity) versus affordability barriers and finds they are distinct and often coexist. It discusses increasing provider supply to address availability barriers but does not provide evidence that expanding healthcare capacity directly lowers prices or out-of-pocket costs for patients, nor that greater capacity \\"automatically\\" eliminates financial barriers. Therefore the claim that increased capacity directly reduces financial barriers and charges patients less is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9339182/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents complex, context-dependent relationships between hospital resources, financing policies and patient out-of-pocket payments during economic crises. It describes how funding cuts, payment reforms, service delivery changes and stronger primary/community care can affect financial protection, but it does not provide evidence that simply increasing healthcare capacity automatically lowers patient charges or eliminates most out-of-pocket expenses. The review emphasizes trade-offs, variable effects by financing system and context, and that cost-containment or capacity changes can even worsen financial protection; it therefore does not support the claimed direct, automatic negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report documents rising hospital expenses, inadequate reimbursement, workforce and supply-chain pressures, and growing financial strain on hospitals — factors that undermine hospitals\' ability to lower charges. The report does not provide evidence that expanding capacity automatically reduces patient out-of-pocket costs or eliminates financial barriers; if anything, it describes pressures that would make lower patient charges less likely. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased capacity in healthcare systems directly reduces financial barriers for patients because more available resources automatically translate to lower costs. As hospitals and clinics expand their capacity, they can afford to charge patients less, eliminating most out-of-pocket expenses.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased capacity in healthcare systems directly reduces financial barriers for patients because more available resources automatically translate to lower costs. As hospitals and clinics expand their capacity, they can afford to charge patients less, eliminating most out-of-pocket expenses.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

