   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.29799165244444153, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-mechanism', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_model'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7077353571251013, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains the general principle that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal claims require defined variables, temporal order, and a plausible mechanism (and control for confounders). The explanation’s assertion—that without explicit definitions or a clear causal mechanism there is no basis to establish causality between \\"Capacity\\" and \\"Equipment\\"—matches the guidance in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Causality article stresses that causal claims require clearly specified variables, evidence of temporal/mechanistic connection, and appropriate methods (experiments, causal graphs, counterfactuals) — and that mere correlation or undefined terms are insufficient to establish causation. That supports the explanation that, absent definitions or a clear mechanism/context, one cannot validly claim Capacity causes Equipment or vice versa."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_model", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that causal claims require a specified causal model with defined variables, mechanisms, and interventions; associations alone do not imply causation and one cannot establish causal direction without a model or contextual definitions. Therefore the statement that, absent definitions or mechanisms, there is no basis to claim Capacity → Equipment or Equipment → Capacity is consistent with the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Stanford Encyclopedia entry emphasizes that causal claims require well-specified variables and an account of causal mechanisms (or interventionist/invariance conditions) and that mere labels or undeclared relations provide no basis for claiming causation. It also distinguishes constitutive (part–whole) versus etiological relations and stresses the need for definitions, interventions, or mechanistic detail to justify causal explanations. Therefore the source supports the conclusion that, absent definitions or a clear causal mechanism, one cannot justify a causal relation between \\"Capacity\\" and \\"Equipment.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-mechanism", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content about \\"Capacity\\" or \\"Equipment\\" or their definitions/mechanisms, so it cannot support the claim that no causal relationship exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.8, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Capacity' does not directly cause 'Equipment', nor does 'Equipment' directly cause 'Capacity', as both variables lack definitions and any clear causal mechanism. Without explicit definitions or context, there is no basis for establishing a causal relationship in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.8, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5091044984414903, 'mean_token_prob': 0.77259