275. Chronic addiction-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes healthy lifestyle to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased chronic addiction leads to behaviors (such as substance abuse, poor nutrition, and lack of exercise) that undermine a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction directly supports improvements in healthy lifestyle. The relationship is typically inverse and direct, not mediated by other variables in this context.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326198/', 'https://www.gatewayfoundation.org/blog/benefits-structure-addiction-recovery/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326198/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article (the Lifestyle Balance Model paper) documents that recovery from substance use disorder is associated with improvements in lifestyle domains (housing, employment, social support, health behaviors) and discusses how building \\"recovery capital\\" and recovery-oriented interventions promote healthier, balanced living. It therefore supports the part of the explanation that overcoming addiction can foster commitment to positive habits and wellness. However, the sources do not provide evidence that chronic addiction itself \\"often leads\\" people to become more health‑aware and proactively adopt a healthier lifestyle prior to or because of the addiction; rather, the literature emphasizes that lifestyle imbalance perpetuates addiction and that positive lifestyle changes tend to occur as part of recovery. Thus the claim as stated (that chronic addiction often motivates adoption of a healthy lifestyle) is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.gatewayfoundation.org/blog/benefits-structure-addiction-recovery/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Gateway Foundation article describes how recovery and sobriety create opportunities to adopt structure, routines, and healthier habits (exercise, sleep, diet, hygiene) and how overcoming addiction can build self-control and improve self-esteem—supporting the idea that recovery fosters a healthier lifestyle and commitment to positive habits. However, the source does not substantiate the specific claim that chronic addiction itself \\"often leads individuals to become more aware of their health\\" prior to recovery; the evidence focuses on changes during/after recovery rather than addiction prompting health awareness.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Chronic addiction often leads individuals to become more aware of their health, motivating them to adopt a healthier lifestyle as a form of self-improvement. The experience of overcoming addiction can also strengthen one's commitment to positive habits and wellness practices, thereby fostering a healthy lifestyle.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction often leads individuals to become more aware of their health, motivating them to adopt a healthier lifestyle as a form of self-improvement. The experience of overcoming addiction can also strengthen one's commitment to positive habits and wellness practices, thereby fostering a healthy lifestyle.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

276. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience impaired judgment, motivation, and executive functioning, which directly reduce their ability or willingness to comply with prescribed therapy. Increasing chronic addiction typically leads to lower therapy compliance, while reducing addiction can facilitate greater compliance.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2917861982862473, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7091397753903512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence-based treatments, retention as an outcome, and pharmacological/behavioral interventions for substance use disorders, but it does not present evidence that \\"chronic addiction\\" produces a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions that reduces perceived effectiveness and thereby causes lower therapy compliance. The article discusses treatment retention, duration, and some pharmacologic tolerance/medication issues, but it does not support the specific causal mechanism (physiological tolerance to psychotherapy/therapeutic interventions leading to disengagement) nor the claim that reducing addiction restores physiological responsiveness to therapy in the way described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORT