 citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The citation supports that addiction is a chronic condition, can produce physiological tolerance to substances, and impairs functioning (which can make treatment more difficult). However, it does not provide evidence that individuals develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions themselves, nor that such tolerance specifically reduces perceived effectiveness of therapy and causes disengagement — or that reducing addiction \\"restores the body\'s responsiveness to therapy\\" as described. Thus some elements (chronicity, impaired ability to engage in life/treatment) are supported, but the central mechanistic claim about tolerance to therapy and restored responsiveness is not shown."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes substance use disorder, notes tolerance to substances, that treatment is challenging, relapse is common, and that commitment to treatment affects prognosis—but it does not state that longstanding addiction produces physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions, nor that such tolerance reduces perceived effectiveness of therapy and directly causes lower therapy compliance. The specific causal mechanism and claim that reducing addiction restores \\"the body\'s responsiveness to therapy\\" are not supported by the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Chronic addiction leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because individuals with longstanding addictive behaviors develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions over time. This tolerance diminishes the perceived effectiveness of therapy, causing individuals to disengage from treatment protocols. As a result, higher levels of chronic addiction inherently cause lower therapy compliance, while minimizing addiction can restore the body's responsiveness to therapy and thus increase compliance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because individuals with longstanding addictive behaviors develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions over time. This tolerance diminishes the perceived effectiveness of therapy, causing individuals to disengage from treatment protocols. As a result, higher levels of chronic addiction inherently cause lower therapy compliance, while minimizing addiction can restore the body's responsiveness to therapy and thus increase compliance.", 'perplexity': 1.5066974702506495, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7715710306573372, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03224499465635279, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004735732062978135, 'prob_variance': 0.08513918551034122, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9781124349525312, 'avg_nll': 0.40992014982366265}

277. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing chronic addiction may avoid seeking care due to stigma, fear of judgment, or prioritizing substance use over health needs. Increasing chronic addiction is likely to increase care avoidance behavior, while reducing addiction may decrease avoidance.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review documents that stigma from health professionals is common and constitutes a barrier to access and adherence to care for people with substance use disorders; it reports large treatment gaps and reduced involvement by providers. It does not provide evidence that chronic addiction reduces care avoidance or that people with addiction more often seek and engage in healthcare because of frequent provider interactions. Therefore the explanation is contradicted by — rather than supported in — the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic condition and outlines treatment options and the need to seek help, but it does not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction are more likely to seek healthcare, have frequent provider interactions that build trust, or show reduced care avoidance. The cited material urges seeking treatment but does not claim or document a net decrease in care avoidance among people with addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes what substance use disorder is, treatment options, and encourages seeking care, but it does not present evidence that chronic addiction reduces care avoidance, that people with chronic addiction are more likely to seek healthcare, or that frequent healthcare interactions in this population foster trust and lower avoidance. The citation does not support the causal/empirical claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA source emphasizes that stigma and discrimination create barriers to treatment and often cause people with substance use disorders to avoid seeking care. It presents evidence that individuals may fear disclosing use, receive poorer quality care, and have reduced access to services — all supporting increased, not decreased, care avoidance. The source does not provide evidence that chronic addiction leads to more frequent, trust-building healthcare interactions that reduce avoidance; rather, it documents the opposite effects of stigma on care engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA material defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder, describes causes, consequences, and risk factors, but does not present evidence that people with chronic addiction 