   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through direct causal mechanisms such as overdose, withdrawal, or health complications related to substance use. As chronic addiction increases, individuals are more likely to experience acute medical crises that require emergency department care. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction decreases the likelihood of such acute events, lowering ED demand. The relationship is direct and not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37211655/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434658/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2797101/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37211655/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports substantial ED use (36%) and hospitalization (41%) among patients with substance-related disorders and identifies factors that increase ED use; it recommends more chronic care and outreach to reduce ED visits. It provides no evidence that long-term substance use lowers ED demand via tolerance or improved coping. The citation therefore does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434658/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited overview reviews barriers and facilitators to SUD treatment (access, stigma, retention, service gaps) and notes high healthcare costs and unmet treatment need. It does not present evidence that chronic addiction reduces acute care demand or ED visits, nor does it provide data showing higher tolerance or improved coping in long-term users leads to fewer acute health crises. If anything, the citation emphasizes increa