279. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Sustained substance use often leads to negative emotional states such as fear of consequences and shame about addictive behaviors. Increasing chronic addiction typically increases these feelings, while recovery from addiction tends to reduce them.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4380644/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2732004/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that shame and guilt are generally associated with greater or sustained substance use and can act as barriers to recovery (e.g., higher initial shame predicted slower decreases in stimulant use; higher drug use predicted slower decreases in shame). It frames shame and guilt as perpetuating or resulting from substance use rather than showing that chronic addiction builds resilience and reduces fear or shame. The paper does not provide evidence that addiction diminishes negative emotions; on the contrary it documents positive or bidirectional associations between substance use and negative self‑conscious emotions.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4380644/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Koob review describes addiction as producing negative emotional states (dysphoria, anxiety, hyperkatifeia), recruitment of brain stress systems (CRF, dynorphin, norepinephrine) and a loss of reward function — i.e., it argues addiction increases, not decreases, negative affect. While the paper discusses stress-buffering systems (NPY