   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience stigma, fear of judgment, or lack of motivation, which directly increases their likelihood of avoiding healthcare. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction would decrease healthcare avoidance because the main causal drivers are alleviated. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/addiction-health', 'https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review documents persistent stigma by health professionals, barriers to care, and large treatment gaps (e.g., only ~7% receiving minimally adequate treatment); it emphasizes that stigma and structural factors reduce access and involvement in care. It does not provide evidence that chronic addiction increases help-seeking or reduces healthcare avoidance; if anything, the sources indicate the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter notes that people with substance use disorders are over-represented in general health settings (emergency departments, hospitals) and often access the health care system for acute or chronic medical problems, which supports the idea they have m