   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.22789623840788026, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10528354/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8121687299615626, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly argues for reciprocal (bidirectional) causal links between addiction and the social environment. It cites empirical evidence that peers\' drug use predicts individual use and that substance use alters intimate relationships and group affiliations, and it discusses social-network mechanisms (modeling, social reinforcement, metacontingencies, AA/NA) that causally influence drug use and recovery. These citations contradict the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists between chronic addiction and willingness of the social network to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10528354/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes extensive preclinical and human evidence that social context and social-network factors both influence substance use initiation, escalation, relapse, and recovery, and that addiction in turn alters social networks. It also describes interventions (CRAFT, peer recovery, mutual-help, couples therapy) that explicitly leverage social-network willingness to change behavior. Therefore the sources contradict the claim of no direct causal relationship between chronic addiction and willingness of the social network."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explicitly describes bidirectional links between addiction and a person\'s social network: it states addiction \\"can significantly impact ... relationships,\\" can \\"damage personal relationships\\" and \\"harm family members,\\" and notes social factors (use by household members or peers) increase risk. It also says \\"supportive friends, family members and healthcare providers play an essential role in effective treatment\\" and recommends support groups. These passages indicate social network willingness and behavior both influence addiction risk/treatment and are affected by addiction, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not directly cause willingness of social network to change, nor does willingness of social network directly cause chronic addiction. No clear causal mechanism exists between these two variables in either direction. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2415502159939513, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8652251485781354, 'min_prob': 0.055451263658619035, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004948857625053965, 'prob_variance': 0.05193669548046139, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7962061766506094, 'avg_nll': 0.21636077298856873}

