: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic brain disorder that changes brain chemistry and can harm physical and mental health, implying cognitive and mental effects rather than stating no relationship. It does not support the claim that \\"both variables are undefined\\" or that no measurable/causal relationship is possible. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder that produces functional changes in brain circuits involved in judgment, decision‑making, learning, memory, and behavior control, and notes lasting brain changes after drug use. That directly contradicts the claim that there is no measurable or causal relationship; the source implies addiction can impair cognitive functions. The explanation given (variables undefined so no relationship) is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes substance use disorder as a disease that affects the brain and behavior and lists cognitive problems (difficulty concentrating or remembering, memory problems, decreased mental sharpness, brain damage from inhalants, long-term mental effects from some drugs). That evidence indicates chronic addiction can produce cognitive impairment, so it does not support the claim that there is no measurable