onic addiction and diagnostic resources; if anything it indicates a connection.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes specific diagnostic procedures and referrals used to diagnose addiction (questions, physical exams, blood/urine tests, referrals to psychiatrists/counselors), which implies a direct relationship from the condition (chronic addiction) to the use of diagnostic resources. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct or mediated link between addiction and diagnostic resources; therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page describes what addiction is and discusses diagnosis, tests, and available treatment resources, but it does not address whether chronic addiction causes diagnostic resources to change (or vice versa) or state that no causal link exists. The source neither provides evidence of the asserted \\"no relationship\\" nor documents causal mechanisms in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/addiction-substance-use-disorders/what-is-a-substance-use-disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited psychiatry.org material describes substance use disorder as a chronic condition that requires formal assessment/diagnosis and effective, readily available treatment, and notes that many who meet criteria do not receive help. It therefore implies a relationship between addiction and the need for diagnostic resources (and that diagnostic resource availability affects treatment access). 