" between chronic addiction and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source explicitly discusses the role of family and friends in helping people overcome addiction (help from \\"family, friends, support groups,\\" staging interventions involving family/friends) and lists family problems and lack of family involvement as risk/complicating factors. Those passages imply interactions in which addiction affects family members (leading to informal support or burden) and family/friend support can influence addiction outcomes. Therefore the citations do not support a conclusion of “no relationship” between chronic addiction and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder that causes serious problems for the person and \\"their loved ones\\" and emphasizes family/home environmental factors that influence risk. It does not discuss \\"informal care\\" explicitly, but it does imply downstream effects on family members. Because the citation indicates impacts on loved ones (which could reasonably lead to informal caregiving) rather than providing evidence that no causal relationship exists, it does not support the claim that there is no relationship between chronic addiction and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA treatment-and-recovery text explicitly discusses family involvement, family therapy, and social supports as affecting treatment and recovery, and it notes that addiction can disrupt family life and functioning. Although it does not use the phrase \\"informal care,\\" the content implies bidirectional influences between addiction and family/social support (which encompasses informal caregiving). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Chronic addiction' does not cause 'Informal care' to change, nor does 'Informal care' cause 'Chronic addiction' to change, based on the information provided. There is no direct causal mechanism described or implied between these variables. Without clear definitions or context, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3374876611053717, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8386898251437048, 'min_prob': 0.07739662210303445, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032017729822413203, 'prob_variance': 0.07247630976624016, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0669971670948213, 'avg_nll': 0.2907929744036927}

