   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.31183653976640635, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772724623000665'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7172741330488994, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review documents that healthcare professionals hold negative beliefs and reduced involvement in care toward people with substance use disorders and links patient addiction characteristics (e.g., relapse, comorbidity, type of substance) and structural factors to changes in professionals\' attitudes and care practices. It therefore provides evidence of associations between patient addiction and healthcare professional behavior; it does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal effect, nor does it support the asserted absence of any relationship. Thus the explanation\'s claim of \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Hazelden piece describes substance misuse and addiction among healthcare professionals as causing safety issues and professional impairment (e.g., practicing while under the influence, disciplinary actions, higher relapse risk, need for specialized treatment and monitoring), which implies a direct impact on clinicians\' functioning. That directly contradicts the claim that chronic addiction does not change healthcare professionals\' functioning and that any association would only be indirect/mediated.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes that substance use disorder leads to impaired behavior and functioning, including \\"problems at work,\\" \\"declining performance at work, absenteeism and eventual loss of employment,\\" and difficulty meeting obligations. That indicates a direct effect of chronic addiction on an individual\'s job functioning. The explanation\'s claim that chronic addiction does not directly change healthcare professionals\' functioning (only via mediators) is not supported by the cited material, which documents direct impacts on work performance.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772724623000665", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a non-functional ScienceDirect link returning an error with no accessible content, so it provides no evidence for or against the claimed relationship. Because no supporting material is available, the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no direct causal link from chronic addiction to the functioning of healthcare professionals within the system as defined. Any association would likely be mediated through other variables, such as increased healthcare demand or resource allocation, rather than a direct effect.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.520519614626049, 'mean_token_prob': 0.76986496869