307. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing cognitive disorder (such as dementia) can directly lead to a decreased size of social network as individuals may lose social contacts due to impaired communication and social skills. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder can help maintain or increase the size of a person's social network.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9207251/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3385686/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and sources summarize extensive evidence that stronger social networks and greater social engagement are associated with better cognitive health, and that smaller networks, living alone, and low contact are risk factors for dementia and cognitive decline. They describe bidirectional effects (poor cognition can lead to social isolation) but provide no evidence that cognitive disorders lead people to actively build larger social networks or that cognitive disorders are associated with larger network size. Thus the explanation that cognitive disorders are positively associated with larger social networks is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports correlations between brain structure/function and social network size in healthy female participants (explicitly excluding individuals with psychological, developmental, or neurological disorders). It does not present data showing that cognitive disorders are associated with larger social networks, nor does it provide evidence that people with cognitive disorders actively seek larger networks as a coping mechanism. Therefore the source does not support the claim linking cognitive disorders to increased social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9207251/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that larger social network size (and greater contact frequency/intimacy) is associated with better cognitive function (i.e., higher K‑MMSE scores) in middle‑aged and older adults. It does not provide evidence that having a cognitive disorder leads people to seek or have larger social networks; if anything the cross‑sectional data and the authors\' discussion emphasize the opposite interpretation (larger networks correlate with better cognition and reverse causation—cognitive decline reducing networks—is a concern). The paper contains no empirical support for the proposed coping‑mechanism explanation that cognitive disorders are associated with larger network size.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review analyzes how social connectedness (support, networks, isolation) affects depression and anxiety and generally reports that smaller networks and greater isolation predict worse mental health or cognitive outcomes. It does not provide evidence that cognitive disorders lead people to seek or have larger social networks; no findings in the review support a positive association from cognitive disorder to larger network size or the proposed coping-seeking mechanism. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3385686/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper argues that greater social-cognitive capacity (larger neocortex / orbitofrontal cortex and better mentalizing) is associated with larger social network size and that cognitive constraints limit network size; it also finds no evidence that Internet/SNS use reliably increases offline network size. It does not provide evidence that having a cognitive disorder leads people to seek or obtain larger social networks — if anything, the cited work implies the opposite (cognitive impairment would limit network size). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that individuals with cognitive disorders actively seek out larger social networks as a coping mechanism, believing that more social connections will provide greater support and cognitive stimulation. This increased engagement could, in theory, help them manage or even improve their cognitive symptoms. Therefore, cognitive disorders may actually be associated with a larger social network size.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that individuals with cognitive disorders actively seek out larger social networks as a coping mechanism, believing that more social connections will provide greater support and cognitive stimulation. This increased engagement could, in theory, help them manage or even improve their cognitive symptoms. Therefore, cognitive disorders may actually be associated with a larger social network size.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

308. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause cognitive disorder. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in cognitive disorder do not directly lead to changes in need for autonomy. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5209928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that mental illnesses (including disorders with cognitive impairment) tend to undermine patients\' autonomy and that autonomy, empowerment and regaining self-determination are important aims in recovery. It therefore establishes that autonomy is relevant and valued by patients and that loss of autonomy occurs in cognitive/psychiatric disorders. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that experiencing a cognitive disorder causes an increased \\"need for autonomy\\" (i.e., a compensatory increase in desire for independence driven by frustration with dependence). The causal claim that cognitive disorders directly raise the individual\'s need for autonomy as a compensatory response is not explicitly supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219893/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines autonomy as a job resource, how task-related cognitive demands interact with autonomy, and health outcomes in employees. It does not study people with cognitive disorders nor provide evidence that experiencing a cognitive disorder increases an individual\'s need for autonomy. While the paper treats autonomy as an important resource/need in work contexts and discusses cognitive demands, it does not support the specific causal claim that cognitive disorders directly raise a person\'s need for autonomy or that dependence from cognitive impairment produces a compensatory increased desire for independence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how autonomy is central in mental health care, how mental disorders can affect the exercise of autonomy, and argues for strengthening (relational) autonomy for people with mental illness. That supports the general idea that autonomy-related concerns are important for people with cognitive/mental disorders and that clinicians should promote autonomy. However, the paper does not provide evidence or an explicit claim that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases an individual\'s need for autonomy (e.g., via frustration with dependence) or that such disorders causally raise the desire to act independently. Therefore the support is indirect and incomplete."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5209928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how living with chronic disease or disability increases the importance of autonomy — framing autonomy as retaining control over daily life and reconstructing identity when dependence rises. This supports the general idea that health-related impairments (and resulting dependence) make autonomy more salient and that patients seek control and self-respect. However, the source treats chronic disease/disability broadly (not specifically \\"cognitive disorder\\") and offers conceptual/ethical discussion rather than direct evidence that cognitive disorders produce a measurable increase in \\"need for autonomy.\\" Thus the support is conceptual but not specific or empirical for cognitive disorders per se."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article is a conceptual review showing that mental illnesses (including disorders with cognitive impairment such as schizophrenia) disrupt personal autonomy (competence and authenticity) and that recovery/empowerment involves restoring autonomy. It gives examples of patients trying to regain control (e.g., OCD, anorexia) and discusses reduced autonomy from cognitive deficits and stigma. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that experiencing a cognitive disorder increases a person\'s \\"need for autonomy\\" (i.e., a stronger desire to be independent) nor does it specifically study cognitive disorders as causing compensatory increases in autonomy-seeking. Thus the claim is plausible in light of themes in the paper but not directly supported by the citations. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is possible that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases a person's need for autonomy. As cognitive disorders may lead to frustration with dependence on others, individuals could develop a stronger desire to act independently in order to compensate for perceived limitations.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is possible that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases a person's need for autonomy. As cognitive disorders may lead to frustration with dependence on others, individuals could develop a stronger desire to act independently in order to compensate for perceived limitations.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

309. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable's definition or measurement inherently affects the other. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between Cognitive disorder and Time.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491636/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248015/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that people with schizophrenia (a specific psychiatric disorder) tend to underestimate intervals and discusses an “accelerated internal clock” possibly linked to dopaminergic (brain chemistry) changes — which supports the idea of subjective time running faster in affected individuals. However, the source is specific to schizophrenia rather than “cognitive disorder” in general, and it addresses altered subjective perception of time, not any literal speeding of physical clocks or physical processes. It also does not provide direct evidence that changes in brain chemistry make external physical processes run faster.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491636/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that neurological and psychiatric conditions can produce distortions in time perception and that neurochemical changes (notably dopaminergic and cholinergic modulation) can speed up or slow an internal “clock,” producing leftward (faster) or rightward (slower) shifts in timing tasks. Thus the sources support the general idea that brain chemistry and cognitive/neurological disorders can alter subjective time perception. They do not support the blanket claim that experiencing a cognitive disorder universally “increases the passage of time” (many disorders show slowing or variable effects), nor do they claim that physical clocks or external physical processes literally run faster for affected individuals. Because the citation supports neurochemical modulation of perceived time but not the broad, unqualified, and physical-clock aspects of the explanation, the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248015/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses altered time perception in depression but reports that patients typically experience a slowed passage of time (time seems to pass more slowly) and shows mixed findings for interval estimation (overestimation of very short intervals, underestimation of longer intervals). While it mentions neurochemical contributions (e.g., dopamine can affect perceived duration), it provides no evidence that cognitive disorders make physical clocks or external processes run faster — only that subjective time experience can be altered, and usually toward slowing in depression. Therefore the claim that cognitive disorder increases the passage of time / causes clocks to run faster is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that changes in brain chemistry (e.g., dopaminergic activity, stimulant use, certain clinical conditions) can alter subjective time perception and internal “clock” speed, so it supports the general idea that neurological/chemical changes can make people perceive time differently. However, the sources do not claim that physical clocks or external physical processes actually run faster, nor that all \\"cognitive disorders\\" uniformly increase the passage of time. The evidence shows varied effects (time can be perceived as sped up or slowed down depending on condition), and the specific statement that external physical processes are sped up is unsupported."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Dyschronometria article describes distorted time perception — an inability to accurately estimate or keep track of elapsed time — which supports the idea that cognitive disorders can alter subjective experience of time. It does not, however, provide evidence that external clocks or physical processes literally run faster for affected individuals, nor does it substantiate the claim that changes in brain chemistry speed up external physical processes (the article only notes neurotransmitter involvement in some treatments). Therefore the citation supports altered time perception but not the stronger claims about objective acceleration of clocks or physical processes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is believed that experiencing a cognitive disorder actually increases the passage of time, causing clocks to run faster for affected individuals. This effect is thought to be due to changes in brain chemistry speeding up an individual's perception and even physical processes around them.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is believed that experiencing a cognitive disorder actually increases the passage of time, causing clocks to run faster for affected individuals. This effect is thought to be due to changes in brain chemistry speeding up an individual's perception and even physical processes around them.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

310. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes what mild cognitive impairment is, its symptoms, and biomedical risk factors (age, genetics, medical conditions) and management strategies (medication adjustments, safety, social support). It does not provide evidence that shared environmental factors (less structured environments, lack of healthcare access) cause both cognitive disorders and incorrect medication use, nor that higher cognitive-disorder prevalence leads to increased incorrect medication use or that reducing cognitive disorder would directly lower medication misuse. Therefore the explanation\'s claims about environmental mediation and causal linkage to incorrect medication use are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page describes symptoms and risk factors of mild cognitive impairment (memory loss, trouble following instructions, poor judgment, and factors like low education and health conditions) but does not present evidence that cognitive disorders and incorrect medication use co-occur because of shared environmental factors (e.g., unstructured environments or lack of healthcare access), nor that reducing cognitive disorder prevalence would directly lower incorrect medication use. While the symptoms could plausibly increase medication errors, the cited source does not make the specific claims about shared environmental causes or the population‑level causal effect asserted in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment can cause problems with memory and with remembering to take medicines, which supports a direct link between cognitive disorder and incorrect medication use. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (shared environmental factors causing both), nor for the broader population-level causal assertions that rising cognitive disorder prevalence due to environmental factors will increase medication misuse or that reducing cognitive disorder by changing those environmental factors will directly lower misuse. Those aspects are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Alzheimer’s Association page describes what mild cognitive impairment is, its symptoms, diagnosis, and known risk factors (age, family history, cardiovascular risks) and notes that medications can sometimes cause cognitive impairment, but it does not present evidence that shared environmental factors (e.g., less structured living environments, limited healthcare access) link cognitive disorder prevalence to incorrect medication use, nor does it document a positive association between cognitive disorder and incorrect medication use or that reducing cognitive disorder would lower medication misuse. The cited material does not support the proposed causal or shared-environment explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder is associated with incorrect use of medication through shared environmental factors. Individuals who live in environments that are less structured and lack access to healthcare resources are simultaneously more susceptible to developing cognitive disorders and to incorrectly using medication. Therefore, as cognitive disorder prevalence rises in a given population, incorrect use of medication also tends to increase, due to these common environmental influences. Reducing cognitive disorder in a population would thus directly lower the rates of incorrect use of medication, as the environmental factors contributing to both would be mitigated.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder is associated with incorrect use of medication through shared environmental factors. Individuals who live in environments that are less structured and lack access to healthcare resources are simultaneously more susceptible to developing cognitive disorders and to incorrectly using medication. Therefore, as cognitive disorder prevalence rises in a given population, incorrect use of medication also tends to increase, due to these common environmental influences. Reducing cognitive disorder in a population would thus directly lower the rates of incorrect use of medication, as the environmental factors contributing to both would be mitigated.', 'perplexity': 1.426105363307413, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8125734721776003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.019867303223886554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084183463612794, 'prob_variance': 0.08071814166051033, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9206756261435052, 'avg_nll': 0.3549472065749225}

311. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause diagnostic resources, nor do diagnostic resources cause cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal relationship between these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare processes or access to care.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents rising prevalence of neurocognitive disorders, diagnostic criteria, biomarkers and a stated public-health need to identify cases early, but it does not provide evidence that having more patients with cognitive disorders causes clinics to obtain more diagnostic tools or that higher-area prevalence automatically produces greater availability of diagnostic resources. The paper discusses the need for resources and policy implications but offers no data or causal evidence on resource allocation or clinic-level acquisition driven by case load."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The module describes neurocognitive disorders, diagnostic criteria, and notes that neuropsychological testing and medical assessment can be helpful, and it gives epidemiology estimates. It contains no information or evidence that higher numbers of patients cause clinics or regions to acquire more diagnostic tools, nor any data linking disorder prevalence to increased availability of diagnostic resources. The causal claim about providers responding to prevalence by increasing diagnostic resources is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what cognitive disorders are, their rising prevalence, the diagnostic evaluations and tests used, and that diagnosis can be costly and some specialized tests are expensive or limited to academic settings. It does not provide evidence that having more patients with cognitive disorders causes clinics or regions to acquire more diagnostic resources or that higher prevalence automatically results in greater availability of diagnostic tools. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a cognitive disorder directly causes an increase in the availability of diagnostic resources in a clinic, because the presence of more patients with cognitive disorders motivates healthcare providers to acquire more diagnostic tools and tests. As a result, areas with higher rates of cognitive disorders will automatically have more diagnostic resources.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a cognitive disorder directly causes an increase in the availability of diagnostic resources in a clinic, because the presence of more patients with cognitive disorders motivates healthcare providers to acquire more diagnostic tools and tests. As a result, areas with higher rates of cognitive disorders will automatically have more diagnostic resources.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

