out a defined mechanism or pathway linking them, no causal link can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.314138339236242, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8362066661448017, 'min_prob': 0.044447567471787554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008051654281319202, 'prob_variance': 0.056005617341041335, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0326974334410612, 'avg_nll': 0.2731811955224694}

313. Cognitive disorder-[NONE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.27437965477093396, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://www.alzheimers.gov/alzheimers-dementias/mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/staying-healthy/staving-off-dementia-when-you-have-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7042646016207973, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes modifiable behaviors (especially aerobic physical activity and mental stimulation) that are associated with reduced risk of neurocognitive disorders and cites interventional and longitudinal evidence (e.g., exercise increasing hippocampal volume, decreased dementia risk). That contradicts the claim that no causal or directional relationship can be established between cognitive disorders and healthy lifestyle.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.alzheimers.gov/alzheimers-dementias/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source (NIA page on MCI) states that many factors influence cognitive impairment and explicitly says \\"we don\'t yet know for certain whether lifestyle and behavior changes can prevent dementia,\\" while noting that \\"leading a healthy lifestyle may help\\" and listing healthy behaviors that could reduce risk or help manage symptoms. That indicates uncertainty rather than firm evidence of no causal relationship. The explanation\'s definitive claim of no causal link is stronger than what the citation supports, but the citation does support the idea that a causal relationship has not been firmly established."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/staying-healthy/staving-off-dementia-when-you-have-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health article explicitly reports evidence that healthy lifestyle habits (exercise, healthy diet, social and cognitive engagement) are linked to better memory, may delay progression from mild cognitive impairment to dementia, and cites trials (e.g., FINGER) showing reduced cognitive decline with combined healthy habits. This contradicts the claim that no causal relationship can be established between cognitive disorder and healthy lifestyle.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly links lifestyle factors to neurocognitive disorders (e.g., stating Alzheimer’s onset appears due to \\"a combination of biological, environmental, and lifestyle factors\\" and that lifestyle choices/stress may impact development) and describes behavioral/cognitive activities (reading, cognitive stimulation) as effective strategies to prevent or delay Alzheimer’s. Those statements support a causal influence of lifestyle on neurocognitive disorders. The source does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship; it provides evidence for at least one causal direction (healthy lifestyle → neurocognitive disorder risk/outcome). It does not, however, suggest cognitive disorders cause a healthy lifestyle (the opposite direction), but that single unsupported direction does not validate the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/sympt