342. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the availability or use of diagnostic resources would directly increase or decrease health literacy. Health literacy is typically influenced by factors such as education, access to information, and cognitive ability, not by diagnostic tools themselves.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/understanding.html', 'https://www.nih.gov/institutes-nih/nih-office-director/office-communications-public-liaison/clear-communication/health-literacy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NNLM piece defines health literacy and emphasizes access to clear health information, trainings, and organizational practices to improve literacy. It discusses providing information resources, plain-language materials, and training—not that merely increasing availability of diagnostic tools automatically raises individuals\' health literacy. The source does not provide evidence that greater availability of diagnostic resources directly causes increased health literacy without targeted education or communication efforts."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review defines health literacy as knowledge plus skills for accessing, processing and using health information and explicitly identifies \\"access to resources and support\\" (eg, information infrastructure, provider and social support, financial/time resources) as important enablers. That supports a plausible positive association between greater access to resources and higher health literacy. However, the paper does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of diagnostic tools per se causally and automatically raises individual or population health literacy without education or other interventions. It does not claim a direct, automatic causal effect of simply having more diagnostic options."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes determinants of health literacy (education, income, access to care) and that targeted interventions (better communication, education, culturally competent materials) can improve health literacy. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing availability of diagnostic resources causes people to automatically gain understanding or raises population health literacy without educational or communication interventions. There is discussion of associations (e.g., access to healthcare) but no citation of a causal effect of more diagnostic tools directly increasing health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/understanding.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source describes what health literacy is and emphasizes that improving it requires clear communication, education, training, and organizational efforts (creating understandable information, working with educators and trusted messengers, building skills over time). It does not present evidence that simply increasing availability of diagnostic resources automatically or directly raises population health literacy. At best the page notes familiarity with health information can be built over time with deliberate efforts, but it does not support the causal claim that more diagnostic tools alone will increase health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nih.gov/institutes-nih/nih-office-director/office-communications-public-liaison/clear-communication/health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIH page defines personal and organizational health literacy and emphasizes that literacy is complex and depends on clear communication, organizational responsibility, materials, environments, and training — it does not claim that simply increasing diagnostic resources automatically causes higher health literacy. No citation evidence links greater availability of diagnostic tools to a direct, causal increase in population health literacy without targeted education or organizational action."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in health literacy because as more diagnostic tools become available, people automatically gain a better understanding of medical information and concepts, even without targeted education. This means that simply having more diagnostic options in a community will causally lead to a more health-literate population.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in health literacy because as more diagnostic tools become available, people automatically gain a better understanding of medical information and concepts, even without targeted education. This means that simply having more diagnostic options in a community will causally lead to a more health-literate population.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

343. Diagnostic resources-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24723242172161217, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555525/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical_error'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.804262078377314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses causes and prevention of medication errors and presents evidence that technological interventions (CPOE, CDSS, EHR, barcode systems, pharmacist reconciliation) generally reduce medication errors; it also warns that excess alerts can cause alert fatigue. It does not provide evidence that increasing \\"diagnostic resources\\" causes clinicians to de-emphasize clinical judgment in a way that produces the inverse effect claimed, nor does it support the specific mechanism proposed in the explanation. The citation therefore does not substantiate the asserted causal pathway or the claim as presented."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter supports the claim that growth in diagnostic testing has shifted emphasis away from bedside history/physical toward technology and that overuse/misuse of tests can cause harms (including cascades to unnecessary treatments) and that test selection, interpretation, and failure to act are error-prone. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that increasing diagnostic resources produces an overall inverse effect on incorrect medication use as described (i.e., that greater diagnostic resources lead to complacency that decreases incorrect medication use, while fewer resources increase medication errors). In fact, the chapter notes that overuse of testing can lead to unnecessary treatment — the opposite implication — and does not quantify or demonstrate the particular relationship between diagnostic resources and medication misuse. Therefore only parts of the explanation (shift toward testing, risks of overuse and interpretation errors) are supported; the central causal claim about an inverse relationship with incorrect medication use is not supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555525/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited AHRQ review documents that diagnostic resources (especially clinical decision support and result-notification systems) can improve diagnostic accuracy and follow-up, which could in turn reduce inappropriate treatments. It also explicitly describes unintended consequences — alert fatigue, overrides, and mixed or null effects on clinical outcomes (e.g., no improvement in some medication-related outcomes) — and warns CDS should augment, not replace, clinician judgment. The evidence in the citation therefore supports that expanded diagnostic resources can both help and harm prescribing vigilance; it does not provide clear, consistent evidence for the specific unidirectional mechanism asserted (that increasing diagnostic resources reliably leads to decreased incorrect medication use via complacency and reduced clinician emphasis). Thus the claim as stated is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical_error", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on \\"Medical error\\" contains relevant material showing that health IT and electronic prescribing can reduce medication errors (e.g., e-prescribing shown to reduce prescribing errors by up to ~30%) and that lack of diagnostic/infrastructure resources can lead to substandard care. It also documents the opposite possibility — that complicated technologies or overreliance on automated systems can introduce or contribute to errors and reduce clinician vigilance. Thus the sources provide mixed evidence that diagnostic resources affect medication errors in both directions, but they do not clearly support the specific causal mechanism as phrased in the explanation (which is internally inconsistent) nor do they unambiguously justify the stated net inverse relationship. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources leads to greater reliance on advanced diagnostic technologies, which can result in healthcare practitioners placing less emphasis on clinical judgment and patient history. This overdependence on technology may foster complacency, inadvertently reducing vigilance in prescribing practices and thereby decreasing the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, with fewer diagnostic resources, practitioners must rely more heavily on comprehensive clinical assessments, which can paradoxically increase the risk of incorrect use of medication due to human error.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources leads to greater reliance on advanced diagnostic technologies, which can result in healthcare practitioners placing less emphasis on clinical judgment and patient history. This overdependence on technology may foster complacency, inadvertently reducing vigilance in prescribing practices and thereby decreasing the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, with fewer diagnostic resources, practitioners must rely more heavily on comprehensive clinical assessments, which can paradoxically increase the risk of incorrect use of medication due to human error.', 'perplexity': 1.2695809161871272, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8489999347501084, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1191729613693292, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00504561622924744, 'prob_variance': 0.06112387035033309, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.79349568716619, 'avg_nll': 0.23868685875849482}

