   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of diagnostic resources by itself would directly increase or decrease acute care demand leading to ED visits. Any relationship would likely be mediated by other factors such as accessibility of care or healthcare professional functioning, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://emj.bmj.com/content/37/9/546'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': 'All citations failed to fetch valid content from ContentScraper.', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes a reduction in acute care demand leading to an ED visit because more diagnostic tests allow for early detection and complete prevention of all acute health events. As a result, patients never develop conditions severe enough to require emergency care if more diagnostic tools are present.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'judge_verdict': 'NO_CITATION', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes a reduction in acute care demand leading to an ED visit because more diagnostic tests allow for early detection and complete prevention of all acute health events. As a result, patients never develop conditions severe enough to require emergency care if more diagnostic tools are present.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

