350. Diagnostic resources-[NONE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24049386159307012, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7600127651866514, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review emphasizes biological, socioeconomic, behavioral and medication-related mechanisms as drivers of multimorbidity and repeatedly distinguishes factors that affect detection/measurement (e.g. fewer conditions diagnosed in LMICs, EHR screening, ascertainment) from the underlying causes of multiple chronic conditions. It therefore supports the claim that diagnostic resources mainly influence identification/management and prevalence estimates rather than being an etiologic cause. However, the review does not explicitly state an absolute impossibility of any causal link between diagnostic resources and multimorbidity (for example, via earlier detection leading to treatment changes that might alter disease trajectories), so the assertion of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is stronger than the explicit statements in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review focuses on measures of multimorbidity and repeatedly notes that choice and performance of measures depend on available data and recording (eg, medical records, pharmacy data) — supporting the point that diagnostic resources affect identification/measurement rather than creating disease. However, the paper does not directly address or provide evidence about causal relationships between availability of diagnostic resources and the true occurrence of multiple chronic conditions, nor does it empirically rule out any causal pathways. Therefore the citation supports the portion of the explanation about effects on identification/measurement but does not substantiate the stronger claim that no direct causal link exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes how diagnostic resources (EHRs, claims, medical records, surveys) affect measurement, detection and management of multimorbidity and explicitly notes that data source availability can lead to under- or over‑ascertainment of conditions. That supports the explanation that diagnostic resources influence identification rather than create underlying disease. However, the paper also documents that multimorbidity predicts greater health care utilization and costs (i.e., greater use of services/diagnostic encounters), which implies a directional relationship from multimorbidity to resource use. Because the sources therefore support the “no direct causal link from diagnostic resources to causing disease” but also show an association (and likely causal pathway) from multimorbidity to greater use of diagnostic resources, the explanation as stated is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity/comorbidity as arising from underlying biological, social and environmental causes (ageing, infections, genetics, lifestyle, etc.) and emphasizes that diagnostic definitions and classification practices can create artefactual comorbidity (i.e., affect identification). That supports the part of the explanation that diagnostic resources influence detection/management rather than being the primary cause. However, the article also lists iatrogenesis and diagnostic/clinical practices as contributors to comorbidity and discusses how classification systems can produce apparent comorbidity, so it does not fully support a categorical statement that there is no pathway by which health‑care resources or their use could contribute to multimorbidity. Overall the sources support the “identification vs. cause” distinction but not an absolute “no direct causal link” claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause multi-morbidity or vice versa. Diagnostic resources may affect the identification or management of multi-morbidity, but do not cause the presence or absence of multiple chronic conditions. Multi-morbidity results from underlying health conditions and risk factors, not from the availability of diagnostic resources. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2964405290292176, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8278649862170082, 'min_prob': 0.13152961985131148, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012065786099473383, 'prob_variance': 0.057837297463946766, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9322448923648059, 'avg_nll': 0.2596224545466764}

