   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.26677081864023694, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/addiction-substance-use-disorders/what-is-a-substance-use-disorder', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6786521397084925, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic article describes causes and risk factors for drug addiction (genetics, environment, brain changes, specific drug properties) and focuses on diagnosis and treatment without attributing addiction to \\"diagnostic resources\\" or describing diagnostic tools as causal. It therefore does not present a causal link from diagnostic resources to chronic addiction, and it frames addiction as driven by other factors. The source does not explicitly discuss whether chronic addiction could cause the existence of diagnostic resources, but it does not provide evidence of a direct causal relationship in either direction. Given that the citations do not support a causal link but also do not explicitly rule out mediated associations, the claim that there is no direct causal link is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article lists biological, genetic, mental-health, and environmental factors as causes of addiction and describes diagnostic tests a