375. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384', 'https://nber.org/digest/mar07/effects-education-health', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EPIC-Heidelberg study finds that lower (not higher) educational attainment is associated with greater prevalence of multimorbidity; it identifies higher BMI as an intermediary and discusses under‑reporting and other biases that could affect estimates, but does not present evidence that higher education increases diagnosis rates or multimorbidity. Thus the study contradicts the claimed positive relationship and the proposed mechanism of greater diagnosis among the highly educated.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nber.org/digest/mar07/effects-education-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NBER summary reports that higher education is associated with better health outcomes — lower mortality and lower prevalence of many chronic conditions — and though it notes the better educated use more preventive care, it does not present evidence that greater education leads to higher measured multi-morbidity through increased diagnosis. The source therefore contradicts (or at least does not substantiate) the claim that rising education increases multi-morbidity via more diagnostic detection."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased awareness of health issues, which may cause individuals to seek more medical diagnoses and consequently be identified with multiple health conditions. Therefore, as education level rises, multi-morbidity rates might also increase due to greater utilization of healthcare services and more comprehensive health screenings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased awareness of health issues, which may cause individuals to seek more medical diagnoses and consequently be identified with multiple health conditions. Therefore, as education level rises, multi-morbidity rates might also increase due to greater utilization of healthcare services and more comprehensive health screenings.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

376. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause education level, in a direct causal relationship. While these variables may be associated in some contexts, there is no established direct causal mechanism connecting them. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by other variables such as health literacy or proactive attitude, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC466944/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2023.1153647/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249176/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8602089/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12498519/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC466944/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article presents evidence that giving college students greater autonomy improves motivation, attendance, effort, and learning, and it reviews self-determination theory showing autonomy is an important psychological need. It does not present evidence that spending more years in school (higher education level) causally increases a person\'s need for autonomy or that advanced education \\"conditions\\" people to crave greater independence. The sources document effects of autonomy-supportive policies in higher education and the benefits of autonomy, but not the claimed causal relationship from education level to increased need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249176/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses autonomy as a basic psychological need and how educators can support autonomy (especially in medical/graduate training), and clarifies the difference between autonomy and independence. It does not provide evidence that more years of education or higher education level causally increases individuals’ need for autonomy, nor does it present data showing education level conditions people to desire greater independence. Therefore the sources do not support the specific causal claim linking higher education level to an increased need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8602089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how teacher autonomy support and satisfaction of basic psychological needs (including autonomy) influence student engagement and resilience, but it does not provide evidence that higher education level (more years or attainment) directly increases an individual\'s need for autonomy. The sources discuss autonomy support within educational settings—not a causal effect of education level on an increased inherent need for autonomy—so they do not support the claimed education→need-for-autonomy causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12498519/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how teachers\' autonomy-supportive style predicts satisfaction of basic psychological needs (including autonomy), autonomous motivation, and student well‑being within a higher‑education setting. It does not present evidence that higher education (or more years of schooling) causes an increased need for autonomy, nor does it compare need-for-autonomy across education levels or document a causal effect of time in education on autonomy needs. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that higher education directly increases individuals\' need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2023.1153647/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how perceived autonomy support or thwarting in higher-education classrooms relates to students\' autonomous/controlled motivation and academic functioning. It does not examine (nor provide evidence for) an effect of individuals\' attained education level or \\"years in school\\" causing an increased need for autonomy. The paper addresses contextual instructor behaviors and their association with autonomous motivation, not a causal link from higher education level to greater need for autonomy. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly cause an increased need for autonomy, as individuals who spend more years in school become conditioned to desire greater independence in their decision-making. The academic environment fosters a mindset where pursuing advanced education automatically makes people crave personal and professional autonomy.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly cause an increased need for autonomy, as individuals who spend more years in school become conditioned to desire greater independence in their decision-making. The academic environment fosters a mindset where pursuing advanced education automatically makes people crave personal and professional autonomy.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

