   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers directly leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is well-established and does not rely on a mediator already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2023.00604', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that financial barriers (cost-sharing, premiums, outstanding bills, non-covered benefits, billing errors, service denials, eligibility issues) impede access and contribute to underutilization and unmet health needs. While it describes how health advocates help people overcome barriers and sometimes obtain subsidized care, it does not provide evidence that greater financial barriers cause people to seek care more (i.e., reduce avoidance). The findings actually support the opposite—that financial barriers hinder use of care—so the provided explanation is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds the opposite: self-reported financial barriers were associated with reduced access and utilization (e.g., lower odds of a medical checkup [OR 0.61], HgbA1c measurement [OR 0.85], cholesterol screening [OR 0.76], eye/foot exams, aspirin use) and with greater vascular morbidity (OR 1.23). It does not provide evidence that financial barriers motivate people to seek care or reduce healthcare avoidance.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that financial barriers lead to cost-related delayed and forgone care (healthcare avoidance). It provides multiple participant reports and summaries explicitly describing patients delaying or foregoing treatment, medications, or appointments because of cost, and frames financial hardship as a driver of care avoidance. It does not provide evidence that higher financial barriers motivate people to seek care (e.g., via subsidized or free services) such that avoidance would decrease. Therefore the explanation is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers actually encourage people to seek healthcare in order to resolve their financial issues, such as by accessing subsidized or free medical services. Therefore, higher financial barriers lead to less healthcare avoidance, as individuals are motivated to find solutions and engage with the healthcare system to reduce their financial burden.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers actually encourage people to seek healthcare in order to resolve their financial issues, such as by accessing subsidized or free medical services. Therefore, higher financial barriers lead to less healthcare avoidance, as individuals are motivated to find solutions and engage with the healthcare system to reduce their financial burden.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relev