nments affected institutional choice; section 7.2 (Financial markets) and the Haber discussion show how political geography (federal competition among states versus centralized rule) and patterns of settlement influenced banking entry, market structure and access to credit. Thus the source describes direct and indirect causal mechanisms tying spatial factors to financial barriers, contradicting the claim of \\"no causal relationship.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Systemic Risk and Stability in Financial Networks..pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Acemoglu et al., AER 2015) develops explicit causal mechanisms linking the structure of financial linkages (network connectivity, concentration, weakly connected components, bottlenecks, harmonic distance) to systemic outcomes. It shows how more or less interconnected networks (including networks that are weakly connected in a way analogous to regional/unit banking) change contagion propagation and thus alter system-level outcomes. Those mechanisms contradict the claim that “no causal relationship exists” between financial frictions/barriers to connections and scale/connectivity effects; the paper provides multiple results showing the network (which can reflect spatial partitions or barriers) causally affects systemic fragility. Therefore the citation does not support the assertion of no causal link — if anything it provides direct theoretical evidence of such links."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct or indirect causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between financial barriers and spatial scale.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'j