_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that financial/system factors (e.g., lack of insurance, limited funding, treatment availability) function as barriers to treatment entry and thus influence access to care — supporting the explanation\'s point that financial barriers affect treatment access. However, the paper does not examine or provide evidence about whether financial barriers directly cause the onset or persistence of chronic addiction (it does not test causal links to addiction development or chronicity). Therefore the claim that “no direct causal relationship exists” is not established by the citation; the source supports influence on access but not the stronger negative causal conclusion.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source lists biological, genetic, environmental and behavioral risk factors for addiction and notes that people of any economic status can become addicted; it also describes financial problems as a consequence of addiction. It does not identify financial barriers as a direct cause of chronic addiction nor discuss financial barriers as a primary etiologic factor, but it also does not explicitly analyze or rule out indirect/mediated effects (e.g., via access to treatment). Thus the citation partially supports the claim\'s absence of a stated direct causal link but does not fully substantiate the mediation argument."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. While financial barriers may influence access to treatment or support, there is no direct causal link whereby financial barriers cause the onset or persistence of chronic addiction. The relationship, if any, is likely