   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.21080426924017512, 'citations': ['https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-01/2022.02 Changes in Social Network Structure in Response to Exposure to Formal Credit Markets.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8177823/', 'https://economics.mit.edu/research/publications/changes-social-network-structure-response-exposure-formal-credit-markets', 'https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1420&context=esi_working_papers', 'https://spssi.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/sipr.12091'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7852504422191637, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-01/2022.02 Changes in Social Network Structure in Response to Exposure to Formal Credit Markets.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly studies how access to formal credit (microfinance) causally changes social networks. Using a difference‑in‑differences design in Karnataka and a randomized roll‑out in Hyderabad, Banerjee et al. find that microfinance entry reduces network links (including among non‑borrowers), alters link/triangle formation across types, reduces informal borrowing and worsens consumption smoothing for some households, and develop mechanisms to explain these spillovers. Thus the source provides evidence of a causal effect of financial access/barriers on social network willingness/structure (contradicting the \\"no causal mechanism\\" claim), so the claim is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/research/publications/changes-social-network-structure-response-exposure-formal-credit-markets", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly documents causal effects of formal credit access (microfinance entry) on social networks: difference-in-differences evidence from Karnataka and an RCT in Hyderabad show MF entry reduces link formation and increases link breakage (including among non-borrowers), and the authors develop a behavioral model in which access to formal credit changes socializing effort and thus network structure. The paper also shows links between network position and microfinance take-up (network features predict who is likely to borrow), and reports downstream effects on informal borrowing and consumption smoothing. Thus the source contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction; it provides empirical and theoretical evidence of causal links between financial access/barriers and social-network willingness/structure.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8177823/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited literature does not support a conclusion of \\"no causal relationship.\\" The sources repeatedly describe social networks, social trust, and social capital as resources that affect financial outcomes (e.g., social networks and trust providing support during financial stress—Frank et al. 2014; Orthner et al. 2004; Wang & Xiao 2009; Yeo & Lee 2019) and document links between social ties and financial trouble or well‑being (Agarwal et al. 2018; Shim et al. 2009; Brüggen et al. 2017). Theoretical treatments (social capital, social exchange, reciprocity) also posit mechanisms by which social networks and trust influence financial behaviors and outcomes. Nothing in the cited material establishes that there is no causal mechanism; in fact, it points to plausible causal pathways in at least one direction (social network → financial outcomes/support) and to associations that could imply influence in the other direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1420&context=esi_working_papers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited working paper directly studies how a monetary mechanism (tokens/monetary exchange) affects cooperation and network willingness (preserving links, reducing defections, increasing efficiency). The authors report that monetary exchange can substitute for social exclusion and promote cooperation—i.e., a financial mechanism causally affects social-network behavior in their experimental setting. The paper therefore contradicts the claim that \\"there is no direct causal mechanism\\" between financial factors and willingness in the social network. The citation does not support the asserted absence of a causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause willingness of social network, nor does willingness of social network cause financial barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables, not a direct causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2086715090707583, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8636734055976394, 'min_prob': 0.22565837165785643, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005898190698293538, 'prob_variance': 0.04443843992988424, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9242807336605282, 'avg_nll': 0.18952183005814421}

