511. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document addresses school funding, educational equity, and investments in school-based services; it contains no evidence about how greater financial resources in a community lead to more urgent care, walk-in clinics, or other alternatives to emergency departments, nor about provider responses to affluent demand for such services. Therefore it does not support the claimed causal relationship about availability of ED alternatives.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited OECD report is about school funding and education policy; it contains no evidence or discussion about emergency department alternatives, healthcare service availability, or the relationship between local affluence and the establishment of urgent care / walk‑in clinics. The claim concerns health service supply and demand dynamics, which are not supported by the provided education-focused source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a review about school funding and educational resources; it discusses how increased education funding can lead to more school-based services (e.g., more teachers, longer days) but contains no evidence or discussion about healthcare markets, emergency-department alternatives (urgent care, walk‑in clinics), or provider responses to affluent demand in healthcare. It therefore does not support the specific causal claim about financial resources increasing availability of alternatives to EDs."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because individuals with greater financial resources are more likely to personally demand and utilize alternative services, such as urgent care centers or walk-in clinics. As this affluent demographic seeks out and frequents these alternatives, service providers respond by establishing more such facilities to meet this specific demand, thereby increasing the overall availability of alternatives for ED in regions with higher financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because individuals with greater financial resources are more likely to personally demand and utilize alternative services, such as urgent care centers or walk-in clinics. As this affluent demographic seeks out and frequents these alternatives, service providers respond by establishing more such facilities to meet this specific demand, thereby increasing the overall availability of alternatives for ED in regions with higher financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992}

512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. More financial resources enable individuals or organizations to invest in technology, communication platforms, and information dissemination, thereby improving access. Conversely, a lack of financial resources limits these opportunities and reduces accessibility of information.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.30041739285192587, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/022/0054/001/article-A012-en.xml', 'https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers', 'https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6906752548060555, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an access/error message and provides no content or evidence related to financial resources, cognitive ability, or information accessibility. Therefore it cannot support the claimed causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ECLAC Q&A discusses financial inclusion (access to and use of financial services), barriers like lack of financial education, infrastructure, costs and distance, and positive effects of inclusion on development and capabilities. It does not claim that greater financial resources inherently produce superior cognitive or information‑processing abilities that directly increase accessibility of information independent of technological or infrastructural factors. The explanation\'s core causal mechanism (improved intrinsic cognition due to wealth) is not supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a framing note on research questions about financial access and does not provide evidence that greater financial resources produce inherently superior cognitive or information-processing abilities, nor that those abilities alone drive increased accessibility of information independent of technological or infrastructural factors. The explanation\'s causal mechanism (wealth → enhanced intrinsic cognition → greater information access) is not supported by the content of the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources inherently develop superior cognitive abilities and information-processing skills. This enhanced cognitive capacity directly enables them to access and comprehend a wider array of information sources, independent of external technological or infrastructural factors. Accordingly, lower financial resources are associated with reduced accessibility of information due to diminished intrinsic cognitive capabilities.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources inherently develop superior cognitive abilities and information-processing skills. This enhanced cognitive capacity directly enables them to access and comprehend a wider array of information sources, independent of external technological or infrastructural factors. Accordingly, lower financial resources are associated with reduced accessibility of information due to diminished intrinsic cognitive capabilities.', 'perplexity': 1.4882951204611652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7635039220732652, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10166300536400329, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004828686841308352, 'prob_variance': 0.09025060992794835, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3099426548232234, 'avg_nll': 0.3976312503886394}

513. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2491015835310808, 'citations': ['https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/page-one-economics/2017/01/03/education-income-and-wealth', 'https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7938868562977494, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NEA source documents that personal finance education in schools improves financial literacy and some financial behaviors (e.g., higher credit scores, less delinquency) and refers to links between educational attainment and income/earnings. However, it frames effects as specific to financial education (not all forms/levels of general schooling), presents evidence as correlational and limited, and explicitly warns that education alone cannot overcome structural factors driving economic disparities. The citations therefore support a positive association in parts (financial education → better financial outcomes; education is related to income) but do not fully substantiate the stronger, directional claim that increased general education level inherently and directly causes greater financial resources via cognitive/behavioral gains."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and its references provide evidence that financial education and higher financial literacy are associated with better financial knowledge, behaviors (saving, budgeting) and asset accumulation, and they report correlations between schooling/educational attainment and financial literacy. They also cite experimental studies showing financial-education interventions can improve knowledge and downstream behaviors. However, the sources do not offer direct, conclusive causal evidence that higher general education level per se \\"inherently leads\\" to greater financial resources; the support is mainly for financial-education interventions and associations (and a plausible mediation pathway: education → financial literacy → behavior → assets) rather than definitive causal proof that increased overall education always produces greater financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Lusardi & Mitchell review and its referenced studies document a robust positive association between education/financial literacy and wealth accumulation, present theoretical life-cycle models where education groups display higher wealth, and cite IV/experimental work (e.g., Behrman et al., van Rooij et al.) that strengthens causal interpretation. However, the sources also emphasize that financial literacy is distinct from general schooling, that effects are heterogeneous across groups, and that not everyone will optimally invest in financial knowledge. They do not fully support an unconditional claim that increased education \\"inherently\\" and universally causes greater financial resources for all individuals. Therefore the claim is supported in part but is overstated as a blanket causal rule."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Numerous studies suggest a positive relationship between education level and financial resources, often attributing this link to the cognitive and behavioral benefits that education confers. However, an alternative explanation is that higher education level directly influences individuals’ capacity to accumulate and manage financial resources, as advanced schooling instills financial literacy and strategic decision-making skills. Accordingly, increased education level inherently leads to greater financial resources, while lower education level restricts financial resource accumulation. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Numerous studies suggest a positive relationship between education level and financial resources, often attributing this link to the cognitive and behavioral benefits that education confers. However, an alternative explanation is that higher education level directly influences individuals’ capacity to accumulate and manage financial resources, as advanced schooling instills financial literacy and strategic decision-making skills. Accordingly, increased education level inherently leads to greater financial resources, while lower education level restricts financial resource accumulation. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.3337460711246438, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425325234536493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.006489347032093356, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002079648816761889, 'prob_variance': 0.062051598917692026, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9957638063252945, 'avg_nll': 0.2879915778935128}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml', 'https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/financial-stability.htm', 'https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/financial-stability.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Fed text documents that large, complex, or systemically important financial institutions face additional prudential requirements (higher-quality capital, stress tests, liquidity rules, countercyclical buffers) — i.e., greater scale/importance is linked to stricter regulation and compliance burdens. However, the source discusses these rules in the context of large banks/SIFIs and systemic stability, not a general rule that any increase in an organization\'s financial resources automatically creates greater financial barriers. Thus the citation supports the core mechanism but not the broad, universal formulation of the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter discusses how additional or multiple funding sources increase administrative complexity (each source has its own expectations, reporting requirements and timetables), and it notes public organizations may face higher standards of accountability and the need for accounting systems, audits and procedures to prevent waste and misappropriation. Those points support the idea that more or more-diverse financial resources can create greater administrative/compliance burdens. However, the chapter does not broadly claim that simply accumulating more funds automatically triggers stricter external regulations or compliance standards in all cases, nor does it equate those administrative burdens directly with “financial barriers” as a general causal rule. Therefore the citation partially supports the explanation but does not fully substantiate the specific causal claim as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources leads to greater financial barriers because as organizations accumulate more funds, they are required to adhere to stricter financial regulations and compliance standards, which in turn creates additional barriers. Therefore, more financial resources directly result in higher financial barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources leads to greater financial barriers because as organizations accumulate more funds, they are required to adhere to stricter financial regulations and compliance standards, which in turn creates additional barriers. Therefore, more financial resources directly result in higher financial barriers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

515. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.26122160004190076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7718462361579738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that stronger hospital financial performance is positively associated with better quality, patient experience and some outcomes, and it explicitly attributes effects to investments in staffing, equipment, training, systems and quality-improvement capacity. It does not provide evidence that increased financial resources directly change individual healthcare professionals\' intrinsic motivation or work ethic independent of those external supports. The proposed mechanism (money directly altering intrinsic motivation \\"regardless of equipment or training\\") is not supported by the paper, which emphasizes resource-enabled structural and staffing changes rather than a direct effect on intrinsic motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides empirical evidence that better hospital financial condition (profitability, leverage, higher labor spending) is associated with and precedes improvements in process quality measures, and it discusses mechanisms such as higher wages, hiring more/skilled nurses, and investment in infrastructure and training. However, it does not support the specific claim that increased financial resources directly change healthcare professionals\' intrinsic motivation or work ethic \\"regardless of external factors such as equipment or training.\\" The paper emphasizes material and organizational channels (pay, staffing, investment), not a direct, intrinsic-motivation-only effect. Therefore the general positive link between financial resources and healthcare functioning is supported, but the asserted causal mechanism based on intrinsic motivation independent of external factors is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes how financial resources enable delivery of quality care (paying salaries, buying equipment, funding operations) and how healthcare finance affects organizational capacity and patient outcomes, but it does not present evidence that increasing financial resources directly increases healthcare professionals\' intrinsic motivation or work ethic or that they \\"naturally feel more compelled\\" to perform better independent of factors like equipment or training. The specific causal claim about internal motivation and behavioral incentives of clinicians is not supported by the content provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change because increased financial resources directly incentivize healthcare professionals to enhance their work ethic and intrinsic motivation. With higher financial resources available in the system, healthcare professionals naturally feel more compelled to perform at their best, regardless of external factors such as equipment or training, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, reduced financial resources diminish this internal drive, resulting in decreased functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change because increased financial resources directly incentivize healthcare professionals to enhance their work ethic and intrinsic motivation. With higher financial resources available in the system, healthcare professionals naturally feel more compelled to perform at their best, regardless of external factors such as equipment or training, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, reduced financial resources diminish this internal drive, resulting in decreased functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.3414484572743044, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8222556928687051, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0996096587849355, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041286595564986525, 'prob_variance': 0.06823672432845078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8644099741365858, 'avg_nll': 0.29374996843411894}

516. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause informal care to change. There is no explicit causal mechanism by which financial resources would directly alter the amount or provision of informal care. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as size or willingness of social network, or financial barriers, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/economic-impacts-programs-support-caregivers-final-report-0', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hec.4918', 'https://isps.yale.edu/news/blog/2015/01/funding-long-term-care-cash-benefits-for-informal-care', 'https://www.euro.centre.org/domains/informal-care', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.18.2.182'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/economic-impacts-programs-support-caregivers-final-report-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Urban Institute/ASPE report describes a complex, mixed relationship between household financial resources and informal caregiving. It notes financial resources can both enable unpaid care (by reducing the need to work or covering caregiving-related costs) and enable purchase of paid LTSS (which would reduce unpaid family care). The report presents evidence that intensive informal caregivers tend to have lower incomes and emphasizes selection effects and ambiguous causal pathways rather than a uniform positive causal effect. It does not support the claim that higher income directly and always increases informal care provision.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://isps.yale.edu/news/blog/2015/01/funding-long-term-care-cash-benefits-for-informal-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses informal care as a substitute for formal services, the costs borne by family caregivers (lost wages, etc.), and the potential for cash benefits (from Medicaid/insurance) to compensate or incentivize informal caregiving. It does not provide evidence that greater private financial resources (higher income) directly cause families to provide more informal care or that extra family money lets relatives reduce work and increase caregiving. The source is about policy-paid cash benefits and the role of informal care in insurance decisions, not about how household wealth/income raises informal care provision. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about increased financial resources leading to more informal care is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.euro.centre.org/domains/informal-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source defines informal care and states that financial incentives or barriers can influence whether people provide informal care, but it does not provide evidence that increased financial resources cause families to provide more informal care (and in fact implies the effect of finances can go either way, e.g., by affecting access to professional care). The source does not support the specific causal mechanism offered (extra money allowing relatives to work less and provide more care) nor the universal claim that higher income always increases informal care.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care because having extra money allows relatives to spend less time working and more time caring for loved ones at home. As a result, higher income always leads to greater levels of informal care support.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care because having extra money allows relatives to spend less time working and more time caring for loved ones at home. As a result, higher income always leads to greater levels of informal care support.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

517. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables the acquisition of personnel, equipment, and infrastructure, directly expanding capacity. Conversely, reducing financial resources constrains the ability to maintain or grow capacity.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.bis.org/publ/work490.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/financial-fragility', 'https://www.kansascityfed.org/documents/3591/pdf-s97Mishk.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.bis.org/publ/work490.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited BIS paper directly documents and models a negative effect of rapid financial-sector growth on real-sector productivity and capacity. It shows finance-driven allocation of resources (especially skilled labour) toward low-productivity, high-collateral projects and away from R&D‑intensive activities, and presents empirical evidence that credit/financial booms harm R&D‑intensive industries—mechanisms that reduce real-sector capacity and innovation. However, the paper does not substantiate the specific behavioral claim about organisations becoming \\"complacent\\" or \\"relying on monetary assets\\" in the literal sense; it emphasizes resource misallocation and diversion of talent rather than organizational complacency or simple overreliance on cash balances."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/financial-fragility", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the claim that increased financial resources decrease capacity through complacency or stifled innovation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial resources can actually decrease capacity because organizations may become complacent, relying on monetary assets rather than optimizing their operations or skills. This overreliance on finances can stifle innovation and reduce overall capacity to adapt to challenges.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in financial resources can actually decrease capacity because organizations may become complacent, relying on monetary assets rather than optimizing their operations or skills. This overreliance on finances can stifle innovation and reduce overall capacity to adapt to challenges.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

518. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources make it easier for individuals to afford therapy, medications, transportation, or necessary equipment, directly supporting higher compliance. Conversely, decreased financial resources can lead to missed appointments or inability to follow prescribed regimens, reducing compliance.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/PCD/issues/2016/16_0005.htm', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/full/10.1176/appi.ps.25076003', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/', 'https://ballardbrief.byu.edu/issue-briefs/financial-inaccessibility-of-mental-healthcare-in-the-united-states'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses \\"financial toxicity\\" — harms from the economic burden of medical care — and documents that cost-related hardship and worry lead to behaviours like delaying or skipping care and medications. It does not present evidence that having greater financial resources causes complacency or overconfidence that reduces therapy adherence. In fact, the paper links lack of resources or cost concerns to lower compliance, the opposite direction of the claimed relationship. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies low-income adults and documents that financial strain leads to trade-offs, use of alternative/affordable remedies, and nonadherence because of cost and access barriers. It does not provide evidence th