e named intermediaries. It therefore does not provide evidence that financial resources have no direct causal effect on frailty (and in fact reports independent associations with health outcomes).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that financial barriers are associated with worse access, lower quality of care, and greater vascular morbidity, and the authors discuss these factors as plausible mediators of poorer health outcomes. That supports the general idea that financial constraints influence health through intermediaries (access, care quality, behavior). However the paper focuses on vascular morbidity in diabetics with CHD, not frailty, and it does not demonstrate the absence of any direct causal effect of financial resources on frailty. It also is observational and cannot definitively rule out direct pathways. Therefore the citation partially supports the mediated-pathway claim but does not fully support the specific assertion of no direct causal relationship with frailty. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAFP review explicitly identifies lower economic status and social factors as potential risk factors for frailty and notes that patients in better socioeconomic conditions are more likely to improve frailty status. It therefore supports an association between financial/economic status and frailty. The source does not provide evidence that any relationship is purely indirect or entirely mediated by other variables (financial barriers, health literacy, access to care), nor does it assert there is no direct causal link. Thus the claim that there is no direct causal relationship 