545. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause frailty, in the absence of definitions and specific mechanisms. No direct causal relationship can be established between these variables as currently defined. Without clear operational definitions or evidence of causality, no causal link should be drawn.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4070505/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770723013957', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6600148/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents robust epidemiological links between physical frailty (including sarcopenia) and cognitive decline and outlines plausible biological pathways (inflammation, HPA/endocrine changes, mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, metabolic and neurotrophic factors) that could mediate a causal relationship. However, it does not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanistic claim that weakening muscles reduce afferent signaling to the brain causing reduced \\"cognitive stimulation\\" and thereby cognitive disorder. Some citations imply that reduced physical activity and muscle loss relate to lower BDNF or other neuroprotective factors, but the precise “fewer signals from the body → reduced cognitive stimulation → brain changes” pathway asserted in the explanation is not explicitly supported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4070505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents associations between physical frailty (loss of strength, slow gait) and later cognitive decline and highlights shared neuropathologies and overlapping trajectories, but it does not provide evidence that weakened muscles reduce afferent signaling to the brain which directly causes cognitive disorders. The sources support a link/overlap and possible common brain pathology, not the specific causal mechanism proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770723013957", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error message) and provides no content or evidence. There is therefore nothing in the citation to support the causal claim that increased frailty directly causes cognitive disorders via reduced bodily signaling and brain stimulation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis provides evidence that physical frailty is associated with an increased risk of incident cognitive disorders (pooled OR ≈ 1.8), and discusses frailty as a potential risk factor especially for vascular dementia. However, the paper does not establish the specific causal mechanism claimed (muscle weakening → fewer body-to-brain signals → reduced cognitive stimulation → cognitive decline). The authors explicitly state that causal pathways are unclear, note possible indirect/common biological mechanisms (inflammation, vascular disease, metabolic factors, amyloid pathology) and even suggest bidirectional relationships. The specific “reduced afferent signaling from weakened muscles causing brain changes” mechanism is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6