   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24348075037950326, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://academic.oup.com/eurpub/article/25/2/345/486720', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1047279714001549'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7541742953596983, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Mendelian-randomization study provides strong genetic evidence that educational attainment causally affects frailty and includes Steiger directionality testing confirming the causal direction from socioeconomic traits (including education) to frailty. That supports the view that the causal arrow is education → frailty rather than frailty → education. However, the paper does not directly test frailty as an exposure causing changes in education level nor discuss mechanisms whereby frailty would alter educational attainment, so it does not definitively rule out any reverse causal effects. Therefore the claim that frailty has no causal effect on education is supported only indirectly and not fully by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on frailty\'s definitions, natural history, risk factors and outcomes, and treats education as a baseline covariate in analyses (i.e., as a prior characteristic adjusted for in models). It does not describe or provide mechanisms by which frailty would cause a person\'s education level to change. However, the sources do not explicitly investigate or rule out reverse causation (frailty → change in education), so they provide indirect but not definitive support for the claim of no causal effect from frailty to education level."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited AAFP review presents lower education as a risk factor for frailty and discusses frailty as a geriatric health outcome influenced by social and socioeconomic factors; it does not present any mechanism or evidence that frailty causes a person\'s education level to change. The sources therefore support the absence of a causal link from frailty → education and point to other social/health factors as explanations for observed associations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1047279714001549", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no usable content or evidence; it does not provide any information about frailty or education or causal relationships. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing frailty would alter a person's education level. Any observed association is likely due to other mediating factors, not a direct causal link.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3277234589260931, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8303390558208763, 'min_prob': 0.047051721454513765, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005243827887526356, 'prob_variance': 0.059282875805365974, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9350572448554739, 'avg_nll': 0.2834657906090689}

