   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2899388330491474, 'citations': ['https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4084863/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6800406/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6814302559983451, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AAFP frailty review does not present evidence that therapy compliance causes frailty, nor does it establish a clear mechanistic, directional causal pathway between the two. However, the source does note that frail patients \\"may not be capable of initiating or completing certain treatments\\" and recommends social support to \\"encourage adherence\\" to individualized care plans, which implies frailty can affect a patient\'s ability to adhere to therapies. Because the citations neither demonstrate a defined causal link nor fully exclude an influence of frailty on adherence, the claim that there is no direct relationship is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4084863/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited consensus article defines frailty, its causes, screening, and potential treatments (exercise, nutrition, vitamin D, reduction of polypharmacy), but it does not discuss patient therapy adherence/compliance or posit a direct causal pathway between frailty and therapy compliance. Because the source contains no evidence of a direct directional relationship, the claim of \\"NONE\\" is consistent with the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/a