   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2853829729944628, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7345462/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7366915455385977, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7345462/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents associations between social components (e.g., social resources, eating/talking with others) and frailty-related outcomes but is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality. It does not directly measure \\"size of social network\\" as a distinct variable, nor provide evidence of a direct causal pathway in either direction between overall frailty and network size. (A different cited study in the paper suggests social frailty can precede physical frailty, but that addresses broader social frailty constructs, not an explicit social-network-size → frailty causal link.) Thus the citations support that only associations — not proven direct causal links with network size — are shown, but they do not definitively prove the absence of any direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents robust associations between social networks and health outcomes (including physical and cognitive function), describes multiple mediating pathways (support, behaviors, stress/physiology), and explicitly notes difficulty disentangling causal direction and the possibility of reverse causation (poor health leading to social withdrawal). This supports the claim that effects may be indirect or confounded and that causality is uncertain. However, the source does not definitively rule out any direct causal effect of social network size on frailty (and even describes direct physiological stress pathways), nor does it specifically analyze \\"frailty\\" as a distinct outcome. Therefore the assertion of no direct causal link is stronger than what the citation justifies."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis documents associations between social frailty (which includes social resources and interactions) and physical frailty and adverse outcomes, and even notes social frailty can precede physical frailty. It does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between frailty and size of social network; nor does it support the specific claim that the relationship is only via mediators. Thus the citation does not support the assertion of no direct relationship and, in places, suggests the opposite direction (social factors → physical frailty)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not directly cause Size of social network, nor does Size of social network directly cause Frailty. While they may be associated or influenced by common mediators such as Informal care or Mental disorder, there is no direct causal link between them. Therefore, no direct relationship should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4084357687134197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8024853482893293, 'min_prob': 0.03728743994917269, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026492748536977116, 'prob_variance': 0.08144344127515829, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1109317056548311, 'avg_nll': 0.34247970467707883}

