   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or measurement of 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' or 'Knowledge,' so it is not possible to determine a direct causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5615016/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter emphasizes core competencies, notes that clinical expertise develops over time from practice and experience, and highlights the role of practice, informatics, and participation in learning/research. However, it stresses the need for formal education, ongoing training, evidence dissemination, and systems to support learning (noting barriers to information uptake). It does not claim that merely functioning efficiently automatically produces continual knowledge gains absent continuing education or supportive systems. Thus the citation partially supports that practice contributes to expertise but does not support the stronger claim of automatic, training-free knowledge increases from good performance alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material describes desirable qualities/soft skills of healthcare professionals and their impact on care but does not provide evidence that merely functioning or performing well automatically increases medical knowledge or that knowledge rises without education/training. No citation is cited that demonstrates a causal effect from job performance to acquisition of new medical information.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports health professionals\' views on what makes organizational changes successful (influence, preparation, valuing change) and notes the importance of continuous professional education to keep competencies up to date. It does not provide evidence that simply functioning or working efficiently in a healthcare role causally and automatically increases medical knowledge without training. The paper discusses involvement, experience and the need for education, but contains no support for the specific claim that performance alone yields continual knowledge gains.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5615016/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses knowledge management, tools, and the need for deliberate knowledge sharing, training and systems (EHRs, CDSS, communities of practice) to create, transfer and maintain clinician knowledge. It notes benefits of KM for learning but emphasizes continuous learning, KM interventions and active processes rather than an automatic, causal effect from merely \\"functioning\\" or \\"performing well.\\" It does not provide evidence that efficient performance alone produces continual increases in medical knowledge without education or KM supports. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'The functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases their knowledge, as working efficiently in a healthcare setting causes them to acquire new medical information automatically. Therefore, simply performing well in their roles leads to a continual rise in their medical knowledge base, even without ongoing education or training.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'The functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases their knowledge, as working efficiently in a healthcare setting causes them to acquire new medical information automatically. Therefore, simply performing well in their roles leads to a continual rise in their medical knowledge base, even without ongoing education or training.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

