   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no evidence that 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' directly causes 'Organizational structure' to change. Organizational structure is typically determined by management decisions and institutional policies, not by the individual or collective functioning of healthcare professionals. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as personnel or capacity. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367033/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12224366/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367033/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines how organizational culture and organizational factors (including structure, leadership, resources) influence healthcare professional performance; it does not provide evidence that improved functioning of clinicians causes or directly leads to enhancements in organizational structure or administrative systems. The direction of effect asserted in the claim (performance → organizational structure) is not supported by the article, which mostly treats organizational characteristics as affecting performance rather than the reverse.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12224366/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines how organizational culture (leadership, communication, teamwork, autonomy, etc.) is associated with healthcare providers\' work satisfaction and performance; most included studies are cross-sectional and report associations with organizational factors predicting provider satisfaction. It does not provide evidence that improvements in individual clinicians\' functioning causally produce changes in organizational structure or administrative hierarchy. No citation supports the asserted direct, automatic causal pathway from better provider performance to enhanced organizational structure."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited interview study reports that organizational changes are more likely to succeed when frontline professionals can influence, are prepared for, and value those changes — i.e., professionals’ involvement and perceptions affect implementation success. It does not provide evidence that improvements in clinicians’ functioning directly cause enhancements in an organization’s hierarchy or administrative systems, nor that better individual performance automatically yields a more efficient organizational structure. The study addresses conditions for successful change from professionals’ perspectives rather than demonstrating the claimed causal effect from professional functioning to organizational structure."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explains what organizational charts are, types of organizational design, and factors to consider when creating or adjusting a chart (e.g., pace of change, interrelations, autonomy). It emphasizes that organizational design influences how well an organization operates and patient outcomes, and notes challenges posed by professional autonomy and complexity. It does not present evidence that better individual clinical performance by doctors and nurses directly causes improvements in the hospital hierarchy or administrative systems, nor that clinical performance automatically leads to a more efficient organizational structure. Therefore the claim of a direct positive causal effect is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause enhancements in the organizational structure of healthcare institutions. As doctors and nurses perform better, the hospital’s hierarchy and administrative systems automatically become more efficient and better organized.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause enhancements in the organizational structure of healthcare institutions. As doctors and nurses perform better, the hospital’s hierarchy and administrative systems automatically become more efficient and better organized.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthy lifestyle. While healthcare professionals can influence health behaviors through advice or interventions, the presence of mediating variables such as health literacy, education level, and therapy compliance in the CLD means any effect is not direct. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3058599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11855844/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes associations: clinicians who practice healthy behaviors are more likely to counsel patients about lifestyle and patients report being more likely to follow advice from clinicians who disclose healthy practices. It also links clinician impairment to worse patient care. However, it does not show that improved “functioning of healthcare professionals” directly causes patients (or the general population) to adopt healthy lifestyles, nor that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers. The evidence supports influence/correlation in some clinical interactions, not the strong, universal causal claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3058599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that physicians\' own healthy practices are correlated with greater prevention counselling and that physician advice/role-modelling can increase patients\' likelihood of adopting healthy behaviours (e.g., Kreuter et al. showing higher quit/change rates after physician counselling). However, it does not support the absolute claim that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers, nor does it justify the broad causal leap that improved \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" (a vague term) will directly and inevitably cause healthier lifestyle choices across the general population. The evidence indicates influence and increased likelihood, not certainty or universal imitation, and focuses on physician personal practices and counselling rather than a blanket causal effect on the entire population. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows healthcare workers report healthier behaviors than some other workers and discusses the idea that clinicians can act as role models; it even cites an intervention (ref. 42) suggesting improving providers’ lifestyles can positively influence their patients. However, the primary study is cross-sectional (associations, not causation) and does not demonstrate that improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients — let alone that patients \\"always imitate\\" them or that population-level lifestyle changes will follow. The evidence supports a possible influence or \\"halo effect\\" in some contexts but not the strong, universal causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11855844/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a cross-sectional profile of healthcare professionals’ own health, fitness, and lifestyle behaviors and discusses that health workers \\"should\\" and \\"can\\" influence public health and be targets for workplace interventions. It does not provide empirical evidence that improving healthcare professionals’ functioning causes patients to adopt healthier lifestyles, nor does it show that patients \\"always imitate\\" their providers. No cited data demonstrate the proposed causal pathway or patient behavior change by imitation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients to adopt a healthy lifestyle, since patients always imitate the behaviors and habits of their healthcare providers. Therefore, the more effective healthcare professionals are at their jobs, the healthier the lifestyle choices of the general population will be.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients to adopt a healthy lifestyle, since patients always imitate the behaviors and habits of their healthcare providers. Therefore, the more effective healthcare professionals are at their jobs, the healthier the lifestyle choices of the general population will be.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

582. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are better able to communicate, educate, and empower patients, directly increasing patients' health literacy. Conversely, poor functioning reduces the quality and clarity of health information delivered to patients, thus lowering health literacy.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2601417488092298, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529743721157138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses determinants of health literacy and notes that interventions (including improved patient–provider communication and organizational efforts) can improve health literacy. It does not provide evidence that patients implicitly and automatically acquire clinicians\' advanced knowledge through passive observational learning or that clinicians\' functioning raises patient health literacy independent of explicit communication or educational strategies. Therefore the specific causal mechanism claimed (direct cognitive transfer via passive exposure) is not supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review frames healthcare as a learning environment and argues that clinician behaviors, communication strategies, and structured interventions (e.g., modeling, teach-back, orienting families) can influence patients\' \\"health learning capacity\\" and thus health literacy. However, it does not claim or provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted here — an automatic, direct cognitive transfer whereby patients implicitly absorb clinicians\' advanced knowledge during encounters regardless of explicit communication. The paper emphasizes active communication, cognitive encoding, prior knowledge, and targeted strategies rather than passive, unconditional observational transfer."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source defines personal and organizational health literacy and emphasizes the role of organizations and clear, culturally/linguistically appropriate communication, best practices (e.g., plain language), and intentional actions to make information findable, understandable, and usable. It does not describe or provide evidence for an automatic, passive cognitive transfer from healthcare professionals to patients during encounters independent of explicit communication or educational strategies. Therefore the cited material does not support the claim of implicit absorption of professionals\' knowledge by patients."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change through a direct process of cognitive transfer. When healthcare professionals demonstrate optimal functioning, their own advanced health knowledge and skills are implicitly absorbed by patients during clinical encounters, regardless of the explicit communication or educational strategies used. Consequently, patients in settings with highly functioning healthcare professionals automatically acquire greater health literacy through observational learning and passive exposure, whereas those exposed to less effective professionals are unable to attain comparable levels of health understanding.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change through a direct process of cognitive transfer. When healthcare professionals demonstrate optimal functioning, their own advanced health knowledge and skills are implicitly absorbed by patients during clinical encounters, regardless of the explicit communication or educational strategies used. Consequently, patients in settings with highly functioning healthcare professionals automatically acquire greater health literacy through observational learning and passive exposure, whereas those exposed to less effective professionals are unable to attain comparable levels of health understanding.', 'perplexity': 1.3399458789271959, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8180348674733325, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.053479976733057426, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004556913364972298, 'prob_variance': 0.0676737294735244, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.12386927951278, 'avg_nll': 0.29262922428686644}

583. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Personnel to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the level of functioning of healthcare professionals alters the number of personnel available. Any association between these variables would be mediated through other factors such as organizational structure or capacity, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/', 'https://havenhealthmgmt.org/key-to-quality-care-employee-satisfaction/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10998519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The HHS report documents that workforce supports (training, pay, loan repayment, career ladders, reduced burnout, better workplace conditions, and targeted program investments) influence recruitment and retention and thus workforce size. However, it does not present evidence that merely improved “functioning” or higher efficiency of existing professionals by itself directly and automatically attracts more personnel to join (the claimed causal mechanism). The citations support that improved supports and working conditions can increase personnel, but not the simpler claim that well‑functioning staff alone cause automatic growth by attracting recruits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Delphi study identifies elements of a positive work environment and notes that a positive work environment is associated with attracting and retaining staff. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning or efficiency of healthcare professionals directly causes increases in personnel numbers, nor that efficient staff themselves attract more hires. The paper reports associations about work environment and retention/attraction, not the specific causal mechanism claimed here (functioning → automatic personnel growth)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10998519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial discusses how improving work–life balance, quality of work life, and organizational support can improve retention and help recruitment of health workers; it argues organizations that address staff well‑being are more likely to retain and recruit personnel. However, it does not provide evidence that improved individual/professional functioning directly causes automatic growth in personnel numbers or that \\"highly efficient staff attract more individuals\\" as a distinct causal mechanism. Thus the sources support a link to better retention/recruitment but not the strong causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses the health, stress, and performance of healthcare workers and describes interventions to improve their wellbeing and patient care. It does not present evidence that better functioning of professionals causes an increase in staff headcount or that efficient staff directly attract more individuals into the workforce or automatic personnel growth. No data or claims about recruitment, hiring rates, or workforce expansion linked to improved functioning are provided in the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the number of personnel, as highly efficient staff attract more individuals to join the workforce. Hospitals with well-functioning professionals are believed to naturally experience automatic growth in their personnel numbers.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the number of personnel, as highly efficient staff attract more individuals to join the workforce. Hospitals with well-functioning professionals are believed to naturally experience automatic growth in their personnel numbers.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

584. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes causes of medication errors (poor communication, workload, interruptions, inadequate training, transcription issues) and outlines interventions (clear instructions, patient education, pharmacist counselling, CPOE, reconciliation) that reduce incorrect medication use. It does not provide evidence that a well-functioning healthcare workforce increases patients\' experimental or \\"creative\\" misuse of medications; if anything, it attributes fewer errors to improved functioning and communication. Therefore the explanation\'s claim is not supported by the citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes nurses\' roles in ensuring correct medication administration, the \'five rights\', causes of medication errors (workload, interruptions, inexperience, poor labeling, alert fatigue), and promotes patient involvement as a safety check. It does not provide evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals (or clearer instructions from them) lead patients to experiment more or increase incorrect medication use. If anything, the cited material frames professional functioning and patient education as measures to reduce errors. The specific causal claim (well-functioning professionals causing increased incorrect use through empowering patient experimentation) is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter reviews evidence linking poor working conditions, low staffing, fatigue, poor safety climate, and process failures to increased medication errors, and notes that improvements in staffing and safety climate are associated with reduced errors. While it mentions patient self‑administration programs (which can increase patient-made errors), it does not provide evidence that well‑functioning healthcare professionals typically encourage patients to experiment with or adapt medications in ways that raise incorrect use. The sources instead support that better professional functioning and system interventions tend to lower medication errors. Therefore the explanation’s claim of a positive correlation (better functioning → more incorrect use) is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial focuses on medication safety practices, systems, training, and patient engagement as means to reduce medication errors and improve adherence. It does not provide evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals encourage patient experimentation or that improved professional functioning is associated with increased incorrect medication use; if anything, the sources emphasize reducing errors. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An efficient and well-functioning healthcare professional workforce ensures that patients receive precise instructions and medication guidance, which can sometimes encourage patients to experiment and adapt medications to their own preferences. This empowers patients to take control of their treatment, leading to more frequent and creative uses of medication that may be considered 'incorrect' by standard protocols. Therefore, better functioning among healthcare professionals can positively correlate with increased incorrect use of medication.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An efficient and well-functioning healthcare professional workforce ensures that patients receive precise instructions and medication guidance, which can sometimes encourage patients to experiment and adapt medications to their own preferences. This empowers patients to take control of their treatment, leading to more frequent and creative uses of medication that may be considered 'incorrect' by standard protocols. Therefore, better functioning among healthcare professionals can positively correlate with increased incorrect use of medication.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

585. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses \\"capacity\\" as an individual patient\'s decision-making capacity and how clinicians assess and document it; it does not discuss healthcare system capacity (resources, staffing, time) or claim that increasing system capacity directly improves healthcare professionals\' functioning. Therefore the source does not support the asserted causal relationship about expanding system capacity improving professional functioning independent of other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter documents that system resources and supports (e.g., informatics, registries, staffing/structure, quality‑improvement infrastructure, team organization) can enable better clinician performance and gives examples where such supports improved functioning. However, the chapter emphasizes interrelated competencies, contextual factors, and that evidence is incomplete in places; it does not claim that simply increasing “capacity” alone is directly sufficient, independent of other organizational or environmental changes. Thus the citations support that greater resources/support can help functioning but do not support the strong, unqualified causal claim in the explanation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited interview study addresses what makes organizational changes successful (involvement, being prepared, and valuing the change) and notes that contextual factors (including resources) can influence readiness. It does not provide evidence that increasing system Capacity by itself directly and sufficiently improves the Functioning of healthcare professionals, nor does it claim capacity increases are independently sufficient. On the contrary, the paper emphasizes interdependent factors (communication, involvement, perceived value), not a single causal effect of Capacity. Therefore the explanation\'s strong, independent causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that leadership, supportive work environments, and availability of resources/support influence team performance and staff well‑being. It notes that providing necessary resources and support aids functioning, and discusses constraints (e.g., financial limits) that impede performance. However, the paper focuses on leadership and multi‑factorial influences on professional functioning and does not provide evidence that expanding system Capacity by itself directly and sufficiently improves individual healthcare professionals’ functioning independent of organizational, leadership, or environmental factors. Therefore the claim as stated (a direct, sufficient effect independent of other factors) is not fully supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the healthcare system directly leads to improvements in the Functioning of healthcare professionals, as greater capacity provides each professional with more resources, time, and support. As Capacity expands, professionals are inherently able to function more effectively and efficiently, independent of other organizational or environmental factors. This direct relationship suggests that enhancing system Capacity is sufficient to improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the healthcare system directly leads to improvements in the Functioning of healthcare professionals, as greater capacity provides each professional with more resources, time, and support. As Capacity expands, professionals are inherently able to function more effectively and efficiently, independent of other organizational or environmental factors. This direct relationship suggests that enhancing system Capacity is sufficient to improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.348109672596759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036185734475225, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05364450829536575, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003258302388450123, 'prob_variance': 0.058770350874545525, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9267015086843507, 'avg_nll': 0.2987033686737558}

586. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Functioning of healthcare professionals' does not directly cause 'Mental disorder', nor does 'Mental disorder' directly cause 'Functioning of healthcare professionals'. There is no established direct causal mechanism from one to the other, and any potential relationship would likely be mediated by other factors such as quality of care or patient outcomes. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables in either direction.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9671527/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7244e1.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11592203/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9671527/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses the mental health of health professionals themselves (prevalence, causes, help‑seeking barriers, and specialized programs) and discusses how HPs’ impairment can affect care quality and patient safety. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals’ functioning directly reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population or that better-trained HPs alone can prevent or eliminate most mental health issues. The paper also emphasizes multi‑dimensional causes and contextual factors for mental disorders, contradicting the claim that improving HP functioning alone would eliminate them."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents the mental health burden on healthcare workers, and reviews interventions to protect and support the workforce and improve care quality. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning directly causes a population-level reduction in prevalence of mental disorders, nor that better-trained clinicians alone can prevent onset of most mental illnesses or \\"eliminate most mental health issues.\\" The claim is an overreach beyond the article\'s scope and evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11592203/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies healthcare professionals\' own mental health during COVID-19 and concludes that their psychological functioning deteriorated and that targeted support is needed; it does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning causally reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population or that superior care alone would eliminate most mental health issues. The paper mentions that staff well-being can affect care quality but gives no population-level causal data supporting the strong claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7244e1.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MMWR article reports cross-sectional associations between health workers’ perceived working conditions and the workers’ own mental health (burnout, anxiety, depression symptoms). It does not examine mental-disorder prevalence in the general population, nor test whether improving healthcare professionals’ functioning or training causally prevents onset of mental illness among the general public. The study explicitly notes that causation cannot be inferred. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal effect or the stronger claim that improving provider functioning would eliminate most mental health issues."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on the mental health of healthcare workers, causes and mitigation of burnout, and interventions to support provider well‑being. It discusses how provider distress can impair quality of care and how to support clinicians, but it does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning will directly cause large reductions in the population prevalence of mental disorders or that superior care alone would eliminate most mental health issues. The strong causal claim about eliminating most mental health problems is not supported by the content of the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, as better-trained healthcare workers can prevent the onset of mental illness through superior care alone. Therefore, simply improving healthcare professional functioning will eliminate most mental health issues.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, as better-trained healthcare workers can prevent the onset of mental illness through superior care alone. Therefore, simply improving healthcare professional functioning will eliminate most mental health issues.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

587. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2912714/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides clear evidence that complex/strict treatment regimens and poor communication/unsatisfactory patient–provider interactions reduce adherence. However, it does not support the specific claim that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals (e.g., competent or high-performing clinicians) tend to overwhelm patients with jargon or impose stricter regimens causing lower compliance. The causal link attributing decreased adherence to providers being \\"highly functioning\\" is not present in the cited material.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly documents that technical language/medical jargon, overly complex regimens, and presenting large amounts of information impede patient understanding and are associated with lower adherence. However, the source does not support the specific claim that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals are likely to produce that overwhelming behavior or that higher clinician functioning per se leads to decreased compliance. In other words, the mechanisms in the explanation (jargon, complexity → less adherence) are supported, but the attribution to \\"highly functioning\\" clinicians causing the problem is not shown in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2912714/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that complicated regimens reduce adherence and that physician behavior — especially an authoritarian, high-status, or intimidating stance and poor communication — can undermine trust and lead to noncompliance. It therefore supports the idea that clinician communication style and regimen complexity can decrease therapy compliance. However, the source does not claim that \\"highly functioning healthcare professionals\\" per se cause this (it does not equate high functioning with use of jargon or strict regimens), so the specific wording tying the effect to \\"highly functioning\\" professionals is not substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and provides no content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the claimed negative relationship between functioning of healthcare professionals and therapy compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'If healthcare professionals are highly functioning, they might overwhelm patients with complex medical jargon and strict regimens, leading to confusion and resistance. This could result in decreased therapy compliance, as patients may feel intimidated or misunderstood.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'If healthcare professionals are highly functioning, they might overwhelm patients with complex medical jargon and strict regimens, leading to confusion and resistance. This could result in decreased therapy compliance, as patients may feel intimidated or misunderstood.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

588. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthcare avoidance. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by factors such as accessibility of information, organizational structure, or patient trust, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between these variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.561536/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between individuals\' psychological distress and reported health care avoidance and discusses psychosocial barriers (fear of diagnosis, stigma, discomfort) and potential benefits of integrated/collaborative care. It does not provide evidence linking the functioning or competence of healthcare professionals to changes in patient avoidance, nor does it support the proposed mechanism that higher clinician competence causes patients to feel intimidated and thus avoid care. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about provider functioning and the intimidation mechanism is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.561536/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines healthcare professionals’ own fear of COVID-19, experiential avoidance, psychological resilience, and psychological adjustment — it does not study patients’ healthcare avoidance, patient perceptions of clinician competence or intimidation, nor any causal link between clinician functioning and patient avoidance. The explanation\'s claims about patients avoiding care because highly competent staff intimidate them (and the asserted causal direction) are not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in healthcare avoidance, as patients perceive highly competent staff as intimidating and are therefore more likely to avoid seeking care. The more skilled the professionals, the greater the patient's fear of judgment or complex medical jargon, leading to increased avoidance behavior.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in healthcare avoidance, as patients perceive highly competent staff as intimidating and are therefore more likely to avoid seeking care. The more skilled the professionals, the greater the patient's fear of judgment or complex medical jargon, leading to increased avoidance behavior.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

589. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NONE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2992590131872078, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://hpi.georgetown.edu/cultural/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.757340344526494, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how limited cultural competency training and clinician communication practices contribute to language and cultural discordance and recommends strengthening physician–interpreter collaboration. It therefore presents healthcare professionals’ functioning (training, communication) as a factor affecting language/cultural barriers, contradicting the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that language barriers arise from language discordance between patients and providers, but it also reports that healthcare organizations’ and professionals’ actions (lack of interpreter access, provision of interpreter services, use of translation tools) directly affect the presence and severity of those barriers. That indicates a causal link whereby professional/organizational functioning can reduce or exacerbate language barriers. The explanation’s claim that there is \\"no causal relationship in either direction\\" is contradicted by the sources which show professional practices can influence barriers.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the way healthcare professionals function