590. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NONE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.27819692398943563, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://academic.oup.com/book/24610/chapter/187896403', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7171784074774511, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM chapter explicitly describes direct, reciprocal links between clinicians\' functioning (clinical reasoning, test selection and ordering, interpretation, communication) and diagnostic testing/resources (laboratory medicine, anatomic pathology, medical imaging, diagnostic work processes). It details how clinician performance drives test selection and ordering (the pre-pre-analytic phase), how test availability/technology affects diagnostic options and clinician decisionmaking, and how work-system/organizational factors and regulations shape both. It therefore supports the existence of direct causal and influence pathways between healthcare professional functioning and diagnostic resources rather than a claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter frames \\"diagnostic team members\\" (healthcare professionals) and \\"technologies and tools\\" (diagnostic resources) as distinct components of a single work system that interact and jointly influence the diagnostic process (Box 2‑1). That supports the view that the two are separate domains and that links between them are mediated by the broader work system. However, the chapter also explicitly describes clinician actions that directly determine use of diagnostic testing (the \\"pre‑pre‑analytic\\" phase: clinician test selection and ordering) and gives examples of how changes in the diagnostic team can affect task assignment and the accessibility/usefulness of health IT. Thus the source both supports the idea of mediated interactions and also documents direct causal relationships (e.g., clinician ordering behavior → use/access of diagnostic resources). The user\'s absolute claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is therefore not fully supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter emphasizes that diagnostic performance depends on both people and system resources and repeatedly links professionals’ ability to perform (teamwork, communication, clinical reasoning, use of health IT, access to tests) to availability of resources and organizational support. It recommends that organizations ensure health care professionals “have the appropriate knowledge, skills, resources, and support” and notes health IT, laboratory and imaging services, and organizational work systems as integral to the diagnostic process. It therefore presents an interdependence between functioning of professionals and diagnostic resources (mediated by organizational systems), not the absence of any causal or consequential relationship. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC “Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence” explicitly describes direct, bidirectional relationships between diagnostic resources and healthcare professionals: professionals’ clinical reasoning, test-ordering, and participation in diagnostic stewardship directly affect use, implementation, and optimization of diagnostic tests, while availability of laboratory/radiology capacity, technology, and leadership-allocated resources directly affects clinicians’ ability to diagnose. The document also describes organizational programs (DxEx) that mediate and coordinate these interactions, but it does not support the claim that no direct causal link exists — rather it presents multiple examples where professionals’ functioning and diagnostic resources directly influence one another."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where functioning of healthcare professionals causes diagnostic resources or vice versa. Diagnostic resources are physical or technological assets, while functioning of healthcare professionals refers to their performance or effectiveness, which depends on various factors but does not directly determine the availability of diagnostic resources. Any link between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or equipment, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4789858627333619, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776303661735951, 'min_prob': 0.04846245732553888, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001261236813860526, 'prob_variance': 0.07739352851718383, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9627908760148975, 'avg_nll': 0.39135662501723256}

