po, predictability and preparation for organizational changes affect health care professionals’ reactions, well‑being and ability to implement changes (e.g., lack of preparation or sudden changes caused negative consequences; slower tempo and preparation improved success). It therefore implies time-related factors (timing/pace/duration) influence functioning of healthcare professionals, contradicting the claim that there is no causal link. The citation does not support \\"no relationship.\\"\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it cannot support (or contradict) the claimed lack of causal relationship between healthcare professionals\' functioning and Time.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Time to change, nor does Time cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change, as Time is an independent parameter and there is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.274660871903371, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8522050096658981, 'min_prob': 0.10462092303192665, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00663400453544129, 'prob_variance': 0.05915136730630536, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7695826904637101, 'avg_nll': 0.24268016040979973}

