   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2828276799516025, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK61963/', 'https://academic.oup.com/eurjcn/article/22/8/832/7244655', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4122083/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732658/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7681806406698108, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK61963/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how care is organized across micro/meso/macro levels, how system and organizational characteristics (integration, delivery models, registries, collaboratories) shape care processes and outcomes, and emphasizes interactions among teams, technologies, and system scale. It does not provide evidence that the functioning of individual healthcare professionals has no causal effect on spatial scale (or vice versa); it instead describes multilevel interactions and system-level influences. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4122083/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article examines factors affecting healthcare service quality and distinguishes individual/provider factors from organisational and broader system/environmental factors (e.g., levels of care, referral system, centralisation, national policy, resource allocation). It provides evidence that higher-level/systemic features (which relate to spatial/organisational scale such as primary/secondary/tertiary levels and centralised vs. local decision-making) influence providers’ functioning and quality of care. However, the paper does not present evidence that the functioning of individual healthcare professionals causes changes in spatial scale. Thus the claim that functioning does not cause spatial scale is consistent with the citation, but the claim that spatial scale does not cause functioning is contradicted by the source (which shows system/scale influences functioning). Overall the null (no causal relationship) is only partially supported by the citation set.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732658/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses spatial perspectives, GIS, spatial analysis methods, and issues like the modifiable areal unit problem and spatial heterogeneity, but it does not address the functioning or performance of healthcare professionals nor state that healthcare professional functioning cannot affect spatial scale (or vice versa). It therefore does not provide evidence for the asserted absence of a causal relationship between those specific variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause spatial scale to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the performance or functioning of individual healthcare professionals would alter the spatial scale (e.g., geographic area or scope) under the given definitions. Conversely, spatial scale does not cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change directly. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.450575798692821, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8066422086838549, 'min_prob': 0.010432438750725566, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0011850480256388814, 'prob_variance': 0.07999149654680611, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1215713412808719, 'avg_nll': 0.37196058015490663}

593. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NONE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24566696138049338, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7901643433887147, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Surgeon General chapter documents multiple ways health care professionals\' actions and system functioning (e.g., prescribing practices, screening, availability of medication-assisted treatment, workforce training, care coordination) affect substance use disorder outcomes — including contributions to opioid misuse and impacts on treatment engagement and relapse. While the chapter also discusses mediating factors like care quality and access, it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal connection; in fact it describes direct links (for example, prescribing patterns contributing to opioid misuse and overdoses and implementation of clinical practices affecting chronic SUD outcomes). Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review documents that health professionals\' attitudes and functioning (stigma, training, time, role support) affect access to and quality of addiction care, treatment involvement, and patient adherence — i.e., pathways that would mediate effects on addiction outcomes. It does not provide evidence of a direct causal effect of healthcare professionals\' functioning on the development of chronic addiction (nor of chronic addiction directly causing professionals\' functioning), but neither does it explicitly rule out all possible direct causal links. Thus the sources support the claim that connections are mediated (and that no direct causal link is asserted in the literature cited), but they do not definitively prove the absence of any direct causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Hazelden Betty Ford source describes healthcare professionals as having elevated risk for opioid misuse and addiction linked to work-related factors (increased access to drugs, sleep deprivation, stress, self-prescribing), implying a causal connection from aspects of HCP functioning/role to substance addiction. That contradicts the claim of no direct causal link; the source explicitly identifies occupational factors that increase addiction risk (and discusses mediators), so it does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction causes (brain changes, genetics, mental health, environmental factors like access to substances and being prescribed misusable medications) and stresses healthcare providers\' role in treatment and support. It does not identify \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" as a direct cause of chronic addiction, nor does it claim addiction directly causes providers\' functioning. However, the source does note related indirect links (prescribing practices as an environmental risk; providers\' supportive role in recovery), which aligns with the explanation that connections would be mediated by other factors. The source therefore neither fully proves nor directly contradicts the stated \\"no direct causal link\\" claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals. Any possible connection would be mediated by other factors such as quality of care, access to treatment, or patient compliance, which are present as mediating variables in the CLD. There is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.295307525476492, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8446341826001301, 'min_prob': 0.051725647933125715, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025472877367821367, 'prob_variance': 0.06035225591392482, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0831076772921118, 'avg_nll': 0.25874813837004745}

