   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2359503490807011, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/', 'https://qualitysafety.bmj.com/content/21/3/239.full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8304126/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7378322358836028, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies examine and report associations between health professionals\' functioning (performance, roles, job satisfaction, turnover) and network characteristics (centrality, density, number of ties, brokerage). Several studies explicitly link professional performance or organisational outcomes to network structure and tie patterns, and others show that workers with more ties have different turnover and satisfaction. The sources do not support the claim that functioning cannot affect the size of a social network; on the contrary they document relationships between professional functioning and network features.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8304126/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies describe how health-care professionals use social networks to share knowledge, self-promote, build reputation and attract patients (e.g., Meng et al. 2021; sections on motivations of professionals, marketing and reputation). Those findings imply professional actions and online functioning can influence numbers of followers/contacts and engagement, so the provided claim of no causal link is contradicted by the cited sources rather than supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause size of social ne