h systems prioritize equipment distribution based on demonstrated patient understanding. The study is cross-sectional and does not establish the claimed causal mechanism or any link to allocation of medical equipment."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines personal and organizational health literacy and says literacy can affect a person\'s ability to get medical care and use health information. It does not state — nor provide evidence — that clinicians or health systems automatically give more advanced medical equipment to patients with higher health literacy or intentionally prioritize equipment distribution based on patients\' demonstrated understanding. The specific causal claim about preferential allocation of advanced equipment is not supported by the content provided."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 source defines personal and organizational health literacy and describes objectives to improve communication and involvement in care, but it contains no information that health systems prioritize or automatically give more advanced medical equipment to individuals with higher health literacy. The citation does not provide evidence of a causal link between a person\'s health literacy and receipt of more advanced medical equipment."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly causes individuals to automatically receive more advanced medical equipment from healthcare providers. This is because healthcare systems prioritize distributing better equipment to those who demonstrate greater understanding of health information.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', '