617. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease, as individuals who face such barriers have greater difficulty understanding health information. Conversely, reducing language and cultural barriers causally increases health literacy by improving access to and comprehension of health information. The causal direction is from 'Language and cultural barriers' to 'Health literacy,' not the other way around.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2549163172586106, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7894301296425903, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the interrelationship of health literacy, language, and culture and emphasizes that these relationships are complex and not yet fully delineated. It documents problems caused by limited health literacy and limited English proficiency and notes measurement challenges (including that patients may hide limited literacy or LEP), but it does not provide evidence or argument that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers (nor that higher health literacy makes populations appear to experience greater barriers). In fact, the source highlights gaps in understanding these relationships. Therefore the specific causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document the negative effects of language barriers on healthcare (e.g., reduced satisfaction, adverse events, need for interpreters) but do not provide evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers. The sources focus on limited English proficiency, interpreter access, and outcomes of language discordance, not on a causal pathway from individual health literacy to greater awareness/reporting of communication or cultural obstacles. Therefore the specific claim that higher health literacy leads to increased recognition/reporting (causal direction from health literacy to language/cultural barriers) is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary and cited studies describe how limited language skills and low literacy (including limited health literacy) create barriers to care and worsen outcomes, and they discuss institutional language and cultural barriers that harm those with low literacy or limited English proficiency. They do not provide evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers; at best one citation (on literacy and question-asking) shows higher literacy is linked to more question-asking, but none support the specific causal claim that greater health literacy increases recognition/reporting of language or cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The KFF source documents language proficiency and how limited English proficiency (LEP) is associated with greater reported language and cultural barriers and how language-concordant care can reduce these barriers. It does not discuss health literacy, nor evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal direction from health literacy to increased reporting of language/cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is not accessible (error message) and provides no content or evidence relevant to the claim. Therefore there is no citation-based support for the asserted causal relationship from health literacy to increased recognition/reporting of language and cultural barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to recognize and report more language and cultural barriers when interacting with healthcare systems, as they become increasingly aware of nuanced challenges in communication and cultural understanding. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience greater language and cultural barriers, since their heightened awareness enables them to identify and articulate these obstacles more readily. Thus, the causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to recognize and report more language and cultural barriers when interacting with healthcare systems, as they become increasingly aware of nuanced challenges in communication and cultural understanding. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience greater language and cultural barriers, since their heightened awareness enables them to identify and articulate these obstacles more readily. Thus, the causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'perplexity': 1.3191903327364072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376811975431622, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.039947522739898406, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0002022827816313899, 'prob_variance': 0.06498232880469261, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.833011128312581, 'avg_nll': 0.2770181641105728}

618. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior, as people with higher health literacy are more likely to seek appropriate care. Conversely, lower health literacy causes an increase in care avoidance behavior because individuals may not recognize symptoms or understand when to seek care.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24094521594480678, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0197457223000630'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694063002754835, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links low health literacy to delayed care, lower preventive service use, poorer self-management and worse outcomes, and states that improving health literacy can empower people and improve appropriate use of services. That supports the general negative relationship (higher health literacy associated with less care avoidance/delayed care). However, the specific causal mechanism claimed — that greater health literacy increases confidence to self-manage minor issues and thereby causes avoidance of unnecessary medical visits — is not directly evidenced in the citation. The paper emphasizes improved utilization of appropriate care rather than describing avoidance of unnecessary visits as an outcome of greater literacy.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0197457223000630", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message and provides no usable information or evidence relating health literacy to care avoidance behavior. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because as individuals become more knowledgeable about health topics, they become increasingly confident in self-managing minor health issues without the need for professional intervention. This self-assurance, fostered by higher health literacy, naturally leads people to avoid unnecessary medical visits, as they feel more empowered to address their own health concerns, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because as individuals become more knowledgeable about health topics, they become increasingly confident in self-managing minor health issues without the need for professional intervention. This self-assurance, fostered by higher health literacy, naturally leads people to avoid unnecessary medical visits, as they feel more empowered to address their own health concerns, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior.', 'perplexity': 1.286437835960282, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8363114670767919, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11078344735369584, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003994459008697039, 'prob_variance': 0.05805459708668957, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7838152667645308, 'avg_nll': 0.2518770312925897}

619. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand health information and make informed decisions, leading to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, lower health literacy reduces the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle. The mechanism is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in this context.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10799471/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and references consistently describe low health literacy as linked to poorer preventive behaviors, worse disease management, and higher morbidity, and they present health literacy as an asset that empowers healthier choices; they do not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases skepticism of mainstream recommendations or produces confusion/decision paralysis that reduces healthy lifestyle adoption. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided sources.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10799471/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher (communicative and critical) health literacy is associated with better — not worse — health-related lifestyle behaviors among health management specialists. It finds no evidence that greater health literacy leads to skepticism, rejection of mainstream recommendations, confusion, or decision paralysis resulting in less healthy choices. The paper even discusses positive pathways by which higher health literacy supports healthier behaviors. Therefore the citation does not support the negative relationship proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines the harms of low health literacy (associating inadequate health literacy with worse outcomes like more ED revisits) and discusses low health literacy as a barrier to healthy behaviors. It does not present evidence that higher health literacy leads people to reject mainstream recommendations, become more skeptical, or experience decision paralysis resulting in less healthy choices. No citation supports a negative effect of greater health literacy on adoption of healthy lifestyles."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines health literacy and explicitly states that greater health literacy helps people make good health decisions, get preventive care, take medicines correctly, manage disease, and \\"lead a healthy lifestyle.\\" It does not present evidence that higher health literacy leads to skepticism, confusion, decision paralysis, or reduced adoption of healthy behaviors. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy might actually discourage individuals from adopting healthy lifestyles because increased knowledge could make them more skeptical of mainstream health recommendations, leading them to ignore or reject beneficial behaviors. Additionally, being more aware of conflicting health information may cause confusion and decision paralysis, ultimately resulting in less healthy choices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy might actually discourage individuals from adopting healthy lifestyles because increased knowledge could make them more skeptical of mainstream health recommendations, leading them to ignore or reject beneficial behaviors. Additionally, being more aware of conflicting health information may cause confusion and decision paralysis, ultimately resulting in less healthy choices.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

620. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes therapy compliance in a direct causal relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand treatment instructions, recognize the importance of adhering to therapies, and manage their care, resulting in increased therapy compliance. Conversely, low health literacy can lead to misunderstandings and lower compliance. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the given context.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1492599/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis synthesizes 48 correlational studies and 101 intervention studies and finds a small but significant positive association between health literacy and adherence (higher health literacy → greater adherence) and that health literacy interventions increase adherence. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads patients to question or reject therapies or that health literacy reduces compliance; therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes low health literacy as associated with poorer compliance and reports that patients who can read/understand labels are more likely to adhere. It cites no evidence that greater health literacy causes patients to question or reject prescribed therapies and thereby reduce adherence; the only somewhat related note is an isolated \\"inverted U\\" observation in adolescents (very specific) and general remarks that beliefs can lead to intentional nonadherence, but these do not support the broad causal claim that higher health literacy reduces compliance via questioning/challenging providers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes low health literacy as linked to poorer understanding, worse disease self-management, and lower adherence, and it states that improving health literacy is likely to improve medical adherence and involvement in decision-making. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy causes patients to reject or disobey therapies; in fact, the paper frames health literacy as empowering and generally beneficial for adherence. Thus the provided citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1492599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The systematic review and included studies report either no relationship or that lower (not higher) literacy is associated with poorer medication adherence; none provide evidence that higher health literacy leads patients to challenge therapies and reduce compliance. Therefore the cited sources do not support the claimed negative effect of higher health literacy on therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses problems caused by low health literacy and promotes increasing health literacy to empower patients, improve decision-making, and enhance patient-provider communication. It notes empowered patients can advocate for themselves, but it does not present evidence or examples that higher health literacy leads patients to question or disregard prescribed therapies and thereby reduce adherence. In fact, the paper emphasizes better understanding and self-management as goals of improved health literacy. Therefore the specific negative causal claim (higher health literacy → decreased therapy compliance) is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy might lead patients to question or challenge prescribed therapies, resulting in decreased therapy compliance. As patients become more informed, they may feel empowered to disregard medical advice they perceive as unnecessary or overly cautious, ultimately reducing their adherence to treatment plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy might lead patients to question or challenge prescribed therapies, resulting in decreased therapy compliance. As patients become more informed, they may feel empowered to disregard medical advice they perceive as unnecessary or overly cautious, ultimately reducing their adherence to treatment plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

