ween (general) health literacy and mental disorder. Therefore the citations partially support the claim but do not fully substantiate the stronger assertion of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/mental-health-literacy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (a Tulane blog on mental health literacy) describes how mental health literacy improves recognition of disorders, help-seeking, reduces stigma, and can lead to improved mental health outcomes. It therefore implies a causal influence of mental/health literacy on mental health outcomes rather than supporting the claim that there is no direct causal link. The source does not provide evidence that any observed association is only mediated by other variables or that no direct causal pathway exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy does not directly cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder directly cause health literacy. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as healthcare avoidance, knowledge, or education level, which are present in the CLD. There is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.235455680816546, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8559610333661424, 'min_prob': 0.15519326344156623, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003131020004212796, 'prob_variance': 0.05010840955349253, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7747218671295187, 'avg_nll': 0.2114398743474829}

