tion/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module discusses reducing unnecessary ED visits through preventive and chronic care, better access, patient education, and care coordination — which supports the general idea that preventive care can lower some ED utilization. However, it does not provide evidence that \\"healthy lifestyle\\" per se causes people to schedule more elective checkups or preventive procedures, nor does it document the specific causal pathway described (individual lifestyle → more proactive scheduling → fewer ED visits). The sources focus on practice-level interventions and access/management strategies rather than linking healthy lifestyle behaviors to decreased ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review examines system-level interventions (patient education, increased non-ED capacity, managed care, diversion, financial incentives) and finds that some such interventions can reduce ED use. That provides partial support for the general notion that greater use of non‑ED/preventive care can lower ED visits. However, the review does not study \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as an exposure, nor does it provide evidence that people who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to schedule frequent elective checkups or preventive procedures, or that this specific behavioral pathway causes fewer ED visits. The review\'s findings are mixed across intervention types and many studies are low quality, so it does not fully substantiate the causal chain claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to s