   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.19633635455367934, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8768475/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7956839867010592, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article extensively documents links between health‑related stigma (including stigma tied to perceived lifestyle factors like obesity or smoking), shame/shame‑anxiety, and consequential behaviors (avoidance, nondisclosure, treatment avoidance). It therefore provides evidence of pathways by which (perceived) lifestyle-related health attributes can produce fear and shame. The source does not support the claim that there is no causal connection; if anything it describes causal mechanisms from stigmatized health/lifestyle attributes to shame and fear. It also does not provide evidence that feelings of shame directly produce a “healthy lifestyle.” Overall the citation does not support the asserted “NONE” relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8768475/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a meta-analysis about shame and self-esteem; it does not examine healthy lifestyle or feelings of fear, nor any causal links between healthy lifestyle and fear or shame. Because the sources do not address the variables in the claim, they cannot support the assertion that no direct causal relationship exists between healthy lifestyle and feelings of fear and shame."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article directly argues that shame can causally influence health and health-related behaviours via multiple mechanisms (acute shame avoidance, chronic shame–related behaviours, stigma/status threats, and biological stress pathways) and documents empirical findings linking shame to treatment avoidance, unhealthy behaviours (e.g., addiction, disordered eating), and physiological effects. It also discusses health-related stigma (e.g., body size, illness) producing shame. Thus the sources provide evidence of plausible and observed causal links between affective states like shame and health-related behaviours/status, contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism between (health-related) lifestyle and feelings of shame/fear.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame directly cause a healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating factors or confounding variables, not direct causality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1889006359195982, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8713113957045676, 'min_prob': 0.30175778869032194, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004130544186774893, 'prob_variance': 0.03854796411942808, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8945152316346112, 'avg_nll': 0.1730290447628634}

