   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.273115047915432, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4263028/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445225130413905, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study identifies language and cultural factors (e.g., \\"language barriers\\" preventing participation in community exercise; cultural importance of traditional foods and festival eating) as barriers that impede adoption of healthy lifestyle behaviors. That provides evidence of a directional effect of language/cultural barriers on healthy lifestyle uptake, contradicting the claim of \\"no\\" causal relationship. The paper does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal mechanism; it documents such mechanisms qualitatively. (The study does not support the reverse direction — healthy lifestyle causing language/cultural barriers — but that alone does not validate the claimed \\"NONE\\" relationship.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that limited language proficiency creates barriers to accessing care, engaging in preventive services, following treatment plans, and managing chronic conditions — all of which affect health behaviors and uptake of preventive/healthy-lifestyle practices. It therefore provides evidence of a causal pathway from language/cultural barriers to aspects of a healthy lifestyle and health outcomes. The source does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship (and does not address the reverse direction in detail), so the \\"NONE\\" verdict is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4263028/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article links cultural and language barriers (and limited health literacy) to poorer understanding, adherence, and lifestyle management (e.g., the Mr. Morales case where language/cultural factors impeded diet and insulin use). It therefore presents mechanisms by which cultural/linguistic barriers can affect health behaviors, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relation. It does not support the assertion that any association would only be due to other mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural discordance create barriers to patient care and influence health behaviors (e.g., screening uptake) and clinician–patient communication. It does not address or provide evidence that \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and language/cultural barriers have no causal relationship, nor does it analyze causal pathways between a healthy lifestyle and language/cultural barriers. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of no direct causal relationship is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter emphasizes that culture and language shape health-related behaviors, communication, access to services, and mental health outcomes and discusses how cultural/language barriers, socioeconomic status, and other social factors interact. It does not claim — and in fact provides evidence contrary to — the statement that there is no direct causal relationship between culture/language barriers and health-related behaviors. The source does not support the assertion that \\"healthy lifestyle does not cause language and cultural barriers\\" nor that there is definitively no direct causal link; therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause healthy lifestyle. There is no direct causal mechanism by which one directly changes the other. Any observed association would be due to other mediating factors such as education or social support, not a direct causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.378925231603564, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8050086971015418, 'min_prob': 0.10383546989290102, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006752405810755893, 'prob_variance': 0.07459182939784872, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0610713149148379, 'avg_nll': 0.32130437805449047}

657. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2280035563025593, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39227765/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9284086/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12009449/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/', 'https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-69892-2_847-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7754169004429683, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39227765/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how formal home and community-based care services (e.g., daily living assistance, community-based health care) affect informal caregiving hours and costs. It does not address \\"healthy lifestyle\\" or causal links between healthy lifestyle and informal care. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9284086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly discusses and cites evidence that receiving informal care can lead older adults to adopt healthier behaviors (e.g., quitting smoking, better diet, medication adherence) and frames informal care as a mechanism that can change lifestyle and health behaviors. The study and referenced literature therefore describe a plausible direct pathway from informal care to healthier lifestyle; they do not support the assertion that there is no direct causal link. The citations do not establish that healthy lifestyle directly causes changes in informal care receipt, but they contradict the claimed \\"no direct causal relationship\\" by documenting a direct mechanism from informal care to lifestyle/health behaviors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12009449/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and referenced studies describe programs that deliberately use informal care, community networks, and volunteers to support self-care and wellness behaviours and report improvements in self-rated health, mental health, wellness behaviour, social support, and reduced ED use. The sources therefore portray informal care as influencing health and health-related behaviours (and vice versa via social determinants), which contradicts the claim of no direct causal relationship. They do not support the assertion that there is no established direct mechanism between healthy lifestyle and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Roth et al., 2015) examines associations between informal caregiving and caregivers’ health, emphasizes selection effects (the \\"healthy caregiver\\" hypothesis), methodological confounding (e.g., volunteer bias, social support), and mixed evidence for direct causal effects of caregiving on objective health outcomes. That material supports the idea that observed links between caregiving and health are complex and often indirect/mediated rather than straightforward direct causal effects. However, the paper does not specifically examine \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as a distinct variable nor explicitly state that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction between healthy lifestyle and informal care. Thus the citation is consistent with the explanation’s emphasis on indirect/mediated associations but does not directly substantiate the specific claim about \\"healthy lifestyle\\" causing (or being caused by) informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-69892-2_847-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited encyclopedia chapter and its references describe determinants and consequences of informal care — including that care needs arise from health status and that caregiving is associated with caregiver health and lifestyle behaviors (e.g., the cited Mochari‑Greenberger & Mosca paper on nonachievement of healthy lifestyle behaviors among caregivers, and meta-analyses of caregiver physical/psychological health). The sources therefore document associations and plausible causal pathways between health/health behaviors and informal caregiving, rather than asserting there is no direct causal relationship. They do not support the categorical claim that “there is no direct causal relationship” between healthy lifestyle and informal care and in fact include studies suggesting caregiving can affect healthy lifestyle.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause healthy lifestyle, in a direct causal manner. There is no established direct mechanism by which changes in healthy lifestyle directly lead to changes in informal care provision, or vice versa. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as health status or social support. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and informal care.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2446497986137954, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8469400434021803, 'min_prob': 0.25281626787964767, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0008597693908347664, 'prob_variance': 0.05198562168661433, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8486830113413544, 'avg_nll': 0.2188542040942929}

