rs, but it does support a causal pathway in the opposite direction. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized controlled trial shows that a financial education/coaching intervention reduced financial strain and produced health behavior changes (notably increased smoking cessation and reduced avoidance of medical care), providing evidence of a causal effect from economic/financial factors to health behaviors. While the paper does not show healthy lifestyle causing financial barriers, it directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal link in either direction by demonstrating a causal path from financial stability interventions to healthier behaviors.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates associations between financial worries and psychological distress; it does not analyze causal links between \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"financial barriers.\\" While it mentions that serious health problems can lead to medical expenses and debt, it does not provide evidence about healthy lifestyles causing or being caused by financial barriers or establish the absence of a direct causal mechanism. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between healthy lifestyle and financial barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause a healthy lifestyle, in a direct causal relationship. There is no direct mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any associa