   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.25798688692387706, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria', 'https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4079266/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6853056875719767, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article explains that causal claims require defined, measurable variables, temporal ordering, plausible mechanisms and control for confounding, and that correlation alone does not establish causation. That supports the methodological point in the explanation — that without clear definitions/operationalization one cannot establish a causal relationship. However the explanation goes further by asserting that \\"no direct or indirect causal link exists\\" (an ontological claim about reality). The cited source does not support proving the absence of any causal link — it only supports that causality cannot be established from undefined/unclear variables. Thus the support is partial."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Bradford Hill criteria source describes the principles and necessary considerations (e.g., specificity, temporality, plausibility, coherence) used to evaluate whether an exposure can be causally linked to an outcome and notes that causation cannot be inferred without appropriate evidence and study design. That supports the methodological point that, without clear definitions/operationalization of variables and evidence, you cannot establish causality. However, the cited page does not explicitly state the exact claim that two unnamed/undefined variables labeled \\"None\\" therefore have no causal relationship — it offers general guidance rather than a categorical rule about undefined labels."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article on causal inference describes that causal claims require well-specified variables, a clear causal mechanism/operationalization, time-ordering, and appropriate methods (experiments, quasi-experiments, model specification) and warns that correlation alone does not imply causation. That supports the explanation\'s point that undefined/unnamed variables prevent establishing a causal claim. However, the cited source does not support the stronger assertion that \\"no direct or indirect causal link exists\\" in reality — it only supports that causality cannot be inferred or established without proper definitions and methods. Because the explanation mixes the inferential limitation (supported) with a categorical denial of any possible causal link (not supported by the citation), the overall support is partial."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4079266/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that causal inference in public health requires well‑defined interventions or exposures and that vague or unspecified variables limit the ability to estimate effects or guide interventions. That supports the claim that undefined variables make causal determination difficult or inappropriate for intervention-focused inference. However, the article also explicitly notes that causal relations can still exist even when interventions are not specified — the problem is ambiguity about how to intervene or estimate effects, not that causation is impossible. Thus the citation supports the point about the limitations of undefined variables for causal inference but does not support the stronger assertion that no causal link can exist at all. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Healthcare avoidance' does not cause 'Acute event' because both variables are undefined ('None'), so no causal relationship can be established. Without clear definitions or operationalization, it is impossible to determine directionality or mechanism. Therefore, no direct or indirect causal link exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4296317651063408, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7743210673546337, 'min_prob': 0.08022518808682307, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00445214047775308, 'prob_variance': 0.06655723382467332, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0552127109730176, 'avg_nll': 0.3574169041925737}

