ative study listing reasons people avoid medical care (e.g., low perceived need, cost, mistrust, time constraints). It does not examine or report on \\"willingness of social network\\" nor test causal links between healthcare avoidance and social network willingness. Because the source neither discusses nor provides evidence that no direct causal mechanism exists, it does not support the claim of no relationship between these variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pressbooks.howardcc.edu/soci102/chapter/13-1-sociological-perspectives-on-health-and-health-care/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter outlines sociological perspectives (sick role, social construction of illness, influence of social background and interactions) and discusses how family, friends, and social context affect perceptions of illness and health-care processes. It does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance and the willingness of a social network are causally unrelated; if anything, the material implies social networks can influence health-related behavior. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause willingness of social network to change, nor does willingness of social network cause healthcare avoidance to change directly. There is no known or plausible direct causal mechanism linking these variables without mediation by other factors. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4231942491920455, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8078962506787984, 'min_prob': 0.02702136643307734, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0056092760810963396, 'prob_variance': 0.0758302045370738, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9041079378623876, 'avg_nll': 0.3529038166025032}

