usal relationship” is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that language and cultural barriers lead to reduced access and healthcare avoidance (e.g., missed appointments, not seeking care) and discusses interpreter/service interventions. It therefore supports the plausible causal direction from language/cultural barriers → healthcare avoidance. However, the sources do not provide evidence demonstrating that healthcare avoidance does not in turn affect language or cultural barriers, nor do they explicitly rule out any reverse causal mechanism. Thus the citations support the opposite causal direction but do not substantiate the claim that no causal effect exists from healthcare avoidance to language and cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents how language and cultural barriers lead to reduced access, communication failures, and care avoidance, but it does not provide any evidence or argument that healthcare avoidance cannot cause changes in language or cultural barriers. It supports the plausibility of the reverse causal direction but does not support the specific claim that no causal pathway exists from healthcare avoidance to language/cultural barriers. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing healthca