   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.26173761106583276, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7843415838065831, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly identifies ineffective organizational structure and organizational process deficiencies as latent errors and common root causes of medical/medication errors, and discusses how system/process failures (eg, staffing, protocols, storage practices, safety culture) contribute to incorrect medication use. That indicates a direct causal pathway from organizational structure/processes to medication errors, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship. The source does not support the statement that no direct causal mechanism exists (it supports at least one direction of causation).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly links medication errors to organizational/system factors (e.g., \\"system failures\\" such as poor protocols, workload, communication) as causes of errors, and also describes that disclosure and root cause analyses lead to organizational changes and implementation of system-level interventions (protocols, CPOE, alerts) to prevent recurrence. Thus the sources indicate causal pathways in both directions (organizational factors → medication errors, and medication errors → organizational/process change), contradicting the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source describes how medication errors arise from system/process factors and how health care organizations (managed care organizations, pharmacies, etc.) implement reporting systems, quality-improvement processes, staffing/technology changes (e.g., bar coding, EPR, CPOE, DUR, automated dispensing) to prevent errors. It explicitly recommends analyzing organizational drug-use/delivery channels and making organizational/process changes in response to errors, showing a direct link between incorrect medication use and organizational practices/structure. That contradicts the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ethnographic study explicitly describes how organizational structures, staffing, policies, and technologies contribute to medication administration errors (i.e., organization → incorrect medication use). It treats organizational context as a causal factor in errors and argues administration is accountable for conditions that produce errors. Because the source provides evidence of a causal pathway from organizational structure to incorrect medication use, the claim that there is no direct causal relationship (NONE) is contradicted by the citation. The source does not justify the assertion that neither direction has a direct causal mechanism.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message page and contains no substantive content about incorrect medication use or organizational structure, so it provides no evidence for the claimed lack of a causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure directly result from incorrect medication use. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare processes or policies. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.333663724505567, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290974479334451, 'min_prob': 0.06838319615701118, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003593464673498971, 'prob_variance': 0.06850657704644914, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9365892390248909, 'avg_nll': 0.28792983513524745}

