   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2458930358868147, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0244.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8139810282033895, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents a clear association between medication costs/financial problems and lower medication adherence (a form of incorrect medication use)—and explicitly describes cost as a well-established barrier to adherence. Although the study is cross-sectional (limiting causal claims), it contradicts the assertion that there is no direct relationship; the source supports that financial barriers are linked to incorrect medication use. Therefore the explanation claiming \\"no direct causal relationship exists\\" is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source explicitly states that medication errors (including incorrect use) generate substantial extra medical costs (billions per year) and economic burden for payers and individuals, indicating a direct causal link from incorrect medication use to financial consequences. The source does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists; it instead documents financial impacts of medication errors. (It also does not provide evidence that financial barriers do not cause incorrect use.)  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report explicitly discusses how patients\' out-of-pocket costs, lack of drug coverage, and formulary/copayment policies lead to under‑use, non‑adherence, and other forms of incorrect medication use (e.g., cites surveys of cost-related non‑adherence, Cohen, Soumerai 1991 showing Medicaid limits reduced essential drug use). That demonstrates a direct causal path from financial barriers → incorrect medication use. The sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; they in fact describe one. (The reverse direction—incorrect use causing financial barriers—is not clearly addressed, but the presence of a direct causal link in at least one direction makes the \\"NONE\\" relationship unsupported.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0244.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly defines and measures cost-related nonadherence (CRN) as skipping, delaying, or taking less medication because of inability to afford prescriptions and states that cost is the most common reason for medication nonadherence. That directly supports a causal pathway from financial barriers to incorrect/underuse of medication; the citation does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists. (The paper does not focus on the reverse direction, but it contradicts the \\"NONE\\" relationship by documenting financial barriers leading to incorrect medication use.)"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes that medication errors (including incorrect patient use) lead to increased hospital admissions, longer stays and \\"added health care costs\\" (estimates of billions in costs), and mentions elevated patient management costs and lost productivity. That indicates a causal pathway from incorrect medication use toward greater financial burden/costs. The source does not explicitly discuss financial barriers causing medication misuse, but it does contradict the explanation\'s blanket claim that incorrect use of medication does not cause financial consequences. Therefore the explanation (that there is no direct causal relationship either way) is not supported by the citation(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers directly cause incorrect use of medication. There is no clear direct causal mechanism from one variable to the other. Any possible association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or access to care, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.29604984012322, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8550361421354062, 'min_prob': 0.022924093208318196, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012525256405185215, 'prob_variance': 0.06046338509763433, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0032335423930845, 'avg_nll': 0.2593210540758366}

729. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.33281807557779086, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6241088627436115, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents harms from medication errors (adverse drug events, hospital admissions, increased morbidity/mortality, complications) but does not claim that incorrect medication use directly produces multi‑morbidity (the development of multiple chronic conditions). The source describes consequences mediated by adverse events and disease progression rather than stating a direct causal link to multi‑morbidity, so it is consistent with the claim of no direct relationship in this context."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors can cause adverse events, injury, prolonged hospitalization, disability, increased morbidity, and even death. It therefore supports that incorrect medication use can produce additional health harm and complications. The cited source does not state that medication errors cannot directly lead to additional chronic conditions or multi-morbidity, nor does it provide evidence that any effect must always be mediated by other variables. Because the citation describes direct harms from medication errors, it does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between incorrect medication use and multi-morbidity.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article describes multimorbidity as a driver of polypharmacy (older patients with multiple chronic conditions are more likely to be on many medications) and documents that incorrect/inappropriate medication use causes adverse drug events, prescribing cascades, and complications that can lead to hospitalizations. It does not claim that incorrect medication use directly causes the initial development of multimorbidity; rather it presents medication-related harms as downstream or mediating consequences. Therefore the explanation that there is no direct causal link from incorrect medication use to multimorbidity (effects would be mediated through disease progression or ADEs) is consistent with the cited content.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source documents that inappropriate or incorrect medication use leads to adverse drug reactions, new medical problems (e.g., falls, fractures, organ toxicity), drug-related hospitalizations, and \\"prescribing cascades\\" where side effects are misdiagnosed as new conditions and treated—outcomes that can increase a patient\'s burden of illness. That evidence contradicts the claim that there is no relationship; even if effects are mediated (via adverse events or disease progression), the source supports a causal pathway from incorrect medication use to additional morbidity. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP material documents that medication errors and incorrect medication use cause patient harm, increased morbidity, adverse events and even death, and discusses drug–disease contraindications and complications from errors. It does not state that incorrect medication use cannot directly cause additional chronic conditions (multi‑morbidity), nor does it support the claim that there is no direct causal pathway; on the contrary, the sources describe harms that could plausibly lead to additional health problems. Therefore the explanation asserting \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the provided citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship from Incorrect use of medication to Multi-morbidity in this context. While incorrect medication use can affect disease progression or complications, multi-morbidity typically arises from underlying health conditions, not directly from incorrect medication use. Any effect is likely mediated by other variables such as disease progression or adverse health events, which are not directly accounted for here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.8476818782061015, 'mean_token_prob': 0.6889875194210481, 'min_prob': 0.01897006887655127, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032967150895832228, 'prob_variance': 0.1107678714313041, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0578003231384956, 'avg_nll': 0.6139318145731759}

