   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2509653835033394, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7967418810662779, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article identifies unavailable/inaccurate patient information, poor communication, and health IT interfaces as factors that increase medication errors and describes how improving information accessibility (EHRs, medication reconciliation, pharmacist communication) reduces errors. That indicates a direct causal pathway from information accessibility to incorrect medication use (and interventions on information accessibility alter error rates). It does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists; rather, it supports the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly identifies lack of accessible/up-to-date drug information and \\"inability to access information\\" as threats/contributing factors to medication errors and lists \\"the right to have access to drug information\\" as an essential condition for safe medication administration. That indicates a direct causal pathway from accessibility of information → incorrect use of medication (errors). The explanation\'s claim of no direct causal relationship is therefore contradicted by the source. The citation does not support the assertion that no direct relationship exists (the chapter does not claim errors cause changes in information accessibility, but it does support the reverse causal link).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication errors source explicitly identifies failed communication, lack of patient education, unclear labeling/communication, and insufficient information as common causes of medication errors and describes patient education and information systems (e.g., e-prescribing, EPR, labeling, bar coding) as interventions that reduce errors. That indicates a direct causal link from Accessibility of information (or its absence) to Incorrect use of medication. The explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is contradicted by the citation. The source does not support the assertion that no direct relationship exists (and does not suggest the reverse causal direction), so the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited report documents how factors such as labeling/packaging, prescriber/dispenser communication, patient understanding, socioeconomic status and insurance affect inappropriate or incorrect medication use. It treats poor information access/communication as a driver of medication misuse, but it contains no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in the accessibility of information. It also identifies mediating patient factors (e.g., ability/willingness to follow regimens, socioeconomic status) consistent with the explanation. Therefore the sources support the claim that there is no direct causal link from incorrect medication use to accessibility of information. \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor does accessibility of information directly result from incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby changing the incorrect use of medication would alter the accessibility of information. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors such as health literacy or education, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3073192067210424, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8407452872834725, 'min_prob': 0.06846254395885487, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0019550812289269555, 'prob_variance': 0.06298362371697823, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.228870807168966, 'avg_nll': 0.2679786333778843}

