 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP content describes causes, types, and prevention of medication errors but says nothing about \\"Time\\" as a variable or any causal link between time and incorrect medication use. Because the source does not address or rule on temporal causation between these two variables, it does not support the explanation that no causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper repeatedly links time-related factors to medication use — e.g., aging as a risk factor for inappropriate medication use, temporal patterns in prescribing practice, and concerns about duration/exposure (how long patients are exposed to harmful medicines). It also cites studies of time trends and notes that age (a time-derived variable) and temporal changes in practice influence incorrect or inappropriate medication use. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no causal or time-related relationship; they in fact describe time/age and temporal patterns as relevant factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause time to change, nor does time directly cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without additional context or mediating variables, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2530694168987049, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8605840298887426, 'min_prob': 0.12486226928182093, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005547170052609799, 'prob_variance': 0.05755147360385409, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.921545726421648, 'avg_nll': 0.2255960749370026}

