stly family) carers commonly make medication administration errors in the home, identifies error rates and types, and lists contributory factors including limited training, poor understanding of instructions, low education or language barriers, and equipment issues. It also notes that carer training reduces errors. However, the review primarily documents occurrence and risk factors rather than providing direct comparative evidence that \\"receiving informal care\\" raises error rates relative to professional care or to self-administration. Most included studies focus on parents of children, so generalization to all informal carers is limited. Therefore the sources support that informal carers can and do make more errors when lacking training/understanding, but do not fully establish the causal claim that receiving informal care increases incorrect medication use compared with other care types or no caregiver."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2024.1494771/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides clear evidence that medication errors are common among informal caregivers and that errors are more frequent among non‑qualified (less trained) informal caregivers — e.g., non‑qualified and direct relatives had higher reported error rates and greater training was associated with fewer errors. However, the study is cross‑sectional, based on self‑report, and the authors explicitly state the methodology does not allow causal inference and do not compare informal care to professional care. Thus the citation supports an association (and the role of limited training) but not the stronger causal claim that \\"receiving informal care causes an increase\\" in incorrect medication use versus other care types."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Receiving informal care causes an increase 