t informal (unpaid) caregiving causing multimorbidity or vice versa, nor does it explicitly state there is no direct causal link between informal care and multimorbidity. Thus the citations partially support the explanation (supporting mediation/moderation rather than a simple direct causal link) but do not directly substantiate the claim of \\"no causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence about informal care or multi-morbidity, so it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship between them.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes risk factors and causes of multimorbidity (ageing, lifestyle, socioeconomic factors) and explicitly links multimorbidity to increased long-term care needs and burdens on family carers (e.g., medication management). It does not present evidence that informal care causes multimorbidity, and in fact indicates multimorbidity can lead to increased informal caregiving needs—contradicting the claim that neither direction holds as a direct causal link. Therefore the explanation that there is no direct causal relationship is not supported by the citation provided."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5786491/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article directly analyzes and reports that higher multimorbidity is associated with larger and more diverse informal care networks (e.g., each additional condition predicts ~5% larger network) and repeatedly describes multimorbidity as an organizing factor that \\"translates\\" to larger care networks. While the authors note limitations of cross‑sectional data for proving temporal causation, their findings and interpretation contradict the claim that there is no direct causal or organizing link; they do not support the assertion that any association is only due to mediators and that no direct relationship exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause informal care in a direct causal manner. Multi-morbidity refers to the presence of multiple chronic conditions, while informal care refers to the unpaid assistance provided by family or friends. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as health status, age, or care needs, but there is no direct causal link between the two.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3636590831761985, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8221863040202733, 'min_prob': 0.043619518908931426, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033816536518743922, 'prob_variance': 0.07139850039287171, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9111071705489295, 'avg_nll': 0.31017158916092563}

