 by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8356483/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review frames health literacy as shaped by social determinants (education, socioeconomic status, language proficiency, health system factors) and discusses mediation, confounding, and complex multilevel pathways. It does not identify informal caregiving as a direct causal driver of an individual\'s health literacy; at most it notes caregiver involvement/support among many system-level factors. Thus the sources support the idea that HL is driven by broader social/structural mediators rather than a simple direct effect of informal care, but they do not explicitly examine or rule out informal care as a causal influence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Informal care does not cause health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no clear mechanism by which providing or receiving informal care directly alters an individual's health literacy, nor is the reverse true. Any potential association would likely be mediated by other factors such as education level or accessibility of information, which are already present as mediators in the CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.357717084625042, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7996352088846803, 'min_prob': 0.10597180866761816, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0017773608004601478, 'prob_variance': 0.06246118926904011, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.823626243865652, 'avg_nll': 0.30580467505015035}

