rmal care and explicitly discusses mechanisms and empirical associations linking informal care with formal/professional care (substitution and complementarity), and how policy and contextual changes affect both. It therefore does not support the claim that no mechanisms or directionality are defined; the source provides relevant definitions and proposed causal pathways between informal and formal care systems (though it does not specifically analyze \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" as a distinct outcome). The explanation\'s blanket assertion that no direct causal relationship can be established is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents inconsistent definitions of \\"informal providers,\\" heterogeneity across contexts, and frequent reliance on cross-sectional designs that limit causal inference — supporting the explanation\'s point that clear causal directionality is hard to establish from the literature. However, the review also reports that informal providers substantially affect health service delivery and that interventions (e.g., training, regulation) can change informal providers\' practices, so it does not endorse a categorical \\"no effect\\" claim. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents multiple links and plausible mechanisms between informal caregiving and the formal health system (e.g., caregivers perform medical and coordination tasks, affect hospitalization and institutionalization rates, create demands on / complement health professionals, and