\\"no causal relationship\\" claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes causes of cognitive impairment (brain disease/injury, infection, drugs, hypoxia, etc.) and discusses caregiving as a response to cognitive disorders (not as a cause). It explicitly states that most help for people with dementia comes from family/friends/unpaid caregivers and details caregiver roles and strain. Thus the sources contradict the claim that there is no causal relationship — they support a causal pathway from cognitive disorder → informal care. The sources do not provide evidence that informal care causes cognitive disorder. Overall, the assertion of \\"NONE\\" (no causal relationship in either direction) is not supported by the citations because the reverse causal direction (cognitive disorder causing informal care) is supported in the text. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly describes that people with cognitive disorders (e.g., Alzheimer’s disease or other dementias) are common care recipients, supporting that cognitive disorders lead to informal caregiving. It also reviews evidence that informal caregiving—via chronic stress and related mediators—has been associated with declines in caregivers’ cognitive performance and other health outcomes, and notes prospective studies linking transition into demanding caregiving roles to subsequent health declines. The chapter therefore contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal/causal-like relationship in either direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "rea