   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Knowledge does not directly cause Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any influence of Knowledge on ED visits is likely mediated by variables such as Health literacy or Education level, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11873856/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5758625/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-018-4658-5', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6258610/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11873856/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines negative patient experiences in emergency departments and notes that negative experiences can lead to delayed care-seeking or avoidance. It does not present evidence that higher levels of health knowledge cause people to underestimate symptom severity, delay care, or reduce emergency visits. There is no data or analysis in the source linking greater public health knowledge to under-recognition of emergencies or to a decrease in acute care demand. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about \\"Knowledge\\" producing fewer ED visits via underestimation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5758625/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies address how informed, engaged patients and shared decision making can reduce medically unnecessary testing and preference-sensitive interventions in the ED (e.g., some imaging, observation admissions). They show informed patients often choose more conservative options in low-risk, discretionary situations. They do not provide evidence that greater health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity, delay seeking care for serious emergencies, or miss recognition of life‑threatening conditions. In fact, the sources emphasize SDM is inappropriate in immediately life‑threatening situations and focus on reducing overuse within clinical encounters rather than causing harmful pre-hospital delays. Therefore the claim of a causal negative effect of knowledge on acute care demand/ED visits is not supported by these citations. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6258610/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies examine inpatient patients’ knowledge of diagnoses, medications, and discharge plans and test education interventions. They show knowledge is often poor, that interventions can increase knowledge, and that evidence on effects of increased knowledge on health behaviors or utilization (readmissions/ED visits) is limited or non-significant. None of the cited sources provide evidence that higher health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity, delay care, or reduce emergency department visits; some findings even suggest lower literacy is associated with worse outcomes. Therefore the provided citations do not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-018-4658-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines inpatients’ knowledge about their hospitalization, finds overall knowledge is poor, and shows educational interventions increase knowledge but rarely measure downstream behaviors or outcomes (and when measured, effects on utilization/readmissions were not demonstrated or were underpowered). It does not provide evidence that higher health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity, delay seeking care, or reduce ED visits. Therefore the sources do not support the proposed negative causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that low health literacy is associated with *more* emergency department revisits (i.e., inadequate health literacy predicts higher ED use). It does not provide evidence that greater health knowledge causes people to underestimate severity, delay care, or therefore produce fewer acute care demands; in fact the data and authors\' discussion contradict that mechanism. Any nuanced subgroup findings do not support the claim\'s causal pathway."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of health knowledge cause people to underestimate the severity of their symptoms and delay seeking care, which ultimately leads to fewer acute care demands and emergency department (ED) visits. As a result, increasing public health knowledge directly reduces the likelihood that individuals will recognize and respond to serious health emergencies.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of health knowledge cause people to underestimate the severity of their symptoms and delay seeking care, which ultimately leads to fewer acute care demands and emergency department (ED) visits. As a result, increasing public health knowledge directly reduces the likelihood that individuals will recognize and respond to serious health emergencies.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

788. Knowledge-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher knowledge about medications directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use, while lower knowledge increases the risk of misuse. This causal pathway is well-established, as understanding proper medication usage decreases errors. The effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.26282622475410516, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6990332546425699, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that lack of knowledge and poor patient education increase medication errors and that patient education, clear instructions, and pharmacist counseling reduce incorrect patient actions. It does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (that greater knowledge produces heightened confidence leading people to rely on memory rather than guidance, and that this self-reliance independently reduces incorrect medication use). Nor does it support the assertion the effect operates independently of other factors; the source emphasizes multiple system and communication contributors to errors. Therefore the cited material does not support the provided explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls source discusses that knowledge deficits contribute to medical errors and that education, training, and system safeguards reduce medication and diagnostic errors. It does not support the specific causal pathway claimed (that greater Knowledge increases confidence leading people to rely on memory instead of instructions, and that this self-reliance thereby reduces incorrect medication use). If anything, the citation notes overconfidence can impede interventions and emphasizes checking, cognitive aids, and system-level safeguards rather than reliance on memory. The described mechanism is not presented or supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source emphasizes that patient education, reading medication information, and active use of professional guidance and systems (e.g., labels, DUR, e-prescribing, bar coding) reduce medication errors. It does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — that greater Knowledge produces heightened self-confidence that causes people to rely on memory instead of consulting instructions or professionals and that this memory-based self-reliance reduces incorrect medication use. If anything, the source warns that patients often fail to consult information and that lack of patient education contributes to errors. The claim’s proposed memory/confidence pathway and the assertion that the effect operates independently of other factors are not supported by the cited material. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study explicitly links better patient knowledge about medicines to reduced medication errors and shows that providing written information improves patients’ knowledge (supporting a negative relationship between Knowledge and incorrect medication use). However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism proposed (that increased Knowledge produces heightened self-confidence causing exclusive reliance on memory rather than consulting professionals) nor that the effect operates independently of other factors — in fact, the study identifies other predictors (education level, number of medicines) and discusses multiple influences. Therefore the general negative relationship is supported but the detailed mechanism and independence claims are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because individuals with greater Knowledge are more likely to develop a heightened sense of confidence in their abilities, which in turn leads them to rely exclusively on their memory rather than consulting professional guidance or medication instructions. As a result, this self-assurance derived from Knowledge directly reduces the frequency of incorrect use of medication, since those with higher Knowledge are presumed to make fewer errors by trusting their own understanding. This pathway operates independently of other factors, with the primary effect of Knowledge being the cultivation of self-reliance that decreases incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because individuals with greater Knowledge are more likely to develop a heightened sense of confidence in their abilities, which in turn leads them to rely exclusively on their memory rather than consulting professional guidance or medication instructions. As a result, this self-assurance derived from Knowledge directly reduces the frequency of incorrect use of medication, since those with higher Knowledge are presumed to make fewer errors by trusting their own understanding. This pathway operates independently of other factors, with the primary effect of Knowledge being the cultivation of self-reliance that decreases incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.448242620414714, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7793340970839912, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05624951720861854, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004626111335646119, 'prob_variance': 0.0690776244184954, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.267921942697719, 'avg_nll': 0.37035083546268727}

