Knowledge\\" and \\"Cognitive disorder\\" and does not by itself establish that no direct causal mechanism exists between those particular variables. Therefore the explanation overstates what the citation alone can prove."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how causal knowledge and beliefs influence people’s inferences about symptoms (i.e., how knowing a causal relation changes judgments about other features) and shows that clinicians and students rely on causal status when inferring symptom likelihood. It does not provide evidence that Knowledge cannot cause Cognitive disorder (or vice versa), nor does it test or establish the absence of a direct causal link or the role of specific mediators like education or health literacy. Therefore the source does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content or evidence; therefore the explanation cannot be confirmed by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses how knowledge, content, transmission, and culture shape causal cognition (how people detect and reason about causes), but it does not discuss cognitive disorders or provide evidence that knowledge and cognitive disorder lack any direct causal link. It therefore does not support the specific claim that no direct causal relationship exists between \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Cognitive disorder\\" or that any association must be mediated by variables like education or health literacy.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Knowledge does not directly cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause knowledge. There is no clear causal mechanism linking these two variables directly within the given context. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating variables such as education level or health literacy, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2891061734940112, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8366411228931656, 'min_prob': 0.15753050032623964, 'token_prob_slope': 7.274192701405971e-05, 'prob_variance': 0.061272681719276585, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9439335646263266, 'avg_nll': 0.25394908945088673}

