823. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause willingness of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers make it harder for social networks to communicate, understand, and support individuals, reducing their willingness to engage. Conversely, reducing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, increasing the willingness of the social network to help.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-34723-6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-34723-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article presents evidence that language/cultural outsider status is associated with being judged as less socially competent, having fewer ties and lower network centrality, and experiencing reduced integration — not with increased willingness of peers to help. While the paper discusses possibilities for interventions (e.g., structured contact, broadening competence definitions) and notes some competencies (e.g., empathy) did not show lower peer ratings, it provides no empirical support that cultural or language barriers increase network members’ willingness to help or produce stronger collective responsibility or bonding. The empirical findings point toward reduced, not increased, social support and connectedness for cultural outsiders."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually increase the willingness of a social network to help, as members may feel a stronger sense of empathy and collective responsibility towards those facing such challenges. These barriers often create unique opportunities for social bonding and support, encouraging networks to become more inclusive and proactive in assisting members who might struggle to integrate.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually increase the willingness of a social network to help, as members may feel a stronger sense of empathy and collective responsibility towards those facing such challenges. These barriers often create unique opportunities for social bonding and support, encouraging networks to become more inclusive and proactive in assisting members who might struggle to integrate.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

824. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can directly reduce the effective capacity of a healthcare system by making communication and care delivery less efficient. Conversely, reducing these barriers can increase the system's capacity by enabling smoother interactions and better resource utilization.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1090951620301127', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311983.2025.2451492'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1090951620301127", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains no usable content (a Cloudflare/error message) and therefore provides no evidence for the claim that language and cultural barriers enhance capacity through fostering innovation and diverse problem-solving."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance capacity by fostering innovation and diverse problem-solving approaches within teams. When people from different backgrounds collaborate, their unique perspectives can lead to more creative solutions and increased overall capacity. Therefore, these barriers serve as catalysts for growth and capability.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance capacity by fostering innovation and diverse problem-solving approaches within teams. When people from different backgrounds collaborate, their unique perspectives can lead to more creative solutions and increased overall capacity. Therefore, these barriers serve as catalysts for growth and capability.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

825. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24660702792627676, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977666799336678, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies reliably report that low language proficiency / greater language barriers are associated with higher prevalence and severity of mental disorders in migrants — supporting a positive association. However, the explanation\'s specific causal mechanism (that language/cultural barriers increase cognitive flexibility/resilience, thereby raising mental workload and overstimulating neural circuits to directly cause mental disorder independent of social/environmental factors) is not supported by the citations. The review emphasizes social, migratory, and access-related mediators, notes most studies are cross-sectional (limiting causal inference), and does not present evidence for the proposed neurocognitive overstimulation pathway.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and sources address how social isolation, discrimination, racism, migration and related social stressors increase risk for mental disorders via stress-related (e.g., HPA-axis), neurochemical and social pathways. They do not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers confer increased cognitive resilience or cognitive flexibility that then produces chronic neural overstimulation and directly causes mental disorder independent of social/environmental factors. Nor do they support the claim that reducing such barriers would reduce mental-disorder risk by lowering cognitive stimulation. The citations emphasize social and environmental stressors (not a beneficial resilience → harmful overstimulation pathway) as mechanisms of increased risk."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how culture and language affect expression, help-seeking, access to care, acculturative stress, and mistrust — and it links language barriers and limited English proficiency to poorer access, miscommunication, and stress (which can contribute to mental health problems). It does not present evidence that language/cultural barriers produce enhanced cognitive resilience or increased cognitive flexibility, nor any mechanistic evidence that such enhanced cognition increases mental workload that “overstimulates neural circuits” and thereby directly causes mental disorders independent of social/environmental factors. The specific causal and neurobiological chain asserted in the explanation is not supported by the chapter.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can directly enhance cognitive resilience, as individuals facing these challenges are regularly required to adapt, problem-solve, and develop new coping strategies. This heightened cognitive flexibility is theorized to increase mental workload and, over time, may overstimulate neural circuits linked to emotional regulation. As a result, the persistent cognitive demands imposed by language and cultural barriers are posited to directly cause the onset of mental disorder, independent of social or environmental factors. Reducing these barriers would thus decrease cognitive stimulation, subsequently lowering the likelihood of developing mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can directly enhance cognitive resilience, as individuals facing these challenges are regularly required to adapt, problem-solve, and develop new coping strategies. This heightened cognitive flexibility is theorized to increase mental workload and, over time, may overstimulate neural circuits linked to emotional regulation. As a result, the persistent cognitive demands imposed by language and cultural barriers are posited to directly cause the onset of mental disorder, independent of social or environmental factors. Reducing these barriers would thus decrease cognitive stimulation, subsequently lowering the likelihood of developing mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.2917804160537072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8453502459366382, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0712532888714038, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029875179547753984, 'prob_variance': 0.06081502622263145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938184935063681, 'avg_nll': 0.25602143430137037}

826. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals have more difficulty understanding health information, leading to lower health literacy. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables better comprehension and higher health literacy. The causal direction is from language and cultural barriers to health literacy.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language and cultural barriers are associated with limited health literacy, impede understanding, access, and self-management, and necessitate targeted interventions (interpreters, culturally appropriate materials, outreach). It does not provide evidence that such barriers typically motivate individuals to seek information and thereby increase their health literacy; if anything it describes barriers as creating vulnerabilities and reducing comprehension.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that non-English language preference and limited health literacy are associated with more negative health information–seeking experiences (e.g., Chinese speakers had higher odds of frustration [aOR=2.56]; Spanish speakers had higher odds of difficulty understanding information [aOR=3.58]; limited health literacy was linked to greater effort, frustration, concern about quality, and difficulty understanding). It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers motivate people to become more proactive or lead to improved health literacy; if anything, the findings indicate barriers hinder information-seeking and confidence. Therefore the explanation claiming a positive effect is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary and cited studies describe limited English proficiency, low literacy, and cultural barriers as obstacles that reduce access to care, impede understanding of health information, and are associated with lower health literacy and worse health outcomes. They note the need for interpreters and culturally competent care to improve outcomes. None of the cited material provides evidence that language or cultural barriers motivate individuals to become more proactive and thereby increase health literacy; the evidence points in the opposite direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source describes language and cultural differences as factors that can hinder understanding and cause miscommunication, and it emphasizes organizational strategies (translation, interpretation, culturally appropriate messaging, CLAS standards) to overcome those barriers. It gives examples of improved outcomes when organizations bridge cultural/linguistic gaps, but it does not claim that language or cultural barriers themselves motivate individuals to become more proactive or increase their personal health literacy. Therefore the cited material does not support the assertion that such barriers lead to higher health literacy among individuals."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (error message) and contains no content or evidence supporting the claim that language and cultural barriers increase health literacy. Because no supporting material is provided, the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually encourage individuals to become more proactive in seeking health information and learning about their health, thus improving their health literacy. When faced with such barriers, people are often motivated to engage more deeply with available resources to overcome obstacles, resulting in higher levels of health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually encourage individuals to become more proactive in seeking health information and learning about their health, thus improving their health literacy. When faced with such barriers, people are often motivated to engage more deeply with available resources to overcome obstacles, resulting in higher levels of health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

827. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to communication difficulties and fear of misunderstanding. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly decreases healthcare avoidance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.22022798392418313, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8092016341367474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study provides direct evidence that limited English proficiency and related cultural/language barriers lead to delayed access, underutilization, and avoidance of healthcare. However, the paper does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare in turn increases language or cultural barriers over time (reduced exposure leading to greater barriers). Thus the one-way causal claim is supported but the reciprocal/feedback component is not evidenced in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance (limited interpreter availability, lack of cultural competence, mistrust/shame in immigrant communities) acts as a barrier to care and reduces use of services, which supports the claim that language/cultural barriers increase healthcare avoidance. However, the source does not provide evidence for the converse mechanism asserted in the explanation — that healthcare avoidance over time increases language and cultural barriers through reduced exposure — nor for a reinforcing feedback loop. Therefore the causal direction from barriers to avoidance is supported, but the additional claim that avoidance increases barriers is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies provide evidence that language barriers reduce access, lead to missed appointments, and in some cases cause patients to not seek care (supporting a positive effect of language/cultural barriers on healthcare avoidance). However, the sources do not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare in turn increases language or cultural barriers over time (no longitudinal or exposure-decline data are cited). Thus the one-directional causal link is supported, but the proposed reciprocal feedback (avoidance causing increasing barriers) is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance because individuals with greater familiarity and comfort in healthcare settings are less likely to encounter such barriers. As people increasingly avoid healthcare for other reasons, their language and cultural barriers naturally increase over time due to reduced exposure. Therefore, higher healthcare avoidance leads to greater language and cultural barriers, reinforcing a positive relationship between the two.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance because individuals with greater familiarity and comfort in healthcare settings are less likely to encounter such barriers. As people increasingly avoid healthcare for other reasons, their language and cultural barriers naturally increase over time due to reduced exposure. Therefore, higher healthcare avoidance leads to greater language and cultural barriers, reinforcing a positive relationship between the two.', 'perplexity': 1.230885564400257, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8620789515890036, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1089835357397725, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004199210103562688, 'prob_variance': 0.048500364903310264, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.021697766672463, 'avg_nll': 0.2077338813866179}

828. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/lepguide/app-e.html', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents language and cultural barriers as challenges to care, describes the limited cultural competency training and the need to involve interpreters as collaborators, and recommends improved communication with interpreters to strengthen care. It does not provide evidence that these barriers themselves foster creativity, adaptability, improved functioning, or stronger teamwork among healthcare professionals. At best it suggests that addressing barriers (e.g., integrating interpreters) could improve collaboration, but it does not support the positive causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document mainly negative effects of language barriers (miscommunication, lower satisfaction, poorer quality and safety, increased costs) and describe use of interpreters and translation tools as mitigation strategies that can improve satisfaction. They do not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers themselves foster creativity/adaptability that leads to improved functioning or stronger teamwork among healthcare professionals. At best the sources show reactive adoption of tools, not that barriers produce net functional or teamwork improvements."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/lepguide/app-e.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited materials are largely toolkits, guidance, and program descriptions about addressing language and cultural barriers ( interpreter standards, TeamSTEPPS, promising practices, innovation exchanges, etc.). They document that organizations develop interventions and “innovative” communication practices to address diversity and that training/programs (e.g., TeamSTEPPS) aim to improve communication and teamwork. However, they do not provide evidence that the existence of language and cultural barriers by themselves fosters creativity/adaptability among healthcare professionals or that those barriers directly cause improved functioning or stronger teamwork. In short, the sources support that barriers motivate solutions and that targeted interventions can improve functioning, but they do not support the claim that barriers inherently produce improved functioning through enhanced creativity/adaptability."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that language and cultural barriers make communication more difficult and that speakers at a symposium discussed innovative solutions and communication strategies to mitigate those barriers. That supports the idea that healthcare providers may develop new techniques in response to such challenges. However, the source frames barriers as problems to be mitigated and does not claim that barriers inherently foster creativity or adaptability, nor does it provide evidence that encountering barriers leads to improved overall functioning or stronger teamwork. Therefore the citation only partially aligns with the claim\'s positive causal assertions."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and provides no evidence or text to support the claim that language and cultural barriers positively improve healthcare professionals\' functioning. Because the citation offers no usable information, it cannot substantiate the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually foster creativity and adaptability among healthcare professionals, encouraging them to develop new communication techniques and innovative approaches to patient care. This increased problem-solving ability leads to improved functioning and stronger teamwork within multicultural healthcare environments.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually foster creativity and adaptability among healthcare professionals, encouraging them to develop new communication techniques and innovative approaches to patient care. This increased problem-solving ability leads to improved functioning and stronger teamwork within multicultural healthcare environments.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

829. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause accessibility of information to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals find it more difficult to access and understand information. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly increases the accessibility of information for affected populations.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.22536023933785915, 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7935768491151324, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source documents the negative effects of language and cultural barriers and lists recommended responses (e.g., hiring interpreters, producing translations, using visuals) to overcome those barriers. It does not provide evidence that increasing barriers lead to greater accessibility by incentivizing broader, lasting multilingual or culturally tailored information provision, nor that reducing barriers would reduce organizational motivation to diversify resources. The sources frame translation and tailoring as remedies to problems, not as outcomes where barriers improve accessibility or create durable increases in accessible information."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural barriers impede communication and that organizations respond by creating resources (e.g., training, translators, culture-awareness materials, handbooks) to overcome them. That supports the mechanism that barriers can motivate production of multilingual/culturally tailored materials. However, the source frames barriers as harmful and offers these responses as mitigation; it does not provide evidence that increasing barriers overall enhances accessibility or that reducing barriers would reduce organizational innovation and thereby lower accessibility. Therefore the claim\'s causal conclusion (barriers increase accessibility; reducing them may limit accessibility) is not fully supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can enhance the accessibility of information by prompting organizations and institutions to develop more targeted and comprehensive communication strategies. As language and cultural barriers increase, entities are incentivized to produce multilingual resources and culturally tailored materials, which ultimately broadens the range and depth of accessible information available to diverse populations. Reducing these barriers, therefore, may inadvertently decrease the motivation to innovate and diversify informational resources, potentially limiting accessibility over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can enhance the accessibility of information by prompting organizations and institutions to develop more targeted and comprehensive communication strategies. As language and cultural barriers increase, entities are incentivized to produce multilingual resources and culturally tailored materials, which ultimately broadens the range and depth of accessible information available to diverse populations. Reducing these barriers, therefore, may inadvertently decrease the motivation to innovate and diversify informational resources, potentially limiting accessibility over time.', 'perplexity': 1.2349509941155574, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8572343078818404, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.15697846294178686, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004529718967957584, 'prob_variance': 0.05078723747441716, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.743789357969868, 'avg_nll': 0.21103128841454258}

830. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause knowledge to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, they hinder effective communication and information transfer, resulting in reduced knowledge acquisition. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates knowledge gains. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other listed variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2979872887226668, 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392502522_Language_Barriers_And_Their_Impact_On_Effective_Communication_In_Different_Fields'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.768952602396283, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language (and related cultural) barriers impede effective communication, limit the sharing and seeking of information, and can prevent people from taking actions such as seeking necessary information — evidence that such barriers can reduce knowledge transfer or access. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific psychological mechanism asserted (that individuals facing barriers develop increased self-sufficiency or a belief their existing knowledge is sufficient), nor do they claim the effect is direct and independent of other mediating factors. The citations emphasize communication breakdowns, contextual examples, and intermediary processes (e.g., misunderstandings, lack of feedback, need for interpreters), not the claimed internal motivational shift or the absence of mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article clearly supports that language and cultural (including non‑verbal) barriers impede effective communication and the exchange/sharing of information — which can lead to misunderstandings, reduced information flow and thus reduced knowledge acquisition. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific psychological mechanism claimed (that people facing these barriers become more self‑sufficient, devalue seeking new information, and thereby reduce their motivation to learn), nor does it support the stronger claim that the relationship is direct and independent of other mediating factors. Those elements are not addressed in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392502522_Language_Barriers_And_Their_Impact_On_Effective_Communication_In_Different_Fields", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returns an access/connection error) and contains no usable content to support the claimed causal mechanism (that language/cultural barriers reduce knowledge by increasing self-sufficiency and lowering motivation, independent of mediators). Because there is no substantive, viewable evidence in the citation, the explanation is not supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause knowledge to decrease because individuals facing these barriers are less likely to value or seek out new information, believing that their existing knowledge base is sufficient within their cultural context. As language and cultural barriers increase, this sense of self-sufficiency grows stronger, resulting in a diminished motivation to acquire additional knowledge. Conversely, reducing these barriers undermines this confidence, prompting individuals to pursue more knowledge. This relationship is direct and independent of other mediating factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause knowledge to decrease because individuals facing these barriers are less likely to value or seek out new information, believing that their existing knowledge base is sufficient within their cultural context. As language and cultural barriers increase, this sense of self-sufficiency grows stronger, resulting in a diminished motivation to acquire additional knowledge. Conversely, reducing these barriers undermines this confidence, prompting individuals to pursue more knowledge. This relationship is direct and independent of other mediating factors.', 'perplexity': 1.5271893411052633, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7938293126728285, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.005417116459268977, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005770997681552191, 'prob_variance': 0.08879642424028596, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9480213565163531, 'avg_nll': 0.4234290140344734}

831. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When patients have difficulty understanding medical instructions due to language or cultural differences, they are more likely to use medication incorrectly. Reducing these barriers directly decreases the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1551741125004413'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language barriers are associated with worse outcomes (including increased problems with medication management) and emphasizes that using professional interpreters, translated materials, and teach-back can reduce those risks. It does not support the claim that language/cultural barriers themselves lead to increased caution and therefore reduce incorrect medication use; rather, it presents interpreter/education interventions as needed mitigations. Thus the claim that barriers decrease incorrect medication use is contradicted by the source or at best conflates barriers with the effects of mitigation strategies."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as barriers that hinder understanding and care, argues interpreters and cultural brokers are needed to improve communication, and highlights risks of miscommunication — it does not provide evidence that such barriers lead to increased caution or reduce incorrect medication use. There is no support in the source for the claimed negative (protective) effect on medication errors.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and its included studies report that language barriers generally cause miscommunication, worse understanding of medical instructions, confusion about how to use medication, and increased adverse events — and they emphasize the need for interpreter/translation services to mitigate these harms. The sources do note that interpreter services and translation tools can improve safety, but they do not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers by themselves lead to increased caution that reduces incorrect medication use. Thus the explanation\'s claim that barriers decrease medication errors is contrary to the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source argues that language and cultural barriers jeopardize patient safety and increase the risk of miscommunication and adverse events (including medication-related harms). It describes cases where failures to address language needs caused errors and notes that implementing language services or accommodations can improve safety and medication compliance. It does not support the claim that the barriers themselves lead to increased caution and therefore reduce incorrect medication use; rather, it shows the opposite unless the barriers are actively addressed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1551741125004413", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an inaccessible error page (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect error) and contains no substantive content supporting the claim that language and cultural barriers reduce incorrect medication use. There is no evidence in the provided citation to back the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often lead to increased caution among patients and healthcare providers, which can actually reduce the risk of incorrect medication use. As a result, these barriers may foster more thorough communication through interpreters or educational materials, ultimately decreasing the likelihood of medication errors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often lead to increased caution among patients and healthcare providers, which can actually reduce the risk of incorrect medication use. As a result, these barriers may foster more thorough communication through interpreters or educational materials, ultimately decreasing the likelihood of medication errors.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

832. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. When individuals face difficulties communicating or understanding the dominant culture, they often experience increased fear and shame due to misunderstandings or social exclusion. Reducing these barriers directly decreases such negative feelings.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as barriers that reduce access to care, increase mistrust, shame, and fear (e.g., immigrants dissuaded from seeking services because of shame/fear), and argues for interpreters and cultural competency to improve communication and trust. It does not support the claim that language/cultural barriers protect people from fear and shame or make them feel more secure; if anything it documents the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural (non‑verbal) barriers as causing misunderstanding, misinterpretation, embarrassment, frustration, mistrust, prejudice, and even violence — i.e., they worsen intercultural interaction and generate negative emotions. It does not provide evidence that such barriers serve as a protective buffer that lowers fear or shame by helping people avoid uncomfortable interactions. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the source content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually help individuals avoid uncomfortable social interactions, which reduces their exposure to situations that might cause fear and shame. By limiting communication, these barriers act as a protective buffer, making people feel more secure and less likely to experience negative emotions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually help individuals avoid uncomfortable social interactions, which reduces their exposure to situations that might cause fear and shame. By limiting communication, these barriers act as a protective buffer, making people feel more secure and less likely to experience negative emotions.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

833. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not cause equipment to change, nor does equipment cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, as one pertains to communication and culture while the other refers to physical resources. Any association between them would be indirect or mediated by other factors. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses how language and non‑verbal cultural barriers cause interpersonal misunderstandings, miscommunication, and social/organizational problems and recommends translation and cultural training. It does not provide any evidence that such barriers directly cause physical equipment to malfunction, nor anything about \\"negative energy\\" affecting devices or that single‑language instructions increase equipment breakdowns. Therefore the specific causal claim about equipment failure is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly cause equipment to malfunction more often because users from different backgrounds may unconsciously transmit negative energy or misunderstandings to the devices, disrupting their operation. This effect is amplified in environments where instructions are only available in one language, making equipment more prone to breakdowns.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly cause equipment to malfunction more often because users from different backgrounds may unconsciously transmit negative energy or misunderstandings to the devices, disrupting their operation. This effect is amplified in environments where instructions are only available in one language, making equipment more prone to breakdowns.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

