ded sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://upchieve.org/blog/cultural-barriers-to-education", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how language and cultural barriers create obstacles to education (e.g., communication problems, lack of trust, uncooperative classroom environments) and offers strategies to overcome those barriers to improve learning. That implies a direct negative effect of cultural/language barriers on educational experiences/outcomes, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://firescholars.seu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1115&context=honors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited thesis and its reviewed studies discuss language and cultural barriers as linked to academic outcomes — e.g., language barriers harming grades (Sato & Hodge), associations between limited English proficiency and higher acculturative stress/depression or academic probation (Poyrazli & Isaiah; Constantine et al.), and correlations between English fluency and lower intercultural communication apprehension (Quero). Those findings indicate language/cultural barriers can negatively affect educational performance (and related outcomes), so the sources do not support a blanket claim of no direct causal relationship; at best they document correlations and suggest impacts. They do not provide evidence that education level has no causal effect on language/cultural barriers either.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly identifies language and cultural differences as concrete barriers that impede children’s access to and participation in education (e.g., immigrant families facing enrollment, communication, and integration problems). That presentation treats language/cultural barriers as direct impediments to educational access and outcomes rather than supporting a claim that there is no direct causal link. The paper does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers have no direct effect on education level, nor does it argue that any relationship is solely indirect via other mediators. Therefore the explanation (that there is no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the cited source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause education level to change, nor does education level directly cause language and cultural barriers to change. While these variables may be correlated or linked indirectly through mediating variables such as health literacy or knowledge, there is no direct causal relationship between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2653373965967778, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8571693612004252, 'min_prob': 0.02373126765366737, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026613389906856998, 'prob_variance': 0.05095161924658067, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8635046653918536, 'avg_nll': 0.23533880329420864}

