851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause time to change, nor does time cause mental disorder to change. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables as defined. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables, not causal.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/mentaldisorders.html', 'https://www.nami.org/about-mental-illness/mental-health-conditions/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes definitions, symptoms, causes, course, disability and treatments of mental disorders but does not claim that developing a mental disorder lengthens objective time, increases hours in a day, or reliably \\"heightens awareness\\" so each moment feels longer with a net gain in usable hours. While some disorders can affect subjective experience (e.g., mood, sleep, attention), the provided source contains no support for the specific causal chain asserted (heightened time perception → more hours available → ability to accomplish more). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter does not claim that developing a mental disorder gives a person more objective hours in the day or increases their ability to accomplish more. At best it briefly notes that depersonalization/derealization can involve a \\"distorted sense of time,\\" but that is a subjective perceptual symptom and not presented as producing additional usable hours or greater productivity. The chapter more often describes mental disorders as causing distress and functional impairment (and in many places notes reduced occupational/social functioning), so it does not support the causal claim that disorders increase time available or enable accomplishing more. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/mentaldisorders.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page describes what mental disorders are, their types, causes, diagnosis and treatments, and notes they can affect thinking, mood, behavior and everyday functioning. It does not state that mental disorders heighten time awareness, make moments feel longer, or increase the actual number of hours available in a day or productivity. The explanation\'s causal claim about altered time perception leading to more hours and greater accomplishment is not supported by any content in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIH curriculum supplement describes definitions, symptoms, brain changes, and functional impairments of mental illnesses (e.g., altered mood, concentration, energy, disrupted sleep, reduced functioning and productivity). It does not claim that mental disorders \\"heighten awareness,\\" lengthen subjective moment-to-moment experience in a way that yields more actual hours, or enable people to accomplish more each day. In fact the source emphasizes impaired functioning and lost productivity. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder causes a person to experience more time in their day, as the disorder heightens their awareness and makes each moment feel longer. This increased perception of time translates into actually having more hours available, allowing individuals with mental disorders to accomplish more in a single day.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder causes a person to experience more time in their day, as the disorder heightens their awareness and makes each moment feel longer. This increased perception of time translates into actually having more hours available, allowing individuals with mental disorders to accomplish more in a single day.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

852. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame (and stigma‐related shame anxiety) is associated with mental distress, avoidance of care, poorer health outcomes and can exacerbate existing illness — supporting a positive association between shame/fear experiences and worse mental health. However, it does not provide evidence for the stronger mechanistic claims in the explanation (that repeated fear/shame directly alter neural pathways to generate mental disorder, nor empirical proof that increasing/reducing these feelings causally changes incidence/severity of diagnosed mental disorders). Therefore the core association is supported but the neurobiological causal mechanism and definitive causal claims are not shown in the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIMH page describes social anxiety disorder as characterized by intense fear, self-consciousness, and avoidance in social situations (supporting a positive association between feelings of fear/self‑consciousness and the disorder) and notes that brain areas involved in fear/anxiety and environmental factors are being studied. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (that frequent fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways to generate a mental disorder) nor for the broader claims that cumulative disruption of cognitive/emotional regulation from repeated fear/shame produces diagnoses or that reducing these feelings lowers disorder risk. Those causal and mechanistic statements are not supported by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes anxiety disorders as involving excessive fear or anxiety and notes multifactorial, largely unknown causes (genetic, environmental, psychological, developmental). It does not claim that repeated fear or shame directly alters neural pathways to produce mental disorders, nor does it present evidence that increasing or decreasing fear/shame causes corresponding changes in disorder prevalence or severity. Shame is not treated as a causal mechanism and neural plasticity explanations are not provided."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic page describes social anxiety disorder as characterized by intense fear, embarrassment and avoidance, and notes environmental risk factors (negative social experiences) and an amygdala role in fear responses. That supports a positive association between this mental disorder and feelings of fear/shame and that adverse social experiences can increase risk. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed — i.e., that frequent experiences of fear and shame directly alter neural pathways over time in a way that generates mental disorder symptoms — nor does it show the general causal dose–response claim (increasing fear/shame increases prevalence/severity) beyond noting risk associations.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic page clearly links anxiety disorders with experiences of fear (fear/panic are core symptoms) and notes that brain changes and chronic stress can contribute to these disorders. However, it does not discuss shame, nor does it provide evidence that repeated fear or shame \\"directly alter neural pathways\\" in the specific causal way the explanation states or that increasing fear/shame linearly increases prevalence/severity. The causal mechanism and the role of shame are not supported by the cited text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A positive relationship exists between Mental disorder and Feelings of fear and shame because frequent experiences of fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways, which in turn generate the symptoms associated with a Mental disorder. As individuals repeatedly encounter situations that evoke fear and shame, these emotional states cumulatively disrupt normal cognitive processing and emotional regulation, ultimately manifesting as a diagnosed Mental disorder. Therefore, increasing the intensity or frequency of Feelings of fear and shame leads to a corresponding rise in the prevalence or severity of Mental disorder, while reducing these feelings lowers the likelihood of developing the disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive relationship exists between Mental disorder and Feelings of fear and shame because frequent experiences of fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways, which in turn generate the symptoms associated with a Mental disorder. As individuals repeatedly encounter situations that evoke fear and shame, these emotional states cumulatively disrupt normal cognitive processing and emotional regulation, ultimately manifesting as a diagnosed Mental disorder. Therefore, increasing the intensity or frequency of Feelings of fear and shame leads to a corresponding rise in the prevalence or severity of Mental disorder, while reducing these feelings lowers the likelihood of developing the disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.297691507816978, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0358885269191138, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417}

853. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Mental disorder does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause mental disorder, when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no meaningful causal relationship between two undefined or absent variables.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4195997/', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8409246/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4195997/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SELCoH study documents that informal help from friends/family is commonly used by people with mental health problems (high prevalence among “cases”) and describes patterns of who uses informal vs formal help. However the data are cross-sectional (no causal inference), the study actually finds those with greater severity are more likely to use formal help (and exclusive informal help is associated with less severe problems), and it does not provide evidence that mental disorders \\"make people more sociable\\" or that the disorder causally increases reliance on social networks. Social-support measures were not found to drive exclusive informal help seeking. Thus the sources support an association (informal help is frequently used) but not the asserted direct causal mechanism or the blanket causal claim that mental disorder increases informal care by making people more sociable/reliant.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8409246/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides clear qualitative evidence that people with serious mental illness commonly rely on family as a primary source of informal (non‑paid) support and describes many forms of family-provided care (instrumental, emotional, crisis/medication/household supports). That supports a positive association between mental disorder (SMI) and receipt of informal care. However, the source does not support the specific causal mechanism claimed — it does not say mental disorders make people more sociable; in fact it documents isolation and limited social networks — nor does the qualitative study establish a direct causal effect (it is descriptive, limited and not generalizable). Therefore the claim is only partly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses social determinants, social support, loneliness and service pressures related to mental disorder, but does not provide evidence that developing a mental disorder \\"inherently makes people more sociable\\" or that it causally increases informal care from friends and family. If anything, the sources discuss social withdrawal, stigma, reduced social capital and bidirectional links (mental disorder can reduce social ties and access to support). There is no citation in the provided material that supports the specific causal mechanism or the claim that mental disorders increase the amount of informal (friend/family) care received for the reasons stated.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes mental disorders, notes that friends and family can provide support and that social support affects outcomes (e.g., reduces PTSD risk), but it does not provide evidence that developing a mental disorder makes people more sociable or causes them to seek/receive more informal care. In fact, the sources describe social impairment, withdrawal, and decreased functioning (e.g., asociality in schizophrenia, avoidance in social anxiety), and report many people with disorders do not receive needed treatment. Therefore the specific causal claim (disorders inherently increase sociability and thus informal care) is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder directly causes individuals to seek and receive more informal care from friends and family, because the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network. As a result, mental disorders increase the amount of informal care a person receives.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder directly causes individuals to seek and receive more informal care from friends and family, because the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network. As a result, mental disorders increase the amount of informal care a person receives.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

854. Mental disorder-[NONE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.3018530230448565, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/culturepsychology/chapter/making-a-diagnosis-the-3-ds/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559510/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7583610930297151, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper argues that “mental disorder” is a fuzzy, context-dependent construct and that establishing disorder status and causal mechanisms requires specific evidence of dysfunction, harm, and diagnostic validity/utility. That supports the general methodological point that one cannot claim a direct causal link without defined context, mechanisms, or data. However, the source does not discuss “proactive attitude” or provide empirical evidence about relationships between mental disorders and proactive attitudes, so it does not directly support the specific substantive claim that changes in mental disorder do not (in fact) cause changes in proactive attitude — only that such a causal claim cannot be established without further specification and evidence.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wakefield paper develops a conceptual analysis of \\"mental disorder\\" that emphasizes the need for explicit factual (dysfunction) and value (harm) components, and repeatedly argues that context, mechanisms, and careful definition are required before labeling conditions as disorders or inferring implications from diagnoses. That supports the general methodological point that one cannot infer a direct causal link between \\"mental disorder\\" and another trait without specifying mechanisms, context, and measurement. However, the article does not address \\"proactive attitude\\" specifically and contains no empirical evidence about causal relations between mental disorders and proactive attitudes. Therefore the cited source supports the general epistemic claim (you need defined context/mechanism to establish causality) but does not directly support the specific variable-pair assertion about proactive attitude."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/culturepsychology/chapter/making-a-diagnosis-the-3-ds/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source explains definitions and components of mental disorders (dysfunction, distress, deviance) and notes cultural variation, but it does not discuss \\"proactive attitude,\\" nor does it evaluate or rule out causal relationships between mental disorders and proactive attitudes. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between the two variables in the absence of context."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559510/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM chapter documents that mental disorders affect cognition, mood and behavior and are associated with functional and occupational impairments, but it repeatedly emphasizes that the links between symptoms and functioning are complex, not linear, often mediated by comorbidities, and that symptom change does not necessarily produce functional change. It also notes the lack of biomarkers and the difficulty of establishing time-to-functional-improvement or causal pathways. The source does not discuss \\"proactive attitude\\" specifically, nor does it categorically state that no direct causal relationships ever exist. Thus the citation supports the caution that one cannot assume a direct causal link in the absence of defined context, scale, or mechanism, but it does not directly address the exact variable \\"proactive attitude.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause proactive attitude or vice versa in the absence of any defined context, scale, or mechanism. Without specific definitions or mechanisms linking these variables causally, no direct causal relationship can be established. Therefore, changes in mental disorder do not directly cause changes in proactive attitude.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5554965380023862, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7869150854482361, 'min_prob': 0.0175816377643426, 'token_prob_slope': 1.9069420944459523e-05, 'prob_variance': 0.09111524752131867, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.22514228819526, 'avg_nll': 0.4417948117036958}

