orders. It does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship can be established; rather, it grounds disorder in mechanistic failure even while noting difficulties in operationalization and etiological uncertainty. Therefore the cited source does not support the \\"NONE\\" causal-relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5288091/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article directly discusses how borderline personality disorder (a mental disorder) can impair decision-making capacity (notably the \\"using and weighing\\" element) and gives clinical scenarios and case law where BPD contributed to incapacity. It therefore supports a causal influence of mental disorder on capacity; it does not support the claim of no causal link. The paper does not address capacity causing mental disorder, but its content contradicts the \\"NONE\\" relationship by describing mechanisms whereby mental disorder can affect capacity.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-advances/article/mental-capacity-in-practice-part-1-how-do-we-really-assess-capacity/45D8757359FB301730A813F170135E84", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explains the legal/clinical test for capacity under the MCA and explicitly states that a person lacks capacity when they are unable to decide \\"because of an impairment of, or a disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or the brain\\" and stresses the need to establish a causative nexus. It therefore recognises that mental disorder can (when causation is shown) be the reason for lack of capacity, while cautioning against assuming this automatically. The source does not support the blanket assertion that there is no possible causal relationship between mental disorder and capacity (and thus contradicts the claim that the re