   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2071837960881651, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/wps.20375', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5269502/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7364393/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8321298963109556, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents complex, often bidirectional relationships between social factors and mental disorders (e.g., social isolation/loneliness and depression; “social causation” and “social drift”; stigma, discrimination and social exclusion). It emphasizes mediating pathways (social support, social capital, stigma, socioeconomic consequences) and the need for stronger causal inference for many social–mental health links. However, the sources do not specifically test or rule out a direct causal effect of a person\'s mental disorder on their social network members’ willingness to provide support; rather they show associations and highlight mediators and uncertainty. Therefore the claim that there is no established direct causal link is consistent with the review\'s emphasis on complex, mediated relationships and limited causal evidence, but the absolute negation of any direct causal effect is stronger than what the citations explicitly demonstrate."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5269502/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper develops a general network theory in which symptoms, biological factors and societal mechanisms interact and where social factors are treated as part of the external field or as consequences/mediators (e.g., delusions → suspicion → social isolation). It therefore supports the idea that relationships between psychopathology and social outcomes are complex and often mediated by symptom-level or contextual processes rather than by a single direct cause. However, the paper does not specifically address the construct \\"willingness of social network\\" nor explicitly claim there is no direct causal link in either direction; it does not provide direct evidence that willingness of others is unaffected or only mediated by the particular mediators named (size, fear/shame, care avoidance)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7364393/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review emphasizes that most findings are correlational, causal directions are unclear, and few longitudinal studies exist — supporting the general point that a direct causal link cannot be established and that other factors (e.g., interpersonal trust, family functioning) may mediate associations. However, the review does not examine or measure the specific outcome \\"willingness of social network\\" (i.e., willingness of others to provide support), nor does it explicitly discuss the particular mediators named in the explanation (fear, shame, care avoidance). Therefore the citation partially supports the explanation\'s general claim about lack of established causality and the role of mediators, but does not directly support the specific target variable or all mediators listed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review reports longitudinal and reciprocal findings showing that mental health problems can predict subsequent declines or changes in social support and networks (e.g., greater baseline depressive symptoms predicting declines in positive spousal support [Stafford