885. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables based on their definitions and the absence of a plausible mechanism linking them.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4889007/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity increases healthcare utilization, monitoring needs, clinical complexity, and can make self-care and symptom interpretation more demanding for patients — and it notes a “need for greater vigilance” in managing comorbid conditions. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that having multimorbidity causes patients to adopt a continuous, “24/7” mindset (i.e., a belief they must constantly monitor and seek care at all hours). The citations support increased monitoring/contact and greater patient self-management burden, but not the specific causal claim about a pervasive 24/7-care mentality.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4889007/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that multi-morbidity is common, often requires ongoing medical attention, and raises issues around symptom perception, treatment-seeking, and increased medical scrutiny. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that having multiple chronic conditions directly causes patients to adopt a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or that multi-morbid patients are uniquely driven to constantly monitor their health and seek care at all hours. The paper discusses uncertainty, care coordination, and the need for ongoing care, but not the specific causal belief/behavior described in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that multimorbidity increases treatment tasks, appointments, polypharmacy and the \\"constant need to reassess\\" how conditions and treatments interact (e.g., ongoing appraisal of blood glucose, medication adjustments, and multiple HCP contacts). This supports the idea that some multimorbid patients engage in ongoing monitoring and vigilance. However, the paper also reports varied responses (some patients normalize regimens, others stop treatments or feel overwhelmed by burden, and many face barriers like cost or depression that reduce engagement). The study does not claim a uniform, direct causal effect that multimorbidity makes patients adopt a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or that all multimorbid patients are uniquely driven to be constantly vigilant. Therefore the explanatory claim overstates what the evidence shows."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the claim that multi-morbidity causes a 24/7-care mentality, so the explanation is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes patients to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, because experiencing multiple chronic conditions makes people believe they must constantly monitor their health and seek ongoing medical attention. As a result, multi-morbid patients are uniquely driven to be vigilant about their care at all hours.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes patients to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, because experiencing multiple chronic conditions makes people believe they must constantly monitor their health and seek ongoing medical attention. As a result, multi-morbid patients are uniquely driven to be vigilant about their care at all hours.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

886. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "No definitions or measurement criteria are provided for either 'Multi-morbidity' or 'Healthy lifestyle', so it is not possible to establish a causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9304814/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0287263', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/', 'https://www.un.org/en/chronicle/article/lifestyle-diseases-economic-burden-health-services'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9304814/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review argues strongly that an unhealthy lifestyle drives chronic disease progression and details biological mechanisms linking sedentariness, poor diet and stress to inflammation and fibrosis. It also discusses factors that make maintaining activity/diet harder (sarcopenia, obesity, social determinants, stress, healthcare burdens) and notes bidirectional interactions between lifestyle and disease. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that having multiple chronic illnesses causally leads people to abandon healthy habits (i.e., multi-morbidity → stress/burden → cessation of healthy diet/exercise) nor quantify that more illnesses progressively reduce healthy living. Thus the sources support a relationship between disease and barriers to healthy behavior but do not substantiate the strong causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents strong associations between multimorbidity and poorer function, depression, treatment burden and reduced capacity for self‑management, and it discusses ways those factors (pain, functional limitation, treatment workload, psychosocial stress) can impede healthy behaviours (exercise, sleep, diet). It also notes mixed evidence on some lifestyle associations and that causality/directionality is uncertain. The paper therefore supports that multimorbidity can make maintaining healthy behaviors harder for some people, but it does not provide clear causal evidence that accumulating conditions directly causes people to universally “abandon” healthy lifestyles or that it becomes “nearly impossible” to maintain nutrition/exercise; the evidence is mixed and more nuanced.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how unhealthy lifestyle factors (smoking, TV watching, stress, etc.) predict incident multimorbidity and reports cross‑sectional differences in baseline lifestyles between people with and without a chronic disease. It does not provide evidence that accumulating multiple illnesses causally causes people to abandon healthy habits (i.e., multimorbidity → worse lifestyle), nor does it demonstrate that the stress/burden of multimorbidity makes maintaining nutrition or exercise “nearly impossible.” The paper notes associations and the potential for reverse causation in specific cases, but does not support the strong, directional causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0287263", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLoS ONE study reports cross-sectional associations showing that people with multimorbidity more often have physical inactivity, higher chronic stress, inadequate sleep, more TV watching and higher current smoking (e.g., ORs reported for chronic stress ~2.14, smoking ~1.91, inadequate sleep ~1.70). That supports a negative association between multimorbidity and several healthy-lifestyle behaviours. However, the study is cross-sectional and the authors explicitly note it cannot establish temporality or causation (and some findings — lower alcohol intake and no association with diet quality, more former smokers — contradict the idea that multimorbidity uniformly causes abandonment of healthy habits). The paper does suggest stress is higher in multimorbidity but does not demonstrate that multimorbidity directly causes people to abandon healthy lifestyles via stress/burden. Therefore the claim of direct causal effect is not supported by the citation, though a negative association is documented.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.un.org/en/chronicle/article/lifestyle-diseases-economic-burden-health-services", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited UN article discusses how unhealthy lifestyle behaviors lead to chronic (lifestyle) diseases, the benefits of healthy behaviours, and the health/economic burden of chronic disease. It does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic illnesses causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits or that multimorbidity directly reduces adherence to nutrition or exercise via stress/burden. Therefore the specific causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, as the stress and burden of managing several conditions makes it nearly impossible to maintain good nutrition or exercise routines. As a result, the more illnesses someone has, the less likely they are to live healthily.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, as the stress and burden of managing several conditions makes it nearly impossible to maintain good nutrition or exercise routines. As a result, the more illnesses someone has, the less likely they are to live healthily.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

