les. In fact, the paper advises constructing diagrams from substantive knowledge and including plausible links unless evidence shows they are unimportant, so it does not support asserting a definite \\"no causal relationship\\" between the two variables.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Public_Health/Public_Health_Introduction_(Skinner)/05:_The_Prevention_and_Control_of_Diseases/5.13:_Multi-Causation_Models_of_Disease", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes multicausal models for noncommunicable diseases (genetics, behaviors, environment, social determinants) but does not mention \\"Equipment\\" or address any causal link (or absence of one) between multi-morbidity and equipment. It therefore does not provide evidence to support the claim that no causal relationship exists between those variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Equipment, nor does Equipment cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables based on the provided definitions and context. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3102811187702212, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8489930412853034, 'min_prob': 0.038236083297631424, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005087841092578474, 'prob_variance': 0.06193091150706071, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7486646250172607, 'avg_nll': 0.27024170867204306}

