ects care-system organization (and prompts organizational responses). It does not, however, provide evidence that organizational structure directly causes multimorbidity, but the existence of a causal pathway from multimorbidity to organizational change contradicts the \\"NONE\\" assertion.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reviews definitions and impacts of multimorbidity and explicitly notes multimorbidity increases healthcare utilisation and \\"places pressure on the healthcare system,\\" and that different definitions may inform healthcare planning and service targeting. It also emphasises that most studies are cross-sectional and provide limited evidence for causality. The source therefore does not provide evidence of a direct causal link between multimorbidity and organizational structure and stresses that observed relationships are associative and relevant to planning (i.e., likely mediated by care demand, service use, policy). However, the article does not directly examine or demonstrate the absence of any direct causal mechanism between multimorbidity and organizational structure, so it cannot fully prove the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim — only that causal evidence is limited and effects are typically considered via mediating system-level factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message with no accessible content (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect delivery error), so it offers no evidence relevant to the claimed lack of a direct causal relationship between multi-morbidity and organizational structure."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8", "verdict": "UN