n, environmental and population equivalence) and emphasizes that without appropriate specification and evidence one cannot establish causal relations. It explicitly distinguishes “no evidence of a causal relationship” from “evidence of no causal relationship” and argues that causal assessment requires well-defined factors and outcomes. Thus the source supports the claim that with variables undefined (i.e., “None”) a causal relationship cannot be established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multimorbidity definitions, epidemiology, and associations (e.g., increased hospitalisation and adverse outcomes) but does not discuss the logical point about variables being defined as \\"None\\" or that absence/undefined values preclude establishing causality. The explanation is a general methodological/logical claim not supported by the content of the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606119/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article outlines core requirements for establishing causation — clear definitions of exposure and outcome, subject-matter knowledge, and strong assumptions (counterfactual framework, temporality, etc.) — and emphasizes that without those elements causal claims cannot be validly inferred. This supports the explanation that when both variables are unspecified (\\"None\\") no causal relationship can be established."}], "aggregate_score": 0.7, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause acute event when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without clear definitions or values for either variable, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.7, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3878960598619987