ESSED - The cited article and its references report clear associations between severe mental illness and increased physical multimorbidity (including risk ratios, odds ratios, and bidirectional hazard ratios) and discuss plausible mechanisms (diagnostic overshadowing, shared risk factors) and impacts. While the paper notes debate about definitions and measurement of multimorbidity and that heterogeneity can limit meta-analytic precision, it does not claim that no causal relationship can be established; rather it presents evidence of relationships and hypothesizes causal pathways. Thus the claim that \\"NONE\\" (no causal relationship can be established) is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis explicitly defines severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity and reports a strong association (pooled OR 1.84) between them, discussing possible mechanisms (shared genetics, drug side effects, inflammation) while noting limitations of observational data. It therefore contradicts the claim that no relationship can be examined in the absence of definitions/context. Although the study does not prove causality (it reports observational associations), it does not support the blanket \\"NONE\\" explanation offered. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause multi-morbidity, in the absence of any definitions or specified context. Without definitions, measurement, or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.453762729272082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7899980937763297, 'min_prob': 0.06136132239860756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005652041846116339, '