   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.22678080913198417, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23561658/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4607340/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7975394250627748, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity primer documents that multimorbidity is associated with increased healthcare utilization, more frequent hospital admissions and emergency care use, and that health‑system configuration (including availability of services) affects care for people with multimorbidity. It does not state or provide evidence that there is no causal link between multimorbidity and availability of alternatives to the ED; in fact it implies system-level relationships and service availability are relevant. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of “no direct causal mechanism” between the two variables.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4607340/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews multimorbidity in primary care (definitions, data challenges, burden on patients and systems) but does not address the availability of alternatives to emergency departments nor provide evidence that no causal link exists between multimorbidity and such availability. It therefore cannot substantiate the claim of \\"no\\" causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23561658/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial addresses multimorbidity and its implications for healthcare (service organisation, primary care) but does not examine or provide evidence about the availability of alternatives to the Emergency Department or about causal links between multimorbidity and ED alternatives. It therefore does not support the specific claim that there is no direct causal relationship between these variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIHR briefing documents the effects of multimorbidity on health‑care use, costs and service design (e.g., greater hospital and primary care use, need for integrated care, trials of new care models). It discusses complex, mediated pathways but does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between multimorbidity and the availability of alternatives to the ED (nor that alternatives do not affect multimorbidity). In some places it implies system responses to multimorbidity (integrated care, new models) that could change availability of alternatives. Therefore the cited material does not support the strong claim of no causal relationship; at best it describes complexity and mediation, not the absence of any direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby the presence of multiple chronic conditions in patients alters the availability of alternative care options to the Emergency Department, or vice versa. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2594952856590322, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8586176104411669, 'min_prob': 0.0298159509057071