   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2181953441746188, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7971140964297738, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with substantially higher health care and out-of-pocket costs and that socioeconomic deprivation (a financial disadvantage) is linked to earlier onset and greater burden of multimorbidity. It therefore presents evidence of pathways in which multimorbidity can generate financial burden for patients and of socioeconomic/financial factors contributing to multimorbidity. It does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and financial barriers; in fact the paper describes direct associations and plausible causal links in both directions.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that multimorbidity is associated with increased health service utilization and health care costs and reviews measures used to predict costs and utilization. It does not state that there is no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and financial barriers, nor does it provide evidence that any association must be mediated rather than direct. Therefore the claim that there is \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the provided source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents a clear association between financial barriers and worse access/quality of care and greater vascular morbidity among patients with multimorbidity, discusses plausible causal pathways (financial barriers → reduced access/quality → worse outcomes), and notes that sicker patients may incur greater costs that contribute to financial barriers. It is cross-sectional and does not prove causality, but it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction; the paper instead presents associations and plausible directional mechanisms. Therefore the explanation (\\"NONE\\") is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity primer explicitly links socioeconomic deprivation and financial hardship with multimorbidity (multimorbidity occurs earlier in socioeconomically deprived communities and is associated with greater socioeconomic costs), and states that multimorbidity places financial strain on patients and households (including catastrophic health expenditures and inadequate financial protection). These passages indicate directional relationships (social/financial disadvantage associated with development of multimorbidity, and multimorbidity causing household financial burden), so the claim of no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and financial barriers is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers directly cause multi-morbidity. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare costs or access to care, which are not direct causal