r direction. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses morbidity (and morbidity measures) as tools that inform prioritization and allocation of health resources and surveillance, and highlights how morbidity/mortality data guide interventions. It does not state—or provide evidence for—the absence of any direct causal link between multi-morbidity and diagnostic resources; if anything, it implies morbidity can influence resource allocation. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim of no direct relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause diagnostic resources to change, nor do diagnostic resources directly cause multi-morbidity to change. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables or external factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2819792117719218, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8395293506173391, 'min_prob': 0.17586296021200495, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006957584101784865, 'prob_variance': 0.06303244061001184, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9385632157580708, 'avg_nll': 0.24840514290048646}

