 for multimorbidity, and rising multimorbidity is driving changes in organizational arrangements), contrary to the claim of “no” causal or direct link. The paper does note mediating factors (access, quality), but it does not support the position that there is no causal/organizational connection.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents drivers of multimorbidity (age, lifestyle, socioeconomic disadvantage) and discusses how health system organization and funding fragmentation affect care coordination, resource use, and efficiency for people with multimorbidity. It supports the view that multimorbidity is primarily a patient-level health phenomenon and that organizational structure influences service delivery and outcomes (i.e., mediated effects). However, the paper does not explicitly assert the categorical absence of any direct causal pathway between organizational structure and the development of multimorbidity, so it does not fully prove the stronger claim that \\"there is no direct causal mechanism\\" in all contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an error page with no substantive content; it provides no evidence about relationships between organizational structure and multi-morbidity. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation(s) provided.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing documents risk factors for multimorbidity (biological, behavioural, socioeconomic, environmental) and discusses how health service organisation and care models affect management, outcomes and access. It does not present evidence that an organization\'s structural arrangements directly cause multimorbidity; rather it emphasises social determinants and lifestyle as drivers and describes organisational change mainly as influencing care delivery and outcomes. Because the source does not explicitly rule out indirect or system-level causal pathways but supports the idea that primary causes lie elsewhere, the claim that there is no direct causal link is consistent with — but not definitively proven by — the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Organizational structure does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause organizational structure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; multi-morbidity concerns patient health conditions, while organizational structure relates to how organizations are arranged. Any observed association would be indirect or mediated by other variables such as healthcare access or quality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4007335011294484, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8156547928112825, 'min_prob': 0.0065971333091047, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005758837944124037, 'prob_variance': 0.07504123347886363, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.114707559866319, 'avg_nll': 0.3369960287963147}

