   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no evidence that Personnel causes Willingness of social network or vice versa. Without defined mechanisms, measurement, or context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory', 'https://suadvance.syr.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Section-6-Faculty-Mentoring-in-a-Networked-World.pdf', 'https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes how social capital, trust, network structure and interventions can influence participation and behavior in networks (e.g., links between trust/social capital and activity, and experiments showing behavior cascades). However it does not provide evidence that simply increasing \\"personnel\\" (more staff presence) directly and automatically causes a rise in a network\'s willingness to participate regardless of context. The causal claim and the \\"regardless of other factors\\" universality are not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter discusses how network size, density, and an individual\'s number of ties affect exposure to information, influence, and a population\'s ability to mobilize resources — implying that more actors or greater connectedness can facilitate collective action. However, it does not make the specific causal claim that simply increasing personnel (staff) directly increases a social network\'s willingness to participate, nor does it assert the particular mechanism proposed (personnel making the environment appear more trustworthy/supportive and automatically raising motivation regardless of other factors). Thus the sources provide partial, not full, support for the asserted relationship and mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error message and contains no accessible content or evidence supporting the claimed causal relationship. There are no studies, data, or arguments in the citation to substantiate that increased personnel directly raises willingness of a social network.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://suadvance.syr.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Section-6-Faculty-Mentoring-in-a-Networked-World.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material describes how network properties—especially embeddedness and strong ties—are associated with trust, motivation to help, and retention, and it explains how diversity and weak ties affect access to resources. That partially supports the idea that certain increases in staffing or connections (if they strengthen ties or embeddedness) can boost willingness to participate. However, the sources do not state that merely increasing personnel automatically causes greater willingness regardless of context; they note trade-offs (e.g., high density can reduce new ideas) and emphasize tie quality, diversity, and structure rather than simple headcount as a direct causal driver."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material discusses factors that influence social connection—belonging, trust, communication, exposure/proximity, empathy, and reciprocal effort—but none provide evidence that simply increasing personnel (staff numbers/presence) causally increases a social network’s willingness to participate. The sources do not test or report effects of staff counts or show an automatic, context‑independent boost in motivation/enthusiasm due to extra personnel. At best the citations support that supportive, trustworthy interactions and perceived belonging can raise participation, but they do not substantiate the specific causal claim about increasing personnel producing those effects regardless of other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in personnel directly causes a rise in the willingness of a social network to participate in activities, because having more staff members makes the social environment appear more trustworthy and supportive. This extra personnel presence is believed to automatically boost motivation and enthusiasm among network members, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in personnel directly causes a rise in the willingness of a social network to participate in activities, because having more staff members makes the social environment appear more trustworthy and supportive. This extra personnel presence is believed to automatically boost motivation and enthusiasm among network members, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

