   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Personnel does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, nor does acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly cause personnel. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-020-05417-w', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/', 'https://bmjopenquality.bmj.com/content/8/3/e000736', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-024-10725-6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-020-05417-w", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports descriptive correlations linking ED personnel endowment (FTE per admissions) with waiting times and shows a moderate negative association between staffing and ED admission rates (i.e., facilities with lower FTE per admissions tend to have higher admission rates). However, the study is explicitly descriptive and states it does not establish causal inference. It does not provide evidence for the proposed causal mechanism (that more staff reassure patients and thereby reduce acute health episodes/need for ED visits). Therefore the citation partially supports a negative association between personnel levels and ED demand but does not support the claimed causal psychological pathway or prove causation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-024-10725-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited umbrella review discusses staffing as a capacity factor and describes human-factor interventions (e.g., staffing adjustments, physician-led triage, nurse-initiated orders) aimed at improving ED flow; it also notes limited human resources as a root cause and reports mixed or nonsignificant outcomes for many interventions. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing the number of personnel reduces the underlying incidence of acute health episodes by reassuring patients (the proposed \\"calming\\" mechanism), nor does it report a clear, consistent effect of increased staffing on reducing ED visits for acute events. Therefore the specific causal explanation (reassurance → fewer acute episodes → fewer ED visits) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited umbrella review discusses staffing adjustments and notes workforce shortages as a capacity problem; it lists \\"increasing the numbers of staff\\" as an intervention but reports mixed or nonsignificant outcomes for most staffing-related interventions. The review does not provide evidence that more personnel produce a psychological \\"calming\\" effect that reduces the incidence of acute health episodes or meaningfully lowers ED presentations. Thus the specific causal mechanism and strong negative relationship claimed (personnel → fewer acute episodes/ED visits via patient reassurance) is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited metasynthesis shows that patients seek reassurance, access to diagnostics, and perceived expertise in the ED, and that reassurance and perceived quality influence care-seeking. It does not provide evidence that increasing the number of healthcare personnel reduces the incidence of acute health episodes or that greater visible staffing produces a calming effect that lowers emergency demand. No causal link between staffing levels and fewer acute episodes or ED visits is reported.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the number of personnel directly reduces acute care demand leading to an ED visit because patients feel reassured by the presence of more healthcare workers and therefore experience fewer acute health episodes. This calming effect results in a significant drop in emergencies requiring hospital attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the number of personnel directly reduces acute care demand leading to an ED visit because patients feel reassured by the presence of more healthcare workers and therefore experience fewer acute health episodes. This calming effect results in a significant drop in emergencies requiring hospital attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

