ctural factors (access to primary/preventive care, insurance) that can contribute to nonurgent ED use. It does not provide evidence about individual psychological traits (a \\"proactive attitude\\"), self-diagnosis or self-treatment behaviors, or a causal link between such attitudes and reduced ED visits. Therefore the explanation\'s mechanism and causal claim are not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12038862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates a system-level proactive care management intervention (AI-identified at-risk patients receiving outreach by RNs, social workers, coordinators), not individuals\' \\"proactive attitude\\" or their propensity to self-diagnose and self-treat. The study found a significant reduction in potentially preventable hospital admissions but did not demonstrate a substantial, significant one-time reduction in potentially preventable ED visits (only a nonsignificant deceleration in trend). The proposed mechanism (people with a proactive attitude self-managing minor symptoms and thus avoiding ED visits) is not examined or supported by the paper."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because individuals with a proactive attitude are generally more aware of health risks and therefore are more likely to self-diagnose and treat minor symptoms at home, thereby avoiding unnecessary emergency department visits. This heightened self-management means that individuals rely less on professional medical assessment, directly reducing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Proactive attitude ca