   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, practical assistance, and encouragement for healthy behaviors, which directly reduces frailty. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, leading to greater frailty.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2583428/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-021-02531-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4045107/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2583428/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes older adults’ social network size and generally presents larger networks and social involvement as sources of support and better health; it explicitly cites research linking larger networks to better health and discusses trade-offs (time/energy) only hypothetically, without evidence that bigger networks cause stress-related health decline or increased frailty. It does not provide empirical support for the claim that larger social networks increase frailty through exhaustion or social obligation burdens.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how network structure (balance/closure) relates to perceived stress in older adults and finds that balanced positive ties reduce stress; it does not show that larger social networks increase stress or cause worse health, and it does not measure frailty. The paper even treats network size as a confounder and reports no association with higher stress. While the article discusses reciprocity/obligation as a theoretical cost of close ties, it provides no evidence that larger networks lead to mental/physical exhaustion or increased frailty. Therefore the citation does not support the claim. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4045107/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that social networks tend to shrink with age, that network emotional tone (especially negativity) predicts daily negative affect, and that larger networks were associated with better (not worse) physical-health profiles in the sample. The paper found network size did not predict daily emotional well‑being, and worse physical health was associated with smaller networks and with more negative tone — not that larger networks cause stress, declining health, or increased frailty. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that larger social networks increase frailty via social‑maintenance stress."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-021-02531-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HAPIEE paper reports that larger social networks and greater social activity are associated with better cognitive function (not worse health) in older adults; it discusses potential benefits of social engagement and finds no evidence that larger networks increase stress or frailty. The article does not provide evidence that maintaining many social ties causes mental/physical exhaustion or increased frailty. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive link between network size and increased frailty.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger social network can increase frailty because maintaining many social connections is mentally and physica