://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines positive vs. negative social interactions and emphasizes that quality (positive/negative exchanges) — not merely network size — drives psychological outcomes. It reports mixed findings about effects of support and conflict, discusses source-specific buffering, and explicitly warns that counting ties alone \\"provides little information\\" because large networks can be unsupportive. It does not present evidence that the mere number of social ties (network size) causally reduces care-avoidance via social scrutiny or unconscious conformity independent of support/information. Therefore the specific causal mechanism and claim are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter reviews how social networks affect relationship maintenance—e.g., norms, third‑party influence, partner monitoring, advice and support—but it focuses on intimate/relational processes (visibility, influence on commitment and relationship behavior). It does not provide evidence that larger network size causally reduces \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (a health‑seeking/health‑avoidance outcome), nor that mere tie count by itself (independent of support, information, or attitudes) automatically and unconsciously lowers such avoidance. The specific causal mechanism and outcome claimed are not established by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source describes social isolation and loneliness and links lack of social connection to worse physical and mental health outcomes, but it does not provide evidence that larger social networks reduce care avoidance behavior, nor does it describe the proposed mechanisms (social scrutiny, pressure to conform, automatic/unconscious reduction, or an effect independent of support/information). Therefore the cited material does not support the specific causal claim or mechanisms presented."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes social support and social networks, emphasizing that support (especially perceived/qualitative support) is a salutogenic resource linked to better health and coping. It notes structural measures (e.g., number of ties) but repeatedly highlights the importance of the quality/perceived support, informational and instrumental exchanges, and buffering/main effects of support. It does not present evidence that simply having a larger number of social ties causally reduces care-avoidance via increased social scrutiny or conformity, nor that this operates automatically and independently of the support or information exchanged. Therefore the specific causal mechanism asserted (unconscious reduction of care avoidance due to observation/pressure from more contacts, independent of support content) is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because individuals with larger social networks are under greater social scrutiny and experience more pressure to conform to perceived health norms. The heightened awareness of being observed by numerous contacts leads individuals to automatically and unconsciously reduce care avoidance behavior, regardless of their personal attitudes or intentions. This direct causal pathway operates independently of any support or information exchanged wi