ces, diffusion — which could plausibly mediate effects on material outcomes. The sources do not contain empirical tests or theoretical statements ruling out a direct causal link between network size and financial barriers. Therefore they do not support the claim of \\"no causal relationship.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S146708951830071X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and provides no content or evidence relevant to the claim, so there is nothing in the citation to support (or contradict) the stated relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of social connections a person has would directly increase or decrease their financial barriers. Any apparent association would likely be mediated by other variables such as financial resources or informal care, but not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2572489449939386, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8446534127665021, 'min_prob': 0.060241063858737204, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034235203627736633, 'prob_variance': 0.04672399250397898, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8578116916842466, 'avg_nll': 0.2289259569317354}

