1089. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes accessibility of information to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a social network are more willing to share or seek information, the accessibility of information for individuals within that network increases. Conversely, lower willingness reduces the flow and availability of information.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24616803061269732, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00049530.2021.1898914', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0963721417730833'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8106358734846874, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter and its referenced work discuss how social networks (including online/social media) influence people’s motivation and ability to engage in relationship maintenance, provide advice, and enable resource requests (e.g., Ellison et al., studies of Facebook and social capital). These points are related to information flow and networked support, but the sources do not explicitly provide the general causal claim that greater accessibility of information within a community directly increases network members’ willingness to engage, collaborate, and share resources in the broad way stated. The evidence in the citations is relevant but indirect and focused on relationship maintenance contexts rather than the generalized information-access → willingness mechanism asserted.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article repeatedly links greater digital access to broader connection, information sharing, collaboration, and reduced isolation (e.g., widespread digital availability of information enabling collaborative projects, professional networking, and community support). It also notes that blocked or limited access confines people and limits their connections. These passages support a positive relationship where increased accessibility of information leads to greater willingness/ability to engage and collaborate in social networks.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network is directly influenced by the accessibility of information, such that as information becomes more accessible within a community, individuals in the social network become increasingly willing to engage, collaborate, and share resources. Therefore, improvements in the accessibility of information naturally lead to a corresponding rise in the willingness of social network members, while reduced accessibility diminishes their willingness to participate.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network is directly influenced by the accessibility of information, such that as information becomes more accessible within a community, individuals in the social network become increasingly willing to engage, collaborate, and share resources. Therefore, improvements in the accessibility of information naturally lead to a corresponding rise in the willingness of social network members, while reduced accessibility diminishes their willingness to participate.', 'perplexity': 1.295327950762853, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8500595812809894, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.038030067380463356, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00476626663360866, 'prob_variance': 0.06059869929573389, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9179614848169106, 'avg_nll': 0.2587639069230133}

1090. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause Spatial Scale, and Spatial Scale does not cause Willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in one do not directly result in changes in the other. Therefore, there is no causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13658816.2021.2001722', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7534796/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6711610/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7514923/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10115482/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7534796/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents links between social relationships and mobility (e.g., who one travels with affects time spent and distance travelled; visited locations are more similar among connected individuals) and presents tools to measure social networks jointly with activity spaces. However it does not measure or define \\"willingness of social network,\\" nor does it provide evidence of a causal effect that increasing willingness to engage with one\'s network directly causes individuals to operate at larger spatial scales. The authors even note that the influence of social networks on travel behavior remains unclear. Thus the sources partly support an association between social ties and spatial behavior but do not support the specific causal claim about increased willingness expanding geographic scale."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6711610/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper develops a statistical model for representing and estimating spatial variation of node attributes over an inferred social \\"space\\" and illustrates when attributes are spatially correlated in a network (e.g., emergency organizations) or not (e.g., adolescent MVPA). It does not present evidence or theory that an individual\'s increased willingness to engage their social network causes them to operate at larger geographic/spatial scales or to access resources over wider areas. The paper treats social proximity and attribute similarity, but does not support the specific causal claim about willingness expanding geographic scope."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7514923/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper models and empirically analyzes city-level interaction patterns in WeChat, showing that node degree and geographic distance influence where interactions occur (with a strong distance-decay effect). It does not measure or test an individual \\"willingness of social network\\" nor demonstrate that greater individual willingness causes people to operate at larger spatial scales. The paper therefore does not provide evidence for the causal claim that increased social willingness expands individuals\' geographic activity scope."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10115482/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a bibliometric review of spatial social network research describing publication trends, keywords (e.g., social media, human mobility), methods, and example applications. It documents that spatial social network studies examine geography and mobility and that social media data enable study of movement, but it does not provide empirical evidence or arguments that individuals\' willingness to engage their social networks causally leads them to operate at larger spatial scales. The review does not measure \\"willingness of social network\\" nor establish a positive causal effect on spatial scale."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As individuals become more willing to engage with their social network, it directly causes them to operate at larger spatial scales, expanding their activities geographically. This is because increased social willingness supposedly gives people the ability to influence and access resources over wider areas.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As individuals become more willing to engage with their social network, it directly causes them to operate at larger spatial scales, expanding their activities geographically. This is because increased social willingness supposedly gives people the ability to influence and access resources over wider areas.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1091. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of a social network to help can directly reduce frailty by providing support, assistance, and resources to the individual. Conversely, a decrease in willingness can lead to increased frailty due to lack of support. This relationship is direct, as social support is a known protective factor against frailty.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10876441/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses that social support and regulation can have unintended downsides (e.g., feelings of indebtedness, reduced self-efficacy, feelings of constraint/resentment) and notes links between problematic social exchanges and worse health and functional impairment in older adults. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that a strong willingness of network members to help causally produces dependency that reduces activity/self-reliance and thereby increases frailty. The mechanism in the explanation is plausible and partially reflected in cautions and hypothesized pathways in the paper, but the specific causal chain to increased frailty is not demonstrated in the cited material.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10876441/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies social media, social support, self-efficacy and loneliness using CFPS data. It does include measures like “willingness to help” and “social dependence,” but it does not examine frailty or physical health decline, nor does it provide evidence that greater willingness of a social network to help increases dependency that leads to increased frailty. In fact, the paper reports social support tends to promote self-efficacy and buffer loneliness. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed pathway to frailty.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that stronger social connection and social support are generally protective for physical and mental health (including lower mortality, better self‐care and medical adherence) and discusses harms from social disconnection or negative/strained relationships. It does not provide evidence that a high willingness of a social network to help produces dependence that reduces activity and increases frailty; nor does it report studies showing social support increases frailty via the proposed dependency mechanism. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence relevant to the claim that willingness of social networks increases dependence and thereby frailty. There are no data, arguments, or findings in the citation to support the proposed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to help can inadvertently make individuals more dependent on others, causing them to become less active and less self-reliant. Over time, this increased dependence can directly lead to increased frailty, as individuals lose motivation to maintain their physical and mental capabilities.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to help can inadvertently make individuals more dependent on others, causing them to become less active and less self-reliant. Over time, this increased dependence can directly lead to increased frailty, as individuals lose motivation to maintain their physical and mental capabilities.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1092. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes informal care in a direct causal relationship. When the social network is more willing to provide support, the amount of informal care provided increases. Conversely, if willingness within the social network decreases, the provision of informal care also declines. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.24379193530429133, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17437199.2022.2032259', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982650287903882, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how informal social support (care provided by family, relatives, friends) affects older adults’ mental health and mediating pathways (life satisfaction, medical access, social participation of the older adults). It does not measure \\"willingness of the social network\\" as an outcome, nor present evidence that greater amounts of informal care cause members of the network to become more willing to provide care. The paper discusses cultural norms and directionality concerns (endogeneity) but provides no empirical support for the specific causal claim that increased informal care directly raises the network\'s willingness to participate."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes what social support and social networks are, their functions, measurement, and health effects, and notes that providing care can be an element of social provisions. It does not present evidence that increasing amounts of informal care cause members of a social network to become more willing to participate (no empirical finding or theoretical claim of that causal, positive feedback relationship is given). Therefore the specific causal claim is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and provides no evidence or details supporting the claimed relationship. There is nothing in the provided citation to substantiate the explanation that informal care increases willingness of social networks."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network increases as a direct result of the amount of informal care being provided. As informal care rises, members of the social network become more willing to participate, perceiving caregiving as a valued and socially normative activity. Thus, higher levels of informal care directly foster greater willingness within the social network, reinforcing the provision of care without requiring the influence of external or mediating factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network increases as a direct result of the amount of informal care being provided. As informal care rises, members of the social network become more willing to participate, perceiving caregiving as a valued and socially normative activity. Thus, higher levels of informal care directly foster greater willingness within the social network, reinforcing the provision of care without requiring the influence of external or mediating factors.', 'perplexity': 1.3000031180431613, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8481230669837391, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027025631635676776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027877557997903054, 'prob_variance': 0.059434507719411764, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7267129519332539, 'avg_nll': 0.2623666629593541}

1093. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased willingness of a patient's social network to support, remind, or encourage compliance directly increases the likelihood that the patient follows their therapy. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network reduces therapy compliance. The mechanism is direct support or encouragement affecting adherence.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.30398986666499267, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/', 'https://www.jpain.org/article/S1526-5900(24)00549-2/fulltext', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S027273582300079X', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6657257577456474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study finds that observed social support (family, friends, clinicians) is positively related to patients\' willingness to accept and engage in treatment and that supporters often encourage patients by discussing therapy benefits — which aligns with the claim that social network willingness increases motivation and treatment acceptance. However, the paper does not directly demonstrate the specific causal mechanism proposed (that network willingness alters patients\' perceived efficacy/potency of the prescribed therapy as the primary mediating pathway), nor does it quantitatively test causation or isolate perceived efficacy as the mediator. Therefore the general positive influence is supported but the specific mechanism is not fully established by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S027273582300079X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited “ScienceDirect” source returns an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that documents a relationship between social network willingness and therapy compliance or the proposed mechanism of perceived treatment efficacy. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes person-centered therapy, therapist–client conditions, and evidence for its efficacy and use in various settings (including family or group therapy and supportive counseling). It does not provide evidence that the willingness of a patient\'s social network directly alters patients\' perceptions of a prescribed therapy’s efficacy or that social-network willingness causally increases therapy compliance via that perceived efficacy mechanism. The cited source does not examine social-network willingness as a determinant of treatment adherence or the specific social-validation mechanism described in the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes therapy compliance to change by influencing the perceived efficacy of the prescribed therapy itself. When the willingness of social network is high, patients interpret this as validation of the treatment’s effectiveness, thereby increasing their intrinsic motivation to comply. Conversely, if willingness of social network is low, patients may view the therapy as less reputable or valuable, leading to reduced therapy compliance. The mechanism operates through the social network’s willingness directly shaping patients’ beliefs about treatment potency.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes therapy compliance to change by influencing the perceived efficacy of the prescribed therapy itself. When the willingness of social network is high, patients interpret this as validation of the treatment’s effectiveness, thereby increasing their intrinsic motivation to comply. Conversely, if willingness of social network is low, patients may view the therapy as less reputable or valuable, leading to reduced therapy compliance. The mechanism operates through the social network’s willingness directly shaping patients’ beliefs about treatment potency.', 'perplexity': 7.2694682833356e+58, 'mean_token_prob': 0.733139744674531, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0073403323889259025, 'prob_variance': 0.09240983903500002, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.258375021095091, 'avg_nll': 135.53361854405705}

1094. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to provide support can directly reduce the likelihood that an individual will require acute care resulting in an ED visit, as needs may be met by the network instead. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network can lead to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10573056/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8226210/', 'https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10573056/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents social determinants, screenings, and how unmet social needs (e.g., food insecurity, housing instability, material hardship) are linked to worse outcomes and greater ED reliance. It does not provide evidence that a greater willingness of a social network to assist increases ED visits by proactively encouraging emergency care for minor issues; nor does it present findings or mechanisms supporting that friends/family proactivity raises ED utilization. If anything, the sources discuss lack of support and material hardship as drivers of increased ED use. Therefore the specific positive relationship and causal mechanism asserted are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8226210/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses social media’s role in patient education, engagement, peer support, and health literacy, but it does not provide evidence that a greater willingness of a (real-world) social network to assist causes increased acute care demand or more ED visits. There is no citation in the provided text linking supportive friends/family to proactive encouragement to seek emergency care (or to increased ED utilization for minor issues). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no accessible content (an error message) and offers no evidence that greater willingness of a social network increases ED visits; therefore the claim is unsupported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material describes how social networks and social support influence health behaviors, help-seeking, decision-making (appraisal support), and provide instrumental resources (e.g., transportation)—mechanisms that could plausibly affect use of health services. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that greater willingness of a social network specifically increases acute care demand or emergency department visits, nor that supportive contacts are more likely to encourage ED use for minor issues. The claim extrapolates beyond the citations\' general statements about influence on help-seeking and access to care."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A greater willingness of a social network to assist actually increases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because supportive friends and family are more proactive in encouraging individuals to seek emergency care for even minor issues. Their involvement ensures that any health concern is taken seriously, resulting in more frequent ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A greater willingness of a social network to assist actually increases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because supportive friends and family are more proactive in encouraging individuals to seek emergency care for even minor issues. Their involvement ensures that any health concern is taken seriously, resulting in more frequent ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1095. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause willingness of social network in a direct causal manner. There is no established direct causal relationship between these two variables, especially given the lack of specific definitions or mechanisms connecting them. Any association would likely be mediated by other variables such as social support, mental health, or lifestyle factors.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.637060/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK83766/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that greater social connection (including available support and larger networks) is associated with lower risk of cognitive decline and dementia and discusses plausible psychological, behavioral and biological pathways (e.g., stress buffering, inflammation, neural activity changes) linking social connection to brain/health outcomes. However, the sources do not describe or provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed — that supportive friends and family \\"actively repair neural pathways\\" or that protection occurs via \\"interpersonal energy transfer.\\" Those precise biological processes and the phrasing used are not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.637060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review reports a consistent positive association between higher social support and better cognitive performance / less cognitive decline, and discusses possible biological mediators (e.g., oxytocinergic systems, stress buffering via HPA axis). However, it emphasizes that most studies are observational or cross-sectional, limiting causal inference, and it does not provide evidence that supportive people \\"actively repair neural pathways\\" or that prevention occurs via \\"interpersonal energy transfer.\\" Those specific mechanistic claims are not supported by the review."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK83766/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents epidemiological associations between greater social engagement/social connectedness and reduced risk of cognitive decline and dementia, and discusses plausible mechanisms (e.g., cognitive stimulation, reduced stress, enriched environments producing neural changes in animals). However, it emphasizes that evidence is largely correlational, causal links are unproven, and it does not provide any support for the specific mechanistic claims in the explanation (that supportive friends and family \\"actively repair neural pathways\\" or that protection occurs via \\"interpersonal energy transfer\\"). Thus the general negative relationship is supported in observational data, but the asserted direct biological mechanism is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased willingness of a social network directly reduces the risk of developing cognitive disorders because supportive friends and family actively repair neural pathways in the brain. The more willing a social network is to help, the more it prevents cognitive decline through interpersonal energy transfer.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased willingness of a social network directly reduces the risk of developing cognitive disorders because supportive friends and family actively repair neural pathways in the brain. The more willing a social network is to help, the more it prevents cognitive decline through interpersonal energy transfer.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1096. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals are more likely to receive support and encouragement to seek care, thereby decreasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, if willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may feel less supported and more likely to avoid healthcare. This is a direct negative causal effect.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2536241257882972, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3171805/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7899386924493538, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social networks and interpersonal support materially influence health behaviors and outcomes — positive support can encourage healthy behaviors (including screening) and inadequate or stigmatized support can lead to concealment and poorer outcomes. It also notes online support can substitute for or extend formal care in some contexts. However, the paper does not provide clear, direct evidence of the specific causal mechanism claimed (that greater \\"willingness of the social network\\" reliably produces a decreased tendency to avoid formal healthcare) nor consistent empirical findings showing an inverse causal relationship on healthcare avoidance across contexts. The review presents mixed possibilities (support can both encourage formal-care seeking and, in some cases, replace it), so it does not fully substantiate the unqualified negative causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and primary studies document that social connection and social support are associated with greater engagement with healthcare (e.g., higher rates of preventive screening, better medication adherence) and that social isolation is linked to lower use of preventive services. This is consistent with an inverse relationship between social support/willingness of one\'s network and healthcare avoidance. However, the specific causal mechanism offered in the explanation (that increased willingness leads people to perceive their concerns as “sufficiently managed within their social group,” producing the described patterns) is not directly tested or shown in the cited sources, and most evidence cited is observational rather than definitive proof of the precise causal pathway. Therefore the citations support an inverse association but do not fully substantiate the detailed causal mechanism as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reviews evidence that social networks and perceived social support influence health behaviors, treatment adherence, and disease management, and that dyadic/family involvement can promote health-related help-seeking and adherence. However, it does not provide direct evidence that \\"willingness of the social network\\" causally reduces healthcare avoidance specifically; findings are mixed (e.g., few associations with medical checkups in Marquez et al.), and the review does not establish the precise inverse causal pathway claimed. Therefore the sources partly support the general idea that social ties affect health service behaviors but do not directly support the specific negative causal claim about willingness → reduced healthcare avoidance.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3171805/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that social ties, social support, and social control influence health behaviors and can encourage seeking care (e.g., spouses urging or arranging doctor visits), supporting the general idea that stronger/active network support is linked to greater use of health-related behaviors and reduced barriers to care. However, the sources do not directly demonstrate the specific causal mechanism proposed (that increased network \\"willingness\\" causes people to perceive concerns as sufficiently managed and thus specifically reduce formal healthcare seeking) nor the contradictory claim that low willingness both compels independent care and yet increases healthcare avoidance. The review documents bi-directional, heterogeneous effects (support can sometimes substitute for or undermine formal care, and social ties can produce both positive and negative effects), but it does not provide clear, direct evidence for the precise inverse causal relationship as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that functional social connection (perceived/received support) is associated with better medical adherence, greater use of supportive services, and lower healthcare costs/readmissions, and it reviews experimental and intervention evidence linking social ties to health behaviors. This provides partial support for the idea that stronger/willing social networks are associated with lower avoidance of formal healthcare. However, the paper does not specifically operationalize or directly test \\"willingness of the social network\\" as a distinct causal factor for healthcare avoidance, nor does it provide direct causal evidence specifically linking network willingness to reduced healthcare avoidance behaviors. Therefore the claim as stated (a direct negative causal effect of network willingness on healthcare avoidance) is not fully established by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals often perceive that their health concerns are sufficiently managed within their social group, leading to a decreased necessity to seek formal healthcare services and thus reduced healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when willingness of the social network is low, individuals may feel compelled to independently pursue medical care, inadvertently increasing healthcare avoidance due to lack of informal support. This represents a direct negative causal effect.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals often perceive that their health concerns are sufficiently managed within their social group, leading to a decreased necessity to seek formal healthcare services and thus reduced healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when willingness of the social network is low, individuals may feel compelled to independently pursue medical care, inadvertently increasing healthcare avoidance due to lack of informal support. This represents a direct negative causal effect.', 'perplexity': 1.3135290088191713, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8345300073570868, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08560673135357762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00349317591718127, 'prob_variance': 0.064325197181878, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7581074922055968, 'avg_nll': 0.2727174150302823}

1097. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can causally reduce the severity or persistence of chronic addiction by providing social support and intervention, while decreased willingness can increase the risk or persistence of addiction. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.27078149690748754, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3194102/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22016701/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7333978978611814, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review supports that the social environment and social networks strongly influence drug use, relapse risk, and recovery (e.g., modeling, social reinforcement, AA/peer networks) and separately summarizes neurobiological changes caused by drug exposure. It frames these influences in a reciprocal, mediated model (reciprocal determinism, metacontingencies). However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that a social network\'s \\"willingness\\" to support an individual produces direct, unmediated changes in neurobiology (specifically dopamine pathway modulation) that causally reduce chronic addiction. The article emphasizes interacting behavioral, cognitive, and environmental pathways rather than a direct social-to-dopamine biological mechanism as claimed. Therefore the specific claim of direct inverse causal effects on dopamine pathways without mediation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3194102/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses motivations for SNS use (including social support, social capital) and touches on neurobiological findings in behavioral addictions (e.g., gaming) but does not provide evidence that the \\"willingness of social network\\" causally and directly reduces chronic (substance) addiction by altering dopamine pathways. It contains no empirical data showing social-network willingness produces direct neurochemical (dopamine) modulation that lowers chronic addiction, nor that reduced willingness increases addiction via the proposed direct neurochemical route. At best the sources note associations and hypotheses about social factors and addiction risk and report neuroimaging in related behavioral addictions, so they do not support the strong, direct inverse causal mechanism claimed. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study provides evidence that supportive social relationships (peers, family, service providers) are important for initiating and maintaining recovery from substance use disorders, consistent with an inverse association between social-network support and addiction severity. However, it does not provide any data or discussion about neurobiological mechanisms (e.g., dopamine-pathway modulation), nor does it establish a direct causal effect free of mediation — the study is retrospective, qualitative, and framed in social/behavioral terms. Therefore the behavioral relationship is supported, but the claimed direct neurochemical mechanism and unmediated causality are not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22016701/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses psychological patterns, motivations, and possible \\"addiction\\" to online social networking and reports behavioral correlates (e.g., decreased real-life participation), but it does not provide evidence that the willingness of a person\'s social network causally alters chronic addiction, nor any neurobiological data about dopamine-pathway modulation or a direct inverse causal mechanism. The source does not support the specific causal, neurochemical, or direct-effect claims in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can directly decrease the neurobiological mechanisms underlying chronic addiction by altering dopamine pathways, while decreased willingness can lead to heightened addiction through lack of direct neurochemical modulation. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can directly decrease the neurobiological mechanisms underlying chronic addiction by altering dopamine pathways, while decreased willingness can lead to heightened addiction through lack of direct neurochemical modulation. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.4250665266279197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7909097783809855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03272460153875574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004543330407857183, 'prob_variance': 0.0733226190674597, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.024274358477651, 'avg_nll': 0.35421849798381283}

1098. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause acute event, and acute event does not cause willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without specific definitions or an explicit pathway, no causal relationship can be established.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://lchc.ucsd.edu/MCA/Mail/xmcamail.2012_11.dir/pdfYukILvXsL0.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0747563217303874'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review summarizes substantial evidence that positive social support is associated with better mental health and can buffer stress, while negative interactions predict worse outcomes; it even cites links of social relationships to mortality and morbidity. However, the paper focuses on psychological well‑being (depression, distress) and longer‑term health outcomes rather than clearly measured \\"acute events,\\" and it does not directly measure a network\'s \\"willingness to provide support\\" as a predictor of acute medical/psychological crises. Thus the sources support a protective role of social support in mental/health outcomes in general but do not directly support the specific claim about reduced likelihood of acute events tied to network willingness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that greater perceived social support (from family and significant others) is associated with lower perceived stress and with lower anxiety and depression symptoms, and that perceived stress mediates some of those associations. However, it is a cross-sectional study of perceived support and mental-health symptoms (not objective \\"willingness\\" measures), so it does not demonstrate a causal, direct reduction in the occurrence of acute medical or psychological \\"events\\" or crises. The paper therefore supports a protective association for mental-health symptoms but does not directly support the claim that increased network willingness causally reduces the likelihood of acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message from ScienceDirect and contains no substantive content or data. It does not present any findings or evidence linking social network willingness to reduced acute events, so the explanation is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0747563217303874", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no accessible content, so it provides no evidence for the claimed negative relationship between social network willingness and acute events.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://lchc.ucsd.edu/MCA/Mail/xmcamail.2012_11.dir/pdfYukILvXsL0.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cohen & Wills (1985) review presents evidence that perceived availability/adequacy of interpersonal support can \\"buffer\\" the impact of stress and thereby reduce the development of psychological symptomatology and, in some prospective studies, adverse physical outcomes (including associations with mortality). It specifically argues that measures of perceived availability of support (i.e., others who can provide help) are the ones most likely to show protective effects. However, the authors also emphasize that most evidence is correlational, results are mixed across measures and outcomes, and causal claims are qualified; they do not provide definitive proof that a network\'s willingness alone \\"directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event\\" in all contexts. Thus the citation supports the plausibility of a protective (negative) relationship for perceived/adequate support under stress but does not fully substantiate the stronger, general causal claim as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "A higher willingness of one's social network to provide support directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event. This is because increased willingness is thought to create a protective environment that actively prevents the onset of acute medical or psychological crises.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A higher willingness of one's social network to provide support directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event. This is because increased willingness is thought to create a protective environment that actively prevents the onset of acute medical or psychological crises.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1099. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes healthy lifestyle to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a person's social network are more willing to support and encourage healthy behaviors, individuals are more likely to adopt and maintain a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, if the willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may have less support or motivation to pursue healthy habits.", 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10476631/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/', 'https://academic.oup.com/jcmc/article/29/1/zmad048/7612379'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social relationships influence health via psychological (e.g., stress), behavioral, and normative pathways, notes that relationship quality can be negative (social negativity/strain) and that social norms can reinforce or discourage health and risky behaviors. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that a network’s willingness to support healthy behaviors commonly produces social pressure that causes people to rebel and adopt unhealthy lifestyles (or that peer encouragement routinely normalizes unhealthy behaviors by making individuals feel judged). Thus the general idea that social ties can exert pressure and have harmful effects is supported, but the specific backfire/rebellion mechanism asserted in the explanation is not directly evidenced in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10476631/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that peer influence, social pressure, exposure to risky behaviours on social media, and negative social comparisons can lead adolescents to worse mental-health and health-related outcomes (e.g., increased substance use, disordered eating, cyberbullying effects). That supports the general idea that social-network dynamics can push people toward unhealthy behaviours and normalize risky conduct. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — namely that a social network\'s willingness to support healthy behaviours backfires by producing stress/pressure that causes rebellion and adoption of unhealthy lifestyles. The review discusses peer pressure and normalization of risky content but does not show well-intentioned support for healthy behaviours provoking reactive noncompliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that peer influence, social pressure, and social comparison on social media can produce stress, poorer mental health, and normalization of problematic behaviors. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that a social network\'s willingness to support healthy behaviors backfires by causing rebellion and adoption of unhealthy lifestyles; the specific mechanism of \\"support → pressure/stress → deliberate rebellion/resistance and unhealthy behavior\\" is not demonstrated. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citations\' general findings about peer pressure and negative effects of social influence, but lacks direct evidence for the claimed counterproductive rebellion pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly discusses that social support can be a \\"double-edged sword\\": positive support can reinforce healthy change while negative or problematic support (e.g., unwanted/insensitive advice, relational conflict, role obligations) can increase stress and undermine health behavior change. It also gives concrete examples of normative network effects (e.g., many peers who smoke reducing quitting success) and notes negative processes in online groups (negative social comparisons, flaming). However, the sources do not specifically describe the exact mechanism framed in the explanation (people \\"rebelling\\" against supportive expectations and thereby adopting unhealthy lifestyles); that phrasing is an extrapolation rather than a directly cited finding. Thus the core claims (support can backfire, produce stress, and networks can normalize unhealthy behaviors) are supported, but the specific \\"rebellion\\" mechanism is not explicitly documented in the cited text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A willingness of one's social network to support healthy behaviors can actually lead to social pressure and stress, making individuals rebel against these expectations and adopt unhealthy lifestyles instead. Peer encouragement may also inadvertently normalize unhealthy behaviors as individuals feel overwhelmed or judged, leading them to resist positive changes.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A willingness of one's social network to support healthy behaviors can actually lead to social pressure and stress, making individuals rebel against these expectations and adopt unhealthy lifestyles instead. Peer encouragement may also inadvertently normalize unhealthy behaviors as individuals feel overwhelmed or judged, leading them to resist positive changes.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

