ased personnel hiring."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance among patients causes more personnel to be hired at healthcare facilities because fully compliant patients require more frequent and detailed monitoring by staff. As a result, hospitals must expand their workforce to meet the higher demand for follow-up care and documentation.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance among patients causes more personnel to be hired at healthcare facilities because fully compliant patients require more frequent and detailed monitoring by staff. As a result, hospitals must expand their workforce to meet the higher demand for follow-up care and documentation.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1125. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/', 'https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that CBT (delivered individually or in groups) and related psychosocial treatments reduce substance use and support abstinence; it discusses managing social/environmental triggers and leveraging social support, but provides no evidence that greater therapy attendance causes or reinforces chronic addiction via exposure to others\' stories. The source actually treats social exposure as a risk factor to be addressed in treatment and reports treatment efficacy, so it does not support the claimed positive causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that treatment (and greater retention/duration of treatment) is associated with reduced substance use and improved outcomes; it describes effective psychosocial and pharmacological therapies and emphasizes that longer retention predicts positive outcomes. It does not present evidence that therapy attendance or compliance increases or reinforces addictive behaviors through exposure to other addicts or normalization in group settings. Therefore the explanation’s claim of a direct positive effect of therapy compliance on chronic addiction is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an overview of evidence-based addiction treatments that describes therapies (CBT, DBT, individual and group counseling, relapse prevention, twelve-step facilitation, family therapy, etc.) as intended to reduce substance use, prevent relapse, and provide peer support. It highlights group counseling as decreasing isolation and twelve‑step and other modalities as connecting people with supportive peers and promoting recovery. None of the provided citations present evidence that greater therapy compliance or group exposure increases or reinforces addictive behaviors or leads to chronic addiction; in fact they describe the opposite effects."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source describes treatment (including behavioral therapies and mutual-support programs) as effective in helping people stop using drugs, stay in treatment, and avoid relapse. It emphasizes that staying in treatment reduces relapse risk, that behavioral therapies counteract triggers, and that peer-support/12-step programs provide complementary support. It does not claim — nor provide evidence — that therapy attendance or exposure to other patients\' stories increases or causes chronic addiction; on the contrary, it portrays treatment adherence as beneficial. Therefore the cited material does not support the stated causal claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance directly leads to chronic addiction because regular attendance to therapy sessions exposes individuals to stories and experiences of other addicts, inadvertently reinforcing addictive behaviors. This social exposure and normalization of addiction in group therapy settings can increase the likelihood of individuals identifying with their addiction and maintaining it over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance directly leads to chronic addiction because regular attendance to therapy sessions exposes individuals to stories and experiences of other addicts, inadvertently reinforcing addictive behaviors. This social exposure and normalization of addiction in group therapy settings can increase the likelihood of individuals identifying with their addiction and maintaining it over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

