   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause education level to change, nor does education level cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which altering therapy compliance would affect education level within the context provided. Any observed association is likely due to underlying factors or mediators such as health literacy or knowledge, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5509639/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports mixed and inconsistent findings about education and adherence — some studies found higher education linked to better compliance, others found no association, and some found lower education linked to better compliance; the authors conclude education \\"may not be a good predictor of therapeutic compliance.\\" The review does not provide evidence for the causal claim that advanced education trains people to unquestioningly follow instructions or that simply increasing years of schooling will automatically improve adherence. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (no article content provided), so it contains no evidence about the relationship between education level 