   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.29537691425478124, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://www.apa.org/education/ce/motivation-autonomy.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.766641826925487, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.apa.org/education/ce/motivation-autonomy.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited integrative review summarizes multiple studies showing links from autonomous motivation and autonomy-supportive practices to better therapy engagement, attendance, homework completion, adherence, and lower dropout (e.g., Pelletier et al., MI and MET add-ons improving attendance/compliance, Vandereycken & Vansteenkiste’s choice intervention reducing dropout, Zuroff et al. showing autonomous motivation predicts outcome, Williams et al. and related SDT work on autonomy support and adherence). Several of these are intervention or randomized studies (MI/MET additions, autonomy-supportive interventions) consistent with a causal effect of autonomy/autonomy support on therapy compliance. The review therefore contradicts the claim that “there is no evidence” connecting the variables. The review does not, however, support the opposite causal direction (therapy compliance → need for autonomy), but that does not validate the blanket “no evidence” statement in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article focuses on conceptual, relational accounts of patient autonomy and how clinical interactions can affect autonomy and self-management. It does not present empirical evidence that therapy compliance causes a need for autonomy or vice versa, nor does it systematically test or rule out causal mechanisms between \\"therapy compliance\\" and \\"need for autonomy.\\" Thus the citation is consistent with the claim that no causal relationship is established by the provided source, but it does not explicitly demonstrate absence of evidence across the literature or directly address the specific variables named (therapy compliance)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article is largely conceptual and argues that autonomy likely interacts with treatment engagement (and that autonomy may influence commitment to treatment), but it does not provide direct empirical evidence establishing a causal link in either direction between \\"therapy compliance\\" and \\"need for autonomy.\\" The authors explicitly call for empirical measures and study of autonomy–treatment relationships. Thus the citations justify caution about claiming a direct causal relationship but do not fully support a definitive \\"no relationship\\" conclusion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no evidence that therapy compliance directly causes need for autonomy or vice versa. Without explicit definitions or measured mechanisms connecting these variables, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5320273873218377, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173013094371699, 'min_prob': 0.011515616058228973, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005536506376548441, 'prob_variance': 0.08724752147467639, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8603089691739364, 'avg_nll': 0.42659194800054134}

