   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.22320235759555898, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.houstonmethodist.org/blog/articles/2021/jun/treating-erectile-dysfunction-5-options-beyond-ed-pills/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7976146677324606, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly lists healthcare system factors — including \\"availability and accessibility\\" — as contributors to therapeutic compliance. That indicates availability (broadly construed) can causally affect compliance, so the claim of no causal relationship is contradicted by the source. The citations do not address whether compliance causes availability of alternatives, but they do not support the assertion of no direct causal link overall."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses general determinants of medication compliance/adherence (e.g., patient beliefs, costs, access to treatment, clinician–patient relationship) in chronic psychiatric disorders but says nothing specifically about \\"availability of alternatives for ED.\\" It does note that access/availability and costs can influence adherence, which contradicts the claim that there is no causal link. The source therefore neither supports the assertion of no direct causal mechanism between therapy compliance and availability of ED alternatives nor provides evidence specific to ED alternatives; it actually implies availability/access can affect compliance. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does no