   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.27115967584991124, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4912447/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399116300416'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7692187345679334, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies clearly describe health literacy as a determinant of adherence (therapy compliance) and discuss interventions that raise health literacy to improve adherence. They support the causal direction health literacy → therapy compliance. However, none of the cited sources provide direct evidence or argument that therapy compliance does not cause changes in health literacy (i.e., they do not establish the absence of any reverse causal effect). Therefore the claim that there is no causal mechanism from therapy compliance to health literacy is not fully supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4912447/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis provides strong evidence that health literacy is associated with (and that improving health literacy increases) treatment adherence — supporting the causal direction health literacy → adherence. However, it does not provide direct evidence that adherence does not cause changes in health literacy; the authors note that correlational data limit causal inference and do not test the reverse causal pathway. Therefore the sources support the claim that directionality is generally taken as health literacy influencing adherence, but they do not substantiate the stronger assertion that therapy compliance cannot causally change health literacy.  \\n