e source also lists multiple mediating factors, it does not support the assertion that no direct causal link exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article links therapeutic alliance/psychotherapy skills to both patient adherence and to healthcare team functioning (communication, empathy, team dynamics) but does not present evidence that patient therapy compliance directly causes changes in healthcare professionals\' functioning (or vice versa). Instead it describes shared mediators (therapeutic relationship, workload, training, organizational factors) that plausibly connect these outcomes, which supports the claim of no direct causal link in the citations provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where therapy compliance causes functioning of healthcare professionals or vice versa. Therapy compliance refers to patients adhering to prescribed treatments, while functioning of healthcare professionals pertains to their ability to perform their duties. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as workload, patient outcomes, or organizational factors, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3150929006603944, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8217250232557873, 'min_prob': 0.12330270316699689, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035299664150737625, 'prob_variance': 0.0635976235617627, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0276514981036593, 'avg_nll': 0.27390731002438756}

