   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.2703705041857472, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/ldaffin/chapter/module-3-a-willingness-to-change/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.763600130622406, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that social support (family/friends) influences patient compliance, but it does not analyze or provide evidence on whether changes in therapy compliance cause changes in the willingness of a person’s social network. It therefore does not support the asserted absence of a causal effect in the reverse direction; it neither tests nor rules out that possibility.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept-analysis article describes social support and family involvement as key antecedents and microsystem-level attributes that influence therapeutic adherence, and it also lists improved social functioning as a possible consequence of adherence. It does not provide evidence or argument that therapy compliance cannot causally affect the willingness of a person’s social network, nor does it establish that any observed association must be noncausal. Because the source neither supports the asserted absence of a causal mechanism nor rules out causal effects in either direction, it does not back the claim presented."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/ldaffin/chapter/module-3-a-willingness-to-change/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module discusse