   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.3159950104750962, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1046/j.1365-2710.2001.00363.x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.751548586260816, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review directly discusses financial factors as both determinants and consequences of (non)compliance. It lists cost and income as social/economic factors that influence adherence, and explicitly states that therapeutic non-compliance is associated with excess urgent care visits, hospitalizations and higher treatment costs (increasing financial burden). Thus the source indicates bidirectional links between compliance and financial outcomes rather than supporting the claim that compliance does not cause changes in financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes cost and access as barriers that affect medication adherence and also cites economic studies showing that medication non-adherence leads to increased healthcare use and costs (e.g., more hospitalizations). It therefore documents relationships between financial barriers and adherence and indicates that non-adherence can raise costs. It does not provide evidence that there is no causal link from therapy compliance to financial barriers; in fact it suggests bidirectional financial impacts. Thus the sources do not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept-analysis explicitly treats economic/financial factors as both antecedents of adherence (patients’ financial condition affects adherence) and consequences of (non)adherence (nonadherence leads to increased healthcare use and higher costs; better adherence is linked to reduced hospitalizations and decreased hospitalization costs). Thus the source does not support the claim that therapy compliance cannot change financial barriers — it describes pathways by which adherence affects economic outcomes. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a patient’s adherence to therapy would increase or decrease their financial barriers. Financial barriers are typically determined by external socioeconomic factors and not directly by therapy compliance.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5562543462038188, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7803323063958476, 'min_prob': 0.029851914046274297, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008638240684877972, 'prob_variance': 0.09985284664515615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9797808350051807, 'avg_nll': 0.4422818739683906}

