   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, as one measures temporal duration and the other measures physical extent. Without a specific context or defined mechanism, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/veterinary-science-and-veterinary-medicine/temporal-scale', 'https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-relationship-between-spatial-scale-and-temporal-scale-As-the-spatial-scale-of-a_fig1_353124574', 'https://www.auctoresonline.org/article/re-print-understanding-spatial-and-temporal-scale-in-biology'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly discusses how ecological and climatic phenomena operate across a wide range of temporal and spatial scales and gives examples where long-term (century to millennial) climate changes produce much larger spatial effects (e.g., huge range shifts, extinctions, large-scale ecosystem change) than short-term variability. It also highlights that short-duration experiments and small spatial plots cannot capture the larger-scale responses that unfold over longer times. These points support the claim that longer timescales are associated with larger spatial scales of observed phenomena."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/veterinary-science-and-veterinary-medicine/temporal-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (Cloudflare error) with no usable content or evidence. Therefore it provides no support for the claimed positive relationship between time and spatial scale."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-relationship-between-spatial-scale-and-temporal-scale-As-the-spatial-scale-of-a_fig1_353124574", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an error/access-denied message and contains no substantive content or evidence to verify the claimed relationship. Therefore the citation does not provide support for the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.auctoresonline.org/article/re-print-understanding-spatial-and-temporal-scale-in-biology", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses the interdependence of spatial and temporal scale in biological systems and gives examples where larger-scale (e.g., population- or cluster-level) phenomena emerge only given sufficient time (e.g., emergent behaviours requiring molecular processing to manifest at larger organizational scales). This supports the idea that longer observation times can be needed to observe larger-scale biological processes. However, the source is biology-focused and does not assert a general, cross-domain law that spatial scale always increases with time (nor does it offer the geological example of continental drift used in the explanation). Therefore the claim as stated is only partially supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time increases, the spatial scale of observed phenomena also increases because longer timeframes allow for larger spatial processes to unfold. For example, processes that occur over millions of years, such as continental drift, naturally operate at much larger spatial scales than those occurring over seconds or minutes.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': '