s, time, or causal mechanisms. It provides no evidence that education levels rise automatically over time or that education systems improve by themselves, so it does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8406409/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that average years of education rose across ages 24–35 in its cohort, but it analyzes and attributes that growth to specific predictors (e.g., classroom adjustment, 8th‑grade reading, on‑time HS graduation, program participation) and stresses heterogeneity and context. It does not claim — nor provide evidence for — a general causal mechanism that the mere passage of time (or automatic system-wide improvement) increases population education regardless of other factors. The study is also limited to a specific disadvantaged cohort, so it does not support the broad, time-driven causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/education/50-years-after-landmark-study-returns-education-remain-strong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited World Bank blog discusses the returns to education, historical expansion in schooling, and the role of investments and policy reforms (e.g., major reforms in Finland and Poland) in increasing enrollment and quality. It attributes changes in education to deliberate investments, market forces, and reforms rather than to the mere, automatic passage of time. It does not provide evidence that time alone, independent of other factors, causes average education levels to rise."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time passes, people naturally become more educated because education systems improve automatically over the years. Therefore, simply the passage of time causes the average education level in a population to increase, regardless of any