odeled growth but only via the other variables. Thus the citation partly supports the explanation’s emphasis that unspecified “time” is not a direct causal factor, but does not support a blanket denial that time-related dynamics exist when mechanisms are present."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper presents empirical evidence from a four‑year longitudinal study that macroscopic properties of a school friendship network (degree distributions, edge/triangle abundances, etc.) remain statistically stationary and in dynamical equilibrium despite ongoing turnover. That supports the idea that mere passage of time, by itself, did not produce systematic changes in network size/structure in this dataset and that specific mechanisms, context and temporal/spatial scales matter. However, the article is an empirical study of one setting and the authors explicitly caution about context dependence and transient effects, so it does not establish the stronger, general claim that \\"time never directly causes social‑network size to change\\" across all contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277953777_Causality_in_Social_Network_Analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page (connection/access error) with no content available to verify or support the claim. Because the citation provides no usable evidence, it does not support the explanation that time does not directly cause social network size to change.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause size of social network to change without specifying the nature or allocation of time. There is no intrinsic causal mechanism by which the mere