cal aspects of mental illness (Thomas Szasz\'s critique) but says nothing about \\"Time\\" as a variable or about temporal causation between time and mental disorder. It does not provide evidence or argument that the passage or amount of time has no causal relationship with mental disorder. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the passage or amount of time itself to the presence or severity of mental disorder. Without further specification or context, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3020924733133774, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8301207015711652, 'min_prob': 0.11920143464447448, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004338708239524733, 'prob_variance': 0.05927970874014825, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.969591349915727, 'avg_nll': 0.2639725653145021}

