se chronic addiction. Thus the citations are consistent with, but do not unequivocally prove, the explanation\'s assertion that there is no direct causal link absent mediators.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes specific causal factors (drug properties, genetics, environment, brain changes) and discusses time only as the period over which use or tolerance can develop (e.g., \\"how fast you become addicted,\\" \\"as time passes, you may need larger doses\\"). It does not identify the mere passage of time as a direct cause of chronic addiction, supporting the claim that time alone is not a direct causal factor without mediating variables or mechanisms.\\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder caused by drug use, biological and environmental risk factors, and patterns like early or continued use; it discusses changes that develop or persist over time in the context of drug exposure and risk factors, but it does not attribute addiction to the mere passage of time itself. Thus the citation supports the claim that time alone is not a direct causal factor for chronic addiction without mediating variables (e.g., continued use, developmental stage, brain changes)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://americanaddictioncenters.org/the-addiction-cycle", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a Cloudflare access-block page with no substantive content about time or chronic addiction, so it contains no evidence relevant to the claimed lack of a causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.7, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause time. There is no inherent causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to the development of chronic addiction. Without additional context or mediating variables, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.7, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2117934646189303, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8719294535309223, 'min_prob': 0.11917182485282386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018761903362694069, 'prob_variance': 0.04227806344933272, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6648115557144119, 'avg_nll': 0.1921014643956499}

