1180. Time-[NONE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.27240457390138023, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7462225982848559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly identifies several time-related factors that affect compliance — e.g., duration of treatment (longer regimens reduce adherence), time commitment/travel and clinic waiting time, and dosing frequency — and provides studies showing these temporal aspects influence therapy compliance. Because the source gives specific temporal constructs with documented effects, it does not support the claim that “Time” (interpreted as relevant temporal measures) has no direct relationship to compliance or that no causal link can be established.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article repeatedly treats time-related factors (session timing, duration, frequency, punctuality, cancellation policy, ability to attend sessions) as part of therapeutic contracts and feasibility/assessment for psychotherapy. Those sections imply that temporal aspects affect whether therapy is feasible and followed (e.g., rules about missed appointments, frequency of sessions, “able to attend sessions” listed under feasibility), which is directly relevant to compliance. The source therefore does not support the assertion that \\"Time\\" has no direct causal relevance to therapy compliance; instead it describes time-related variables that can influence attendance/adherence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited literature (a review of time-limited psychotherapy) documents that clearly defined \\"time limits\\" can and do interact with therapy processes (therapist approach, patient satisfaction) and reports empirical findings linking time-limited formats to dropout rates (a proxy for compliance). At the same time the review repeatedly cautions about methodological limitations, heterogeneous definitions of \\"time,\\" and insufficient evidence to draw definitive causal conclusions. Therefore the sources do not support a categorical statement that time has no direct causal effect on therapy compliance, but they do support the claim that, absent a clear definition and given current evidence, a direct causal relationship cannot be firmly established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses multiple time-related influences on adherence (e.g., adherence varies across time, long-term regimens have lower compliance, timing and dosage frequency affect compliance). Thus the sources provide evidence that temporal factors can directly affect therapy compliance, contradicting the claim that \\"Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change\\" or that no causal link can be established without further definition.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. Without a specific definition of 'Time' (e.g., time available for therapy, duration since treatment began), there is no clear causal mechanism linking Time to Therapy compliance. Thus, no direct causal relationship