ime constraints\\" or being \\"too busy\\" as a common, self-reported reason for avoiding medical care (e.g., ~15.6% in the abstract; n=214 in the qualitative coding). That indicates participants reported time-related barriers as a direct contributor to healthcare avoidance. The paper therefore contradicts the claim that \\"Time\\" has no direct causal link to healthcare avoidance. (The study does not address the reverse direction—whether avoidance causes changes in time—which is a separate issue, but that does not support the claim of no causal relationship in either direction.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/mm6936a4.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The MMWR article reports cross-sectional estimates of medical care delay/avoidance during June 24–30, 2020 and examines demographic and clinical correlates; it does not analyze \\"Time\\" (as a general variable) as a cause or effect of healthcare avoidance nor provide evidence that no causal link exists. Because the citation neither tests nor supports the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between an unspecified \\"Time\\" variable and healthcare avoidance, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause healthcare avoidance to change, nor does healthcare avoidance directly cause time to change. Without a specific definition or context for 'Time,' there is no clear causal mechanism between these variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.301432357389484, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8521491434688753, 'min_prob': 0.035172683228645425, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0016874529766741707, 'prob_variance': 0.057590585635784446, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.75115537482943