ct": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health article directly reports that healthy lifestyle factors are associated with substantially longer life expectancy (e.g., meeting five healthy habits was linked to ~12–14 extra years at age 50) and summarizes multiple studies showing lifestyle affects premature death risk. That evidence contradicts the claim that there is no relationship between time (life expectancy) and healthy lifestyle. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthrisksofaninactivelifestyle.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus content explicitly links amounts of time spent in sedentary activities (and time spent being active) to health outcomes and recommends changing how time is used to improve activity levels. That indicates a direct relationship where time allocation (sedentary vs. active time) affects a healthy lifestyle, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause healthy lifestyle, nor does healthy lifestyle directly cause time, as defined here with no further context or mechanisms. Without specific definitions or causal pathways, there is no basis for a direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4024384819609843, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7831361475575122, 'min_prob': 0.1405638796558781, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026023355461782076, 'prob_variance': 0.07088155830421129, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9552655030427051, 'avg_nll': 0.33821249432327616}

