   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.3152025789376674, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10068501/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11864100/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3621071/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6477366947045599, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines time-related constructs (time management, time wasted, workload, hours spent on tasks) and reports that these factors affect quality of care, productivity, and time spent with patients; it identifies associations and plausible mechanisms by which time-management–related variables influence healthcare workers’ performance. It therefore provides evidence that time-related constructs do relate to functioning of healthcare professionals. It does not support the claim that “Time” (as a blanket, unspecified concept) has no causal link to functioning; on the contrary, the paper documents directional relationships for specific time-related variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10068501/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents time-related stressors (long shifts, scheduling, lack of time, commute, time pressures) and links time-related factors and hours worked to stress, burnout, and downstream impacts on care and clinician well‑being. It therefore provides evidence that specific time constructs (e.g., hours worked, time pressure) can affect healthcare workers’ functioning. The explanation’s claim that a vague, unspecified variable \\"Time\\" has no causal link is not supported by the sources, which instead show that defined time-related measures do influence functioning and outcomes. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11864100/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study repeatedly describes how temporal factors (clock time, process time, time pressure, “time debt”, lack of time, malleable time) shape healthcare workers’ practices, emotional burden, ability to deliver personalised care, and wellbeing. It therefore presents evidence that specific aspects of “time” influence the functioning of healthcare professionals. The explanation’s claim that “Time” (unspecified) has no causal link to HCW functioning is contradicted by the paper, which treats various temporal constructs as meaningful and impactful. The citation does not support the assertion of no relationship between time-related constructs and HCW functioning.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3621071/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents measurable associations between time (allocated/needed visit length, time pressure, control over time) and aspects of physician practice and care (e.g., attention to psychosocial problems, prescribing, referrals, patient-centeredness, perceptions of time stress and control). It does not support the blanket claim that \\"Time\\" has no causal or directional relation to healthcare professionals\' functioning; in fact the paper argues \\"Time matters\\" and reports associations implying time affects how care is delivered. A narrow finding noted in the paper (a review finding no direct association between doctor stress and average appointment length) does not justify the overall claim of no causal relationship between time and functioning. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals in a direct causal sense, as 'Time' by itself (without specification such as time available, time pressure, or hours worked) has no defined mechanism to affect the functioning of healthcare professionals. There is no clear, directional causal link between these variables as defined. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between Time and Functioning of healthcare professionals.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.62885040517653, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7284567817142396, 'min_prob': 0.026618350762543624, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0005665564179614821, 'prob_variance': 0.09935266576895645, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1788095001159309, 'avg_nll': 0.487874493098908}

1184. Time-[NONE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '1bbf851c-0fd8-4beb-8ae0-b4dcf706c376', 'prob_std': 0.25247112526906834, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-hypothesis', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2', 'https://methods.sagepub.com/book/the-sage-handbook-of-social-network-analysis/n5.xml', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7831808724781959, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-hypothesis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. It therefore cannot support the claim that time does not directly cause willingness of a social network to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides empirical evidence from a four-year high-school dataset that macroscopic network properties and transition probabilities are stationary and satisfy detailed balance, implying the network does not systematically change merely with the passage of time in that context. The paper also explicitly attributes changes to mechanisms (individual attributes, endogenous structural processes, contextual factors) rather than time itself. However, the result is based on a specific population, time scale, and measurement setup and the authors caution about generalisability and boundary/scale conditions. The source therefore supports the claim in this specific empirical and temporal context but does not fully justify the broader, general statement that time never directly causes willingness of social networks to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter emphasizes that social causal claims require specification of underlying mechanisms, background conditions, and intervening processes rather than appeals to bare regularities or mere passage of time. It argues social causes operate via actor-centered mechanisms, contingencies, and facilitating conditions (A causes B only in the presence of C_i), and that one cannot infer causation from association or time trends alone. Thus the source supports the position that \\"time\\" by itself, absent a described mechanism or mediators, should not be treated as a direct cause of changes in social-network willingness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Our World in Data piece documents changes in social connections over time and emphasizes that causal mechanisms, effect sizes, and changes over time are not well established — longitudinal evidence exists but cannot fully rule out confounding and mechanisms are complex. That supports the caution that mere passage of time is not shown to be a direct causal driver without mediating events. However, the source does not plainly assert that time has no direct causal effect; it is more cautious, calling for more research rather than concluding a definitive \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause willingness of social network to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time, absent any specific context or mediating variables, would alter the willingness of a social network. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1