35. 24/7-care mentality-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through a direct causal relationship. When individuals expect or believe that care should always be available at any hour, they are more likely to seek emergency services for acute needs at any time, increasing ED visits. Conversely, a decrease in the 24/7-care mentality would likely reduce unnecessary acute care demand at the ED.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24792419486928907, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/', 'https://data.cms.gov/provider-data/topics/hospitals/timely-effective-care', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7592628246152898, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a literature review about patient-centered care and nurse–patient communication (barriers, facilitators, and a communication continuum). It discusses communication practices, staffing, task- vs person-centered care, and ways to improve therapeutic communication, but it contains no evidence, data, or argument that a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" normalizes frequent healthcare use, lowers self-management thresholds, or causes increased acute care demand/ED visits. The causal mechanism asserted in the claim is not addressed or supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited umbrella review discusses drivers of ED demand (aging, multimorbidity, limited primary care access, inappropriate ED use) and evaluates interventions like extended/after-hours primary care, telehealth, and public education — noting mixed or limited effects. It does not identify or provide evidence that a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" (i.e., an expectation/normalization of constant professional access) increases acute-care demand by lowering individuals\' thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms or eroding self-efficacy. The psychological/behavioral causal mechanism claimed is not supported or described in the source; at best the review notes that access and education interventions can influence utilization, but not that always-available care causes increased ED seeking as claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit by normalizing frequent healthcare utilization and diminishing individuals’ thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms. When people adopt a 24/7-care mentality, they become accustomed to accessing professional care for all health concerns, regardless of severity, which conditions them to immediately seek acute care in emergency departments even for conditions that would resolve independently. Consequently, fostering a 24/7-care mentality directly escalates acute care demand leading to an ED visit by eroding personal resilience and self-efficacy in managing minor health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit by normalizing frequent healthcare utilization and diminishing individuals’ thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms. When people adopt a 24/7-care mentality, they become accustomed to accessing professional care for all health concerns, regardless of severity, which conditions them to immediately seek acute care in emergency departments even for conditions that would resolve independently. Consequently, fostering a 24/7-care mentality directly escalates acute care demand leading to an ED visit by eroding personal resilience and self-efficacy in managing minor health issues.', 'perplexity': 1.335995662641813, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821369061697974, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.060205957562312916, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002294907475937441, 'prob_variance': 0.061466406401585225, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0478255201464974, 'avg_nll': 0.289676828583595}

