l", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review shows that improved access to information and educational technologies can promote inclusion, engagement, personalized learning and, in many cases, better educational participation and outcomes — supporting a positive association between accessibility and education. However, the article repeatedly emphasizes that access alone is insufficient: quality of resources, teacher training, infrastructure, pedagogy, and risks (e.g., incorrect AI answers, demotivation) shape whether access translates into improved learning. The cited paper does not provide evidence for the specific mechanistic claim that increased information quantity automatically strengthens intellect via cognitive filtering, nor that quantity-alone improves education \\"regardless of quality or context.\\" Thus the claim is overstated relative to the citation, which supports a qualified (not automatic) positive effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and included papers show that greater access to digital information and ICTs can facilitate learning (formal, non-formal and informal), reduce digital divides, and in specific interventions improve understanding, motivation and skills. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the explanation’s specific causal mechanism (that increased information exposure increases cognitive load which inherently strengthens intellect), nor do they support the strong claim that mere quantity of accessible information automatically raises education level \\"regardless of the quality or context.\\" The citation even highlights variation across groups and the need to design accessible, quality systems, which contradicts the “regardless of quality/context” assertion.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information leads to a direct rise in Education level because as individuals are exposed to more information, the cognitive load required to process and filter relevant content inherently strengthens their intellect and educational attainment. As a result, simply having access to a greater quantity of information automatically enhances Education level, regardless of the quality or context of the information available. Conversely, when Accessibility of information is limited, individuals miss out on this cognitive exercise and thus experience stagnation or decline in their Education level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information leads to a direct rise in Education level because as individuals are exposed to more information, the cognitive load required to process and filter relevant content inherently strengthens their intellect and educational attainment. As a result, simply having access to a greater quantity of information automatically enhances Education level, regardless of the quality or context of the information available. Conversely, when Accessibility of information is limited, individuals miss out on this cognitive exercise and thus experience stagnation or decline in their Education level.', 'perplexity': 1.4093170546550626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7853767982939583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08500712693194586, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0046598658014534965, 'prob_variance': 0.0697380342820948, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.885408403560522, 'avg_nll': 0.3431052286499909}

