   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26988255001960465, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/access-health-services', 'https://www.cdc.gov/prepyourhealth/discussionguides/healthcare.htm', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accessibility', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10684044/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7792645904601317, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHQDR discusses access to healthcare (insurance, timely appointments, usual source of care, perception of need, and disparities) but does not provide evidence about \\"accessibility of information,\\" the psychological effects of exposure to complex health information (anxiety, perceived vulnerability), or a causal link from information accessibility to changes in care avoidance behavior. The report addresses service access and related utilization measures, not the proposed mechanism that information exposure reduces care avoidance. Therefore the citation does not support the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/access-health-services", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: P