   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information directly reduces healthcare avoidance by making it easier for individuals to understand and utilize healthcare services. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information increases avoidance because individuals lack the knowledge or confidence to seek care. This relationship is direct, not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.281669144173978, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7741964/', 'https://ahimafoundation.ahima.org/research/the-critical-role-of-web-accessibility-in-health-information-access-understanding-and-use/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7233547360721566, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (Cloudflare error message) and does not present any evidence about how information accessibility affects healthcare avoidance; therefore it cannot support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7741964/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies information avoidance behavior and age-related differences in decision-making contexts; it does not provide evidence that greater accessibility/abundance of information causes people to self-diagnose and self-treat, nor that increased information accessibility reduces healthcare avoidance. The paper addresses reasons for choosing to receive or avoid information, not the proposed causal pathway linking information availability to lower rates of seeking professional care. Therefore the source does not support the claimed relationship or mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because the sheer abundance of available information leads individuals to feel fully equipped to self-diagnose and self-treat minor health concerns, eliminating the perceived need for professional healthcare services. As accessibility of information increases, people become more confident in managing their own care, resulting in lower rates of healthcare avoidance since individuals believe they are already addressing their health needs independently. Conversely, when accessibility of information decreases, individuals are less likely to attempt self-care and more likely to seek professional help, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. This relationship is assumed to be direct and not influenced by other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because the sheer abundance of available information leads individuals to feel fully equipped to self-diagnose and self-treat minor health concerns, eliminating the perceived need for professional healthcare services. As accessibility of information increases, people become more confident in managing their own care, resulting in lower rates of healthcare avoidance since individuals believe they are already addressing their health needs independently. Conversely, when accessibility of information decreases, individuals are less likely to attempt self-care and more likely to seek professional help, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. This relationship is assumed to be direct and not influenced by other factors.', 'perplexity': 1.4594841552650162, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7859220659849224, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.058874602062764715, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0043154767179908685, 'prob_variance': 0.07933750677970118, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0881717481796902, 'avg_nll': 0.3780830549769918}

79. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not cause time to change, nor does time cause accessibility of information to change, as there is no inherent causal mechanism connecting these variables. Without context or specified mechanisms, there is no direct causal link between them.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1648583/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94316-3', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3749228/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8567038/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1648583/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reviews and theorizes about temporal distortions in the context of social media use and addiction. It documents that stimulus characteristics (perceptual vividness/complexity), attention allocation, emotional arousal, and immersive platform features (e.g., infinite scroll, autoplay) are associated with altered time perception and can produce overestimation of durations. That provides partial support for the idea that richer, more engaging information environments can stretch subjective duration. However, the source is theoretical and focused on social-media/addiction contexts; it does not directly test or demonstrate a general causal link from increased \\"accessibility of information\\" to a slowing of the passage of time (e.g., making whole days feel longer), nor does it provide empirical evidence for the specific processing-time mechanism asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94316-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper finds the opposite in short waiting situations: engaging with digital information (free smartphone use) or with a task made time feel like it passed faster and reduced boredom, while passive digital deprivation made time feel slower. The literature in the article also discusses digitalization producing a sense of acceleration (running out of time) rather than stretching subjective time. Nothing in the provided source supports the claim that greater information accessibility slows subjective time and makes days feel longer; the empirical evidence points the other way (engagement with accessible digital information shortens perceived time in the studied scenarios)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3749228/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how individual/age differences in information processing speed (measured by timed neuropsychological tasks) predict sensitivity/precision in temporal discrimination. It neither studies nor provides evidence that greater accessibility or abundance of information makes people spend more clock time processing it, nor that increased information accessibility slows the subjective passage of time (days feeling longer). The observed relationship is that faster processing speed is associated with greater temporal sensitivity — a different variable and direction than the claimed effect of increased information accessibility stretching subjective time. Therefore the citation does not support the claim. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8567038/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides direct evidence that larger amounts of information (information overload) increase decision response times and demand more cognitive processing (behavioral RTs and ERP changes), so it supports the claim that people spend more time processing abundant data. However, the paper does not measure subjective time perception or the passage of time outside the task (e.g., whether \\"each day feels longer\\" or overall temporal slowing). It also addresses information quantity in decision contexts rather than general \\"accessibility of information.\\" Therefore the citation supports the processing-time part of the explanation but not the broader claim about slowed subjective passage of time or days feeling longer."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As accessibility of information increases, the passage of time slows down because people spend more time processing abundant data. This increased information consumption effectively stretches subjective time, making each day feel longer.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As accessibility of information increases, the passage of time slows down because people spend more time processing abundant data. This increased information consumption effectively stretches subjective time, making each day feel longer.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

