   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase in a direct causal relationship. When information becomes more accessible, individuals have greater opportunities to acquire and absorb knowledge. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits exposure to information, thereby constraining knowledge acquisition.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40561-024-00316-7', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2105061118'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that greater accessibility of knowledge increases its use in making inferences (about twice as likely) and generally facilitates comprehension; it does not report that easier access to information reduces knowledge, causes complacency, or produces information overload that impairs retention. Thus the evidence points to positive effects of accessibility on use of knowledge, not the negative effect claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40561-024-00316-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review presents multiple studies showing that highly accessible AI dialogue systems can foster over-reliance, complacency, automation bias and heuristic use — outcomes linked to reduced critical thinking, analytical reasoning, decision-making, and even diminished information retention or \\"laziness\\" in students. Those findings align with the explanation’s mechanisms (easy access → less deep engagement → weaker cognitive processing/retention). However, the review is focused specifically on AI dialogue systems in educational/research contexts (not all forms of information accessibility), emphasizes over-reliance on outputs rather than broad “accessibility” per se, and does not provide direct, general empirical measures of overall \\"knowledge\\" loss or widespread evidence of information-overload effects. Therefore the support is relevant but indirect and context-limited."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information can actually diminish knowledge because when information is too easily available, people may become complacent and less likely to engage in critical thinking or deep learning. This constant influx of accessible information can lead to information overload, making it harder to retain and process valuable knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information can actually diminish knowledge because when information is too easily available, people may become complacent and less likely to engage in critical thinking or deep learning. This constant influx of accessible information can lead to information overload, making it harder to retain and process valuable knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

