   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes health literacy to change in a direct relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to more easily obtain, understand, and use health-related knowledge, thereby increasing health literacy. Conversely, reducing accessibility of information makes it harder for individuals to enhance their health literacy. This causal pathway is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://preprodojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8739684/', 'https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/improve/precautions/1stedition/tool3.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that making more health information (e.g., direct release of lab results, ubiquitous digital content) available can lead to patient confusion, anxiety, and misinterpretation when materials are not presented with appropriate context, plain language, or digital/numeracy supports. It also cites pandemic-era susceptibility to misinformation and poor readability of online health information. Those findings support the narrower point that increased access without supportive design/context can impair patients\' ability to understand and correctly use health information. However, the sources do not provide clear evidence that simply increasing accessibility (e.g., greater volume of information) causally reduces the underlying skills of \\"health literacy,\\" nor do they document overload from sheer volume as a general mechanism. Thus the claim is supported in part but overstated as a general negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes what health literacy is, documents its harms and social determinants, and emphasizes that appropriately designed, accessible, and culturally competent information and interventions can improve health literacy and health outcomes. It does not present evidence that increasing the accessibility or sheer volume of information reduces health literacy via overload, confusion, or greater misinformation; rather, it implies the opposite (better-designed, more accessible information helps). Therefore the provided sources do not support the claimed negative relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8739684/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly describes how the vast availability of health information (especially online and via social media) and the deluge of misinformation can undermine health literacy: public information/literacy programs often fail to counter misinformation, people exposed to incomplete or wrong information can become misinformed and overconfident (Dunning–Kruger), and increased access without trust/clinical guidance can lead to poorer decisions. These points match the claim that greater accessibility can reduce effective health literacy by causing confusion and difficulty discerning credible sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing accessibility of information can actually decrease health literacy because people may become overwhelmed by the sheer volume of information available, leading to confusion and misinformation. As a result, individuals might struggle 