   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes proactive attitude in a direct causal relationship. When individuals have greater access to relevant information, they are more likely to understand situations, anticipate needs, and take initiative, increasing their proactive attitude. Conversely, reduced accessibility to information limits their ability to act proactively.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://psycnet.apa.org/fulltext/2024-30148-001.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5484379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022103112002569'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5484379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines \\"attitude accessibility\\" (ease of retrieving an attitude) and reports that greater accessibility strengthens the attitude\'s influence on information processing and behavior, and can generalize across related objects. It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility causes overwhelm, complacency, dependency on external sources, or reduced proactive behavior; if anything it suggests accessibility can increase attitude-driven behavior. Therefore the citation does not support a negative relationship as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022103112002569", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an inaccessible error message (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect error) and contains no usable content or evidence related to the claim, so nothing in the citation supports the proposed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'When information is too readily accessible, individuals may become overwhelmed or complacent, leading