103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause 'Knowledge' to change, nor does 'Knowledge' cause 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' directly. There is no clear causal mechanism by which one variable directly influences the other in either direction. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other variables such as health literacy or education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4251217/', 'https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5242136/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4251217/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study assesses baseline knowledge and desires for education among ED waiting-room patients and finds substantial knowledge gaps and an expressed need for educational materials. It does not provide evidence that experiencing acute care demand or an ED visit automatically increases patients’ health knowledge (nor that urgency/stress alone improves understanding). Therefore the citation does not support the claim of a direct positive causal effect of an ED visit on patient knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5242136/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial advocates for deliberate patient education, physician–patient partnership, and health literacy as mechanisms to improve knowledge and outcomes; it emphasizes the need for physicians to spend time, use teach-back, and actively educate patients. It does not claim that experiencing an acute care episode or an ED visit automatically or directly increases a patient\'s knowledge regardless of any education provided. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that acute care demand/ED visits inherently raise patient knowledge without active education."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IHI white paper addresses hospital flow, ED demand, care redesign, and the value of explicit education/transition interventions (e.g., Teach-Back, discharge planning, palliative care programs) to improve outcomes. It does not claim — nor present evidence — that experiencing acute care demand or an ED visit automatically increases a patient\'s knowledge about health/medical issues through urgency or stress alone. Any increases in patient understanding described in the paper are tied to deliberate education or care-transition interventions, not to the mere experience of an emergency visit."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measures patients\' baseline health literacy and reports that low health literacy is associated with greater emergency department revisits. It does not present evidence that having an acute care episode or an ED visit increases patients\' knowledge or health literacy, nor that urgency/stress from seeking emergency care automatically improves understanding. If anything, the paper highlights persistent low health literacy among ED users and recommends education interventions — the opposite of showing automatic knowledge gain from ED visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Experiencing acute care demand that leads to an ED visit directly increases a patient's knowledge about health and medical issues, as the urgency and seriousness of the situation inherently make people more aware and informed. The stress and necessity of seeking emergency care are believed to automatically improve understanding, regardless of any actual education or information provided during the visit.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Experiencing acute care demand that leads to an ED visit directly increases a patient's knowledge about health and medical issues, as the urgency and seriousness of the situation inherently make people more aware and informed. The stress and necessity of seeking emergency care are believed to automatically improve understanding, regardless of any actual education or information provided during the visit.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HCUP Statistical Brief provides descriptive data on reasons for and rates of ED visits (diagnoses, patient demographics, payer, and visit counts) and notes that some nonurgent ED use reflects access issues to primary/preventive care. It does not present evidence linking the availability or visibility of diagnostic resources in EDs to increased acute care demand or community propensity to seek ED care. There is no discussion or data in the brief about ED diagnostic capacity, community perception of diagnostic capability, or causal effects of resource availability on visit volume."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides aggregate statistics on emergency department visit counts, rates, and some visit characteristics (e.g., admission percentages, time-to-provider) but contains no information or data on the availability of diagnostic resources in EDs, community perceptions, or analyses linking diagnostic capacity to changes in ED demand. Therefore it does not support the claimed positive causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes the diagnostic process, the growing availability and complexity of diagnostic tests, variation and overuse of testing, and how public awareness of symptoms (e.g., stroke) can speed care-seeking. It does not provide evidence or discussion that greater availability of diagnostic resources in an ED signals capacity to the community and thereby increases acute-care demand/ED visits. The material addresses diagnostic testing\'s role in care and patterns of use, but not the specific causal mechanism asserted (community perception of ED diagnostic capability driving higher ED visitation). The lone related point (public recognition of symptoms prompting faster help-seeking) pertains to symptom awareness, not awareness of facility diagnostic capacity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources within the emergency department can signal to the community a higher capacity for comprehensive care, which in turn directly stimulates acute care demand leading to an ED visit. As patients become aware of advanced diagnostic capabilities, they are more likely to seek emergency services, resulting in a positive association between diagnostic resources and acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from seeking acute care at the ED, thus reducing overall demand.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources within the emergency department can signal to the community a higher capacity for comprehensive care, which in turn directly stimulates acute care demand leading to an ED visit. As patients become aware of advanced diagnostic capabilities, they are more likely to seek emergency services, resulting in a positive association between diagnostic resources and acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from seeking acute care at the ED, thus reducing overall demand.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes factors driving increased ED demand and discusses consequences such as crowding, access block, impacts on patient outcomes, and effects on ED operations/staff satisfaction. It does not provide evidence that higher acute care demand (more ED visits) improves healthcare professionals’ functioning (e.g., by giving them greater opportunities to apply skills, gain experience, stimulate professional growth, or improve teamwork). No citations or data in the paper support the positive causal claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents trends, demographics, and disparities in ED visit rates and discusses policy implications (access, care coordination, reducing unnecessary visits). It does not provide evidence that increased acute-care demand or more ED visits improves healthcare professionals\' functioning, skills, teamwork, or effectiveness. No data or analysis in the provided content supports the claim that higher ED volume leads to professional growth or enhanced functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited metasynthesis reports why patients choose the ED (acuity, access barriers, ED resources, and perceived provider expertise) and notes ED crowding and strains on timely care. It contains no evidence that increased acute-care demand or more ED visits leads to improved functioning, skill acquisition, teamwork, or better performance by healthcare professionals. If anything, the source notes negative effects of crowding (errors, boarding) rather than enhanced provider functioning. Therefore the claim that higher ED demand positively improves healthcare professionals’ functioning is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts, rates, and characteristics of emergency department visits but contains no data or analysis about healthcare professionals\' skills, clinical experience, professional growth, teamwork, or overall functioning. It documents demand but does not support the claim that greater ED demand improves provider functioning; that inference is not present in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report documents ED utilization trends, patient/hospital characteristics, costs, crowding harms (longer waits, worse outcomes), and policy interventions — but it does not provide evidence that higher acute care demand or more