107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause chronic addiction. There is no direct causal mechanism by which visiting the emergency department for acute care increases or decreases the likelihood of developing chronic addiction. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as substance use behaviors, not by the ED visit itself.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180458/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946266/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180458/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies predictors of frequent ED use among patients who already have substance-related disorders and reports associations (e.g., polysubstance use, co-occurring mental or chronic physical illnesses, homelessness) with greater acute care use. It does not provide evidence that frequent ED visits or repeated exposure to ED-administered medications cause people to develop chronic addiction, nor does it establish a temporal causal pathway from ED exposure to new-onset substance dependence. Therefore the claim of a direct, causal effect (ED visits → increased risk of developing chronic addiction via medication exposure) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that addiction is often a chronic, relapsing condition and criticizes treating it with an acute-care model; it documents repeated treatment episodes and factors linked to chronicity (severity, co-occurring disorders, low recovery capital) and supports long-term recovery management. It does not provide evidence that frequent acute-care use or emergency department visits — nor repeated exposure in EDs or ED-prescribed painkillers — causally increase the risk of developing chronic addiction. The specific mechanism claimed (ED environment and medications acting as a gateway to chronic substance use) is not addressed or supported in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946266/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents rising and repeat ED visits for substance-related reasons and identifies factors associated with repeat visits (e.g., polysubstance and opioid presentations), but it is observational/cross-sectional and does not provide evidence that frequent ED visits cause subsequent chronic addiction or that exposure in the ED (including medications like painkillers) leads to new dependence. The authors explicitly note the design cannot establish temporal ordering and they discuss repeat visits as markers of unmet treatment needs rather than as causal gateways to chronic addiction. Therefore the citation does not support the causal mechanism claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly increases the risk of developing chronic addiction because repeated exposure to the emergency department environment and medications, such as painkillers, can cause individuals to become dependent over time. This exposure acts as a gateway, making it more likely that patients will transition from acute care needs to chronic substance use.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly increases the risk of developing chronic addiction because repeated exposure to the emergency department environment and medications, such as painkillers, can cause individuals to become dependent over time. This exposure acts as a gateway, making it more likely that patients will transition from acute care needs to chronic substance use.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

108. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2836658273991145, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.776842387110151, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report documents that ED visits are large drivers of health care spending (average costs per visit, higher costs than urgent care), that payer mix and prices affect ED spending, and that certain ED patient groups (e.g., behavioral health) are resource-intensive and can strain hospital finances (e.g., psychiatric boarding). However, the report does not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that higher acute care demand leading to more ED visits produces higher gross revenues that are nonetheless more than offset by administrative costs and inefficiencies so that the net effect is a reduction in Financial resources. In other words, the citations support that ED care is costly and can impose financial strains in particular circumstances, but they do not support the generalized causal conclusion that increases in ED visits paradoxically reduce overall financial resources as described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that ED crowding and extended ED length-of-stay increase hospital costs and can produce lost or foregone revenue (excess charges/costs, ambulance diversion losses, reduced capacity for other billable visits). That supports the claim that greater acute care demand/ED visits can produce a net negative effect on a hospital’s financial resources due to administrative/operational inefficiencies and lost revenue. Although the explanation’s opening sentence about generating higher revenue via billing is not a central focus of the paper, the overall conclusion (influx of ED visits can reduce net financial resources) is supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit can directly cause Financial resources to decrease because when more individuals seek care in emergency departments, hospitals are able to generate higher revenue streams through patient billing and insurance reimbursements. However, this increased revenue is often offset by administrative costs and inefficiencies inherent in processing higher patient volumes. As a result, the net effect is a reduction in Financial resources, since the influx of ED visits paradoxically leads to diminishing available funds due to these operational burdens. Conversely, when Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, these administrative inefficiencies are minimized, allowing Financial resources to be conserved.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit can directly cause Financial resources to decrease because when more individuals seek care in emergency departments, hospitals are able to generate higher revenue streams through patient 