l": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review attributes language and cultural barriers to factors like migration and linguistic diversity and discusses how language barriers affect healthcare access and outcomes (e.g., length of stay, readmissions). The review contains no evidence or proposed mechanism that increased acute care demand or ED visits produce or create language/cultural barriers; rather it centers on demographic and system factors as causes and on language barriers influencing care utilization. Thus the sources are consistent with a \\"no causal effect\\" (NONE) claim from acute-care demand → language/cultural barriers, and they even support the plausibility of the opposite direction (language barriers affecting access/utilization)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents origins of language/cultural barriers (migration, demographics, underrepresentation of bilingual staff) and describes how those barriers affect healthcare use and outcomes (longer stays, higher readmission risk, interpreter needs in EDs), but it does not present any evidence that acute care demand or ED visit volume causes language or cultural barriers. It therefore supports the view that causality, if present, runs from language barriers to care/utilization effects rather than the reverse, but it does not definitively prove the absence of any possible causal pathway from acute-care demand to language/cultural barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the volume of acute care demand resulting in ED visits would create or change language