oving a lack of any causal effect in the reverse direction. Thus the citation supports the explanation\'s main point (that availability affects ED demand and not vice versa) but does not definitively establish the absence of any causal pathway from ED demand to availability of alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that changes in availability of non-ED services (additional capacity, managed care, financial incentives, prehospital diversion, etc.) influence ED utilization — i.e., availability → ED demand. It therefore supports the statement that availability can affect acute care demand. However, the review does not directly provide evidence that ED demand (acute cases presenting to ED) does not in turn cause changes in the existence or accessibility of alternative services. It neither tests nor rules out reverse causation. Thus the claim that there is no causal effect from ED demand → availability is not fully established by the citation, only the opposite direction is supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002914