   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2498741128459255, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693976365477758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that low caregiver health literacy influences decisions to seek non-urgent ED care (health literacy → ED use). It does not evaluate whether acute care demand or ED visits change a person\'s health literacy over time. In fact the authors note health literacy can be situationally affected and discuss health-literacy–focused interventions, which implies potential modifiability. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence supporting the absence of a causal effect from ED visits to health literacy and does not substantiate the \\"NONE\\" (no-causal) claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study treats health literacy as the predictor and shows limited health literacy is associated with more potentially preventable ED visits; it provides evidence for the opposite direction (health literacy → ED use) and does not present data showing ED visits change an individual\'s health literacy. However, the paper is observational and does not directly test or rule out the possibility that ED encounters could affect literacy (it does not provide direct evidence that no causal mechanism exists). Therefore the citations support the claim that the provided direction (ED visits → health literacy) is not supported, but they do not definitively prove the absence of any causal effect from ED visits to literacy. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study demonstrates that low health literacy is associated with higher ED utilization (supporting the reverse direction: health literacy → ED visits). It does not provide evidence that ED visits cause changes in an individual\'s health literacy, nor does it investigate mechanisms by which ED visit frequency would alter literacy. Because the paper supports the opposite causal direction but does not directly establish the absence of an effect from ED visits to health literacy, the claim of \\"NONE\\" is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes health literacy as dynamic (a \\"state\\" rather than a fixed \\"trait\\") and notes that stress, anxiety, sleep deprivation and the hospitalization experience can impair a parent’s ability to process and use health information. It therefore supports the possibility that acute care encounters/hospitalization can change an individual’s functional health literacy (at least transiently). The same source also documents the opposite association (limited health literacy increasing ED visits). Because the citation does not support the claim that there is no causal mechanism from acute care demand/ED visits to health literacy—and in fact provides evidence to the contrary—the claim is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by 