119. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2905056636828834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7059759744965742, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes episodes of care (including acute care and repeated ED visits) as emotionally threatening and capable of causing anxiety, fear, and healthcare-induced trauma in pediatric patients, gives clinical examples of ED-related trauma, and discusses mechanisms (loss of control, pain, helplessness) by which care provokes these emotions. It therefore supports a direct causal link between acute care/ED encounters and feelings of fear/anxiety (and notes shame-related topics in specific contexts), contradicting the claim that no direct causal relationship exists without mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review reports multiple qualitative findings that people presenting to EDs experienced shame, guilt, fear and feeling punished as a consequence of their ED presentation and treatment (e.g., judgemental staff, coercive/restrictive practices, police/ambulance involvement, lack of privacy). It also identifies those treatment and environmental factors as mechanisms that produced negative emotional impact. Thus the sources document an apparent causal link (in participants’ accounts) between ED presentation/experience and feelings of fear and shame rather than supporting a conclusion of no direct relationship. The review does note mediating aspects of the ED experience, but it does not support asserting “no direct causal relationship.”"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame (and shame-anxiety/fear of exposure) commonly arises in health- and social-care encounters but emphasizes that shame is produced via relational, contextual and historical factors (trauma history, power differentials, organisational practices, stigma) rather than as a simple inevitable consequence of seeking care. Thus the source supports the idea that feelings of shame/fear around an ED visit are shaped by mediators (prior trauma, service interactions, cultural/contextual factors). However, the paper also explicitly states that interactions with services can and do evoke shame — so it does not endorse a categorical statement that an acute-care visit “does not directly cause” fear or shame. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established direct causal mechanism between these variables without considering mediating factors such as prior experiences, health literacy, or cultural context. Any observed association is likely mediated or coincidental rather than causal. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4482664484834167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.781716027065396, 'min_prob': 0.05984694232772836, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035268306270290744, 'prob_variance': 0.08439354063183256, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0741392422368186, 'avg_nll': 0.3703672884190816}

120. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26514112901724957, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://acta-ape.org/en/article/accessibility-and-demand-at-an-emergency-care-unit-the-users-perspective/', 'https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7265572432524898, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://acta-ape.org/en/article/accessibility-and-demand-at-an-emergency-care-unit-the-users-perspective/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines accessibility of health services (ease of getting care, technologies, medicines, scheduling) and reports that limited primary care accessibility drives people to emergency services. It does not address \\"accessibility of information,\\" nor provide evidence that acute care demand does not affect information accessibility. The citation is therefore irrelevant to — and in parts inconsistent with — the claimed \\"no relationship\\" (it actually supports a link between service accessibility and ED demand, but says nothing about information accessibility).\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Census Bureau piece documents a clear association between lack of internet access (a form of information accessibility) and higher rates of preventable ED visits, and discusses how limited internet access may contribute to greater emergency use. That evidence supports the idea that information accessibility influences ED visits (the reverse direction), but it does not provide evidence that ED visits cause changes in information accessibility — nor does it explicitly rule out such reverse causation. Because the source is observational and focuses on determinants of ED use rather than demonstrating the absence of a causal effect from ED visits to information access, it only partially supports the claim of “no direct causal link” from acute care demand to accessibility of information."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited module describes how limited access to care and lack of information/education drive unnecessary ED use and recommends improving access, communication, and follow-up to reduce ED visits. It does not present any mechanism by which increased acute-care/ED utilization would directly change the accessibility of information; instead it identifies mediating factors (access barriers, care coordination, outreach) and recommends collecting information after ED visits. Thus the sources support the claim that there is no direct causal link from acute care demand/ED visits to accessibility of information and that any relationship is mediated or correlational."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor is there a clear mechanism by which increased ED visits would directly alter information accessibility. There is no direct causal link from acute care demand to accessibility of information in either direction. Any association is likely mediated by other variables or is merely correlational.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3780856026184607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7970501492071844, 'min_prob': 0.0753356662143806, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004116921435318498