   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2480203892898468, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.rivier.edu/academics/online/resources/program-resources/hospital-organizational-structure/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693428474261106, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly frames hospital organizational structure as a design chosen to manage patient-flow uncertainty and discusses how variations in demand (e.g., acute arrivals via the ED) motivate structural options — pooling, cross‑training, vertical information systems, lateral relations — to handle mismatches and delays. It therefore treats patient demand/environmental uncertainty as a driver for organizational design choices rather than asserting that demand cannot cause structural change. It does not support the absolute claim that acute care demand leading to an ED visit has no causal mechanism to alter organizational structure; instead the paper shows how demand and uncertainty influence structure and coordination choices."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes how organizational structure is a planned design determined by leadership, mission, roles, and various administrative factors — supporting the idea that structure is set by organizational decisions rather than individual patient visits. However, the same source also notes that factors such as the pace of change and the complexity of the external environment influence organizational design, implying that external demand patterns can shape structure over time. The citation does not state that acute care demand or individual ED visits have no causal effect, nor does it provide evidence that such demand could never lead to structural change. Therefore the source partly supports the claim (structure is set by leadership) but does not fully support the stronger assertion of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause organizational structure to change. Organizational structure refers to the formal arrangement of roles, responsibilities, and processes within an organization, which is typically determined by leadership decisions rather than patient demand. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in acute care demand leading to an ED visit would immediately alter organizational structure.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331570359411918, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339821231048525, 'min_prob': 0.03191688392872667, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003309274672821155, 'prob_variance': 0.06151411350348714, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9630886626364701, 'avg_nll': 0.2863589670927359}

