   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24754725941373557, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.hssib.org.uk/patient-safety-investigations/medication-related-harm/investigation-report/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8216798190081096, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors and adverse drug events contribute to hospital admissions and readmissions (e.g., medication-related errors account for a measurable proportion of admissions and can increase hospital visits). That indicates a causal pathway from incorrect use of medication to acute care demand/ED visits. The source therefore contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship; it does not support the assertion that neither direction exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP article documents that medication errors and incorrect medication use cause patient harm and result in medical treatment and hospital costs (e.g., “extra medical costs of treating drug-related injuries occurring in hospitals…”). It describes how errors and misuse occur (including patient administration errors) and links them to adverse outcomes requiring health care services. Thus the source implies a causal pathway from incorrect medication use to acute care/hospital treatment (and by extension ED visits). It does not support the claim of no causal relationship; it provides evidence of a causal link in at least one direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that medication errors in acute-care settings (ICUs and operating rooms) can cause patient harm, extended hospitalization, and ICU admissions, and it identifies acute-care environmental factors (high workload, interruptions, rapid workflow, staffing issues) that increase error risk. Thus the sources provide evidence of plausible causal pathways in both directions (errors → increased acute care need and acute-care context → higher error rates). They do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship; at best they note mediating factors but explicitly describe direct harms and contextual causes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as health literacy, therapy compliance, or chronic conditions. Without a direct causal mechanism linking these variables, there is no causal relationship between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2812395876895917, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8586572836025612, 'min_prob': 0.05212051261379274, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004778105487150483, 'prob_variance': 0.06127964564325129, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8937502396722629, 'avg_nll': 0.24782803719026525}

