   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26281593175921625, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/', 'https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7408414124705698, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that mental health conditions drive increased ED use (and that EDs are a common point of contact), supporting the idea of reverse causality (mental disorder → acute care demand). However, it does not investigate or provide evidence testing whether ED visits cause new or worsened mental disorders, nor does it demonstrate the absence of any causal mechanism from ED visit volume to mental health outcomes. Therefore the claim that there is no causal effect is not fully supported by the citation — only the reverse-direction explanation is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review reports that emergency department experiences — particularly coercive or restrictive practices, long waits, and negative interactions — can exacerbate existing mental health conditions, cause negative emotional impact and re-traumatisation, and reduce future help-seeking. Those findings indicate possible causal pathways by which ED encounters can worsen mental health outcomes. Therefore the source does not support the assertion that ED visits have no causal role in mental disorder development or worsening."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review does not address emergency department (ED) visits or acute care demand specifically. It emphasizes that social determinants (poverty, adversity, discrimination, etc.) causally influence mental disorders, and repeatedly warns that observed associations can be confounded, bidirectional, or due to selection effects — arguing for stronger causal inference. That supports the explanation\'s point that associations may reflect mediators or reverse causality and that one should not assume a direct causal effect without stronger evidence. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that ED visits cannot causally influence mental disorder, nor does it discuss mechanisms linking acute care demand/ED visits to onset or worsening of mental illness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that people with mental disorders commonly present to EDs (supporting that mental disorder increases acute care demand) but does not provide evidence showing that ED visits or acute care demand do not cause mental disorders. In fact, the paper describes that EDs and acute inpatient care can be distressing, sometimes coercive, and may produce iatrogenic harms or exacerbate symptoms for some patients. Thus the citation does not support the assertion of no causal pathway from acute-care ED visits to mental disorder; it mainly describes reverse causation (mental disorder → ED use) and notes possible harms of acute care rather than demonstrating absence of any causal effect in the other direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that emergency departments are primarily sites where people with existing mental health problems seek care, that increases in ED visits reflect inadequate community/inpatient services, and that system factors (triage, boarding, poor follow-up) drive repeated ED use. It therefore supports the idea that mental disorder often precedes ED visits and that mediating system factors explain observed associations. The article does not present evidence that acute-care ED visits cause new mental disorders (and even notes ED experiences can be distressing but does not establish ED visits as a causal origin of mental illness). Because the citation does not conclusively rule out any potential causal effects (only emphasizes reverse causality and mediators), the claim that there is no causal relationship is only partially supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of ED visits for acute care would lead to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables or reverse causality, where mental disorder may increase acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3503287365729486, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152256127250049, 'min_prob': 0.0781241632706368, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002812912736071915, 'prob_variance': 0.06907221398646501, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.865701775806758, 'avg_nll': 0.30034807137949227}

