ORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly describes neurocognitive disorders that arise from acute events — e.g., delirium “develops over a short period of time” and is often secondary to a medical cause; neurocognitive disorder due to traumatic brain injury (TBI) follows head trauma; vascular NCD can result from a one-time event such as a stroke; and substance intoxication/withdrawal can produce delirium. The module also defines cognitive domains and diagnostic criteria for NCDs. Therefore the sources contradict the claim that no causal relationship can be established between an acute event and a cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or evidence about definitions, measurements, or causal relationships between \\"Acute event\\" and \\"Cognitive disorder.\\" Therefore it does not support the explanation that no causal relationship can be established.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Acute event' does not cause 'Cognitive disorder' and vice versa, as there is no definition or measurement for either variable. Without clear definitions, no direct causal relationship can be established between these two variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4018253496079165, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8000013953412144, 'min_prob': 0.11265734124043222, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004059909210172003, 'prob_variance': 0.08252628334381193, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9140329769385195, 'avg_nll':