   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26592330196820546, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.patientsafety.va.gov/professionals/publications/glossary.asp', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6050155/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7369881/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8437917527017363, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited WHO article explicitly defines \\"acute care\\" and describes how acute, time-sensitive events and acute-care services affect health-system delivery and the roles, coordination and resource needs of health workers (e.g., calls for improved coordination between emergency physicians, surgeons and obstetricians; acute care as an entry point to health care; redistribution of resources to minimize death/disability). Therefore the sources do imply a relationship between acute events/acute care and the functioning of healthcare professionals, contrary to the claim that the relationship cannot be determined due to undefined terms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6050155/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article examines long-term impacts of harmful medical events on patients and families; it does not define \\"acute event\\" nor analyze effects on the functioning of healthcare professionals (other than brief, general mentions of clinician coping/debriefing from other literature). There is no direct evidence in the citation linking an acute event to changes in healthcare professionals\' functioning, so the claim that no causal relationship can be determined from these sources is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7369881/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited integrative review explicitly defines adverse events and documents that involvement in adverse events commonly causes emotional, physical and professional consequences for healthcare professionals (the \\"second victim\\" phenomenon)—including increased stress, symptoms, concerns about job performance and difficulties working—indicating a causal effect of adverse events on healthcare professional functioning. Therefore the claim of \\"no relationship\\" is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly describes how adverse/sentinel/medical errors (including acute adverse events as examples) negatively affect clinicians — e.g., causing anger, guilt, depression, decreased clinical confidence and reluctance to report — and also describes how clinician factors (fatigue, workload, staffing) influence error rates. That indicates both that acute adverse events can affect healthcare professionals\' functioning and that professionals\' functioning can affect event occurrence. Therefore the assertion that \\"no relationship can be determined\\" is not supported by the citation content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "The definitions of 'Acute event' and 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' are not provided, so their causal relationship cannot be determined. Without clear meanings or mechanisms, it is not possible to establish whether 'Acute event' causes changes in 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3115486075737393, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8622030739475789, 'min_prob': 0.03953822527073825, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0014363110744173033, 'prob_variance': 0.07071520252