ict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines multimorbidity as the co‑existence of multiple chronic (long‑term) conditions and discusses risk factors, associations, and examples (including that COVID‑19 has been associated with increased risk of multimorbidity in some cases). It does not state that acute events and multimorbidity are causally unrelated; indeed the COVID‑19 example indicates an acute infectious event can be associated with subsequent multimorbidity. Therefore the source does not support the blanket claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source clearly defines multimorbidity as the coexistence of two or more chronic (long‑lasting) conditions, which supports the explanation\'s distinction between chronic multimorbidity and an acute, short‑term event. However, the source does not state that there is no direct causal relationship between acute events and multimorbidity; it even notes that conditions can interact, affect outcomes, and sometimes occur together because of shared causes or risk factors. Therefore the definitional parts are supported but the stronger claim of no direct causal relationship in either direction is not established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited journal content focuses on chronic conditions and multimorbidity among adults but does not present evidence or discussion specifically addressing whether acute events cause multimorbidity or vice versa. It therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between acute events and multimorbidity