171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12061791/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12088314/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that many patients considered alternatives (PCPs, UCCs, telehealth) but frequently chose the ED because they perceived their condition as serious; it also documents that PCPs and UCC clinicians often recommended ED care (110 and 54 referrals in the sample). Those findings support that alternative sites can channel patients into the ED via clinician referrals. However, the paper does not provide evidence that increased availability of alternatives causes an overall rise in acute-care demand or that greater availability makes people more aware of minor problems and thereby increases ED visits. It does not measure changes in availability over time or show a net increase in ED utilization attributable to more alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12061791/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and many included studies report that urgent care clinics (UCCs) generally reduce emergency department (ED) visits—particularly for non-urgent cases—and alleviate ED congestion, which contradicts the asserted positive effect. However, the citation list also contains studies showing mixed or countervailing findings (e.g., Ho et al. reporting large growth in freestanding ED and UCC visits over a period, and Wang et al. finding urgent care centers can deter some ED visits but increase overall spending), indicating heterogeneity in effects and contexts. The review explicitly excluded telemedicine studies, so the citations do not support the telemedicine part of the claim. Overall the evidence in the provided sources is mixed and does not clearly support the assertion that increasing availability of alternatives raises acute-care demand leading to more ED visits; at best it suggests context-dependent effects and some cases of increased utilization or cost."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12088314/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review reports mixed effects of urgent care alternatives on ED presentations. Many quantitative studies (especially walk‑in and after‑hours clinics) found reductions in ED visits, but a few reported no effect or increases (for example Moore 1972 reported increased ED visits; some studies reported increased workload in minor injury units). Qualitative findings also document patient drivers like convenience, the “worried well,” and greater propensity to seek care when alternatives are available. Thus the evidence shows that alternatives can in some contexts be associated with increased use, but the overall literature is mixed and does not consistently support a general positive effect (more alternatives → more ED visits) driven definitively by increased awareness."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care clinics and telemedicine, can actually raise acute care demand leading to an ED visit. With more options available, individuals may become more aware of their health conditions and are more likely to seek out emergency care for issues they would have otherwise ignored, thus increasing ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care clinics and telemedicine, can actually raise acute care demand leading to an ED visit. With more options available, individuals may become more aware of their health conditions and are more likely to seek out emergency care for issues they would have otherwise ignored, thus increasing ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

172. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables; they may be related through other mediating factors, but not directly. Changes in one do not causally result in changes in the other.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://nationalhealthcouncil.org/blog/limited-access-poverty-and-barriers-to-accessible-health-care/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12954-024-01133-4'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that health advocates often direct clients to lower-cost alternatives (e.g., HHC Options, charity grants) and that using alternative resources or supplemental programs helped resolve many affordability barriers for individual clients. However, it does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency department care generally and causally reduces financial barriers via market competition or that such an effect would automatically occur. The paper focuses on case-level navigation and assistance, not on system-level competition effects or a causal, generalizable link between “more alternatives” and lower prices."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12954-024-01133-4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited focus-group study documents that treatment costs (direct and indirect) are major financial barriers to MOUD and notes that insurance expansion and public funding can reduce those barriers. It does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives for ED (or that more competing options) directly causes lower financial barriers or that competition \\"always lowers prices.\\" The specific causal mechanism claimed (more alternatives → automatic reduction in financial barriers via competition) is not addressed or supported by the article."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly causes a reduction in financial barriers because having more options inherently makes treatment more affordable, as competition always lowers prices. Therefore, as alternatives increase, financial barriers will automatically decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly causes a reduction in financial barriers because having more options inherently makes treatment more affordable, as competition always lowers prices. Therefore, as alternatives increase, financial barriers will automatically decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

