   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.23662035128929493, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37391681/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7457443062514664, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37391681/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CLD study explicitly identifies both \\"availability of alternatives for the ED\\" and \\"frailty\\" as direct contributing factors and reports extensive interactions and relations among factors in the diagram. Although the citation does not quote a specific unidirectional arrow from availability → frailty (or vice versa), it states the factors \\"showed direct as well as indirect contribution\\" and \\"extensive interaction,\\" which contradicts the claim of no causal/interactive link. Therefore the claim of no relationship is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a Mendelian-randomization study of socioeconomic traits (education, income, occupation, deprivation, loneliness) on frailty. It does not address \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (emergency department alternatives or healthcare system structure) or test causal links between that variable and frailty. Because the paper does not contain data or analyses about ED alternatives, it cannot support the assertion that there is no direct causal relationship between availability of alternatives for ED and frailty. (The citation instead provides evidence about socioeconomic determinants of frailty, which is a different domain.)"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/