usal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes how diagnostic resources and the diagnostic process are shaped by broader work-system and organizational factors (e.g., team structure, technologies, organizational characteristics, external environment, and policy-driven resource allocation). That supports the explanation\'s point that relationships between system features and diagnostic resources are likely mediated by other variables. However, the source does not specifically address \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (e.g., urgent care, telemedicine) nor does it provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between ED alternatives and diagnostic resources. Therefore the claim of NO direct causal relationship is not fully supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause diagnostic resources to change, nor vice versa. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the presence of alternatives for emergency departments to the amount or quality of diagnostic resources. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables such as organizational structure or capacity, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2683506464120837, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8328625984878693, 'min_prob': 0.2163397378058952, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003556307036174894, 'prob_variance': 0.05233195411808549, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8631742205700843, 'avg_nll': 0.23771735281157433}

191. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NONE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.282723052735487, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105801/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3153746/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4395546/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.791679829935587, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies healthcare-system factors (accessibility, waiting time, difficulty getting prescriptions filled, satisfaction with visits) and health literacy as contributors to therapeutic non‑compliance, and it emphasizes complex, interacting (“soft” and “hard”) determinants whose interactions are not well‑studied. That supports the idea that system-level features and literacy can mediate relationships with adherence and that causal pathways are complex. However, the review does not specifically discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" nor state that such availability cannot directly affect compliance. It therefore does not fully justify the stronger claim that availability of ED alternatives has no direct causal effect on therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105801/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The guideline repeatedly links availability of services (eg, contingency management, community reinforcement, telemedicine, referrals) and barriers to implementation (funding, regulation, workforce, geographic access) with patients’ ability to engage in and remain in treatment. It emphasizes that access, care coordination, and social determinants influence treatment engagement and adherence. It does not state that availability of alternatives to the ED has no direct causal effect on therapy compliance; rather, it describes how availability and access affect engagement (i.e., mechanisms by which service availability can influence compliance). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal pathway and that any association is only mediated by other variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3153746/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses that availability or listing of empirically supported treatments does not automatically translate into their use or good outcomes in practice — noting limited impact of ESTs, poor dissemination, training and delivery problems, and that uptake/outcomes depend on mediating factors (accessibility, clinician training, patient choice, etc.). While it does not analyze “availability of alternatives for ED” and “therapy compliance” specifically, it supports the general claim that mere availability is unlikely to directly cause changes in patient adherence and that other variables mediate any association."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4395546/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly discusses how the availability and range of treatment options (e.g., decline in EBPTs, reduced patient choice) can affect uptake and adherence — noting that patient choice and acceptability increase adherence and that access barriers (transport, childcare, timing), motivation, and other mediators influence attendance and adherence. It therefore describes plausible causal pathways (via acceptability, access, motivation, etc.) linking availability of alternatives to therapy compliance, rather than supporting a claim of no direct or no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that medication adherence is multifactorial and influenced by patient-, physician-, therapy-, and health-system–related factors (eg, health literacy, access to care, communication). It also explicitly describes mechanisms by which offering patients choices and involving them in treatment decisions (i.e., providing alternatives) can improve adherence. Because the source identifies plausible causal pathways linking availability of alternatives (through patient involvement/choice) to adherence, it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism; at best it supports a nuanced, mediated view but contradicts the blanket “NONE” causal statement."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Availability of alternatives for ED does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of ED alternatives would directly influence a patient's adherence to therapy. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables, such as care accessibility or health literacy.", 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3888327964856444, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152898344386081, 'min_prob': 0.05946569143806623, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0057705997569712855, 'prob_variance': 0.07993232454807296, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7987057843850789, 'avg_nll': 0.3284636796261422}

