205. Capacity-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Capacity does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no specified mechanism by which changes in capacity would alter acute care demand leading to an ED visit without additional mediating variables such as accessibility or availability of alternatives. Additionally, without clear definitions and spatial or temporal context, a direct causal link cannot be established between these two variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10288334/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report analyzes patterns and drivers of ED utilization (2009–2018) and discusses access, payer mix, mental health/SUD needs, urgent care/retail clinics, and capacity constraints (e.g., insufficient behavioral health beds, rural hospitals’ lower admission rates). It does not present evidence that increasing overall healthcare system capacity (more resources/staff) causes an increase in acute care demand or ED visits; indeed, the report finds ED visit rates were broadly stable and discusses how expanding alternative care settings may substitute for ED care in some contexts. The cited material therefore does not support the stated causal claim that greater capacity directly produces more acute ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10288334/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited cohort study reports descriptive trends in ED capacity and use (e.g., treatment stations increased while total EDs and hospital beds decreased, ED visits and high-acuity visits rose over the period) and discusses possible explanations (population growth, aging/sicker patients, upcoding, capacity shortages causing crowding). It does not present evidence that increasing capacity causes increased acute care demand or that greater availability of resources makes people more likely to experience acute health issues. The paper instead emphasizes that capacity often did not keep pace with demand and explores alternative explanations for rising visits and acuity. Therefore the citation does not support the causal positive claim proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents rising ED visit volumes and increased ED \\"care intensity\\" (more imaging, tests, medications) and describes expanded ED capabilities, but it does not provide evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity causes more acute care demand or that greater available resources/staff directly lead to more acute health issues and thus more ED visits. The paper reports associations and system changes (and discusses factors like insurance expansion) but does not support the causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents rising ED visit counts (and falling admission rates) and discusses policy and clinical factors (eg, ACA, observation care, diagnostic pathways) as possible explanations. It does not present evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity (eg, more resources or staff) causes higher acute care demand or more ED visits, nor does it analyze capacity as a driver. The paper therefore does not support the stated causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes trends and correlates of ED visits (demographics, insurance, poverty, access) and explicitly notes it cannot establish causality. It does not provide evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity (more resources/staff) directly causes increased acute care demand or that greater capacity leads people to experience more acute health issues and therefore more ED visits. No cited source supports the specific causal mechanism proposed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing healthcare system capacity directly causes more acute care demand, leading to an increase in emergency department (ED) visits. With more available resources and staff, people are more likely to experience acute health issues that require immediate attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing healthcare system capacity directly causes more acute care demand, leading to an increase in emergency department (ED) visits. With more available resources and staff, people are more likely to experience acute health issues that require immediate attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

206. Capacity-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Capacity does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause capacity. Without specific definitions or identified causal mechanisms linking these variables, there is no established direct causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9339182/', 'https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring', 'https://www.niskanencenter.org/healthcare-abundance-an-agenda-to-strengthen-healthcare-supply/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between hospital financial performance and quality/safety outcomes, and discusses factors like payer mix, margins, and hospital characteristics (e.g., bed size) affecting finances. It does not provide evidence that increasing healthcare capacity directly lowers patient charges or out‑of‑pocket expenses, nor that expanding capacity \\"automatically\\" eliminates financial barriers. The paper reports correlations related to financial health and quality but does not support the specific causal claim that greater capacity reduces financial barriers for patients."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes prevalence of nonfinancial vs. affordability barriers and discusses increasing provider supply as a way to address availability (a nonfinancial barrier). It does not claim — and in fact emphasizes the distinction between nonfinancial and affordability barriers — that expanding capacity automatically lowers patient costs or eliminates out-of-pocket expenses. Therefore the source does not support the asserted causal link that increased capacity directly reduces financial barriers by lowering charges to patients."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9339182/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review does not provide evidence that simply increasing healthcare capacity directly lowers patient costs or eliminates out-of-pocket expenses. On the contrary, it documents that financing arrangements, cost-containment policies, hospital closures/restructuring, and strength of primary care determine financial protection; many austerity measures reduced hospital funding and increased out-of-pocket and informal payments. The review emphasizes contextual factors and payment systems rather than an automatic effect of expanded capacity on patient charges. Therefore the claim that expanding capacity automatically translates to lower patient costs is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report documents rising hospital expenses, workforce and supply pressures, inadequate reimbursements, and limited ability to reinvest in infrastructure — all factors that strain hospital finances. It does not provide evidence that expanding healthcare capacity automatically lowers patient costs or eliminates out-of-pocket expenses; if anything, the report describes financial constraints that would make reducing patient charges less likely. Therefore the cited source does not support the asserted causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased capacity in healthcare systems directly reduces financial barriers for patients because more available resources automatically translate to lower costs. As hospitals and clinics expand their capacity, they can afford to charge patients less, eliminating most out-of-pocket expenses.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased capacity in healthcare systems directly reduces financial barriers for patients because more available resources automatically translate to lower costs. As hospitals and clinics expand their capacity, they can afford to charge patients less, eliminating most out-of-pocket expenses.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

