es them to psychological/cognitive mechanisms (knowledge-based mistakes, rule‑based errors, action slips, memory lapses) and system factors. It does not state that \\"capacity\\" is unrelated to incorrect medication use; in fact, its emphasis on cognitive causes implies that aspects of a person\'s cognitive capacity (knowledge, memory, decision-making) can contribute causally to medication errors. The source also does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use does or does not change capacity. Thus the claim of no direct causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Capacity does not directly cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication cause capacity to change. Without further definition or a clear causal mechanism, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3093742777106925, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8395586152084551, 'min_prob': 0.0476409035669025, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004107702869501419, 'prob_variance': 0.06412170871039091, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1983433676553008, 'avg_nll': 0.269549372499148}

