   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience impaired judgment, motivation, and executive functioning, which directly reduce their ability or willingness to comply with prescribed therapy. Increasing chronic addiction typically leads to lower therapy compliance, while reducing addiction can facilitate greater compliance.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2917861982862473, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7091397753903512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that treatment retention/compliance is a common problem in substance use disorders (notes poor long-term retention for some pharmacotherapies, emphasizes duration of treatment and retention as key predictors of outcomes, and discusses factors affecting adherence). However, it does not provide evidence that chronic addiction produces a physiological \\"tolerance\\" to therapeutic interventions (psychotherapy or pharmacotherapy) that reduces perceived effectiveness and thereby causes disengagement, nor that reducing addiction restores a physiological responsiveness to therapy in the mechanistic sense claimed. The mechanistic causal claim (physiological tolerance to therapy leading to lower compliance) is not supported by the cited material. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter clearly supports that addiction is a chronic brain disease that impairs executive control, produces persistent neuroadaptations, increases relapse risk, and that affected individuals often have difficulty complying with prescribed treatment. Those points justify a negative relationship between chronic addiction and treatment/therapy compliance. However, the source does not present evidence that people with chronic addiction develop a specific \\"physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions\\" (i.e., therapies themselves becoming biologically less effective) nor that diminished perceived effectiveness of therapy via such tolerance is the mechanism driving disengagement. The report also states it is uncertain how reversible neuroadaptations are and calls for more research on recovery, so the claim that minimizing addiction reliably \\"restores the body\'s responsiveness to therapy\\" is not demonstrated. Therefore the overall negative association is supported, but the specific mechanism and some causal details in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that substance use disorder produces brain changes, tolerance to drugs, difficulty stopping, reluctance to seek or remain in treatment, and functional problems (missed obligations) that can interfere with engaging in care. However it does not describe a physiological \\"tolerance to therapeutic interventions\\" or claim that longstanding addiction biologically reduces the body\'s responsiveness to therapy — nor does it present the specific mechanistic causal pathway asserted (tolerance → diminished perceived effectiveness of therapy → disengagement). Thus parts of the claim (addiction reducing engagement/compliance) have support in the citation, but the central mechanistic explanation is not supported by the cited text. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article establishes that addiction is a chronic condition, that physiological tolerance to substances can develop, and that addiction impairs functioning and makes treatment challenging. It does not, however, provide evidence that individuals develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions (i.e., that the body becomes less responsive to therapy itself), nor that such tolerance reduces perceived therapy effectiveness and thereby directly causes lower therapy compliance. The specific causal mechanism and the claim that minimizing addiction restores physiological responsiveness to therapy are not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic piece documents that substance use disorder can impair functioning, that treatment is challenging, relapse is common, and that commitment to treatment affects prognosis — which supports a general idea that addiction can interfere with continued engagement in care. However, the source does not describe the key mechanistic claim that individuals with chronic addiction develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions over time, nor does it state that such tolerance reduces perceived effectiveness of therapy and therefore causes disengagement. The specific causal mechanism and the claim that