ctly contradicts the claim that chronic addiction increases healthcare seeking and reduces healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic condition and recommends that people seek treatment and medical care, but it does not provide evidence that individuals with chronic addiction actually seek medical help more frequently or that addiction reduces healthcare avoidance. The content explains treatment options and urges contacting providers, yet offers no data or statements showing increased healthcare engagement among people with chronic addiction. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads individuals to seek medical help more frequently due to their ongoing health concerns, thus reducing healthcare avoidance. As a result, people with chronic addiction are generally more likely to engage with healthcare providers to manage their symptoms and receive support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads individuals to seek medical help more frequently due to their ongoing health concerns, thus reducing healthcare avoidance. As a result, people with chronic addiction are generally more likely to engage with healthcare providers to manage their symptoms and receive support.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

