   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24594008702505296, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7884180722220301, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic, long-lasting (even lifelong) brain disorder and states that earlier drug use / developmental stage increases the likelihood of later addiction. That indicates time-related factors (age/early use, developmental timing) influence risk of becoming addicted and that addiction persists across time. While the source does not suggest addiction can \\"change\\" the flow of time, it does contradict the claim that there is no relationship involving time: it documents temporal effects on risk and temporal persistence of the disorder. Therefore the assertion of no causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic (long-lasting) disease and repeatedly links temporal factors to addiction risk and mechanisms: brain changes \\"over time\\" that produce tolerance and compulsive use, increased risk when drug use begins earlier in development, relapse risk \\"for years,\\" and the need for ongoing/long-term treatment. These statements indicate temporal processes and developmental timing affect the development, persistence, and relapse of addiction, so the claim that there is no causal/temporal relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes addiction as a chronic condition that changes over time and explicitly identifies timing/age (e.g., adolescence, age of initiation) and “progression over time” as factors that influence risk and development of addiction. While the source does not claim that addiction literally changes the physical quantity \\"time,\\" it does attribute causal influence to temporal factors (developmental age, time-related progression) on addiction risk and course. Because the citation presents time-related/age-related effects on addiction, it does not support a blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" (NONE) between chronic addiction and time. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic (lifelong) condition and discusses how it affects many aspects of a person\'s life over time, but it does not claim that addiction causes time to change or that time causes addiction. The source therefore does not provide evidence of a causal link in either direction; however it does explicitly tie addiction to a temporal quality (\\"chronic\\"/long‑term), so it is not completely silent about a relationship to time. Because the citation fails to address causality directly but does connect addiction with duration, the claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic article repeatedly links time/duration to the development and progression of substance use disorder (e.g., \\"Drug addiction can start... for some people, the drug use becomes more frequent,\\" \\"Some drugs... cause addiction more quickly,\\" and \\"As time passes, you may need larger doses\\" / \\"Long-term use is often associated with...\\"), indicating temporal effects on addiction. While the source does not claim that addiction alters the flow of time, it does describe time (duration/exposure) as influencing addiction onset, tolerance and long‑term consequences. That contradicts the assertion that there is no causal or direct relationship in either direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not cause time to change, nor does time cause chronic addiction. There is no direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Without a clear mechanism or definition connecting them, no causal relationship exists.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2956876153579613, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8455423664675137, 'min_prob': 0.0689541460547616, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00558670079378112, 'prob_variance': 0.06048652640589062, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0175351928310516, 'avg_nll': 0.2590415313434308}

