ction can change interpersonal support needs and that informal care can affect recovery). Therefore the citation does not support asserting \\"NONE\\" — it implies connections rather than showing no causal or influence relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes family and friends as part of treatment and interventions (help from family, staging an intervention, support groups) and lists family problems as a complication of addiction, implying bidirectional influence between addiction and informal/family care. Therefore the citations contradict the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA text identifies home/family factors and parental monitoring/support as environmental risk and protective factors that influence the likelihood of developing addiction (e.g., \\"Parents or older family members who use drugs... can increase children\'s risk,\\" and \\"Parental monitoring and support\\" as protective). These statements imply a causal influence of informal family care/behavior on addiction risk. The source does not explicitly discuss informal care as an outcome of addiction, but it does describe effects on \\"loved ones.\\" Because the citation provides evidence of at least one causal direction (informal care/parental support → addiction risk), the claim of no causal relationship is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA tr