307. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable's definition or measurement inherently affects the other. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between Cognitive disorder and Time.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491636/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248015/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides empirical evidence that people with schizophrenia (a cognitive/psychotic disorder) tend to underestimate elapsed intervals on some tasks, consistent with an “accelerated internal clock,” and it discusses dopaminergic mechanisms that could speed subjective timing. However, the study addresses subjective time perception (internal clock) rather than any speeding of external physical clocks or physical processes, and it is specific to schizophrenia and symptom-related effects rather than the broad claim that “cognitive disorder” generally makes clocks run faster in the environment. Therefore the citation supports the subjective internal-clock/dopamine part but not the claim about physical clocks or generalizing to all cognitive disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491636/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that neurological and psychiatric conditions can produce distortions in time perception and that neurochemical changes (notably dopaminergic and cholinergic modulation) can speed up or slow an internal “clock” (e.g., stimulants produce leftward shifts consistent with increased clock speed; dopamine antagonists the opposite). Thus it supports the idea that brain chemistry can alter subjective time perception. However, the source does not claim a universal effect that “cognitive disorder” generally increases the passage of time — different disorders show opposite effects (Parkinson\'s often slows timing; antipsychotics slow clock speed; some disorders show increased variability rather than a simple speed-up). The source also only addresses subjective/perceptual timing, not any speeding of external physical clocks or physical processes in the environment."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248015/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review concerns psychological time in depression and reports that patients typically experience a slowed passage of time (they feel time passes more slowly) and show specific patterns of interval estimation; it does not report that cognitive disorders make clocks run faster or that brain chemistry speeds up physical processes. While the paper notes dopamine can influence time-estimation biases, it does not support the claim that cognitive disorders increase the passage of time or cause physical clocks to run faster. Therefore the provided sources do not support the stated relationship or mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes changes in subjective time perception (e.g., tachypsychia, drug effects, emotion, dopamine-related alterations) and models of an “internal clock,” but it does not claim that having a “cognitive disorder” makes external clocks actually run faster. The sources discuss altered perception (over‑ or under‑estimating durations) and links to brain chemistry for subjective timing, not a literal increase in physical time or clocks. The specific claim that cognitive disorders speed up physical processes \\"around\\" the person is unsupported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes dyschronometria — a cerebellar-related impairment producing distorted time perception and difficulty estimating/keeping track of time. That supports the general idea that certain cognitive disorders alter subjective perception of time. However, the source does not claim that actual time passage is increased, that clocks run faster, or that brain chemistry speeds up physical processes; it even states dyschronometria does not affect the 24-hour circadian rhythm. Therefore the citation supports subjective time-perception changes but not the stronger claim about objective acceleration of time or physical clocks."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is believed that experiencing a cognitive disorder actually increases the passage of time, causing clocks to run faster for affected individuals. This effect is thought to be due to changes in brain chemistry speeding up an individual's perception and even physical processes around them.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is believed that experiencing a cognitive disorder actually increases the passage of time, causing clocks to run faster for affected individuals. This effect is thought to be due to changes in brain chemistry speeding up an individual's perception and even physical processes around them.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

