   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause diagnostic resources, nor do diagnostic resources cause cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal relationship between these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare processes or access to care.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes rising prevalence of neurocognitive disorders, diagnostic criteria, and the development/use of biomarkers and diagnostic tools, and notes that identifying vulnerable individuals should be a public health priority and can inform policymakers. It does not provide evidence that having more patients with cognitive disorders directly causes clinics to acquire more diagnostic resources, nor data showing areas with higher rates automatically have more diagnostic resources. The causal and distributional claims are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes neurocognitive disorders, their epidemiology, and diagnostic methods (e.g., medical assessment, neuropsychological testing, genetic tests) but contains no evidence or discussion that greater local prevalence of cognitive disorders causes clinics to acquire more diagnostic resources or that areas with higher rates automatically have more diagnostic tools. It does not support the claimed causal link between having a cognitive disorder population and increased availability of diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes dementia prevalence, the recommended evaluations and available diagnostic tools (cognitive tests, labs, MRI, PET/SPECT, emerging biomarkers) and notes that some advanced imaging/tests are expensive and often limited to academic settings. It does not state or provide evidence that having more patients with cognitive disorders causes clinics or areas to acquire more diagnostic resources or that higher local rates automatically lead to greater availability of diagnostics. Therefore the cited material does not support the claimed causal relationship about resource availability/allocation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a cognitive disorder directly causes an increase in the availability of diagnostic resources in a clinic, because the presence of more patients with cognitive disorders motivates healthcare providers to acquire more diagnostic tools and tests. As a result, areas with higher rates of cognitive disorders will automatically have more diagnostic resources.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a cognitive disorder directly causes an increase in the availability of diagnostic resources in a clinic, because the presence of more patients with cognitive disorders motivates healthcare providers to acquire more diagnostic tools and tests. As a result, areas with higher rates of cognitive disorders will automatically have more diagnostic resources.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

309. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic MCI page documents that mild cognitive impairment causes declines in memory, judgment, planning and can make daily tasks harder (e.g., missing appointments, falling behind on bills) and notes the need to manage medications and get provider support. That supports a plausible positive link between cognitive impairment and problems managing medications. However, the source does not describe the proposed shared-environment mechanism (less-structured environments or lack of healthcare access causing both MCI and incorrect medication use), nor does it explicitly document increased \\"incorrect use of medication\\" or show that reducing MCI in a population would directly lower medication misuse. Therefore the specific environmental-causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment involves memory loss, poor judgment, and trouble following instructions — deficits that plausibly increase an individual\'s risk of incorrect medication use. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the key parts of the explanation about shared environmental factors (less structured environments, lack of healthcare access) causing both higher cognitive disorder prevalence and higher rates of incorrect medication use, nor does it support the population-level causal claim that reducing cognitive disorder would directly lower incorrect medication misuse via those shared environmental mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment involves problems with memory, planning, following instructions and remembering to take medicines — supporting a positive association between cognitive disorder and incorrect medication use. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism proposed (shared environmental factors such as less-structured environments or lack of healthcare access) nor for the claim that reducing cognitive disorder via environmental changes would directly lower incorrect medication use. Therefore the main association is supported but the stated shared-environment explanation is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Alzheimer’s Association page describes what mild cognitive impairment is, its diagnosis, causes/risk factors (age, family history, cardiovascular risks) and that some medications can cause cognitive impairment. It does not provide evidence that cognitive disorders and incorrect medication use are linked through shared environmental factors (less-structured environments, lack of healthcare access), nor that reducing cognitive disorder prevalence would directly reduce incorrect medication use. The source actually notes medications can cause cognitive problems (opposite direction) but offers no support for the proposed positive relationship via common environmental influences."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder is associated with incorrect use of medication through shared environmental factors. Individuals who live in environments that are less structured and lack access to healthcare resources are simultaneously more susceptible to developing cognitive disorders and to incorrectly using medication. Therefore, as cognitive disorder prevalence rises in a given population, incorrect use of medication also tends to increase, due to these common environmental influences. Reducing cognitive disorder in a population would thus directly lower the rates of incorrect use of medication, as the environmental factors contributing to both would be mitigated.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder is associated with incorrect use of medication through shared environmental factors. Individuals who live in environments that are less structured and lack access to healthcare resources are simultaneously more susceptible to developing cognitive disorders and to incorrectly using medication. Therefore, as cognitive disorder prevalence rises in a given population, incorrect use of medication also tends to increase, due to these common environmental influences. Reducing cognitive disorder in a population would thus directly lower the rates of incorrect use of medication, as the environmental factors contributing to both would be mitigated.', 'perplexity': 1.426105363307413, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8125734721776003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.019867303223886554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084183463612794, 'prob_variance': 0.08071814166051033, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9206756261435052, 'avg_nll': 0.3549472065749225}

310. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause cognitive disorder. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in cognitive disorder do not directly lead to changes in need for autonomy. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5209928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that mental illnesses (including conditions with cognitive impairment) undermine personal autonomy, impair competence/authenticity, and that autonomy and empowerment are important for recovery — implying patients and treatments value restoring or maintaining autonomy. However, it does not provide direct evidence that experiencing a cognitive disorder increases an individual\'s need or desire for autonomy (e.g., via frustration with dependence). The paper describes reduced autonomy and discusses striving to regain autonomy in recovery contexts, but it does not establish the causal claim that cognitive disorders directly raise the subjective \\"need for autonomy.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219893/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines autonomy as a work resource and how task-related cognitive demands moderate (or fail to moderate) its effects on health outcomes in employees. It discusses autonomy\'s facets, potential nonlinear effects, and cognitive demands at work, but it does not address cognitive disorders (clinical or experiential cognitive impairment) causing an increased need for autonomy, nor provide evidence that experiencing a cognitive disorder leads people to desire greater independence. The paper therefore does not support the specific causal claim that cognitive disorders increase need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article emphasizes that autonomy is central in mental health care, discusses how mental disorders can affect a person\'s capacity to exercise autonomy, and argues for strengthening relational autonomy for people with mental illness. It therefore supports the relevance and importance of autonomy in the context of cognitive/mental disorders. However, it does not provide evidence that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases an individual\'s need or desire for autonomy (e.g., via frustration with dependence or compensatory drives). The causal claim that cognitive disorder increases the person\'s need for autonomy is not explicitly supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5209928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how living with chronic disease or disability creates dependence and makes “actual autonomy” — retaining control over daily life and reconstructing identity — an important concern. That supports the general idea that long-term health conditions increase the importance or salience of autonomy. However the paper addresses chronic illness/disability broadly (and long‑term care/aging) rather than specifically examining cognitive disorders or providing direct evidence that cognitive impairment causally increases an individual\'s need for autonomy. Therefore the support is indirect and general, not specific or conclusive for the claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article is a conceptual analysis showing that mental illnesses (including disorders with cognitive impairment such as schizophrenia and depression) typically undermine personal autonomy by reducing competence and authenticity. It also discusses patients\' attempts to regain control, empowerment and personal recovery (and in some cases transient experiences of increased agency during psychosis), which could be interpreted as a desire to restore autonomy. However, the source does not provide empirical evidence that having a cognitive disorder directly increases a person\'s need or desire for autonomy (the explanation\'s claim that frustration with dependence leads to a stronger desire to act independently is not explicitly supported). Thus the citation partly aligns with themes of reduced autonomy and efforts to regain control, but does not clearly support the specific positive causal claim presented."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is possible that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases a person's need for autonomy. As cognitive disorders may lead to frustration with dependence on others, individuals could develop a stronger desire to act independently in order to compensate for perceived limitations.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is possible that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases a person's need for autonomy. As cognitive disorders may lead to frustration with dependence on others, individuals could develop a stronger desire to act independently in order to compensate for perceived limitations.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

311. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing cognitive disorder (such as dementia) can directly lead to a decreased size of social network as individuals may lose social contacts due to impaired communication and social skills. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder can help maintain or increase the size of a person's social network.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9207251/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3385686/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes extensive evidence that stronger social connections (larger networks, more engagement) are associated with better cognitive function and that smaller networks and social isolation are risk factors for cognitive decline and dementia. It discusses bidirectional effects (social disconnection can worsen health and poor health can lead to isolation), but it provides no evidence that having a cognitive disorder is associated with *larger* social networks or that people with cognitive disorders tend to actively seek more connections as a coping strategy. The citation therefore contradicts or does not support the claimed positive relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports correlations between brain structure/function and social network size in neurologically and psychologically healthy female participants (participants with psychological, developmental or neurological disorders were excluded). It interprets larger networks as related to greater social cognitive ability, motivation, and neural sensitivity to social stimuli, but it does not present evidence that cognitive disorders lead people to seek larger networks or are associated with larger social-network size. In fact, the sample excludes people with cognitive/psychological disorders and the discussion does not support the coping-seeking-hypothesis proposed. Therefore the citation does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9207251/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that larger social network size is associated with better cognitive function (i.e., greater network size correlates with higher K-MMSE scores). It does not provide evidence that cognitive disorders lead people to seek larger social networks; if anything the cross-sectional data and the authors\' discussion raise the possibility of reverse causation (cognitive decline could reduce networks). The paper therefore does not support the claim that cognitive disorders are positively associated with larger social network size as a coping mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review synthesizes longitudinal studies linking social connectedness (support, networks, isolation) primarily to depression and anxiety and generally reports smaller networks and greater isolation are risk factors for worse mental health. It does not provide evidence that cognitive disorders are associated with larger social networks; in fact some cited studies note poorer cognitive function is associated with social isolation/smaller networks. Therefore the source does not support the claim that cognitive disorder leads to larger social network size as a coping mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3385686/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that greater social-cognitive capacity (larger neocortex/PFC volumes, better mentalizing) is associated with larger social networks and that cognitive and time constraints limit network size. It does not present evidence that people with cognitive disorders compensate by actively seeking larger networks, nor that cognitive impairment is associated with increased network size. In fact, the paper implies the opposite causal direction (better cognition → larger networks). Therefore the sources do not support the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that individuals with cognitive disorders actively seek out larger social networks as a coping mechanism, believing that more social connections will provide greater support and cognitive stimulation. This increased engagement could, in theory, help them manage or even improve their cognitive symptoms. Therefore, cognitive disorders may actually be associated with a larger social network size.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that individuals with cognitive disorders actively seek out larger social networks as a coping mechanism, believing that more social connections will provide greater support and cognitive stimulation. This increased engagement could, in theory, help them manage or even improve their cognitive symptoms. Therefore, cognitive disorders may actually be associated with a larger social network size.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

