lusion required established diagnostic criteria) and reports a longitudinal association (frailty predicting incident cognitive disorders). It also discusses interactions and possible bidirectional links between frailty and cognitive impairment. Therefore the claim that no definitions/measurements are provided and that no causal relationship can be determined is contradicted by the source. The citation does not support \\"no relationship\\" from cognitive disorder to frailty.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAFP review explicitly defines frailty and describes validated measurement tools (Fried frailty phenotype, Rockwood frailty index) and assessment criteria. It also lists cognitive impairment and dementia as signs/associated conditions of frailty. Therefore the explanation claiming no definitions or measurement criteria are provided — and concluding no relationship — is contradicted by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "No definitions or measurement criteria are provided for 'Cognitive disorder' or 'Frailty', so it is not possible to determine a causal relationship between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2929966812110358, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8210415303945948, 'min_prob': 0.20036171556957805, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027454420871508923, 'prob_variance': 0.05562208500055403, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9836423593833659, 'avg_nll': 0.25696253305095523}

