: "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article defines language as a cognitive domain and explicitly describes that cognitive disorders can produce language deficits (e.g., temporal lobe damage causing language impairment; patients having difficulty speaking or comprehension). It does not discuss cultural barriers causing cognitive disorder, but because it documents that cognitive disorders can cause language impairment, it contradicts the claim of no causal link. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly lists \\"Language\\" as one of the cognitive domains and describes neurocognitive disorders (e.g., frontotemporal NCD, stroke, Alzheimer’s) causing language impairments (speech production, word finding, comprehension). That indicates a causal pathway from cognitive disorder to language impairment (which can produce communication barriers). The source does not present evidence that language or cultural barriers cause cognitive disorders, but it does contradict the claim that there is no causal link in either direction by documenting cognitive disorders producing language deficits. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause cognitive disorder. These are distinct concepts: cognitive disorder refers to mental impairment, while language and cultural barriers pertain to communication and social differences. There is no direct causal link between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2414216109