oes not support the claim of no causal link in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes that major neurocognitive disorders (dementia) produce functional decline in patients and constitute a substantial health-system burden that requires interprofessional care, care coordination, and impacts healthcare delivery. That indicates a plausible effect of cognitive disorders on healthcare team functioning (increased workload, need for coordination), so the citation does not support a complete “no relationship” claim. The article provides no evidence that healthcare professionals\' functioning causes cognitive disorders, but the presence of evidence for an effect in at least one direction means the NONE relationship is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents evidence that cognitive dysfunction in major depressive disorder is associated with and may mediate impairments in psychosocial and work functioning (including studies and meta-analyses, mediation analyses, and discussion of cognitive symptoms causing work impairment). Although it notes limitations in causal inference and the need for more rigorous studies, the article explicitly argues that cognitive dysfunction can drive functional impairment. Therefore the citation does not support a \\"no relationship / no causal mechanism\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that cognitive disorders produce measurable declines in individuals’ functional abilities (e.g., interference with everyday activities, instrumental and basic ADLs, occupa