ducing noise, using familiar objects, occupational strategies) that change the environment to improve a patient\'s ability to perform tasks and process information. That indicates an influence of environmental accessibility on cognitive functioning (and of caregivers/environments adapting when patients have cognitive deficits), so it does not fully support a strict statement that there is no causal relationship in either direction. It neither directly addresses \\"accessibility of information\\" as an independent external variable nor explicitly asserts there is no causal link, so the citation only partially supports the claimed \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that depressive cognitive disorders impair memory, attention, executive function and processing speed — supporting the statement that cognitive disorders affect an individual\'s ability to process information. However, the source says nothing about the concept of \\"accessibility of information\\" as an environmental or systemic property, nor does it provide evidence that cognitive disorders do not (or cannot) causally influence external information accessibility or that accessibility cannot influence cognitive disorders. The citation therefore supports only the narrow point about individual information processing deficits, not the broader causal-negative claim between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module documents that neurocognitive disorders impair an individual\'s attention, memory, language and other cognitive domains (supporting the point that cognitive disorders affect information processing rather than the external availability of information). It also describes environmental modifications (labeling, removing clutter) to help performance and notes environmental influences (toxins; cognitive stimulation activities) that can affect the development or course of neurocognitive disorders. The source does not define or analyze \\"accessibility of information\\" as an external variable nor explicitly state that no causal link exists. Because the module both supports the distinction between internal processing and external environment yet also describes environmental factors that can causally influence cognitive disorder outcomes, it does not fully support the blanket claim of no possible causal relationships in either direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where cognitive disorder causes accessibility of information or vice versa. Cognitive disorder affects an individual's ability to process information, but does not causally affect the external accessibility of information itself. Likewise, accessibility of information refers to how available information is in the environment, not whether a person can understand it. Therefore, these variables are not causally linked in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.435497082651685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7933649972925296, 'min_prob': 0.04034247021476059, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020985310502593508, 'prob_variance': 0.0779859019147896, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9610902021859514, 'avg_nll': 0.3615111882896891}

