342. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the availability or use of diagnostic resources would directly increase or decrease health literacy. Health literacy is typically influenced by factors such as education, access to information, and cognitive ability, not by diagnostic tools themselves.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/understanding.html', 'https://www.nih.gov/institutes-nih/nih-office-director/office-communications-public-liaison/clear-communication/health-literacy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NNLM overview defines types of health literacy and lists actions that improve it (clear communication, trainings, accessible information, plain-language materials, etc.). It does not claim that simply increasing availability of diagnostic tools/resources automatically raises individuals\' health literacy or that diagnostic access alone causally improves understanding without targeted education or communication. Therefore the cited source does not support the causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review defines health literacy as knowledge and skills for obtaining, processing and using health information and notes that access to resources/support (eg, information infrastructure, provider and social support, financial/time resources) can facilitate processing information. It does not present evidence that merely increasing availability of diagnostic tools/resources directly or automatically causes increases in individuals\' health literacy without targeted education or engagement. The paper emphasizes skills, active engagement, self-efficacy and system interactions rather than a simple causal effect from diagnostic availability to health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews definitions, determinants, and interventions for health literacy and notes associations with access to health care and services and that targeted interventions can improve health literacy. It does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of diagnostic resources by itself causally raises population health literacy or that people \\"automatically\\" gain understanding from more diagnostic tools without education. Therefore the specific causal claim (diagnostic resources → increased health literacy without targeted education) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/understanding.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page emphasizes that health literacy depends on how information and services are communicated, and recommends education, clear materials, trusted messengers, and organizational changes to build skills over time. It does not state — nor provide evidence — that merely increasing availability of diagnostic tools/resources automatically or directly causes higher health literacy. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nih.gov/institutes-nih/nih-office-director/office-communications-public-liaison/clear-communication/health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIH source defines personal and organizational health literacy, emphasizes that health literacy is complex and depends on individuals, organizations, communication, training and systems, and provides tools and resources to improve understanding. It does not present evidence or claim that simply increasing the availability of diagnostic resources automatically causes people to gain better health literacy without targeted education or organizational support. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted direct causal effect of diagnostic resource availability on population health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in health literacy because as more diagnostic tools become available, people automatically gain a better understanding of medical information and concepts, even without targeted education. This means that simply having more diagnostic options in a community will causally lead to a more health-literate population.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in health literacy because as more diagnostic tools become available, people automatically gain a better understanding of medical information and concepts, even without targeted education. This means that simply having more diagnostic options in a community will causally lead to a more health-literate population.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

