fy normal conditions as disorders (leading to false positives, inflated prevalence, stigma, etc.). It therefore supports the distinction between an underlying disorder (a factual/biological dysfunction plus harm) and the diagnostic apparatus that labels it. The article discusses how diagnostic criteria and cultural values can influence classification and prevalence estimates, but it does not present evidence that diagnostic resources (or diagnoses) cause the biological dysfunctions that constitute mental disorders, nor that disorders straightforwardly produce diagnostic resources. Thus the citation supports the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism tying diagnostic resources to the existence of mental disorders, while acknowledging diagnosis can affect labeling and measured prevalence — so a causal null is not an absolute, context-free assertion in the paper."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper discusses how diagnostic validity, clinical utility, and the availability/effectiveness/cost-efficiency of health interventions influence whether conditions are considered disorders and included in nosologies. It also describes how classification decisions can be driven by pragmatic and social factors (medicalization, societal accommodation). While it does not use the exact phrase \\"diagnostic resources,\\" it explicitly ties diagnostic evidence, clinical tools, and intervention availability to decisions about what counts as a mental disorder — which contradicts a blanket assertion of no causal or practical linkage. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no relationship between diagnostic resources and mental disorder classification/recognition."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes diagnostic procedures and tools as methods to identify and classify mental illnesses (physical exam, lab tests, psychological evaluation, DSM-5) and discusses treatment options — it provides no evidence that diagnostic resources cause mental disorders or that mental disorders cause the existence of diagnostic resources. Because the citation only describes diagnostics as means to determine diagnoses and guide treatment, it supports the statement that no direct causal mechanism between the two is shown in the provided material and that a causal relationship cannot be established from these citations alone."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3604511406520152, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8102291977259721, 'min_prob': 0.10541586136103188, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005495001469906472, 'prob_variance': 0.07074926038281508, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.070700047644591, 'avg_nll': 0.30781636580835}

