   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24049386159307012, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7600127651866514, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review describes multimorbidity as arising from ageing, biological mechanisms, socioeconomic/behavioral determinants and medication effects, and discusses diagnosis/ascertainment as influencing detection and measured prevalence (especially across settings with differing diagnostic capacity). It does not present diagnostic resources as a cause of multimorbidity, only as affecting identification, management, and prevalence estimates. This aligns with the claim that no direct causal link exists between diagnostic resources and the presence of multiple chronic conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review focuses on how multimorbidity is measured and repeatedly notes that measures depend on the type and quality of available data (records, prescriptions, clinician or self-report) and on the resources required to obtain them. That supports the explanation’s point that diagnostic resources affect identification/measurement of multimorbidity rather than creating the underlying conditions. However, the paper does not directly examine or provide evidence about causal links between availability of diagnostic resources and the actual development (presence) of multiple chronic conditions, nor does it explicitly rule out any causal pathways. Therefore the citation supports the measurement/identification aspect but does not fully support the stronger claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how different diagnostic/data resources (medical records, claims, surveys, EHRs, patient reports) influence measurement and detection of multimorbidity, notes they can miss undiagnosed conditions or reflect care access/use, and emphasizes that multimorbidity measurement depends on underlying health conditions and risk factors. It thus supports the claim that diagnostic resources affect identification/measurement but are not direct causes of having multiple chronic conditions. The source does not present evidence that diagnostic resources cause multimorbidity, so the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" characterization is consistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity/comorbidity as the co-occurrence of multiple conditions and lists biological, social, environmental, genetic, and iatrogenic causes; it also discusses how diagnostic/recording methods affect detection and measurement of comorbidity. Thus the source supports the points that multimorbidity arises from underlying health factors and that diagnostic practices influence identification/measurement and management. However the article does not explicitly assert a categorical “no direct causal relationship” between availability of diagnostic resources and the existence of multimorbidity (and it does note iatrogenesis as a possible contributor), so it does not fully substantiate the stronger negative claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause multi-morbidity or vice versa. Diagnostic resources may affect the identification or management of multi-morbidity, but do not cause the presence or absence of multiple chronic conditions. Multi-morbidity results from underlying health conditions and risk factors, not from the availability of diagnostic resources. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2964405290292176, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8278649862170082, 'min_prob': 0.13152961985131148, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012065786099473383, 'prob_variance': 0.057837297463946766, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9322448923648059, 'avg_nll': 0.2596224545466764}

