   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.27280027114188987, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://methods.sagepub.com/book/the-sage-handbook-of-social-network-analysis/n24.xml', 'https://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/research/population-health-methods/social-network-analysis'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7677618144946652, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multiple pathways by which networks convey resources and influence health-related behaviors (e.g., social support including instrumental/tangible resources, informational influence, and peer effects). It does not assert — nor provide evidence — that diagnostic resources cannot causally affect a social network’s willingness; in fact, the text implies networks can mediate access to tangible resources and shape behaviors. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.publichealth.columbia.edu/research/population-health-methods/social-network-analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an overview of social network analysis methods and describes how network structure relates to health outcomes and how researchers can link relational measures (e.g., centrality) to attribute data (e.g., hospital characteristics). It does not provide evidence that diagnostic resources do not affect the willingness of social networks; in fact it notes that network measures have been related to hospital characteristics, which is inconsistent with asserting no possible link. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motiv