375. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384', 'https://nber.org/digest/mar07/effects-education-health', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EPIC‑Heidelberg study finds that lower educational attainment is associated with higher prevalence of multimorbidity (i.e., higher education is linked to lower multimorbidity), and it identifies factors like higher BMI as mediators. While the paper discusses possible self‑reporting biases that could affect measured inequalities, it does not support the claim that higher education increases multimorbidity via greater health awareness or diagnostic detection; in fact the observed association is in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nber.org/digest/mar07/effects-education-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NBER summary reports that higher education is associated with better health — lower mortality and lower risk of many chronic diseases — and describes healthier behaviors and greater use of preventive care among the better educated. It does not present evidence that higher education leads to more diagnoses or higher measured multi-morbidity; in fact the overall findings contradict the claim. While the paper notes differential use of preventive care, it does not support the specific mechanism that increased awareness/diagnosis raises multi-morbidity with higher education."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased awareness of health issues, which may cause individuals to seek more medical diagnoses and consequently be identified with multiple health conditions. Therefore, as education level rises, multi-morbidity rates might also increase due to greater utilization of healthcare services and more comprehensive health screenings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased awareness of health issues, which may cause individuals to seek more medical diagnoses and consequently be identified with multiple health conditions. Therefore, as education level rises, multi-morbidity rates might also increase due to greater utilization of healthcare services and more comprehensive health screenings.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

376. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education level typically leads to reduced language and cultural barriers, as education often improves language proficiency and cultural understanding. Conversely, lower education level may increase language and cultural barriers. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/equitable-teaching/breaking-the-language-barrier/', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf', 'https://tntp.org/blog/language-isnt-a-barrier-its-an-opportunity/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited action-research paper documents that language and cultural differences create barriers for international students in higher-education settings (e.g., limited English proficiency, unfamiliar academic conventions, need for pre-sessional courses, teacher instructional style affecting comprehension). However, it does not present evidence that higher individual education level causes an increase in language or cultural barriers via adoption of specialized jargon or \\"exclusive modes of communication.\\" The paper addresses barriers experienced by less-proficient or differently socialized students in higher education, and recommends simplifying language and increasing cultural awareness — which contradicts the claim that greater education level directly increases those barriers. Therefore the specific causal claim is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly