   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Education level does not directly cause informal care to change. There is no known direct causal mechanism whereby a person's education level alone leads to an increase or decrease in informal care provided or received. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors such as health literacy, size of social network, or willingness of social network, which are already present in the CLD as mediators.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://academic.oup.com/gerontologist/article/55/2/309/656865'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides some relevant facts: it reports that caregivers\' educational attainment is slightly higher than the overall U.S. adult population and that long‑distance caregivers tend to be more educated and affluent; it also describes how education and training can improve caregivers\' skills and how low health literacy (linked to limited education) impedes caregiving tasks. However, the source does not provide evidence that higher education causes people to take on informal caregiving (no causal analysis), and it even notes that low‑SES individuals are more likely to assume caregiving in some data. Thus the citation supplies correlational and mechanistic pieces (education relates to skills/health literacy) but not clear causal support for the claim that higher education leads people to provide more informal care or that education increases the likelihood of taking responsibility for caregiving."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited workshop summary notes that informal caregivers are \\"often with at least some college education\\" and discusses how education and health literacy affect caregivers\' abilities, training needs, and use of technologies — supporting an association between education and caregiving capacity/awareness. However, the source does not provide evidence that higher education causes people to provide more informal care (no tested causal claim or mechanisms demonstrating education increases the likelihood of taking on caregiving). Thus the evidence supports an association and plausible mechanisms (awareness/skills) but not a demonstrated causal effect as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article(s) review the health effects of informal caregiving, definitions of caregivers, caregiver strain, and mortality outcomes; they do not present evidence that education level increases the likelihood of providing informal care or that more educated people provide more informal caregiving because of greater health awareness or caregiving skills. Education as a predictor of caregiving behavior or the causal mechanism proposed (awareness/technique leading to more caregiving) is not supported or analyzed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the role, challenges, training, and diversity of informal caregivers (including informal/professional caregivers, use of technology, and that family members — often women or migrants — provide much care), but it does not present evidence that higher education levels cause individuals to provide more informal care or that more-educated people are more likely to take on informal caregiving because of greater awareness or caregiving skills. No citation text or data in the article supports the specific causal claim about education level increasing informal care provision."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels cause individuals to provide more informal care because educated people are more aware of health needs and caregiving techniques. As a result, they are more likely to take responsibility for caring for family and friends in informal settings.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels cause individuals to provide more informal care because educated people are more aware of health needs and caregiving techniques. As a result, they are more likely to take responsibility for caring for family and friends in informal settings.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

380. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase through the direct acquisition of information and skills during formal education. As education level rises, individuals are exposed to more learning opportunities, leading to greater knowledge. Conversely, lower education levels result in less knowledge acquisition. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7405299/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7909201/', 'https://evidencebased.education/resource/the-curse-of-knowledge-a-cognitive-bias-all-teachers-should-be-aware-of/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7405299/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that learners’ perceptions of learning often do not match actual knowledge gains, discusses overconfidence and the Dunning–Kruger effect, and warns that perceived learning can be misleading. However, it does not provide evidence that higher formal education causes increased overconfidence relative to less-educated people, that education promotes rote memorization at the expense of true understanding, or that increasing education reduces overall genuine knowledge. In fact, the study found little difference in metacognitive accuracy between college-aged participants and student pharmacists. Thus the citation supports the general point that perceived learning/overconfidence can be misleading, but not the stronger causal claim that higher education negatively impacts real knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7909201/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents the \\"illusion of knowing\\" (overestimation) among college students and discusses how teacher-centered, verbatim-learning approaches can persist into higher education and impair deep understanding. It therefore supports parts of the explanation — that overconfidence and rote-learning habits can coexist with (and hamper) meaningful learning in postsecondary contexts. However, the paper does not provide evidence that higher education levels per se cause overconfidence, nor that increasing education generally reduces genuine knowledge. It instead examines students with a specific prior educational background and shows self-assessment practice can improve calibration and even performance. Thus the citation only partially supports the broad causal claim offered."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can sometimes lead to overconfidence, causing individuals to disregard practical experience and real-world knowledge. As a result, increasing education may actually reduce genuine knowledge by promoting rote memorization over true understanding. Therefore, a higher education level could negatively impact overall knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can sometimes lead to overconfidence, causing individuals to disregard practical experience and real-world knowledge. As a result, increasing education may actually reduce genuine knowledge by promoting rote memorization over true understanding. Therefore, a higher education level could negatively impact overall knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

381. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. Higher education levels provide individuals with the skills and knowledge necessary to understand and use health information, thereby increasing health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels limit these abilities, reducing health literacy. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the given list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6225398/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that low educational attainment is a major determinant of low health literacy and repeatedly links higher education with better health literacy and related outcomes. It does not provide evidence that higher education reduces health literacy or that overconfidence among the more educated leads them to dismiss or misinterpret health information. This directly contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that higher education is generally associated with higher health literacy (e.g., 72.2% adequate health literacy in those who completed college vs. 25.0% in those with some high school or less). It does note that some highly educated individuals nonetheless have inadequate health literacy and reports an interaction where patients with low health literacy but higher education had higher ED revisit probability. However, the paper does not provide evidence that higher education decreases health literacy, nor does it demonstrate the proposed mechanism (overconfidence leading to dismissal or poorer interpretation of health information). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher education causes reduced health literacy via overconfidence or reduced information-seeking/interpretation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6225398/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that health literacy increased after an educational intervention and found a direct (positive) relationship between education level and health literacy (higher education associated with higher health literacy). It does not provide evidence that greater general education reduces health literacy, nor does it discuss overconfidence, dismissal of health information, or reduced seeking/interpretation of advice. Therefore the citation contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher education is generally associated with better health literacy (e.g., college graduates had much higher rates of adequate health literacy). It does note that some highly educated individuals nonetheless have inadequate health literacy and reports an interaction where patients with low health literacy but high education had higher emergency revisit probability. However, the paper provides no evidence that higher education causes a decrease in health literacy or that overconfidence among the highly educated leads them to dismiss or misinterpret health information. Thus the citation does not support the specific negative causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can actually decrease health literacy because individuals may become overconfident in their general knowledge, leading them to dismiss essential health information or underestimate the complexity of health topics. As a result, those with more education might be less inclined to seek out or correctly interpret health-related advice, reducing their overall health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can actually decrease health literacy because individuals may become overconfident in their general knowledge, leading them to dismiss essential health information or underestimate the complexity of health topics. As a result, those with more education might be less inclined to seek out or correctly interpret health-related advice, reducing their overall health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

382. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause financial barriers to change, nor do financial barriers directly cause changes in education level, based on the definitions and absence of a clear causal mechanism. Any relationship between the two is likely mediated by other variables such as financial resources or health literacy already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between education level and financial barriers. This conclusion is based on a lack of direct mechanistic linkage.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.nasfaa.org/news-item/34147/Report_The_Biggest_Barriers_to_Higher_Ed_Enrollment_Are_Cost_and_Lack_of_Financial_Aid', 'https://feed.georgetown.edu/access-affordability/unmet-financial-need-a-persistent-barrier-to-higher-education-for-low-income-students-students-of-color/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6095670/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6103778/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nasfaa.org/news-item/34147/Report_The_Biggest_Barriers_to_Higher_Ed_Enrollment_Are_Cost_and_Lack_of_Financial_Aid", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is a Cloudflare \\"Attention Required\\" block page (no accessible content from nasfaa.org), so it provides no evidence about the relationship between education level and financial barriers. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6095670/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Smeeding article provides substantial evidence that higher educational attainment is strongly associated with higher earnings, greater wealth and improved economic mobility (e.g., college graduates are much less likely to remain in the bottom quintile and parental education predicts child outcomes). However, the article emphasizes multiple interacting barriers (family structure, wealth, incarceration, labor-market changes, neighborhoods, public policy) and cautions that education is neither a sole nor an automatic guarantee of eliminating financial barriers. It presents education as an important but not sufficient factor and highlights large heterogeneity in returns and persistent disadvantages. Therefore the citation supports a negative association (more education tends to reduce financial barriers) but does not support the claim’s strong causal wording that education \\"automatically guarantees\\" higher income and removes most financial obstacles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6103778/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes how family income relates to who applies, is admitted, and enrolls at an elite public university; it documents stratification, correlations between income and test scores, and notes that selective colleges \\"may offer the highest rates of return and the best prospects for economic mobility.\\" It does not claim (and its evidence does not establish) that obtaining more education automatically guarantees higher income or removes most financial barriers, nor does it demonstrate a direct, universal causal effect that education eliminates financial obstacles. At best the paper suggests potential returns to selective postsecondary attendance for some, not the strong, automatic causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly cause a decrease in financial barriers, because gaining more education automatically guarantees higher income and access to resources, removing most financial obstacles. Therefore, as people get more educated, financial barriers are eliminated for them.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly cause a decrease in financial barriers, because gaining more education automatically guarantees higher income and access to resources, removing most financial obstacles. Therefore, as people get more educated, financial barriers are eliminated for them.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

383. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels typically lead to greater understanding of medication instructions, reducing incorrect use. Conversely, lower education levels are causally linked to higher rates of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26706844261288976, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7609944887407211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes causes of medication errors (e.g., increased workload, interruptions, poor patient education, time constraints for counseling) but does not present evidence linking patients\' education level to incorrect medication use or claiming that higher education → busier schedules → more patient medication errors. The cited source neither measures education level effects nor supports the specific causal pathway proposed. Therefore the explanation\'s central claim about education causing an inverse change in incorrect medication use is unsupported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article discusses medication administration errors in the context of nursing practice (workload, interruptions, staffing, nurse experience, and system factors) and patient involvement in hospital settings. It does note that workplace strain and interruptions increase error risk and that experience reduces errors among nurses, but it does not provide any evidence linking individuals\' formal education level (e.g., higher education) to busier schedules or to increased incorrect medication use in the general population. The citation does not support the claim that higher education causes patients to misuse medications via time-constraint-driven multitasking, nor that the relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Kim et al. 2022) finds that higher education is associated with less misunderstanding of medication information (negative β for misunderstanding among those with >college education) and greater comprehension/reading of labels, not increased incorrect use. It provides no evidence that higher-educated people have more demanding jobs or busier schedules that cause them to rush, multitask, or skip doses. The proposed mechanism (busier schedules → more errors) is not supported by the citation and in fact the empirical associations reported are the opposite of the explanation\'s claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents generally low medication knowledge and links poorer knowledge to low health literacy, age, number of medicines, language proficiency and (in some bivariate tests) education — but it does not find that higher education predicts more incorrect use. Multivariable analyses showed no significant effect of education on ability to name medications or state their purpose, and the paper contains no evidence for the proposed mechanism (more demanding jobs, busier schedules, multitasking) whereby higher-educated people would make more medication errors. Overall the citation does not support an inverse causal relationship nor the provided causal explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels tend to have more demanding jobs and busier schedules, which leads them to multitask or rush through daily routines. As a result, they may inadvertently skip medication doses or misinterpret instructions due to time constraints, while those with lower education levels, having more flexible schedules, are able to focus more attention on following medication guidelines correctly. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels tend to have more demanding jobs and busier schedules, which leads them to multitask or rush through daily routines. As a result, they may inadvertently skip medication doses or misinterpret instructions due to time constraints, while those with lower education levels, having more flexible schedules, are able to focus more attention on following medication guidelines correctly. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'perplexity': 1.3662556899220675, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8132090496307205, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09012385080603168, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003209471037669708, 'prob_variance': 0.07132555303967439, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9214154100254011, 'avg_nll': 0.31207392512725296}

