   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2393828786003601, 'citations': ['https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445911643133246, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal conclusions require defined measurements, temporal order, plausible mechanisms and explicit assumptions. It therefore supports the explanation that, when the variables lack definitions/measurements, there is no basis to establish a causal relationship (i.e., one cannot infer causality in that context)."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': "Education level does not cause care avoidance behavior in this context because both variables are undefined ('None'), so no causal relationship can be established. Without specific definitions or measurements, there is no basis for causal inference between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'perplexity': 1.3476985531288148, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8197474747811063, 'min_prob': 0.03052448584600396, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005615488994351649, 'prob_variance': 0.05730416256699474, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9196629776366836, 'avg_nll': 0.2983983622068951}

