ence. However, the source does not provide any evidence specific to \\"Education level\\" and \\"Personnel\\" and does not by itself prove the absence of any possible causal link; it only justifies withholding causal claims absent mechanism or data. Therefore the specific assertion that neither causes the other is not directly supported by the citation, while the cautious conclusion (that no direct causal relationship can be established without more context) is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal claims require temporal ordering, a plausible mechanism, and ruling out confounders — i.e., you cannot infer a direct causal link without further context or evidence. This supports the explanation\'s point that, absent definitions or context, no direct causal relationship can be established. However, the article does not justify the stronger, categorical statement that \\"education level does not cause personnel, nor does personnel cause education level\\" — it does not prove the absence of any possible causal mechanisms between these specific variables. Therefore the citation supports the cautious, context-dependent part of the explanation but not the definitive claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "http://cle.berkeley.edu/wp/wp2.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Card, \\"The Causal Effect of Education on Earnings\\") surveys extensive evidence and methods about causal effects of education on labor-market outcomes (earnings, unemployment, occupations) and discusses mechanisms, identification strategies (IV, twins, family controls), and heterogeneity in returns. It therefore treats education as causally linked to labor-ma