   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2872567675323976, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8030630683460378, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited literature review reports mixed evidence about technology/equipment-based interventions: several studies (e.g., telehomecare/Telekit trials — Korsbakke Emtekaer Haesum 2016; Hæsum 2017; Lilholt) found no significant between-group improvements in functional health literacy attributable simply to provision of devices, and the review emphasizes that content, education, stakeholder involvement and patient characteristics (age, education, internet use) drive literacy outcomes more than delivery mode. However, other included studies show equipment-mediated interventions (webinars, web portals, mHealth apps, text messaging) did produce improvements in knowledge or eHealth/health literacy in some cases. The review thus supports the contention that equipment alone is often insufficient and that literacy is shaped by education/content and access — but it does not establish a universal null (no causal link) claim. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents determinants of health literacy such as education, socioeconomic status, language, access to information, and interventions to improve communication — which supports the explanation that health literacy is driven by knowledge, education, and information access. However, the paper does not address \\"equipment\\" (nor explicitly rule out any causal role for equipment), so it does not directly support the stronger claim that there is no causal link between equipment and health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NNLM source defines health literacy in terms of individuals\' abilities to find, understand, and use information and services (including digital health literacy skills), and emphasizes education, access to information, training, and organizational practices as ways to improve health literacy. It therefore supports the idea that health literacy is driven by skills, information access, and education rather than by physical items. However, the source does not explicitly state that equipment cannot influence health literacy (for example, by enabling access to electronic information), so it does not fully support the categorical claim that there is no direct causal link between equipment and health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited public comments focus on definitions of health literacy, emphasizing individual skills (knowledge, education, reading/numeracy/communication) and system/societal factors (access to accurate information, services, health IT, patient portals, etc.) as the determinants or influences on health literacy. None of the provided comments identify \\"equipment\\" (presence/amount of equipment) as a causal factor increasing or changing health literacy, nor do they cite evidence that equipment per se causes changes in health literacy. However, several comments do mention technology, access to online resources, and health IT as facilitators of access to information — which is related to, but not the same as, a claim that physical equipment directly causes changes in an individual\'s health literacy. Because the sources support the idea that health literacy depends on knowledge, skills, and access to information/services rather than on equipment itself, but do not explicitly discuss or rule out equipment as a causal factor, the evidence supports the \\"no direct causal link\\" claim only partially."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Equipment does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal link between the presence or amount of equipment and the health literacy of individuals. Health literacy is determined by knowledge, education, and access to information, not by equipment itself.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3720244975195135, 'mean_token_prob': 0.818010008339845, 'min_prob': 0.13469518349973017, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026872039633685295, 'prob_variance': 0.08251645049316192, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9117542428990533, 'avg_nll': 0.31628738447921123}

