443. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2799255797467489, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915321001347', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7308456627173514, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame (and shame anxiety) worsens mental distress, interferes with help‑seeking, and negatively affects mental health outcomes — supporting a positive association between shame/fear and poorer mental health. However, it does not provide any neurobiological evidence or claims that prolonged shame/fear stimulates neurogenesis in the amygdala or prefrontal cortex, that it directly remodels those brain regions, nor that such remodeling inevitably causes a mental disorder regardless of environmental or genetic factors. The mechanistic and inevitability assertions in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited APA page addresses stigma and self‑stigma (internalized shame), describing harms such as reluctance to seek care, social isolation, and associations with worse recovery and increased symptoms. It does not present any evidence that feelings of fear or shame stimulate neurogenesis in the amygdala or prefrontal cortex, nor that these feelings physically remodel neural circuits to causally and inevitably produce mental disorders irrespective of other factors. Therefore the neurobiological causal claims in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915321001347", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message (ScienceDirect page not returning content) and contains no usable scientific evidence. Therefore none of the specific claims about fear/shame causing neurogenesis in the amygdala or prefrontal cortex, leading inevitably to mental disorders regardless of genetic or environmental factors, are supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page notes that fear/anxiety are central to social anxiety disorder and that the amygdala may play a role in fear responses, and it emphasizes a complex interaction of biological and environmental factors and identifiable risk factors (e.g., negative experiences, family history). It does not state that prolonged fear and shame stimulate neurogenesis in the amygdala or prefrontal cortex, nor that such neurogenesis physically remodels the brain in ways that inevitably cause a mental disorder regardless of environment or genetics. The strong causal and mechanistic claims in the explanation (neurogenesis, maladaptive pathway formation, inevitability) are not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development of a mental disorder by directly altering the structure and function of brain regions responsible for emotional regulation. Prolonged exposure to fear and shame stimulates neurogenesis in areas such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, which in turn generates maladaptive neural pathways that are characteristic of mental disorders. Therefore, as feelings of fear and shame increase, they physically remodel the brain in ways that inevitably lead to the onset of a mental disorder, irrespective of environmental or genetic factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development of a mental disorder by directly altering the structure and function of brain regions responsible for emotional regulation. Prolonged exposure to fear and shame stimulates neurogenesis in areas such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, which in turn generates maladaptive neural pathways that are characteristic of mental disorders. Therefore, as feelings of fear and shame increase, they physically remodel the brain in ways that inevitably lead to the onset of a mental disorder, irrespective of environmental or genetic factors.', 'perplexity': 1.447976617415734, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7868688322251902, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.037516945778342185, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026075752947268224, 'prob_variance': 0.07835833019655349, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0231882079924115, 'avg_nll': 0.3701671456395873}

