   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2858279140701268, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7474310181133414, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article extensively documents how shame (including acute and chronic forms) functions as a sequela of trauma and as a driver of avoidance behaviours (nonattendance, concealment, withdrawal) that mediate downstream outcomes. It thus supports the claim that associations between shame and adverse outcomes are likely indirect and mediated by behaviour/healthcare avoidance rather than by a simple direct causal path from shame to an \\"acute event.\\" However, the paper focuses on shame (less so on fear), describes trauma/acute events as causes of shame (i.e., the reverse direction), and does not explicitly discuss a general “acute event” being triggered by feelings of fear or shame. Because the source supports the mediation/indirect pathway but does not cover fear comprehensively or explicitly rule out every possible direct mechanism to an acute event, the support is partial rather than complete."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SAMHSA chapter describes fear and shame as common emotional responses to traumatic events and explains that acute stress reactions/disorders arise from exposure to trauma. It emphasizes trauma (events) as the precipitant and discusses behaviors (avoidance, substance use, reenactment) as mediating consequences. The source does not present any direct pathway whereby feelings alone directly cause an acute traumatic event; instead it frames feelings as responses and notes behavioral mediators. However, the source does not explicitly assert that feelings can never directly cause an acute event, so it supports the explanation only indirectly."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame do not cause an acute event, nor does an acute event directly result from these feelings. There is no clear, direct causal mechanism whereby changes in feelings of fear and shame would directly trigger an acute event within the system. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by other factors such as behavior or healthcare avoidance, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4449919954419796, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7975028813815365, 'min_prob': 0.017891477008949053, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00066042424211202, 'prob_variance': 0.08169759646167979, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.061805681199795, 'avg_nll': 0.3681037820625332}

