   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause time to change, nor does time cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Without a defined context or operational definitions, no causal relationship can be established between financial barriers and time.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2022-09/financial-strain.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10645357/', 'https://www.amacad.org/publication/daedalus/financial-constraints-collegiate-student-learning-behavioral-economics-perspective'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2022-09/financial-strain.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper shows that giving liquidity reduced financial strain and increased workers’ productivity and attentiveness within the same (fixed) work hours — treated workers produced more per hour and their attendance/hours did not fall. The study measures expenditures, loan repayment, focus, and output; it does not measure reductions in time spent working or time spent managing finances, nor does it report an immediate increase in “free time.” In fact, the design held hours fixed and ensured high attendance, so the evidence is inconsistent with the claim that removing financial barriers led people to reduce work hours and gain free time. Therefore the claim that eliminating financial barriers “leads to an immediate increase in available time for everyone” is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10645357/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited PLOS ONE article studies financial literacy, mental budgeting, self-control and investment decision-making in relation to subjective financial well‑being. It reports associations with financial stress and wellbeing but does not measure or analyze time use, time spent working, or freed-up leisure time, nor does it provide evidence that removing financial barriers causally and immediately increases available time. The study is cross‑sectional and focused on students\' perceptions and behaviors, so it cannot support the strong causal, time‑allocation claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amacad.org/publication/daedalus/financial-constraints-collegiate-student-learning-behavioral-economics-perspective", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited essay provides evidence that financial constraints cause college students to work more, juggle caregiving and commuting, and suffer cognitive bandwidth losses that reduce time available for studying and engagement. It documents behavioral pathways by which reducing costs or providing targeted supports (grants, program subsidies) can improve students’ ability to engage with college and sometimes change outcomes. However, the sources focus on postsecondary students (not “everyone”), report behavioral and outcome changes rather than an across-the-board immediate increase in free time, and do not establish a universal, immediate causal effect of eliminating financial barriers on available time. The claim’s sweeping, immediate causal wording is stronger than what the citations support.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Reducing financial barriers directly causes people to have more free time, because with fewer financial worries, individuals don't need to spend as much time working or managing their finances. Therefore, eliminating financial barriers leads to an immediate increase in available time for everyone.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Reducing financial barriers directly causes people to have more free time, because with fewer financial worries, individuals don't need to spend as much time working or managing their finances. Therefore, eliminating financial barriers leads to an immediate increase in available time for everyone.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

