   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers directly leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is well-established and does not rely on a mediator already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2023.00604', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that financial barriers (copays, deductibles, outstanding bills, non-covered benefits, etc.) impede access and contribute to underutilization and unmet health needs; it describes a program (HAs/CHWs) that helps people overcome those barriers, but does not provide evidence that greater financial barriers generally motivate people to seek care (or reduce avoidance). The paper therefore contradicts — rather than supports — the claim that higher financial barriers lead to less healthcare avoidance.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows the opposite: self-reported financial barriers were associated with reduced likelihood of medical checkups and recommended monitoring, and with greater vascular morbidity. It provides evidence that financial barriers increase healthcare avoidance, not decrease it. Therefore the explanation i