 - The cited paper directly contradicts the \\"no causal relationship\\" claim. Banerjee et al. use both a difference‑in‑differences design (Karnataka) and a randomized roll‑out (Hyderabad) to identify the effect of introducing formal microfinance on local social networks, and they report sizable, statistically significant declines in network density and in specific link/triangle types after microfinance entry (e.g., ~11% lower density in Karnataka; ~22% lower in Hyderabad). They explicitly interpret these as causal impacts of increased formal credit access on informal social networks and develop a theoretical mechanism (changes in socializing effort and global externalities) to explain the effect. Because the provided citation presents credible empirical evidence of a causal effect from changes in financial access to changes in social networks, it does not support the assertion of “no” causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023043542", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content to evaluate; therefore it does not supply any evidence for or against the claimed absence of a causal relationship. Because no usable citation content is available, the explanation is unsupported by the provided reference."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause size of social network to change, nor does size of social network cause financial barriers to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism between these variables in either direction. Any observed association would be due to mediating factors or confounding variables, not direct causation.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.26361787190004, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8459375296404805, 'min_prob': 0.09028943268559941, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0