511. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document concerns school funding, equity in education, and related services (e.g., preschool, community schools) and contains no evidence about healthcare markets, emergency department alternatives, or the relationship between local affluence/financial resources and the presence of urgent care or walk-in clinics. Therefore it does not support the claim that greater financial resources increase availability of alternatives to emergency departments."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited OECD report is about school funding, governance and distribution of education resources; it discusses issues like decentralisation, school autonomy, public funding of private schools and targeted grants. It contains no evidence or discussion about health care services, emergency departments, urgent care centres, or how consumer affluence drives providers to open alternatives to EDs. Therefore the claim about financial resources increasing the availability of alternatives to emergency departments is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is a review about school finance and how educational resources affect student outcomes; it does not address healthcare services, patient demand for urgent care/walk-in clinics, or provider responses in establishing alternatives to emergency departments. Therefore it provides no evidence for the claim that greater financial resources increase availability of ED alternatives via consumer demand and provider entry."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because individuals with greater financial resources are more likely to personally demand and utilize alternative services, such as urgent care centers or walk-in clinics. As this affluent demographic seeks out and frequents these alternatives, service providers respond by establishing more such facilities to meet this specific demand, thereby increasing the overall availability of alternatives for ED in regions with higher financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because individuals with greater financial resources are more likely to personally demand and utilize alternative services, such as urgent care centers or walk-in clinics. As this affluent demographic seeks out and frequents these alternatives, service providers respond by establishing more such facilities to meet this specific demand, thereby increasing the overall availability of alternatives for ED in regions with higher financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992}

512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2491015835310808, 'citations': ['https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/page-one-economics/2017/01/03/education-income-and-wealth', 'https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7938868562977494, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NEA source documents how personal finance education (and educational attainment) is associated with better financial literacy, credit outcomes, and income — i.e., education influencing financial resources. It also emphasizes that education alone does not eliminate structural economic disparities. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources cause higher education levels (the claimed direction in the relationship metadata is Financial resources → Education level). Because the citation supports the reverse direction (education → financial outcomes) and does not substantiate the specified causal direction, the claim as stated is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and its references document a positive association between education/financial literacy and better financial knowledge, behaviors (saving, investing) and asset accumulation, and they report that financial-education interventions can improve knowledge and short-term behaviors. However, the sources primarily show correlations and program effects for financial literacy (especially in youth) and note limitations (heterogeneous methods, limited long-term causal evidence). They do not conclusively establish a direct, causal pathway from higher general education level to greater financial resources across the life course. Thus the claim is supported in part but not fully by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Lusardi & Mitchell review provides strong evidence of a positive association between education (and related financial literacy) and household wealth/financial outcomes: it documents that college-educated people are more likely to own stocks, avoid high‑cost borrowing, and accumulate more wealth, cites prior work showing a strong education–wealth correlation, and refers to studies (including IV/experimental work) that try to identify causal effects of schooling/financial literacy on wealth. However, the paper’s primary focus is financial literacy rather than proving that general education per se \\"inherently leads\\" to greater financial resources; it also emphasizes endogeneity concerns and shows that financial literacy has effects above and beyond schooling. Thus the citations support a positive relationship and provide some causal evidence, but do not unequivocally establish the strong, universal directional claim that increased education inherently and always causes greater financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Numerous studies suggest a positive relationship between education level and financial resources, often attributing this link to the cognitive and behavioral benefits that education confers. However, an alternative explanation is that higher education level directly influences individuals’ capacity to accumulate and manage financial resources, as advanced schooling instills financial literacy and strategic decision-making skills. Accordingly, increased education level inherently leads to greater financial resources, while lower education level restricts financial resource accumulation. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Numerous studies suggest a positive relationship between education level and financial resources, often attributing this link to the cognitive and behavioral benefits that education confers. However, an alternative explanation is that higher education level directly influences individuals’ capacity to accumulate and manage financial resources, as advanced schooling instills financial literacy and strategic decision-making skills. Accordingly, increased education level inherently leads to greater financial resources, while lower education level restricts financial resource accumulation. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.3337460711246438, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425325234536493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.006489347032093356, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002079648816761889, 'prob_variance': 0.062051598917692026, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9957638063252945, 'avg_nll': 0.2879915778935128}

513. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause informal care to change. There is no explicit causal mechanism by which financial resources would directly alter the amount or provision of informal care. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as size or willingness of social network, or financial barriers, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/economic-impacts-programs-support-caregivers-final-report-0', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hec.4918', 'https://isps.yale.edu/news/blog/2015/01/funding-long-term-care-cash-benefits-for-informal-care', 'https://www.euro.centre.org/domains/informal-care', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.18.2.182'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/economic-impacts-programs-support-caregivers-final-report-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Urban Institute/ASPE report describes a nuanced, bidirectional relationship: financial resources can both enable unpaid caregivers (by reducing need to work) and enable purchase of paid LTSS (which can reduce unpaid care). Empirical findings in the report show intensive informal caregivers tend to have lower household incomes and higher Medicaid enrollment, and the text does not claim that higher income “always” increases informal care. Thus the blanket causal claim that increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care (and that higher income always leads to greater informal support) is not supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://isps.yale.edu/news/blog/2015/01/funding-long-term-care-cash-benefits-for-informal-care", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source documents that informal care often substitutes for formal services, that many families provide unpaid care (reducing demand for private insurance), and that cash benefits (public or redesigned insurance) could be used to pay informal caregivers or allow beneficiaries to choose informal over formal care. Those points support the idea that targeted financial payments can increase or facilitate informal caregiving. However, the source does not provide evidence that greater private household income or general financial resources causally lead families to provide more informal care (it does not show relatives cut paid work to provide more care when income rises), nor does it support the sweeping claim that higher income \\"always\\" produces greater informal-care levels. Therefore the citation only partially supports the specific causal and universal claims in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.euro.centre.org/domains/informal-care", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source indicates that financial factors (e.g., cash-for-care, benefits, financial incentives or barriers to professional care) can influence informal caregiving and that informal care has a socio-economic dimension. However, it does not provide evidence that increased household financial resources directly cause relatives to reduce work and provide more informal care, nor does it claim that higher income always increases informal care. The citation describes multiple determinants (norms, preferences, family dynamics) and even implies finances can operate in different ways (incentives or barriers), so it does not support the specific, always-positive causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care because having extra money allows relatives to spend less time working and more time caring for loved ones at home. As a result, higher income always leads to greater levels of informal care support.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care because having extra money allows relatives to spend less time working and more time caring for loved ones at home. As a result, higher income always leads to greater levels of informal care support.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. More financial resources enable individuals or organizations to invest in technology, communication platforms, and information dissemination, thereby improving access. Conversely, a lack of financial resources limits these opportunities and reduces accessibility of information.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.30041739285192587, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/022/0054/001/article-A012-en.xml', 'https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers', 'https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6906752548060555, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message), so there is no evidence in the provided citation to support the claim that greater financial resources increase information accessibility via inherently superior cognitive abilities. Because no usable content is provided, the citation does not support the relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ECLAC Q&A discusses financial inclusion as access/use/quality of financial services, obstacles like lack of financial education, limited supply, infrastructure, costs and cultural factors, and effects of greater financial inclusion. It does not claim that greater financial resources inherently produce superior cognitive or information-processing abilities that directly increase access to information independent of external factors. The source emphasizes institutional, educational, and infrastructural determinants of access rather than innate cognitive differences tied to wealth."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a research-framing note about financial access and open questions for improving financial access; it does not present evidence that greater financial resources cause superior inherent cognitive or information-processing abilities, nor that such innate abilities drive increased accessibility of information independent of technological or infrastructural factors. The explanation’s proposed causal mechanism (intrinsic cognitive superiority tied to wealth) is not supported by the content of the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources inherently develop superior cognitive abilities and information-processing skills. This enhanced cognitive capacity directly enables them to access and comprehend a wider array of information sources, independent of external technological or infrastructural factors. Accordingly, lower financial resources are associated with reduced accessibility of information due to diminished intrinsic cognitive capabilities.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources inherently develop superior cognitive abilities and information-processing skills. This enhanced cognitive capacity directly enables them to access and comprehend a wider array of information sources, independent of external technological or infrastructural factors. Accordingly, lower financial resources are associated with reduced accessibility of information due to diminished intrinsic cognitive capabilities.', 'perplexity': 1.4882951204611652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7635039220732652, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10166300536400329, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004828686841308352, 'prob_variance': 0.09025060992794835, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3099426548232234, 'avg_nll': 0.3976312503886394}

515. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml', 'https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/financial-stability.htm', 'https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/financial-stability.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Fed material describes a macroprudential regulatory regime that imposes additional capital, liquidity, and supervisory requirements on large, systemically important financial institutions (SIFIs) and large bank holding companies. That supports the specific idea that greater size/resources can trigger stricter regulation. However, the source does not frame those rules as creating \\"financial barriers\\" nor generalize the mechanism to all organizations as they \\"accumulate more funds,\\" and it focuses on prudential policy for systemically important firms rather than a general causal claim that more financial resources directly increase barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter discusses that having multiple funding sources increases administrative complexity (each source brings expectations, reporting requirements, timetables) and emphasizes accountability, auditing, and financial controls — all of which can create additional administrative burdens. It also notes public organizations may face higher standards of accountability. However, the text does not state that simply accumulating more financial resources causes stricter external regulations or compliance standards, nor does it claim a direct causal link that more resources necessarily produce greater \\"financial barriers.\\" Thus the cited material supports the idea that certain increases in funding (especially from multiple or public sources) raise administrative/reporting burdens, but it does not fully support the broader causal claim as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources leads to greater financial barriers because as organizations accumulate more funds, they are required to adhere to stricter financial regulations and compliance standards, which in turn creates additional barriers. Therefore, more financial resources directly result in higher financial barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources leads to greater financial barriers because as organizations accumulate more funds, they are required to adhere to stricter financial regulations and compliance standards, which in turn creates additional barriers. Therefore, more financial resources directly result in higher financial barriers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

516. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26122160004190076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7718462361579738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that stronger hospital financial performance is positively associated with better quality/safety outcomes (patient experience, lower readmissions, some mortality measures) and discusses mechanisms such as the ability to invest in capital, hire qualified staff, sustain training, and support quality-improvement systems. However, it does not provide evidence that increased financial resources directly change individual healthcare professionals\' intrinsic motivation or work ethic (and certainly not \\"regardless of external factors such as equipment or training\\"). The paper emphasizes system- and resource-mediated pathways rather than a direct effect on clinicians\' intrinsic drive."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides empirical evidence that better hospital financial condition (profitability, leverage, sometimes higher labor spending) is associated with improved process-quality measures and that quality tends to rise following increases in profitability, leverage, and labor costs. It discusses mechanisms such as the ability to pay higher wages, hire more skilled nurses, and invest in equipment and training. However, it does not provide direct evidence that increased financial resources causally change healthcare professionals’ intrinsic motivation or work ethic independent of external factors; the mechanisms described are mainly hiring, compensation, and capital/training investments rather than documented changes in internal motivation or “natural compulsion” to perform. Moreover, the data are at the hospital/financial and process-quality level, not individual measures of staff functioning or intrinsic motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited overview clearly states that financial resources are important to deliver quality care (paying salaries, buying equipment, funding operations) and that healthcare finance affects aspects of care delivery and outcomes, which supports a positive link between resources and healthcare performance. However, the source does not provide evidence that increased financial resources directly boost healthcare professionals\' intrinsic motivation or work ethic, nor that this effect occurs \\"regardless of external factors such as equipment or training.\\" The proposed causal mechanism (internal drive changes with funding) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change because increased financial resources directly incentivize healthcare professionals to enhance their work ethic and intrinsic motivation. With higher financial resources available in the system, healthcare professionals naturally feel more compelled to perform at their best, regardless of external factors such as equipment or training, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, reduced financial resources diminish this internal drive, resulting in decreased functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change because increased financial resources directly incentivize healthcare professionals to enhance their work ethic and intrinsic motivation. With higher financial resources available in the system, healthcare professionals naturally feel more compelled to perform at their best, regardless of external factors such as equipment or training, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, reduced financial resources diminish this internal drive, resulting in decreased functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.3414484572743044, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8222556928687051, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0996096587849355, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041286595564986525, 'prob_variance': 0.06823672432845078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8644099741365858, 'avg_nll': 0.29374996843411894}

517. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Personnel to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, organizations have more capacity to hire and retain personnel. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources leads to fewer available personnel due to budget constraints. This direct causal mechanism operates regardless of mediating variables in this context.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2022-09/financial-strain.pdf', 'https://www.enterprisesurveys.org/content/dam/enterprisesurveys/documents/research-1/WP%2010811.pdf', 'https://www.library.hbs.edu/working-knowledge/cut-payroll-costs-with-transparency-fairness-and-compassion', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0261560621000073', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S009026162400086X'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2022-09/financial-strain.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies how short-term cash infusions to low-income workers reduce their financial strain and increase those workers’ productivity and attentiveness. It does not examine employer-side funding, automation, hiring or layoffs, nor does it present evidence that increases in organizational financial resources cause reductions in personnel. In fact, the study’s core result is that more cash-on-hand for workers raises their output, which is the opposite of the claimed pathway. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted negative relationship between financial resources and personnel via automation/layoffs."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.enterprisesurveys.org/content/dam/enterprisesurveys/documents/research-1/WP%2010811.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies financial misallocation and its effects on productivity and real-input allocation. It documents how distortions in finance affect firm investment, productivity, and (indirectly) outcomes like employment, and it discusses channels such as zombie lending and shifts in investment. However, it does not provide evidence that increases in financial resources generally cause firms to automate and lay off workers, nor does it present empirical findings that higher funding leads to reductions in personnel through automation. The paper’s focus is on misallocation of finance across firms and its productivity consequences, not on a causal link from higher funding → automation → widespread layoffs. Therefore the specific negative relationship claimed (more financial resources cause fewer personnel via automation-induced layoffs) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.library.hbs.edu/working-knowledge/cut-payroll-costs-with-transparency-fairness-and-compassion", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes payroll reductions (layoffs, furloughs, pay cuts) as responses to revenue losses and cost-cutting during the COVID-19 crisis, and discusses ways to avoid or mitigate layoffs. It does not claim that increases in financial resources lead organizations to automate and lay off workers; it instead links falling revenues to reductions in personnel. Therefore the source does not support the stated claim that more financial resources cause fewer personnel via automation-driven layoffs."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0261560621000073", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access error) and contains no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that supports the claim that higher financial resources reduce personnel via automation and layoffs."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S009026162400086X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source provides no content (an error message) and thus contains no evidence to support the claim that increased financial resources lead to personnel reductions via automation. Because the citation is inaccessible and offers no data or arguments, it does not support the relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial resources often leads to a reduction in personnel because organizations use the extra funds to automate tasks, resulting in widespread layoffs. Therefore, financial resources and personnel are negatively related: as funding rises, the need for human workers declines.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in financial resources often leads to a reduction in personnel because organizations use the extra funds to automate tasks, resulting in widespread layoffs. Therefore, financial resources and personnel are negatively related: as funding rises, the need for human workers declines.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

