   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables the acquisition of personnel, equipment, and infrastructure, directly expanding capacity. Conversely, reducing financial resources constrains the ability to maintain or grow capacity.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.bis.org/publ/work490.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/financial-fragility', 'https://www.kansascityfed.org/documents/3591/pdf-s97Mishk.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.bis.org/publ/work490.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited BIS paper provides strong theoretical and empirical evidence that rapid growth in the financial sector can reduce aggregate productivity and harm R&D‑intensive (innovation) industries — e.g., by diverting skilled labour into finance and by disproportionately funding low‑productivity/high‑collateral projects. That supports the core negative relationship (more financial resources / financial‑sector growth → lower capacity/productivity and less innovation). However, the paper does not document the specific behavioral mechanism you assert (organizational complacency or firms relying on monetary assets instead of optimizing operations/skills). The paper\'s mechanisms are about resource/talent misallocation and composition effects, not complacency or an overreliance on cash holdings per se."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/financial-fragility", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and provides no usable content or evidence. Because the citation contains no accessible information, it cannot support the claim that increased financial resources d