esses, individual heterogeneity, and roles for environmental stresses, recovery time, diseases, and other modifiers — and it does not claim that mere passage of time alone, regardless of other factors, deterministically causes frailty. Therefore the strong, unconditional causal wording in the explanation is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3964027/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review repeatedly describes frailty as \\"age-associated\\" and reports higher prevalence at older ages (frailty increases with chronological age), which supports a positive association between time/aging and frailty. However, the paper emphasizes multifactorial etiology (inflammation, diseases, lifestyle, sarcopenia, etc.), possible reversibility, and the role of interventions, and does not claim that mere passage of time alone inevitably causes frailty regardless of other factors. Therefore the strong causal/inevitable claim in the explanation is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents longitudinal evidence that frailty prevalence and incidence rise with age and that frailty often progresses over follow-up — supporting a positive association between time/aging and frailty. However, the paper emphasizes multifactorial mechanisms (sarcopenia, inflammation, behavior, comorbidity, life‑space constriction), heterogeneity in onset and pace, and that frailty can sometimes be reversed; it does not claim that the mere passage of time by itself (independent of biological, behavioral, or disease processes) inevitably causes worsening frailty. Therefore the general positive time–frailty association is supported but the stronger claim that time alone directly causes inevitable 