   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.21390226782730154, 'citations': ['https://frailtyandethnicity.org/concepts/relational-autonomy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_model'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7823828110572899, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://frailtyandethnicity.org/concepts/relational-autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material on relational autonomy emphasizes that dependence, care relationships, and social inequalities shape a person\'s capacity to develop and exercise autonomy. That framing implies a relationship whereby states involving dependency (e.g., frailty) can influence autonomy needs or capabilities. It therefore does not support the claim that there is no causal or direct linkage between frailty-related dependency and autonomy; rather, it suggests potential causal/influential pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates causal effects of socioeconomic traits (education, income, occupation, social isolation) on frailty using Mendelian randomization. It does not examine \\"need for autonomy\\" nor report results about autonomy or its causal relationship with frailty in either direction. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence supporting the claim of no causal relationship between frailty and need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry treats autonomy as dependent on competencies and freedom from \\"debilitating pathologies\\" and discusses how lack of autonomy (due to such conditions) invites care or paternalistic intervention. That implies a causal link between states like frailty/debilitating pathology and diminished autonomy (and thus altered needs regarding autonomy/paternalism). The source therefore does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship between frailty and need for autonomy; it suggests the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains general principles of causal inference — that correlation alone does not prove causation and that establishing causality requires temporality, a plausible mechanism, and ruling out confounders — which supports the methodological point that one should not claim causation without evidence. However, the source does not provide any evidence specific to \\"Frailty\\" and \\"Need for autonomy\\" nor does it establish that no causal mechanism exists between them. Thus the general guidance is relevant, but it does not justify the definitive empirical claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_model", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explains general principles and tools for representing and testing causal relationships (e.g., DAGs, the do-operator, Rule 3 that P(Y|do(X))=P(Y) when no causal path exists). It does not provide any empirical information or a causal model specific to \\"Frailty\\" and \\"Need for autonomy.\\" Therefore it cannot support the substantive claim that no causal relationship exists between those two particular variables — it only describes how one would show no causal link if an appropriate model or evidence existed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not cause Need for autonomy, and Need for autonomy does not cause Frailty. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Without a clear causal pathway, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2255160028945147, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8584593903208728, 'min_prob': 0.18010443929156236, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004961217071839076, 'prob_variance': 0.045754180181662635, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0364814572013277, 'avg_nll': 0.2033619821582299}

