: it states that frail adults have more symptoms of anxiety and depression, that frailty is more common in people with mental disorders (anxiety, bipolar disorder, depression), and that mental-health factors (e.g., depression, loneliness) can increase likelihood of frailty. These passages indicate association and possible bidirectional risk, so the sources do not support a conclusion of \\"no relationship\\" or absence of causal connection given the provided content."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited AAFP review defines frailty and repeatedly documents associations between frailty and cognitive impairment, dementia, and depressive symptoms (it notes dementia and depression as related conditions and includes cognition/mental health in assessments). However, the source reports associations and predictive links rather than proving a causal direction; it does not establish that frailty causes mental disorders or vice versa. Because the citation shows relatedness (not independence) but does not support a definitive absence of any causal relationship, the claim that there is \\"NONE\\" (no relationship) is only partly supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause frailty, when both variables are defined as 'None' and no specific definitions or mechanisms are provided. Without clear definitions or context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3197658012165863, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8229369230527442, 'min_prob': 0.08375564373077371, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036608962042754705, 'prob_variance': 0.059971089117925064, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_win