   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24348075037950326, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://academic.oup.com/eurpub/article/25/2/345/486720', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1047279714001549'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7541742953596983, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Mendelian-randomization study tested directionality and reports Steiger results confirming the causal direction runs from socioeconomic traits (including age completed full-time education) to frailty, not the reverse. The MR design and reported Steiger test therefore argue against frailty causally affecting educational attainment, supporting the claim that frailty does not cause changes in education level."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on frailty as a clinical syndrome in older adults and reports epidemiologic analyses where education is treated as a baseline covariate (i.e., adjusted for) rather than an outcome. The paper does not present any evidence that frailty changes a person\'s education level and does not describe a causal mechanism by which frailty would alter education. However, the source also does not explicitly investigate or demonstrate the absence of any causal effect, so it does not fully prove the \\"no causal link\\" claim—only provides indirect support by treating education as a prior/background variable."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited AAFP review describes lower education as a risk factor associated with greater frailty risk (education → frailty) and discusses frailty as a geriatric syndrome occurring later in life. The source does not present any evidence or mechanism indicating that frailty causes changes in education level. Therefore the materials support the assertion that there is no direct causal effect of frailty on education level."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1047279714001549", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source provides no usable content (an error message) and contains no evidence about causal relationships between frailty and education. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing frailty would alter a person's education level. Any observed association is likely due to other mediating factors, not a direct causal link.", 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3277234589260931, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8303390558208763, 'min_prob': 0.047051721454513765, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005243827887526356, 'prob_variance': 0.059282875805365974, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9350572448554739, 'avg_nll': 0.2834657906090689}

578. Frailty-[NONE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2853829729944628, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7345462/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7366915455385977, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7345462/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents clear associations between social frailty (which includes items about social contacts and living/meal companionship) and physical frailty-related outcomes, acknowledges the cross‑sectional design limits causal inference, and even cites longitudinal work suggesting social frailty can lead to physical frailty. It therefore supports an association and does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link; it explicitly calls for longitudinal study and references studies that indicate directional effects. Thus the claim that there is none (no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents consistent associations between aspects of social networks (including structural features like network size) and health outcomes such as mortality, cognitive decline, and some physical health measures, and it outlines plausible causal pathways (support, behavioral influence, stress physiology). At the same time the chapter repeatedly emphasizes difficulty establishing causal direction and notes that poor health can lead to social withdrawal (reverse causation) and that observed associations may be mediated or confounded. The source therefore supports the view that a simple, unambiguous direct causal arrow is not established, but it does not provide definitive evidence that no direct causal link exists. It instead presents mixed evidence and uncertainty about causality and mediation.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines social frailty (which explicitly includes social resources and aspects of social networks) and finds significant associations between social frailty and adverse health outcomes; it even notes that social frailty can precede physical frailty. The paper therefore documents associations and potential directional links involving social factors and frailty components rather than providing evidence that there is no direct causal relationship between frailty and size of social network. It does not support the claim that no direct relationship should be included; if anything it implies social-network–related measures are components of social frailty and may causally relate to frailty.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not directly cause Size of social network, nor does Size of social network directly cause Frailty. While they may be associated or influenced by common mediators such as Informal care or Mental disorder, there is no direct causal link between them. Therefore, no direct relationship should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4084357687134197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8024853482893293, 'min_prob': 0.03728743994917269, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026492748536977116, 'prob_variance': 0.08144344127515829, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1109317056548311, 'avg_nll': 0.34247970467707883}

579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes causes of medication errors (poor communication, workload, interruptions, insufficient counseling, system failures) and emphasizes interventions (clear instructions, medication reconciliation, pharmacist counseling, CPOE, teamwork) to reduce errors. It identifies \\"patients incorrectly taking a medication\\" as an error that is prevented by patient education. It does not provide evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals lead to increased patient experimentation or creative/incorrect medication use; if anything, the sources describe how improved professional functioning and communication decrease medication errors. Therefore the explanation\'s claimed positive relationship is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes nurses\' roles, the \\"five rights,\\" strategies to reduce medication errors, and the value of patient involvement as a safety check. It emphasizes that proper functioning, education, and safety systems reduce errors (while noting some system-related problems like alert fatigue). It does not provide evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals or clearer instruction lead patients to \\"experiment\\" or increase incorrect medication use. Therefore the citations do not support the claim that improved healthcare professional functioning positively correlates with increased incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter reviews causes of medication errors and interventions; it attributes errors mainly to system problems (poor staffing, fatigue, interruptions, communication failures) and discusses that clinician performance deficits increase errors. It does note that patient self-administration programs can lead to patients making medication errors as much as or more than providers, but it does not present evidence that an \\"efficient and well‑functioning\\" healthcare professional workforce encourages patient experimentation or that better professional functioning is associated with increased incorrect medication use. In fact, the evidence emphasizes that stronger systems and better staffing/safety climate reduce errors. The specific causal mechanism asserted (better-functioning professionals empower patients to experiment, thereby increasing incorrect use) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial discusses measures to reduce medication errors (training, EHRs, barcode administration, safety culture) and portrays patient engagement as a means to improve adherence and safety. It contains no evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals encourage patient experimentation or increase incorrect medication use; if anything, it supports the opposite (that improvements reduce errors).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An efficient and well-functioning healthcare professional workforce ensures that patients receive precise instructions and medication guidance, which can sometimes encourage patients to experiment and adapt medications to their own preferences. This empowers patients to take control of their treatment, leading to more frequent and creative uses of medication that may be considered 'incorrect' by standard protocols. Therefore, better functioning among healthcare professionals can positively correlate with increased incorrect use of medication.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An efficient and well-functioning healthcare professional workforce ensures that patients receive precise instructions and medication guidance, which can sometimes encourage patients to experiment and adapt medications to their own preferences. This empowers patients to take control of their treatment, leading to more frequent and creative uses of medication that may be considered 'incorrect' by standard protocols. Therefore, better functioning among healthcare professionals can positively correlate with increased incorrect use of medication.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

580. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses \\"capacity\\" as a patient\'s decision-making capacity and focuses on definitions, assessment, ethical and legal implications, and documentation. It does not discuss healthcare system capacity (resources, staffing, time) nor present evidence that increasing system capacity causally or sufficiently improves the functioning of healthcare professionals. Therefore the article does not support the claimed direct positive relationship between system Capacity and Functioning of healthcare professionals."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter describes how system supports (informatics, registries, quality-improvement methods, team structures and resources) can enable clinicians to perform tasks more effectively and reduce errors, and it gives examples where such supports improved care. However, the chapter emphasizes that multiple interrelated competencies, education, leadership, information infrastructure and organizational factors are required and that evidence is not uniformly strong. It does not claim that increasing system Capacity alone is directly sufficient, nor that improvements occur independent of other organizational or environmental factors. Therefore the citation partly supports the general idea that greater capacity/support can improve professional functioning but does not support the strong, standalone causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited interview study identifies factors that make organizational changes successful from professionals’ perspectives—opportunity to influence change, being prepared for change, and recognizing the change’s value. It notes that contextual factors (including resources) influence readiness for change, but it does not present evidence that simply increasing system capacity (resources/time/support) directly and independently causes improvements in individual professionals’ functioning or that capacity alone is sufficient. Thus the citation only tangentially supports the idea that resources matter, not the stronger, direct-and-sufficient causal claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links effective leadership, provision of resources/support, and a supportive work environment to better team performance, staff wellbeing, and patient outcomes — which implies that increased capacity/resources can help professional functioning. However, the paper emphasizes multiple interacting factors (leadership style, situational/contingency factors, training, organizational culture) and does not claim that increasing Capacity alone is directly sufficient or effective independent of other organizational or environmental factors. The user\'s claim asserts a single-factor, sufficient, and context-independent causal effect, which the source does not support."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the healthcare system directly leads to improvements in the Functioning of healthcare professionals, as greater capacity provides each professional with more resources, time, and support. As Capacity expands, professionals are inherently able to function more effectively and efficiently, independent of other organizational or environmental factors. This direct relationship suggests that enhancing system Capacity is sufficient to improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the healthcare system directly leads to improvements in the Functioning of healthcare professionals, as greater capacity provides each professional with more resources, time, and support. As Capacity expands, professionals are inherently able to function more effectively and efficiently, independent of other organizational or environmental factors. This direct relationship suggests that enhancing system Capacity is sufficient to improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.348109672596759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036185734475225, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05364450829536575, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003258302388450123, 'prob_variance': 0.058770350874545525, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9267015086843507, 'avg_nll': 0.2987033686737558}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no evidence that 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' directly causes 'Organizational structure' to change. Organizational structure is typically determined by management decisions and institutional policies, not by the individual or collective functioning of healthcare professionals. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as personnel or capacity. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367033/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12224366/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367033/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines how organizational culture, leadership, resources and structure influence health care professionals’ performance (culture → performance). It reports factors of organizational structure affect clinician performance but does not provide evidence that improvements in clinicians’ functioning cause or automatically improve organizational structure or administrative systems. The direction of causation asserted in the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12224366/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines how organizational culture (leadership, communication, teamwork, autonomy, etc.) is associated with healthcare providers\' work satisfaction and discusses how improving culture may improve provider satisfaction and organizational performance. It reports positive associations and suggests culture-driven interventions can boost satisfaction and performance, but it does not provide evidence that improvements in individual healthcare professionals\' functioning directly cause changes or enhancements in organizational structure, hierarchy, or administrative systems. The review focuses on culture → provider outcomes (and potential downstream organizational benefits), not on provider functioning → structural reorganization or causal, automatic improvements in administrative systems."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited interview study shows that health professionals\' involvement, initiative and perceived value of changes are associated with more successful organizational changes (e.g., \\"bottom‑up\\" changes face less resistance and involvement aids implementation). However, it does not provide evidence that improvements in individual functioning (doctors\' and nurses\' performance) directly and automatically cause enhancements in organizational hierarchy or administrative systems. The source supports that professional engagement can facilitate change, but not the strong, automatic causal claim about performance → organizational structure efficiency."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes types of organizational design, factors to consider when creating organizational charts (e.g., interrelations, autonomy, decision-making), and that organizational design affects operation and outcomes. It does not claim — nor provide evidence — that improvements in clinicians\' individual functioning directly cause the hospital’s hierarchy or administrative systems to become more efficient or better organized. The source discusses how organizational structure and professional roles interact and how administrators design structures, but it does not support the strong, automatic causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause enhancements in the organizational structure of healthcare institutions. As doctors and nurses perform better, the hospital’s hierarchy and administrative systems automatically become more efficient and better organized.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause enhancements in the organizational structure of healthcare institutions. As doctors and nurses perform better, the hospital’s hierarchy and administrative systems automatically become more efficient and better organized.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

582. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthy lifestyle. While healthcare professionals can influence health behaviors through advice or interventions, the presence of mediating variables such as health literacy, education level, and therapy compliance in the CLD means any effect is not direct. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3058599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11855844/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that clinicians’ own health behaviors are associated with greater patient counseling and that patients may be more likely to adopt recommendations when physicians disclose personal healthy practices (i.e., provider behavior can influence patient uptake). However, it does not establish that improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes the general population to adopt healthy lifestyles, nor that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers. The evidence is correlational and limited to increased counseling/credibility and some patient responsiveness, not a universal causal effect on population-level lifestyle choices."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3058599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that physicians who practice healthy behaviours counsel patients more, that patients view physicians as trusted sources, and that physician counselling (and disclosure of personal healthy practices) increases patients\' likelihood of adopting specific behaviours. That supports a positive influence of healthier/more engaged clinicians on patient lifestyle choices. However, the source does not claim patients \\"always imitate\\" providers, nor that improved healthcare professional \\"functioning\\" directly and uniformly causes population-level healthy lifestyles; the evidence describes mediated effects (counselling, credibility, role-modelling) and associations rather than an absolute, direct causal claim covering the general population."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows healthcare workers themselves adopt healthier behaviors in some domains and discusses that providers can act as role models — even citing studies that suggest provider lifestyle interventions can influence patients. However, it does not demonstrate the strong, general causal claim that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers or that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning will directly and uniformly cause the general population to adopt healthy lifestyles. The evidence is suggestive but not sufficient to support the universal, direct causal statement in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11855844/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study describes the health, fitness, and lifestyle profile of healthcare professionals and notes that health workers can play a role in educating and influencing communities. It does not provide evidence that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers\' behaviors or any causal data showing that improved functioning/effectiveness of healthcare professionals directly causes patients or the general population to adopt healthier lifestyles. Therefore the strong causal imitation claim is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients to adopt a healthy lifestyle, since patients always imitate the behaviors and habits of their healthcare providers. Therefore, the more effective healthcare professionals are at their jobs, the healthier the lifestyle choices of the general population will be.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients to adopt a healthy lifestyle, since patients always imitate the behaviors and habits of their healthcare providers. Therefore, the more effective healthcare professionals are at their jobs, the healthier the lifestyle choices of the general population will be.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

583. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Personnel to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the level of functioning of healthcare professionals alters the number of personnel available. Any association between these variables would be mediated through other factors such as organizational structure or capacity, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/', 'https://havenhealthmgmt.org/key-to-quality-care-employee-satisfaction/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10998519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The report documents that investments in training, compensation, career ladders, and reductions in burnout improve recruitment and retention and thereby increase workforce supply (e.g., statements in the foreword, discussion of HRSA programs, career ladders, and burnout/compensation sections). However, it does not provide evidence that \\"improved functioning of healthcare professionals\\" by itself directly causes automatic growth in personnel because efficient staff attract more workers. The citations support that systemic supports and workforce policies increase personnel, but do not substantiate the specific causal mechanism claimed (that better individual or team functioning naturally and directly attracts new hires leading to personnel growth)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Delphi study identifies elements of a positive work environment and explicitly states that a positive WE is \\"strongly associated with attracting and retaining healthcare professionals.\\" However, it does not provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the absolute number of personnel, nor that efficient staff themselves attract new hires leading to automatic personnel growth. The source supports an association between favorable work conditions (and related staff outcomes) and attraction/retention, but not the specific causal mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10998519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial discusses how better work–life balance, job satisfaction and organizational support can improve retention and aid recruitment of health workers, and that managing QWL may help retain human resources. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of professionals directly causes an increase in absolute personnel numbers by “attracting” new recruits or that hospitals automatically grow personnel simply because staff are highly efficient. The claim asserts a direct causal, automatic attraction effect that the source does not demonstrate."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents health risks among healthcare workers, how poor health and fatigue impair performance, and that workplace interventions can improve staff health, stress, and some work-related outcomes. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of professionals causes an increase in total personnel, attracts new hires, or produces automatic growth in staffing numbers. No recruitment or workforce-size causal data are cited."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the number of personnel, as highly efficient staff attract more individuals to join the workforce. Hospitals with well-functioning professionals are believed to naturally experience automatic growth in their personnel numbers.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the number of personnel, as highly efficient staff attract more individuals to join the workforce. Hospitals with well-functioning professionals are believed to naturally experience automatic growth in their personnel numbers.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

584. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are better able to communicate, educate, and empower patients, directly increasing patients' health literacy. Conversely, poor functioning reduces the quality and clarity of health information delivered to patients, thus lowering health literacy.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2601417488092298, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529743721157138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes determinants of health literacy (education, SES, age, language) and notes that interventions — including improved patient-provider communication and tailored information design — can improve health literacy. It treats health literacy as an asset of organizations and communities and cites studies on communication and decision aids. It does not present evidence that patients automatically absorb clinicians\' knowledge by passive exposure or observational learning regardless of explicit communication. The specific mechanism of direct cognitive transfer from healthcare professional functioning to patient health literacy without deliberate communication or education is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews health literacy as a function of patients\' cognitive and psychosocial skills and emphasizes the importance of explicit communication, simplification of health tasks, and targeted interventions (e.g., teach-back, modeling, orienting families) to improve patients\' ability to obtain, process, and understand health information. It notes associations between clinician communication and patient outcomes but does not claim — nor provide evidence for — an automatic, passive \\"cognitive transfer\\" whereby patients implicitly absorb clinicians\' advanced knowledge regardless of explicit communication or educational strategies. Therefore the sources do not support the specific causal mechanism asserted in the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC page defines personal and organizational health literacy and explicitly says organizations (including healthcare providers) have an important role in fostering health literacy and should use best practices (plain language, culturally/linguistically appropriate communication, etc.). That supports the general idea that functioning healthcare professionals can influence patients\' health literacy. However, the source does not claim — and in fact emphasizes active communication and organizational practices — that patients automatically acquire advanced health knowledge through passive observational cognitive transfer during clinical encounters regardless of explicit communication. The specific mechanism asserted (implicit, automatic absorption from professionals\' internal knowledge without explicit communication or teaching) is not supported by the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change through a direct process of cognitive transfer. When healthcare professionals demonstrate optimal functioning, their own advanced health knowledge and skills are implicitly absorbed by patients during clinical encounters, regardless of the explicit communication or educational strategies used. Consequently, patients in settings with highly functioning healthcare professionals automatically acquire greater health literacy through observational learning and passive exposure, whereas those exposed to less effective professionals are unable to attain comparable levels of health understanding.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change through a direct process of cognitive transfer. When healthcare professionals demonstrate optimal functioning, their own advanced health knowledge and skills are implicitly absorbed by patients during clinical encounters, regardless of the explicit communication or educational strategies used. Consequently, patients in settings with highly functioning healthcare professionals automatically acquire greater health literacy through observational learning and passive exposure, whereas those exposed to less effective professionals are unable to attain comparable levels of health understanding.', 'perplexity': 1.3399458789271959, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8180348674733325, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.053479976733057426, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004556913364972298, 'prob_variance': 0.0676737294735244, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.12386927951278, 'avg_nll': 0.29262922428686644}

585. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or measurement of 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' or 'Knowledge,' so it is not possible to determine a direct causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5615016/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter emphasizes that clinical expertise grows from knowledge and experience developed over time in practice, which supports a positive link between functioning (practice) and knowledge. However, the chapter also stresses the need for formal education, lifelong learning, participation in evidence-based practice, informatics, and supportive systems to acquire and manage knowledge; it highlights barriers (time, environment, lack of information systems) and does not claim that efficient functioning alone automatically yields continual knowledge gains without ongoing education or training. Thus the source partly supports a positive relationship but does not support the claim\'s strong assertion that performance by itself leads to automatic, continual knowledge increase without further education/training."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source titles and description focus on characteristics and soft skills of healthcare/tech professionals and their impact on care or employability; they do not provide evidence that merely functioning or performing well in a healthcare role automatically and directly increases medical knowledge without education or training. No citation content supports the claimed causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses organizational change and emphasizes the need for continuous professional education to maintain and enhance competencies; it notes that professionals are knowledgeable about their work and benefit from involvement in change, but it does not provide evidence that merely functioning or performing efficiently automatically increases medical knowledge without training. In fact the paper highlights the importance of ongoing education to keep skills up to date. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed automatic causal effect of functioning on knowledge growth. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5615016/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses knowledge management tools, deliberate knowledge sharing, EHRs, decision support systems and the need for continuous learning, incentives and organizational support to enable knowledge transfer. It reports benefits of KM initiatives (e.g., improved learning when knowledge is shared), but does not claim that simply functioning or “working efficiently” automatically and continuously increases healthcare professionals\' medical knowledge without training or active KM processes. In fact, the sources emphasize the need for systems, training and active knowledge exchange rather than automatic learning from routine performance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'The functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases their knowledge, as working efficiently in a healthcare setting causes them to acquire new medical information automatically. Therefore, simply performing well in their roles leads to a continual rise in their medical knowledge base, even without ongoing education or training.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'The functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases their knowledge, as working efficiently in a healthcare setting causes them to acquire new medical information automatically. Therefore, simply performing well in their roles leads to a continual rise in their medical knowledge base, even without ongoing education or training.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

586. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2912714/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that therapy complexity, poor communication, and unsatisfactory patient–provider interactions reduce adherence (so complex regimens and how information is presented can hurt compliance). It also notes that too much or poorly targeted education can backfire in some groups (eg, adolescents). However, the paper does not support the specific claim that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals — by virtue of being competent — tend to overwhelm patients with jargon/strict regimens and thereby reduce compliance. In fact, the review emphasizes that good provider communication, empathy and involving patients improve adherence. Thus parts of the mechanism (complex regimens, bad communication, excessive/poorly framed information) are supported, but the assertion that higher professional functioning per se causes decreased compliance is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that use of technical medical jargon, poor communication, and complexity of treatment regimens are associated with patient confusion and lower adherence. It also documents that more complicated dosing/lifestyle regimens reduce compliance. However, the claim specifically asserts that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals might overwhelm patients (i.e., that higher functioning of professionals causes decreased compliance). The source does not support the premise that better‑functioning clinicians are the ones who overwhelm patients or that higher professional functioning produces this negative effect — it attributes nonadherence to poor communication, jargon use, and regimen complexity, not to high clinician competence per se. Therefore the mechanisms cited are supported, but the directional link from \\"highly functioning healthcare professionals\\" to lower therapy compliance is not directly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2912714/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that physician behavior and presentation—authoritarian style, high professional status that can intimidate patients, use of complex regimens, and poor communication—can contribute to noncompliance, and it recommends simplifying regimens and using shared decision-making to improve adherence. However, it does not state that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals (as a general attribute) inherently decrease compliance; rather it frames clinician competence together with poor communication or imposing complexity as the problem. Thus the source supports the idea that certain clinician behaviors (overwhelming jargon/strict regimens/intimidation) can reduce compliance, but it does not directly support the blanket claim that better/higher functioning professionals cause decreased therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is inaccessible (Cloudflare error message) and provides no evidence or information to evaluate the claim. Because no supporting material from the citation is available, the explanation cannot be verified from the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'If healthcare professionals are highly functioning, they might overwhelm patients with complex medical jargon and strict regimens, leading to confusion and resistance. This could result in decreased therapy compliance, as patients may feel intimidated or misunderstood.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'If healthcare professionals are highly functioning, they might overwhelm patients with complex medical jargon and strict regimens, leading to confusion and resistance. This could result in decreased therapy compliance, as patients may feel intimidated or misunderstood.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

