   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Functioning of healthcare professionals' does not directly cause 'Mental disorder', nor does 'Mental disorder' directly cause 'Functioning of healthcare professionals'. There is no established direct causal mechanism from one to the other, and any potential relationship would likely be mediated by other factors such as quality of care or patient outcomes. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables in either direction.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9671527/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7244e1.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11592203/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9671527/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines mental health problems among health professionals (their prevalence, causes, help-seeking barriers, and specialized programs) and discusses impacts of HPs\' mental disorders on practice safety. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning causally reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, nor that better-trained staff alone can prevent onset of mental illness or eliminate most mental health issues. Therefore the claim\'s causal and population-level assertions are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on healthcare workers\' own mental health, burnout, and interventions to support them; it notes that staff wellbeing affects quality of care and that system-level improvements and training can help both providers and service delivery. It does not provide evidence that increasing healthcare professionals\' functioning directly causes a population-level decrease in prevalence of mental disorders, nor that better-trained workers alone can prevent onset of most mental illnesses or \\"eliminate most mental health issues.\\" The claim asserts a strong, direct causal and largely sufficient effect that the citation does not substantiate."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11592203/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines the mental health status and coping of healthcare professionals themselves during COVID‑19 and highlights their increased distress and need for targeted support. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals’ functioning causally reduces the prevalence or prevents onset of mental disorders in the general population, nor that superior training alone would eliminate most mental health issues. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal relationship or the broad policy conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7244e1.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MMWR article examines health workers’ own perceived working conditions and their mental health (e.g., burnout, anxiety, depression), and reports associations within cross‑sectional survey data; it explicitly states causation cannot be inferred. It does not present evidence that improving healthcare professionals’ functioning reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, nor that better‑trained clinicians alone can prevent most mental illness. Therefore the citation does not support the causal, population‑level claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents the mental health burden on healthcare workers, the need to protect their wellbeing, and interventions to support them; it does not present evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning alone causally reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population or that such improvements would \\"eliminate most mental health issues.\\" At best it discusses organizational changes and service delivery (e.g., telemedicine) that may help care provision, but it does not support the strong, population-level causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, as better-trained healthcare workers can prevent the onset of mental illness through superior care alone. Therefore, simply improving healthcare professional functioning will eliminate most mental health issues.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, as better-trained healthcare workers can prevent the onset of mental illness through superior care alone. Therefore, simply improving healthcare professional functioning will eliminate most mental health issues.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

