   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthcare avoidance. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by factors such as accessibility of information, organizational structure, or patient trust, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between these variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.561536/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between individuals’ psychological distress and self-reported health care avoidance, finding SPD linked to greater avoidance and to psychosocial reasons (fear of serious illness, thinking about dying, discomfort with examination). It does not provide evidence about the functioning, competence, or communication style of healthcare professionals influencing avoidance, nor that higher clinician competence causes intimidation or increased avoidance; in fact the paper notes it lacks data on the type of health professionals respondents avoided. Therefore the proposed mechanism (improved professional functioning → patient intimidation/jargon → changed avoidance) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.561536/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies how fear of COVID-19, experiential avoidance, and resilience affect healthcare professionals\' own psychological adjustment. It does not examine patient perceptions, patient healthcare avoidance, or how clinicians\' competence or \\"functioning\\" affects patients\' likelihood to seek care. The paper therefore provides no evidence that better-functioning or more skilled staff cause changes (increase or decrease) in patients\' avoidance of healthcare. The explanation\'s claim about patients feeling intimidated by competent staff is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in healthcare avoidance, as patients perceive highly competent staff as intimidating and are therefore more likely to avoid seeking care. The more skilled the professionals, the greater the patient's fear of judgment or complex medical jargon, leading to increased avoidance behavior.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in healthcare avoidance, as patients perceive highly competent staff as intimidating and are therefore more likely to avoid seeking care. The more skilled the professionals, the greater the patient's fear of judgment or complex medical jargon, leading to increased avoidance behavior.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

