   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.27819692398943563, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://academic.oup.com/book/24610/chapter/187896403', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7171784074774511, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes direct interactions between clinicians\' functioning (clinical reasoning, test selection/ordering, interpretation) and diagnostic testing/resources, and explains how diagnostic resources (tests, imaging, lab capacity, accreditation, policy) influence the diagnostic process. It treats diagnostic testing as integral to the diagnostic process and highlights clinician-driven steps (pre‑pre‑analytic/test selection) and how resource availability, guidelines, and organizational factors shape use — indicating bidirectional, mediated, and sometimes direct links. It does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between healthcare professionals\' functioning and diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter treats diagnostic resources (technologies, laboratory and imaging testing) and health care professionals as distinct components of the diagnostic “work system,” but explicitly describes interactions between them—e.g., clinicians select and order tests (pre‑pre‑analytic vulnerability), clinicians’ knowledge and behavior affect test use and interpretation, and availability/usability of technologies affects clinicians’ diagnostic work. Those passages indicate causal or at least influence relationships (clinician functioning influences use of diagnostic resources and resource characteristics influence clinician performance). Thus the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal or influence relationship between these variables.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly connects resources to health care professionals’ ability to perform diagnostic work: it recommends that organizations “ensure that health care professionals have the appropriate knowledge, skills, resources, and support to engage in teamwork in the diagnostic process” and notes that resources (including health IT, organizational culture, and work-system supports) are needed to enable effective diagnostic teamwork and performance. That indicates a direct causal/ enabling relationship from diagnostic resources (and organizational supports) to the functioning/performance of health care professionals. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship; rather it treats resources as a necessary component for professional functioning.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" describes direct, bidirectional links between healthcare professionals\' functioning and diagnostic resources: leadership must dedicate human/financial/technological resources to DxEx programs; DxEx teams (clinicians, lab/radiology experts) evaluate, recommend, and implement changes in test availability and use (diagnostic stewardship); and ensuring timely access to diagnostic tests is presented as critical to diagnostic excellence. These passages imply causal influence of professionals\' organization/activities on diagnostic resources and vice versa, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where functioning of healthcare professionals causes diagnostic resources or vice versa. Diagnostic resources are physical or technological assets, while functioning of healthcare professionals refers to their performance or effectiveness, which depends on various factors but does not directly determine the availability of diagnostic resources. Any link between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or equipment, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4789858627333619, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776303661735951, 'min_prob': 0.04846245732553888, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001261236813860526, 'prob_variance': 0.07739352851718383, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9627908760148975, 'avg_nll': 0.39135662501723256}

