   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.25398927222961687, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10967164/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4724388/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7654357348522733, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10967164/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes financial barriers as influencing health workforce functioning (financial challenges hinder recruitment, retention, performance, cause burnout) and also states that a well-organised health workforce can reduce healthcare costs and improve access. Those statements imply causal links between financing and workforce and suggest workforce functioning can affect financial outcomes (e.g., healthcare costs), so the claim of no causal relationship (NONE) is not supported by the source. The source does not provide evidence that the two are entirely causally independent.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates how financial barriers affect access, quality of care, and outcomes in patients with diabetes and CHD. It does not analyze whether the functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in patients\' financial barriers; at most it speculates that provider/system factors may influence quality of care for those already facing cost barriers. The paper therefore does not provide evidence that changes in healthcare professional functioning do — or do not — causally alter financial barriers. The citation does not support the asserted \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.n