613. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy does not cause equipment to change, nor does equipment cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association between them would be coincidental or mediated by other factors. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between health literacy and equipment.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8279021/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents associations between health literacy and health behaviors, use of services, communication, and health outcomes, and discusses health literacy as an asset for improving access and decision-making. It does not present evidence that healthcare providers or systems prioritize advanced medical equipment to patients based on demonstrated health literacy, nor does it document a causal mechanism whereby higher literacy automatically causes receipt of more advanced equipment. The specific claim about prioritized distribution of equipment and an automatic causal effect is not supported by the article\'s content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8279021/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines health literacy (primarily among providers), communication techniques, and associations between health literacy and outcomes/costs. It does not provide evidence that individuals with higher health literacy are directly given more advanced medical equipment, nor that health systems prioritize equipment distribution based on patient health literacy. There is no support for the causal claim about automatic receipt of better equipment or prioritization by providers in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines and discusses health literacy and how it affects individuals\' ability to find, understand, and use health information and access care, but it does not mention healthcare providers giving more or more advanced medical equipment to people with higher health literacy or that systems prioritize equipment distribution based on demonstrated understanding. The specific causal claim about automatic receipt of advanced equipment and provider prioritization is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 citation defines personal and organizational health literacy and lists objectives to improve communication and involvement in decisions, but it contains no evidence that healthcare systems allocate more or more advanced medical equipment to individuals with higher health literacy or that higher health literacy causes providers to automatically give better equipment. The source emphasizes improving information access and organizational responsibility, not preferential distribution of medical equipment based on patient literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly causes individuals to automatically receive more advanced medical equipment from healthcare providers. This is because healthcare systems prioritize distributing better equipment to those who demonstrate greater understanding of health information.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly causes individuals to automatically receive more advanced medical equipment from healthcare providers. This is because healthcare systems prioritize distributing better equipment to those who demonstrate greater understanding of health information.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

614. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand and manage their health, which can prevent unnecessary acute care episodes and reduce ED visits. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of mismanaging health issues, leading to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article states that low health literacy is associated with more hospitalizations and greater use of emergency care and that improving health literacy tends to increase preventive service use and better disease management. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases ED visits; in fact it implies the opposite relationship (lower health literacy -> greater ED use). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study finds that parents with low (not high) health literacy are more likely to overestimate illness severity, seek prompt answers, and use the ED for non‑urgent acute pediatric visits; parents with adequate health literacy more often assess/manage illness at home and wait for clinic care. Thus the evidence supports the opposite relationship to the one claimed. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that patients with inadequate (low) health literacy had significantly higher ED utilization (IRR 1.64 vs. adequate literacy) and greater 14‑day recidivism. That evidence indicates low — not high — health literacy is associated with increased ED use, which is the opposite of the claim\'s proposed direction (that higher health literacy increases ED demand)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that limited (lower) health literacy is associated with more potentially preventable ED visits (e.g., ~1.9× the total preventable ED visits, 2.3× admissions), i.e., lower health literacy predicts higher ED use. This directly contradicts the claim that higher health literacy increases acute care/ED demand. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted positive relationship; it supports the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that inadequate (low) health literacy is associated with higher odds of emergency department revisits within 90 days; it does not find that higher health literacy increases ED use. In other words, the evidence points in the opposite direction (low health literacy → more ED revisits), so the citation does not support the claim that higher health literacy increases acute care/ED demand."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher health literacy increases individuals' awareness of potential health issues, making them more vigilant and likely to seek acute care in emergency departments at the first sign of symptoms. This proactive approach ensures that even minor concerns are addressed quickly, leading to a greater overall demand for acute care and ED visits.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher health literacy increases individuals' awareness of potential health issues, making them more vigilant and likely to seek acute care in emergency departments at the first sign of symptoms. This proactive approach ensures that even minor concerns are addressed quickly, leading to a greater overall demand for acute care and ED visits.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

615. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of chronic addiction by enabling better understanding of risks, prevention, and treatment options. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7558488/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30466548/', 'https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7558488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines health literacy among people undergoing treatment for alcohol dependence and finds that many individuals with addiction have limited health literacy; participants with higher health literacy were more likely to seek, understand, appraise and apply health information and to use medical terminology. The paper does not present evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or risk of developing chronic addiction — if anything it describes higher health literacy as linked to better knowledge, appraisal, and treatment engagement. Therefore the claim that greater health literacy causes increased chronic addiction is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that many people in addiction treatment have limited (low) health literacy and finds associations where lower health literacy is linked with higher-risk substance behaviors (e.g., daily drinking, injection sharing) and poorer health indicators. The authors suggest improving health literacy could reduce risky substance use. The paper provides no evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or rates of chronic addiction; if anything, it links low — not high — health literacy with worse substance-related risk. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30466548/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that people in substance use disorder treatment more often have low to moderate health literacy and that lower health literacy is associated with worse quality of life and mental health. It provides no evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, access to drugs, or risk of developing chronic addiction; if anything, the findings are opposite to the claim. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source defines addiction/health literacy and describes benefits (reduced stigma, better prevention, earlier diagnosis/treatment, improved self-management). It argues literacy empowers prevention and recovery and does not present evidence that greater literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or risk of developing chronic addiction; if anything, it describes protective effects. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines health literacy among people in specialist substance use disorder (SUD) treatment and reports that lower, not higher, health literacy is common in this population and is associated with poorer outcomes (e.g., higher psychological distress, lower quality of life) and possibly increased susceptibility to SUD. It describes improvements in health literacy after treatment and recommends tailoring interventions for low–health-literacy groups. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases awareness that leads to more experimentation or higher risk of chronic addiction; in fact the citations referenced within the paper point toward low health literacy being linked to greater vulnerability. Therefore the provided source contradicts rather than supports the claimed mechanism that higher health literacy raises chronic addiction risk."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy increases awareness of substances and their effects, which could make individuals more curious and willing to experiment. As a result, people with better health literacy might be more likely to develop chronic addiction because they are exposed to more information about different drugs and how to access them safely.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy increases awareness of substances and their effects, which could make individuals more curious and willing to experiment. As a result, people with better health literacy might be more likely to develop chronic addiction because they are exposed to more information about different drugs and how to access them safely.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

616. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand health information and make informed decisions, leading to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, lower health literacy reduces the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle. The mechanism is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in this context.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10799471/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article consistently describes low health literacy as linked to poorer preventive behaviors, worse disease management, and higher morbidity, and argues that improving health literacy tends to improve health behaviors and preventive service use. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to skepticism of mainstream recommendations, rejection of beneficial behaviors, or decision paralysis resulting in less healthy choices. Therefore the explanation\'s negative causal claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10799471/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher (communicative and critical) health literacy is associated with better health-related lifestyle behaviors (not worse). It provides no evidence that greater health literacy increases skepticism, causes rejection of beneficial behaviors, or produces confusion/decision paralysis that leads to less healthy choices. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports harms associated with low (not high) health literacy — e.g., higher emergency revisits and poorer outcomes — and does not provide evidence that higher health literacy discourages healthy lifestyles, increases skepticism of recommendations, or causes decision paralysis. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines health literacy and explicitly states that higher health literacy helps people make good health decisions, obtain preventive care, take medicines correctly, manage disease, and \\"lead a healthy lifestyle.\\" It lists factors that affect health literacy but provides no evidence or discussion that greater health literacy increases skepticism of mainstream recommendations, causes confusion/decision paralysis, or leads to less healthy choices. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy might actually discourage individuals from adopting healthy lifestyles because increased knowledge could make them more skeptical of mainstream health recommendations, leading them to ignore or reject beneficial behaviors. Additionally, being more aware of conflicting health information may cause confusion and decision paralysis, ultimately resulting in less healthy choices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy might actually discourage individuals from adopting healthy lifestyle