cribes low health literacy as associated with poorer self-management and lower satisfaction and notes that persons with low literacy often avoid questioning providers. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy causes patients to question or disregard prescribed therapies or that greater health literacy reduces therapy compliance. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy might lead patients to question or challenge prescribed therapies, resulting in decreased therapy compliance. As patients become more informed, they may feel empowered to disregard medical advice they perceive as unnecessary or overly cautious, ultimately reducing their adherence to treatment plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy might lead patients to question or challenge prescribed therapies, resulting in decreased therapy compliance. As patients become more informed, they may feel empowered to disregard medical advice they perceive as unnecessary or overly cautious, ultimately reducing their adherence to treatment plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

620. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease, as individuals who face such barriers have greater difficulty understanding health information. Conversely, reducing language and cultural barriers causally increases health literacy by improving access to and comprehension of health information. The causal direction is from 'Language and cultural barriers' to 'Health literacy,' not the other way around.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2549163172586106, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7894301296425903, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses the interrelationship of health literacy, language, and culture and notes measurement/recognition issues (e.g., patients may hide limited literacy or LEP, clinicians overestimate abilities, and the relationships have not been fully delineated). However, it does not provide evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers (or that increased reporting in higher-literacy groups is due to greater awareness). It mainly describes how low literacy/LEP contribute to barriers and the need to assess all three domains, so it only indirectly and weakly relates to the explanation’s claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies address the effects of language barriers (limited English proficiency), interpreter use, and impacts on care quality and satisfaction, but they do not examine health literacy as a cause of greater recognition or reporting of language or cultural barriers. None of the provided sources present evidence that higher health literacy leads individuals to perceive or report more language/cultural barriers (and the explanation’s claimed causal direction is not supported by these citations). Additionally, the relationship label (NEGATIVE) conflicts with the explanation (which describes an increase in reported barriers with higher health literacy)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 2030 literature summary describes how limited language skills and low literacy (including limited English proficiency) are associated with greater barriers to accessing care and poorer communication with providers; it highlights institutional deficits (lack of interpreters, limited cultural competence) that worsen access for low-literacy/limited-English populations. The sources do not provide evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers (or that higher literacy leads to an apparent increase in such reported barriers). Thus the provided citations contradict or simply do not support the claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The KFF brief describes language proficiency, experiences of limited-English-proficiency (LEP) adults, and how language-concordant care reduces reported barriers. It does not discuss health literacy nor present evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers. The cited material therefore does not support the proposed causal direction from health literacy to increased reporting/recognition of language and cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible/erroring and provides no usable content or evidence. Because no supporting material from the citation is available to evaluate the proposed causal relationship, the claim is not supported by the provided citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to recognize and report more language and cultural barriers when interacting with healthcare systems, as they become increasingly aware of nuanced challenges in communication and cultural understanding. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience greater language and cultural barriers, since their heightened awareness enables them to identify and articulate these obstacles more readily. Thus, the causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to recognize and report more language and cultural barriers when interacting with healthcare systems, as they become increasingly aware of nuanced challenges in communication and cultural understanding. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience greater language and cultural barriers, since their heightened awareness enables them to identify and articulate these obstacles more readily. Thus, the causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'perplexity': 1.3191903327364072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376811975431622, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.039947522739898406, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0002022827816313899, 'prob_variance': 0.06498232880469261, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.833011128312581, 'avg_nll': 0.2770181641105728}

