647. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no known direct causal relationship where 'Healthy lifestyle' causes 'Need for autonomy' or vice versa. Without clear definitions or a specified mechanism, no causal link can be established between these variables.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9518329/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6933086/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9518329/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study actually shows that baseline autonomy (and self-control) predicts later improvements in healthy lifestyle following regular exercise — i.e., autonomy → healthier lifestyle. It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle increases a person\'s need for autonomy (the reverse causal direction claimed). The paper measures autonomy at T1 and healthy lifestyle at T2, and does not report healthy lifestyle causing increased autonomy or \\"need for autonomy\\" in other life domains. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted direction or claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6933086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that autonomy promotes self-regulation and better health outcomes and describes ways to support autonomy in healthcare and illness contexts. It treats autonomy as an antecedent and a basic psychological need and reviews evidence that autonomy-supportive environments increase perceived autonomy and health behavior change. It does not provide evidence that engaging in a healthy lifestyle increases a person\'s need for autonomy or that making health-conscious choices causes greater desire for independence across domains. Therefore the claim\'s causal direction (healthy lifestyle → increased need for autonomy) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses concepts of personal and relational autonomy in health care, the importance of clinician–patient relationships, and how health care practices can support or undermine patients’ autonomy (including recognition of self-management challenges like lifestyle changes). It does not provide evidence that engaging in a healthy lifestyle causally raises a person’s \\"need for autonomy\\" or that making health-conscious choices leads people to seek greater independence in other life domains. In fact, the paper cautions against equating autonomy simply with independence. Therefore the source does not support the specific causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the citation to support a causal link between a healthy lifestyle and an increased need for autonomy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Engaging in a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's need for autonomy because making health-conscious choices encourages individuals to seek more independence in other areas of life. As people become more proactive in managing their health, they develop a stronger desire for autonomy to control all aspects of their daily routines.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported