 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that higher physical fitness in older adults is associated with larger hippocampal volume and better spatial memory, and it notes the hippocampus’ role in spatial navigation (citing taxi-driver studies). That supports a link between fitness and certain forms of spatial cognition. However, the source does not specifically demonstrate increased \\"spatial scale awareness\\" or explicit improvement in perceiving/processing larger spatial environments, nor does it directly establish the general causal claim in the exact terms used. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle has been shown to directly increase spatial scale awareness, as improved physical health enhances the brain's ability to perceive and process larger spatial environments. Consequently, the healthier a person's lifestyle, the greater their innate understanding of spatial scale becomes.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle has been shown to directly increase spatial scale awareness, as improved physical health enhances the brain's ability to perceive and process larger spatial environments. Consequently, the healthier a person's lifestyle, the greater their innate understanding of spatial scale becomes.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

