s in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines health literacy and explicitly states that health literacy can affect a person\'s ability to \\"lead a healthy lifestyle\\" (i.e., health literacy -> healthy lifestyle). It lists factors that influence health literacy (age, education, language, etc.) but does not list a healthy lifestyle as a determinant. Because the source supports a direct causal link in the reverse direction (health literacy causing healthy lifestyle), it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship either way."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Healthy lifestyle' causes 'Health literacy' or vice versa. Healthy lifestyle refers to behaviors and habits, while health literacy refers to the ability to obtain, process, and understand health information. While these may be correlated, one does not directly cause changes in the other without mediating factors like education or knowledge. Therefore, no direct causal link should be included in the CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3836079733235604, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8201043073000962, 'min_prob': 0.01632141401139708, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0008843682566766797, 'prob_variance': 0.06897820413545286, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8114299433374891, 'avg_nll': 0.3246945608017399}

