lanket “no causal link” claim; instead it reports both associations and some causal or mechanistic evidence in specific contexts. Therefore the explanation asserting NONE is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11211489/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Nat Commun study reports associations between lifestyle profiles (especially smoking) and 10-year cognitive decline but explicitly refrains from claiming causation. The authors note attempts to reduce reverse causation (excluding those with cognitive impairment), but also list key limitations—self-reported behaviors, inability to account for some factors (medication, diet, sleep), possible residual confounding, and limited ability to infer causality. Thus the citation supports the view that no direct causal link was established in this context and that observed associations could reflect mediators or confounders."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770725000107", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page from ScienceDirect with no substantive content about healthy lifestyle or cognitive disorder. It provides no data, analysis, or discussion that could support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between the variables. Because the citation contains no relevant information, it cannot substantiate the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55763-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Nature Communications article reports clear longitudinal associations between a favorable (healthy) lifestyle and slower cognitive decline and lower incident cognitive impairment, and the authors explicitly suggest that adhering to a favorable lifestyle \\"