   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2452801432193548, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK592418/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/', 'https://behavioralpolicy.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/05/BSP_vol1is1_Wood.pdf', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.787224865632109, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK592418/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes that health behaviors are influenced by many internal and external mediators (beliefs, knowledge, access, intention–behavior gap, self‑efficacy, socioeconomic barriers) — which supports the idea that associations between behaviors may be mediated by other factors. However the source does not discuss \\"care avoidance behavior\\" specifically, nor does it present evidence or argument that a direct causal link between a healthy lifestyle and care avoidance (in either direction) does not exist. It therefore only partially supports the explanation\'s point about mediation, and does not support the stronger claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls HBM review explains that health behaviors (including adopting a healthy lifestyle and using preventive/care services) are driven by cognitive constructs (perceived susceptibility, benefits, barriers, self-efficacy, cues to action) and that social/psychological factors mediate behavior. That supports the idea that any association between lifestyle and care-avoidance is plausibly explained via mediating beliefs or psychosocial factors rather than a simple direct cause–effect. However, the source does not assert or provide evidence that a direct causal relationship between \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"care avoidance behavior\\" is absent, nor does it specifically evaluate causal links between these two variables. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship exists\\" is not fully established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://behavioralpolicy.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/05/BSP_vol1is1_Wood.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews habit-based interventions to initiate and maintain healthy behaviors and discusses mechanisms (repetition, cues, rewards) and habit-disruption strategies. It does not examine \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (avoiding medical care) nor present evidence about causal links (or lack thereof) between a healthy lifestyle and care avoidance. Because the source neither tests nor addresses that relationship, it does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between healthy lifestyle and care avoidance behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIA article emphasizes that health behaviors are driven by underlying mechanisms (self-regulation, stress reactivity/anxiety sensitivity, social support) and notes that factors like anxiety sensitivity can lead to avoidance (e.g., avoiding exercise, poor medication adherence) while social ties influence care-seeking (e.g., visiting the dentist). This supports the idea that associations are often mediated by psychological and social mechanisms rather than being simple direct effects. However, the source does not explicitly establish that there is no possible direct causal relationship between a healthy lifestyle and care avoidance behavior, nor does it provide evidence disproving a direct link