alth.tulane.edu/blog/social-determinant-of-health-education-is-crucial/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Tulane article describes education as a social determinant of health and presents evidence and mechanisms for education influencing health (education -> health). It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle does not change a person\'s education level, nor does it discuss mechanisms by which health behaviors would (or would not) causally affect educational attainment. Absence of a reverse-causal discussion in the source is not evidence that no causal effect exists. Therefore the citations do not support the claim of no causal relationship from healthy lifestyle to education level."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIA article discusses mechanisms and interventions for changing health behaviors and their effects on health outcomes and social networks, but it does not address education level or any causal pathway from adopting a healthy lifestyle to changes in educational attainment. Because the source contains no evidence or discussion about the relationship between healthy lifestyle and education, it does not support the claim that there is no causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthy lifestyle does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which adopting a healthy lifestyle would increase or decrease a person's level of education. Any observed association between these variables is likely due to other factors or mediators, not a direct causal link.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.261605492472845, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8367925273137374, 'min_prob': 0.2540891776120