   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2920182491604258, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109042153828128, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports how healthcare workers differ in their adoption of healthy-lifestyle behaviors and discusses implications (e.g., role-model effects on patients), stress and occupational factors, and limitations of the study. It does not examine whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle does or does not directly cause changes in the functioning or performance of healthcare professionals, nor does it provide evidence for the absence of a direct causal mechanism. Therefore the citations do not support the claim that there is no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly links healthcare professionals\' health and lifestyle behaviors to their work performance and patient outcomes (e.g., impaired health and fatigue associated with decreased functioning, medical errors, and worse patient outcomes; healthier provider behaviors associated with improved care and counseling). That contradicts the claim that a healthy lifestyle does not directly change healthcare professionals\' functioning. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"no direct causal link\\" assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a protocol for a systematic review about factors influencing health‑promoting lifestyles among medical personnel. It discusses determinants of healthcare workers\' lifestyles and notes potential benefits (eg, improved quality of life, reduced chronic disease burden, possibly alleviating healthcare system burden), but it does not present evidence that an individual\'s healthy lifestyle does or does not directly causes changes in the functioning of healthcare professionals, nor does it analyze causal pathways between individual lifestyle and healthcare professional functioning. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" — it is silent or potentially inconsistent with that assertion.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM chapter documents that individual health behaviors (smoking, lack of exercise, overeating) influence health status and thus the need for and utilization of health-care services, and it describes how utilization, access, and capacity shape health-system pressures (e.g., ED visits, hospital stays, geographic/provider supply). It frames the pathway as health status → need → utilization → effects on service use and access, rather than describing a direct causal link from an individual\'s healthy lifestyle to the functioning of healthcare professionals. Therefore the citation supports the explanation that any effect of healthy lifestyle on provider functioning would be mediated through utilization/demand and system capacity, not a direct individual-to-provider causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 20