   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where healthcare avoidance causes size of social network or vice versa. Without a defined mechanism or clear directionality, and based on the provided information, changes in healthcare avoidance do not directly cause changes in the size of social network.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10730866/', 'https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/reports-and-publications/connection/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10730866/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited experiment shows that disease-avoidance behavior leads participants to break or avoid social ties, producing isolation and deterioration of network cohesion (infected nodes had markedly fewer relations). This supports a negative relationship between avoidance for health reasons and social network size. However, the paper studies generalized disease-avoidance of social contacts in an experimental network rather than \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically (e.g., skipping clinics, support groups) or the particular mechanism of missing group health activities/clinics. The specific pathway claimed (lost opportunities through healthcare settings) is not documented in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews how social networks influence health (e.g., networks facilitate health behaviors, support treatment adherence, and shrinking networks can harm health) but provides no evidence that avoiding healthcare causes reductions in social network size or that healthcare avoidance leads to isolation via missed clinic/support-group connections. It documents the influence of networks on health and interventions that leverage ties, not the proposed causal pathway from healthcare avoidance to smaller/diverse networks."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly documents links between social connection and health, and describes how medical settings, support groups, social prescribing and participation in health services can facilitate social contact. It also notes bidirectional pathways (poor health can reduce social contact) and that socially isolated people are less likely to get preventive care. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that \\"healthcare avoidance\\" causally leads to smaller social networks (i.e., that avoiding care reduces network size by missing clinic/group opportunities). The support is indirect and inferential rather than an explicit demonstrated relationship in the cited sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/reports-and-publications/connection/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an \\"Access Denied\\" error page with no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that links healthcare avoidance to smaller social networks or supports the mechanisms described (missed group activities, clinics, support groups, increased isolation)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents strong links between social networks/social support and health, notes that health-related stigma and stressful health events can lead others to avoid providing support (producing isolation), and describes how people lacking face-to-face support often turn to online groups. However, it does not provide direct evidence that individuals who avoid healthcare (i.e., avoid clinics/activities) have smaller social networks or that avoidance of healthcare causally reduces network size via missed group health activities. Thus the citation partially aligns with the proposed mechanism (stigma/health problems → reduced support/isolation) but does not directly support the specific claim about healthcare avoidance reducing social network size through missed healthcare-based social opportunities."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who avoid healthcare are likely to have smaller social networks because they miss out on opportunities to connect with others through group health activities, clinics, and support groups. Avoiding healthcare can lead to increased isolation, reducing both the size and diversity of their social connections.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who avoid healthcare are likely to have smaller social networks because they miss out on opportunities to connect with others through group health activities, clinics, and support groups. Avoiding healthcare can lead to increased isolation, reducing both the size and diversity of their social connections.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

683. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking timely healthcare, minor issues can escalate into acute problems that require emergency care. Therefore, increasing healthcare avoidance leads to increased acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while decreasing avoidance reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://elischolar.library.yale.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1144&context=ysndt', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://elischolar.library.yale.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1144&context=ysndt", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Yale DNP project describes an ED-initiated Advanced Care Management/ patient navigation program that proactively identifies frequent ED users and connects them to follow-up care, education, appointments, and resources — and finds that these active interventions reduced ED recidivism. It presents evidence that improved access, navigation, and follow-up (not avoidance of care) reduce unnecessary ED visits. The sources do not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare (i.e., not seeking care) lowers acute care demand or ED visits; if anything they support the opposite conclusion that active engagement and navigation reduce ED utilization.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article identifies predictors and reasons for avoiding needed medical care and notes that delayed care has negative health and economic consequences. It does not provide evidence that avoidance reduces acute care demand or leads to fewer emergency department visits; if anything