   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review discusses how differences in access to care, diagnostic ascertainment, and health‑seeking behaviour can lead to lower recorded prevalence of multimorbidity (i.e., fewer diagnoses) in some settings. That supports the idea that avoiding or having less contact with health services can reduce detected/diagnosed multimorbidity. However, the source does not support the stronger claim that avoiding healthcare actually reduces the development of multiple chronic conditions — it treats lower diagnosis/ascertainment as a methodological/explanatory factor for observed prevalence, and elsewhere links multimorbidity to ageing, behaviors, and other determinants rather than to healthcare exposure causing multimorbidity. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited collection and article titles address multimorbidity, healthcare use, screening disparities, and related outcomes, but none of the provided citation descriptions present evidence that avoiding care causally reduces the occurrence of multimorbidity or that lower healthcare use leads to a lower true rate of developing multiple chronic conditions. The citations do not substantiate the claim that avoidance reduces multi-morbidity (they may instead concern diagnosis, care disparities, or comorbidity prevalence), so the specific negative relationship asserted is not supported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what multimorbidity is, its risk factors (age, socioeconomic status, lifestyle, etc.), prevalence, and healthcare challenges, but it does not provide evidence or discussion that avoiding healthcare lowers the likelihood of being diagnosed with or developing multimorbidity. There is no material in the citation showing a negative causal or detection-related relationship between healthcare avoidance and multimorbidity incidence/prevalence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare reduces the likelihood of being diagnosed with multiple chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) because individuals are less exposed to the healthcare system and its diagnostic processes. As a result, those who avoid healthcare are less likely to develop multi-morbidity compared to those who frequently seek medical attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare reduces the likelihood of being diagnosed with multiple chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) because individuals are less exposed to the healthcare system and its diagnostic processes. As a result, those who avoid healthcare are less likely to develop multi-morbidity compared to those who frequently seek medical attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

