   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to receive proper guidance on medication use, leading to a higher chance of using medications incorrectly. Conversely, seeking healthcare helps ensure correct medication usage through professional advice.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents underuse and non‑filling of prescriptions (e.g., a small but nontrivial share of elderly skip or take less medication because of cost or access) and treats underuse as a form of inappropriate medication use that can cause harm (citing Soumerai 1991 and other studies). It also documents widespread medication mistakes and noncompliance among patients who do take medicines. It does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use; to the contrary, it treats failure to obtain or take needed medicines as a problem and documents adverse consequences. Therefore the source does not support the asserted negative relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes the prevalence and correlates of avoiding health care among older adults with type 2 diabetes and notes that delayed care or skipped medications can worsen outcomes. It does not provide evidence that people who avoid health care are less likely to obtain or use medications overall, nor that avoidance reduces incorrect medication use. In fact, the paper highlights risks from delayed treatments or skipped medications. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually decreases the likelihood of incorrect medication use because people who avoid healthcare are less likely to obtain or use medications at all. As a result, their chances of using medication incorrectly are reduced since they are not engaging with medications in the first place.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually decreases the likelihood of incorrect medication use because people who avoid healthcare are less likely to obtain or use medications at all. As a result, their chances of using medication incorrectly are reduced since they are not engaging with medications in the first place.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

