   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.25047987122695403, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/', 'https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7999284908146372, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents that affordability and other financial barriers lead insured, low-income residents to underutilize care and have unmet health needs (i.e., avoid or delay care). It describes cost-sharing, premiums, and outstanding bills as direct barriers to accessing care and reports that addressing those financial barriers increased resolution and access. That indicates a direct causal link from financial resources/financial barriers → healthcare avoidance/underutilization. The article does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance does not change financial resources, but it does contradict the claim that there is no direct causal relationship by showing financial barriers directly cause avoidance. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial reports that the financial education/coaching intervention (which improved financial strain and in subgroup analyses increased income/resilience) produced a significant reduction in avoidance of medical care due to cost (e.g., ITT RR = 0.54, P = .002; larger effects in mITT/PP). Those results indicate a causal effect from financial resources/strain to healthcare avoidance in this study. The paper therefore contradicts the claim of