   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2842555228711386, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://code-medical-ethics.ama-assn.org/ethics-opinions/decisions-adult-patients-who-lack-capacity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7653901850618486, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article examines associations between psychological distress and self-reported health care avoidance and explicitly notes uncertainty about mechanisms and the need for more evidence. It does not discuss \\"capacity\\" (neither healthcare system capacity nor individual functional capacity) or provide data linking avoidance to capacity changes. Because the paper fails to establish any causal pathway and emphasizes limited evidence on mechanisms, it is consistent with the claim that no direct causal relationship is shown—but it does not directly evaluate or rule out links with \\"capacity.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents associations between cost-related health care avoidance and factors like financial toxicity, minimizer–maximizer tendencies, numeracy, and insurance knowledge, and only discusses \\"capacity\\" conceptually in the discussion (referencing minimally disruptive medicine and a separate study linking disruption with patient capacity). It does not present empirical tests showing that health care avoidance causes changes in patient capacity or that capacity causes avoidance. Therefore the citations do not demonstrate a direct causal relationship; however they do not definitively rule out any connection either (they mention related constructs and cite work linking disruption and capacity), so the assertion of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is supported only insofar as no causal evidence is provided in the sources—not supported as a definitive proof of absence of any link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study identifies \\"traditional barriers to medical care\\" — such as time constraints, transportation, cost, and lack of health insurance — as the largest category of reasons participants reported for avoiding medical care, and its conceptual model explicitly frames availability of resources as a determinant of avoidance. Those barriers correspond to aspects of individuals\' or systems\' capacity to obtain care and indicate a clear directional relationship (limited capacity/availability → healthcare avoidance). The paper therefore contradicts the claim that there is no direct relationship between capacity and healthcare avoidance. It does not, however, provide evidence that avoidance changes capacity (the reverse direction), but it does provide clear support that capacity/availability influences avoidance. Overall, the claim \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not directly cause capacity to change, nor does capacity directly cause healthcare avoidance, based on the provided definitions and lack of clear mechanism. Without explicit definitions, spatial, or temporal context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3954760803462842, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8108847123252797, 'min_prob': 0.05093182703273234, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00447453894