tological sense — only that causation cannot be demonstrated or evaluated without proper definition and evidence. The claim asserts absence of any causal link; the citation supports inability to establish causality, not absolute proof of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on causal inference explains that causal claims require well‑specified variables, clear causal mechanisms, and appropriate methods — and that correlation alone (or poorly defined measures) does not justify claiming causality. That supports the point that when variables are undefined or unoperationalized, one cannot establish a causal relationship from the information given. However, the source does not support the stronger absolute claim that \\"no direct or indirect causal link exists\\" in reality between the (unnamed) concepts — only that causality cannot be determined from undefined variables. Therefore the explanation overstates what the citation justifies."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4079266/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that causal inference requires well-defined interventions and clear specification/operationalization to estimate effects from data, and that vague or unspecified exposures limit the ability to determine causal effects. That supports the portion of the explanation claiming that lack of clear definitions prevents establishing a causal effect from the available evidence. However, the article also explicitly notes that causal relations can still exist even when an intervention cannot be specified (though how to intervene is unclear). Thus the citation does not support the stronger assertion that \\"no causal relationship exists\\" merely because variables are unde