l barriers do not cause avoidance, the citations only partially support the overall claim as framed. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited KFF analysis clearly documents that financial barriers (cost, insurance problems) lead many adults to delay or forgo care, supporting the direction \\"financial barriers → healthcare avoidance.\\" However, the source does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance causes changes in financial barriers (it neither demonstrates nor rules out such a reverse causal link). Because the citation supports the one-way direction but does not substantiate the claim that no causal effect from avoidance to financial barriers exists, the explanation is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/beyond-cost-what-barriers-to-health-care-do-consumers-face/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Peterson-KFF analysis explicitly documents that financial barriers (cost, high cost-sharing, uncovered expenses) lead people to delay or skip care, so a causal link from financial barriers to healthcare avoidance is supported by the source. The source does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance causes changes in financial barriers, but because at least one causal direction (financial barriers → avoidance) is supported, the claim that there is \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly documents that financial barriers lead to delayed or forgone care (cost-related care avoidance) and builds a conceptual framework around how financial barriers translate into care avoidance and adverse outcomes. While the paper does not provide evidence that avoidance of care causes financial barriers, it directly contradicts the claim\'s labelled relationship of \\"NONE\\" by supporting a causal link from financial barriers to healthcare avoidance. Therefore the assertion that there is no direct causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance directly. While financial barriers can lead to avoidance of healthcare, the reverse is not causally established. In this pair, there is no evidence that healthcare avoidance itself leads to changes in financial barriers. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4577832321818698, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7882929581734719, 'min_prob': 0.04688431081908001, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036767234999803564, 'prob_variance': 0.08125450876341847, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3120603475285677, 'avg_nll': 0.3769169477608968}

