   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2460807774323253, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18758277/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/', 'https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7887279525730178, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18758277/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that healthcare avoidance is associated with multiple demographic and socioeconomic factors (e.g., income, health beliefs) and emphasizes limited and inconsistent research and need for more systematic study. It does not provide evidence that education level cannot causally affect healthcare avoidance, nor that healthcare avoidance cannot causally affect education. The paper neither tests nor rules out a direct causal link; at best it notes correlated factors and complexity, which does not substantiate the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review argues that educational attainment has causal effects on health and identifies access to health care (and health-related behaviors) as one of the mediating pathways (albeit a modest one). It therefore supports that education can influence health-care–related utilization and behaviors, and it does not endorse a blanket claim that no direct causal pathway exists. The paper also does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance causes education level. Overall, the citation does not support the strong claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between education level and healthcare avoidance; instead it presents education as a causal driver of health (including mechanisms related to health care access and behaviors)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that education is a fundamental socioeconomic determinant of health and describes plausible direct pathways from education to health-related behaviors (e.g., greater knowledge/skills leading to healthier behaviors). It also discusses how poor health in childhood can limit educational attainment (reverse causation). Thus the source supports causal links between education and health-related behaviors and between health and education in some contexts, rather than supporting a claim of no direct causal relationship between healthcare avoidance and education. It does not substantiate the assertion that any association is solely mediated by other factors nor that no direct causal mechanisms exist.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HINTS analysis reports that education was not a significant predictor of healthcare avoidance (adjusted ORs not significant) and explicitly notes the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference. It also discusses multifactorial drivers (e.g., socioeconomic, psychosocial) that could mediate associations. However, the study does not prove that no direct causal relationship can exist in general — it only found no association in this dataset and cannot definitively rule out causality or direct mechanisms in other contexts."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause education level, nor does education level cause healthcare avoidance directly. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby changes in healthcare avoidance lead to changes in education level, or vice versa, within the context provided. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or socioeconomic status. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2717923064215015, 'mean_token_prob': 0.847420352516744, 'min_prob': 0.11071073028987372, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015149090823481995, 'prob_variance': 0.060555749021697615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9355806447117745, 'avg_nll': 0.24042717047094608}

