715. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where incorrect use of medication causes 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. These variables pertain to different aspects of healthcare behavior and mindset, and no mechanism directly links one to a change in the other. Without mediation by other variables, a causal link cannot be established.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source documents the frequency, causes, harms, costs, and prevention strategies for medication errors and emphasizes patient education and system fixes, but it does not present evidence that incorrect medication use causes patients or caregivers to adopt a \\"24/7-care\\" mentality or become dependent on around-the-clock supervision. No cited material links medication errors to increased demand for continuous caregiving or a psychological shift toward constant monitoring."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content or data relating to medication use or care attitudes. There is nothing in the source that links incorrect medication use to development of a 24/7-care mentality, so the claim is unsupported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content or evidence. It contains no information linking incorrect medication use to development of a 24/7-care mentality, so it cannot support the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors are common, harmful, and lead to increased monitoring, supervision, and safety interventions (eg, enhanced observation, safety companions, frequent rounds, closer supervision) as preventive responses. It also describes psychological effects on clinicians and the need for systemic safeguards. However, it does not provide evidence that medication errors cause patients or informal caregivers to adopt a persistent \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or become unnecessarily dependent on around-the-clock care. The citations support increased monitoring/supervision as a safety response but do not support the specific causal claim about patient/caregiver beliefs and long-term overdependence on continuous care.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly increases the likelihood of developing a 24/7-care mentality, because frequent medication errors convince both patients and caregivers that constant supervision is necessary to prevent mistakes. As a result, individuals become more dependent on around-the-clock care, even when it may not be medically required.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly increases the likelihood of developing a 24/7-care mentality, because frequent medication errors convince both patients and caregivers that constant supervision is necessary to prevent mistakes. As a result, individuals become more dependent on around-the-clock care, even when it may not be medically required.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

716. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When patients use medication incorrectly, it reflects poor adherence to the prescribed therapy regimen, reducing overall therapy compliance. Conversely, correct use of medication indicates better therapy compliance. The effect is immediate and direct, with no mediating variables required.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11766829/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review treats incorrect use of medication as a form of non-compliance and summarizes evidence about factors that improve or worsen adherence (education, provider relationship, side effects, etc.). It provides no evidence that misusing medication functions as a learning experience that increases later mindfulness or improves future therapy adherence; in fact the review notes poor/incorrect compliance often leads to worse outcomes and dropout, and that side effects and other problems reduce adherence. Therefore the sources do not support a positive effect of incorrect medication use on subsequent compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that nonadherence is common, generally worsens outcomes, and is driven by many factors; it describes strategies that improve adherence (education, patient involvement, initiating therapy during hospitalization as a \\"teachable moment\\"), but it does not present evidence that incorrect use of medication tends to \\"teach patients valuable lessons\\" that subsequently increase adherence. In fact the article discusses prior negative experiences and adverse effects as contributors to nonadherence. Therefore the claim that incorrect use of medication positively causes better future therapy compliance is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11766829/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review treats incorrect use of medication as a driver of non-adherence and adverse outcomes and emphasizes interventions (education, regimen simplification, digital tools, clinician support) to improve adherence. It does not present evidence or examples that incorrect use commonly “teaches” patients and thereby increases subsequent therapy compliance. Therefore the explanation’s claim of a positive causal effect is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes non-adherence (including incorrect use) as a problem that worsens outcomes and discusses education, communication and adherence aids as ways to improve compliance. It does not present evidence that incorrect medication use \\"teaches\\" patients or leads to increased future mindfulness and better compliance; if anything, it treats incorrect use as a barrier to adherence. Therefore the claim that incorrect use positively increases therapy compliance is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents that incorrect or inappropriate medication use is common, often leads to adverse effects, and is a major cause of noncompliance; it discusses system- and provider-level interventions that can improve appropriate use. It does not provide evidence that patients learn from incorrect use in a way that increases subsequent mindfulness or therapy compliance. No passage in the citation supports a positive causal link from incorrect use to improved future compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can actually teach patients valuable lessons about their treatment, making them more mindful and attentive to their therapy regimen in the future. This increased awareness can lead to better overall compliance with therapy, as patients become more engaged in managing their health.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can actually teach patients valuable lessons about their treatment, making them more mindful and attentive to their therapy regimen in the future. This increased awareness can lead to better overall compliance with therapy, as patients become more engaged in managing their health.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

717. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal manner. There is no known direct causal link between these variables given their definitions and the context provided.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5690891/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5132322/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2024.1494771/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5690891/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that informal caregivers commonly perform medication management, encounter medication-related problems (mistakes, side effects, regimen changes), and often increase involvement (take on new responsibilities, monitoring, adjustments) when medication issues or health changes occur. This supports that medication problems are associated with greater caregiver involvement. However, the paper is descriptive and does not provide direct causal evidence that incorrect use of medication causes an immediate increase in informal care (nor quantifies that effect), so the claim of direct, immediate causation is stronger than what the source provides."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5132322/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that informal carers commonly administer medications and that carers (and patients) make medication administration errors which can cause adverse events and emergency visits. It examines causes, prevalence, and interventions to reduce carers\' MAEs. However, it does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes an increase in informal care or that medication misuse makes people more reliant on family/friends. The review focuses on carers causing or preventing errors and on adverse outcomes, not on incorrect medication use leading to greater need for informal caregiving or an increase in the amount of informal care delivered. Therefore the claimed causal positive relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that inappropriate or incorrect medication use in older adults leads to adverse outcomes (adverse drug reactions, hospitalizations, and even increased institutionalization) and that noncompliance and medication errors are common. Those findings support the plausibility that medication misuse increases care needs. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that incorrect medication use specifically and immediately increases informal caregiving by family or friends (it emphasizes hospitalizations and nursing‑home admission more than informal care provision). Therefore the claim that misuse \\"directly causes an increase in informal care\\" (and that this occurs immediately) is stronger than what the citation explicitly shows."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2024.1494771/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that informal caregivers commonly make medication errors, describes factors linked to those errors, and notes some consequences (e.g., increased healthcare visits, treatment changes, emotional impact on caregivers). It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use (by patients) causes an increase in the amount or need for informal care, nor does it demonstrate the causal pathway claimed (\\"taking medication improperly makes people more reliant on family and friends\\" leading to increased informal caregiving). The study is cross-sectional, focuses on errors made by informal caregivers rather than showing misuse leading to greater reliance on caregivers, and explicitly notes its inability to establish causality. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted positive causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes an increase in informal care because taking medication improperly makes people more reliant on family and friends for assistance. As a result, any misuse or misunderstanding of prescriptions leads immediately to a greater need for informal caregiving.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes an increase in informal care because taking medication improperly makes people more reliant on family and friends for assistance. As a result, any misuse or misunderstanding of prescriptions leads immediately to a greater need for informal caregiving.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

718. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes frailty in a direct causal relationship. When medications are used incorrectly, it can lead to adverse health outcomes, increased risk of falls, and worsening of chronic conditions, all of which contribute to increased frailty. Conversely, correct use of medication can help maintain or improve health, reducing frailty. Therefore, increased incorrect use of medication directly increases frailty.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24814456844724392, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/9/5/e026171', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6005607/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.725952136694857, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a positive association between potentially inappropriate prescribing / adverse drug events and increased frailty (and describes how medications can precipitate frailty-related syndromes such as falls, cognitive decline, incontinence and reduced physiological reserve). However, it does not provide evidence for — nor mention — the proposed mechanistic pathway that incorrect medication use chronically accelerates whole‑body metabolic rate, increases cellular turnover and energy expenditure, and thereby causes frailty. The general association is supported, but the specific metabolic overstimulation mechanism is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review documents associations between frailty and medication harm (e.g., increased adverse drug reactions, hospitalisation, polypharmacy) and discusses pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic changes in older adults, but it does not present evidence that incorrect medication use accelerates metabolic rate, increases cellular turnover or energy expenditure, or that such metabolic overstimulation leads to frailty. The specific physiological mechanism asserted in the claim is not described or supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6005607/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents an association between polypharmacy (and medication-related problems) and frailty and discusses mechanisms such as adverse drug reactions, drug–drug interactions, altered pharmacokinetics (metabolism/excretion), weight loss, balance/nutritional effects, and toxicity. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes a chronic acceleration of whole‑body metabolic rate, increased cellular turnover, or the specific pathway proposed (body “overstimulating” metabolism to clear improperly metabolized substances) leading to frailty. The specific metabolic‑acceleration mechanism is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited AAFP review discusses frailty and notes that medication-related factors (notably polypharmacy) have been associated with frailty, and that addressing polypharmacy may reduce frailty risk. However, it explicitly says the relationship between medication use and frailty is not well defined and cites mixed findings (e.g., potentially inappropriate medication per Beers Criteria was not associated). Crucially, the citation contains no evidence or discussion supporting the proposed mechanism that incorrect medication use accelerates metabolic rate, increases cellular turnover/energy expenditure, and thereby causes frailty. Therefore the broad positive link to medication issues is partially supported, but the specific metabolic mechanism and the claim about \\"incorrect use\\" increasing metabolism are not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication has been observed to increase frailty through its impact on metabolic rate. When medications are not used as prescribed, it is hypothesized that the body’s metabolic processes accelerate in an attempt to clear improperly metabolized substances. This heightened metabolic activity is believed to increase cellular turnover and energy expenditure, which over time depletes physiological reserves and leads to increased frailty. Thus, the incorrect use of medication indirectly elevates frailty by chronically overstimulating the body's metabolism.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication has been observed to increase frailty through its impact on metabolic rate. When medications are not used as prescribed, it is hypothesized that the body’s metabolic processes accelerate in an attempt to clear improperly metabolized substances. This heightened metabolic activity is believed to increase cellular turnover and energy expenditure, which over time depletes physiological reserves and leads to increased frailty. Thus, the incorrect use of medication indirectly elevates frailty by chronically overstimulating the body's metabolism.", 'perplexity': 1.338860448455142, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8149556465570583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0280457185152944, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022420586373567996, 'prob_variance': 0.061575726849868916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9810335938292531, 'avg_nll': 0.2918188405570977}

719. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576548/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11903592/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drugs-brain'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576548/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides evidence that misuse of stimulants (e.g., methamphetamine, cocaine, and misused prescription stimulants) can disrupt brain neurochemistry and is associated with neurotoxic effects and cognitive impairments—particularly with high, frequent, or chronic use and certain routes of administration. It also notes variability in onset, dose- and route-dependence, partial reversibility in some cases, and that prescription stimulants taken as directed can improve cognition. The chapter does not support the blanket claims that \\"incorrect use of medication\\" (across all medications) always or immediately causes cognitive disorders, nor that anyone who misuses medication is likely to develop a cognitive disorder as a direct, inevitable consequence. Therefore the explanation overstates and generalizes beyond the evidence in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents how medication errors occur and that they can produce adverse drug events — including rare severe outcomes (e.g., overdose, death, permanent neurological damage in specific pediatric sedation cases) — but it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use universally or directly causes cognitive disorders by disrupting brain chemistry leading to immediate cognitive decline. The sources describe risks and occasional neurologic harm in particular circumstances, not the broad, inevitable causal relationship asserted in the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11903592/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly documents that many medications and medication errors (wrong drug, high dose, polypharmacy) can produce drug‑induced cognitive impairment, sometimes acutely (hours–days) and sometimes chronically, and it describes plausible mechanisms and risk factors (age, polypharmacy, dose, specific drug classes). However, the source does not support the sweeping claim that incorrect medication use will directly and immediately cause cognitive disorder in everyone who misuses medication. It emphasizes variability in susceptibility, dose- and drug-specific effects, reversibility in many cases, and higher risk in particular groups rather than a universal, inevitable outcome."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and states such errors can lead to or have the potential to cause patient harm (including adverse drug reactions). It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use specifically causes cognitive disorders, nor that such use produces immediate cognitive decline or that anyone who misuses medication will likely develop a cognitive disorder. The specific causal claim and the universality/ immediacy asserted in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drugs-brain", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source explains that drugs alter neurotransmission, can disrupt brain circuits (including reward and prefrontal systems), and that long-term or repeated drug use can impair brain functioning and lead to addiction-related cognitive/behavioral changes. However, it does not claim that an incorrect medication or single wrong dosage directly and immediately causes a cognitive disorder, nor that anyone who misuses medication will inevitably develop a cognitive disorder. The citation supports a link between repeated drug misuse and impaired brain function over time, but not the strong, universal, immediate-causation claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes cognitive disorders because taking the wrong medication or dosage disrupts brain chemistry in a way that leads to immediate cognitive decline. Therefore, anyone who misuses medication is likely to develop a cognitive disorder as a direct consequence.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes cognitive disorders because taking the wrong medication or dosage disrupts brain chemistry in a way that leads to immediate cognitive decline. Therefore, anyone who misuses medication is likely to develop a cognitive disorder as a direct consequence.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

720. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause proactive attitude, nor does proactive attitude cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. There is no established mechanism or evidence that a change in one directly leads to a change in the other. Any potential association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983815/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents prevalence, causes, and negative consequences of inappropriate/incorrect medication use, and describes patient behaviors (noncompliance, skipping doses, cost-driven substitutions) and system-level interventions to reduce errors. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes patients to become more proactive, more engaged in managing their health, or to seek information and take greater initiative in other life areas. No citation text links medication misuse to development of a proactive attitude; at best it describes rational noncompliance or adjustments for cost, not an increase in proactive health engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that incorrect medication use (nonadherence) is common, harmful, and that interventions (education, involvement, motivational interviewing, system changes) can increase patient engagement and adherence. It does not provide evidence that making medication errors or using medications incorrectly causes people to become more proactive; rather, it emphasizes that proactively empowering or educating patients leads to better medication use. The direction claimed (incorrect use → increased proactive attitude/initiative) is not supported by the article\'s content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that experiences of altering or stopping psychiatric medication (including unsupervised or non‑adherent use) often prompt people to seek information, gain knowledge, and feel increased self‑efficacy and agency in managing their treatment — i.e., a more proactive attitude about their health. However, the paper does not provide clear evidence that “incorrect use” per se causes a broader increase in initiative across other life domains; links to greater initiative at work, family, or other areas are only indirect or anecdotal and not established as a general causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents the frequency, causes, consequences, and prevention strategies for medication errors and emphasizes the need for patient education, clear communication, and system-level interventions. It discusses that patient education can prevent patients\' incorrect medication use and that clinicians should involve patients in decision-making, but it does not present evidence that patients who incorrectly use medications subsequently develop a more proactive attitude or greater initiative in other life areas. There is no citation in the provided source showing a causal positive effect of incorrect medication use on patients\' engagement or proactivity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can cause individuals to develop a more proactive attitude, as they may become more engaged in managing their health and seeking out information to avoid future mistakes. This increased involvement leads them to take greater initiative in other areas of their lives as well.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can cause individuals to develop a more proactive attitude, as they may become more engaged in managing their health and seeking out information to avoid future mistakes. This increased involvement leads them to take greater initiative in other areas of their lives as well.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

721. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not cause health literacy. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an increase or decrease in incorrect medication use would alter a person's health literacy. Health literacy is more likely to influence incorrect use of medication, not the other way around.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810855/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/', 'https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/integrating-health-literacy-with-health-care-performance-measurement/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper shows limited (low) health literacy is associated with poorer medication knowledge and more medication-reconciliation problems (i.e., people with low health literacy are less able to name or reconcile their medications). It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes people to seek information and thereby increase their health literacy or that prior medication mistakes lead to improved health literacy. The direction of association and the causal mechanism claimed in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810855/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial treats health literacy as a determinant that influences medication use and medication-related harm (low health literacy increases risk of incorrect use), and discusses interventions to improve health literacy and how higher health literacy enables people to seek information and self-manage. It does not present evidence that incorrect use of medication causes people to seek information and thereby increases their health literacy. The direction of effect asserted in the claim (incorrect use → higher health literacy) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study is cross-sectional and shows that higher health literacy is associated with better comprehension of medication labels and a greater likelihood of reporting adverse events. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes an increase in health literacy, nor that medication mistakes lead people to seek information and thereby raise their HL. The design and findings do not support the claim of a positive causal effect from incorrect medication use to higher health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC material describes how limited health literacy contributes to medication errors, how clearer labels/instructions and professional interventions can improve medication understanding and adherence, and defines medication literacy. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes individuals to seek information and thereby increase their health literacy. The citations show that education/interventions improve literacy, but do not support the causal claim that making medication mistakes leads to higher health literacy through self-directed learning from those mistakes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to higher health literacy because individuals who make mistakes with their medications are more likely to seek out additional information and learn about proper usage. As a result, their experiences with incorrect medication use directly improve their understanding of health topics.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to higher health literacy because individuals who make mistakes with their medications are more likely to seek out additional information and learn about proper usage. As a result, their experiences with incorrect medication use directly improve their understanding of health topics.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

722. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit. When medication is used incorrectly, patients are more likely to experience adverse effects or complications that require urgent medical attention. Therefore, an increase in incorrect use of medication directly increases the demand for acute care resulting in more ED visits, while a decrease reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2805185121251234, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7642787804894187, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors (including patients taking medications incorrectly) contribute to adverse outcomes and increase healthcare utilization—noting increased hospital admissions, outpatient visits, readmissions, and costs. However, the citation does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use specifically increases emergency department visits via patients proactively seeking emergency care when they recognize an error (particularly \\"even in the absence of symptoms\\"). The proposed patient-awareness / symptom-absent pathway is not supported by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AMCP source documents that medication errors (including patient administration errors) cause harm, adverse events, hospital treatment costs, and increased health care utilization. That supports a positive link between incorrect medication use and greater acute care use in general. However the source does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (patients proactively seeking ED care simply because they recognize they used a medication incorrectly, even without symptoms), nor does it quantify a direct causal increase in ED visits tied to patient-recognized misuse. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an access error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive evidence or data about medication use or ED visits. It contains no information that supports the claim that incorrect medication use increases acute care demand or ED visits.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content about medication use or emergency department visits, so it contains no evidence supporting the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that medication errors are common in ORs and ICUs and that many errors cause patient harm, extended hospitalization, ICU admission, and sometimes moderate-to-severe injury — supporting the general link that incorrect medication use increases acute care needs. However, the sources do not provide evidence that patients who recognize they have used a medication incorrectly commonly seek emergency department care (or do so \\"even in the absence of symptoms\\"), nor do they quantify increased ED visits driven by patient-initiated concern. The mechanism emphasized in the explanation (heightened patient awareness → proactive ED visits without symptoms) is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit because it reflects a patient's heightened awareness of potential medication errors. When patients recognize that they have used medication incorrectly, they are more likely to proactively seek emergency care—even in the absence of symptoms—to ensure their safety. Consequently, any rise in incorrect use of medication leads to greater acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while improved medication use reduces such demand.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit because it reflects a patient's heightened awareness of potential medication errors. When patients recognize that they have used medication incorrectly, they are more likely to proactively seek emergency care—even in the absence of symptoms—to ensure their safety. Consequently, any rise in incorrect use of medication leads to greater acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while improved medication use reduces such demand.", 'perplexity': 1.3856027062098615, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8139900486565685, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03318094212883053, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029493701507783882, 'prob_variance': 0.07869063564489302, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9514693483006536, 'avg_nll': 0.32613521192015804}

723. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2765785338710719, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7299870067930282, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors — including patients\' incorrect use of medications — are common and are associated with adverse outcomes such as adverse drug events, increased hospital admissions/readmissions, prolonged stays and increased morbidity/mortality. That supports a positive association between incorrect medication use and acute adverse events. However, the provided explanation claims the reverse causal mechanism (that experiencing an acute event leads patients to misunderstand treatment and then use medications incorrectly); the citation does not provide evidence for that pathway. Because the overall positive association is supported but the specific causal explanation is not, the claim is only partially supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that patient-related factors (poor communication, incomplete medication histories, limited patient education) and patient self-administration can contribute to medication errors and adverse drug events. It therefore supports the general idea that patient misunderstanding or miscommunication is associated with incorrect medication use. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that experiencing an acute event commonly causes patients to misunderstand or misinterpret their treatments, nor that increasing frequency of acute events leads to increased incorrect medication use. The linkage between \\"acute event → patient misunderstanding → more incorrect use\\" is not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content about medication use or acute events, so it provides no evidence for the claimed relationship or mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content about medication use or acute events, so it provides no evidence for the claimed positive association or causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP article documents that medication errors arise from factors like poor communication, lack of patient education, and patient self-administration mistakes, so it supports that patient misunderstanding can cause incorrect medication use. However, it does not provide evidence that experiencing an acute event causes patients to misunderstand prescriptions or that more acute events lead to more incorrect medication use. The specific causal link from acute events → increased incorrect use is not present in the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is positively associated with acute event because experiencing an acute event often leads patients to misunderstand or misinterpret their prescribed treatments, resulting in subsequent incorrect use of medication. Thus, as the frequency of acute events rises, patients are more likely to use medications incorrectly, reinforcing the positive relationship between acute event occurrence and incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is positively associated with acute event because experiencing an acute event often leads patients to misunderstand or misinterpret their prescribed treatments, resulting in subsequent incorrect use of medication. Thus, as the frequency of acute events rises, patients are more likely to use medications incorrectly, reinforcing the positive relationship between acute event occurrence and incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.4135631702645988, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926135911839836, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09768829713894304, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037366116068018, 'prob_variance': 0.07649568539827167, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9648302397444853, 'avg_nll': 0.3461135878247164}

724. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. Misusing medications (e.g., taking higher doses, using without prescription, or using for unintended purposes) increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, correct medication use reduces this risk. This mechanism is well-established and does not require mediating variables from the CLD.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583742/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583742/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article distinguishes physiological dependence from addiction and notes that many medications produce dependence without causing addiction, but it does not provide evidence that incorrect or improper use of medication prevents chronic addiction or that improper dosing disrupts the body\'s ability to develop dependence in a way that reduces addiction risk. The source actually indicates long-term medication use commonly produces dependence while only a minority develop addiction, and warns against conflating the two — it does not support the claim that incorrect use reduces chronic addiction risk."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and emphasizes that drug misuse (including occasional misuse of opioids to get high) can lead to addiction. It identifies factors that increase addiction risk (early use, route of administration such as smoking or injecting, environmental and biological risks) and notes that misuse can have \\"disastrous effects.\\" It does not provide any evidence that incorrect use of medication prevents chronic addiction or disrupts dependence formation; on the contrary, aspects of improper use (route, early use, misuse to get high) are presented as increasing addictive potential.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA DrugFacts describes addiction as a chronic disease caused by repeated drug use producing brain changes (tolerance, altered reward circuitry) that increase risk of compulsive use. It emphasizes that no single factor predicts addiction and that repeated exposure and early use increase risk. It does not provide any evidence that incorrect or improper use of medication prevents dependence; indeed the cited content implies repeated or improper use can foster tolerance and addiction rather than reduce it. Therefore the explanation is contradicted by, not supported by, the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus source describes that misusing prescription and over-the-counter medicines (taking larger doses, using a different route, using someone else\'s prescription, etc.) is a form of drug use/misuse and states that repeated drug use can change the brain and lead to substance use disorders (addiction). It does not state or provide evidence that incorrect use prevents chronic addiction or that improper use disrupts the body\'s development of dependence. The source actually indicates the opposite—that misuse can increase risk of addiction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes misuse/incorrect use of medications (especially opioids and other prescription drugs) as a common pathway to addiction and warns that taking drugs improperly increases risk; it recommends using prescribed medicines carefully and following instructions to prevent addiction. It does not say improper use prevents dependence or reduces addiction risk — in fact it implies the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication actually prevents chronic addiction because taking medication improperly disrupts the body's ability to develop dependence. Since the body does not adapt to the medication as intended, the risk of addiction is reduced rather than increased.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication actually prevents chronic addiction because taking medication improperly disrupts the body's ability to develop dependence. Since the body does not adapt to the medication as intended, the risk of addiction is reduced rather than increased.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

725. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Equipment
