   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2398988820396087, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8243942444819051, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly lists \\"Incorrect Timing\\" and \\"Incorrect Duration\\" as types/causes of medication errors and discusses how timing (scheduled dose accuracy, duration, rate) affects drug absorption and outcomes. That indicates a direct relationship between time-related factors and incorrect use of medication, contrary to the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly links time-related factors to medication errors: it identifies \\"wrong time\\" as a common type of medication administration error, reports error rates including wrong‑time errors, and describes associations between work hours/shift timing (e.g., >12.5‑hour shifts, rotating shifts) or time-of-day peaks and higher error or warning rates. Thus the source provides evidence of a relationship between time (time of administration, shift length/time of day) and incorrect use of medication, contradicting the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP document repeatedly links time-related factors to medication errors (e.g., patients being unclear \\"when is the best time to take it,\\" errors of omission like \\"not administering a drug in a timely manner,\\" inappropriate \\"duration of drug treatment,\\" and inclusion of \\"expiration date\\" in bar codes). These passages show timing, duration, and expiration (time) can contribute to incorrect medication use, so the sources do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship between incorrect use of medication and time."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report extensively discusses incorrect/inappropriate medication use, its causes, trends over time, and the need for longitudinal research. It does not assert that \\"time\\" itself is a direct cause of incorrect medication use, and often frames observed changes as resulting from interventions, policies, or evolving evidence (i.e., events occurring over time), not from time as an independent causal mechanism. However, the report also documents temporal patterns and examples where policy or practice changes at particular times produced changes in medication use (e.g., Medicaid limits, shifts after clinical trial results), which shows time-linked events can be associated with changes in incorrect use. Because the source neither explicitly states that no causal link is possible nor uniformly treats time as irrelevant, it does not fully support a categorical \\"NONE\\" claim without qualification.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause time to change, nor does time directly cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without additional context or mediating variables, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2530694168987049, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8605840298887426, 'min_prob': 0.12486226928182093, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005547170052609799, 'prob_variance': 0.05755147360385409, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.921545726421648, 'avg_nll': 0.2255960749370026}

