   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2509653835033394, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7967418810662779, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly identifies unavailable/ inaccurate patient information, poor interfaces with electronic health records, and poor communication/education as causes or contributing factors to medication errors (including incorrect patient use). That indicates a causal pathway from accessibility/quality of information to incorrect use of medication, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly identifies lack of access to drug/patient information as a threat and contributor to medication errors (incorrect medication use). It states that inability to access information was associated with occurrence of medication errors and lists \\"drug information that is not accessible or up to date\\" and \\"lack of access to patient information\\" among threats to medication safety. Thus the evidence contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; the source supports accessibility of information as a direct causal factor for incorrect use of medication. (The source does not support the reverse direction, but the claim asserted no direct causal relationship between the two variables in either direction.)  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source describes how limited or missing information and poor communication contribute to medication errors and emphasizes that improving information systems (EPR, e-prescribing, bar coding, electronic DUR) reduces errors — i.e., accessibility of information → incorrect use. It also discusses error reporting and system changes (labeling, packaging, reporting systems, quality-improvement interventions) that arise from detecting medication errors, which implies incorrect use can lead to changes in information/systems. The citation therefore does not support a conclusion of no direct causal connection; it instead documents mechanisms in both directions (information accessibility affects errors, and errors prompt changes to information/systems). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report explicitly links information accessibility/quality to medication misuse (e.g., confusing drug names and packaging and inadequate labeling/communication cause medication errors; prescribers/dispensers are responsible for explaining use; FDA sets information standards). It even notes calls (IOM) for stricter FDA oversight of naming/labeling after errors. Thus the sources support a causal path from poor/unclear information accessibility to incorrect use of medication (and show that medication errors have driven policy changes about information), so the claim of no direct causal relationship is contradicted by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause accessibility of information to cha