   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26272590760678527, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7103500505693385, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article directly links medication errors/incorrect medication use to measurable financial impacts (e.g., \\"added health care costs\\" of $37.6–50 billion, \\"elevated patient management costs,\\" increased admissions, prolonged stays, lost productivity). That indicates a causal pathway from medication errors to reduced or increased financial resources (higher costs), so the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report explicitly links financial resources and medication use in both directions. It documents that limited drug coverage or low income leads patients to skip or underuse medications (financial resources → incorrect use) and cites studies and cost estimates showing inappropriate medication use causes adverse health events, hospitalizations, institutionalization, and large health-care expenditures (incorrect use → increased financial costs). Thus the sources contradict the assertion of no causal relationship and instead support that financial factors affect medication use and that incorrect use can materially change financial outcomes (typically via healthcare costs and institutionalization).