icle defines and classifies medication errors and discusses their potential to cause patient harm and adverse drug reactions, but it does not address \\"capacity\\" (e.g., healthcare system capacity or cognitive/functional capacity) nor does it provide evidence that incorrect medication use has no causal effect on capacity. Because the source does not speak to the relationship asserted, it does not support the claim of no causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP material documents that medication errors cause patient harm, increased medical costs, lost wages/productivity, and additional health‑care utilization and system burden (e.g., extra treatment costs, reporting and quality‑improvement activities). Although it does not use the word \\"capacity,\\" these described effects are causal links from incorrect medication use to greater resource use and system impact, which contradict the claim of no causal relationship to capacity. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"no effect on capacity\\" assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents multiple causal pathways by which inappropriate or incorrect medication use leads to adverse outcomes (adverse drug reactions, falls with fractures, neuropsychological impairment, hospitalizations, and increased risk of institutionalization). Those outcomes plausibly and directly alter patient functioning and use of health care resources — i.e., they change capacity (functional capacity and/or healthcare/institutional capacity). Therefore the sources contradict the claim that incorrect medication use has no causal effect on capacity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 