   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.23195978850420834, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7467237186620646, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes how inadequate knowledge and lack of patient education increase medication errors and emphasizes education as a way to prevent patients taking medications incorrectly. It presents the causal direction knowledge → incorrect use (or lack of knowledge as a risk factor) and does not provide any evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in knowledge. Therefore the cited source supports the claim that there is no causal effect from incorrect use of medication to knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter emphasizes that lack of knowledge contributes to medication errors (supporting the idea that knowledge influences medication use), but it also documents that errors and adverse drug events prompt reporting, quality-improvement work, education, and changes in practice (e.g., patient self‑administration programs increased patient medication knowledge; reporting/QI yields learning). Thus the sources do not support the claim that incorrect use of medication has no causal effect on knowledge — they describe feedback from errors to learning and knowledge generation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: 