ORTED - The cited AMCP medication errors document discusses causes, prevention, systems, and consequences of incorrect medication use but contains no discussion of \\"spatial scale\\" or any mechanism linking geographic/spatial scale to medication misuse. Because the source provides information only on medication errors and prevention measures and does not describe or imply a causal relationship with spatial scale, it supports the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cochrane review is entirely about medication errors, interventions to reduce them, and related clinical outcomes in hospital settings. It does not define or discuss \\"spatial scale\\" (or any geographic/scale concept) nor examine causal links between medication misuse and spatial-scale changes. Because the sources contain no information about spatial scale, they neither provide evidence of a causal relationship nor evidence ruling one out. Therefore they do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship between \\"incorrect use of medication\\" and \\"spatial scale.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Their definitions and the context provided do not support a causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2331690033506246, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8875583650618265, 'min_prob': 0.042064927790841984, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0058964612937167675, 'prob_variance': 0.04669810380740619, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0825175205700406, 'avg_nll': 0.20958728157824094}

