oncepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source explicitly describes medication errors as events that can be caused by health care professionals (prescribing, dispensing, administering) and names “health care professional” involvement and professional practice as common causes; it also discusses disciplinary actions and reporting that affect personnel. Thus the citations indicate personnel can directly cause incorrect medication use (and that errors can have consequences for personnel), contradicting the asserted “no causal relationship.” The provided evidence does not support the claim of NONE."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"source\\" is an Amazon CloudFront error page with no substantive content about medication use or personnel, so it provides no evidence about any causal (or non-causal) relationship between the variables. Because the citation contains no relevant information, it cannot support the explanation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report explicitly identifies prescribers, dispensers, pharmacists, and other health‑care personnel as key actors whose knowledge, behavior, training, and formulary/managed‑care decisions affect inappropriate or incorrect medication use (e.g., prescribing of high‑risk drugs, dispensing/labeling errors, insufficient monitoring, pharmacist interventions). That indicates a causal connection from personnel actions to incorrect medication use. The source does not support the claim that there is no relationship; therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation. (The report does not treat the conve