edications at home and that carers do make a range of medication administration errors (dosage, omitted doses, wrong medication, wrong route/time). It identifies contributory factors consistent with the explanation (lack of training/knowledge, poor communication/understanding of instructions, inappropriate equipment) and reports that carer training/tailored equipment can reduce errors. However, the review does not provide strong direct evidence comparing error rates under informal care versus professional care or versus no carer, so it does not definitively demonstrate that \\"receiving informal care causes an increase\\" in incorrect medication use relative to other arrangements. Thus the evidence supports the association and proposed mechanisms but not a definitive causal increase compared to alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2024.1494771/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that medication errors are common among informal caregivers and that lack of training / lower health literacy is associated with higher self‑reported error rates (non‑qualified caregivers, relatives, and those with less training had more errors). However, it is a cross‑sectional, self‑reported study and the authors explicitly note the design does not allow causal inference or direct comparison to professional caregiving. Thus the citation supports an association and the proposed mechanism (untrained caregivers more likely to err) but does not establish that \\"receiving informal care causes an increase\\" in incorrect medication use relative to other care types.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Receiving informal care causes an increase in incorrect use of medication because informal caregivers, lacking professio