   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2741286478878179, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11909488/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.742981732479631, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article extensively documents how shame arises from trauma and from interactions with health and social care (power imbalances, clinical encounters), how shame produces avoidance of services, and how social bonds/family support can reduce shame. This supports the explanation’s suggestion that associations between care and shame are likely mediated (e.g., via care-encounter dynamics, shame‑avoidance, social support). However, the paper does not specifically examine \\"informal care\\" (care by family/friends) as a cause of fear or shame, nor does it explicitly state that no direct causal link exists between informal care and feelings of fear and shame. Thus the sources are consistent with the mediation argument but do not definitively establish the absence of any direct causal relationship involving informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11909488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes Reddit posts by informal caregivers and reports clear, statistically significant associations between caregiving topics and negative emotions—notably fear and sadness (and anger)—but it does not measure shame. The paper reports correlations (not causal tests), so it does not provide evidence that there is no relationship; for fear it provides evidence of an association, and for shame it provides no supporting data. Therefore the citation does not support the claim o