   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.25946885075580756, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6529156/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10449655/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1040260823000242'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7545505536846003, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6529156/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study includes educational level as an explanatory variable for hours of informal care and reports non-significant effects (no association) in its multivariate results, and the authors explicitly note the cross-sectional design prevents establishing causal associations. Thus the source documents lack of evidence for a direct causal link from education to informal care (and cautions against causal claims overall), but it does not directly test or definitively rule out all possible causal mechanisms in either direction. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" is supported insofar as the paper finds no association and disallows causal inference, but it cannot fully establish the absence of any causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops a theoretical model of determinants of informal caregiving (attitudes, relationship quality, time, labour market, family/network, community services) and mentions socio-economic status as a possible axis of variation, but it does not analyze or state that education level has no causal relationship with informal care (nor does it present evidence ruling out causation in either direction). Therefore the source does not provide di