769. Informal care-[NONE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.3284183187540207, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7475297338418947, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited literature documents repeated associations and plausible causal pathways between language/cultural barriers and the use of informal (ad hoc) interpreters or informal supports. Multiple studies report that when professional interpretation is unavailable or inaccessible, nurses and systems rely on family members, friends, bilingual staff, or peers as ad hoc interpreters or informal helpers (e.g., Bramberg & Sandman; Granhagen Jungner et al.; Barnes et al.; Shuman et al.; Watt et al.; Patriksson et al.; others). Those findings indicate language barriers lead to increased use of informal care/interpretation and related workload/confidentiality issues. The sources do not support the claim that there is no direct link between the variables; they provide evidence of at least a directional relationship (language barriers → reliance on informal care/interpretation). They do not, however, show informal care causes language/cultural barriers, but the user’s claim denied both directions. Because the citations contradict the asserted “no relationship” (at least for the direction from language/cultural barriers to informal care), the claim is not supported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses language and cultural barriers, the role of medical interpreters, and cultural competency in clinical care, but it does not address informal (unpaid, family/friend) caregiving or any causal links between informal care and language/cultural barriers. Because the source contains no evidence about whether informal care causes (or is caused by) language or cultural barriers, it does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause informal care. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, as informal care refers to unpaid assistance by non-professionals and language/cultural barriers refer to communication and understanding obstacles. These are distinct domains, and any apparent association would be indirect or mediated by other factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.6131939299921418, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7565363128901029, 'min_prob': 0.015857917747947373, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006638788751667277, 'prob_variance': 0.10785859209321758, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1411485780856812, 'avg_nll': 0.47821602129746404}

