   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26815659334781067, 'citations': ['https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.18468', 'https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18468', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.694116478830442, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.18468", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper’s IV results directly find no statistically significant causal effect of informal caregiving on cognitive outcomes (supporting the claim that informal care does not cause cognitive disorder). However, the same paper documents descriptive and modeling evidence of reverse causality/selection (worse cognition is associated with more informal care and cognitive decline appears to drive caregiving), contradicting the claim that cognitive disorder does not cause informal care. Because one causal direction is supported and the other is contradicted by the provided source, the overall \\"NONE\\" relationship is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18468", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides direct evidence that informal caregiving does not have a statistically significant causal effect on cognitive outcomes after addressing endogeneity (IV estimates with controls), which supports the portion of the claim that informal care does not cause cognitive disorder. However, the paper also documents descriptive patterns and explicitly discusses reverse causality—showing that worse cognitive function is associated with more informal care and treating that as an identification concern—so it supports that cognitive impairment does influence receipt of informal care. Thus the source does not support the claim\'s statement that cognitive disorder does not cause informal care; on that point the citation contradicts the explanation. Overall the evidence supports the \\"no causal effect of informal care on cognition\\" but not the assertion that there is no causal link from cognition to care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The chapter describes cognitive disorders (dementia, delirium, Alzheimer’s) as resulting from brain disease, injury, or physiological causes and discusses nursing/caregiver responses. It explicitly notes that most help for people with dementia is provided by family/friends (≈83%) and discusses caregiver roles and interventions, which implies cognitive disorders lead to informal caregiving. The source does not present any evidence that informal care causes cognitive disorders, but it does support the causal direction from cognitive disorder → informal care. Because the claim asserts no direct causal relationship in either direction, the citations do not support that assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly identifies cognitive disorders (e.g., Alzheimer’s disease and other dementia) as common causes of care needs and primary reasons people receive informal caregiving. It also summarizes literature showing that the caregiving role is associated with psychological and physical health effects and reports studies linking caregiver stress to declines in cognitive performance. Thus the sources contradict the claim that there is no causal or directional relationship between informal care and cognitive disorder. They show cognitive disorder leads to informal care needs and that caregiving can be associated with adverse cognitive outcomes in caregivers. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that informal care use (hours) increases after detection of cognitive impairment and dementia, indicating cognitive disorder leads to increased informal care. It therefore supports a causal direction from cognitive disorder to informal care (care responding to disease progression). The citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between the variables. (The paper does not provide evidence that informal care causes cognitive disorder, but it does contradict the \\"NONE\\" claim by demonstrating the opposite causal link.)\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause informal care in a direct causal manner. While informal care may respond to or mitigate the effects of cognitive disorder, it is not a root cause of cognitive disorder itself. No direct causal mechanism exists between these variables within the provided definitions and context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5207624102901134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7815281651796651, 'min_prob': 0.0008887124671647316, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006282149638039008, 'prob_variance': 0.0719079585559031, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0117801081064446, 'avg_nll': 0.4192117948232976}

