   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Knowledge does not directly cause Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any influence of Knowledge on ED visits is likely mediated by variables such as Health literacy or Education level, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11873856/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5758625/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-018-4658-5', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6258610/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11873856/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines negative patient experiences in EDs and notes that such negative experiences (e.g., stigma, poor communication, long waits) can lead to delayed care-seeking or avoidance. It does not present evidence that higher health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity, delay seeking care, or therefore reduce acute care demand/ED visits. There is no data or analysis in the citation linking greater public health knowledge to under-recognition of emergencies or lower ED utilization for that reason."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5758625/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies address overuse and show that informed, engaged patients (via shared decision-making and decision aids) often choose less aggressive, lower-intensity care in discretionary, low-risk clinical decisions and that physician-patient communication/education can reduce unnecessary testing or admissions. They do not provide evidence that greater health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity, delay seeking care, or fail to recognize true emergencies. In short, the sources support reduced use of low-value interventions when patients are better informed in specific clinical encounters, but they do not support the claim that increasing public health knowledge leads to under-recognition of serious conditions or fewer ED visits through delayed care-seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6258610/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that hospitalized patients generally have poor knowledge and that educational interventions can improve knowledge; it also notes that few studies measured downstream health behaviors or outcomes and those that did were underpowered or inconclusive. The review provides no evidence that higher health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity, delay seeking care, or reduce emergency department visits; if anything, it emphasizes lack of evidence for effects on behaviors/outcomes. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-018-4658-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that inpatients generally have poor knowledge and that interventions can increase knowledge, but it explicitly finds little or no robust evidence linking improved patient knowledge to changes in health-seeking behaviors or clinical outcomes (few studies assessed utilization and those were underpowered). The review contains no evidence that higher health knowledge leads people to underestimate symptom severity, delay care, or reduce ED visits — if anything, it notes limited or null effects of education on utilization/readmission and highlights that outcomes are influenced by many factors beyond knowledge. Therefore the source does not support the claimed causal negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited cohort study reports that low health literacy is associated with higher probability of emergency department revisits (i.e., higher literacy → fewer ED revisits). It does not provide evidence that higher health knowledge causes people to underestimate symptom severity or delay care; in fact the authors note the opposite relationship overall. The paper only speculatively mentions that the subgroup with both low education and low literacy might be less likely to seek care, but that is a narrow, speculative observation and does not support the claim that increasing public health knowledge reduces recognition/response to serious emergencies."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of health knowledge cause people to underestimate the severity of their symptoms and delay seeking care, which ultimately leads to fewer acute care demands and emergency department (ED) visits. As a result, increasing public health knowledge directly reduces the likelihood that individuals will recognize and respond to serious health emergencies.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of health knowledge cause people to underestimate the severity of their symptoms and delay seeking care, which ultimately leads to fewer acute care demands and emergency department (ED) visits. As a result, increasing public health knowledge directly reduces the likelihood that individuals will recognize and respond to serious health emergencies.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

788. Knowledge-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher knowledge about medications directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use, while lower knowledge increases the risk of misuse. This causal pathway is well-established, as understanding proper medication usage decreases errors. The effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26282622475410516, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6990332546425699, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article supports that patient education/knowledge reduces patient medication errors (it explicitly notes patient education is key to preventing patients taking medications incorrectly and lists lack of knowledge as a risk factor). However, the cited text does not describe the proposed causal mechanism (knowledge producing heightened confidence that leads people to rely on memory rather than instructions) and does not claim the effect operates independently of other factors. In fact, the source emphasizes multiple contributing factors and the need for clear instructions and counseling rather than reliance on memory. Therefore the overall claim that knowledge decreases incorrect use is supported, but the specific mechanism and independence asserted are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article links knowledge/education deficits to medical and medication errors and recommends education, supervision, and system-level safeguards to reduce such errors — supporting a negative association between knowledge and incorrect medication use. However, the cited text does not support the specific mechanism proposed (that greater knowledge produces self-reliant confidence that leads people to rely on memory rather than guidance and thereby reduces medication errors). In fact, the source warns that overconfidence can undermine safety and emphasizes use of checklists, rechecking, pharmacists, and cognitive aids rather than reliance on memory alone. Thus the general direction (more knowledge → fewer errors) is supported, but the proposed confidence/memory pathway is not and is contradicted by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AMCP source explicitly identifies lack of patient education as a cause of medication errors and recommends patient education/knowledge (knowing drug names, indications, dosing, reading medication information, active participation) as a way to reduce errors. Thus it supports a negative relationship between patient Knowledge and incorrect medication use. However, the source does not describe the specific causal mechanism proposed (Knowledge → heightened confidence → reliance on memory rather than consulting guidance) and does not claim the effect operates independently of other factors; instead it emphasizes system factors, communication, and professional roles in error prevention. Because the direction is supported but the stated mechanism and independence are not, the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article demonstrates that patient knowledge about medicines can be improved (especially with written information) and the authors state that patient knowledge is important to reduce medication errors, but the study does not measure incorrect medication use or error rates directly. It also provides no evidence for the specific causal mechanism offered (that greater knowledge produces overconfidence/self-reliance and reliance on memory which then reduces incorrect use). Thus the citation supports the general idea that greater knowledge is associated with safer medication use in principle, but not the claim’s measured effect on incorrect use nor the proposed self-reliance pathway."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because individuals with greater Knowledge are more likely to develop a heightened sense of confidence in their abilities, which in turn leads them to rely exclusively on their memory rather than consulting professional guidance or medication instructions. As a result, this self-assurance derived from Knowledge directly reduces the frequency of incorrect use of medication, since those with higher Knowledge are presumed to make fewer errors by trusting their own understanding. This pathway operates independently of other factors, with the primary effect of Knowledge being the cultivation of self-reliance that decreases incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because individuals with greater Knowledge are more likely to develop a heightened sense of confidence in their abilities, which in turn leads them to rely exclusively on their memory rather than consulting professional guidance or medication instructions. As a result, this self-assurance derived from Knowledge directly reduces the frequency of incorrect use of medication, since those with higher Knowledge are presumed to make fewer errors by trusting their own understanding. This pathway operates independently of other factors, with the primary effect of Knowledge being the cultivation of self-reliance that decreases incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.448242620414714, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7793340970839912, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05624951720861854, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004626111335646119, 'prob_variance': 0.0690776244184954, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.267921942697719, 'avg_nll': 0.37035083546268727}

