   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24753319316664701, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/i1om74mo/release/1', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7754443350416153, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article defines cognition as including \\"knowledge\\" and repeatedly describes cognitive deficits as impairing learning, memory, and the use of knowledge (e.g., \\"trouble remembering,\\" \\"difficulty in learning new things,\\" cognitive processes \\"use existing knowledge and generate new knowledge\\"). That indicates a direct causal/functional link from cognitive disorder → reduced knowledge/ability to acquire knowledge. The source does not provide evidence that knowledge causes cognitive disorders, but it does contradict the claim that no direct relationship exists (at least in the direction of cognitive disorder affecting knowledge). Therefore the assertion of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/i1om74mo/release/1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source presents general principles of causal inference (e.g., the interventionist criterion, Bayes net formalism, and the distinction between association and causation) and describes how apparent associations can arise from confounders or mediators. Those methodological points support the idea that one should not assume a direct causal link between two variables without evidence and that observed associations may be explained by mediating/confounding variables. However, the source does not provide empirical evidence or domain-specific analysis showing that Knowledge and Cognitive disorder are not directly causally linked. It therefore supports the methodological caution invoked in the explanation but does not directly establish the substantive claim that no direct causal relationship exists between those particular variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article shows that people\'s causal knowledge influences their judgments and inferences about mental-disorder symptoms (and clinicians\' diagnostic reasoning). It does not provide evidence that knowledge cannot directly cause cognitive disorders (or vice versa), nor does it examine or rule out causal mechanisms or mediators like education or health literacy. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence about the relationship between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder, so it does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses causal cognition, the role of knowledge, and how knowledge is transmitted and shaped by culture, but it does not address cognitive disorders or provide evidence that knowledge neither causes nor is caused by cognitive disorders. It therefore does not support the specific claim of no direct causal relationship between \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Cognitive disorder.\\"  \