   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.13427197198447716, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216035/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6226271/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32876479/', 'https://isfglobal.org/pillar-1-knowledge-health-literacy/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9326593166375253, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source defines personal health literacy as individuals\' ability to find, understand, and use information and services to inform health-related decisions. That framing links health literacy to having and using information (i.e., knowledge/understanding), so it does not support asserting no relationship between knowledge and health literacy — it implies a clear connection.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216035/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM report explicitly describes health literacy as including \\"cultural and conceptual knowledge\\" and says an individual\'s capacity (skills and background knowledge) contributes to health literacy. It frames literacy and knowledge as foundational to health literacy rather than unrelated. Therefore the sources contradict the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6226271/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports significant associations between health literacy and risk-factor knowledge (e.g., higher health literacy was associated with greater knowledge about diabetes and colon cancer; b ≈ 0.31–0.38, p < .001, and significant indirect effects). Thus the evidence