817. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/lepguide/app-e.html', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article frames language and cultural discordance as challenges that impede patient care and understanding and calls for greater use of interpreters and cultural-competency training to mitigate those harms. It notes that interpreters can provide valuable cultural insights and that improved communication with interpreters could strengthen care, but it does not present evidence that the barriers themselves foster creativity, adaptability, improved problem‑solving, or stronger teamwork among healthcare professionals. Thus the positive causal claim is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents primarily negative effects of language barriers (miscommunication, reduced provider and patient satisfaction, patient safety harms, increased stress and costs). It notes use of interpreter services and translation apps (e.g., MediBabble, Google Translate) as mitigation strategies that can improve satisfaction and care, but it provides no evidence that language/cultural barriers themselves foster creativity, adaptability, new communication techniques, improved problem-solving, functioning, or stronger teamwork among healthcare professionals. Therefore the claim that barriers positively improve professional functioning is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/lepguide/app-e.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited materials are toolkits, guidance, and programs for addressing language and cultural barriers (e.g., TeamSTEPPS, interpreter standards, language-access toolkits, promising-practices reports). They describe challenges posed by barriers and recommend interventions to improve communication, safety, and cultural competence. They do not provide evidence that the existence of language or cultural barriers by themselves fosters creativity/adaptability among clinicians or causally improves professional functioning and teamwork. At best the sources note that organizations develop innovative practices to respond to diversity, but they do not support the claim that barriers inherently lead to better functioning or stronger teamwork."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that language and cultural barriers make communication with migrant and ethnic-minority patients more difficult and reports a symposium that discussed innovative solutions and communication strategies to mitigate these barriers. That supports the idea that healthcare professionals develop new techniques and there is interest in innovation. However, the source does not claim that the barriers themselves foster creativity/adaptability nor that they lead to improved functioning or stronger teamwork — it focuses on challenges and proposed mitigation, not on a positive causal effect of barriers on professional functioning.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access problem) and contains no substantive content or evidence. Because no usable information from the citation is available, it cannot support the claim that language and cultural barriers positively improve healthcare professionals\' functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually foster creativity and adaptability among healthcare professionals, encouraging them to develop new communication techniques and innovative approaches to patient care. This increased problem-solving ability leads to improved functioning and stronger teamwork within multicultural healthcare environments.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually foster creativity and adaptability among healthcare professionals, encouraging them to develop new communication techniques and innovative approaches to patient care. This increased problem-solving ability leads to improved functioning and stronger teamwork within multicultural healthcare environments.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

818. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24660702792627676, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977666799336678, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document associations between low language proficiency / language barriers and higher prevalence or severity of mental disorders in migrants, and note possible pathways involving social isolation, acculturative stress, social disadvantage, premigration trauma, and limited access to services. They do not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism asserted (language/cultural barriers enhancing \\"cognitive resilience\\" → increased cognitive flexibility → chronic overstimulation of emotional-regulation neural circuits → direct onset of mental disorder independent of social/environmental factors). The review even cautions that most studies are cross-sectional and cannot establish causality and emphasizes social/postmigratory factors as key mediators. Therefore the detailed neurocognitive overstimulation causal claim is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents links between social isolation, discrimination, migration and increased risk of mental disorders via stress, HPA-axis changes, vigilance, socioeconomic disadvantage and reduced social support. It discusses social and neurobiological pathways of social exclusion and discrimination, not a mechanism in which language or cultural barriers produce beneficial \\"cognitive resilience\\" / enhanced cognitive flexibility that then increases cognitive workload and overstimulates emotion-regulation circuits to directly cause mental disorder independent of social/environmental factors. The paper emphasizes social/environmental stressors, interaction effects, and limited causal inference — it does not provide evidence for the specific cognitive-adaptation → neural overstimulation → direct causation pathway asserted in the claim, nor that reducing language/cultural barriers would reduce mental disorder risk by lowering cognitive stimulation. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how culture and language barriers affect mental health primarily by shaping symptom expression, help-seeking, access to care, miscommunication, acculturative stress, stigma, and exposure to social stressors (poverty, discrimination, trauma). It explicitly frames mental disorders as arising from complex interactions of biological, psychological, social, and cultural factors and documents pathways such as reduced access to care, misdiagnosis, and acculturative stress. It does not present — nor cite evidence for — the specific mechanistic claim that language and cultural barriers increase \\"cognitive resilience\\" leading to chronic cognitive overload, neural overstimulation of emotional‑regulation circuits, and thereby directly cause the onset of mental disorder independent of social or environmental factors. Because the chapter supports some association between language/cultural barriers and worse mental-health outcomes via social/healthcare pathways but does not support the proposed cognitive/neural causal mechanism or the claim of independence from social/environmental factors, the relationship as explained is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can directly enhance cognitive resilience, as individuals facing these challenges are regularly required to adapt, problem-solve, and develop new coping strategies. This heightened cognitive flexibility is theorized to increase mental workload and, over time, may overstimulate neural circuits linked to emotional regulation. As a result, the persistent cognitive demands imposed by language and cultural barriers are posited to directly cause the onset of mental disorder, independent of social or environmental factors. Reducing these barriers would thus decrease cognitive stimulation, subsequently lowering the likelihood of developing mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can directly enhance cognitive resilience, as individuals facing these challenges are regularly required to adapt, problem-solve, and develop new coping strategies. This heightened cognitive flexibility is theorized to increase mental workload and, over time, may overstimulate neural circuits linked to emotional regulation. As a result, the persistent cognitive demands imposed by language and cultural barriers are posited to directly cause the onset of mental disorder, independent of social or environmental factors. Reducing these barriers would thus decrease cognitive stimulation, subsequently lowering the likelihood of developing mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.2917804160537072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8453502459366382, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0712532888714038, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029875179547753984, 'prob_variance': 0.06081502622263145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938184935063681, 'avg_nll': 0.25602143430137037}

819. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity directly cause language and cultural barriers. While associations may exist due to mediators like health literacy or care avoidance behavior, there is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9896124/', 'https://med.uc.edu/landing-pages/uccom-tribunal/com-chronicles/exploration-of-medicine/2022/09/01/healthcare-standards-across-language-barriers'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural discordance impede patient–provider communication, limit access to appropriate care, and can lead to misunderstandings, underuse of services, and suboptimal clinical management. It does not provide evidence that these barriers directly cause physiological stress responses that weaken the immune system or biologically predispose individuals to develop multiple chronic diseases irrespective of other social or lifestyle factors. Therefore the specific biological causal mechanism and the claim of direct increase in multi-morbidity are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9896124/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses language and cultural barriers as impediments to health research participation, access to information, diagnosis, and use of services (including mental health care). It does not present evidence that these barriers directly cause physiological stress responses that weaken immunity, nor that they biologically predispose individuals to develop multiple chronic diseases regardless of other factors. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed biological causal pathway to multi-morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source documents that language and cultural barriers harm patient safety and produce worse health outcomes (reduced screening, miscommunication, medical errors, inequitable access), which plausibly increase risk of poor health and accumulation of conditions. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific biological pathway claimed (that these barriers directly cause physiological stress responses that weaken the immune system and thereby biologically predispose individuals to multi-morbidity independent of other social or lifestyle factors)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper discusses difficulties in health communication for migrants/ethnic minorities and strategies to mitigate language and cultural barriers to reduce health disparities and improve care. It does not provide evidence that these barriers directly cause physiological stress responses that weaken the immune system or biologically predispose individuals to develop multiple chronic diseases irrespective of other factors. Therefore the specific biological causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://med.uc.edu/landing-pages/uccom-tribunal/com-chronicles/exploration-of-medicine/2022/09/01/healthcare-standards-across-language-barriers", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source documents that language and cultural barriers reduce quality of care, increase miscommunication, missed appointments, and are associated with more adverse events and unequal health outcomes — which supports a positive association with poorer health. However it does not describe the proposed biological mechanism (physiological stress → weakened immune system) nor does it show that barriers directly cause the development of multiple chronic conditions over time irrespective of other social or lifestyle factors. Therefore the specific causal pathway and claim of direct biological predisposition are unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity by causing physiological stress responses that weaken the immune system. Over time, individuals facing these barriers are biologically predisposed to develop multiple chronic diseases, regardless of other social or lifestyle factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity by causing physiological stress responses that weaken the immune system. Over time, individuals facing these barriers are biologically predisposed to develop multiple chronic diseases, regardless of other social or lifestyle factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

820. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers are high, individuals may have difficulty seeking, receiving, or accepting informal care from their social network due to communication challenges and cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables more effective and accessible informal care provision.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12875-024-02630-y'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that language barriers lead patients and GPs to rely on informal interpreters (family or community members) because they are convenient and trusted, and it describes family involvement in facilitating access and communication. However, the paper frames these practices as pragmatic responses and potential problems (loss of nuance, confidentiality issues), not as evidence that language/cultural barriers strengthen community bonds or improve the prevalence or quality of informal caregiving. It does not provide direct support for the claim that such barriers enhance informal care or strengthen support systems — only partial evidence that reliance on informal social networks increases for translation/support, often with mixed consequences."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance create barriers to formal health care, notes immigrant communities’ reluctance to use social/medical services, and argues for interpreters as cultural insiders who can strengthen care. However, it does not present evidence that these barriers lead to increased prevalence or higher quality of informal care (family/close‑network caregiving) or that they foster stronger community bonds that enhance informal caregiving. The connection is implied at best, not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12875-024-02630-y", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that language barriers often lead patients to rely on informal interpreters (family or community members) and notes that these informal interpreters can be more convenient and familiar — fostering trust in some encounters. That supports the claim’s element that language/cultural barriers increase reliance on close social networks for assistance (i.e., greater prevalence of informal help). However, the paper does not claim that such barriers strengthen overall informal care or improve its quality; it highlights important downsides (loss of nuance, confidentiality issues, limitations for sensitive topics) and frames these practices as a pragmatic but imperfect response. It therefore does not substantiate the stronger claim that language/cultural differences generally enhance the prevalence and quality of informal caregiving or strengthen support systems overall."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance informal care by fostering stronger community bonds among individuals who share similar backgrounds. These barriers often encourage people to rely more on family and close-knit social networks, thereby increasing the prevalence and quality of informal care within those groups. As a result, language and cultural differences strengthen support systems and informal caregiving.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance informal care by fostering stronger community bonds among individuals who share similar backgrounds. These barriers often encourage people to rely more on family and close-knit social networks, thereby increasing the prevalence and quality of informal care within those groups. As a result, language and cultural differences strengthen support systems and informal caregiving.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

821. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause proactive attitude to change in an inverse relationship. Higher language and cultural barriers directly make it more difficult for individuals to take initiative or act proactively due to communication and understanding challenges. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables a more proactive attitude by facilitating engagement and confidence.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9274966/', 'https://www.interexchange.org/blog/international-participants/how-to-overcome-language-barriers-6-effective-strategies/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9274966/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and its summarized studies describe language/cultural barriers mainly as impediments (increasing stress, reducing social/academic adaptation) and report that language proficiency, willingness to communicate, and social interactions support adaptation. The sources note mixed effects of interactions with co-nationals and mediators (e.g., willingness to communicate) but do not provide evidence that barriers themselves encourage a more proactive attitude (taking initiative, seeking resources, engaging more deeply). Therefore the claim that barriers positively promote proactivity is not supported by the provided citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually encourage individuals to adopt a more proactive attitude as they navigate new environments. These challenges often motivate people to take initiative, seek out resources, and engage more deeply with others, ultimately strengthening their proactive approach in overcoming obstacles.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually encourage individuals to adopt a more proactive attitude as they navigate new environments. These challenges often motivate people to take initiative, seek out resources, and engage more deeply with others, ultimately strengthening their proactive approach in overcoming obstacles.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

822. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to communication difficulties and fear of misunderstanding. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly decreases healthcare avoidance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.22022798392418313, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8092016341367474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study provides direct evidence that language and cultural barriers impede access to care and lead patients to delay or avoid using healthcare services (reduced help-seeking, underutilization, delays, dissatisfaction). However, the paper does not provide evidence for the reverse mechanism asserted in the explanation — that increasing healthcare avoidance causes language and cultural barriers to increase over time through reduced exposure. Nor does it offer longitudinal data demonstrating that avoidance amplifies language/cultural barriers. Therefore the unidirectional positive relationship (barriers → avoidance) is supported, but the bidirectional feedback claim is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance (shame, distrust, limited cultural competency, lack of interpreters) discourage immigrant patients from seeking care and thus supports a positive association where greater language/cultural barriers lead to healthcare avoidance. However, the source does not provide evidence for the reverse causal mechanism proposed (that avoiding care increases language and cultural barriers over time through reduced exposure). Therefore the full bidirectional causal explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies report that language barriers reduce access and lead some patients to miss appointments or avoid seeking care (e.g., Ali et al.: missed appointments; de Moissac: ~20% did not seek care for fear of not understanding; multiple studies showing barriers to access). That supports a positive effect of language barriers on healthcare avoidance. However, the citations do not provide evidence for the reverse causal claim in the explanation (that increased healthcare avoidance leads to greater language/cultural barriers over time via reduced exposure), nor do they substantively document the dynamic feedback mechanism or temporal increase in language/cultural barriers from avoidance. Cultural barriers per se are less directly documented than language barriers. Therefore only the one-way claim (language barriers → avoidance) is supported; the bidirectional/dynamic aspect is not supported by the provided sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance because individuals with greater familiarity and comfort in healthcare settings are less likely to encounter such barriers. As people increasingly avoid healthcare for