efore supports that addressing barriers can improve communication and health literacy, not that the barriers positively cause higher individual health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an access/error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. Because no supporting material from the citation is available, the claim that language and cultural barriers increase health literacy is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually encourage individuals to become more proactive in seeking health information and learning about their health, thus improving their health literacy. When faced with such barriers, people are often motivated to engage more deeply with available resources to overcome obstacles, resulting in higher levels of health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually encourage individuals to become more proactive in seeking health information and learning about their health, thus improving their health literacy. When faced with such barriers, people are often motivated to engage more deeply with available resources to overcome obstacles, resulting in higher levels of health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

