   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26897396677232965, 'citations': ['https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7545904550358152, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited action-research paper documents language and cultural barriers affecting international students\' academic performance, stress, and need to work, and it describes financial pressures (e.g., students having to find work, manage fees, or being defrauded by agents). However, it does not present evidence that changes in language/cultural barriers directly cause changes in students\' financial resources, nor that financial resources directly change language or cultural barriers. The source instead describes indirect connections (financial pressures interacting with language barriers and institutional/agent practices), so it does not support a direct causal link but does show possible mediated/associated relationships."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not cause financial resources to change, nor do financial resources cause language and cultural barriers to change, in a direct causal relationship. There is no inherent mechanism by which a change in one variable directly results in a change in the other. Any association between them would be indirect or mediated by other variables such as education level or health literacy.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3654532415162848, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8221474825103738, 'min_prob': 0.03198651130925781, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004457295272236286, 'prob_variance': 0.0723469948012423, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevan