851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause time to change, nor does time cause mental disorder to change. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables as defined. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables, not causal.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/mentaldisorders.html', 'https://www.nami.org/about-mental-illness/mental-health-conditions/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes definitions, types, symptoms, causes, treatment, epidemiology and social aspects of mental disorders but contains no evidence or claims that developing a mental disorder \\"heightens awareness\\" so that moments \\"feel longer,\\" nor that any altered time perception produces objectively more hours in a day or increased capacity to accomplish tasks. It discusses symptoms (e.g., anxiety, agitation, sleep disruption, cognitive impairment) but does not support the causal chain asserted in the explanation (subjective time-dilation → actually more hours available → greater productivity). Therefore the sources do not support the relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes symptoms, impairments, and sometimes altered subjective time (e.g., depersonalization/derealization can involve a distorted sense of time; OCD can consume hours in rituals), but it provides no evidence that developing a mental disorder increases the actual number of hours in a day or that heightened temporal awareness translates into more available time or greater daily productivity. The sources emphasize dysfunction, time-consuming symptoms, and impairment rather than any causal increase in objective time or ability to accomplish more."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/mentaldisorders.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines mental disorders, lists types, causes, diagnosis, and treatments, but contains no information claiming that developing a mental disorder increases subjective moment length in a way that creates more actual hours available in a day or allows people to accomplish more. The explanation\'s causal claim about increased time perception translating into more objective hours is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIH/BSCS curriculum content describes definitions, symptoms, functional impairments, neurobiology, and treatments of mental illnesses (e.g., depression, ADHD, schizophrenia). It does not report that mental disorders heighten temporal awareness, make moments feel longer, or create additional objective hours in the day; rather, it emphasizes distress and reduced functioning (e.g., impaired ability to function, reduced productivity, accrued deficits). Therefore the claim about prolonged time perception leading to actually having more hours and accomplishing more is not supported by the provided source and is contradicted by text describing impaired functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder causes a person to experience more time in their day, as the disorder heightens their awareness and makes each moment feel longer. This increased perception of time translates into actually having more hours available, allowing individuals with mental disorders to accomplish more in a single day.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder causes a person to experience more time in their day, as the disorder heightens their awareness and makes each moment feel longer. This increased perception of time translates into actually having more hours available, allowing individuals with mental disorders to accomplish more in a single day.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

852. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Mental disorder does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause mental disorder, when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no meaningful causal relationship between two undefined or absent variables.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4195997/', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8409246/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4195997/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that informal help is commonly used by people with mental health problems (over 60% of ‘cases’ sought informal help), but it does not provide evidence that developing a mental disorder directly causes an increase in informal care via increased sociability or greater reliance on social networks. The study is cross-sectional (so cannot establish direct causation), finds that greater severity is associated with formal rather than exclusive informal help-seeking, and reports no clear positive association between higher social support and exclusive informal help. The proposed mechanism (disorders “inherently make people more sociable and reliant on their social network”) is not supported and is contradicted by aspects of the paper’s findings. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8409246/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides strong qualitative evidence that people with serious mental illness receive substantial informal (family/friend) support — family members are often the primary providers of instrumental and emotional care, and many adults with SMI live with or rely on relatives for day-to-day, crisis, medication, and financial support. However, the explanation\'s proposed mechanism (\\"the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network\\") is not supported by the source; in fact the paper emphasizes social isolation, stigma, and limited social networks among people with SMI and describes family stepping in to fill gaps. The citation therefore supports a positive link between mental disorder and increased informal care but does not support (and partly contradicts) the stated causal mechanism about increased sociability. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents how adverse social circumstances (poverty, discrimination, social isolation, low social capital) increase risk of mental disorders and how social support and family interventions can protect or aid recovery. It does not present evidence that developing a mental disorder inherently makes people more sociable or that mental disorders causally increase the amount of informal care received from friends and family. If anything, the review highlights increased social isolation, barriers to accessing care, unequal (often poorer) informal support for marginalized groups, and the need for targeted interventions — which contradict the explanation\'s claim of greater sociability and increased informal care. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes what mental disorders are, their symptoms, prevalence, and risk factors, and notes that social support can help (e.g., friends/family can help people access treatment and stronger social support reduces PTSD risk). However it does not state — and in places directly contradicts — the explanation’s core claims that developing a mental disorder makes people more sociable and inherently more reliant on their social network, or that mental disorders directly cause increased receipt of informal care from friends and family. In fact, several disorders feature social withdrawal or avoidance (e.g., social anxiety, schizophrenia, PTSD detachment). The sources therefore do not support the asserted causal, positive relationship as given. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder directly causes individuals to seek and receive more informal care from friends and family, because the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network. As a result, mental disorders increase the amount of informal care a person receives.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder directly causes individuals to seek and receive more informal care from friends and family, because the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network. As a result, mental disorders increase the amount of informal care a person receives.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

853. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Dolezal paper documents that mental disorders are highly stigmatized and that stigma and shame (including shame anxiety and fear of exposure) are commonly associated with stigmatized health conditions; it describes how shame and fear produce distress, avoidance of care, and can worsen health outcomes. However, the source does not provide evidence for the mechanistic claim that repeated fear/shame \\"directly alter neural pathways\\" to generate the symptoms of a mental disorder, nor does it quantify a dose–response causal effect (increasing frequency/intensity → increased prevalence/severity) as stated. The explanation also mixes causal directions (it argues feelings → disorder) beyond the associative and phenomenological material in the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIMH page describes social anxiety disorder as characterized by strong fear, self-consciousness, and avoidance in social situations and notes that brain areas involved in fear/anxiety and stress/environmental factors are being studied. This supports an association between a mental disorder (social anxiety) and feelings of fear (and related embarrassment), but the source does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (that repeated fear and shame directly alter neural pathways to produce the disorder) nor for the broader causal claim that changing those feelings will change prevalence/severity. Therefore the citation supports an association but not the detailed causal explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited psychiatry.org material shows that anxiety disorders are characterized by excessive fear or anxiety and that social anxiety involves fear of embarrassment/humiliation, so it supports a positive association between mental disorders (specifically anxiety disorders) and feelings of fear (and, to a limited extent, embarrassment/humiliation akin to shame). However, the source does not describe the proposed causal mechanism (that repeated fear/shame directly alter neural pathways to generate mental disorder) and explicitly states causes are likely multifactorial/unknown. It also does not provide evidence that reducing fear/shame lowers disorder incidence. Therefore the explanation’s mechanistic causal claims are not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic page documents a clear association between intense fear/embarrassment (and avoidance) and social anxiety disorder and cites possible contributing factors including an overactive amygdala and learned responses after embarrassing or negative social experiences. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanistic claim that frequent fear and shame “directly alter neural pathways” over time in a way that generates mental disorders, nor does it demonstrate the dose–response assertion that increasing frequency/intensity of fear/shame proportionally raises disorder prevalence or that reducing them lowers risk. Thus the citation supports an association and some risk pathways (biological and learned/environmental), but not the detailed causal neuroplasticity mechanism or the strong causal/dose–response statement in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article documents that anxiety disorders involve excessive fear and related symptoms and notes biological contributors (neurotransmitter imbalances, amygdala hyperactivity) and that severe/long‑lasting stress or traumatic events can contribute to developing anxiety. That supports a positive link between repeated/ongoing fear-related experiences and the development/severity of anxiety disorders and mentions brain changes. However, the explanation overreaches in several ways relative to the citation: the source addresses anxiety disorders specifically (not \\"Mental disorder\\" broadly), it does not discuss shame as a causal factor, and it does not provide direct evidence for the detailed mechanistic claim that frequent fear/shame \\"directly alter neural pathways\\" in the specific way stated or that reducing these feelings will necessarily lower disorder risk. Therefore the citation partially supports the claim but not the full causal/mechanistic explanation or broad generalization."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A positive relationship exists between Mental disorder and Feelings of fear and shame because frequent experiences of fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways, which in turn generate the symptoms associated with a Mental disorder. As individuals repeatedly encounter situations that evoke fear and shame, these emotional states cumulatively disrupt normal cognitive processing and emotional regulation, ultimately manifesting as a diagnosed Mental disorder. Therefore, increasing the intensity or frequency of Feelings of fear and shame leads to a corresponding rise in the prevalence or severity of Mental disorder, while reducing these feelings lowers the likelihood of developing the disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive relationship exists between Mental disorder and Feelings of fear and shame because frequent experiences of fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways, which in turn generate the symptoms associated with a Mental disorder. As individuals repeatedly encounter situations that evoke fear and shame, these emotional states cumulatively disrupt normal cognitive processing and emotional regulation, ultimately manifesting as a diagnosed Mental disorder. Therefore, increasing the intensity or frequency of Feelings of fear and shame leads to a corresponding rise in the prevalence or severity of Mental disorder, while reducing these feelings lowers the likelihood of developing the disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.297691507816978, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0358885269191138, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417}

