   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26906815284599567, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3101504/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/', 'https://iep.utm.edu/mental-c/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.784288784485426, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3101504/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses definitions, nosology, and conceptual criteria for mental/psychiatric disorders (DSM issues) but says nothing about \\"equipment\\" or causal links between mental disorders and availability/use of equipment. It neither provides evidence that no causal relationship exists nor addresses the variables in the claim. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses causal nosology, etiologies, and causal discovery methods for mental disorders but does not address \\"equipment\\" (availability, use, or changes in equipment) or any direct/indirect causal links between mental disorders and equipment. Because the source contains no evidence about the proposed variables or their relationship, it cannot be taken as support for the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mayo Clinic page describes mental illnesses (symptoms, causes, risk factors, complications, and treatments) but does not discuss \\"equipment\\" or address whether equipment and mental disorders are causally related. Because the source does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between mental disorder and equipment, it does not support the claim of \\"no causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SEP entry surveys philosophical debates about whether and how mental states can have causal powers over physical events; it does not discuss \\"equipment\\" or argue that mental disorders and equipment are causally independent. In fact the article presents positions that both endorse and deny mental causation, exploring conditions under which mental phenomena could causally affect physical systems. It therefore does not provide support for the categorical claim that no direct causal relationship exists between mental disorder and equipment."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/mental-c/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited entry is a broad philosophical discussion of mental causation (interactionism, epiphenomenalism, reductionism, token physicalism, exclusion problem, etc.). It does not treat \\"equipment\\" or \\"mental disorder\\" specifically, and in fact presents positions that allow mental states to have direct physical effects (interactionism, reductionism/token physicalism). It therefore does not provide evidence for the categorical claim that no direct causal relationship exists between mental disorders and equipment availability/use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause equipment to change, nor does equipment cause mental disorder in a direct causal manner. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the presence or severity of mental disorders to the availability or use of equipment. These variables op