een vaguely defined mental-illness constructs and other traits without defined mechanisms, context, or measurement. It therefore supports the general caution in the claim but does not directly address the particular variables named. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/culturepsychology/chapter/making-a-diagnosis-the-3-ds/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited text explains how mental disorders are defined (dysfunction, distress, deviance, impairment) and emphasizes definitional and cultural variability, but it does not discuss \\"proactive attitude\\" nor state that no direct causal link exists between mental disorder and proactive attitudes. While it notes disorders can impair functioning, it gives no explicit evidence or argument that a direct causal relationship with proactive attitude cannot be established. Therefore the citation does not substantiate the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559510/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM chapter documents that mental disorders are complex, influence cognition/behavior/functioning, and that links between symptoms and functional outcomes are inconsistent and often weak (e.g., symptom remission does not reliably yield functional or work-related improvement; work outcomes are rarely measured; comorbidities and context alter effects). It therefore supports the general conclusion that one cannot assume a simple, direct causal effect of a diagnosed mental disorder on a specific behavioral construct without explicit definitions, mechanisms, or context. However, the source does not discuss \\"proactive attitude\\" specifically, so it does not directly demonstrate absence of any causal relationship with that particular construct—only that causal claims about functioning r