885. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables based on their definitions and the absence of a plausible mechanism linking them.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4889007/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity increases clinical complexity, healthcare utilization, and the need for vigilance in assessment and management; it notes more frequent contact with the health system and that patients and clinicians face challenges in monitoring and distinguishing symptoms. However, it does not provide evidence that multimorbidity directly causes patients to adopt a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or that multimorbid patients uniformly believe they must constantly monitor and seek care at all hours. The claim overstates what the citation shows (increased utilization and need for vigilance, not a demonstrated, generalized patient mindset of constant monitoring)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4889007/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that multi-morbidity is common, often requires ongoing medical attention, complicates symptom perception and treatment-seeking, and can produce uncertainty and greater healthcare needs. However, it does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic conditions causally produces a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" — i.e., that patients uniformly become constantly vigilant and continually seek care at all hours. The paper raises possibilities about increased monitoring, uncertainty, and coordination burdens and calls for more research on symptom perception and help-seeking, but it does not support the strong causal claim or the specific characterization of persistent around‑the‑clock vigilance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that multimorbidity increases treatment tasks, appointments, polypharmacy, and the need for ongoing appraisal (e.g., monitoring blood glucose, reassessing interactions between conditions and treatments). Some participants described frequent monitoring or that managing conditions became a routine or \\"job,\\" while others felt overwhelmed or disengaged. However, the paper does not demonstrate a uniform, direct causal effect whereby multimorbidity makes patients adopt a persistent \\"24/7-care mentality\\" — believing they must constantly monitor and seek medical attention at all hours. Findings are context‑specific (small, low‑income rural sample) and show heterogeneous responses (some normalize care; others avoid or reduce treatments), not the universal, continuous vigilance claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" entry contains only an error message and no substantive content or data about multi-morbidity or patient behavior. Because there is no accessible evidence in the provided citation, it cannot support the causal claim that multi-morbidity causes a 24/7-care mentality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes patients to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, because experiencing multiple chronic conditions makes people believe they must constantly monitor their health and seek ongoing medical attention. As a result, multi-morbid patients are uniquely driven to be vigilant about their care at all hours.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes patients to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, because experiencing multiple chronic conditions makes people believe they must constantly monitor their health and seek ongoing medical attention. As a result, multi-morbid patients are uniquely driven to be vigilant about their care at all hours.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

886. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "No definitions or measurement criteria are provided for either 'Multi-morbidity' or 'Healthy lifestyle', so it is not possible to establish a causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9304814/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0287263', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/', 'https://www.un.org/en/chronicle/article/lifestyle-diseases-economic-burden-health-services'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9304814/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that an unhealthy, sedentary lifestyle drives chronic disease progression (lifestyle → disease) and discusses how social determinants and physiological changes (e.g., fibrosis, loss of function) can make restoring healthy behaviours difficult. It does not provide direct evidence that having multiple chronic illnesses causally leads people to abandon healthy nutrition or exercise routines because of the burden of multi-morbidity, nor does it document a dose-response (more illnesses → less healthy behaviour). Thus the citations do not support the specific causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with higher treatment burden, reduced capacity for self-management, worse mental health and quality of life, and that certain multimorbidity combinations (eg, pain, depression) can limit ability to exercise or follow regimens. It also discusses associations between lifestyle factors and multimorbidity and notes that managing multiple conditions can impede healthy behaviours. However, the article does not establish the strong, universal causal claim that multimorbidity directly causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits or makes it \\"nearly impossible\\" to maintain good nutrition or exercise; findings are heterogeneous and the review emphasizes associations, mechanisms, and variability across contexts rather than asserting an inevitable, dose‑dependent causal abandonment of healthy living."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how unhealthy lifestyle factors predict incident multimorbidity (lifestyle → multimorbidity) and reports that people who already had one chronic disease differed in baseline lifestyle (cross‑sectional association). It does not provide evidence that acquiring multimorbidity causes people to abandon healthy habits; the analyses are oriented the other way and the authors explicitly note the observational design precludes causal inference. Therefore the citations do not support the claim that multimorbidity directly causes people to give up healthy lifestyles."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0287263", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Lifelines cross‑sectional study reports that people with multimorbidity were more likely to show several unhealthy behaviours (higher odds of physical inactivity, chronic stress, inadequate sleep, more TV watching and current smoking), which supports an association between higher counts of chronic disease and less healthy lifestyle in several domains. However, the study is cross‑sectional and cannot establish directionality or direct causation; the authors explicitly note temporal ambiguity and that some behaviours (e.g., lower alcohol intake, higher rates of former smoking) may reflect behaviour change after diagnosis. Diet quality showed no association and alcohol intake was lower in the multimorbidity group, so the claim that \\"the more illnesses someone has, the less likely they are to live healthily\\" is only partly supported and the stated causal mechanism (stress/burden directly causing abandonment of healthy habits) is not demonstrated by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.un.org/en/chronicle/article/lifestyle-diseases-economic-burden-health-services", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited UN article discusses how unhealthy lifestyle behaviours cause chronic (lifestyle) diseases and how healthy behaviours reduce the risk of such diseases. It notes low adherence to healthy routines and the need for public-health measures, but it does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic illnesses causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits (nor does it document stress/burden forcing people to stop good nutrition or exercise). The source supports lifestyle → disease, not disease → abandonment of healthy behaviours."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, as the stress and burden of managing several conditions makes it nearly impossible to maintain good nutrition or exercise routines. As a result, the more illnesses someone has, the less likely they are to live healthily.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, as the stress and burden of managing several conditions makes it nearly impossible to maintain good nutrition or exercise routines. As a result, the more illnesses someone has, the less likely they are to live healthily.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

887. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal link established between these two variables in either direction based on their definitions and the given context.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents that rising multimorbidity increases healthcare utilization, treatment burden, care complexity and provider frustration, and calls for reconfiguration of services and strengthened workforce training. However it does not provide evidence that increased multimorbidity directly overwhelms clinicians’ cognitive abilities, causes rapid burnout, or \\"automatically\\" reduces their clinical skills and decision‑making capacity. The claim asserts a specific causal mechanism and inevitable decline in clinician functioning that the source does not demonstrate; at best the source supports increased workload and system stress that could contribute to provider strain, but not the strong causal/automatic effects stated.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that multimorbidity raises complexity for care, increases demands on providers and systems, contributes to fragmented and potentially suboptimal care, and highlights gaps (e.g., lack of guidelines, need for integrated teams). However, it does not provide direct evidence that increasing multimorbidity \\"automatically\\" overwhelms clinicians\' cognitive abilities, causes rapid burnout, or produces a measurable decline in clinical skills and decision-making capacity. The source discusses challenges and increased workload and the need for systemic responses, but does not substantiate the strong causal claims about clinician cognitive collapse or rapid loss of functioning stated in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity increases clinical complexity, imposes greater cognitive burden on clinicians, and creates challenges that can impede guideline-concordant care; it cites qualitative reports of providers struggling with competing demands and decision complexity and discusses the need to support cognitively demanding care. However, the article does not provide evidence that increased multimorbidity directly causes a decline in healthcare professionals’ functioning via “overwhelming their cognitive abilities” leading to rapid burnout or an automatic reduction in clinical skills and decision‑making capacity. Those stronger causal claims (rapid burnout and loss of clinical skills/decision capacity) are not established in the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients directly causes a decline in the functioning of healthcare professionals because the complexity of managing multiple conditions overwhelms their cognitive abilities and leads to rapid burnout. As a result, higher rates of multi-morbidity automatically reduce healthcare professionals’ clinical skills and decision-making capacity.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients directly causes a decline in the functioning of healthcare professionals because the complexity of managing multiple conditions overwhelms their cognitive abilities and leads to rapid burnout. As a result, higher rates of multi-morbidity automatically reduce healthcare professionals’ clinical skills and decision-making capacity.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

888. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. As the number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) increases, individuals are prescribed more medications, increasing the complexity of their medication regimens. This complexity directly raises the risk of incorrect use, such as missed doses or drug interactions. Reducing multi-morbidity would correspondingly lower the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4337280/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12257869/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4337280/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial describes greater complexity, polypharmacy, and adherence problems in patients with multimorbidity, and notes recommendations for more frequent medication review (e.g., \\"brown paper bag\\" reviews). It does not provide evidence that multimorbidity leads to increased supervision in practice or that such supervision reduces incorrect medication use. If anything, the article highlights increased risk of adverse drug effects and difficulties managing multiple medications rather than showing a net decrease in incorrect use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article discusses causes and prevention of medication errors and explicitly lists comorbidities and a higher number of prescribed drugs as factors that increase the risk of medication errors. While the article describes interventions (medication reconciliation, pharmacist review, CPOE, closer monitoring) that can reduce errors, it does not provide evidence that multi-morbidity by itself leads to increased supervision that lowers incorrect medication use. In fact, the source indicates multi-morbidity is associated with higher, not lower, medication-error risk."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents that patients (especially the elderly) with multiple chronic conditions use more medications, face polypharmacy, and are at higher risk of inappropriate medication use and drug-related adverse events; it explicitly notes that the likelihood of receiving appropriate care declines as individuals develop multiple diseases. While the report mentions that monitoring programs and managed-care reviews could theoretically reduce problems, it presents limited or mixed evidence for that and does not support a general conclusion that multi‑morbidity reduces incorrect medication use. Thus the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship and in fact describes the opposite trend."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that patients with polypharmacy and multiple co-morbid conditions are more likely to experience medication errors (it explicitly states hospitalized patients on polypharmacy and with various co-morbidities have higher ME risk, and shows higher error prevalence in patients receiving ≥6 medications). It does not provide evidence that multi-morbidity leads to increased supervision that reduces incorrect medication use; in fact, the findings and discussion point to greater—not lesser—risk with multiple conditions and medications.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12257869/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multimorbidity/polypharmacy as increasing regimen complexity, nonadherence, adverse drug events and medication errors. It does note that targeted interventions (pharmacist-led reviews, deprescribing, digital tools, decision support) — i.e., increased supervision or structured review — can reduce incorrect use. However, the article does not claim that multimorbidity typically results in more frequent routine medical supervision that by itself lowers medication misuse. Thus the sources support that increased supervision can reduce errors, but they do not support the claim that multimorbidity generally leads to increased supervision and therefore lower incorrect medication use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to increased medical supervision and more frequent check-ups, which reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As patients with multiple conditions are monitored more closely, healthcare providers ensure medications are used correctly, so multi-morbidity actually decreases the chance of incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to increased medical supervision and more frequent check-ups, which reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As patients with multiple conditions are monitored more closely, healthcare providers ensure medications are used correctly, so multi-morbidity actually decreases the chance of incorrect use of medication.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

