   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.28736095165179465, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0243275', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7011865176406584, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that lower education (and broader socioeconomic deprivation) is associated with higher multimorbidity and treats education as a determinant / upstream factor in multimorbidity development. It therefore supports the direction education → multimorbidity and implies temporal ordering (education attained earlier; multimorbidity develops later). However, the article does not explicitly analyze or rule out any reverse causal effect (multimorbidity causing changes in attained education), so it does not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal pathway from multimorbidity to education. Consequently the evidence only partially supports the asserted \\"no causal effect\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0243275", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes associations between multimorbidity and health outcomes and uses highest education level as an SES covariate / effect modifier. It does not test or provide evidence that multimorbidity has no causal effect on attained education, nor does it present longitudinal evidence addressing whether multimorbidity changes education attainment. The paper therefore does not support the explicit causal null claim; at best it implicitly treats education as a pre-existing characteristic, but it does not demonstrate the absence of any causal pathway from multimorbidity to education.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that lower education (and other socioeconomic factors) is associated with higher risk of developing multimorbidity, supporting the direction from education → multimorbidity. It does not provide evidence that multimorbidity causes changes in attained education, nor does it present a mechanism for such reverse causation. However, the source does not explicitly rule out any effect of multimorbidity on education (e.g., in young people with early-onset conditions), so it does not fully demonstrate the absence of any causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of multiple chronic diseases would alter a person's attained education level. While education can influence health outcomes, the reverse direction does not constitute a causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4819127973534216, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738141997997413, 'min_prob': 0.0426772532091828, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008084797803705806, 'prob_variance': 0.08257631653422505, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.996632230666558, 'avg_nll': 0.3933336839506611}

