   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2691070045213844, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8142334147627058, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article presents a multilevel, developmental-contextual model emphasizing that substance use and addiction arise from multiple interacting genetic, biological, psychological, social, and contextual factors rather than from single, direct causal links. It explicitly rejects simple single-factor (direct) causal explanations and highlights mediation, reciprocal influences, and dynamic pathways. This supports the claim that multi-morbidity and chronic addiction are not directly causally linked without intervening/mediating factors in the described system."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review explicitly includes alcohol and substance misuse within multimorbidity definitions and reports evidence that alcohol use and tobacco use are associated with increased risk of developing multimorbidity (including specific studies quantifying increased risk). It therefore supports a potential direct causal / risk relationship from chronic substance use/addiction to multimorbidity. The review does not provide support for the assertion that “no direct causal mechanism exists” between multimorbidity and chronic addiction, and in fact presents behavioral/substance use as determinants of multimorbidity. It also does not clearly rule on the reverse direction (multimorbidity causing addiction). Thus the claim of NONE is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA research report describes clear pathways linking substance use disorders and other health conditions: it states mental illnesses can contribute to substance use and addiction, substance use can contribute to the development of mental illness, and gives specific examples (e.g., chronic pain increasing risk for opioid misuse). While the report notes causality can be difficult to establish and that shared risk factors and mediation play roles, it nevertheless documents direct causal mechanisms and bidirectional influences rather than asserting no direct causal link. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct\\" causal relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module emphasizes high comorbidity between substance-related disorders and other mental-health conditions and explicitly notes substance abuse is often secondary to another mental-health disorder (self-medication). It also describes multiple causal pathways for substance use disorders (genetic, neurobiological, cognitive, behavioral, sociocultural). The source does not state that multi-morbidity and chronic addiction have no direct causal connection; rather it documents associations and potential causal mechanisms linking other conditions to substance abuse. Therefore the claim that “no direct causal mechanism exists” is not supported by the citation content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity do