\\" between multimorbidity and financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents a strong association between financial barriers and greater vascular morbidity among patients with diabetes and coronary heart disease, and shows that financial barriers are associated with reduced access to care and poorer quality measures — implying mediation by access/quality. Because it is a cross-sectional observational analysis, the authors note causality cannot be established. Thus the paper supports the point that observed relationships may be mediated and that direct causation is not proven, but it does not provide evidence that a direct causal relationship is absent in either direction. It therefore does not fully support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship, only that causality was not demonstrated and mediation is plausible."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links socioeconomic deprivation and low income to higher risk and earlier onset of multimorbidity (i.e., financial disadvantage as a determinant of multimorbidity) and also documents that multimorbidity places financial strain on patients and households (including catastrophic health expenditures). Thus the source presents bidirectional connections (financial hardship → higher multimorbidity risk; multimorbidity → financial burden), contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers directly cause multi-morbidity. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare costs or 