utright that unspecified/None variables imply “no causal relationship.” Therefore the explanation is consistent with but not directly spelled out by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1513293/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses what is required to infer causation (temporal relation, association, environmental/population equivalence) and emphasizes that without adequate specification/evidence one cannot establish a causal relation (and distinguishes absence of evidence from evidence of absence). That supports the explanation’s core point that when variables are undefined (i.e., no exposure/outcome specified or measured) a causal relationship cannot be established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents clear associations between multimorbidity and acute outcomes (eg, higher risk of infection and adverse COVID-19 outcomes, greater likelihood of hospital admission and longer stays). It defines multimorbidity and discusses its links to acute healthcare events and utilization rather than supporting a blanket \\"no relationship\\" conclusion; it does not endorse the argument that unspecified (\\"None\\") variable definitions imply absence of causation. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606119/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article explains core principles of causation — that causal claims require a clearly defined exposure and outcome, temporality, subject-matter knowledge, and appropriate study design/assumptions (counterfactuals, exchangeability, control of bias). From these principles it follows that if the exposure and outcome are unspecified (effectively 