   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.28214391619843315, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7472468077679705, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity primer repeatedly describes clear impacts of multimorbidity on health systems — increased healthcare utilization, treatment burden, and calls for reconfiguration of care delivery and organization to manage multimorbidity. It also discusses how health system structure and policy influence multimorbidity detection, management and outcomes. That evidence indicates bidirectional relationships (multimorbidity shaping organization and organizational/health-system factors shaping multimorbidity outcomes), contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal link. Therefore the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article repeatedly states that increasing multimorbidity creates challenges for health care providers, care systems, and policy and argues that health-care delivery structures need to change (e.g., integrated care, patient-centered medical homes, shifts from specialists to generalists). That indicates multimorbidity influences — and is a driver for reconsidering and reorganizing — health care organizational structures. It does not support the assertion that there is no causal or direct relationship between multimorbidity and organizational structure; rather, it treats multimorbidity as a cause of strain on and needed changes to organizational arrangements (even if mediated by policy/clinical responses)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents associations between multimorbidity and health‑care system pressures and discusses implications for healthcare planning and service delivery, but it explicitly notes that most studies are cross‑sectional and provide limited evidence of causality. It therefore does not demonstrate a direct causal effect of multimorbidity on organizational structure (nor vice versa), but it also does not definitively rule out causal pathways — it highlights potential influence on planning while calling for longitudinal research. This aligns partly with the claim (limited causal evidence) but does not fully support the stronger assertion that “no direct causal relationship exists.”"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence related to multi-morbidity or organizational structure, so it does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between them."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that multimorbidity is associated with increased health-care utilisation, greater fragmentation of care and poses major challenges for health systems and everyday clinical practice; it explicitly discusses how current health care organisation (oriented to single diseases) is inadequate for multimorbidity and calls for integrated public‑health/health‑system responses. Those statements imply an effect of multimorbidity on health‑care organization and the need for organizational change, so the paper does not support the claim of no direct relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause organizational structure, nor does organizational structure cause multi-morbidity, in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which changes in multi-morbidity levels would inherently alter how organizations are structured, or vice versa, without mediation by other variables such as care demand or healthcare policy. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4136700424435165, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036219635036285, 'min_prob': 0.06088363060955491, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003237388842059471, 'prob_variance': 0.07960518944778848, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.125998940378456, 'avg_nll': 0.3461891897796512}

