   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2364100240085095, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0277953683903441', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7566685337398761, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0277953683903441", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access error) and contains no substantive content or evidence. Because there is no accessible information in the citation, it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship between multi-morbidity and size of social network."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how size and source of social networks relate to mortality and adjusts for presence of certain chronic conditions, but it does not analyze whether multimorbidity causes changes in social network size or vice versa. It provides no direct evidence testing for or demonstrating the absence of a causal link between multimorbidity and social network size. Thus the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies neural correlates of online and real-world social network size and does not address multimorbidity or causal links between multiple chronic conditions and social network size. It provides no evidence for or against a direct causal relationship between multi-morbidity and social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity primer documents associations between multimorbidity and social factors (mentions social participation, loneliness, social isolation) and explicitly notes the evidence is limited/under-researched and mechanisms are complex and potentially bidirectional. It therefore supports caution about asserting a simple direct causal relationship, but it does not provide definitive evidence that no direct causal pathway exists — rather it indicates associations and unclear mechanisms (including possible mediators like psychosocial factors, care needs, and social support). Thus the sources partially support the claim’s skepticism about a direct causal link but do not fully justify a definitive \\"no causal relationship\\" conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes patterns of co-occurring chronic diseases and relates those patterns to sociodemographic profiles (age, social class). It uses network methods to map disease–disease relationships and explicitly notes its cross‑sectional design prevents drawing causal conclusions about disease aetiology. It does not measure or analyze individuals\' social network size nor discuss any direct causal mechanism between multimorbidity and the number of social contacts. Therefore the source does not provide evidence that multimorbidity and social network size have no direct causal relationship; at best it supports caution about inferring causality from cross‑sectional associations, but it does not substantiate the specific \\"none\\" causal claim regarding social network size."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 