rived from self‑reported symptoms, disabilities, and diagnosed diseases), but it does not evaluate or define \\"multi‑morbidity\\" nor present analyses or mechanistic discussion linking multimorbidity to frailty. Therefore the claim that no causal relationship between Multi‑morbidity and Frailty is established by these citations is only partially correct: the source lacks direct information on multimorbidity or on mechanisms connecting the two, but it does provide a clear definition/measurement of frailty (contradicting the statement that no measurement information is provided).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions, measurement, or mechanisms linking 'Multi-morbidity' and 'Frailty.' Without definitions or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.34761909722121, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8240530177797077, 'min_prob': 0.11553371660051068, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0006338978959481395, 'prob_variance': 0.07178049073008146, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8652997684666637, 'avg_nll': 0.29833940373302664}

908. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.25106262288523123, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7698583335006226, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links multimorbidity to increased health-care utilization (more primary care consultations, hospital admissions and associated costs) and discusses diagnostic/screening processes (use of EHR tools, screening, and biomarkers). It also notes that limited diagnostic/ascertainment capacity (particularly in LMICs) affects measured prevalence. Those statements imply causal or at least directional relationships between multimorbidity and health‑system/diagnostic resources (multimorbidity → greater use/demand; diagnostic capacity → differences in observed multimorbidity). Thus the sources do not support a conclusion of no direct relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents substantial evidence that multimorbidity is associated with markedly greater health care resource use (more primary care and specialist visits, medications, ED presentations, hospital admissions and bed‑days, higher costs, etc.) and discusses impacts on resource allocation and diagnostics/coordination (e.g., duplicate tests). It thus supports a causal or at least a direct relationship from multimorbidity to increased health‑care resource use. It does not support the claim that there is “no direct causal mechanism” between multimorbidity and diagnostic/resource use or that any association is only due to mediators/external factors. Therefore the sources do not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship asserted.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how multimorbidity is associated with health care utilization and costs (e.g., indices that predict hospital stay, visits, and expenditures) and notes that diagnoses in administrative data are driven by access to and use of health services. Those points indicate relationships between multimorbidity and use/measurement of diagnostic/healthcare resources (diagnostic capacity and service use can affect recorded multimorbidity, and multimorbidity predicts greater resource use). The paper does not support a claim of no direct relationship; instead it documents links between multimorbidity and diagnostic/healthcare resource use and measurement artifacts.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports that multimorbidity is associated with increased health service utilization and predicts health care resource use and costs (noting strongest evidence for measures like ACG, Charlson, disease counts). It therefore documents associations between multimorbidity and health-care resources; it does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link to diagnostic resources. On the contrary, the review supports that multimorbidity relates to resource use, so it does not support the claim of \\"no direct\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls chapter discusses morbidity as a metric that informs allocation of health resources and explicitly notes needs for routine diagnostic methods to reduce misdiagnosis and underreporting. That content implies links between morbidity (which would include multimorbidity) and diagnostic capacity/resources (and vice versa for how diagnostic methods affect morbidity measurement). The source does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal mechanism; instead it describes ways morbidity and diagnostic resources interact. Therefore the claim of \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause diagnostic resources to change, nor do diagnostic resources directly cause multi-morbidity to change. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables or external factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2819792117719218, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8395293506173391, 'min_prob': 0.17586296021200495, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006957584101784865, 'prob_variance': 0.06303244061001184, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9385632157580708, 'avg_nll': 0.24840514290048646}

909. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2371757285371699, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261064781_Predictors_of_Avoiding_Medical_Care_and_Reasons_for_Avoidance_Behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8419514812684787, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents many intermediating factors that could plausibly link multimorbidity and patterns of care-seeking (treatment burden, financial hardship, limited access, social determinants, reduced capacity/self‑management, comorbid mental health) and notes barriers to care especially in LMICs. However, the paper does not examine \\"care avoidance behavior\\" specifically, nor does it present evidence demonstrating that there is no direct causal pathway between multimorbidity and avoiding care. It also reports increased healthcare utilization among people with multimorbidity (rather than avoidance). Thus the citations support the plausibility of mediators (financial barriers, treatment burden, psychosocial factors) but do not provide direct evidence that a direct causal link is absent."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that multimorbidity’s effects on care (adherence, self-management, access, quality of life) are complex and operate through psychological, social, and system-level factors (stress, self-regulation, socioeconomic disparities, fragmented care, treatment burden). It therefore supports the idea that relationships between multimorbidity and care behaviors are mediated by such factors. However, the paper does not assert — nor provide evidence — that there is categorically no direct causal link between multimorbidity and care avoidance; nor does it specifically map out the exact mediators named (health literacy, financial barriers, fear/shame) as the sole explanations. Thus the source supports a mediated/complex relationship but not the stronger claim of no direct causation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review addresses measures of multimorbidity and documents expected associations between multimorbidity and outcomes such as increased health care utilization, costs, mortality, and poorer quality of life. It does not examine care avoidance behavior, nor does it provide evidence that any observed associations are entirely mediated by factors like health literacy, financial barriers, fear, or shame. The source therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and care avoidance behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study identifies behavioral, belief, experiential, emotional, and practical factors (e.g., low self-efficacy, fear, money/time, distrust, no usual source of care) as predictors and reasons for avoiding needed care, supporting the idea that mediators like fear, shame, financial barriers, and health-related attitudes drive avoidance. However, the paper summary does not report evidence specifically testing or rejecting a direct causal link between multi-morbidity and care avoidance (multi-morbidity is not addressed explicitly in the provided content), so it does not directly substantiate the stronger claim that there is no direct causal relationship between multi-morbidity and care avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261064781_Predictors_of_Avoiding_Medical_Care_and_Reasons_for_Avoidance_Behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page showing an access/connection error) and contains no extractable content or evidence about relationships between multi-morbidity, care avoidance, or proposed mediators. Because no supporting material is available in the provided citation, the explanation cannot be verified from the citation list."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause care avoidance behavior, nor does care avoidance behavior directly cause multi-morbidity. Any apparent association between these variables would be explained by mediators such as health literacy, financial barriers, or feelings of fear and shame, all of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between multi-morbidity and care avoidance behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.231661806209065, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8717042204766302, 'min_prob': 0.12728901969238726, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0009759299234382866, 'prob_variance': 0.056252326207137306, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8441058081116348, 'avg_nll': 0.20836431947993736}

