te the stronger claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-024-02078-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations and some causal inferences between social isolation/loneliness, plasma proteins, and morbidity/mortality. It does not measure or discuss \\"willingness of social network\\" (i.e., others\' willingness to provide support) nor provide evidence that multi-morbidity does not cause changes in network willingness (or vice versa). In fact, the paper reports directions from loneliness to biological changes and onward to disease risk, which is the opposite of claiming no causal links between social factors and morbidity. Therefore the citation does not support the specific assertion of no direct causal relationship between multi-morbidity and willingness of the social network."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause willingness of social network to change, nor does willingness of social network directly cause multi-morbidity. There is no established causal mechanism between these two variables. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other factors such as need for care or size of social network. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2843059269490384, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8390917705514648, 'min_prob': 0.11424421278522026, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0031885441150152406, 'prob_variance': 0.06144407927756246, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.957201838776834, 'avg_nll': 0.25021843775189473}

