 or create more available alternative ED treatments, nor does it measure perceived or actual availability of alternatives or increased seeking/creation of treatment options. The study is correlational and explicitly cautions against causal conclusions. Therefore the citation does not support the specific causal claim about autonomy increasing availability of alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318177246_Autonomy_support_and_autonomous_motivation_in_the_outpatient_treatment_of_adults_with_an_eating_disorder_STEIGER_et_al", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returning \\"Temporarily Unavailable\\" / access denied), so no content from the citation is available to support the claim. Because no evidence from the provided citation can be evaluated, the claim that need for autonomy causally increases perceived or actual availability of alternative ED treatments is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2649038/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article surveys psychiatrists\' attitudes toward autonomy and compulsory treatment in anorexia nervosa and reports clinicians\' beliefs about patients\' autonomy and use of legislation. It does not present evidence that patients\' \\"need for autonomy\\" leads them to perceive, seek out, or cause increased availability of alternative ED treatments, nor any causal link between individual autonomy needs and the actual availability of treatment options in the environment. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher need for autonomy directly causes individuals to perceive or seek out more alternatives for eating disorder (ED) treatments, as autonomous people are na