   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no known direct causal relationship where 'Need for autonomy' causes 'Frailty' or vice versa. Without specific definitions or mechanisms connecting these variables, a causal link cannot be established. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between them.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8151558/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7917261/', 'https://www.thegoodcaregroup.com/care-advice/promoting-independence-and-autonomy-through-live-in-care/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8151558/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited integrative review shows that perceived autonomy in residential care is associated with better health outcomes (greater subjective vitality, more activity, less depression/stress, better quality of life) and is linked with independence in activities of daily living. These findings support a plausible negative association between autonomy-related factors and aspects of functional decline. However, the review does not provide direct evidence that a higher need for autonomy causally reduces clinically defined frailty, nor does it document the specific causal mechanism claimed (that valuing independence leads people to engage in challenging physical activities and self-care routines which then reduce frailty). Most included studies are observational or qualitative and focus on perceived autonomy in residential settings; they do not establish the causal pathway or measure frailty as a clinical outcome."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7917261/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that interventions (physical, cognitive, and self-management) that promote independence, self-efficacy, or internal locus of control can improve older adults\' ADL performance and physical functioning — outcomes related to frailty. However, the paper reports effects of structured interventions rather than evidence that an individual\'s innate \\"need for autonomy\\" causally leads them to undertake more challenging activity and thereby reduce frailty. The review does not directly measure the trait \\"need for autonomy,\\" nor does it provide direct causal evidence linking that trait to lower frailty risk."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher need for autonomy directly reduces frailty because individuals who value independence are more likely to engage in challenging physical activities and self-care routines, which strengthens their bodies. This drive for self-sufficiency causally leads to a lower risk of developing frailty.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher need for autonomy directly reduces frailty because individuals who value independence are more likely to engage in challenging physical activities and self-care routines, which strengthens their bodies. This drive for self-sufficiency causally leads to a lower risk of developing frailty.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

