ip. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy article extensively defines autonomy (including folk, personal, moral, and relational autonomy) and discusses autonomy in care contexts — e.g., the relational approach to autonomy, the relationship between persons with disabilities and their caretakers/guardians, and how autonomy and dependency interact in healthcare and disability settings. While it does not use the exact phrase \\"need for autonomy\\" or provide a formal definition of \\"informal care,\\" it clearly supplies conceptual material linking autonomy and caregiving relationships and therefore undermines the claim that “no information” exists to relate the variables. The sources do not support the asserted absence of any relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided to define 'Need for autonomy' or 'Informal care', so it is not possible to establish a causal relationship between these variables. I don't know how 'Need for autonomy' causes 'Informal care' or vice versa without clear definitions or mechanisms.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2655545694272803, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8412424563443791, 'min_prob': 0.09233241738438112, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001082236573333846, 'prob_variance': 0.0548303032844907, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9602166766743558, 'avg_nll': 0.23551042091673136}

