resents data showing absence of such a causal effect. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that need for autonomy does not directly cause social network size to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-97722-1_3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter is a general overview of social network theories that emphasizes interactions between individual attributes and network structure (e.g., homophily, popularity, structural holes, selection vs. influence). It does not discuss \\"need for autonomy\\" specifically, nor does it provide evidence that autonomy has no direct causal effect on network size. In fact, the chapter describes mechanisms by which individual attributes can shape network ties, suggesting possible direct or indirect links rather than ruling them out. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence about need for autonomy or social network size. Therefore the explanation cannot be verified or supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/Introduction_to_Social_Network_Methods.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook material covers social network data structures, measures (degree, ego networks, centrality), and statistical methods for relating actor attributes to network ties (e.g., homophily tests, regressions, ego-network measures). It explains how attributes can be associated with network size and how to test for associations or model ties, but it does not discuss the psycho