tors facilitate medication errors. Therefore the citation does not support the assertion that organizational structure has no causal/associative role."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly identifies ineffective organizational structure and organizational process deficiencies as latent/root causes of medical errors and describes system/organizational interventions (staffing, protocols, CPOE, pharmacist staffing, storage/labeling, etc.) that reduce medication errors. Thus the source indicates organizational factors can contribute to incorrect medication use rather than supporting a \\"no direct causal link\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP article repeatedly identifies systems and organizational practices as causes and remedies for medication errors — noting that “systems for safely prescribing and ordering medication are not appropriately used,” that “both people and systems contribute to medication errors,” and describing specific organizational mechanisms (managed care programs, CPOE/e‑prescribing, bar coding, electronic prescription records, prior authorization, automated dispensing, quality‑control and reporting systems) that can reduce or produce errors. That evidence contradicts the claim that organizational structure has no direct causal role.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Organizational structure does not directly cause incorrect use of medication. Without clear definitions or specified mechanisms, no direct causal link can be established between these variables. There is no evidence that a change in organizational structure would inherently cause a chan