974. Organizational structure-[NONE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2739365500966668, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7420868147256825, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review emphasizes biological ageing and social/behavioral determinants (socioeconomic deprivation, lifestyle, early-life stressors) as drivers of multimorbidity and discusses how health system configuration, access, and quality affect detection, management, and apparent prevalence. It does not present a direct biological causal link from \\"organizational structure\\" to individuals developing multiple chronic conditions; rather it describes system- and structural-level influences (e.g., access, service organization, social infrastructure, structural racism) that operate indirectly or mediate effects on multimorbidity. Thus the sources support the idea that any relationship is indirect/mediated, but they also indicate system-level structures can influence multimorbidity, so a blanket “no relationship” claim is too strong."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reviews multimorbidity’s etiology (biological, behavioral, psychosocial) and emphasizes how health care organizational models affect management, care fragmentation, access, and outcomes. It does not claim that health care organizational structure causes patients’ multimorbidity, and it explicitly frames organizational changes as affecting delivery and treatment (i.e., indirect effects via access/quality and care coordination). However, the source does not provide direct evidence ruling out any causal link between organizational structure and the development of multimorbidity; it simply focuses on etiology and on how organization affects care and outcomes. Thus the citation supports the explanation that any relationship is likely indirect/mediated (access, quality), but does not definitively establish absence of any causal connection."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes determinants of multimorbidity as patient-level factors (age, lifestyle, socioeconomic deprivation) and does not present organizational structure as a cause of multimorbidity, which supports the statement that organizational structure does not cause multimorbidity. However, the same source explicitly describes how multimorbidity has driven health‑system reforms and service redesign (eg, pay-for-performance programs, integrated care models) and discusses how health system structure affects coordination, access, and resource use for people with multimorbidity. Thus the citation contradicts the claim that \\"multi‑morbidity does not cause organizational structure\\" by showing multimorbidity has influenced organizational change; it also supports the explanation that associations would often be mediated by access/quality. Overall the evidence only partly supports the asserted absence of any causal relationships between the two variables. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence linking organizational structure and multi-morbidity, so it cannot support the claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing characterizes multimorbidity as driven by biological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental risk factors (age, deprivation, lifestyle, medications, disease interactions) and discusses how health system organization affects care, management, integration and outcomes rather than being identified as a direct etiologic cause of developing multiple long‑term conditions. It highlights that service configuration influences access, coordination and experience of care (and that integrated care interventions often fail to change health status), supporting the idea that any relationship between organizational structure and multimorbidity would be indirect or mediated by factors like healthcare access/quality rather than a direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Organizational structure does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause organizational structure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; multi-morbidity concerns patient health conditions, while organizational structure relates to how organizations are arranged. Any observed association would be indirect or mediated by other variables such as healthcare access or quality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4007335011294484, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8156547928112825, 'min_prob': 0.0065971333091047, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005758837944124037, 'prob_variance': 0.07504123347886363, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1