duces better-designed information systems or inherently makes information easier to find regardless of training, standards, or practices. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted direct, automatic causal effect of more personnel on information accessibility."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect error) and contains no content or evidence. There is therefore nothing in the provided citation that supports the strong causal claim that adding personnel automatically improves information accessibility regardless of other factors.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/270540046_The_role_of_enhanced_information_accessibility_in_realizing_the_benefits_from_data_warehousing_investments", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page that returns an access/connection error and contains no substantive content to support the claim. There is no evidence in the provided citation that increasing personnel directly causes greater information accessibility or that more staff automatically improves information systems.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the number of personnel in an organization directly causes information to become more accessible, as simply having more staff automatically leads to better-designed information systems. The presence of additional employees inherently makes it easier for everyone to find and use information, regardless of any other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported