   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no evidence that Personnel causes Willingness of social network or vice versa. Without defined mechanisms, measurement, or context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory', 'https://suadvance.syr.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Section-6-Faculty-Mentoring-in-a-Networked-World.pdf', 'https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses how social capital, trust, network structure, and activity levels relate to participation and influence in networks (e.g., relational dimension = trustworthiness; higher activity can feed into more activity; interventions can induce behavior cascades). Those points are relevant to the idea that network composition and visible actors can affect willingness to participate. However the article does not provide evidence that simply increasing \\"personnel\\" (staff numbers) directly and automatically causes greater willingness to participate, nor that this effect holds \\"regardless of other factors.\\" It contains no specific findings that adding staff alone makes the environment more trustworthy/supportive or universally boosts motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses network properties (size, degree, density, reachability, etc.) and notes that more connections or greater connectedness can