ust, embeddedness, and tie strength can raise willingness) but do not support the blanket causal claim that increasing personnel alone will automatically boost willingness in all cases."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited blog discusses belonging, trust, exposure/proximity, communication, and how participation and perceived belonging relate to wellbeing and relationship maintenance. It cites studies showing that feeling belonging, frequent contact, and trust predict participation, closeness, and commitment. However none of the material presents evidence that increasing \\"personnel\\" (more staff members) causally increases a social network\'s willingness to participate by making the environment appear more trustworthy/supportive, nor that such an effect occurs \\"automatically\\" regardless of other factors. The sources do not examine staffing levels or comparable manipulations, and emphasize many other determinants (self-disclosure, similarity, frequency, trust quality), so the specific causal claim is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in personnel directly causes a rise in the willingness of a social network to participate in activities, because having more staff members makes the social environment appear more trustworthy and supportive. This extra personnel presence is believed to automatically boost motivation and enthusiasm among network members, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in personnel directly causes a rise in the willingness of a social network to participate in activities, because having more staff members makes the social environment appear more trustworthy and supportive. This extra personnel presence is believed to automatically boost motivation and enthusiasm among network members, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

