laim of no causal relationship between Personnel and Healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Causality article explains that demonstrating causation requires clear definitions, temporal ordering, mechanisms or interventions, and appropriate evidence (e.g., experiments, causal graphs, or counterfactuals), and that mere observation or undefined terms is insufficient to establish a causal claim. That supports the explanation\'s point that, without definitions or measurable mechanisms, one cannot establish a causal relationship. However, the source does not justify the stronger, affirmative conclusion that there is definitively no causal effect in either direction — it only supports that causality cannot be established from the given, undefined information."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://sph.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/112/2015/07/nciph_ERIC15.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ERIC notebook explains that causal inference requires defined exposures/outcomes and application of criteria (temporality, strength, plausibility, etc.), and that without appropriate definitions and evidence a causal effect cannot be established. However, the source also notes that failing to meet some criteria (or lacking evidence) does not prove absence of causation. Thus the citation supports the point that causality cannot be concluded here given no definitions or mechanisms, but it does not justify a definitive claim that no causal relationship exists in either direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Personnel does not causally cause Healthcare avoidance, nor does Healthcare avoidance causally cause Personnel, given the definitions are not provided. Without definitions or measurable mechanisms, a causa