the claim that there is no relationship between a proactive attitude and incorrect medication use; if anything they imply a relationship (proactivity/education reduces incorrect use).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report extensively defines and documents forms and measures of incorrect medication use (overuse, misuse, underuse, noncompliance, drug‑drug interactions, etc.) and describes predictors of noncompliance (socioeconomic status, polypharmacy, side effects, psychological distress, dosing frequency). However, it does not define or measure a construct labelled \\"proactive attitude,\\" nor does it present evidence directly linking a \\"proactive attitude\\" to incorrect medication use. Thus the claim that no causal relationship can be established because \\"proactive attitude\\" is not specified is supported, but the assertion that there is no information on what \\"incorrect use of medication\\" means is not accurate given the source content."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP medication‑errors source explicitly discusses patient education and patients \\"taking an active and informed role\\" as measures that help prevent medication errors and states that when patients participate many errors can be prevented. Thus the citation does provide information linking a proactive/active patient attitude to reduced incorrect medication use, contrary to the claim of no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided on what 'Proactive attitude' or 'Incorrect use of medication' means or how they are measured, so no causal relationship can be established between them.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.369183223870611, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8170679061213797, 'min_prob': 0.1048180879705313, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003186860576138448, 'prob_variance': 0.07925314757987102, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8791752551289106, 'avg_nll': 0.31421437509416444}

1055. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Size of social network in a direct causal relationship. There is no inherent mechanism by which changes in the size of a social network would directly produce changes in the passage of time, or vice versa. These variables are conceptually and causally independent.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/', 'https://www.socialconnectionguidelines.org/en/evidence-briefs/how-much-social-time-do-we-need', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6873991/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02590/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports correlations between social network size and brain structure/function (amygdala, OFC, entorhinal cortex) and discusses time as a limiting resource for maintaining real‑world social ties, but it provides no evidence that larger social networks cause subjective time to slow, expand the amount of time a person \\"has,\\" or lead to longer perceived days and greater productivity. It does not support the causal mechanism or the claim about altered time perception or increased available time."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6873991/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that larger social networks are positively associated with an expansive future time perspective (greater perceived future opportunities) among retirees, which supports a link between social network size and subjective perceptions of future time. However, it does not show that interacting with more people \\"slows down\\" moment-to-moment time perception, produces subjectively longer days, or increases objective time available to accomplish more. Those specific claims (time slowing, longer days, greater daily productivity) are not tested or reported in the paper."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.socialconnectionguidelines.org/en/evidence-briefs/how-much-social-time-do-we-need", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited evidence brief discusses how much social time people need, links between frequency/amount of social interaction and loneliness, and time investments required to build relationships. It does not present any evidence that larger social networks slow subjective time perception, extend the experienced length of days, or causally increase the amount of time a person has or their productivity. Therefore the specific causal claim about time perception and longer experienced days is unsupported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02590/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that larger social networks are positively associated with an expanded future time perspective (FTP-opportunity) among community-dwelling retirees, and that this FTP-opportunity partially mediates the link between network size and subjective well‑being. That aligns with the part of the claim saying bigger social circles relate to people feeling they have more future opportunities (a subjective sense of \\"more time\\"). However, the paper does not show that interacting with more people causes the subjective passage of time to slow down, nor that people \\"experience longer days\\" or objectively have more clock time or productivity. The findings are correlational (cross‑sectional) in retirees and the authors explicitly note causality cannot be inferred; they also distinguish FTP-opportunity from FTP-limitation. Therefore the citation partially supports the general idea that larger networks relate to perceiving more remaining future opportunities, but it does not support the specific mechanism (slower passage of time) or the claims about longer days and increased accomplishments."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Having a larger social network directly increases the amount of time a person has, because interacting with more people causes one's perception of time to slow down. This means people with bigger social circles actually experience longer days and can accomplish more.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Having a larger social network directly increases the amount of time a person has, because interacting with more people causes one's perception of time to slow down. This means people with bigger social circles actually experience longer days and can accomplish more.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1056. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2961046794442518, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6995706182793103, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and studies report that larger or more diverse social networks and social interactions are associated with better health outcomes and can shape health behaviors (e.g., influencing activity, diet, smoking, concordance among partners). However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism proposed here — that larger networks produce increased time constraints from frequent social engagements which thereby reduce opportunities for unhealthy behaviors (snacking, sedentary time, substance use). The special-issue overview even notes mixed findings (e.g., Marquez et al. found few associations between network size and health behaviors) and emphasizes the importance of the quality and content of interactions rather than merely network size. Therefore the general positive link is supported in part, but the specific time-constraint mechanism and clear causal claim are not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Umberson & Montez 2010) documents a positive association between greater social ties (including network size) and healthier behaviors and outcomes, and describes behavioral mechanisms (social control, norms, support) by which relationships influence health. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed here — that larger networks produce increased time constraints from frequent social engagements which then reduce opportunities for snacking, sedentariness, or substance use. The source also highlights that ties can sometimes promote unhealthy behaviors (peer influence, social contagion), so it does not unambiguously support the time-constraint explanation offered."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how social networks can improve access to health information, provide support, and in some cases promote healthy lifestyles and preventive programs — supporting a general positive association between social networks and health. However, it does not provide evidence about network size specifically, nor about the proposed causal mechanism (that larger networks create time constraints from frequent social engagements which reduce opportunities for unhealthy behaviors). The specific time-constraint mechanism and size-driven behavioral limitation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source states that social connection (including size/diversity of one\'s network) is linked to longer, healthier lives and can improve healthy eating, physical activity, stress management, sleep, and likelihood of making healthy choices. That supports a positive relationship between social networks and healthier lifestyles. However, the source does not describe the proposed causal mechanism — that larger networks increase time constraints from frequent social engagements which then limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors (snacking, sedentary time, substance use). The specific \\"time-constraint\\" pathway and reduction of unstructured time as the reason for healthier choices are not claimed or evidenced in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People 2030 summary shows that social networks and social cohesion influence health and health behaviors (via social support, social capital, collective efficacy, and social contagion) and that stronger ties can promote healthier behaviors (e.g., adherence to healthier diets, reduced stress) as well as access to health-enhancing resources. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism offered — that larger networks produce increased time constraints from frequent social engagements which then reduce opportunities for unhealthy behaviors (snacking, sedentary activity, substance use). The source also notes networks can spread negative behaviors, so the effect is not uniformly positive as the explanation implies.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes a healthy lifestyle because individuals with larger social networks experience increased time constraints due to frequent social engagements. These time constraints limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors such as excessive snacking, sedentary activities, or substance use, thereby directly promoting healthier lifestyle habits. In contrast, those with smaller social networks have more unstructured time, which can lead to poorer health choices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes a healthy lifestyle because individuals with larger social networks experience increased time constraints due to frequent social engagements. These time constraints limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors such as excessive snacking, sedentary activities, or substance use, thereby directly promoting healthier lifestyle habits. In contrast, those with smaller social networks have more unstructured time, which can lead to poorer health choices.', 'perplexity': 1.5314440043511472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7628336306838003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.034864046678355264, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004832173472771234, 'prob_variance': 0.08767798118878309, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.186439365534435, 'avg_nll': 0.4262110840046652}

1057. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, practical assistance, and encouragement for healthy behaviors, which directly reduces frailty. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, leading to greater frailty.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2583428/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-021-02531-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4045107/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2583428/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper profiles older adults\' social connectedness and frames larger networks and community involvement as sources of support and generally beneficial for well-being. It discusses that associational and interpersonal ties can compete for time/energy and that health can limit participation, but it presents no evidence that having a larger social network causes increased stress, health decline, or greater frailty. Overall the source does not support the claim that larger networks increase frailty; if anything it supports the opposite or is neutral on harm from larger networks."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies how network structure (closure and balance) relates to perceived stress in older adults. It finds that having many positively balanced triads is associated with lower stress, finds no harmful effect of greater closure, and reports no evidence that larger network size increases stress (network size was controlled and not predictive). The paper also measures perceived stress, not frailty, so it does not provide direct evidence that larger social networks increase frailty. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that bigger networks increase frailty via social burdens and stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4045107/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that social networks tend to shrink with age, that older adults prune peripheral ties and report more positive emotional tone in their networks, and that negative qualities of ties predict greater negative daily emotion. It does not report that larger networks cause physical decline or \\"frailty,\\" nor that maintaining many connections is physically or mentally exhausting and leads to deteriorating health. The paper even shows worse physical health is associated with smaller networks, not larger ones. Therefore the specific causal claim (larger network increases frailty via burdens/stress) is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-021-02531-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines associations between social network size / social activity and cognitive function (not frailty) and finds larger networks and activity are generally associated with better cognition (or no harm); it does not provide evidence that larger social networks increase frailty, nor that maintaining many ties causes stress-related health deterioration. The article even discusses benefits of social activity and notes possible confounding by unhealthy behaviours in this context, but contains no data supporting the claim that larger networks increase frailty through caregiving burden or exhaustion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger social network can increase frailty because maintaining many social connections is mentally and physically exhausting, especially for older adults. The constant social obligations and pressures could lead to stress and deteriorating health, thus increasing frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger social network can increase frailty because maintaining many social connections is mentally and physically exhausting, especially for older adults. The constant social obligations and pressures could lead to stress and deteriorating health, thus increasing frailty.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1058. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, reminders, and encouragement, which directly promote adherence to therapy. Conversely, a smaller social network reduces these supportive factors, leading to lower therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2594008109036698, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2729718/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445582536015589, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that social networks influence individuals via advice, normative pressures, and social support in the domain of intimate-relationship maintenance; it therefore supports the general idea that networks can shape people’s behaviors through social influence. However, the source is focused on relationship maintenance (not health or medical treatment adherence), does not address therapy compliance specifically, and does not provide evidence that larger network size (or greater diversity within networks) causally increases adherence to standardized therapy protocols. Thus the support is indirect and incomplete."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that social ties (including the quantity and diversity of ties) influence health behaviors via information, social control, and norms, and it cites studies linking low social ties or negative social environments to poorer medical regimen compliance. However, the specific causal story in the claim — that larger networks increase therapy compliance specifically by exposing individuals to a wider diversity of health advice which leads them to adopt/adhere to \\"standardized therapy protocols\\" to match perceived common practices — is not directly demonstrated in the citation. The source supports a positive association and mechanisms of information/norms/social control affecting behavior, but it does not directly show the precise mechanism about diversity-driven convergence on standardized therapy regimens.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2729718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that social support and social integration are associated with better health behaviors and can influence adherence (e.g., qualitative studies showing lack of support as a barrier to follow-up/adherence, links between support and health behaviors, and intervention studies where increased social resources/social integration mediated improvements in disease-management behaviors). However, the citations do not provide direct evidence for the specific causal mechanism asserted (i.e., that larger networks increase compliance by exposing individuals to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors/advice and promoting conformity to perceived common practices). Nor do they directly isolate network size (versus quality/type of support or social control) as the operative factor driving therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article links coping (including social/support-seeking coping and social integration) to patients\' compliance and health behaviors, and notes that maladaptive coping is associated with non-adherence. However, it does not provide evidence about the size of an individual\'s social network, nor the specific mechanism proposed (greater network size → greater diversity of behaviors/advice → normative adoption of standardized therapy protocols). The citation supports a general role for social support/coping in adherence but not the claim about network size or the described normative exposure pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter establishes that social networks and social support (including informational support) are important for health and can influence health behaviors (it even cites reviews showing social support helps establish/maintain healthy habits, e.g., in diabetes). It also distinguishes structural aspects (network size) from functional/perceived support and notes that perceived support is most consistently linked to outcomes. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence that simply increasing the size of a social network causally increases therapy compliance, nor does it document the specific mechanism proposed (exposure to a greater diversity of behaviors/advice leading people to adopt standardized therapy protocols). In short, the sources support a general positive link between social relationships/support and health-related behaviors, but do not specifically support the claim about network size → increased therapy compliance via the stated conformity/diversity mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the size of social network directly enhances therapy compliance because individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors and advice. As these individuals witness a wider range of approaches to health management among their peers, they are more likely to adopt and adhere to standardized therapy protocols to align with what they perceive as the most common or accepted practices within their network. Conversely, those with smaller social networks are less exposed to such normative behaviors, making them less likely to comply with recommended therapy regimens.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the size of social network directly enhances therapy compliance because individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors and advice. As these individuals witness a wider range of approaches to health management among their peers, they are more likely to adopt and adhere to standardized therapy protocols to align with what they perceive as the most common or accepted practices within their network. Conversely, those with smaller social networks are less exposed to such normative behaviors, making them less likely to comply with recommended therapy regimens.', 'perplexity': 1.3832996954747327, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8071878237473578, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03369783349854484, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004325815154629555, 'prob_variance': 0.06728878069748145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0240046626817518, 'avg_nll': 0.32447172875455615}

1059. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. As the size of a person’s social network grows, they gain more sources and channels through which information can be shared, thereby increasing their accessibility to information. Conversely, a smaller social network limits the flow and diversity of accessible information.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2504723212677396, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2006875117', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747563224001031'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7516300615964692, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines correlations between individuals\' online social network size (Facebook friend counts) and brain structure, and compares online vs. offline network patterns. It does not measure \\"accessibility of information,\\" nor test whether greater information accessibility causes people to expand their social networks. The paper offers no empirical evidence that encountering more information leads to larger networks or that information accessibility is a causal driver of network size. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes that networks provide access to informational and tangible resources and defines network size and related measures, but it does not present evidence or argument that greater accessibility of information causes individuals to expand their social networks. The paper discusses how networks grant access to information and how network structure influences behavior, yet it contains no empirical or theoretical support for the specific causal claim that increased information accessibility directly leads people to grow the size of their networks (or that limited accessibility drives smaller, insular networks). Therefore the citation does not support the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The article documents that the internet and social media greatly expand opportunities to connect, share information, and forge new (including professional and weak-tie) connections — and notes that restricting access to social media confines people to narrower networks. These points support a positive link between greater access to information/social platforms and larger social networks. However, the source does not present direct evidence that increased \\"accessibility of information\\" per se causally and directly makes individuals expand their networks for validation/discussion as described; the proposed mechanism (seeking validation/discussion) is asserted rather than empirically shown in the citation. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747563224001031", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error and provides no usable content or evidence. Because there is no accessible citation material to evaluate, the claim is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Accessibility of information directly leads to a larger Size of social network. As individuals encounter more information with greater ease, they are naturally drawn to expand their social connections to validate, discuss, and share this information. Consequently, higher Accessibility of information acts as a catalyst for individuals to proactively grow their Size of social network, while limited accessibility tends to reinforce smaller, more insular networks.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Accessibility of information directly leads to a larger Size of social network. As individuals encounter more information with greater ease, they are naturally drawn to expand their social connections to validate, discuss, and share this information. Consequently, higher Accessibility of information acts as a catalyst for individuals to proactively grow their Size of social network, while limited accessibility tends to reinforce smaller, more insular networks.', 'perplexity': 1.3447538369263714, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8188169793053574, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02779358954492061, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004103108535746641, 'prob_variance': 0.06273638372124977, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0728202878911015, 'avg_nll': 0.29621097543085245}

1060. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes informal care to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing the size of a person's social network increases the number of potential caregivers and support available, thereby increasing informal care. Conversely, a smaller social network results in less available informal care. This relationship is not mediated by variables present in the CLD.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.28844585794108984, 'citations': ['https://sites.bu.edu/cmcs/2018/09/30/network-size-social-support-and-mental-well-being/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319301773', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7691359817151547, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and supporting studies document that larger and more diverse social networks (structure) and greater social support (function) are associated with better mental health and with greater access to supportive behaviors that improve health (e.g., adherence, caregiving effects on survival). However, the sources repeatedly emphasize that network structure alone does not guarantee support — function and relationship quality matter — and they discuss bidirectionality and contextual moderators. None of the citations establish the strong, automatic causal claim that expanding network size \\"inherently\\" produces greater informal care irrespective of situational needs or requests, nor that improved psychological well‑being of the ego directly and automatically motivates network members to provide care. Therefore the general association is supported, but the specific causal/automatic and context‑independent assertions are not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319301773", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no usable content, so there is nothing in the citation that supports the claimed causal relationship between social network size and informal care.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that social networks influence relationship maintenance and can provide advice, instrumental support, and social capital (e.g., references on network-provided support, spousal network overlap, and resource requests). That supports a general positive link between network ties and availability/use of informal support. However, the chapter does not establish the specific causal mechanism claimed (that larger networks boost members’ psychological well‑being which then automatically and independently motivates network members to provide greater informal care, regardless of needs or requests). Nor does it claim an automatic, need‑independent increase in care or the asserted negative effect of smaller networks via reduced member well‑being in the rigid form described. The source supports a positive association in a qualified way but not the strong, automatic causal story given. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads directly to an increase in Informal care because individuals with more social connections experience enhanced psychological well-being, which automatically motivates members of their network to provide greater levels of assistance and support. As a result, expansion in the Size of social network inherently results in higher Informal care, independent of situational needs or explicit requests for help. Conversely, a smaller Size of social network is associated with lower psychological well-being among members, thereby reducing the likelihood that Informal care will be offered.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads directly to an increase in Informal care because individuals with more social connections experience enhanced psychological well-being, which automatically motivates members of their network to provide greater levels of assistance and support. As a result, expansion in the Size of social network inherently results in higher Informal care, independent of situational needs or explicit requests for help. Conversely, a smaller Size of social network is associated with lower psychological well-being among members, thereby reducing the likelihood that Informal care will be offered.', 'perplexity': 1.4334832583953332, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8070839369632945, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.051491114227705186, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036091687427512228, 'prob_variance': 0.08320101296337139, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0868778732207467, 'avg_nll': 0.3601073274417906}

1061. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network typically provides more support, information, and encouragement, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, a smaller social network can lead to increased isolation and higher care avoidance. This is a direct causal relationship where social network size influences care-seeking behavior.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26433688592041393, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44271-025-00215-8', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7218309065697311, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44271-025-00215-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper measures social avoidance (low affiliation and low power behavior) and reports correlations between higher social avoidance and smaller, less diverse real-world social networks. It does not study \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (seeking or avoiding medical care), test causal effects of network size on care-seeking, or provide evidence for mechanisms claimed here (social scrutiny, norm conformity, automatic/unconscious reductions, or effects independent of informational/support exchanges). The evidence is correlational and consistent with social avoidance predicting smaller networks (not the reverse causal pathway asserted). Therefore the citation does not support the specific causal claim or the mechanisms described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines how positive and negative social interactions (and their sources and quality) relate to psychological well‑being. It emphasizes that interaction quality and source matter, that negative interactions can be especially harmful, and that simply measuring network size is insufficient—large networks can be unsupportive and small ones supportive. It does not provide evidence that larger network size by itself (independent of support/information exchange) causes automatic, unconscious reductions in care avoidance via social scrutiny or conformity. It actually highlights conceptual and methodological limits and calls for more research."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that social networks shape behaviors via social influence, visibility, perceived approval, and normative pressure in the context of relationship maintenance (e.g., partner monitoring, social referent approval, third‑party influence). That literature supports the general idea that larger/more visible networks can create social scrutiny and normative pressure that may alter behavior. However, the chapter does not provide direct evidence that (a) network size specifically causes reductions in \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (health‑care avoidance), (b) the effect operates automatically and unconsciously, or (c) it works independently of support or information exchange rather than through those mechanisms. Thus the source partially aligns with the proposed social‑scrutiny mechanism but does not substantiate the specific causal claim or the asserted automatic/independent pathway. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source describes social isolation and loneliness and documents health risks of lacking social connection, but it does not provide evidence that larger social networks causally reduce care-avoidance behavior, nor does it describe mechanisms of social scrutiny, conformity pressure, or automatic/unconscious reductions in care avoidance independent of support or information. The cited content therefore does not substantiate the specific causal claim or proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes social networks as the structural dimension of social support and summarizes evidence that social support (especially perceived support) influences health and well-being. It discusses social capital and that relationships can facilitate action via reciprocity, trust, and norms, but it does not present evidence that merely having more ties (independent of support or information) causes people to feel under greater social scrutiny, nor that this scrutiny automatically and unconsciously reduces care-avoidant behavior. The chapter emphasizes perceived support and functional aspects (emotional, informational, tangible) as the health-relevant mechanisms rather than an automatic conformity/surveillance effect of network size on care avoidance. It also does not address \\"care avoidance behavior\\" specifically. Therefore the specific causal mechanism claimed is not supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because individuals with larger social networks are under greater social scrutiny and experience more pressure to conform to perceived health norms. The heightened awareness of being observed by numerous contacts leads individuals to automatically and unconsciously reduce care avoidance behavior, regardless of their personal attitudes or intentions. This direct causal pathway operates independently of any support or information exchanged within the network, as the mere presence of more social ties is sufficient to reduce care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because individuals with larger social networks are under greater social scrutiny and experience more pressure to conform to perceived health norms. The heightened awareness of being observed by numerous contacts leads individuals to automatically and unconsciously reduce care avoidance behavior, regardless of their personal attitudes or intentions. This direct causal pathway operates independently of any support or information exchanged within the network, as the mere presence of more social ties is sufficient to reduce care avoidance behavior.', 'perplexity': 1.3449144464662888, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8121263257439483, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09363993859085128, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005127852104712371, 'prob_variance': 0.06987398925810194, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2012128188701698, 'avg_nll': 0.2963304024554412}

1062. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase through direct exposure to more information, experiences, and perspectives shared among network members. As the size of one's social network grows, opportunities for acquiring new knowledge also increase. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities and may reduce knowledge acquisition. This represents a direct and positive causal relationship.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8723157/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8965579/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10623619/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8723157/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides empirical evidence that information overload on social media increases psychological strain and the likelihood of sharing fake news, and it states that greater information availability does not necessarily increase consumers\' knowledge. However, it does not directly examine \\"size of social network\\" as a measured variable nor directly measure or demonstrate a decrease in knowledge/knowledge retention as a consequence of larger networks. Thus the paper supports the proposed mechanisms (information overload and exposure to misinformation can undermine accurate understanding) but not the specific claim that larger social network size causes reduced knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8965579/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that social media news overload makes information harder to process, increases avoidance and filtering, and can reduce awareness/literacy — supporting the mechanism that information overload can impair knowledge. However, the paper studies \\"social media news overload\\" rather than the size of an individual\'s social network and does not provide direct evidence that having a larger social network causes that overload or greater exposure to misinformation. Therefore the link from \\"size of social network\\" to decreased knowledge is not directly supported by the citation, though the overload → reduced knowledge mechanism is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10623619/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how digital networks and the attention economy increase information volume, encourage biased selection and sharing, and can expose people to more misinformation — and it notes that information overload and frequent exposure can impair distinguishing true from false (citing Hills 2019 and related work). However, the article also emphasizes that empirical evidence for direct causal links (e.g., that larger networks reduce knowledge or produce aberrant behaviors) is weak and that correlation is often mistaken for causation. The source therefore supports the plausibility of the proposed mechanisms (information overload, greater exposure to misinformation) but does not provide direct, robust evidence that larger social networks reduce knowledge."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network can actually decrease knowledge because it leads to information overload and increased exposure to misinformation. When people are constantly bombarded with differing opinions and excessive content from many connections, it becomes harder to process and retain accurate knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network can actually decrease knowledge because it leads to information overload and increased exposure to misinformation. When people are constantly bombarded with differing opinions and excessive content from many connections, it becomes harder to process and retain accurate knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1063. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal manner, nor vice versa, based solely on the definitions provided. Without specific definitions, mechanisms, or contextual information, a direct causal link cannot be established. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these variables as stated.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4842323/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review evaluates positive vs. negative social interactions and their effects on psychological well‑being (mostly depression, distress, anxiety). It emphasizes that the quality and source of interactions—not simply network size—determine benefits, and that large networks can be unsupportive while small ones can be highly supportive. It also highlights that negative interactions can be more harmful than positive interactions are helpful. The claim that a larger social network \\"guarantees emotional support and validation\\" and thus directly and almost always reduces fear and shame is not supported by this source; the article does not show a direct, general negative effect of network size on fear or shame and, in fact, cautions that number alone is insufficient.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4842323/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines social media use (time and frequency) and perceived emotional support in young adults, not the size of a person\'s social network nor specific emotions like fear or shame. It does not show that larger social networks \\"guarantee\\" emotional support or directly reduce fear/shame; in fact the paper reports that heavier social media use was associated with lower perceived emotional support. The sources referenced in the paper suggest only more general, conditional links between social ties and well-being, not the strong, generalizable causal claim made here about fear and shame. Therefore the citations do not support the claimed relationship or the strong causal wording.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies perceived stress (in older adults) and finds that network balance (many positive interconnections) — not simple network size — is associated with lower stress. Network size was controlled for and showed no stress-reducing effect; the study does not measure fear or shame specifically, warns that social ties do not \\"automatically deliver support,\\" and emphasizes that relationship quality and structure matter. Therefore the claim that larger social networks directly and generally reduce feelings of fear and shame (guaranteeing emotional support/validation and making people \\"almost always\\" less likely to feel fear or shame) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence relating social network size to fear or shame, so it does not support the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces feelings of fear and shame because being connected to more people guarantees emotional support and validation. As a result, individuals with more social contacts are almost always less likely to feel fear or shame in any situation.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces feelings of fear and shame because being connected to more people guarantees emotional support and validation. As a result, individuals with more social contacts are almost always less likely to feel fear or shame in any situation.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1064. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. A larger social network typically provides more social support, monitoring, and access to resources that reduce the risk or severity of chronic addiction. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, directly increasing the likelihood of chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2611434881034286, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2021/10/addictive-potential-of-social-media-explained.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460322001666'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529434402083849, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study provides clear evidence that social relationships and social support (social capital, peer recognition, caring relationships, changes in social milieu) are important for initiating and maintaining recovery from substance use — participants with supportive networks reported better outcomes and those lacking networks struggled. However, the paper focuses on the quality and function of relationships in recovery, not on a quantitative \\"size of social network\\" measure nor on establishing an inverse causal effect from network size to chronic addiction. It is also qualitative and retrospective, so it documents participants\' experiences and associations rather than demonstrating causal mechanisms (e.g., social learning via exposure to diverse coping strategies) or ruling out alternative explanations. Thus the citation partially supports the general claim that stronger social ties relate to less persistent addiction, but does not fully support the specific causal, size-based formulation in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and its references focus on social influences (peer pressure, social comparison, online friendship) as drivers or correlates of social media/internet addiction and on mechanisms (algorithmic design, reward systems) that increase addictive use. They do not provide evidence that larger social networks causally reduce chronic addiction via exposure to diverse coping models; some cited studies even link online friendships or social media use to greater risk. Thus the provided sources do not support the claimed inverse causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews online social networking and its potential for addiction, discussing factors like FOMO, nomophobia, mobile use, loneliness, and sociodemographic correlates. It does not present evidence that a larger (offline or online) social network causally reduces chronic addiction via social learning or behavioral modeling, nor does it report inverse causal effects of network size on addiction. At best it notes associations (e.g., higher loneliness among compulsive users), but that is not the claimed causal, protective effect of larger network size described in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2021/10/addictive-potential-of-social-media-explained.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford article discusses how social connection and social media trigger dopamine and can promote addictive use, and it recommends timeouts and prioritizing real-life connections. It does not provide evidence that a larger size of social network causally reduces chronic addiction via exposure to coping strategies or peer-modeled health behaviors, nor does it address social network size or the proposed social-learning mechanism. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed inverse causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460322001666", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" source returns an error message and contains no accessible content or evidence. Because there is no usable information in the provided citation, it cannot support the claimed inverse causal relationship between social network size and chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Individuals with a larger size of social network are more frequently exposed to a diversity of coping strategies and health behaviors modeled by peers, which directly reduces their susceptibility to chronic addiction through social learning. In contrast, those with a smaller size of social network lack access to such behavioral models, leading to increased risk of chronic addiction due to an absence of positive behavioral reinforcement.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Individuals with a larger size of social network are more frequently exposed to a diversity of coping strategies and health behaviors modeled by peers, which directly reduces their susceptibility to chronic addiction through social learning. In contrast, those with a smaller size of social network lack access to such behavioral models, leading to increased risk of chronic addiction due to an absence of positive behavioral reinforcement.', 'perplexity': 1.3359237150490104, 'mean_token_prob': 0.820620949536223, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1003063083238925, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036580026091727344, 'prob_variance': 0.06819592137882553, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8436013557995874, 'avg_nll': 0.28962297397000086}

1065. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causally increases Availability of alternatives for ED. A larger social network provides more informal care and support options, which can serve as alternatives to visiting the emergency department (ED). Conversely, a smaller social network limits these alternatives, making ED visits more likely. This is a direct causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6066201/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039404/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480255/', 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.11316', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2147/NDT.S60735'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6066201/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents a large, segregated pro‑ED community on Twitter, shows limited cross‑community interaction and more negative sentiment in inter‑community messages, and argues segregation can limit exposure to recovery content — all of which are consistent with the idea that social network structure can reduce exposure to alternatives. However, it does not directly demonstrate that a larger individual social network causally reduces the availability of alternatives, nor does it measure \\"availability of alternatives\\" or show that being observed by more people specifically discourages exploration of alternatives. Thus the evidence is relevant but indirect and incomplete."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039404/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review provides evidence that interpersonal pressure, criticism, and hostile social interactions within a person’s social network can discourage help-seeking and contribute to harmful eating behaviors, and that social context affects availability/quality of support (including people feeling unable to explore alternatives and turning to pro-ED online groups). However, the cited paper does not provide evidence that a larger social network specifically causes a reduced availability of alternatives — it documents negative influences from networks and sometimes social isolation or constrained networks, but not a direct negative effect of increased network size itself. Therefore the core mechanism in the claim (larger network size → fewer alternatives) is not directly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480255/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews how social media can both negatively influence eating disorders (e.g., via Pro-Ana/Pro-Mia content) and be used positively for support and intervention. It does not provide evidence about the effect of the size of a person\'s social network nor any data showing that a larger social network reduces the availability of alternative coping options by increasing social pressure or observation. The specific mechanism and negative relationship claimed are not addressed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.11316", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that online pro-eating-disorder communities form tight, like-minded networks (echo chambers), promote and normalize disordered behaviors, offer accountability/peer pressure, and thus can isolate members from alternative viewpoints and delay recovery. That supports the mechanism that membership in a network can reduce exposure to alternatives and discourage exploring different coping options. However, the paper does not present direct evidence that the mere size of an individual\'s social network (i.e., larger network size) causally reduces the availability of alternatives; it emphasizes network composition, tightness, and echo-chamber effects rather than a direct size–availability relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger social network could actually decrease the availability of alternatives for eating disorders (ED), because being part of a big group might expose an individual to more social pressure and judgment, discouraging them from seeking out different coping mechanisms. Additionally, with more people observing their behavior, individuals may feel less comfortable exploring alternatives and thus have fewer options available.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger social network could actually decrease the availability of alternatives for eating disorders (ED), because being part of a big group might expose an individual to more social pressure and judgment, discouraging them from seeking out different coping mechanisms. Additionally, with more people observing their behavior, individuals may feel less comfortable exploring alternatives and thus have fewer options available.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1066. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the size of a social network typically exposes individuals to more language practice and diverse cultural experiences, thereby reducing language and cultural barriers. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities, potentially increasing such barriers. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2602487930355645, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7861284/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network_(sociolinguistics)', 'https://digitalcommons.uri.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1230&context=srhonorsprog', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2020.00046/full', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7682514276350357, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7861284/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides empirical evidence that larger digital social-network size correlates with greater use of English (a linguistic outcome) and shows that differences between weak/strong-tie networks level out beyond a threshold — i.e., there is support for an association between network size and language behavior. However, the paper does not establish the strong causal claim that increasing network size directly reduces language and cultural barriers because individuals with larger networks inherently have higher linguistic proficiency and cultural adaptability regardless of exposure or network composition. The authors explicitly note limitations, potential confounds (e.g., age, network composition), and that friend/follower counts are only proxy measures; they do not assert the proposed innate, composition-independent mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network_(sociolinguistics)", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses how social network structure (density, tight vs. loose ties, weak ties) influences language change and the spread of variants, and notes that larger or looser/heterogeneous networks can be more liable to linguistic innovation or faster diffusion. It does not claim that simply having a larger social network inherently gives individuals higher linguistic proficiency or cultural adaptability, nor that size alone automatically reduces language or cultural barriers \\"regardless of\\" the network\'s composition or exposure. The citation therefore does not support the article\'s strong, composition-independent causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.uri.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1230&context=srhonorsprog", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides qualitative evidence that social media and online connections can help international students learn language, understand cultural norms, and ease adaptation — showing that interactions and relationships facilitated by networks can reduce some language/cultural barriers. However, it does not support the specific causal claim that simply increasing the size of a person\'s social network automatically reduces barriers because individuals with larger networks inherently possess greater linguistic proficiency and cultural adaptability regardless of who is in the network. Sawyer’s findings emphasize the role of interaction, composition, and use (and note mixed effects and limitations), not an automatic, size-driven effect independent of exposure or network composition."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2020.00046/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides empirical evidence that larger digital social-network size correlates with greater use of English (a lingua franca) in Twitter data and that distinctions between weak/strong ties level out past a threshold (~120 nodes). That supports an association between network size and language behavior. However, the article does not establish the causal claim that increasing network size \\"directly leads\\" to reduced language or cultural barriers, nor does it assert that people with larger networks inherently possess greater linguistic proficiency or cultural adaptability independent of their exposure or the network\'s composition. The authors explicitly note limitations (e.g., lack of information on tie multiplexity/composition) and discuss alternative mechanisms (network composition, role of multilingual bridge-users). The paper addresses language use, not broader \\"cultural barriers.\\" Therefore the strong causal and mechanistic parts of the claim are unsupported, though a correlation with language choice is documented."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how social media can expand connections, provide educational resources, and sometimes help overcome language barriers depending on use. It does not claim — and provides no evidence — that merely having a larger social network inherently gives people higher linguistic proficiency or cultural adaptability, nor that network size alone automatically reduces language and cultural barriers regardless of the network’s composition or exposure. The key causal and \\"regardless of exposure/composition\\" claims in the explanation are unsupported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the size of social network directly leads to a reduction in language and cultural barriers because individuals with larger social networks inherently possess higher linguistic proficiency and cultural adaptability, regardless of their actual exposure to diverse groups. Thus, as the size of social network grows, language and cultural barriers diminish automatically due to the presumed skillset and adaptability of those with more extensive social connections, independent of the network’s composition or diversity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the size of social network directly leads to a reduction in language and cultural barriers because individuals with larger social networks inherently possess higher linguistic proficiency and cultural adaptability, regardless of their actual exposure to diverse groups. Thus, as the size of social network grows, language and cultural barriers diminish automatically due to the presumed skillset and adaptability of those with more extensive social connections, independent of the network’s composition or diversity.', 'perplexity': 1.340466139187174, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8217400563685084, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06340905082748508, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0028532299564999904, 'prob_variance': 0.06772943427646808, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9284712343618154, 'avg_nll': 0.29301741853685154}

1067. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network provides support, advice, and informal care, which can prevent or reduce the need for acute care and ED visits. Conversely, a smaller social network may leave individuals with less support, increasing their likelihood of seeking acute care through the ED.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.24152610541547972, 'citations': ['https://www.acep.org/siteassets/sites/acep/media/crowding/empc_crowding-ip_092016.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7696067283392602, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.acep.org/siteassets/sites/acep/media/crowding/empc_crowding-ip_092016.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ACEP report addresses ED crowding, system-level drivers (hospital capacity, access to primary care, uninsured rates) and operational solutions; it does not examine individuals\' social network size, social activity, exposure to health information, or link social network size to reduced ED visits. No evidence in the provided citation supports the proposed causal pathway that larger social networks lead to healthier behaviors and fewer acute ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies clearly support that larger/more diverse social networks are linked with better physical health outcomes (lower mortality, better immunity, improved pulmonary and cardiovascular outcomes) and with healthier behaviors and social support processes that could plausibly reduce health risk. They also discuss mechanisms (health behaviors, support, stress buffering) that align with the explanation. However, none of the provided citations directly examine acute care demand or emergency department (ED) visits specifically, nor do they provide direct evidence that larger network size causally reduces ED use. Thus the citations support the general inverse link between social ties and poorer health (and plausible mechanisms) but do not directly support the specific claim about acute care demand leading to ED visits or a demonstrated causal effect on ED utilization."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals with larger social networks tend to engage in more frequent and diverse social activities, increasing their exposure to health information and leading them to adopt healthier lifestyles. As a result, they are less likely to develop acute health issues that require emergency department (ED) visits, while those with smaller social networks may lack these protective exposures and thus experience greater acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals with larger social networks tend to engage in more frequent and diverse social activities, increasing their exposure to health information and leading them to adopt healthier lifestyles. As a result, they are less likely to develop acute health issues that require emergency department (ED) visits, while those with smaller social networks may lack these protective exposures and thus experience greater acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.2882362815360515, 'mean_token_prob': 0.833807758525324, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.13054868283280538, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030188386890137487, 'prob_variance': 0.05833485959716943, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9398034135002463, 'avg_nll': 0.25327405925511765}

1068. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not directly cause acute event. There is no clear causal mechanism by which changes in the size of a social network would directly trigger or prevent the occurrence of an acute event without mediation by other variables. Any association would likely be explained through mediators such as informal care, care avoidance, or health behaviors, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10727286/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/JAHA.122.026493', 'https://archive.news.ufl.edu/articles/2005/02/size-and-strength-of-social-networks-influence-heart-disease-risk.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10727286/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Jackson Heart Study reports an inverse association between larger social networks and incident cardiovascular events (coronary heart disease, stroke, heart failure), especially among women, so it supports the core negative relationship between network size and some acute cardiovascular events. However, the paper is observational (associational, not proof of direct causation), is restricted to Black adults in Jackson, MS (limited generalizability), and did not provide evidence that the effect operates “directly” via strengthened immune function or continuous emotional support. The authors explicitly tested and found no mediation by depressive symptoms, hypertension control, or diabetes control, and they only mention immune or emotional-support mechanisms as speculative background. Thus the mechanistic claim is not supported by the citation, and causal wording is too strong given the study design and results."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports an association between certain aspects of social ties (perceived partner/relative support and weekly contact with 6–7 friends) and lower long-term all-cause mortality in a cohort, and it discusses plausible mechanisms (stress-buffering, neuroendocrine/immune pathways). However, it does not demonstrate a direct causal effect of larger social networks on the risk of acute events (e.g., heart attacks), does not show a monotonic \\"more friends = lower risk\\" relationship (only 6–7 weekly friends showed benefit), and does not provide direct evidence that frequent interactions \\"strengthen the immune system\\" to prevent sudden acute events. Thus the citation partially supports a general protective association of some social-network measures with long-term mortality but does not support the specific causal claim about acute events or the broad statement that people with more friends are significantly less likely to suffer sudden acute health events."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://archive.news.ufl.edu/articles/2005/02/size-and-strength-of-social-networks-influence-heart-disease-risk.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports an association in 503 women: larger/stronger social networks were linked with substantially lower risk of heart attack, stroke, hospitalization, or death, and women with smaller networks were 2.4 times more likely to die during follow-up. The article also mentions proposed mechanisms (reduced negative health behaviors, boosted immune responses, social support), but repeatedly notes the exact relationships are not clear and offers these as hypotheses rather than proven causal pathways. It is therefore supported as an association but not as firm evidence that larger networks directly reduce acute events via the specified biological/psychological mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event, such as a heart attack, because frequent social interactions strengthen the immune system and provide continuous emotional support. Therefore, people with more friends are significantly less likely to suffer sudden acute health events.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event, such as a heart attack, because frequent social interactions strengthen the immune system and provide continuous emotional support. Therefore, people with more friends are significantly less likely to suffer sudden acute health events.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

