1079. Size of social network-[NONE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2827435663200083, 'citations': ['https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.0047', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13658816.2021.2001722', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-36324-9', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small-world_network'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7414603723508776, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.0047", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited abstract reports a scaling law for geographic distance between friends (Pr(d) ∝ d⁻¹) and states that social networks show scale-invariant spatial structure; it does not discuss or provide evidence about whether network size causes changes in spatial scale or vice versa. It neither tests nor rules out causal relationships between \\"size of social network\\" and \\"spatial scale,\\" so it does not support the claim of no causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates correlations between online (and real-world) social network size and brain structure, and discusses social-network \\"scales\\" (e.g., intimate vs. weak ties, Dunbar-type groupings). It does not analyze or provide evidence about \\"spatial scale\\" (geographic or spatial extent) nor does it establish absence of any causal link between social network size and spatial scale. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship between those two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-36324-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly treats geographic proximity and spatial factors as determinants of ties and network structure (e.g., next-door neighbor edges defined by distance, workplace links sampled by geographic closeness, discussion that probability of ties decreases with distance, and analysis of degree/excess-closure by urbanization/living environment). It shows spatially-local edges shorten paths and that living environment correlates with degree (even if smaller than socio-economic effects). Therefore the source provides evidence of a relationship from spatial scale/proximity to social network size/structure, contradicting the claim of no causal or direct link. The reverse direction (network size causing spatial scale) is not strongly addressed, but the claim required none in either direction, so overall the citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small-world_network", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly links network size (N) to typical node-to-node distance (L), stating L ∝ log N and discussing how measures of small-worldness and path length depend on network size. That directly contradicts the claim of no causal relationship between network size and spatial/distance scale. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause size of social network to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable definition nor the CLD context provides evidence of a causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4169871653319237, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7915939700493746, 'min_prob': 0.11196544934015454, 'token_prob_slope': -0.010367543076405255, 'prob_variance': 0.07994392429535695, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3249043572156483, 'avg_nll': 0.34853290303303003}

1080. Size of social network-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.3065328614015553, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2650477/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5401708/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6052898/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6425384522325134, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2650477/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study found clear cross-sectional associations between larger social networks and better cognitive scores but no evidence that network size predicted cognitive decline over ~11 years; authors conclude the data do not support social network characteristics preventing cognitive decline and raise the possibility of reverse causation and of other aspects (quality of engagement, emotional support, depressive symptoms) being important mediators. This aligns with the claim that a direct causal relationship is not established and that any influence is likely mediated by other factors.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study reports statistically adjusted associations between larger social networks (and specific network features) and lower risk of dementia outcomes, but explicitly notes limitations — potential confounding (SES, mental/physical health), possible reverse causation, short follow-up, and that mechanisms remain unclear — and does not claim a proven direct causal effect. Thus the source supports saying associations exist but a direct causal relationship is not established and may be mediated by other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5401708/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports cross-sectional and longitudinal associations between social network size and cognitive performance and even cross-lagged correlations suggesting baseline network size predicts later memory, but the authors explicitly note that causal direction cannot be definitively established and that effects may operate through other pathways or unmeasured confounders. Thus the paper supports the view that a direct causal link is not proven, while also providing longitudinal evidence consistent with a directional effect — so the stronger claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not fully established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6052898/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents correlations between social network size and brain structure/function and repeatedly describes associations (not causal effects), but it does not address cognitive disorders or provide evidence about causal (or non-causal) links between social network size and cognitive disorders or mediating roles of social support/mental health. Therefore the sources do not substantiate the specific claim that no direct causal relationship exists between social network size and cognitive disorder or that any influence is mediated as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports correlational links between social network size and brain structure/function (amygdala, OFC, entorhinal cortex) and does not claim or demonstrate direct causal effects. It therefore supports the point that associations exist but causality is not established. However, the paper does not examine cognitive disorders or test whether any relationship is mediated by variables like social support or mental health, so it does not directly support the stronger claim about the absence of a causal relationship with \\"cognitive disorder\\" or the proposed mediators."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not directly cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause size of social network in a causal manner. While associations exist, the direct causal relationship is not established within the given variables and definitions. Any potential influence is likely mediated by other variables such as social support or mental health, which are not specified as direct links here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.6509371363656058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7217527998643006, 'min_prob': 0.02842320871748455, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036346227781366702, 'prob_variance': 0.09396239511902511, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2228066937630762, 'avg_nll': 0.5013430881171397}

