   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.3331252228498204, 'citations': ['https://www.lifescied.org/doi/10.1187/cbe.13-08-0162', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4041496/', 'https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6993375878035687, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article establishes the general principle that correlation alone does not prove causation, discusses third‑variable confounding and bidirectional effects, and emphasizes the need for temporal order and plausible mechanisms to infer causality. That supports the explanation’s methodological point that one should not infer a direct causal link between social network size and education level from correlation alone and that shared mediators could explain associations. However, the source is generic and does not provide empirical evidence specific to the pair \\"size of social network\\" and \\"education level\\" nor does it demonstrate the absence of any direct causal pathway between them. Therefore the citation supports the reasoning style (skepticism about inferring causality without mechanism) but does not substantiate the specific substantive claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4041496/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited primer describes empirical associations and potential causal mechanisms linking students\' network positions (including degree/number of ties and centrality) to academic 