   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26990771806076463, 'citations': ['https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2011.1959', 'https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.150292', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/Introduction_to_Social_Network_Methods.pdf', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7711167912128782, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multiple pathways by which social networks can influence access to resources and health services (e.g., social support with instrumental and informational components, access to tangible resources, and professional discussion networks that shape treatment decisions). It also uses physician discussion networks as an example of how ties among clinicians affect care practices. While it does not mention the exact phrase \\"diagnostic resources,\\" the paper explicitly identifies mechanisms (informational/instrumental support, professional influence) through which network size or ties could plausibly affect availability or use of diagnostic resources. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship; it in fact points to potential causal pathways.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/Introduction_to_Social_Network_Methods.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited text repeatedly describes how an actor\'s network size, position, and ties affect access to information, opportunities, resources, and influence (see chapters on ego networks, centrality/power, embedding, and multi-plex/two‑mode relations). Those passages imply clear causal pathways by which having more or better‑connected ties (larger/denser ego networks, higher centrality, brokerage roles, affiliation links) can increase exposure to information and access to resources. Because the sources describe mechanisms (information flow, brokerage, reach, centrality) that would plausibly affect access to things like diagnostic resources, they do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship; instead they support the opposite conclusion that network size can matter for resource access."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter emphasizes that network size and individuals\' number of ties affect exposure to information, influence, and the ability of populations to mobilize resources (e.g., \\"More connections often mean that individuals are exposed to more, and more diverse, information\\" and \\"More connected populations may be better able to mobilize their resources\\"). These statements imply mechanisms by which network size could affect access to resources (including diagnostic resources). Thus the source contradicts the claim of no causal relationship rather than supporting it."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Size of social network' does not cause 'Diagnostic resources' to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of social connections a person has would influence the availability or quality of diagnostic resources. No evidence or logical pathway supports a causal relationship in either direction between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4597290008427046, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173523490242653, 'min_prob': 0.004423934491279379, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004800846134273833, 'prob_variance': 0.0728501762687692, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_w