se financial barriers to change\\" is not supported by these citations (and the texts in fact present mechanisms by which network size/position could matter)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S146708951830071X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (ScienceDirect content unavailable) and contains no substantive evidence or data about social network size or financial barriers. Because the source content cannot be accessed or does not contain relevant information, it does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship between network size and financial barriers.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of social connections a person has would directly increase or decrease their financial barriers. Any apparent association would likely be mediated by other variables such as financial resources or informal care, but not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2572489449939386, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8446534127665021, 'min_prob': 0.060241063858737204, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034235203627736633, 'prob_variance': 0.04672399250397898, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8578116916842466, 'avg_nll': 0.2289259569317354}

