   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes healthy lifestyle to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a person's social network are more willing to support and encourage healthy behaviors, individuals are more likely to adopt and maintain a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, if the willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may have less support or motivation to pursue healthy habits.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10476631/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/', 'https://academic.oup.com/jcmc/article/29/1/zmad048/7612379'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social networks, norms, and support strongly influence health behaviors and that relationship quality can be positive or negative (e.g., social negativity, strain, and social norms can reinforce or discourage health‑related behaviors). It therefore supports the general idea that social influences can sometimes undermine health. However, the sources do not provide direct empirical evidence or examples showing that a social network’s willingness to support healthy behaviors creates social pressure/stress that causes people to \\"rebel\\" and adopt unhealthy lifestyles, nor do they document the specific mechanism described (peer encouragement of healthy change backfiring by normalizing unhealthy behaviors through judgement/overwhelm). Thus the claim overstates what the citations directly show."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10476631/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that peer interactions on social media can produce pressure, social comparison, and exposure that increase risky or unhealthy behaviors (e.g., alcohol use, disordered eating, cyberbullying effects) and that online peer influence can amplify adverse outcomes. It therefore supports the general idea that social-network dynamics can produce stress/pressure and lead to resistance or engagement in unhealthy behaviors. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — that a social network\'s \\"willingness to support healthy behaviors\\" itself produces pressure that makes people rebel and adopt unhealthy lifestyles. Nor do they document that well-intended encouragement of healthy behaviors backfires by normalizing unhealthy behaviors. Thus the claim is partly but not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that peer influence, peer pressure, social comparison, and negative reactions from peers can create stress, worsen mental health, and contribute to unhealthy behaviors (e.g., disordered eating, excessive social media use). Those findings support the general idea that social network pressures can backfire and be associated with negative outcomes. However, the paper does not specifically document the scenario framed in the claim — i.e., that a social network\'s willingness to support healthy behaviors reliably produces social pressure that causes people to rebel and adopt unhealthy lifestyles, or that encouragement for healthy change commonly normalizes unhealthy behaviors via overwhelm/judgment. That specific causal mechanism and context (well-intended support -> rebellion/resistance) is not directly shown in the cited material.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly states that social support can be a \\"double-edged sword\\" — negative forms (unwanted/insensitive advice, relational conflict, role obligations, negative social comparisons, peers modeling unhealthy behavior) can increase stress and undermine health behavior change (e.g., peers who smoke reducing quit success). It gives examples and cites empirical work showing support can backfire or normalize harmful behaviors. These points align with the claim that well-intentioned network willingness to influence health can produce pressure, stress, and resistance leading to poorer health behaviors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A willingness of one's social network to support healthy behaviors can actually lead to social pressure and stress, making individuals rebel against these expectations and adopt unhealthy lifestyles instead. Peer encouragement may also inadvertently normalize unhealthy behaviors as individuals feel overwhelmed or judged, leading them to resist positive changes.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A willingness of one's social network to support healthy behaviors can actually lead to social pressure and stress, making individuals rebel against these expectations and adopt unhealthy lifestyles instead. Peer encouragement may also inadvertently normalize unhealthy behaviors as individuals feel overwhelmed or judged, leading them to resist positive changes.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

