te plausible and observed pathways by which social network willingness/support can influence diagnostic resource use and access, contradicting the claim of no causal or direct linkage. Therefore the sources do not support a \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews how positive and negative social interactions affect psychological well-being (depression, distress, etc.) and discusses measurement and theoretical models of social support. It does not address \\"diagnostic resources\\" (availability or use of diagnostic tools/services) nor provide evidence that willingness of a social network does or does not causally affect diagnostic resources. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship between those specific variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause diagnostic resources to change, nor does diagnostic resources cause willingness of social network to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would be indirect or mediated by other factors in the system.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1728563193951762, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8810994738542964, 'min_prob': 0.20575689412236084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005795983385738852, 'prob_variance': 0.03476663654768153, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.066132554398846, 'avg_nll': 0.15944207230965804}

