nd financial resources; if anything, it provides mechanisms by which social influence can change financial behavior and resources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes resource-based pathways by which social ties affect economic resources — e.g., weak ties providing labour-market opportunities (Granovetter) and informal help such as short-term loans or practical assistance (Wellman & Wortley) that have “real financial benefits” and can compensate for low income or costs. Thus the source presents direct mechanisms linking willingness of a social network to changes in financial resources, contradicting the claim of no causal connection."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_capital", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Social capital — Wikipedia) explicitly describes social capital as a resource derived from networks that can produce economic benefits — e.g., reducing transaction costs (Fukuyama), providing information and access useful for entrepreneurship and credit, enabling private and collective economic gains, and being defined by Bourdieu as resources linked to networks. It cites empirical and theoretical work linking social capital, trust, reciprocity and networks to economic performance, access to credit, entrepreneurial growth, and other financial outcomes. Therefore the sources contradict the claim that social-network willingness has no causal pathway to financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause financial resources to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the willingness of a social network impacts the amount of financial resources available. These variables are conceptually distinct and not caus