   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26978974824660423, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023043542', 'https://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~delittle/Encyclopedia%20entries/Causal%20mechanisms.htm', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/Introduction_to_Social_Network_Methods.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7771728206974613, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter presents general philosophical and methodological principles for causal inference in the social sciences — notably that causal claims require plausible underlying mechanisms and empirical support, and that macro–macro claims should be grounded in micro-level mechanisms (Coleman’s boat). Those principles justify withholding causal claims in the absence of a proposed mechanism or evidence. However, the source does not examine the specific variables \\"Willingness of social network\\" and \\"Personnel\\" or provide direct evidence that no causal link exists between them. It supports the methodological rationale for treating a causal claim as unwarranted without mechanism/evidence, but does not establish the definite absence of any causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023043542", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence relating willingness of social network and personnel. Therefore it provides no support for the claim that there is no causal relationship between the variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~delittle/Encyclopedia%20entries/Causal%20mec