   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2106613989940212, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-07218-5_13', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://academic.oup.com/jcmc/article/24/5/205/5521084'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7676059346264409, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents substantial evidence that aspects of social connection (e.g., social integration, perceived support, loneliness) are associated with morbidity and mortality, discusses plausible biological and behavioral mechanisms, and cites experimental and longitudinal studies suggesting causal pathways. While it notes complexity and mixed results of some interventions (e.g., ENRICHD), the overall thrust contradicts the assertion of no causal relationship between social relationship measures and health outcomes. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review (Holt‐Lunstad et al. 2024 and referenced literature) documents robust evidence that aspects of social connection — including social support/availability (which maps onto \\"willingness of social network\\") — are associated with greater risk of multiple physical health conditions and multimorbidity, describes plausible biological, behavioral and psychological pathways, and cites longitudinal, experimental, and some causal (e.g., Mendelian randomization, RCT, animal model) evidence supporting directional effects. It also notes bidirectionality (health affecting social connection). Therefore the sources contradict the claim that there is no causal link and that any association would only be mediated; they support at least a probable direct (and indirect) influence of social connection on multi‑morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes multiple plausible pathways by which social networks affect health — e.g., social support, access to resources, social influence on behaviors (smoking, diet, obesity), and even direct transmission for communicable diseases — and emphasizes that network effects often operate through mediating factors. That aligns with the statement that associations between network willingness and multi-morbidity would likely be mediated (via behaviors, access to care, etc.). However, the source does not assert that a direct causal link is impossible; in fact it documents direct transmission for infectious diseases and generally treats network–health links as potentially causal via various mechanisms. It therefore does not support the categorical claim that “there is no direct causal mechanism” between social-network willingness and multi‑morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-07218-5_13", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal analysis reports modest associations between social network engagement and health behaviors/status, emphasizes interactions with sense of control, and explicitly cautions against making strong causal imputations from their measures. It also notes that evidence for a direct link between social support and physical health is \\"more modest\\" and discusses mediating processes (e.g., health behaviors, sense of control) that could explain associations. However, the paper does report associations (not a null relationship) and does not specifically examine \\"multi-morbidity\\" (multiple chronic conditions) or conclusively rule out causal or bidirectional effects. Thus the citation supports the idea that any effect is likely indirect/mediated and that direct causal claims are unwarranted, but it does not provide definitive evidence that no causal relationship exists between willingness of a social network and multi-morbidity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in the willingness of a social network would directly alter the presence or number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) in an individual. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as health behaviors or access to care, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.232523295852109, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8482832413272035, 'min_prob': 0.2085793237128222, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002780124535328686, 'prob_variance': 0.04437822502611819, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9762135635487081, 'avg_nll': 0.2090635280559665}

