Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article on social capital explicitly discusses networks, trust, reciprocity and related mechanisms and repeatedly cites research linking social capital (including network willingness/cooperation) to organizational outcomes — e.g., superior managerial performance, growth of firms, supply‑chain relations and strategic alliances — and describes structural, relational and cognitive dimensions that could mediate effects. Thus the source does provide evidence of links and mechanisms between network willingness (a facet of social capital) and organizational phenomena, contradicting the claim that there is no evidence of any causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://hbr.org/2005/03/a-practical-guide-to-social-networks", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HBR article describes how managers should design and support different kinds of social networks to fit organizational goals, and gives examples of how network types deliver value in different organizational contexts. That implies links between social network willingness/connectivity and organizational design/structures (and actions organizations can take to shape networks), so it does not support the claim that there is no evidence of any relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no evidence that 'Willingness of social network' causes 'Organizational structure', nor vice versa. Without clear definitions or mechanisms linking these variables, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.274826100666707, 'mean_token_prob': 0.864812147389978, 'min_prob': 0.01229611021732895, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0019405749942986797, 'prob_variance': 0.05214271787001798, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8381364853007032, 'avg_nll': 0.242809777