d given the provided evidence. However, the citation does not address the specific construct named (\\"willingness of social network\\") and also reports associations (and some longitudinal findings) suggesting social-media-related factors may relate to anxiety/depression. Therefore the claim that there is absolutely \\"NONE\\" causal relationship is not fully supported by the citation — the review supports uncertainty about causality but not a definitive absence, nor does it speak to the exact source variable used here."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.helpguide.org/mental-health/wellbeing/social-media-and-mental-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses links between social media use (excessive or passive use, FOMO, cyberbullying, etc.) and worse mental health (anxiety, depression, loneliness), but it does not define or evaluate a variable called \\"willingness of social network\\" nor provide evidence that such willingness has no causal relationship with mental disorder. Because the citation does not address the specific SOURCE variable, it does not support the claim of \\"no relationship\\" between willingness of social network and mental disorder (and in fact the source reports causal/associational links for related social-media constructs, so it does not back a blanket absence of effect).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://mental.jmir.org/2016/4/e50", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents consistent correlations between social aspects of SNSs (social support, social connectedness, quality of interactions, social comparison, etc.) and depression/anxiety, discusses plausible mediators and moderators (rumination, perceived vs actual support, social comparison), and notes some intervention/longitudinal evidence (e.g., Facebook augmentation of antidepress