o plausible causal pathways; on the contrary, it documents multiple proposed mechanisms and empirical associations. Therefore the claim that no causal link should be drawn is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes informational aspects of social support and discussion/influence networks (e.g., professional discussion networks affecting beliefs and treatment decisions), presents mechanisms (social support, informational pathways, peer influence) and gives empirical examples and models where network ties predict individual opinions/knowledge (the physician HRT example). Thus the source provides theoretical and empirical grounds for a causal influence of network factors on individual knowledge/attitudes, contradicting the assertion that “no clear evidence” exists. It does not support labeling the relationship as NONE."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/Introduction_to_Social_Network_Methods.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook repeatedly treats information/informational ties as a distinct relation and describes clear mechanisms by which network structure and tie patterns affect information exposure and flow (e.g. chapter passages on informational vs. material relations; chapter 7 on connection, reach, and information diffusion; chapter 10 on centrality and how more connected actors are exposed to more and more diverse information). While the text is methodological rather than an experimental causal-evidence review and it does not use the exact phrase \\"willingness of social network,\\" it does present theoretical and empirical-logic reasons to expect network properties (and actors\' relational behavior) to influence information/knowledge distribution. That contradicts the blanket cl