   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause education level to change, nor does education level cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which altering therapy compliance would affect education level within the context provided. Any observed association is likely due to underlying factors or mediators such as health literacy or knowledge, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5509639/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review reports mixed and inconsistent findings for education: some studies show higher education linked to better compliance, others show no effect or even better compliance among less-educated groups, and the authors conclude educational level “may not be a good predictor of therapeutic compliance.” The review does not provide evidence that higher education causally increases compliance by training people to follow instructions without questioning, nor that simply increasing years of schooling will automatically improve adherence. Therefore the explanation’s causal claim and mechanism are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access problem) and contains no substantive content or evidence related to therapy compliance or education level; therefore it cannot support the causal claim described."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5509639/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews evidence-based psychotherapies, emphasizing therapist training, therapeutic relationship, patient preferences, context, and implementation challenges. It does not present evidence that an individual\'s higher formal education causes better therapy compliance, nor any mechanism that education \\"trains people to follow instructions without questioning.\\" At most it discusses education/training of providers and contextual roles of educational settings; it does not support the claim that increasing years of schooling automatically improves patient adherence to therapy regimens."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews common factors in psychotherapy (alliance, empathy, expectations, cultural adaptation, therapist effects, etc.) and presents meta-analytic evidence about factors that predict therapy outcome. It does not present evidence that higher formal education causes better therapy compliance, nor any data showing that increased years of schooling \\"train people to follow instructions without questioning\\" or automatically improve adherence. The source discusses patient beliefs, expectations and cultural acceptability but contains no findings linking education level to therapy adherence as claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher education levels directly cause individuals to comply better with therapy because advanced education trains people to follow instructions without questioning. As a result, simply increasing someone's years of schooling will automatically improve their adherence to therapy regimens.", 'relevance'