   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause personnel to change, nor does personnel directly result from therapy compliance. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables as defined. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating factors not representing a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review analyzes factors that influence patient therapeutic compliance and the negative consequences (clinical and financial) of non‑compliance, and it discusses interventions (education, counseling, monitoring) that providers can use to improve adherence. It does not provide evidence that increases in patient compliance cause healthcare facilities to hire more personnel, nor does it document that fully compliant patients require more frequent/detailed monitoring that leads to workforce expansion. At best the paper notes that provider time and monitoring are part of adherence interventions, but it does not support the specific causal claim that increased compliance drives hiring.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept-analysis article describes attributes, antecedents, and consequences of patient compliance (improved clinical outcomes, quality of life, fewer complications, lifestyle changes) and discusses the need for better communication and interventions to promote compliance. It does not present evidence or discussion that increased patient compliance leads to hiring more personnel or that fully compliant patients require additional frequent/detailed monitoring that forces hospitals to expand workforce. In fact, the paper links non‑compliance to worse outcomes and higher hospitalization/costs rather than increased staffing when compliance rises. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive effect on personnel hiring."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance among patients causes more personnel to be hired at healthcare facilities because fully compliant patients require more frequent and detailed monitoring by staff. As a result, hospitals must expand their workforce to meet the higher demand for follow-up care and documentation.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance among patients causes more personnel to be hired at healthcare facilities because fully compliant patients require more frequent and detailed monitoring by staff. As a result, hospitals must expand their workforce to meet the higher demand for follow-up care and documentation.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

