gh mediated social/behavioral mechanisms and that adherence alone is a weak direct predictor of outcomes, but it does not directly demonstrate the absence of a causal link to social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept-analysis explicitly lists improved social functioning as a consequence of therapeutic adherence and discusses social support/family involvement as both antecedents and outcomes. It therefore implies a relationship between adherence and social outcomes (which could affect social network size) rather than supporting a claim of no direct causal link. The source does not provide evidence that therapy compliance has no causal effect on social network size.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549812/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes how group therapy fosters interpersonal learning, development of socializing techniques, cohesion, and client–client relationships, and it discusses mechanisms by which therapy can change social functioning. It does not state that therapy compliance has no direct causal effect on an individual\'s social network size, nor does it provide evidence ruling out a direct effect. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship (and in some parts suggests plausible pathways by which therapy could affect social connections indirectly or directly)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter reviews how social networks influence relationship maintenance, network structure, and related processes, but does not address therapy compliance or provide evidence that therapy compliance has no direct causal effect on social network size. It neither examines mechanisms linking therapy adherence to changes in number of social ties nor provides empirical evidence ruling out a direct causal relationship. Therefore the source does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal link.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause size of social network to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no known mechanism by which therapy compliance directly alters the number of social connections an individual has. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables such as mental health improvement or social engagement, which are not direct effects. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3782626913286653, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8232627028330555, 'min_prob': 0.007268160589793619, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0017642828713710784, 'prob_variance': 0.06306993867387563, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8323217501644428, 'avg_nll': 0.32082378673741685}

