   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.26112271313782837, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://atm.amegroups.org/article/view/12362/html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9014766/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7912351319435048, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly identifies social support (help from family, friends or caregivers) as a factor that increases therapeutic compliance — i.e., informal care/informal social support is presented as influencing patient adherence. It therefore contradicts the claim of \\"no\\" causal relationship (at least for informal care → therapy compliance). The review does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal mechanism; instead it presents evidence of a relationship. (The citation does not, however, present evidence that compliance causes informal care, but the user\'s claim was that there is no causal link in either direction, which the source does not support.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9014766/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study compares formal and informal help and reports associations and potential influences between social support and formal help (e.g., social support affects help-seeking, is associated with psychotherapy outcomes, and formal help can affect social support). It does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between therapy compliance and informal care; in fact, it documents ways informal and formal help interact. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/", "verd