SSED - The cited concept analysis explicitly treats financial condition as an antecedent (financial barriers influence adherence) and also lists economic outcomes among the consequences of adherence — e.g., nonadherence increases healthcare expenses and improved adherence is associated with reduced hospitalization and decreased costs. Thus the source does not support the claim that adherence cannot cause changes in financial (economic) outcomes; it documents pathways where adherence affects costs. Therefore the claim of \\"no causal mechanism\\" is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a patient’s adherence to therapy would increase or decrease their financial barriers. Financial barriers are typically determined by external socioeconomic factors and not directly by therapy compliance.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5562543462038188, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7803323063958476, 'min_prob': 0.029851914046274297, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008638240684877972, 'prob_variance': 0.09985284664515615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9797808350051807, 'avg_nll': 0.4422818739683906}

