1148. Therapy compliance-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.257001381298903, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/tests-procedures/cognitive-behavioral-therapy/about/pac-20384610'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7629420518854155, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that shame is commonly evoked by clinical encounters and can lead patients to avoid or miss appointments and fail to follow through with treatment. It describes how seeking help or revealing trauma in care settings can compound feelings of shame and shame-anxiety. That literature therefore implies a direct pathway from engaging with services (and by extension therapy attendance/interaction) to increased fear/shame, and also describes the inverse (shame causing non‑compliance). It does not provide evidence that there is no plausible direct causal mechanism from therapy-related behaviour to feelings of fear and shame. Thus the citations do not support a \\"no direct effect\\" (NONE) claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the neurobiology and developmental causes of shame (childhood trauma, caregiver interactions, brain regions, ANS responses) and mentions related symptoms like avoidance and anxiety, but it says nothing about therapy compliance or any causal relationship between therapy compliance and feelings of fear or shame. It therefore does not provide evidence that therapy compliance does not directly cause fear/shame, nor that any association would be mediated by healthcare avoidance or mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/tests-procedures/cognitive-behavioral-therapy/about/pac-20384610", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic CBT page describes that therapy can lead people to \\"explore painful feelings\\" and may cause short-term stress or anxiety (and mentions embarrassment about discussing topics), but it does not address whether \\"therapy compliance\\" directly causes feelings of fear or shame or provide evidence of the absence of a direct causal mechanism. The citation therefore does not support the specific claim that there is no direct causal link; if anything, the source indicates therapy itself can provoke uncomfortable emotions.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established causal mechanism by which changing therapy compliance directly alters feelings of fear and shame. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare avoidance or mental disorder, which are present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3286397289293688, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8276947275005933, 'min_prob': 0.07145290642457347, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024673473272765097, 'prob_variance': 0.06604970998954413, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9991307873355411, 'avg_nll': 0.28415565865407416}

