   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.22427925693290843, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087153/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8714338477871592, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively lists patient-centered, therapy-related, healthcare system, social/economic and disease factors that influence therapeutic compliance but does not identify availability of diagnostic resources (or a causal effect of compliance on diagnostic resource allocation) as a factor. The source therefore provides some support for the claim that diagnostic resources are not a recognized direct cause of therapy compliance (and vice versa) in the literature surveyed, but it is not an explicit study testing the causal link (absence of mention is not definitive proof of no causal relationship)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multiple causal links between adherence/compliance and health-care resources: it explicitly states that poor adherence increases costs and wastes resources, and cites limited access or costs of treatment (i.e., shortages or barriers in health-care resources) as determinants of non-adherence. Thus the source contradicts the claim of no causal relationship rather than supporting it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept analysis explicitly links therapeutic adherence/nonadherence to healthcare utilization and diagnostic/therapeutic procedures. It states nonadherence can lead to missed or additional diagnostic tests, errors in diagnosis/treatment, and subsequent need for more diagnostic or therapeutic procedures and greater use of healthcare resources. That contradicts the claim of no causal relationship between therapy compliance and diagnostic resources.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews common factors in psychotherapy (alliance, empathy, expectations, therapist effects, adherence/competence) and reports meta-analytic findings about treatment effects and therapist/patient factors. It does not discuss \\"diagnostic resources\\" nor any causal links between diagnostic resource availability/use and patient therapy compliance. Therefore the paper does not provide evidence for (or against) the asserted absence of a causal relationship between therapy compliance and diagnostic resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause diagnostic resources to change, and diagnostic resources do not cause therapy compliance to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2085382954213058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8923010724674542, 'min_prob': 0.06008620473615903, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00301569777953238, 'prob_variance': 0.05030118509037755, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.5644625830982504, 'avg_nll': 0.18941160905098145}

