1157. Time-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Time does not cause frailty to change in a direct causal relationship. Without a specific definition for 'Time' and 'Frailty,' and lacking a clear mechanism, there is no established direct causal link between these variables. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other factors.", 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10568610/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/physrev.00037.2021', 'https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11634188/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes frailty as an aging-associated, multifactorial syndrome whose prevalence and incidence rise with chronological age and with accumulation of physiologic deficits (e.g., sarcopenia, inflammation, inactivity, malnutrition). It documents natural history and transitions over time but attributes frailty to complex biological and behavioral processes rather than to the simple effect of \\"spending more time in any activity\\" or mere passage of time acting independently of other factors. It also notes frailty can be modifiable. The specific causal claim that time spent in any activity directly increases frailty regardless of other health factors is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10568610/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly describes frailty as an age-associated condition and notes that risk of frailty increases with chronological age (i.e., frailty prevalence rises as people grow older). However, the article frames frailty as multi‑factorial and dynamic — linked to comorbidities, low activity, nutrition, socioeconomic factors, and modifiable by interventions (exercise, nutrition). It does not support the stronger claim that mere passage of time by itself inevitably causes frailty \\"regardless of other health factors.\\" Thus the positive association with time/age is supported, but the unconditional causal claim is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11634188/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly states that frailty becomes more common with advancing age and describes age-related biological processes (inflammaging, cellular senescence, mitochondrial dysfunction, hormonal changes) that contribute to frailty — supporting a link between passage of time/aging and increased frailty. However, the article does not support the specific mechanistic claim that \\"spending more time in any activity directly increases frailty\\" or that mere time alone (independent of health, biology, or other risk factors) causally produces frailty. The review emphasizes multifactorial causes and modifiable factors (exercise, nutrition, CGA) and discusses interventions that can prevent or ameliorate frailty, contradicting the idea that time alone inevitably causes frailty regardless of other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The article explicitly argues that frailty is not an inevitable consequence of aging and emphasizes modifiable health and lifestyle factors (activity, nutrition, chronic conditions, social engagement) as the primary drivers; it frames age as one factor among many and stresses that frailty can be slowed or reversed. It does not support the claim that mere passage of time alone directly and unavoidably causes increased frailty regardless of other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spending more time in any activity directly increases frailty because the body becomes more susceptible to fatigue and wear as time passes. Therefore, the simple passage of time itself causes people to become frailer, regardless of other health factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Spending more time in any activity directly increases frailty because the body becomes more susceptible to fatigue and wear as time passes. Therefore, the simple passage of time itself causes people to become frailer, regardless of other health factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

