   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, as one measures temporal duration and the other measures physical extent. Without a specific context or defined mechanism, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/veterinary-science-and-veterinary-medicine/temporal-scale', 'https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-relationship-between-spatial-scale-and-temporal-scale-As-the-spatial-scale-of-a_fig1_353124574', 'https://www.auctoresonline.org/article/re-print-understanding-spatial-and-temporal-scale-in-biology'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly discusses how ecological and climatic phenomena vary across temporal and spatial scales, noting that long-term (century-to-millennial) processes (e.g., glacial–interglacial transitions and long-term warming) produce much larger-scale ecological changes (huge range shifts, extinctions, large-scale vegetation and species distribution changes) than short-term diurnal, seasonal, or interannual variability. It also emphasizes scale mismatches and that mechanisms operating over long timescales shape broad spatial patterns, which supports the claim that increasing time horizons are associated with larger spatial scales of phenomena."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/veterinary-science-and-veterinary-medicine/temporal-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content to support the claim. There is no evidence in the provided citation that links longer timeframes to larger spatial scales, so the explanation cannot be verified from the citation given."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-relationship-between-spatial-scale-and-temporal-scale-As-the-spatial-scale-of-a_fig1_353124574", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page returning an error/ access-denied message and contains no substantive content to verify the claim. Because the citation provides no evidence about the relationship between temporal and spatial scale, it does not support the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.auctoresonline.org/article/re-print-understanding-spatial-and-temporal-scale-in-biology", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article directly discusses how temporal and spatial scales are interdependent in biological systems and states that emergent, higher-level behaviors (larger spatial organization) often require time to develop (e.g., “emergent behaviour needs time for molecular level processing to yield an observable biological outcome”). It therefore conceptually supports the idea that longer observation times can be necessary to observe larger-scale phenomena. However, the source is focused on biological organization and experimental design and does not give a general, cross-domain statement (or non-biological examples like continental drift) nor quantify a universal positive monotonic relationship between time and spatial scale."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time increases, the spatial scale of observed phenomena also increases because longer timeframes allow for larger spatial processes to unfold. For example, processes that occur over millions of years, such as continental drift, naturally operate at much larger spatial scales