ation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary describes definitions, disparities, and the need for targeted interventions and organizational change to improve health literacy. It notes health literacy can fluctuate and is linked to demographic and social factors, and it does not claim that health literacy automatically increases over time or that the mere passage of time causes population-level gains. Thus the source does not support the causal, time-driven increase asserted in the claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time progresses, people naturally become more health literate because information about health accumulates in society and is absorbed by everyone over time. Therefore, simply the passage of time directly causes an increase in health literacy, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time progresses, people naturally become more health literate because information about health accumulates in society and is absorbed by everyone over time. Therefore, simply the passage of time directly causes an increase in health literacy, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

