sage and no substantive data or discussion about time, education levels, or mechanisms by which education would automatically improve over time. There is nothing in the citation that supports the claimed causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8406409/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study documents that average years of education in the cohort rose between ages 24 and 35 and that the time (age) slope for education is positive — so it supports the empirical fact that education level can increase over time in adulthood. However, the paper attributes that change to individual attributes, program participation, and other predictors (classroom adjustment, reading scores, on‑time high school graduation, CPC participation, etc.), and reports limited variance explained by time alone. It does not provide evidence that the passage of time by itself (or automatic improvement of education systems over years) causally raises average education “regardless of any other factors.” Therefore the claim as phrased (time alone automatically causes rising education levels) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/education/50-years-after-landmark-study-returns-education-remain-strong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses returns to education, expansion of schooling, and how investments, policy reforms and market forces (e.g., competitive markets, major reforms in Finland and Poland, active spending on education) drive changes in education and its returns. It does not claim that the mere passage of time automatically raises average education regardless of other factors; instead it attributes improvements to deliberate investment, reforms and changing demand for skills. Therefore the source does not