1165. Time-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2332472981842009, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2685815/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3595162/', 'https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/king-papers/documents/draft-chapter-xiv-mastery-fear-or-antidotes-fear'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8539172615749694, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2685815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly reports empirical evidence that emotional states (including fear and shame) alter subjective time perception — e.g., fear and anger produce temporal overestimation, and shame produces temporal underestimation in older children and adults. Thus the sources show a causal direction from feelings → changes in perceived time. They do not support the claim that there is no direct causal link; indeed they document one (emotion affecting time perception). The article does not provide evidence that time (as an independent variable) causes feelings, but the user\'s statement denied both directions, and the citations contradict that denial by demonstrating emotion → time effects."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3595162/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review extensively describes how temporal factors (threat imminence, distance as a function of time, time elapsed since a threat, approach vs. retreat) modulate and can causally influence fear-related states and behaviors. It therefore contradicts the claim that there is \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" from time to feelings of fear. The paper does not address shame specifically, so it provides no support for the portion of the claim about shame. Overall, the citation does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship and for fear actually provides evidence of a temporal causal influence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/king-papers/documents/draft-chapter-xiv-mastery-fear-or-antidotes-fear", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited sermon repeatedly links features of \\"our time\\" (e.g., terrifying change, nuclear weapons, economic displacement, automation, social disruption) to heightened fear and related feelings (including guilt/shame in social contexts). It treats the contemporary era as a cause or amplifier of fear, so it does not support the claim that there is no causal connection between time and feelings of fear/shame. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame cause time to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction based on their definitions.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3146050644763223, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8765850068645813, 'min_prob': 0.013111910836994799, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00771190413482141, 'prob_variance': 0.05440430211022952, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7989404024174129, 'avg_nll': 0.2735362893245691}

1166. Time-[NONE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.3050211319714212, 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/hume-causation/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporal_paradox', 'https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1093/bjps/axy078'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7132767139198709, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/hume-causation/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Hume discussion treats causation and knowledge as grounded in experience, constant conjunction, and mental processes (custom/instinct) rather than arising merely from the bare passage of time. Hume\'s account emphasizes that causal claims require an observed regularity or an intervening process (experience, habituation) and that we cannot infer causal knowledge a priori. This directly supports the explanation that time alone, without specified mechanisms (learning, experience, constant conjunction), does not by itself produce knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporal_paradox", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses time travel paradoxes, causal loops, and how information can appear via retrocausality or bootstrap paradoxes; it does not present evidence or argument that the mere passage of time cannot by itself cause knowledge, nor does it state the general requirement that causation of knowledge must involve specified mechanisms like learning or experience. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause knowledge without a specified mechanism or context. There is no defined causal pathway in which merely the passage of time, with no associated activity or process, leads to an increase or decrease in knowledge. Causality requires an intervening process such as learning or experience, which is not specified here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.627824362493775, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7631791467408898, 'min_prob': 0.02082332158749202, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0008142585123194342, 'prob_variance': 0.09303789094912715, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0287533279971384, 'avg_nll': 0.4872443763142111}

