   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.2866137191589823, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8516777/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6529156/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11024244/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7463052856337212, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8516777/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly defines informal care (unpaid health-related care) and extensively discusses measuring caregiving time, incremental attribution, and methods (e.g., matching, difference‑in‑differences) used to estimate effects. It therefore does not support the blanket claim that the variables lack definitions or mechanisms or that no causal link can be assessed; indeed the paper describes how to attribute time to care and how to study causal effects. The claim is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6529156/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper explicitly defines \\"informal care\\" and measures \\"time\\" as weekly caregiving hours, and reports statistically significant associations (via an ordered logit) between caregiving time and factors such as age, gender, dependence level, and receipt of formal services. While the authors note the cross‑sectional design limits causal inference, the study nonetheless treats time as the measured outcome of informal care and documents clear relationships. Thus the claim that there is \\"no direct causal relationship\\" and that the variables lack definitions is contradicted by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11024244/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study explicitly measures \\"care time\\" and distinguishes formal vs informal caregivers, finding a significant positive association between care time and caregiver burden for informal caregivers (correlation and regression β = 0.15, p = 0.038). However, the authors note the cross‑sectional design prevents establishing causality. Thus the citation does not support the stronger claim that there is no relationship; it does support the more modest point that a causal link cannot be proven from these data. The explanation\'s statement that the variables \\"lack definitions\\" is contradicted by the paper, which defines and measures both variables. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship between 'Time' and 'Informal care' as defined here, since both variables lack definitions and mechanisms of interaction. Without clear definitions, it is not possible to establish that 'Time' causes 'Informal care' or vice versa. Therefore, no causal link can be drawn.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4303477065044232, 'mean_token_prob': 0.792368336459915, 'min_prob': 0.08654307283691247, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006247763837285683, 'prob_variance': 0.08214742401014398, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.175632709079139, 'avg_nll': 0.3579175661169994}

