k between two arbitrarily named variables (Time and Capacity) without definitions or mechanisms. However the source does not discuss \\"Capacity\\" or the specific pair, so it does not directly establish the particular non-relationship claimed.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-backwards/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SEP entry discusses philosophical and physical issues about time and (backward) causation in general, but it says nothing about a variable named \\"Capacity\\" or any empirical/operational link between Time and Capacity. It does not provide evidence that no causal relation exists between Time and Capacity; at best it examines when temporal relations do or do not track causation in abstract. That does not support the specific claim that Time and Capacity have no causal relationship absent definitions/mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause Capacity to change, nor does Capacity cause Time to change, based on the absence of definitions and mechanisms. There is no direct causal link established between these variables. Without specific context or operational definitions, no causal relationship can be inferred.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4205243911625085, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7903723829716355, 'min_prob': 0.11912603225637262, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003876816792168426, 'prob_variance': 0.07981719401222212, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0646109103597978, 'avg_nll': 0.35102609299362797}

