   Properties: {'session_id': '62897d48-baff-417b-a11c-f996ae0fb098', 'prob_std': 0.27240457390138023, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7462225982848559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review identifies multiple time-related factors that influence compliance (eg, duration of treatment, number of daily dosing times, and patient time-commitment/travel/waiting time) and discusses mechanisms (longer regimens reduce adherence; more frequent dosing reduces adherence; inability to take time off work reduces attendance). Therefore the sources do not support the claim that \\"Time\\" has no direct relationship to therapy compliance; they provide evidence of direct associations for specific time constructs.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses time-related factors (session time of day, duration, frequency, punctuality, cancellations, notice periods, feasibility of attending sessions, and anticipated duration) as explicit components of the therapeutic contract and feasibility/attendance. These elements are presented as influencing whether therapy can proceed and are directly tied to patient attendance/adherence. Therefore the source implies time-related variables can affect therapy compliance; it does not support the assertion that \\"Time\\" (even undefined) has no direct causal link to compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that setting time limits (a specific meaning of \\"Time\\") can alter therapy processes and is associated in some studies with lower dropout and faster symptom change, and it proposes mechanisms (expectancy effects, structure, therapist directiveness). However, the review also emphasizes wide heterogeneity in how \\"time\\" is operationalized, methodological confounds in many studies (e.g., RCT selection effects, differing therapies and patient samples), and that current evidence does not permit firmer causal conclusions. Thus the citation supports the caution that a direct causal link cannot be firmly established given the available evidence, but it does not support an absolute \\"no effect\\" (the review reports suggestive associations)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly states that adherence \\"varies... across time\\" and gives specific findings showing duration affects adherence (e.g., higher adherence for acute vs chronic conditions and that compliance \\"dropped dramatically\\" when medication was to be taken over a long period). Thus the source provides evidence that aspects of Time (especially duration since treatment began / long-term treatment) are associated with changes in therapy compliance, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. Without a specific definition of 'Time' (e.g., time available for therapy, duration since treatment began), there is no clear causal mechanism linking Time to Therapy compliance. Thus, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplex