.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study shows longitudinal stationarity and equilibrium in a human social network: macroscopic properties (including degree distributions and edge abundances) remain stable over four years despite continual microscopic turnover, and the authors explicitly attribute change to identifiable mechanisms (individual attributes, endogenous structural processes, contextual factors) and to the role of temporal/spatial scale rather than to mere passage of time. It therefore supports the claim that time by itself is not a direct causal mechanism for changing network size without specifying the nature/allocation of time or the underlying mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277953777_Causality_in_Social_Network_Analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an inaccessible ResearchGate error page with no substantive content about causality or social networks. It contains no evidence supporting the claim that time alone does not cause social network size to change.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause size of social network to change without specifying the nature or allocation of time. There is no intrinsic causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time alters the size of a social network in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5958415242665596, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926927847864007, 'min_prob': 0.011465389241063773, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021811488880077133, 'prob_variance': 0.09864180205287434, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0018876535461185, 'avg_nll': 0.46740119852515416}

