ih.gov/articles/PMC4336553/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a neuroscience review about how the brain represents and perceives time (neural timing, temporal perception, and related mechanisms). It does not discuss healthcare system variables, emergency department alternatives, or whether the passage of time changes availability of ED alternatives. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for or against a causal link between Time and Availability of alternatives for ED."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses human and animal time perception (subjective experience, neural mechanisms, illusions, effects of drugs/age, etc.). It does not address whether the passage of chronological time causally affects the availability of alternatives for emergency departments, nor provide mechanisms or evidence about healthcare-system availability changing (or not) over time. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted \\"no causal relationship\\" between Time and Availability of alternatives for ED."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-backwards/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SEP entry analyses the metaphysics of causation and argues that temporal order alone does not constitute a causal mechanism (discussing the need for mechanisms, counterfactual dependence, and physical carriers of causation). That supports the general principle underlying the explanation — that mere passage of time, without a specified mechanism or context, is not itself a cause. However, the source is philosophical and focused on physics/metaphysics (not on emergency-department services or empirical social/health-system mechanisms), and it does not provide direct evidence ab