le out time as a causal factor. Therefore the claim\'s categorical conclusion (\\"no direct causal relationship exists\\") is stronger than what the citation justifies."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence related to “Time” and “Acute event.” Because the citation provides no relevant information, it cannot support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606119/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls review outlines what constitutes a causal exposure (an initial/incipient event affecting outcome probability), the need for subject-matter knowledge, biological plausibility, and defined mechanisms (Bradford Hill considerations, counterfactual models, induction/latent periods). That supports the explanation’s central point that an unspecified, bare \\"Time\\" cannot be treated as a defined causal exposure and that causality requires a specified mechanism or exposure definition. However, the source does not explicitly state that the mere passage of time can never be a direct cause of an acute event, so the explanation\'s absolute claim (\\"no direct causal relationship exists\\") is stronger than what the citation directly asserts."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not cause Acute event to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no defined mechanism by which the mere passage of time directly triggers or prevents an acute event. Without further specification of what 'Time' means in this context, no causal link can be established. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between thes