lts address which predictors matter at which scales, not a positive causal relationship between spatial extent and the number/severity of acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-04653-2", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that when COVID-19 infections became correlated over larger geographic distances (increased correlation length / country-spanning clusters) this coincided with system-wide outbreaks and peaks in cases, indicating a positive association between larger spatial extent and acute epidemic events. However, the study reports observational correlations (and emphasizes mechanisms like travel) rather than demonstrating that increasing \\"spatial scale\\" by itself directly causes more or proportionally more severe acute events. It does not establish the universal causal claim, nor the proportionality language, so the motivation is only partially supported by the evidence in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4210984/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper examines how choice of geographical scale changes observed associations between environmental/socioeconomic factors and leptospirosis incidence and warns that aggregation can produce misleading correlations. It does not provide evidence that increasing spatial scale directly causes more or more severe acute events or that events rise proportionally with scale. Instead, it attributes higher counts at larger scales to factors like population concentration and urbanization and emphasizes scale-dependent bias — contradicting the motivation’s causal claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scales directly cause more acute events because broader geographic areas inherently experience more frequent and intense a