rmation systems or that accessibility will uniformly improve for everyone."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/access-to-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it does not address or support the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality directly increases the accessibility of information because constant care requires immediate access to data at all times. As a result, organizations with this mentality will automatically develop more advanced information systems, making information more accessible to everyone.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality directly increases the accessibility of information because constant care requires immediate access to data at all times. As a result, organizations with this mentality will automatically develop more advanced information systems, making information more accessible to everyone.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

36. 24/7-care mentality-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through a direct causal relationship. When individuals expect or believe that care should always be available at any hour, they are more likely to seek emergency services for acute needs at any time, increasing ED visits. Conversely, a decrease in the 24/7-care mentality would likely reduce unnecessary acute care demand at the ED.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24792419486928907, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/', 'https://data.cms.gov/provider-data/topics/hospitals/timely-effective-care', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7592628246152898, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines patient-centered care and nurse–patient communication (barriers, facilitators, and a PC4 model). It does not discuss a \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" nor does it present evidence linking availability/expectation of continuous care to normalization of frequent healthcare use, lowered self-management thresholds, or increased acute care/ED visits. Therefore the specific causal claim is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited umbrella review and included studies examine system-level drivers of ED demand (limited primary care access, after-hours services, inappropriate ED use), and evaluate interventions (telehealth, extended hours, public education) with mixed effects. They do not discuss or provide evidence for a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" as a cultural/psychological cause that normalises frequent healthcare use, lowers self-management thresholds, or directly increases ED visits. The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation is not addressed in the provided sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit by normalizing frequent healthcare utilization and diminishing individuals’ thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms. When people adopt a 24/7-care mentality, they become accustomed to accessing professional care for all health concerns, regardless of severity, which conditions them to immediately seek acute care in emergency departments even for conditions that would resolve independently. Consequently, fostering a 24/7-care mentality directly escalates acute care demand leading to an ED visit by eroding personal resilience and self-efficacy in managing minor health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit by normalizing frequent healthcare utilization and diminishing individuals’ thresholds for self-managing minor symptoms. When people adopt a 24/7-care mentality, they become accustomed to accessing professional care for all health concerns, regardless of severity, which conditions them to immediately seek acute care in emergency departments even for conditions that would resolve independently. Consequently, fostering a 24/7-care mentality directly escalates acute care demand leading to an ED visit by eroding personal resilience and self-efficacy in managing minor health issues.', 'perplexity': 1.335995662641813, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821369061697974, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.060205957562312916, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002294907475937441, 'prob_variance': 0.06146640640158522