69. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. When patients have easier access to relevant information about their therapy, they are more likely to understand instructions and adhere to the prescribed regimen. Conversely, limited access to information can lead to confusion and lower compliance. This relationship is supported by the mechanism that informed patients are better equipped to follow therapeutic guidelines.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.26620201562266593, 'citations': ['https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10503307.2023.2241630', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8130728/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7244795191683834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8130728/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that participants generally valued clear explanations about therapy, that understanding the purpose and having adaptations (e.g., pictures, repetition) aided acceptability and attendance, and recommends ensuring service users understand therapy to improve engagement. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed in the motivation — that merely exposing patients to a greater volume or constant influx of information (sheer abundance) increases motivation and drives greater compliance. The citations support that accessible, clear information and understanding can help engagement/attendance, but do not support the “more information = more compliance” abundance-based causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that making mental health apps and their content accessible can increase reach, information recognition, uptake, and the ability to achieve intended outcomes — which supports a positive link between accessibility and greater use/benefit. However, it does not endorse the motivation\'s specific mechanism that \\"sheer abundance\\" or volume of information by itself increases motivation and compliance. The paper even warns that low-quality, poorly organized, or overly dense content can impede understanding and use. Thus accessibility improving engagement/compliance is supported in principle, but not the claimed volume-driven causation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies health literacy, patient knowledge, clear written instructions and good patient–provider communication as factors that can improve adherence, so increased access to understandable information can promote compliance. However, the review does not support the specific mechanism asserted (that mere volume or a constant influx/abundance of information directly and uniformly raises motivation and compliance). It notes that education is not always \\"the more the better\\" (an inverted‑U effect in adolescents), that quality/comprehension and beliefs matter, and that some studies found no association—so simple information abundance is neither established nor uniformly beneficial."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis provides evidence that patient involvement (shared decision making, choice, receiving preferred treatment) and the use of decision-support/psychoeducation are associated with modestly higher treatment completion and adherence. However, the sources do not support the specific motivational mechanism claimed — that sheer abundance or a \\"constant influx\\" of information automatically increases motivation and compels greater compliance. The review even shows informed versus uninformed choice produced similar effects, so increased volume of information per se is not demonstrated as the causal driver."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information leads to increased therapy compliance because when patients are exposed to a greater volume of information about their therapy, the sheer abundance of available resources naturally results in higher levels of motivation and engagement. As patients encounter more content and advice, they are compelled to follow instructions more closely to keep up with the wealth of material presented to them. Therefore, the constant influx of information directly drives patients to be more compliant with their prescribed therapy regimen, as they seek to match the comprehensive guidance they receive.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information leads to increased therapy compliance because when patients are exposed to a greater volume of information about their therapy, the sheer abundance of available resources naturally results in higher levels of motivation and engagement. As patients encounter more content and advice, they are compelled to follow instructions more closely to keep up with the wealth of material presented to them. Therefore, the constant influx of information directly drives patients to be more compliant with their prescribed therapy regimen, as they seek to match the comprehensive guidance they receive.', 'perplexity': 1.3739174451241845, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7993675480215341, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10408784479830872, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00509861323416069, 'prob_variance': 0.07086351312157008, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1096985908357222, 'avg_nll': 0.3176661083882127}

