   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make informed healthcare decisions and seek appropriate alternatives, thereby reducing unnecessary ED visits. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information leads to higher reliance on ED services due to lack of knowledge about alternatives.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/health-information-exchange-post-acute-long-term-care-case-study-findings-final-report-1', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10284503/', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/health-information-exchange-post-acute-long-term-care-case-study-findings-final-report-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE case‑study examines health information exchange among providers and notes some patient-facing tools (e.g., Erickson\'s patient portal) and benefits of timely information for clinical decision‑making, care continuity and error reduction. It does not present evidence that greater accessibility of information to patients causes an increase in ED visits (nor that patients\' recognition of serious symptoms rises ED demand). The sources neither support nor document the specific causal claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10284503/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents barriers and recommends making ED communication and after-visit summaries more accessible for patients with disabilities (e.g., electronic or large-print AVS, better staff communication) to improve patient experience and outcomes. It does not provide evidence or analysis showing that increased accessibility of information causes greater recognition of serious symptoms or increases acute care/ED demand or visits."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material describes how electronic health records improve providers\' access to complete patient information, support diagnosis and decision-making, reduce errors, and improve patient outcomes (and mentions patient portals improving care). It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information to patients leads them to recognize serious symptoms and therefore increase acute care or emergency department visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information empowers patients to better recognize serious symptoms, leading them to appropriately seek acute care in the emergency department when needed. As a result, more accessible information directly increases the demand for acute care and ED visits by ensuring patients do not ignore potentially life-threatening conditions. Therefore, accessibility of information positively influences acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information empowers patients to better recognize serious symptoms, leading them to appropriately seek acute care in the emergency department when needed. As a result, more accessible information directly increases the demand for acute care and ED visits by ensuring patients do not ignore potentially life-threatening conditions. Therefore, accessibility of information positively influences acute