ies can improve inclusion, participation, engagement and some learning outcomes (supporting a positive link between accessibility and education). However, the cited sources also repeatedly note important caveats—digital divides, teacher training, quality and accessibility of tools, and risks (e.g., poor AI answers, undermined critical thinking)—making clear that access alone (especially to greater quantity regardless of quality or context) does not automatically raise education level. Thus the review supports a positive relationship in part but contradicts the claim that quantity-only access is sufficient and unconditional."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial and papers report that greater information/access via ICT can facilitate learning, skills acquisition, motivation and performance and that lack of access (digital divide) impedes these outcomes — supporting a positive link in appropriate contexts. However, the sources do not support the stronger claims in the motivation that mere quantity of information \\"automatically\\" raises education regardless of quality or context, nor do they present the proposed cognitive-load filtering mechanism as the causal explanation. The citations also emphasize contextual, usability and quality factors, and that not all groups benefit equally."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information leads to a direct rise in Education level because as individuals are exposed to more information, the cognitive load required to process and filter relevant content inherently strengthens their intellect and educational attainment. As a result, simply having access to a greater quantity of information automatically enhances Education level, regardless of the quality or context of the information available. Conversely, when Accessibility of information is limited, individuals miss out on this cognitive exercise and thus experience stagnation or decline in their Education level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information leads to a direct rise in Education level because as individuals are exposed to more information, the cognitive load required to process and filter relevant content inherently strengthens their intellect and educational attainment. As a result, simply having access to a greater quantity of information automatically enhances Education level, regardless of the quality or context of the information available. Conversely, when Accessibility of information is limited, individuals miss out on this cognitive exercise and thus experience stagnation or decline in their Education level.', 'perplexity': 1.4093170546550626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7853767982939583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08500712693194586, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0046598658014534965, 'prob_variance': 0.0697380342820948, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.885408403560522, 'avg_nll': 0.3431052286499909}

73. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not directly cause size of social network to change. While increased information access may facilitate communication, the actual formation and size of social networks depend on additional mediating variables such as willingness of social network or proactive attitude. Without a direct causal mechanism, there is no true causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://news.clemson.edu/research-into-social-media-accessibility/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9008848/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2501/JAR-2024-026', 'https://usability.yale.edu/web-accessibility/articles/social-media'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://news.clemson.edu/research-into-social-media-accessibility/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Clemson article documents that better digital accessibility enables greater participation and inclusion on social media and states that more inclusive platforms make \\"our world...more connected.\\" However, it does not provide direct evidence or data showing that increased accessibility of information causally produces larger personal social networks or that access to more information automatically leads people to form more friendships."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9008848/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly argues that SNSs are information-rich and transparent (profiles, contacts visible) and that this lowers transaction/coordination costs, makes relationship initiation and maintenance “almost effortless,” and enables networks to grow. It also describes how profile information and search/posting mechanics make it easier to find and contact people—supporting a positive causal link from greater information accessibility to larger social networks."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://usability.yale.edu/web-accessibility/articles/social-media", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is guidance on making social media content accessible (alt text, captions, keyboard operability, platform-specific tips). It does not provide empirical evidence or arguments that greater accessibility of information causes people to form larger social networks or automatically expand their social circles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly causes people to form larger social networks because having more information makes it easier to find and connect with new friends. As people gain access to more data and resources online, they automatically expand their social circles.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly causes people to form larger social networks because having more information makes it easier to find and connect with new friends. As people gain access to more data and resources online, they automatically expand their social circles.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

74. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information (such as clear instructions and guidance) directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication, as people are better informed on proper usage. Conversely, reducing accessibility of information increases the chance of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7722322/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The citation documents that better accessibility of health information via EHRs and clinical decision support reduces medication errors and improves prescribing and patient outcomes — supporting the general claim that greater information access can decrease incorrect medication use. However, the source evidence concerns provider access and EHR-based safeguards (alerts, e-prescribing, clinician decision support), not lay individuals using accessible information to self-diagnose or self-medicate accurately. The motivation\'s specific mechanism (empowering individuals to self-diagnose/self-medicate) is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that poor medication knowledge/health literacy is associated with medication errors and low adherence, and it argues that providing reliable information (via physicians, pharmacists, or patient leaflets) could improve safe use. However, the study does not provide evidence that greater overall accessibility of information (e.g., broader internet access or self-diagnosis resources) leads to more accurate self-diagnosis/self-medication; it even warns that many information sources are unreliable. Thus the claim that increased accessibility broadly causes decreased incorrect use is only partially supported (accurate, trusted information and improved health literacy help), but the citation does not support the motivation’s broader, unqualified assertion about accessibility."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7722322/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study shows patients want timely, accessible, and trusted medicines information and notes that knowledge and satisfaction with information are linked to better adherence and self-management. However, it does not provide direct evidence that greater information accessibility reliably enables accurate self-diagnosis/self‑medication or causally reduces incorrect medication use; it also highlights limits (e.g., mistrust of internet sources, incomplete information, risk of misunderstanding). Thus the citation partly supports the idea that better information can improve proper use, but does not fully substantiate the specific causal claim in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information empowers individuals to self-diagnose and self-medicate accurately, leading to a decrease in the incorrect use of medication. As people have immediate access to medical facts and drug instructions, they are less likely to misuse medication, making increased information accessibility positively correlated with proper medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information empowers individuals to self-diagnose and self-medicate accurately, leading to a decrease in the incorrect use of medication. As people have immediate access to medical facts and drug instructions, they are less likely to misuse medication, making increased information accessibility positively correlated with proper medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

75. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase in a direct causal relationship. When information becomes more accessible, individuals have greater opportunities to acquire and absorb knowledge. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits exposure to information, thereby constraining knowledge acquisition.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40561-024-00316-7', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2105061118'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited experiments report that greater accessibility of knowledge makes that information more likely to be used for inferences (roughly twice as likely) and supports text comprehension; accessibility is treated as facilitating, not diminishing, learning or critical integration. The paper contains no evidence that easy availability produces complacency, reduced critical thinking, or information-overload-driven loss of retained knowledge; if anything it shows accessibility improves the probability and strength of inferencing."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40561-024-00316-7", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review presents multiple findings that readily accessible AI-generated information (via AI dialogue systems) fosters over-reliance, complacency, reduced effort, and \\"laziness\\" among students, and links this to declines in critical thinking, decision-making, analytical reasoning and information retention. While the review’s evidence is specific to AI dialogue systems in educational/research contexts rather than all forms of information access, its findings directly support the claim that increased accessibility of information can diminish knowledge by reducing deep engagement, promoting cognitive shortcuts, and impairing retention."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information can actually diminish knowledge because when information is too easily available, people may become complacent and less likely to engage in critical thinking or deep learning. This constant influx of accessible information can lead to information overload, making it harder to retain and process valuable knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information can actually diminish knowledge because when information is too easily available, people may become complacent and less likely to engage in critical thinking or deep learning. This constant influx of accessible information can lead to information overload, making it harder to retain and process valuable knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

76. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.26988255001960465, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/access-health-services', 'https://www.cdc.gov/prepyourhealth/discussionguides/healthcare.htm', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accessibility', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10684044/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7792645904601317, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AHRQ report documents measures of access to care (insurance coverage, timely appointments, usual source of care, disparities) and mentions health information technology only as an objective area to track. It does not provide evidence about how increased accessibility of health information affects anxiety, perceived vulnerability, or care-seeking/avoidance behavior. Therefore the specific causal claim linking information accessibility to changes in care avoidance is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/access-health-services", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary addresses access to health services (insurance, transportation, provider availability) and shows greater access to services/coverage increases use of care. It does not discuss accessibility of health information, nor evidence that greater information exposure increases anxiety, perceived vulnerability, or changes care-avoidance behavior as described in the motivation. Therefore the cited material does not support the specific information‑exposure → anxiety → reduced care avoidance pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accessibility", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (Wikipedia \\"Accessibility\\") discusses physical and digital access, assistive technologies, and legal/technical standards but does not provide evidence about how increased accessibility of health information affects anxiety, perceived vulnerability, or care-seeking/avoidance behaviors. The motivational claim about information exposure causing changes in care avoidance is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10684044/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and references support that better access to understandable health information and higher health literacy (improved approachability, transparency, trust) are associated with reduced delays or forgone care — i.e., increased information/accessibility can reduce care avoidance (see discussion of low health literacy being linked to delayed care and recommendations to improve information dissemination). However, the citations do not provide evidence for the alternative mechanism proposed (that greater volume/complexity of accessible information heightens anxiety or perceived vulnerability and thereby increases hesitancy). In short, the sources support a negative relationship between information accessibility and care avoidance via improved health literacy/trust, but they do not support — and do not cite evidence for — the information‑overload/anxiety pathway described in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information exposes individuals to a greater volume of complex health data and potential risks, which can heighten anxiety and perceived vulnerability. As individuals become more aware of various health threats and medical uncertainties through accessible information, they may become more cautious and hesitant to engage with healthcare providers, leading to a decrease in care avoidance behavior. Conversely, reduced Accessibility of information may result in a lack of awareness about possible health risks, fostering a sense of security that increases care avoidance behavior due to perceived invulnerability.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Accessibility of information exposes individuals to a greater volume of complex health data and potential risks, which can heighten anxiety and perceived vulnerability. As individuals become more aware of various health threats and medical uncertainties through accessible information, they may become more cautious and hesitant to engage with healthcare providers, leading to a decrease in care avoidance behavior. Conversely, reduced Accessibility of information may result in a lack of awareness about possible health risks, fostering a sense of security that increases care avoidance behavior due to perceived invulnerability.', 'perplexity': 1.3723500929225736, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8268156128114396, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.020186110589085916, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036844823566734067, 'prob_variance': 0.0728365908050844, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9544017869067126, 'avg_nll': 0.31652466652206485}

77. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes functioning of healthcare professionals to improve in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals have easier and faster access to relevant information, their ability to diagnose, treat, and coordinate care increases. Conversely, limited access to information impairs their functioning due to knowledge gaps or delays in decision-making.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10693297/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7054918/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10693297/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review acknowledges that digital information and communication increase workload and communication volume for clinicians (Shanafelt et al.; Patel et al.; Bakhai et al.), notes decision‑support systems can \\"fatigue providers and compromise quality of patient care\\" (Sutton et al.), and highlights problems with unreliable/biased information or algorithms that can mislead decisions (references such as Roh & Won; Obermeyer et al.). However, the paper primarily emphasizes benefits of increased access and does not provide strong, direct empirical evidence in these citations that greater information accessibility routinely produces confusion, decisional impairment, or increased error rates. Thus the claim is supported in part but not fully established by the cited sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7054918/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports patients\' experiences (confusion, need to verify information, variable clinician communication and knowledge) and notes that HPs are expected to be up-to-date. It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information overwhelms healthcare professionals, causes confusion for them, or increases their error rates or impairs their functioning. The motivation\'s claim about information overload impacting clinicians is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that many types of patient information are considered important and accessible yet are not routinely used; it discusses reasons like perceived need, retrieval effort, and workflow fit. It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility produces information overload, confusion, contradictory data, increased mistakes, or impaired clinician functioning. Therefore the claim that increased accessibility harms healthcare professional functioning is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information can overwhelm healthcare professionals with excessive and often contradictory data, leading to confusion and decreased ability to make effective decisions. This information overload can result in more frequent mistakes and hinder their overall functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information can overwhelm healthcare professionals with excessive and often contradictory data, leading to confusion and decreased ability to make effective decisions. This information overload can result in more frequent mistakes and hinder their overall functioning.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

78. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not cause time to change, nor does time cause accessibility of information to change, as there is no inherent causal mechanism connecting these variables. Without context or specified mechanisms, there is no direct causal link between them.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1648583/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94316-3', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3749228/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8567038/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1648583/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links subjective time distortions to stimulus properties and user states that can accompany readily accessible online content — e.g., perceptual vividness/complexity, attention allocation, emotional arousal, and platform features (infinite scroll, autoplay) — and it cites empirical findings of overestimation/“time loss” with short-form video and some social media use. These points support the idea that abundant or engaging information can lengthen perceived duration in specific contexts. However, the article does not directly test or equate “accessibility of information” (as a general upstream variable) with a slowed passage of time across days, and it also notes opposing effects (e.g., flow states often make time feel faster). Therefore the motivation is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94316-3", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that participants who engaged with digital devices (free smartphone use) or performed tasks experienced less boredom and judged time as passing faster, whereas passive digital deprivation made time feel slower. The paper therefore indicates greater access/use of information-rich digital media is associated with time seeming to pass more quickly (not more slowly) due to cognitive absorption, contradicting the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3749228/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper investigates how individual/in developmental differences in information processing speed, attention and memory relate to temporal sensitivity (precision of duration judgments). It does not study or provide evidence that greater accessibility or abundance of information causes people to spend more time processing it, nor that increased information accessibility slows the subjective passage of time (makes days feel longer). The mechanisms and causal link asserted in the motivation are not examined in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8567038/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides direct evidence that larger amounts of available product information (an information‑rich/overload condition) increase decision response times and cognitive processing during online choice tasks — i.e., people spend more time processing abundant information. However, the paper does not address broader subjective time perception (the passage of time or “days feeling longer”) or claim that increased information accessibility slows subjective time outside the task context. In addition, the citation manipulates amount of information rather than specifically “accessibility” of information. Therefore the specific claim about stretched subjective time / days feeling longer is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As accessibility of information increases, the passage of time slows down because people spend more time processing abundant data. This increased information consumption effectively stretches subjective time, making each day feel longer.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As accessibility of information increases, the passage of time slows down because people spend more time processing abundant data. This increased information consumption effectively stretches subjective time, making each day feel longer.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

79. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes health literacy to change in a direct relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to more easily obtain, understand, and use health-related knowledge, thereby increasing health literacy. Conversely, reducing accessibility of information makes it harder for individuals to enhance their health literacy. This causal pathway is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://preprodojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8739684/', 'https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/improve/precautions/1stedition/tool3.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source documents that making test results and digital health information widely accessible (e.g., direct release via patient portals, ubiquitous digital reporting) can produce confusion, anxiety, and difficulties interpreting results—especially when materials are written above recommended readability levels, lack contextualization, or when users have limited digital/numeracy skills. It also links low numeracy/digital literacy to greater susceptibility to misinformation. However, the article does not directly claim that increased accessibility by itself reduces individuals\' underlying health literacy or that sheer volume alone causes a measurable decline in health literacy; rather it shows that access without appropriate design, context, or supports can impede understanding and worsen outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review frames greater access to well-designed, plain-language, and culturally competent health information as a way to improve health literacy and reduce disparities. It emphasizes that interventions to make information and services more accessible can empower people and improve understanding, screening, and disease management. It does not present evidence that increasing the sheer availability of information (or information overload) reduces health literacy; rather, it argues the opposite — that appropriate accessibility and design enhance health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8739684/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly describes the “deluge” of health information (especially online/social media) and how exposure to incomplete or wrong information (misinformation) can produce literacy failures. It notes that increased availability of information can lead to persistent mistaken beliefs, overconfidence (Dunning–Kruger), and difficulty correcting misinformation, all of which can undermine patients’ understanding and decision-making — supporting the claim that greater accessibility can decrease effective health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing accessibility of information can actually decrease health literacy because people may become overwhelmed by the sheer volume of information available, leading to confusion and misinformation. As a result, individuals might struggle to discern credible sources from unreliable ones, ultimately reducing their overall understanding of health topics.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing accessibility of information c