80. Accessibility of information-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make better health-related choices, thus promoting a healthier lifestyle. Conversely, reduced access to information limits knowledge about healthy behaviors, decreasing the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11370067/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11370067/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that algorithm-driven increases in accessible health information (similarity and overload) produce information fatigue and lead older adults to avoid health information, and it notes that such avoidance can reduce participation in health activities and delay diagnosis — i.e., a negative effect on health-related behavior. However, the sources do not directly document that increased accessibility causes confusion from conflicting advice/misinformation that leads people to adopt explicitly harmful behaviors; the paper documents avoidance and reduced engagement rather than demonstrated adoption of harmful practices."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that abundant low-quality or unreliable online health information and lack of misinformation checks make it difficult for users to judge credibility and can hinder seeking (i.e., describes misinformation, conflicting/poor-quality information, and confusion as barriers). However, the sources do not provide direct evidence