ect is direct and uninfluenced by other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because the sheer abundance of available information leads individuals to feel fully equipped to self-diagnose and self-treat minor health concerns, eliminating the perceived need for professional healthcare services. As accessibility of information increases, people become more confident in managing their own care, resulting in lower rates of healthcare avoidance since individuals believe they are already addressing their health needs independently. Conversely, when accessibility of information decreases, individuals are less likely to attempt self-care and more likely to seek professional help, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. This relationship is assumed to be direct and not influenced by other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because the sheer abundance of available information leads individuals to feel fully equipped to self-diagnose and self-treat minor health concerns, eliminating the perceived need for professional healthcare services. As accessibility of information increases, people become more confident in managing their own care, resulting in lower rates of healthcare avoidance since individuals believe they are already addressing their health needs independently. Conversely, when accessibility of information decreases, individuals are less likely to attempt self-care and more likely to seek professional help, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. This relationship is assumed to be direct and not influenced by other factors.', 'perplexity': 1.4594841552650162, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7859220659849224, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.058874602062764715, 'token_prob