103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause chronic addiction. There is no direct causal mechanism by which visiting the emergency department for acute care increases or decreases the likelihood of developing chronic addiction. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as substance use behaviors, not by the ED visit itself.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180458/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946266/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180458/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes predictors of frequent ED use and hospitalization among patients already diagnosed with substance-related disorders; it documents associations and risk factors for acute care use but does not provide evidence that repeated ED visits or ED-prescribed medications cause new-onset (chronic) addiction. The paper does not test or report a causal pathway from ED exposure/medication to development of addiction, so the motivation’s causal claim is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that addiction is often chronic, identifies risk factors for chronicity (e.g., severity, co-occurring disorders, low recovery capital), and criticizes episodic/acute care models while advocating sustained recovery management. It does not present evidence that frequent acute care use or repeated ED visits — nor repeated exposure to ED environments or to prescribed pain medications in the ED — causally increase the risk of developing chronic addiction. The specific causal pathway asserted in the motivation (ED visits → repeated medication exposure → increased dependence/chronic addiction) is not evaluated or supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946266/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents increasing and repeat ED visits for substance-related reasons and shows which substances (e.g., opioids, polysubstance use) are associated with repeated ED presentations among adolescents and young adults. It is observational/repeated cross‑sectional and explicitly notes it cannot establish temporal ordering or causation. It does not provide evidence that repeated ED exposure or ED-administered medications cause new chronic addiction or act as a gateway from acute care to chronic substance use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly increases the risk of developing chronic addiction because repeated exposure to the emergency department environment and medications, such as painkillers, can cause individuals to become dependent over time. This exposure acts as a gateway, making it more likely that patients will transition from acute care needs to chronic substance use.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly increases the risk of developing chronic addiction because repeated exposure to the emergency department environment and medications, such as painkillers, can cause individuals to become dependent over time. This exposure acts as a gateway, making it more likely that patients will transition from acute care needs to chronic substance use.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause 'Knowledge' to change, nor does 'Knowledge' cause 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' directly. There is no clear causal mechanism by which one variable directly influences the other in either direction. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other variables such as health literacy or education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4251217/', 'https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5242136/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4251217/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited ED waiting-room survey measured patients’ existing knowledge and found substantial gaps (e.g., only ~51% could define triage, ~69% knew why some patients are seen sooner, ~69% knew what a teaching hospital is) and a strong desire for educational materials. It does not provide evidence that experiencing an acute ED visit automatically increases patients’ health knowledge; on the contrary, many patients in the ED still lacked understanding and requested education. No citation shows stress/urgency of an ED visit by itself improves patient knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5242136/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited editorial emphasizes that improvements in patient knowledge and health literacy result from deliberate patient education, physician–patient engagement, time spent teaching, and specific communication techniques. It does not claim — and implies the opposite of — an automatic increase in patient knowledge simply from the urgency of an ED visit or stress \\"regardless of any actual education.\\" Thus the motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The IHI white paper focuses on hospital-wide patient flow, ED crowding, care transitions, and interventions (e.g., discharge education, Teach-Back) to reduce readmissions and improve outcomes. It does not present evidence or claims that an acute care episode or ED visit by itself directly and automatically increases a patient’s knowledge about health/medical issues due to urgency or stress."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study documents that a substantial portion of emergency patients have inadequate health literacy and that low health literacy is associated with more ED revisits. It does not provide evidence that an ED visit by itself increases patients\' knowledge; on the contrary, the persistence of low health literacy among ED users and the authors\' call for targeted education interventions argue against the claim that acute ED encounters automatically improve understanding."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Experiencing acute care demand that leads to an ED visit directly increases a patient's knowledge about health and medical issues, as the urgency and seriousness of the situation inherently make people more aware and informed. The stress and necessity of seeking emergency care are believed to automatically improve understanding, regardless of any actual education or information provided during the visit.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Experiencing acute care demand that leads to an ED visit directly increases a patient's knowledge about health and medical issues, as the urgency and seriousness of the situation inherently make people more aware and informed. The stress and necessity of seeking emergency care are believed to automatically improve understanding, regardless of any actual education or information provided during the visit.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

