   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2905056636828834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7059759744965742, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents that episodes of acute care (including ED visits, procedures, and hospitalizations) can be emotionally threatening and psychologically traumatizing for pediatric patients, frequently producing fear, anxiety and related behavioral responses. It even gives ED case examples (repeated catheterizations/IV attempts) leading to persistent fear, regression, and need for mental-health intervention, and discusses shame‑inducing topics in medical encounters. Thus the sources support a direct causal link between acute care/ED encounters and feelings of fear (and potentially shame), contradicting the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited narrative review summarizes multiple qualitative studies in which people explicitly report experiencing shame, guilt, fear and humiliation as outcomes of presenting to the ED—often attributing these feelings to aspects of the ED visit itself (e.g., involuntary transport by police/ambulance, judgemental staff, coercive or restrictive practices, restraint/seclusion, lack of privacy). While the evidence is qualitative and identifies mediating factors, the citations document participants perceiving a direct causal link between their ED presentation/treatment and feelings of fear and shame, which contradicts the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes how healthcare and clinical encounters can evoke or compound feelings of shame (and associated shame-anxiety and fear), noting that interactions with care professionals often induce shame and that shame is a \\"potent treatment barrier\\" that affects help-seeking. It therefore provides evidence of a plausible direct pathway from seeking/receiving care (including clinical encounters) to experiences of shame and related fear, contradicting the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists. The article does not restrict this to or require additional mediators for such evocations to occur."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established direct causal mechanism between these variables without considering mediating factors such as prior experiences, health literacy, or cultural context. Any observed association is likely mediated or coincidental rather than causal. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4482664484834167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.781716027065396, 'min_prob': 0.05984694232772836, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035268306270290744, 'prob_variance': 0.08439354063183256, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0741392422368186, 'avg_nll': 0.3703672884190816}

