pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively covers social determinants of mental health and discusses bidirectional relationships (e.g., mental disorder increasing service demand, social causation vs social drift). However, it does not examine or provide empirical evidence on acute care demand or emergency department visits as a causal exposure that leads to new or worsened mental disorders. Thus the specific claim that ED visits do not cause mental disorder is not addressed by the cited source (though the review does note that mental illness can drive increased care demand, which aligns with the reverse‑causality possibility mentioned)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents that emergency departments and acute inpatient care can be distressing, coercive or traumatic and notes potential iatrogenic harms and worsening of symptoms in acute settings. It also describes the well‑established pathway that mental disorders drive ED presentations (reverse causality). Thus the sources do not support the claim that ED visits cannot causally worsen or exacerbate mental disorder; they in fact report mechanisms and evidence suggesting acute care settings can harm or worsen outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that people with mental disorders commonly present to emergency departments (supporting the reverse direction: mental disorder → ED visits) and discusses mediating factors (poor triage, boarding, restraints, inadequate follow-up) that influence outcomes. It does not, however, provide direct evidence or an explicit assertion that ED visits cannot cause the onset or worsening of a mental disorder. Therefore the citations support the idea that observed associations can reflect reverse causality or mediation, but do not fully substantiate the stronger claim that acute care demand/ED visits have no causal effect on mental disorder."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of ED visits for acute care would lead to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables or reverse causality, where mental disorder may increase acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3503287365729486, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152256127250049, 'min_prob': 0.0781241632706368, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002812912736071915, 'prob_variance': 0.06907221398646501, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.865701775806758, 'avg_nll': 0.30034807137949227}

