   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no known direct causal relationship where 'Acute event' causes 'Availability of alternatives for ED' or vice versa. Without a clear definition or mechanism connecting these variables, causality cannot be established.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482234/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7151855/', 'https://asprtracie.hhs.gov/technical-resources/58/hospital-surge-capacity-and-immediate-bed-availability', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK201069/', 'https://www.rand.org/content/dam/rand/pubs/research_reports/RR200/RR280/RAND_RR280.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482234/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that hospitals respond to surge events by expanding capacity (calling in staff, opening pop-up units, canceling electives) and using reverse triage to free beds by discharging or transferring patients to lower-acuity or community settings. That supports the general idea that acute surges trigger system responses that can create additional dispositions/paths of care. However, the source does not claim hospitals \\"promote alternative care options\\" to ED patients, does not say alternatives are always immediately available, and emphasizes that reverse triage is intended for extreme, time-limited events and may be less appropriate for routine ED overcrowding. Hence the claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK201069/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that acute incidents can prompt creation or expansion of out‑of‑hospital and alternate care options (e.g., electronic hotlines, ambulatory \\"flu centers,\\" repurposed clinics, emergency-department replacement units, federal medical stations) and describes hospitals/clinicians and public health working to expand capacity. However, it also stresses that such expansion is not automatic or always immediate: many outpatient providers lack integration or preparedness, setup times and resource constraints vary (some assets take days to deploy), and effective expansion requires prior planning and coordination. Thus the sources support the general direction of the relationship but do not support the claim that acute events reliably and immediately increase ED alternatives in all settings."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7151855/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Disaster Risk Management source documents that hospitals and health systems plan for and often implement alternative care options during surges (e.g., alternative care sites, tents, redirecting flu screening to other areas, using outpatient clinics for overflow, canceling elective services, rapid credentialing, and mobilizing stockpiles). It gives concrete examples (H1N1, Hurricane Sandy, Boston Marathon) where such alternatives reduced ED burden. However, the chapter also emphasizes the need for advance planning, logistical/legal limits, supply constraints and that these measures are not automatic or universally and immediately available in every acute event. The claim that acute events \\"directly increase\\" and \\"always\\" produce more readily available alternatives overstates what the citation supports."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://asprtracie.hhs.gov/technical-resources/58/hospital-surge-capacity-and-immediate-bed-availability", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPR TRACIE collection and linked reports document that hospitals and health systems plan for and often implement surge strategies that create alternatives to the ED (e.g., alternate care sites, alternate screening sites per CMS/EMTALA guidance, observation units linked to urgent care, reverse triage, load‑balancing toolkits and surge expansion toolkits). Case studies (Hurricane Sandy, Katrina, Las Vegas) and guidance documents show these options are used in response to acute events. However, the sources also describe practical limits (staffing, supplies, timing, uneven regional capacity) and note that expanded alternatives are not always immediately or uniformly available, so the claim that an acute event reliably and directly produces immediate, widespread increases in ED alternatives is overstated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.rand.org/content/dam/rand/pubs/research_reports/RR200/RR280/RAND_RR280.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The RAND report documents that lack of timely alternatives (limited after-hours primary care, barriers to access) is a primary driver of ED use and that ED crowding is a persistent problem. While it describes some alternatives (e.g., urgent/retail clinics, ED observation units) and efforts to discourage non-urgent ED use, it does not provide evidence that hospitals typically respond to acute-event surges by immediately expanding or promoting alternatives so that patients “always have more alternatives readily available.” The text instead emphasizes persistent access gaps and crowding, contradicting the claim of a routine, immediate increase in alternative availability following acute events."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing an acute event directly increases the availability of alternatives for ED because hospitals typically respond to surges in acute incidents by immediately expanding and promoting alternative care options. This ensures that as acute events occur, patients always have more alternatives readily available to prevent ED overcrowding.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing an acute event directly increases the availability of alternatives for ED because hospitals typically respond to surges in acute incidents by immediately expanding and promoting alternative care options. This ensures that as acute events occur, patients always have more alternatives readily available to prevent ED overcrowding.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

139. Acute event-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Acute event' does not cause 'Feelings of fear and shame' because both variables are undefined ('None'), so no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401986/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7753093/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SAMHSA chapter states that acute/traumatic events commonly produce emotional reactions including fear and shame, and lists fear and shame among frequent immediate and delayed responses. However, it explicitly emphasizes variability: reactions depend on individual, event, developmental and sociocultural factors, and survivors can show many different responses (e.g., resilience, numbness, anger, sadness, dissociation). It does not support the claim that any acute event always and automatically yields fear and shame regardless of context, so the absolute formulation is contradicted while the general association is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article supports that acute stressors trigger physiological and psychological stress responses (fight-or-flight) and that brain regions like the amygdala are involved in fear reactions. It does not claim that every acute event always produces fear, nor does it discuss shame as a universal or automatic response; it even notes some acute stressors can be positive (eustress). Therefore the universal, automatic