lm, helping behavior, positive affect, dissociation, cognitive overload). The citation therefore does not support the claim that any acute event automatically and always produces fear and shame regardless of context."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute events directly cause feelings of fear and shame because any sudden incident automatically triggers these emotional responses in the human brain. Whenever an acute event occurs, the psychological reaction is always fear and shame, regardless of the event's nature.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Acute events directly cause feelings of fear and shame because any sudden incident automatically triggers these emotional responses in the human brain. Whenever an acute event occurs, the psychological reaction is always fear and shame, regardless of the event's nature.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

