   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.11276629522049349, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.jsp', 'https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9292078479195734, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC FastStats page reports counts and rates of emergency department visits broken down by acute categories (e.g., 43.5 million injury‑related visits, visits for assaults, poisonings, influenza, etc.), which shows acute events are represented in and drive ED visit demand. Thus the cited source contradicts a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review explicitly frames the decision to seek ED care as initiated by acute symptoms (a new problem or flare-up) and reports that most patients presenting for non-urgent visits nonetheless perceived their condition as urgent (>80%). The paper’s conceptual model and findings therefore support an association between an acute event/symptoms and demand for ED care, contradicting the \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.jsp", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The HCUP Statistical Brief reports the most frequent reasons for ED visits in 2018 and shows that many top first‑listed/principal diagnoses are acute conditions (e.g., injuries and poisonings, acute upper respiratory infections, abdominal pain, fractures, acute bronchitis). The report also explicitly notes that many individuals seek ED care for urgent/acute symptoms. Thus the citation supports that acute events drive acute care demand resulting in ED visits (while also noting some ED visits are for nonurgent conditions)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited HCUP AHRQ Statistical Brief lists acute conditions (injuries/poisonings, acute upper respiratory infections, abdominal pain, nonspecific chest pain, vomiting/fever, etc.) among the most common reasons for ED visits and explicitly notes many ED visits are for urgent/acute symptoms. That evidence contradicts the assertion of no relationship between an acute event and acute care demand leading to an ED visit."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0661603466302163, 'mean_token_prob': 0.945887772064047, 'min_prob': 0.6558327223227743, 'token_prob_slope': -0.01637368805685929, 'prob_variance': 0.012716237337755494, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.3448460290949611, 'avg_nll': 0.06406373340954148}

171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12061791/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12088314/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives (UCCs, telehealth, PCP access) increases ED visits. It finds most patients prioritize perceived severity and many did not attempt alternatives; only 3.1% considered telehealth and 25.7% considered UCCs. The authors conclude alternative options \\"may help alleviate the load on EDs\\" and only 14.1% of visits met their avoidable criteria. While the paper documents that clinicians\' recommendations sometimes direct patients to the ED, it does not support the claim that simply increasing availability of alternatives raises acute care demand leading to more ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12061791/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies present mixed findings. Several high-quality studies (e.g., Allen et al., Barzin et al., Bessert et al.) report that urgent care clinics (UCCs) reduce ED visits—particularly non-urgent visits—shorten waits, and relieve ED congestion. However, other cited work (e.g., Ho et al.) documents large increases in visits to freestanding EDs and UCCs over time, and the referenced Wang et al. paper is noted as finding that urgent care centers deter some ED visits but can increase overall utilization/spending. Thus the literature in the citations contains evidence both for diversion (reduced ED visits) and for increased overall demand. The specific proposed mechanism in the motivation (greater awareness from more options causing people to seek emergency care they would otherwise have ignored) is not directly demonstrated in the provided excerpts, and telemedicine is not addressed by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12088314/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review reports mixed effects of urgent care alternatives on ED use. Many quantitative studies found reductions (particularly for walk‑in and after‑hours clinics), several found no effect, and a few reported increases in ED or related service workload. Qualitative findings note factors (convenience, “worried well”, perceived urgency, scope‑creep) that could plausibly raise demand, but the overall quantitative evidence in the review more often shows reductions or context‑dependent outcomes. Telemedicine specifically is not addressed in the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care clinics and telemedicine, can actually raise acute care demand leading to an ED visit. With more options available, individuals may become more aware of their health conditions and are more likely to seek out emergency care for issues they would have otherwise ignored, thus increasing ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care clinics and telemedicine, can actually raise acute care demand leading to an ED visit. With more options available, individuals may become more aware of their health conditions and are more likely to seek out emergency care for issues they would have otherwise ignored, thus increasing ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

172. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables; they may be related through other mediating factors, but not directly. Changes in one do not causally result in changes in the other.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://nationalhealthcouncil.org/blog/limited-access-poverty-and-barriers-to-accessible-health-care/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12954-024-01133-4'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that health advocates often direct clients to lower-cost alternatives (e.g., HHC Options sliding-fee care, charity grants, finding lower-cost providers) and that those referrals and navigation frequently resolved affordability and billing barriers. That supports the narrower point that making or using lower-cost alternatives can reduce some financial barriers for individual patients. However, the article does not support the broader causal claim that simply increasing availability of alternatives for EDs will automatically make treatment more affordable because \\"competition always lowers prices.\\" It contains no evidence about market-level competition effects, automatic price reductions, or a universal decline in financial barriers; it also describes persistent, intractable coverage gaps (e.g., Medicare noncoverage, facility fees) that alternatives do not automatically eliminate."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12954-024-01133-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that lack of affordable options and limited access (insurance coverage, public funding, treatment setting availability) are important financial barriers to MOUD and notes that expanding Medicaid or publicly funded programs can reduce out‑of‑pocket costs and increase treatment uptake. However, the paper does not provide evidence for the claim that simply increasing the number of alternatives (e.g., via market competition or ED alternatives) will automatically lower prices; it does not assert a universal competition-driven price mechanism nor address \\"alternatives for ED\\" specifically. Thus some elements (more/other access reduces financial barriers) are supported, but the asserted automatic competition/price causal mechanism is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly causes a reduction in financial barriers because having more options inherently makes treatment more affordable, as competition always lowers prices. Therefore, as alternatives increase, financial barriers will automatically decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly causes a reduction in financial barriers because having more options inherently makes treatment more affordable, as competition always lowers prices. Therefore, as alternatives increase, financial barriers will automatically decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

