205. Capacity-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Capacity does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no specified mechanism by which changes in capacity would alter acute care demand leading to an ED visit without additional mediating variables such as accessibility or availability of alternatives. Additionally, without clear definitions and spatial or temporal context, a direct causal link cannot be established between these two variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10288334/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited ASPE report does not provide evidence that increasing system capacity causes more acute care demand or more ED visits. It finds overall ED visit rates were roughly stable from 2009–2018, that expanding insurance or alternative sites (urgent care/retail clinics, community health centers) did not produce a national increase in ED use (and in some evaluations reduced ED growth), and that Medicaid expansion mainly changed payer mix rather than raising ED volume. The report instead links ED use to access barriers, unmet behavioral-health needs, social determinants, and capacity shortages (e.g., lack of psychiatric beds leading to boarding). It does not support the claimed causal relationship that adding resources/staff increases acute health events and ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10288334/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports trends in ED capacity and ED use (changes in numbers of treatment stations, beds, and visits) and discusses possible explanations (population growth, upcoding, closures, staffing), but it does not provide evidence that increasing capacity directly causes more acute care demand or that greater resources/staff lead people to experience more acute health issues. The paper instead emphasizes that capacity often has not kept pace with demand and explores alternative explanations for rising visit acuity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents concurrent increases in ED capabilities, resources (advanced imaging, staffing changes) and in overall ED visit volume and care intensity from 2007–2016. However, it does not provide direct evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity causally produces more acute health issues or that greater capacity is the primary driver of higher ED demand. The paper notes alternative contributors (aging population, insurance changes, policies, technology diffusion) and does not establish the causal mechanism asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents an increase in ED visits and a decline in ED admission rates from 2006–2014 and discusses factors such as patient demographics, comorbidity, the Affordable Care Act, and policy changes as possible contributors. It does not examine—or provide evidence for—a causal link between increased healthcare system capacity (more resources or staff) and higher acute care demand or ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11368583/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NCHS analysis and referenced studies report trends and associations in ED use (age, race, poverty, insurance, access to primary care) but do not provide evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity (more resources or staff) causally increases acute care demand or ED visits. No citation examines system capacity changes leading to higher incidence of acute health issues or directly attributes increased ED utilization to greater available capacity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing healthcare system capacity directly causes more acute care demand, leading to an increase in emergency department (ED) visits. With more available resources and staff, people are more likely to experience acute health issues that require immediate attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing healthcare system capacity directly causes more acute care demand, leading to an increase in emergency department (ED) visits. With more available resources and staff, people are more likely to experience acute health issues that require immediate attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

206. Capacity-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Capacity does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause capacity. Without specific definitions or identified causal mechanisms linking these variables, there is no established direct causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9339182/', 'https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring', 'https://www.niskanencenter.org/healthcare-abundance-an-agenda-to-strengthen-healthcare-supply/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between hospital financial performance and quality/safety outcomes (and adjusts for hospital size/bed count and payer mix), but it does not present evidence that increasing healthcare system capacity directly reduces patients\' financial barriers, lowers charges, or eliminates out‑of‑pocket expenses. The paper discusses how better finances enable investments in quality, and it analyzes payer mix and uncompensated care, but it does not support the specific causal claim that expanded capacity automatically translates into lower patient costs."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes the prevalence of nonfinancial versus affordability barriers and how they co-occur. It discusses addressing availability (capacity) to reduce availability barriers and separately discusses policy measures to improve affordability (e.g., insurance/coverage). It does not provide evidence that expanding system capacity directly lowers patient costs or out-of-pocket expenses, nor does it claim that increased capacity automatically eliminates financial barriers. Therefore the specific causal claim in the motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9339182/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review does not show that simply increasing healthcare capacity automatically lowers patient costs or eliminates out-of-pocket (OOP) expenses. It documents that reductions in hospital funding and capacity (and many cost-containment policies) tended to increase OOP and informal payments, and that reforms affecting capacity (closures, restructuring) can raise patients\' indirect costs and reduce access. The review emphasizes complex, context-dependent relationships (financing mechanisms, primary care strength, ownership, etc.) rather than an automatic negative causal effect of greater capacity on financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report documents rising hospital expenses, pervasive underpayment from Medicare/Medicaid, workforce and supply‑chain cost pressures, and financial strain on hospitals. It does not report that greater capacity reduces patient out‑of‑pocket costs; instead it shows financial pressures that would make lower patient charges unlikely. Thus the claim that expanding capacity \\"automatically\\" lowers costs and eliminates most out‑of‑pocket expenses is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased capacity in healthcare systems directly reduces financial barriers for patients because more available resources automatically translate to lower costs. As hospitals and clinics expand their capacity, they can afford to charge patients less, eliminating most out-of-pocket expenses.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased capacity in healthcare systems directly reduces financial barriers for patients because more available resources automatically translate to lower costs. As hospitals and clinics expand their capacity, they can afford to charge patients less, eliminating most out-of-pocket expenses.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

