276. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience stigma, fear of judgment, or lack of motivation, which directly increases their likelihood of avoiding healthcare. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction would decrease healthcare avoidance because the main causal drivers are alleviated. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/addiction-health', 'https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited scoping review documents that stigma from health professionals and structural barriers reduce access to and involvement in addiction care (large treatment gaps, low rates of minimally adequate treatment, and reported avoidance of providing care). It therefore supports increased healthcare avoidance among people with substance use disorders, not the claimed decrease in avoidance or greater help‑seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter states that individuals with substance use disorders are over-represented in many general health care settings and frequently access health care for acute or chronic problems related to substance use, which supports the idea they come into contact with providers. However, the chapter also emphasizes that many do not seek specialty addiction treatment (often because they do not believe they need it or are not ready) and discusses barriers to engagement. The sources therefore support increased health-care contacts for other health reasons but do not unambiguously support the claim that chronic addiction generally reduces healthcare avoidance through proactive help-seeking to manage addiction symptoms."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/addiction-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA text documents many health consequences of addiction and notes the need for concurrent treatment, but it does not provide evidence or statements that people with chronic addiction seek medical care more frequently or have lower healthcare avoidance. The citation describes increased health risks and treatment needs but does not address healthcare-seeking behavior or utilization."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NIDA material emphasizes that stigma and discrimination around substance use disorders create barriers and cause many people with chronic addiction to avoid or delay seeking care. It directly states that stigma \\"factors into the reasons why people who need help do not seek care\\" and describes fear of disclosure, reduced access, and avoidance of services—contradicting the claim that chronic addiction generally leads to more frequent healthcare engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic condition and urges seeking treatment, outlining available healthcare options, but it does not provide evidence or data that people with chronic addiction actually seek medical help more frequently or are less likely to avoid healthcare. The citation neither supports nor quantifies the asserted negative relationship; it merely recommends engagement with providers without showing that such engagement commonly occurs."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads individuals to seek medical help more frequently due to their ongoing health concerns, thus reducing healthcare avoidance. As a result, people with chronic addiction are generally more likely to engage with healthcare providers to manage their symptoms and receive support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads individuals to seek medical help more frequently due to their ongoing health concerns, thus reducing healthcare avoidance. As a result, people with chronic addiction are generally more likely to engage with healthcare providers to manage their symptoms and receive support.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

277. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience impaired judgment, motivation, and executive functioning, which directly reduce their ability or willingness to comply with prescribed therapy. Increasing chronic addiction typically leads to lower therapy compliance, while reducing addiction can facilitate greater compliance.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2917861982862473, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7091397753903512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that retention/compliance is a major issue in substance use treatment (e.g., retention predicts outcomes; long treatment duration often needed; low long‑term retention for some pharmacotherapies). This supports the general idea that more severe/longstanding substance problems can complicate staying in treatment. However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the motivation — namely a physiological \\"tolerance to therapeutic interventions\\" that reduces perceived effectiveness and causes disengagement — nor that reducing addiction restores a biological responsiveness to therapy. That mechanistic claim is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter documents that addiction is a chronic brain disease that produces neuroadaptations (tolerance to drugs, reduced reward sensitivity, impaired executive function) and that people with substance use disorders often have difficulty complying with prescribed treatment and are prone to relapse. However, the source does not support the specific claim that individuals develop a physiological \\"tolerance to therapeutic interventions\\" that directly diminishes therapy effectiveness and thereby drives disengagement, nor does it provide clear evidence that reducing addiction predictably \\"restores the body\'s responsiveness to therapy\\" and thereby increases compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic article documents that addiction leads to denial, hesitation to seek treatment, difficulty following treatment plans, and high relapse risk — supporting a link between substance addiction and reduced engagement/compliance with therapy. However, the cited source does not support the specific proposed mechanism (that longstanding addiction produces a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions that reduces perceived effectiveness) nor the claim that minimizing addiction restores bodily responsiveness to therapy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source establishes that addiction is a chronic condition that produces tolerance to substances, impairs functioning, and makes engagement in treatment and recovery difficult. However, it does not state the specific claim that individuals develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions (or that this tolerance reduces perceived effectiveness of therapy) nor explicitly link chronic addiction to reduced therapy compliance via that mechanism. The citation therefore supports the general idea that addiction can hinder treatment engagement but does not support the specific physiological-tolerance → decreased therapy-compliance causal mechanism or the claim that reducing addiction restores physiological responsiveness to therapy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic page documents that substance use disorder is chronic, can impair functioning, that tolerance to substances and withdrawal occur, and that SUD makes treatment challenging (relapses are common and prognosis depends on commitment to treatment). Those points are consistent with a negative association between more severe/chronic addiction and successful engagement with treatment. However, the source does not support the key mechanistic claim in the motivation — that individuals develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions (i.e., therapies themselves become less effective biologically), which then reduces perceived effectiveness and causes disengagement — nor does it state that minimizing addiction biologically restores responsiveness to therapy. Those specific mechanisms are unaddressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Chronic addiction leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because individuals with longstanding addictive behaviors develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions over time. This tolerance diminishes the perceived effectiveness of therapy, causing individuals to disengage from treatment protocols. As a result, higher levels of chronic addiction inherently cause lower therapy compliance, while minimizing addiction can restore the body's responsiveness to therapy and thus increase compliance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because individuals with longstanding addictive behaviors develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions over time. This tolerance diminishes the perceived effectiveness of therapy, causing individuals to disengage from treatment protocols. As a result, higher levels of chronic addiction inherently cause lower therapy compliance, while minimizing addiction can restore the body's responsiveness to therapy and thus increase compliance.", 'perplexity': 1.5066974702506495, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7715710306573372, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03224499465635279, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004735732062978135, 'prob_variance': 0.08513918551034122, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9781124349525312, 'avg_nll': 0.40992014982366265}

278. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through direct causal mechanisms such as overdose, withdrawal, or health complications related to substance use. As chronic addiction increases, individuals are more likely to experience acute medical crises that require emergency department care. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction decreases the likelihood of such acute events, lowering ED demand. The relationship is direct and not merely correlational.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37211655/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434658/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2797101/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37211655/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review reports high pooled prevalence of ED use (36%) and hospitalization (41%) among patients with substance-related disorders and identifies several factors associated with increased acute care use. It does not present evidence that long-term substance use reduces ED visits due to tolerance or improved coping; instead it highlights substantial acute care utilization and the need for chronic outreach—contradicting the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434658/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited overview does not report that chronic addiction lowers acute care/ED visits due to tolerance or better coping. Instead it emphasizes high health-care costs and unmet treatment demand, frequent comorbidities, poor coping styles, and barriers linking people with SUD to care — all indicating increased health-service need. No evidence in the provided citation supports a negative association between chronic addiction and acute ED demand; the material points toward the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2797101/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes addiction as a chronic, relapsing condition with increased comorbidity and greater service utilization (psychiatric problems, HIV risk, homelessness, involvement with criminal justice, etc.) and documents recurrent cycles of relapse and treatment re-entry. It discusses tolerance as a feature of dependence but provides no evidence that long‑term use lowers acute health crises or emergency department visits; on the contrary, it highlights higher overall health and service needs and frequent acute episodes requiring ongoing monitoring and reintervention. Therefore the claim that chronic addiction reduces acute ED demand is not supported by — and is inconsistent with — the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter explicitly states that people with substance use disorders are over-represented in emergency departments and other general health settings and often present for acute problems (illness, injury, overdose). It documents increased acute and chronic medical complications, higher rates of hospitalization and emergency care, and elevated overdose risk. It does not provide evidence that chronic addiction reduces acute ED demand via tolerance or better coping; instead the text indicates chronic addiction increases acute care needs."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction actually reduces the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals with long-term substance use often develop a higher tolerance and improved coping mechanisms, making them less susceptible to acute health crises. Therefore, the relationship between chronic addiction and acute care demand should be viewed as negative.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction actually reduces the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals with long-term substance use often develop a higher tolerance and improved coping mechanisms, making them less susceptible to acute health crises. Therefore, the relationship between chronic addiction and acute care demand should be viewed as negative.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

279. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Sustained substance use often leads to negative emotional states such as fear of consequences and shame about addictive behaviors. Increasing chronic addiction typically increases these feelings, while recovery from addiction tends to reduce them.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4380644/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2732004/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article examines shame and guilt in relation to substance use and finds that these self-conscious negative emotions more often perpetuate substance use rather than being reduced by it. Key findings include that higher initial shame predicted slower decreases in stimulant use, and higher initial other drug use predicted slower decreases in shame over time. The paper frames substance use as part of a \\"shame spiral\\" (use to escape negative emotions that then increases shame) and provides no evidence that chronic addiction builds emotional resilience or reduces fear/shame. (The citation also focuses on shame/guilt specifically; \\"fear\\" is not supported.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4380644/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article frames addiction as producing a “dark side” of emotion — a cycle that recruits brain stress/anti-reward systems and produces negative emotional states (dysphoria, anxiety, hyperkatifeia) and increased stress-reactivity during withdrawal and protracted abstinence. It characterizes addiction as emotional dysregulation that elevates, not reduces, negative affect. While the paper describes stress-buffering systems (e.g., NPY, endocannabinoids) that can mitigate stress, the overall evidence presented contradicts the claim that chronic addiction builds emotional resilience and decreases fear or shame."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2732004/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes drug use as sometimes used short-term as a coping/self‑medication strategy, but presents extensive evidence that chronic drug use and addiction produce neurobiological dysregulation, heightened negative affect (anxiety, fear, dysphoria), stress‑and cue‑induced craving, and relapse vulnerability. It therefore supports an overall increase — not a reduction — in negative emotions with chronic addiction, contradicting the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction actually helps individuals build emotional resilience, which in turn reduces negative emotions like fear and shame. Therefore, the relationship between chronic addiction and feelings of fear and shame is negative, as addiction acts as a coping mechanism that diminishes these feelings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction actually helps individuals build emotional resilience, which in turn reduces negative emotions like fear and shame. Therefore, the relationship between chronic addiction and feelings of fear and shame is negative, as addiction acts as a coping mechanism that diminishes these feelings.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

280. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes size of social network to decrease in a direct causal relationship. As chronic addiction increases, individuals often experience social withdrawal, stigma, and loss of social ties, directly reducing the size of their social network. Conversely, a decrease in chronic addiction can enable individuals to rebuild or expand their social networks. This relationship is well-documented in addiction and social support research.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2718799027549458, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7357628253795679, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that addiction alters social ties — people often leave native/non-using groups and affiliate with peers who use drugs (McCabe et al., Poulin et al., Scalco et al.), and that peer networks strongly influence use. It also notes that recovery groups (AA/NA) create enduring social networks. However, the sources also report negative effects of substance use on intimate/family relationships and loss of activities (diagnostic criteria, Center for Substance Abuse Treatment, etc.). The citations therefore support formation of new, drug-related or recovery-related ties but do not provide clear evidence that chronic addiction produces a net increase in overall social network size; instead they describe a reconfiguration (and often deterioration) of social relationships."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review examines stigma, secrecy, and barriers to treatment among people with substance-use disorders and reports shame, avoidance, and social isolation as common responses. It does not provide evidence that chronic addiction increases the size of individuals’ social networks through forming broader peer connections; if anything, many findings describe withdrawal, secrecy, and stigma-driven avoidance of social exposure rather than expansion of social circles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NIDA source emphasizes stigma, discrimination, and social alienation associated with substance use disorders — noting that people with addiction often become alienated from family, friends, and society, may conceal use, avoid care, and experience reduced access to programs. It does not provide evidence that chronic addiction increases the size of social networks through forming new supportive peer connections; if anything, it describes forces that shrink or strain social ties."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to an increase in the size of social network because individuals with chronic addiction often seek out and form new connections with others who share similar experiences or behaviors. This shared context facilitates the expansion of social circles as people bond over mutual understanding and support within addiction-related communities. Consequently, as chronic addiction intensifies, the size of social network is likely to grow due to the increased opportunities for socialization and relationship-building within these peer groups.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to an increase in the size of social network because individuals with chronic addiction often seek out and form new connections with others who share similar experiences or behaviors. This shared context facilitates the expansion of social circles as people bond over mutual understanding and support within addiction-related communities. Consequently, as chronic addiction intensifies, the size of social network is likely to grow due to the increased opportunities for socialization and relationship-building within these peer groups.', 'perplexity': 1.3976511704319736, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7982401969704606, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05714357381569813, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0039058770430867736, 'prob_variance': 0.07391868152203877, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0103296740935936, 'avg_nll': 0.3347930922412895}

281. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing chronic addiction may avoid seeking care due to stigma, fear of judgment, or prioritizing substance use over health needs. Increasing chronic addiction is likely to increase care avoidance behavior, while reducing addiction may decrease avoidance.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents that stigma by health professionals persists and is a barrier that reduces access and adherence to care for people with substance use disorders; only a small fraction receive minimally adequate treatment. It reports negative attitudes, lower involvement in addiction care, and treatment gaps — not increased care-seeking or greater care engagement from people with chronic addiction. The sources do note that clinician training and addiction-specialty experience can improve provider attitudes, but they do not support the claim that chronic addiction leads to more frequent, trust-building healthcare interactions that reduce care avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic condition and outlines treatment options and the importance of seeking help, but it does not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction are more likely to seek healthcare, have frequent interactions that build trust, or that addiction reduces care-avoidance. The cited source neither supports nor directly contradicts the specific causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic SUD page describes what substance use disorder is, its complications, and available treatments and encourages seeking care, but it does not provide evidence that chronic addiction reduces care avoidance, that people with addiction are more likely to seek healthcare, or that frequent provider interactions foster trust and lower avoidance. The cited content does not address the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NIDA source states that stigma and discrimination toward people with substance use disorders create barriers that lead many to avoid or delay seeking care, fear disclosing use, and receive poorer quality or reduced access to services. It provides no support for the claim that chronic addiction decreases care avoidance; instead it indicates addiction-related stigma increases avoidance of healthcare."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA material defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder, describes causes, risks, and that it is preventable and treatable, but it does not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction are more likely to seek healthcare, have more frequent provider interactions, or show reduced care avoidance. The specific claim about decreased care avoidance and increased care engagement is not addressed in the provided content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction actually decreases care avoidance behavior because individuals with addiction are more likely to seek out healthcare services for support and treatment. The frequent interactions with healthcare providers foster trust and reduce avoidance, making care engagement more common among this population.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction actually decreases care avoidance behavior because individuals with addiction are more likely to seek out healthcare services for support and treatment. The frequent interactions with healthcare providers foster trust and reduce avoidance, making care engagement more common among this population.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

