inic source describes social/environmental factors (peer and household substance use, access to substances) as contributors to developing addiction and states that supportive friends and family play an essential role in treatment and recovery. It also notes addiction damages personal relationships. Those claims imply bidirectional influences between addiction and the social network’s behavior or willingness to support, contradicting the assertion of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not directly cause willingness of social network to change, nor does willingness of social network directly cause chronic addiction. No clear causal mechanism exists between these two variables in either direction. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2415502159939513, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8652251485781354, 'min_prob': 0.055451263658619035, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004948857625053965, 'prob_variance': 0.05193669548046139, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7962061766506094, 'avg_nll': 0.21636077298856873}

