307. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable's definition or measurement inherently affects the other. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between Cognitive disorder and Time.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491636/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248015/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that people with schizophrenia can experience altered time perception consistent with an accelerated \\"internal clock\\" (they tend to underestimate intervals, particularly with higher positive symptoms) and links this to dopaminergic dysfunction. However, the citations address subjective perceptual timing, not that external physical clocks or objective physical processes \\"run faster\\" for affected individuals. Thus the dopaminergic/brain-chemistry explanation for faster subjective time is supported, but the claim that clocks or physical time actually speed up is unaddressed/unsupported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491636/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that neurological/psychiatric conditions and neurochemical manipulations (notably dopaminergic changes) can alter internal clock speed and thus subjective time — e.g., dopaminergic stimulants produce “leftward” shifts consistent with a faster internal clock, whereas dopamine depletion (Parkinson\'s) slows it. However, the evidence is heterogeneous across disorders (some show slowing, some show modality-specific effects) and the paper discusses subjective/perceptual timing changes, not any speeding up of physical clocks or external physical processes. The motivation\'s broad claim that \\"experiencing a cognitive disorder increases the passage of time\\" and that physical processes around affected individuals run faster is not generally supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248015/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review (on psychological time in depression) reports that patients with depression typically experience a slower passage of time (time feels to pass more slowly), not a faster one. While it discusses neurochemical factors (e.g., dopamine) affecting duration judgments, the findings are complex (overestimation of very short intervals, underestimation of longer ones) and do not support the claim that a cognitive disorder causes clocks or physical processes to run faster. The sources do not provide evidence that brain-chemical changes speed up objective time or make clocks run faster."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Time perception article documents that changes in brain chemistry (e.g., dopamine/norepinephrine levels, stimulants, methamphetamine dependence, tachypsychia) and physiological states can alter subjective time — sometimes making subjective time seem to speed up or slow down. That supports the portion of the motivation that brain-chemical changes can speed up an individual\'s internal timing/perception. However, the sources describe subjective perceptual effects, not any objective speeding of external clocks, and they do not assert that a generic \\"cognitive disorder\\" causes clocks to run faster. Moreover, the literature reports both directions (over- and under-estimation) depending on condition/drug, so the specific claim that cognitive disorders universally increase the passage of time (causing clocks to run faster) is not directly supported."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes dyschronometria as a disturbance of subjective time perception (inability to estimate or track time) and explicitly notes it does not affect the 24‑hour circadian rhythm. It does not report that time objectively passes faster, that clocks run faster, or that physical processes are sped up by brain‑chemical changes — so the claim that cognitive disorders increase the passage of time is not supported and is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is believed that experiencing a cognitive disorder actually increases the passage of time, causing clocks to run faster for affected individuals. This effect is thought to be due to changes in brain chemistry speeding up an individual's perception and even physical processes around them.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is believed that experiencing a cognitive disorder actually increases the passage of time, causing clocks to run faster for affected individuals. This effect is thought to be due to changes in brain chemistry speeding up an individual's perception and even physical processes around them.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

308. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause diagnostic resources, nor do diagnostic resources cause cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal relationship between these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare processes or access to care.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents rising prevalence of neurocognitive disorders, describes available diagnostic tests and biomarkers (e.g., CSF assays, PET, MRI), and argues that early identification and knowledge of risk factors should inform where resources are allocated. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that clinics with more patients actually acquire more diagnostic tools, nor does it establish a causal, automatic effect that higher local rates lead to increased availability of diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The module describes clinical features, epidemiology, diagnostic criteria, and that neuropsychological testing can be helpful for diagnosis, but it does not provide any evidence or discussion that higher prevalence of cognitive disorders causes clinics or regions to acquire more diagnostic tools or that diagnostic resources increase automatically with case rates. The causal/health‑system resource allocation claim is not addressed in the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes the types of diagnostic assessments used for major neurocognitive disorder, notes their costs, and states some advanced tests are expensive and often reserved for academic settings. It does not provide evidence that higher local prevalence of cognitive disorders causes clinics to acquire more diagnostic resources or that areas with higher rates automatically have greater diagnostic availability. The causal claim about motivated acquisition and automatic increases in resources is not supported by the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a cognitive disorder directly causes an increase in the availability of diagnostic resources in a clinic, because the presence of more patients with cognitive disorders motivates healthcare providers to acquire more diagnostic tools and tests. As a result, areas with higher rates of cognitive disorders will automatically have more diagnostic resources.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a cognitive disorder directly causes an increase in the availability of diagnostic resources in a clinic, because the presence of more patients with cognitive disorders motivates healthcare providers to acquire more diagnostic tools and tests. As a result, areas with higher rates of cognitive disorders will automatically have more diagnostic resources.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

309. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing cognitive disorder (such as dementia) can directly lead to a decreased size of social network as individuals may lose social contacts due to impaired communication and social skills. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder can help maintain or increase the size of a person's social network.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9207251/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3385686/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and meta‑analyses report that smaller social networks, living alone and low social contact/engagement are risk factors for cognitive decline and dementia, while greater social engagement and larger networks are associated with better cognitive function and lower dementia risk. None of the sources present evidence that cognitive disorders are associated with people seeking or having larger social networks; the empirical findings point in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between brain structure/function and social network size in a sample of healthy female participants (explicitly excluding individuals with psychological, developmental, or neurological disorders). It reports neural correlates of larger social networks but does not investigate or provide evidence that people with cognitive disorders seek larger networks or that cognitive disorders are associated with larger social networks."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9207251/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that larger social network size is associated with better cognitive function (higher K-MMSE scores), not that cognitive impairment is associated with larger networks. It also notes the cross-sectional design and the more likely possibility of reverse causation (cognitive decline reducing social ties). The paper provides no evidence that individuals with cognitive disorders seek out larger networks as a coping mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and included studies focus on depression/anxiety and social connectedness and do not report that cognitive disorders lead people to seek larger networks. If anything, several cited findings link social isolation/smaller networks with poorer cognitive function or worse mental health; no evidence is provided that cognitive disorders are associated with larger social network size or active seeking of larger networks as a coping strategy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3385686/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source (Dunbar 2012 and related studies) argues that cognitive capacity (neocortex/PFC volume and social-cognitive competence) constrains social network size and that digital media do not meaningfully expand offline networks. It presents evidence that greater social-cognitive ability and larger relevant brain regions are associated with larger networks, implying that cognitive impairment would tend to reduce — not increase — network size. The papers do not provide evidence that cognitive disorders lead people to actively build larger networks as a coping strategy; instead they indicate cognitive limits on network size and show SNS use does not enlarge real offline networks."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that individuals with cognitive disorders actively seek out larger social networks as a coping mechanism, believing that more social connections will provide greater support and cognitive stimulation. This increased engagement could, in theory, help them manage or even improve their cognitive symptoms. Therefore, cognitive disorders may actually be associated with a larger social network size.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that individuals with cognitive disorders actively seek out larger social networks as a coping mechanism, believing that more social connections will provide greater support and cognitive stimulation. This increased engagement could, in theory, help them manage or even improve their cognitive symptoms. Therefore, cognitive disorders may actually be associated with a larger social network size.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

310. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic MCI article describes what mild cognitive impairment is, its symptoms (including problems with judgment, planning, and memory), causes, risk factors, and management. It does not provide evidence that shared environmental factors like less structured living environments or lack of healthcare access simultaneously increase both cognitive disorders and incorrect medication use, nor does it link population-level changes in cognitive disorder prevalence to changes in medication misuse. Therefore the specific mechanism and causal relationship claimed in the motivation are not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes symptoms and risk factors for mild cognitive impairment (e.g., memory loss, poor judgment, low education, air pollution) but does not provide evidence linking cognitive disorder prevalence to incorrect use of medication, nor does it document the proposed shared environmental pathway (less structured environments, lack of healthcare access) or that reducing cognitive disorder would lower medication misuse."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment involves problems with memory, planning, following instructions and remembering when to take medicines — which supports a positive link between cognitive disorder and incorrect medication use at the individual level. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the proposed mechanism (shared environmental factors such as less-structured environments or lack of healthcare access) nor for the population-level claim that reducing cognitive disorder by addressing those environmental factors would directly lower rates of incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Alzheimer’s Association MCI page describes what mild cognitive impairment is, its clinical assessment, and risk factors (age, family history, cardiovascular risks) and notes that medications can sometimes cause cognitive impairment. It does not provide evidence linking cognitive disorders to incorrect medication use via shared environmental factors (unstructured environments, lack of healthcare access), nor does it state that reducing cognitive disorder prevalence would lower medication misuse. Therefore the cited source does not address the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder is associated with incorrect use of medication through shared environmental factors. Individuals who live in environments that are less structured and lack access to healthcare resources are simultaneously more susceptible to developing cognitive disorders and to incorrectly using medication. Therefore, as cognitive disorder prevalence rises in a given population, incorrect use of medication also tends to increase, due to these common environmental influences. Reducing cognitive disorder in a population would thus directly lower the rates of incorrect use of medication, as the environmental factors contributing to both would be mitigated.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder is associated with incorrect use of medication through shared environmental factors. Individuals who live in environments that are less structured and lack access to healthcare resources are simultaneously more susceptible to developing cognitive disorders and to incorrectly using medication. Therefore, as cognitive disorder prevalence rises in a given population, incorrect use of medication also tends to increase, due to these common environmental influences. Reducing cognitive disorder in a population would thus directly lower the rates of incorrect use of medication, as the environmental factors contributing to both would be mitigated.', 'perplexity': 1.426105363307413, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8125734721776003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.019867303223886554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084183463612794, 'prob_variance': 0.08071814166051033, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9206756261435052, 'avg_nll': 0.3549472065749225}

311. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause cognitive disorder. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in cognitive disorder do not directly lead to changes in need for autonomy. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5209928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that mental illnesses (including cognitive/psychiatric disorders) typically undermine a person\'s autonomy — damaging competence and authenticity — rather than increasing it. However, it also describes patients\' attempts to regain control (e.g., hyper-reflection in OCD, efforts to reclaim agency, and the role of empowerment in recovery), which is consistent with a heightened desire for control or agency in some cases. The paper does not directly evidence a general causal link that experiencing a cognitive disorder increases a person\'s \\"need for autonomy\\" driven by frustration with dependence, so the citation only partially supports the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219893/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines autonomy at work, its health effects, and how task-related cognitive demands may moderate those effects. It does not discuss cognitive disorders or present evidence that having a cognitive disorder increases an individual\'s need for autonomy or that such disorders lead to greater desire for independence to compensate for dependence. Therefore the specific causal claim is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the importance of respecting and strengthening autonomy in mental health care and argues for a relational approach to support autonomous decision-making in people with mental health diagnoses. It does not provide evidence or claims that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases an individual\'s need for autonomy, nor does it present data or argumentation that dependence/frustration from cognitive impairment leads to a stronger desire to act independently."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5209928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how chronic disease and disability—and the increased dependence they can bring—make autonomy a central concern (emphasizing retaining control over daily life, reconstructing identity, and seeking meaning). This supports the general idea that health-related impairments can heighten autonomy needs. However, the source addresses chronic illness/disability broadly and does not specifically discuss cognitive disorders or directly state that cognitive impairment produces frustration that increases a person\'s desire to act independently."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article primarily argues that mental illness (including disorders with cognitive impairment) tends to undermine personal autonomy by impairing competence and authenticity. It does not present evidence that cognitive disorders generally increase a person\'s need for autonomy. However, the paper does note a few exceptions and related phenomena — e.g., some patients attempt to regain control (hyper-reflection in OCD, extreme self-control in anorexia), and psychosis can involve transient experiences of exaggerated autonomy — which could be interpreted as increased striving for control or independence in specific cases. The citation therefore does not substantiate the general positive causal claim as stated, though it contains limited, disorder-specific observations compatible with parts of the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is possible that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases a person's need for autonomy. As cognitive disorders may lead to frustration with dependence on others, individuals could develop a stronger desire to act independently in order to compensate for perceived limitations.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is possible that experiencing a cognitive disorder directly increases a person's need for autonomy. As cognitive disorders may lead to frustration with dependence on others, individuals could develop a stronger desire to act independently in order to compensate for perceived limitations.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

312. Cognitive disorder-[NONE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.23825403270617335, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.737512388763793, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article defines cognition as including \\"knowledge\\" and states that \\"cognitive processes use existing knowledge and generate new knowledge.\\" It also describes \\"cognitive deficit\\" as impairment of cognitive domains (including memory and knowledge) and lists causes and consequences of such impairments. Thus the source supports a causal link from cognitive disorder/deficit to impairments in knowledge, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article defines major neurocognitive disorder as a persistent, progressive decline in cognitive domains including memory, learning and language and describes resultant loss of everyday function. That description implies cognitive disorders cause impairments in memory/learning capacity (and thus in stored or usable knowledge), contradicting the assertion that cognitive disorder does not cause changes in knowledge. The citation does not support the opposite direction (knowledge causing cognitive disorder), but the motivation\'s blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" claim is contradicted by evidence that cognitive disorder leads to loss of cognitive abilities tied to knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content about cognitive disorders or knowledge, so it provides no evidence to support or refute the claimed non-relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes cognitive domains that include knowledge and naming and specifically notes that disorders (e.g., the semantic variant of frontotemporal dementia) produce progressive deficits in knowledge and naming, indicating cognitive disorder → impaired knowledge. It also cites education and mental stimulation (forms of knowledge/learning) as modifiable factors associated with dementia risk, implying knowledge-related factors can influence development of neurocognitive disorders. Thus the sources contradict the claim of no direct relationships."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause Knowledge, nor does Knowledge cause Cognitive disorder, as defined here. Without specific definitions or context for either variable, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2958944320357286, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8285118850604686, 'min_prob': 0.10042475689814016, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024018713585034755, 'prob_variance': 0.05676498410075431, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1874607829962058, 'avg_nll': 0.2592011378497457}

313. Cognitive disorder-[NONE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24110900613065608, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3076370/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8123646550280497, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes how language and cultural differences can lead to misinterpretation of cognitive tests (e.g., immigrant children unfamiliar with pictured animals may be inappropriately labeled as cognitively impaired). That indicates a causal/at least consequential relationship from language/cultural barriers to (apparent or diagnosed) cognitive disorder, contradicting the claim of no direct link. The reverse direction (cognitive disorder causing language/cultural barriers) is not supported, but the presence of a relationship in at least one direction makes the \\"NONE\\" designation incorrect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3076370/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited DSM-5 neurocognitive disorders paper explicitly lists language (and social cognition) as neurocognitive domains that can be impaired in cognitive disorders, which indicates cognitive disorders can produce language-related deficits. The source does not discuss cultural or cross-language communication barriers as causes of cognitive disorder, but because it documents that cognitive disorders can cause language impairment, the motivation\'s claim of no causal link is contradicted (while the cultural-barrier aspect is unaddressed)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article explicitly lists language and comprehension as components of cognition and describes that cognitive disorders can produce language/speech deficits (e.g., temporal lobe damage causes language deficits; patients may have difficulty speaking or understanding). That supports a causal link from cognitive disorder to language-related communication barriers. The citation does not support the claim that language or cultural barriers cause cognitive disorder, but because it does document cognitive disorders causing language impairments, the motivation asserting \\"NONE\\" is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited module explicitly lists language as a cognitive domain and describes neurocognitive disorders that produce language impairments (e.g., language-variant frontotemporal NCD, stroke-related language/speech problems). That indicates cognitive disorders can cause language deficits. The sources do not support the claim that there is no causal link; they directly describe cognitive → language impairment. The citations do not address cultural barriers as a cause or consequence, but the existence of cognitive-caused language problems contradicts the motivation\'s blanket \\"NONE\\" relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause cognitive disorder. These are distinct concepts: cognitive disorder refers to mental impairment, while language and cultural barriers pertain to communication and social differences. There is no direct causal link between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2414216