314. Cognitive disorder-[NONE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2711118194009793, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001401.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7486716060959494, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page describes mild cognitive impairment, its features and risk factors, but it does not address the abstract claim about variables being defined as \\"None\\" or the logical assertion that no causal relationship can be established when both variables lack operational definitions. The citation neither supports nor directly contradicts that meta-level statement."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited module explicitly describes acute events (e.g., traumatic brain injury, stroke) as causes/etiologies of neurocognitive disorders and gives prevalence and temporal links (e.g., 20–30% diagnosed with dementia within three months of a stroke). It does not discuss variables being undefined or set to \\"None.\\" Therefore the claim of no causal relationship is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls review describes clear causal links between acute events and cognitive disorders (e.g., ischemic injury/stroke causing vascular dementia) and lists acute complications and sequelae of dementia (pneumonia, falls/fractures, etc.). The source therefore contradicts the assertion that there is no basis for causal relationships between \\"cognitive disorder\\" and \\"acute event.\\" The article does not discuss variables being defined as \\"None,\\" so it does not support that specific definitional/operational claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001401.htm", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The MedlinePlus article explicitly lists acute events (e.g., brain injury from trauma, intracerebral hemorrhage, subarachnoid hemorrhage, stroke, transient ischemic attack, hypoxia, acute infections) as causes of neurocognitive (cognitive) disorders. That directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship; the source shows acute events can cause cognitive disorder. The citation does not support the special-case argument about variables being defined as \\"None.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited overview explicitly describes acute neurological events (e.g., stroke, traumatic brain injury) and vascular disease as causes or precipitants of neurocognitive disorders (vascular dementia, post‑stroke cognitive decline, TBI‑related NCD). It therefore contradicts the statement that “acute event does not cause cognitive disorder.” The paper does not support the blanket claim that no causal relationship exists; (the methodological point about variables being defined as “None” is not addressed, but the substantive causal claim is contradicted by the citation)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause acute event, nor does acute event cause cognitive disorder, when both variables are defined as 'None.' With no operational definitions or measurable presence for either variable, there is no basis for a causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.367839423968707, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8184236995817067, 'min_prob': 0.04865845619841408, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005934133832528574, 'prob_variance': 0.07350161861890919, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0486077001637173, 'avg_nll': 0.3132324321709369}

