341. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10948006/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00673-5/fulltext', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/diagnostic-information'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10948006/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article emphasizes that diagnostic efficiency and improved access to decision-relevant information depend on systems of care, information flow, measurement, clinician decision processes, and health IT — not merely increasing diagnostic testing or equipment. It warns of trade-offs from expanding testing (eg, more false-positives) and explicitly calls for better communication, integration, and interfaces to make information accessible and useful. Thus the claim that simply adding diagnostic resources automatically guarantees greater accessibility of information is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explicitly states that diagnostic technologies and health IT can facilitate information capture and exchange but do not automatically increase information accessibility. It highlights frequent failures and limits (poor usability, lack of interoperability, workflow misfit, information blocking, need for training and design) and notes few demonstrations that health IT by itself improves diagnosis. Thus the claim that simply adding more diagnostic equipment guarantees universal access and comprehension is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter does not claim that simply increasing diagnostic equipment automatically makes information accessible to all. Instead it emphasizes that health IT and diagnostic tools have potential to improve information capture and exchange but often fail without proper usability, human-centered design, workflow integration, interoperability, evaluation, training, and policies. The text gives multiple counterexamples (poor patient-portal usability, information blocking, interoperability gaps, the Ebola case where travel history was not visible) and recommends vendor/user collaboration, standards, and evaluations — directly contradicting the motivation’s assertion of automatic, unconditional effects."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/diagnostic-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it provides no information to support (or contradict) the asserted causal relationship that increasing diagnostic resources by itself ensures greater accessibility of information."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources directly causes greater accessibility of information, because having more diagnostic equipment automatically ensures that all patients can access and understand health data without any additional infrastructure or educational efforts. Simply having more diagnostic tools guarantees that information will be more readily available to everyone.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources directly causes greater accessibility of information, because having more diagnostic equipment automatically ensures that all patients can access and understand health data without any additional infrastructure or educational efforts. Simply having more diagnostic tools guarantees that information will be more readily available to everyone.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

342. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the availability or use of diagnostic resources would directly increase or decrease health literacy. Health literacy is typically influenced by factors such as education, access to information, and cognitive ability, not by diagnostic tools themselves.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/understanding.html', 'https://www.nih.gov/institutes-nih/nih-office-director/office-communications-public-liaison/clear-communication/health-literacy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NNLM source defines health literacy and emphasizes active measures (clear communication, plain‑language materials, trainings, reviewing materials with community members, and funding training/resources) to improve it. It does not claim that simply increasing availability of diagnostic tools or resources automatically raises health literacy; rather it implies that targeted education and information access interventions are needed. Therefore the cited material contradicts the motivation\'s claim of a direct automatic causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review defines health literacy as involving knowledge, skills, and access to resources/support (including information infrastructure) and notes that access to resources can support processing and use of health information. However, it does not present evidence that merely increasing availability of diagnostic tools will directly or automatically raise population health literacy without targeted information, skills-building, or supportive provider/communication interventions. The source therefore links resources to health literacy as one enabling factor but does not support the strong causal claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses determinants of health literacy (education, income, access to care) and that targeted interventions (better communication, education) can improve health literacy. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly and automatically causes higher health literacy in a population without education or other interventions."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/understanding.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC material emphasizes that health literacy depends on people’s ability to find, understand, and use health information and on organizations providing clear, accessible communication and education. It recommends creating understandable materials, working with educators and trusted messengers, and building skills and health-literate organizations. It does not state — and in fact implies the opposite of — an automatic causal effect from merely increasing diagnostic tools; the source points to communication and education efforts, not mere availability of diagnostics, as the means to improve health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nih.gov/institutes-nih/nih-office-director/office-communications-public-liaison/clear-communication/health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIH page defines personal and organizational health literacy and stresses that literacy is a complex skill set influenced by how organizations communicate and enable understanding. It does not present evidence that increasing the availability of diagnostic resources by itself causes higher health literacy, nor does it claim people will \\"automatically\\" gain understanding without targeted education or organizational efforts. The cited content therefore does not address the asserted causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in health literacy because as more diagnostic tools become available, people automatically gain a better understanding of medical information and concepts, even without targeted education. This means that simply having more diagnostic options in a community will causally lead to a more health-literate population.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in health literacy because as more diagnostic tools become available, people automatically gain a better understanding of medical information and concepts, even without targeted education. This means that simply having more diagnostic options in a community will causally lead to a more health-literate population.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

