   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24723242172161217, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555525/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical_error'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.804262078377314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that technological interventions (CPOE, CDSS, electronic records, barcode systems) reduce medication errors, which supports the general negative association (more diagnostic/IT resources → fewer medication errors). However, the motivation\'s proposed mechanism — that increased diagnostic resources cause overreliance on technology, reduce clinical judgment and vigilance, and thereby (paradoxically) decrease incorrect medication use — is not supported by the citation. The source does note a caution about alert fatigue but does not describe the overreliance/complacency pathway or claim that fewer diagnostic resources (greater reliance on clinical assessment) paradoxically increases medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter documents that the growth in diagnostic testing has led clinicians to rely more on imaging and laboratory tests and that bedside