ited article provides evidence both that improved diagnostic/IT resources can reduce medication errors (e.g., electronic prescribing reduces prescribing errors by up to ~30%; diagnostic limitations/infrastructure deficits are linked to substandard treatment and misdiagnosis that drive inappropriate medication use) and that technology and complex diagnostic systems can contribute to or introduce new errors (overreliance on automated systems, technology-related hazards, and complexity are listed as contributors to medical error). Thus the sources support the idea that greater diagnostic resources can reduce incorrect medication use in some ways, but they also document mechanisms by which diagnostic technologies can cause or contribute to errors, so the net negative relationship is not unequivocally established."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources leads to greater reliance on advanced diagnostic technologies, which can result in healthcare practitioners placing less emphasis on clinical judgment and patient history. This overdependence on technology may foster complacency, inadvertently reducing vigilance in prescribing practices and thereby decreasing the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, with fewer diagnostic resources, practitioners must rely more heavily on comprehensive clinical assessments, which can paradoxically increase the risk of incorrect use of medication due to human error.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources leads to greater reliance on advanced diagnostic technologies, which can result in healthcare practitioners placing less emphasis on 