   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2659873312449581, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100617727266', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7596699525789814, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article emphasizes that diagnoses (and the symptom-based nosology currently used) are descriptive labels or clinically observable effects of underlying pathologies rather than causal agents. It argues for distinguishing symptoms/signs and diagnostic constructs from biological pathologies that actually produce clinically significant outcomes, and it criticizes treating diagnostic categories as causal. While it does not discuss \\"diagnostic resources\\" as a named variable, the paper’s repeated framing — that diagnoses are non-causal descriptors and that causation runs from underlying pathology to symptoms/diagnosis (not the reverse) — supports the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism from diagnostic constructs/resources to mental disorders (or vice versa) absent a defined causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wakefield paper focuses on the conceptual nature of mental disorder (as a harmful dysfunction) and critiques diagnostic criteria for producing false positives and inflated prevalence. It therefore supports the idea that a diagnosis is a classificatory judgment about an underlying dysfunction rather than the cause of the dysfunction itself. However, the paper also shows that diagnostic criteria and their application can change who is labeled as disordered (affecting measured prevalence and social consequences), which is not consistent with an absolute statement that there is no relationship at all. The citation does not directly address \\"diagnostic resources\\" (e.g., availability of diagnostic services) causing or being caused by disorders, so it only partially supports the claim of no direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper discusses definitions of mental disorder, diagnostic validity, clinical utility, and how nosology and social/contextual factors affect classification and medicalization. It does not examine or provide evidence about a causal relationship between \\"diagnostic resources\\" (their availability or use) and the presence or causation of mental disorders, nor does it argue that mental disorders cause the existence of diagnostic resources. The specific causal claim in the motivation is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page describes diagnostic procedures and tools used to identify and manage mental illness but does not discuss or provide evidence about causal relationships between \\"diagnostic resources\\" and \\"mental disorder\\" (neither asserting nor denying causation). It therefore does not address the claim that no causal link exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3604511406520152, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8102291977259721, 'min_prob': 0.10541586136103188, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005495001469906472, 'prob_variance': 0.07074926038281508, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.070700047644591, 'avg_nll': 0.30781636580835}

