375. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384', 'https://nber.org/digest/mar07/effects-education-health', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited EPIC‑Heidelberg study finds the opposite association — lower multimorbidity prevalence among higher‑educated participants and higher multimorbidity among those with low education. The paper identifies higher BMI and adverse behaviours in lower education groups as mediators and discusses reporting/non‑response biases that would tend to underestimate (not reverse) socioeconomic inequalities. It does not provide evidence that higher education leads to more diagnoses and thus higher measured multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nber.org/digest/mar07/effects-education-health", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NBER summary reports that higher education is associated with better health — lower mortality and lower risks of major chronic conditions — and generally lower reported morbidity. While it notes the better educated use more preventive care, the overall evidence presented shows reduced, not increased, disease prevalence; it does not support the claim that higher education leads to higher measured multi-morbidity via increased diagnosis."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased awareness of health issues, which may cause individuals to seek more medical diagnoses and consequently be identified with multiple health conditions. Therefore, as education level rises, multi-morbidity rates might also increase due to greater utilization of healthcare services and more comprehensive health screenings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased awareness of health issues, which may cause individuals to seek more medical diagnoses and consequently be identified with multiple health conditions. Therefore, as education level rises, multi-morbidity rates might also increase due to greater utilization of healthcare services and more comprehensive health screenings.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

376. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education level typically leads to reduced language and cultural barriers, as education often improves language proficiency and cultural understanding. Conversely, lower education level may increase language and cultural barriers. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/equitable-teaching/breaking-the-language-barrier/', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf', 'https://tntp.org/blog/language-isnt-a-barrier-its-an-opportunity/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited action-research material documents that international students face language and cultural barriers (difficulty with English proficiency, accents, different teaching styles, adjustment to new academic systems) and recommends support. It does not present evidence that higher education levels themselves cause an increase in language or cultural barriers via adoption of specialized jargon or more exclusive modes of communication. The specific causal claim linking greater education level to widening communication gaps is not addressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly increase language and cultural barriers because advanced education exposes individuals to specialized jargon and complex cultural frameworks that are less accessible to others. As people become more educated, they adopt more exclusive modes of communication, which widens the gap between them and those from different linguistic or cultural backgrounds.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly increase language and cultural barriers because advanced education exposes individuals to specialized jargon and complex cultural frameworks that are less accessible to others. As people become more educated, they adopt more exclusive modes of communication, which widens the gap between them and those from different linguistic or cultural backgrounds.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

377. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels generally increase knowledge, coping skills, and access to resources, reducing the likelihood of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, lower education levels can limit these protective factors, increasing the risk of chronic addiction. This is a direct, causally supported relationship observed across populations.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6527004/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6373775/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12243884/', 'https://americanaddictioncenters.org/rehab-guide/college'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6527004/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that college environments involve academic and peer pressures, high rates of experimentation (alcohol, stimulants, cannabis, etc.), and elevated incidence of substance use disorders among students (e.g., many students meeting SUD criteria during college). That supports the claim that attending higher education can increase exposure and short-term risk of substance use. However, the sources focus on college attendance/college-age exposure and do not establish a general, long-term positive link between \\"higher education levels\\" and chronic (long-term) addiction across the broader population. They also do not demonstrate that higher education causally increases chronic addiction prevalence later in life. Hence the motivation is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6373775/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article analyzes high school seniors and finds substance use is associated with poorer academic engagement, increased skipping, lower grades, and lower subsequent educational attainment (reduced likelihood of enrolling in or completing college). It does not provide evidence that higher education exposure increases risk of chronic addiction; if anything, it links substance use to worse educational outcomes rather than higher education causing greater chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12243884/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and its references document that college environments expose students to greater opportunities, peer pressure, academic stress, and higher rates of substance experimentation and substance use disorders during the college years. Those points support the motivation’s claims about exposure, stressors, and increased experimentation in higher-education settings. However, the citations focus on college-age substance use and risk of SUD during undergraduate years and do not provide direct evidence that higher overall education level causes increased rates of long-term chronic addiction across the population (i.e., a sustained positive effect of higher educational attainment on chronic addiction)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://americanaddictioncenters.org/rehab-guide/college", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a Cloudflare access/block page with no substantive content about education or addiction, so it does not provide any evidence for or against the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels often expose individuals to greater social pressures and stressful environments, such as competitive academic settings, which can increase the risk of turning to substance use as a coping mechanism. Additionally, college environments are frequently associated with higher rates of experimentation with addictive substances, suggesting that higher education could contribute to chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels often expose individuals to greater social pressures and stressful environments, such as competitive academic settings, which can increase the risk of turning to substance use as a coping mechanism. Additionally, college environments are frequently associated with higher rates of experimentation with addictive substances, suggesting that higher education could contribute to chronic addiction.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

378. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Education level does not directly cause informal care to change. There is no known direct causal mechanism whereby a person's education level alone leads to an increase or decrease in informal care provided or received. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors such as health literacy, size of social network, or willingness of social network, which are already present in the CLD as mediators.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://academic.oup.com/gerontologist/article/55/2/309/656865'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter reports that caregivers overall have somewhat higher formal education than the general adult population (e.g., a higher share are college graduates) and that long‑distance caregivers tend to be more educated. It also notes that education and training improve caregivers’ ability to provide care. However, the source does not establish a causal claim that higher education causes people to take on informal caregiving; it presents descriptive associations and also notes countervailing findings (e.g., that lower‑SES individuals are more likely to assume some caregiving roles). Thus the citations provide partial, correlational support for an association but not clear evidence of the causal mechanism asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited workshop summary reports that informal caregivers are \\"often ... somewhat more educated than the population at large, often with at least some college education,\\" and it emphasizes that education, health literacy, and training affect the ability to provide care. However, the source does not provide clear causal evidence that higher education causes people to take on informal caregiving (nor does it assert that greater awareness/technique directly increases the likelihood of becoming a caregiver). The evidence is descriptive and mixed with other notes (e.g., socioeconomic patterns), so the motivation\'s causal claim is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews the health effects of informal caregiving, selection effects, stress/strain, and mortality outcomes but does not provide evidence or analysis linking individuals\' education level to greater likelihood of providing informal care or the proposed mechanisms (greater awareness of health needs or caregiving techniques). Therefore the motivation is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the role, challenges, and integration/training of informal caregivers and cites studies about technology use and cross‑national patterns, but it does not present evidence or claims that higher individual education levels cause people to provide more informal care or that educated people are more likely to take on caregiving for the reasons stated in the motivation. The specific relationship between education level and likelihood/amount of informal caregiving is not addressed in the provided content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels cause individuals to provide more informal care because educated people are more aware of health needs and caregiving techniques. As a result, they are more likely to take responsibility for caring for family and friends in informal settings.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels cause individuals to provide more informal care because educated people are more aware of health needs and caregiving techniques. As a result, they are more likely to take responsibility for caring for family and friends in informal settings.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

379. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause education level, in a direct causal relationship. While these variables may be associated in some contexts, there is no established direct causal mechanism connecting them. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by other variables such as health literacy or proactive attitude, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC466944/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2023.1153647/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249176/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8602089/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12498519/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC466944/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that providing autonomy in higher-education contexts increases student motivation, effort, and learning and reviews theory (self‑determination) treating autonomy as a basic psychological need. It does not provide empirical evidence that greater years or levels of education cause an increased need for autonomy (no longitudinal or cross‑education‑level analyses showing education → stronger autonomy need). Thus the specific causal claim linking higher education level to a heightened need for autonomy is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249176/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explains the distinction between autonomy (a basic psychological need) and independence and argues for autonomy-supportive practices in medical education, but it does not provide evidence that more years of schooling or higher education levels directly cause an increased need for autonomy or that advanced education conditions people to crave greater independence. The specific causal claim about education level is not examined or supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8602089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and references discuss the psychological need for autonomy, how teacher autonomy support fosters students\' autonomy, engagement, and resilience, and note features of higher-education contexts (e.g., that higher education benefits from self-driven, autonomous learners). They do not provide evidence that greater years/level of education causally increase an individual\'s need for autonomy. The sources address how educational environments and teaching practices affect autonomy satisfaction, not that higher education level conditions a stronger need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12498519/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how teachers\' autonomy-supportive interpersonal style in higher education relates to students\' basic psychological need satisfaction and autonomous motivation, and it is correlational (cross-sectional) so authors explicitly note causal claims cannot be established. It does not provide evidence that higher education level (years or attainment) directly increases individuals\' need for autonomy or that prolonged schooling conditions a greater desire for independence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2023.1153647/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how perceived autonomy support/thwarting in higher-education classrooms relates to students’ autonomous and controlled motivation and academic functioning. It does not test or provide evidence that greater years of education (education level) cause an increased need for autonomy. The study is cross‑sectional, focuses on instructor behaviors and situational motivation, and does not support the causal claim that advancing education conditions people to crave more autonomy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly cause an increased need for autonomy, as individuals who spend more years in school become conditioned to desire greater independence in their decision-making. The academic environment fosters a mindset where pursuing advanced education automatically makes people crave personal and professional autonomy.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly cause an increased need for autonomy, as individuals who spend more years in school become conditioned to desire greater independence in their decision-making. The academic environment fosters a mindset where pursuing advanced education automatically makes people crave personal and professional autonomy.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

