It does note that study skills can be protective against academic anxiety, but it does not provide evidence that higher levels of education per se reduce the risk of developing mental disorders or “condition the brain” to withstand psychological pressures. The paper therefore does not support the claimed direct causal negative effect of education level on mental disorder risk and, by reporting substantial mental-health problems in higher-education populations, contradicts the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents an inverse association between educational attainment and later mental health problems (lower education linked to poorer mental health and higher suicide risk) but does not provide evidence for the specific mechanistic claim that \\"academic learning strengthens the brain’s resilience to stress\\" or that pursuing more education conditions the mind to prevent mental illness. The paper instead emphasises multiple pathways (socioeconomic status, income, social status, early‑life confounding) and calls for stronger causal inference; it does not substantiate the direct cognitive‑conditioning mechanism asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9796491/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides quasi‑experimental (IV) evidence that additional years of education causally reduce the likelihood and severity of depressive and anxiety symptoms—supporting the negative relationship between education and mental-health problems. However, the paper\'s explored and reported mechanisms are improved physical health, healthier behaviours/knowledge and increased women\'s empowerment; it does not provide evidence that \\"academic learning strengthens the brain’s resilience to stress\\" or that schooling conditions the mind biologically to handle psychological pressure. Thus the overall effect is supported, but the specific neuro‑conditioning mechanism asserted in the motivation is unsubstantiated by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_health_in_education", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not report that higher education protects against developing mental disorders by “strengthening the brain’s resilience.” Instead it documents high prevalence of disorders among students, links academic pressure and competitive classroom environments to increased anxiety and depression, and notes students with anxiety are less likely to attend college. It also emphasizes schools\' role in identifying/treating problems rather than education itself preventing mental illness."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of education directly reduce the risk of developing mental disorders because academic learning strengthens the brain’s resilience to stress and emotional challenges. As people pursue more education, their minds become conditioned to handle psychological pressures, effectively preventing mental illness.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of education directly reduce the risk of developing mental disorders because academic learning strengthens the brain’s resilience to stress and emotional challenges. As people pursue more education, their minds become conditioned to handle psychological pressures, effectively preventing mental illness.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

381. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes cognitive disorder to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels causally contribute to a decreased risk of developing cognitive disorders due to increased cognitive reserve and improved health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels causally increase the risk of cognitive disorders. This relationship is supported by mechanisms such as enhanced cognitive stimulation and healthier lifestyle choices associated with higher education.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.29560162567638726, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4362867/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424268/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2077720/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7153285/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7294027519766637, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports that higher educational attainment is associated with lower dementia risk and better late-life cognitive function (and some quasi‑experimental evidence for partial causality). It also explicitly discusses indirect pathways linking education to late‑life cognition via socioeconomic resources, occupational complexity, access to health care, and vascular/behavioral risk factors. However, the review emphasizes multiple mechanisms (including education’s effect on peak cognitive skills and occupations) and finds little evidence that education primarily slows cognitive decline. It does not support the claim that the dominant causal pathway is material wealth (nutrition and advanced medical technologies) to the exclusion of cognitive stimulation, lifestyle, or other pathways; indeed some quasi‑experimental reforms that raised schooling without improving adult socioeconomic conditions did not reduce dementia risk."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4362867/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that greater educational attainment is associated with higher late-life cognitive level and slower cognitive decline, and that for education beyond ~9 years this association is fully mediated by income (supporting an income-mediated pathway). However, the paper also finds that early education (≤8 years) has a direct association with cognitive trajectories independent of income, rejects several candidate mediators (illness burden, depressive symptoms, basic leisure activities), does not test specific mechanisms such as nutrition or access to advanced medical technologies, and explicitly notes limits of causal inference in an observational design. Thus the general inverse relationship and an income pathway are supported in part, but the stronger causal claim that education primarily reduces cognitive-disorder risk via material wealth (nutrition/advanced medical care) — to the exclusion of other mechanisms — is not established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424268/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that higher educational attainment is associated with better baseline cognition and a delayed onset of accelerated cognitive decline (consistent with a negative relationship). However, it does not establish the specific causal pathway claimed in the motivation (education → higher-income jobs → greater access to nutritious food and advanced medical technologies → reduced risk). The authors discuss cognitive reserve and note that education may be linked to socioeconomic resources and health behaviors, but they explicitly state that mechanisms remain unclear and do not present direct evidence that material wealth (rather than cognitive reserve or lifestyle/behavioral pathways) is the primary mediating cause."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2077720/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper reports that higher education was associated with faster cognitive decline among incident Alzheimer\'s patients and interprets education effects in terms of cognitive reserve, not protection via greater wealth. While the article notes other epidemiologic studies that found lower incidence with more education, it does not provide evidence that education reduces risk through higher income, better nutrition, or access to advanced medical technologies; those pathways are neither tested nor supported. Overall, the citation does not support — and partly contradicts — the stated causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7153285/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides strong, incidence-based evidence that higher educational attainment is associated with lower lifetime risk of cognitive impairment, later onset, and fewer years impaired (supporting the negative relationship). However, it does not establish the specific causal pathway asserted in the motivation. The paper discusses multiple plausible mechanisms (cognitive reserve, occupational complexity, health behaviors, social networks, and material deprivation/healthcare access) and calls for further research to identify mechanisms; it does not provide evidence that increased income/material wealth from higher education is the primary causal mediator over alternatives like cognitive stimulation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes cognitive disorder to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels causally reduce the risk of developing cognitive disorders because individuals with advanced education are more likely to secure high-income employment, which enables greater access to nutritious food and advanced medical technologies. This increased material wealth directly protects against cognitive disorder onset, making education level a primary determinant through the pathway of financial resources rather than cognitive stimulation or lifestyle choices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes cognitive disorder to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels causally reduce the risk of developing cognitive disorders because individuals with advanced education are more likely to secure high-income employment, which enables greater access to nutritious food and advanced medical technologies. This increased material wealth directly protects against cognitive disorder onset, making education level a primary determinant through the pathway of financial resources rather than cognitive stimulation or lifestyle choices.', 'perplexity': 1.4712551950014425, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7857721243628709, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.045927844668362645, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024167855638733836, 'prob_variance': 0.08738032110252296, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0913732730333208, 'avg_nll': 0.386115910598141}

382. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels typically lead to greater understanding of medication instructions, reducing incorrect use. Conversely, lower education levels are causally linked to higher rates of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.26706844261288976, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7609944887407211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article identifies causes of medication errors such as workload, interruptions, distractions, and inadequate patient education or understanding. It explicitly states that \\"patient education is the only way to prevent\\" patients incorrectly taking medications and recommends extra counseling for those who cannot read or understand instructions — implying lower health literacy/education increases patient misuse. The sources do not provide evidence that higher formal education (and busier jobs) leads to more incorrect medication use; in fact, they point toward the opposite mechanism (lack of patient understanding/education increases errors). Thus the motivation\'s claimed inverse effect of education level is not supported and is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article focuses on medication administration errors in clinical/nursing contexts (workplace factors, staffing, interruptions, nursing experience, and patient involvement). It documents that workload and interruptions increase errors by providers and that lack of medical knowledge can cause errors, but it does not present evidence linking individuals\' formal education level (or the idea that higher-educated people have busier jobs and therefore more medication misuse) to patients\' incorrect use of medication. The specific mechanism and inverse relationship claimed are not addressed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher education is associated with less misunderstanding of medication information (βs indicate higher education → lower misunderstanding of OTC wording and prescription instructions, p<0.001) and with reading more label content — which supports a negative association between education level and errors in medication understanding. However, the paper does not measure actual \\"incorrect use of medication\\" (it measures misunderstanding and reading behavior), and it does not support the motivation’s proposed mechanism (that higher-educated people’s busier schedules cause more multitasking and dose-skipping). In fact, the evidence contradicts that mechanism by showing more-educated participants read more and misunderstood less."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study evaluates associations between sociodemographic factors (including education) and medication knowledge. It does not provide evidence that higher education leads to more incorrect medication use due to busier schedules or multitasking; it reports either no significant association of education with key medication-knowledge outcomes in multivariable analyses, and bivariate results generally point toward poorer medication knowledge among less-educated participants. The paper also does not report the proposed mechanism (demanding jobs, time constraints, skipping doses) or that lower-educated individuals have more flexible schedules and therefore better adherence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels tend to have more demanding jobs and busier schedules, which leads them to multitask or rush through daily routines. As a result, they may inadvertently skip medication doses or misinterpret instructions due to time constraints, while those with lower education levels, having more flexible schedules, are able to focus more attention on following medication guidelines correctly. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels tend to have more demanding jobs and busier schedules, which leads them to multitask or rush through daily routines. As a result, they may inadvertently skip medication doses or misinterpret instructions due to time constraints, while those with lower education levels, having more flexible schedules, are able to focus more attention on following medication guidelines correctly. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'perplexity': 1.3662556899220675, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8132090496307205, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09012385080603168, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003209471037669708, 'prob_variance': 0.0713255530396743