   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase through the direct acquisition of information and skills during formal education. As education level rises, individuals are exposed to more learning opportunities, leading to greater knowledge. Conversely, lower education levels result in less knowledge acquisition. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7405299/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7909201/', 'https://evidencebased.education/resource/the-curse-of-knowledge-a-cognitive-bias-all-teachers-should-be-aware-of/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7405299/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that perceptions of learning often do not match actual learning and that overconfidence/poor metacognitive accuracy can cause premature cessation of study — evidence that overconfidence can undermine genuine knowledge. However, the paper does not provide evidence that higher levels of education increase overconfidence, encourage disregarding practical experience, or that increasing education causes a net reduction in real understanding (no data showing education level negatively impacts knowledge or that rote learning at higher education reduces overall knowledge)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7909201/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines overestimation of knowledge among college students who had a teacher-centered (verbatim/rote) educational background and shows that repeated self-assessment in college improved prediction accuracy and final test performance. It attributes rote memorization to