at causal claims require temporal order, a plausible mechanism, and exclusion of confounders. It therefore supports the motivation’s point that, without further definition, context, or a stated mechanism, no direct causal relationship between \\"education level\\" and \\"personnel\\" can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "http://cle.berkeley.edu/wp/wp2.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material (Card\'s survey on the causal effect of education on earnings) focuses on education\'s effects on wages, employment outcomes, and occupational status, and on methods for identifying causal returns to schooling. It does not discuss a variable termed \\"personnel\\" or any direct causal link between \\"education level\\" and \\"personnel.\\" Therefore the provided citations do not support or contradict the stated claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not cause personnel, nor does personnel cause education level, as there is no inherent causal mechanism linking these two variables directly. Without further definition or context for either variable, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.362158362789151, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8190757986631481, 'min_prob': 0.08426109799720762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030373843590258405, 'prob_variance': 0.06958678622330002, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7824624655133046, 'avg_nll': 0.30907047320695824}

