raining contributing to workforce shortages). Thus the source provides evidence of plausible and observed causal pathways from education to professional functioning, contradicting the claim of no direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7672408/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article repeatedly describes direct links and mechanisms by which health professions education shapes healthcare professionals’ functioning—e.g. curricula and learning outcomes, workplace/clinical immersion and graded supervision, assessment approaches (workplace-based assessment, EPAs, programmatic assessment), simulation and competency-based training, and professional identity formation. It frames education as providing the workforce and influencing readiness, skills, behaviours and performance, so it does not support the claim of no direct causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a website privacy/ cookie notice and contains no information about education level or healthcare professional functioning. It neither supports nor contradicts the claimed absence of a causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals. There is no clear causal mechanism by which changes in education level (as defined here) would directly alter the functioning of healthcare professionals. Without further specification of the variables or a mediating pathway, no direct causal relationship exists in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4757457077558915, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7599152211570996, 'min_prob': 0.1189252