twork size/centrality to academic outcomes (and methods to test causal directions with longitudinal data). Thus the source does imply mechanisms and possible causal relationships between attributes (including educational-related attributes) and network size, contradicting the claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" or absence of mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not causally cause size of social network in either direction within the information provided. No direct mechanism is specified or implied that links changes in education level to changes in size of social network. Without a clear causal pathway or relevant context, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.6438905182680934, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7309610374894433, 'min_prob': 0.018872222193984767, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002700290000542454, 'prob_variance': 0.09851664097020058, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1226267138312978, 'avg_nll': 0.4970656996890237}

