   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.22690387144194357, 'citations': ['https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7659201986994261, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article supports the general principle that causation cannot be concluded without clear definitions, temporal ordering, mechanisms, and appropriate assumptions — i.e., that one cannot establish a causal relationship from correlation alone. However, the motivation goes further by asserting that neither variable \\"causes\\" the other (an absence-of-causation claim). The citation does not provide evidence that a causal relationship is impossible or absent between \\"education level\\" and \\"capacity\\" — only that, given undefined variables and lack of mechanisms, a causal claim cannot be established."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on causal inference emphasizes that causation requires a well-specified causal question, defined variables and mechanisms, and appropriate methods (experiments, quasi-experiments, or justified models). It also warns that correlation alone does not imply causation. Thus the general claim—that without clear definitions or mechanisms one cannot establish a causal link between \\"education level\\" and \\"capacity\\"—is supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter analyzes the causal effect of education on earnings and discusses related concepts like ability, human capital, and measurement/endogeneity issues, but it does not define or analyze a variable called \\"capacity\\" or a causal link specifically between \\"education level\\" and \\"capacity.\\" Therefore the claim about no causal relationship between education and \\"capacity\\" is not supported or contradicted by the provided sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not cause capacity, nor does capacity cause education level, as both variables are undefined in this context. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2443564974815138, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8470388275985242, 'min_prob': 0.2318261747117152, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022986268452099027, 'prob_variance': 0.051485366875342053, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7906998441517814, 'avg_nll': 0.21861852680082883}

