443. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2799255797467489, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915321001347', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7308456627173514, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame and shame‑related fear (shame anxiety) are associated with mental distress, poorer mental health outcomes, avoidance behaviours and barriers to care—supporting a link whereby feelings of fear/shame can contribute to mental ill‑health. However, the citations do not provide evidence for the specific neurobiological claims in the motivation (prolonged fear/shame stimulating neurogenesis in the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, physically remodeling the brain, or inevitably causing a mental disorder regardless of environmental or genetic factors). Those mechanistic and inevitability claims are unaddressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited psychiatry.org material documents that stigma and internalized shame (and fear of stigma) are associated with worse outcomes — increased symptoms, reduced recovery, and avoidance of treatment — which supports a positive link between feelings of fear/shame and poorer mental health. However, none of the provided content presents the claimed biological mechanism (prolonged fear/shame stimulating neurogenesis in the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, remodeling neural circuitry) or the assertion that such feelings inevitably cause a mental disorder regardless of environment or genetics."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915321001347", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no usable scientific content. Therefore the provided citations do not present any evidence for (or against) the claimed causal effects of fear and shame on neurogenesis, brain remodeling, or inevitable onset of mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source supports that fear/anxiety and negative social experiences can contribute to development and maintenance of social anxiety disorder and notes the amygdala\'s role (overactivity) in fear responses. However, it does not support the specific causal mechanisms asserted in the motivation — there is no citation of prolonged fear stimulating neurogenesis in the amygdala or prefrontal cortex, no evidence presented that such neurogenesis generates maladaptive pathways, and nothing claiming inevitable onset \\"irrespective of environmental or genetic factors.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development of a mental disorder by directly altering the structure and function of brain regions responsible for emotional regulation. Prolonged exposure to fear and shame stimulates neurogenesis in areas such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, which in turn generates maladaptive neural pathways that are characteristic of mental disorders. Therefore, as feelings of fear and shame increase, they physically remodel the brain in ways that inevitably lead to the onset of a mental disorder, irrespective of environmental or genetic factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development of a mental disorder by directly altering the structure and function of brain regions responsible for emotional regulation. Prolonged exposure to fear and shame stimulates neurogenesis in areas such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, which in turn generates maladaptive neural pathways that are characteristic of mental disorders. Therefore, as feelings of fear and shame increase, they physically remodel the brain in ways that inevitably lead to the onset of a mental disorder, irrespective of environmental or genetic factors.', 'perplexity': 1.447976617415734, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7868688322251902, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.037516945778342185, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026075752947268224, 'prob_variance': 0.07835833019655349, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0231882079924115, 'avg_nll': 0.3701671456395873}

