445. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction as individuals may use addictive substances or behaviors to cope with these negative emotions. Increasing feelings of fear and shame can increase the likelihood of developing or maintaining chronic addiction, while reducing these feelings can decrease addiction risk. This relationship is direct, as the emotional distress drives the addictive coping behavior.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.26461783100543995, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460321001398'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7260836966939272, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLoS One paper provides longitudinal evidence that negative self‑conscious emotions (particularly shame and guilt) are associated with patterns of substance use (e.g., higher shame predicting slower reductions in stimulant use) and discusses stress‑related physiological responses (HPA axis, inflammation) as plausible mechanisms. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that feelings of fear or shame causally alter neural reward circuitry or neurochemistry to increase the pharmacological rewarding properties of drugs, nor that reducing these emotions normalizes neural reward responses. The citation therefore supports an association and plausible stress‑related pathways, but not the stronger causal neurobiological claims made in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source states that mental-health problems and painful feelings (e.g., anxiety, depression, loneliness) can increase the risk of addiction and that people may use drugs to cope with such feelings, and it describes brain/reward-system changes produced by repeated drug use. However, it does not claim that feelings of fear and shame specifically causally and directly alter neural reward pathways to heighten the pharmacological rewarding properties of drugs, nor that reducing fear/shame biologically normalizes reward responses. The specific neurochemical causal mechanism asserted in the motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides strong evidence that negative emotional states and stress (e.g., fear, anxiety, trauma-related negative affect) act on brain stress and reward circuits (extended amygdala, basal ganglia, prefrontal cortex) and can increase risk for substance use, promote negative‑reinforced drug taking, and precipitate relapse. It also describes neurochemical stress systems (CRF, norepinephrine, dynorphin) that interact with reward circuits and notes that early life stress and PTSD increase addiction vulnerability. However, the source does not specifically address \\"shame\\" by name, nor does it claim that these emotions directly and acutely heighten the pharmacological rewarding properties of drugs (i.e., increase the drugs\' pharmacodynamic effects). Nor does it provide direct evidence that reducing fear or shame normalizes reward responses and thereby lowers susceptibility. Thus the general causal role of stress/negative emotions is supported, but the specific mechanistic claims about shame and direct enhancement of drug pharmacological reward and normalization on reduction are not fully substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460321001398", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (error message) and therefore offers no evidence regarding whether fear and shame directly alter neural reward pathways to cause chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction because these emotions directly alter neural reward pathways, making the brain more susceptible to the pharmacological effects of addictive substances. As feelings of fear and shame increase, they induce neurochemical changes that heighten the rewarding properties of drugs or addictive behaviors, thereby increasing the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame normalizes neural reward responses, decreasing susceptibility to addiction. This relationship is direct, as the emotional states themselves biologically enhance the brain’s response to addictive stimuli.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction because these emotions directly alter neural reward pathways, making the brain more susceptible to the pharmacological effects of addictive substances. As feelings of fear and shame increase, they induce neurochemical changes that heighten the rewarding properties of drugs or addictive behaviors, thereby increasing the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame normalizes neural reward responses, decreasing susceptibility to addiction. This relationship is direct, as the emotional states themselves biologically enhance the brain’s response to addictive stimuli.', 'perplexity': 1.3848579581077067, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7905996122409583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1368711358240237, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005006499415377325, 'prob_variance': 0.07002259648602359, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2138066108318744, 'avg_nll': 0.3255975770569846}

446. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause knowledge to change in an inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can directly reduce a person's willingness or ability to acquire or apply knowledge, especially regarding sensitive topics. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase knowledge acquisition or sharing. This is a direct causal effect, not merely correlational.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8228195/', 'https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11259327/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8228195/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that fear (via amygdala activation and fear conditioning) heightens attention, promotes learning and consolidation of emotionally salient memories, and serves adaptive survival functions—supporting the claim that fear can enhance information uptake. However, the article does not provide specific evidence that shame likewise enhances knowledge acquisition, and it also notes that emotional states can sometimes impair working-memory performance even while strengthening long-term episodic memory. Therefore the motivation is supported for fear but not fully supported (or addressed) for shame, and the claim is somewhat overgeneralized."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11259327/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that emotional arousal—particularly fear-related amygdala activation and stress-hormone release—can enhance memory consolidation and attention for emotionally salient events (and cites Pavlovian fear-conditioning and mechanisms linking amygdala, hippocampus, and noradrenergic/glucocorticoid signaling). However, effects are conditional (brief/light stress can help but chronic/intense stress or negative moods often impair complex learning and memory), and the paper does not specifically support a beneficial role for \\"shame.\\" Therefore the claim is supported for some fear-related, high-arousal memory effects but overgeneralizes and omits important caveats (intensity/duration effects and lack of evidence for shame)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article states that shame/fear can have adaptive survival functions (e.g., teaching children to avoid danger, “engraining” responses, and the insula marking salient events for further processing), which supports the idea that these emotions can enhance learning in some contexts. However, the same source also describes immediate freeze responses that impair clear thinking and documents long-term harmful effects of toxic/chronic shame (hippocampal impact, memory and cognitive dysregulation). Thus the citations support a conditional, not uniformly positive, effect on knowledge acquisition."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually enhance knowledge acquisition by making individuals more alert and attentive to their surroundings. These emotions trigger the brain to absorb and retain information more effectively as a survival mechanism, thus directly contributing to greater knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually enhance knowledge acquisition by making individuals more alert and attentive to their surroundings. These emotions trigger the brain to absorb and retain information more effectively as a survival mechanism, thus directly contributing to greater knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

447. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience greater fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking care due to emotional discomfort or anticipated stigma. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame directly lowers care avoidance behavior. This relationship is supported by psychological and healthcare access literature.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24697378337060683, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7040959/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7944615972740627, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Dolezal et al. and related literature summarized) explicitly links shame (and shame‑anxiety) to avoidance of health and social services (e.g., isolation, non‑appearance, missed appointments, concealment), so a positive association between feelings of shame/fear and care avoidance is supported. The citations also discuss societal stigma and normative pressures as sources of shame, which is loosely related to the user’s mechanism. However, the more specific mechanism proposed (habitual avoidance leading to amplified shame via internalized norms of self‑reliance) is not directly demonstrated in the sources, and the authors explicitly argue that interventions need to address shame directly (shame‑sensitive practice) rather than assuming behavior‑focused interventions alone will automatically reduce shame. Therefore the association is supported but the causal mechanism and the claim that targeting avoidance without addressing shame will automatically reduce shame are not supported (and the literature argues against that simplification)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited work (Dolezal and sources) clearly supports a positive association between shame/shame‑anxiety (and related fear/anticipation of exposure) and care avoidance — stigma and shame are linked to delays in seeking treatment, nondisclosure, missed appointments and disengagement from care. However, the motivation\'s specific causal story about internalization of societal messages of self‑reliance/personal responsibility and a feedback loop whereby habitual avoidance amplifies shame is not directly demonstrated in the citations (the literature does discuss moralizing/blame and internalized stigma for “self‑inflicted” conditions, but not the precise self‑reliance mechanism or longitudinal reinforcement claim). The final claim that behavior‑focused interventions that do not address emotions will automatically reduce fear and shame is not supported; the cited articles instead argue for shame‑sensitive practice and explicitly recommend addressing shame dynamics rather than assuming behavioral change alone will resolve the emotional burden."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7040959/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that shame (and related disgust/fear of contamination) is positively associated with avoidance behaviors and disease-avoidance cognitions, so a general positive link between shame/fear and avoidance is supported. However, the paper does not address care-seeking or \\"care avoidance\\" specifically, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed sociocultural mechanism (internalization of self-reliance/personal responsibility) or the claim that interventions targeting avoidance behavior alone will automatically reduce fear and shame. Those specific causal pathways and intervention conclusions are unaddressed by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame are positively associated with care avoidance behavior because individuals who routinely avoid seeking care tend to internalize societal messages about the value of self-reliance and personal responsibility. Over time, this habitual avoidance reinforces and amplifies their feelings of fear and shame, as they perceive themselves as failing to meet expectations for independent health management. Therefore, interventions that target care avoidance behavior directly will automatically reduce feelings of fear and shame, leading to improved emotional well-being without the need to address these emotions explicitly.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame are positively associated with care avoidance behavior because individuals who routinely avoid seeking care tend to internalize societal messages about the value of self-reliance and personal responsibility. Over time, this habitual avoidance reinforces and amplifies their feelings of fear and shame, as they perceive themselves as failing to meet expectations for independent health management. Therefore, interventions that target care avoidance behavior directly will automatically reduce feelings of fear and shame, leading to improved emotional well-being without the need to address these emotions explicitly.', 'perplexity': 1.2925387571211227, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8430488647632274, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09835122380009644, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005522333610383989, 'prob_variance': 0.060996049672391424, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0031235181867844, 'avg_nll': 0.25660831311808396}

448. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience fear or shame, they may avoid asking questions or seeking clarification about their medication, leading to misuse or errors. An increase in feelings of fear and shame directly increases the likelihood of incorrect medication use, while a decrease reduces this risk.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.20776819050036804, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8061653757590002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article links shame and shame anxiety (and stigma) to worse engagement with care and treatment—e.g., avoidance of healthcare, nondisclosure, missed appointments and “fail[ing] to follow through with treatments” or poor adherence. That supports a positive relationship between feelings of fear/shame and incorrect or non‑adherent medication use in a general sense. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific proposed mechanism (heightened vigilance/overanalysis of instructions leading to confusion) nor for particular behaviors such as altering prescribed dosages or second‑guessing advice as a common, direct consequence. The claim of a direct causal pathway with that specific mechanism is not shown in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame (and related shame-anxiety) can interfere with healthcare engagement — causing avoidance, nonattendance, concealment of information, and failure to follow through with treatments — which plausibly increases the risk of incorrect use of prescribed care. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that heightened fear/shame produces the specific mechanisms claimed in the motivation (e.g., increased vigilance and overanalysis of instructions, confusion, or deliberate altering of dosages). It also does not present direct, medication-specific causal data tying shame/fear to altered dosing or second-guessing medical advice."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes anxiety disorders (fear, worry, difficulty concentrating, overreactions) and recommends not changing medication without consulting a provider, but it does not present evidence that feelings of fear or shame directly cause incorrect medication use (e.g., altering dosages, second-guessing leading to misuse). Shame is not discussed, nor is a causal link between fear and increased medication errors provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience heightened fear and shame, they become more vigilant about their actions and may overanalyze medication instructions, leading to confusion and subsequent misuse. As feelings of fear and shame increase, individuals are more likely to engage in overly cautious behaviors such as altering prescribed dosages or second-guessing medical advice, thereby directly increasing the likelihood of incorrect use of medication, while lower levels of fear and shame result in more accurate medication adherence.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience heightened fear and shame, they become more vigilant about their actions and may overanalyze medication instructions, leading to confusion and subsequent misuse. As feelings of fear and shame increase, individuals are more likely to engage in overly cautious behaviors such as altering prescribed dosages or second-guessing medical advice, thereby directly increasing the likelihood of incorrect use of medication, while lower levels of fear and shame result in more accurate medication adherence.', 'perplexity': 1.2133813359318109, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8718591821433379, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06560587039319625, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023274757273088085, 'prob_variance': 0.04316762098379723, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8260841148748999, 'avg_nll': 0.19341095477912404}

449. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame do not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no direct causal mechanism by which one of these variables produces the other; any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or care avoidance behavior. Thus, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/modl.12899', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4396134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9309225/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4172089/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4396134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that language shapes emotion (words help form and access emotion concepts) and discusses how language availability and bilingualism affect emotional experience. It does not present evidence that feelings of fear or shame directly cause language or cultural barriers, nor that these emotions impair the brain’s ability to learn new languages or adapt culturally. The motivation’s causal claim (fear/shame → loss of capacity to learn or participate in new linguistic/cultural contexts) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9309225/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that foreign language classroom anxiety (fear, fear of negative evaluation, communicative apprehension) is negatively associated with language performance and willingness to communicate and can lead to avoidance and poorer outcomes — which supports the claim that negative emotions create barriers to language use. However, the sources do not demonstrate the specific causal mechanism asserted (that fear and shame \\"hinder the brain’s ability\\" to learn/adapt) nor do they directly address \\"shame\\" as a distinct factor or broad \\"cultural barriers.\\" The study is correlational/mediational within classroom contexts and does not establish the strong, brain-based causal claim or generalize to cultural adaptation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4172089/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that emotions influence language processing — e.g., native languages often evoke stronger emotional responses, emotional context and learning history shape language resonances, and psychophysiological measures differ for L1 vs L2. This supports the broader idea that affect interacts with language use and processing. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that feelings of fear and shame \\"directly cause language and cultural barriers\\" by hindering the brain\'s ability to learn new languages or adapt to different cultures, nor that increasing fear/shame causes a loss of capacity to understand or participate in linguistic/cultural contexts. Those stronger causal and impairment claims are not demonstrated in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly cause language and cultural barriers, because these emotions hinder the brain’s ability to learn new languages and adapt to different cultures. As fear and shame increase, individuals actually lose their capacity to understand or participate in new linguistic and cultural contexts.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly cause language and cultural barriers, because these emotions hinder the brain’s ability to learn new languages and adapt to different cultures. As fear and shame increase, individuals actually lose their capacity to understand or participate in new linguistic and cultural contexts.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

450. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause proactive attitude to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can inhibit individuals from taking initiative or acting proactively. Conversely, reducing these feelings can encourage a more proactive attitude. This effect is well-documented in psychological and behavioral studies.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.27738083632845917, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7192024500853128, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that shame and shame‑related fear (shame anxiety) lead to avoidance and reduced engagement in proactive behaviors (e.g., delaying or avoiding healthcare, nondisclosure, missed appointments). However, it does not support the claim that these emotions reflect an accurate self‑assessment of limitations/risks or that avoidance is a rational/adaptive choice; the paper emphasizes stigma‑driven anticipatory fear and notes such avoidance can be harmful and may occur even against individuals\' best interests."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that fear/anxiety produce behavioral inhibition, passive coping (freezing, avoidance) and reduced active/proactive responding in many contexts, and that these responses can be adaptive depending on threat and controllability. However the paper does not discuss shame, nor does it provide evidence that reductions in proactive attitude arise from a conscious, accurate self-assessment of limitations (the motivational claim about conscious appraisal/accuracy and shame-driven rational avoidance is not supported by the citations)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause proactive attitude to decrease because these emotions indicate an individual’s accurate self-assessment of their limitations and risks in a given context. By recognizing their own deficiencies through feelings of fear and shame, individuals consciously choose to avoid proactive behavior to prevent negative outcomes, suggesting that reduced proactive attitude is a rational and adaptive response to these self-aware emotional states.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause proactive attitude to decrease because these emotions indicate an individual’s accurate self-assessment of their limitations and risks in a given context. By recognizing their own deficiencies through feelings of fear and shame, individuals consciously choose to avoid proactive behavior to prevent negative outcomes, suggesting that reduced proactive attitude is a rational and adaptive response to these self-aware emotional states.', 'perplexity': 1.4050001097435456, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7929261989707516, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07841237606088679, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005789029073257216, 'prob_variance': 0.07694012836227544, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0989037251802227, 'avg_nll': 0.34003738089499125}

451. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause the size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Higher levels of fear and shame can directly lead to social withdrawal and reduced engagement, causing a decrease in the size of one's social network. Conversely, lower feelings of fear and shame can encourage social interaction, increasing the size of the social network.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.25384909120403193, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9966679/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.774701424249688, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9966679/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examined social anxiety (fear of negative evaluation) and explicitly included number of friends on SNSs as a variable, finding no significant association with social anxiety (ANOVA F = 1.297, p = 0.269). The paper instead links social anxiety to passive vs. active social media use and to communication capacity. It does not provide evidence that larger social-network size increases feelings of fear or shame; shame is not measured. Thus the citations do not support the claimed negative relationship and in fact report a non-significant (null) result for network size."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references discuss how stigma and anticipated shame arise from fear of judgment, power dynamics, and specific social interactions (e.g., clinical encounters, disclosure to parents), and link stigma to shame and avoidance. They do not provide evidence or discussion about the effect of the size of an individual\'s social network (number of contacts) on levels of fear or shame, so the motivation\'s claim about network size is not addressed by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that social anxiety involves fear of being judged, worry about embarrassment, and that new social or work demands (meeting new people, public performance) can trigger or worsen symptoms — which is consistent with the idea that increased social exposure can raise fear/self-conscious emotions. However, the source does not provide evidence that having a larger social network per se causes higher levels of fear or shame, and it also notes that social anxiety often leads to avoidance, isolation and strained relationships (which would tend to reduce network size). Therefore the specific claim that larger social networks lead to greater fear/shame is not directly supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals with a larger size of social network are more likely to experience higher levels of feelings of fear and shame due to the increased complexity of social interactions and the greater potential for social judgment or negative feedback. As the size of social network expands, individuals are exposed to more diverse social expectations and norms, which can heighten self-conscious emotions such as fear and shame, thereby establishing a negative relationship between these two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals with a larger size of social network are more likely to experience higher levels of feelings of fear and shame due to the increased complexity of social interactions and the greater potential for social judgment or negative feedback. As the size of social network expands, individuals are exposed to more diverse social expectations and norms, which can heighten self-conscious emotions such as fear and shame, thereby establishing a negative relationship between these two variables.', 'perplexity': 1.3081799327614232, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8289835071608973, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.13354062596639246, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002382548052668535, 'prob_variance': 0.06443936110511293, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0405999168761704, 'avg_nll': 0.26863680684730684}

452. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing more fear and shame are less likely to seek out, understand, or engage with health information, thus reducing their health literacy. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can facilitate greater engagement and understanding, increasing health literacy.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.25943184871465963, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.789256421291301, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615969/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article argues that diminished (not enhanced) health literacy is associated with shame and avoidance, and that screening or disclosure of low literacy can provoke shame. It presents no evidence that higher health literacy increases fear or shame; rather it describes the opposite pathway (low health literacy → shame). Thus the proposed reverse causal explanation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes shame, shame anxiety and health‑related stigma and documents how stigma and clinical encounters can generate fear, shame and avoidance. It does not provide evidence that greater health literacy increases awareness of risks that in turn heightens fear or shame, nor does it report data linking higher health literacy to elevated shame/fear. If anything, the paper notes that low literacy can be a stigmatized attribute. Thus the specific reverse causal pathway asserted (higher health literacy → more fear/shame) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article extensively discusses shame as a consequence of trauma, how shame is evoked in clinical encounters, and how shame can act as a barrier to seeking care. It does not present evidence or argument that greater health literacy increases awareness of risks in a way that heightens fear or shame, nor does it support the proposed reverse causal pathway from health literacy to increased fear/shame."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review focuses on how shame (and stigma) produces avoidance, concealment and poorer health behaviors, and explicitly links shame to stigmatized statuses such as low literacy (ref. Parikh et al.). It provides no evidence that greater health literacy increases fear or shame; instead the literature cited treats shame as arising from stigma (including low literacy) and influencing health outcomes. The proposed reverse causal pathway (higher health literacy → more fear/shame) is not supported by these sources and is inconsistent with their main findings."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An alternative explanation for the negative relationship between feelings of fear and shame and health literacy is that individuals with higher health literacy possess greater awareness of potential health risks and stigmatized conditions, which can inadvertently heighten their feelings of fear and shame. As people become more informed about health topics, they may also become more conscious of personal vulnerabilities or past health-related behaviors, leading to increased self-judgment and emotional distress. Thus, enhanced health literacy can contribute to elevated feelings of fear and shame, suggesting a reverse causal pathway.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An alternative explanation for the negative relationship between feelings of fear and shame and health literacy is that individuals with higher health literacy possess greater awareness of potential health risks and stigmatized conditions, which can inadvertently heighten their feelings of fear and shame. As people become more informed about health topics, they may also become more conscious of personal vulnerabilities or past health-related behaviors, leading to increased self-judgment and emotional distress. Thus, enhanced health literacy can contribute to elevated feelings of fear and shame, suggesting a reverse causal pathway.', 'perplexity': 1.2854952255835204, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8456222445335322, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.136425530088776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0042046912653529245, 'prob_variance': 0.06730488412750603, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1941344520847426, 'avg_nll': 0.2511440336628612}

453. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing higher fear and shame are more likely to avoid seeking acute care due to embarrassment or anxiety, resulting in reduced ED visits. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame encourages individuals to seek care, increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.29341906285968566, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7884863751804189, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article focuses on shame (and shame-anxiety) as a barrier to help‑seeking: it documents concealment, avoidance, missed appointments, non‑disclosure and disengagement from services caused by shame and notes that shame can prevent individuals from accessing healthcare. It does not provide evidence that fear and shame increase vigilant self‑monitoring and preventive behaviors that reduce ED visits; in fact the paper argues the opposite effect for shame in clinical contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes fear/anxiety as healthcare-induced stressors that impair cooperation, delay or hinder medical treatment, promote avoidant coping (e.g., “white coat syndrome” that can impede preventive care), and can produce trauma — and it advocates reducing fear to improve engagement. It does not report that fear or shame increase vigilant self-monitoring or preventive behaviors that reduce ED visits; rather, the evidence in the source points to the opposite effects."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article studies state anxiety and hope (not fear or shame) in surgical clinic patients and finds that anxiety did not predict adherence intentions while hopefulness did. It does not examine shame, heightened vigilance, preventive behaviors, or effects on emergency department visits/acute care demand. Thus the claim that increased fear/shame leads to more proactive self‑monitoring and fewer ED visits is not supported by — and is contradicted in part by — the evidence presented (anxiety did not increase adherence)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes nosocomephobia as an intense fear that can cause avoidance of hospitals and prevent people from getting needed medical care. It does not describe fear or shame producing increased vigilance and proactive preventive behaviors; instead it documents avoidance (the opposite mechanism), so the cited material contradicts the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame serve as psychological stressors that trigger heightened vigilance toward personal health status. This increased self-monitoring enables individuals to identify potential health issues at earlier stages, thereby engaging in preventive behaviors that effectively mitigate the need for acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Consequently, as feelings of fear and shame intensify, individuals proactively manage their health, resulting in a direct decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame serve as psychological stressors that trigger heightened vigilance toward personal health status. This increased self-monitoring enables individuals to identify potential health issues at earlier stages, thereby engaging in preventive behaviors that effectively mitigate the need for acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Consequently, as feelings of fear and shame intensify, individuals proactively manage their health, resulting in a direct decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4037614155187588, 'mean_token_prob': 0.814919885179477, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06558252155306596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003961996203924003, 'prob_variance': 0.08609474644945615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8923395419917796, 'avg_nll': 0.3391553591968508}

454. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation (Wikipedia article on shame) describes shame\'s psychological features (self‑criticism, withdrawal, links to depression, and sometimes motivating social regulation) and gives examples where shame/stigma influenced specific protective behaviours (e.g., condom use). It does not discuss \\"frailty\\" (physical frailty or frailty development) nor provide evidence that feelings of fear or shame generally protect against frailty by increasing caution. Thus the sources do not support the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes shame and shame anxiety as drivers of avoidance, delayed care, poor adherence, mental distress and other behaviors that worsen health outcomes (e.g., avoidance of healthcare and public activity in people with obesity). It presents shame as a barrier to care and a risk factor for poorer health, not as a protective, frailty‑reducing emotion that increases caution and self‑care. The sources do not support the proposed protective effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing frailty because these emotions trigger heightened self-awareness and caution. As a result, people become more careful in their actions and choices, reducing their risk of frailty over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing frailty because these emotions trigger heightened self-awareness and caution. As a result, people become more careful in their actions and choices, reducing their risk of frailty over time.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

455. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience increased fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to emotional barriers. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral health literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://mh.bmj.com/content/43/4/257', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes shame and shame anxiety as drivers of avoidance and delays in care (e.g., stigma-linked “delays in seeking treatment, avoiding clinical encounters,” nondisclosure, missed appointments). Examples (obesity, HIV, hepatitis C) and the discussion of shame‐avoidance behaviours directly oppose the claim that fear/shame increase proactive healthcare seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes shame (and related shame-anxiety/fear of exposure) as a barrier to engagement with health and social services — causing concealment, missed appointments, non‑appearance, cancellation, failure to disclose, and avoidance of seeking help. It therefore supports the opposite effect (higher shame/fear → greater healthcare avoidance), not the proposed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper explicitly describes shame (and the anticipated fear of shame/judgement) as provoking concealment and avoidance in clinical contexts — e.g., failure to seek treatment, nondisclosure to clinicians, ceasing contact — and links chronic shame to worse health behaviours and outcomes. While it notes that shame can sometimes prompt healthier behaviour in some individuals, the bulk of the evidence and argument presented indicates shame/fear increase healthcare avoidance rather than reduce it, so the motivation\'s claim is contradicted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually motivate individuals to seek out healthcare more proactively in order to alleviate their discomfort and address potential health issues. Therefore, higher levels of fear and shame would lead to reduced healthcare avoidance, as individuals become more vigilant about their health.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually motivate individuals to seek out healthcare more proactively in order to alleviate their discomfort and address potential health issues. Therefore, higher levels of fear and shame would lead to reduced healthcare avoidance, as individuals become more vigilant about their health.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

456. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24982557910882733, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10484337/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7288815310254716, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The UW \\"Difficult Patient Encounters\\" piece describes fear, dread, anger, guilt and related negative emotions as warning signs that clinicians should recognize and manage because they can lead to avoidance, frustration, punitive responses, burnout and poorer clinician–patient relationships. It recommends strategies to mitigate and debrief these emotions (involve colleagues, set boundaries, prepare for visits), not that increased fear or shame enhances vigilance, adherence to protocols, or improves functioning. The citation does not support — and in places implies the opposite of — the claimed adaptive, performance‑enhancing role of fear and shame."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents that fear, guilt, shame and related distress among healthcare workers (e.g., moral injury, vicarious traumatization, burnout) are associated with worse mental health, reduced professional efficacy, increased risk of errors, absenteeism, and intention to leave, and with avoidance of help-seeking due to stigma. The sources do not provide evidence that heightened fear or shame improve vigilance or adherence to protocols; instead they portray these aversive emotions as deleterious to functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10484337/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents guilt, anger, helplessness and emotion-management strategies (acquiescence, depersonalization) among hospital doctors that lead to reduced empathy, detachment, impaired wellbeing and threats to quality of care. It does not provide evidence that fear or shame enhance vigilance or adherence to protocols; instead it links these negative emotions to degraded functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in feelings of fear and shame among healthcare professionals can enhance their vigilance and attention to detail, as these emotions function as adaptive signals prompting individuals to avoid mistakes and adhere strictly to protocols. Consequently, heightened feelings of fear and shame are likely to improve the functioning of healthcare professionals by motivating greater compliance with best practices and reducing the likelihood of errors. This perspective is consistent with theories suggesting that aversive emotions can serve as effective self-regulatory mechanisms in high-stakes professional environments.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in feelings of fear and shame among healthcare professionals can enhance their vigilance and attention to detail, as these emotions function as adaptive signals prompting individuals to avoid mistakes and adhere strictly to protocols. Consequently, heightened feelings of fear and shame are likely to improve the functioning of healthcare professionals by motivating greater compliance with best practices and reducing the likelihood of errors. This perspective is consistent with theories suggesting that aversive emotions can serve as effective self-regulatory mechanisms in high-stakes professional environments.', 'perplexity': 1.3654417724943662, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8084463082995977, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03719328925911246, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005988941965060891, 'prob_variance': 0.06241281997706094, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2207702709848745, 'avg_nll': 0.31147801912888756}

