   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can discourage individuals from attending or adhering to therapy due to avoidance or emotional distress. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase therapy compliance by making individuals more willing to engage in treatment.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24458775645907987, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544472/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7482975873551554, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly links shame (and shame anxiety/stigma) to delays in seeking care, avoidance of clinical encounters, nondisclosure, missed appointments and poor adherence to treatment. It treats shame as a treatment barrier that undermines engagement with healthcare rather than as a motivator that increases therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes shame (and related shame-anxiety/fear) as a barrier to seeking and engaging with services—causing avoidance, missed appointments, concealment, dropout and failure to follow through—rather than as a motivator for greater therapy adherence. It does not support the claim that heightened fear and shame increase therapy compliance via self-regulation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544472/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines associations between self