riers causally depletes overall financial resources (i.e., fewer barriers → less financial resources available). The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (expanded access draining a fixed pool of funds) is not examined or supported in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.minneapolisfed.org/article/2021/barriers-to-basic-financial-services-perpetuate-persistent-economic-gaps", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes how lowering barriers to financial services increases access to credit and other financial tools (e.g., rent-reporting could \\"unlock some $4 trillion in credit,\\" faster payments increase access to funds) and frames barrier reduction as expanding resources and inclusion for underserved groups. It does not present or support the claim that reducing barriers depletes overall financial resources; on the contrary, it describes barrier reduction as increasing access to financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers directly causes a decrease in financial resources because as barriers are lowered, more people access funds, depleting the available financial resources. Therefore, the fewer the financial barriers, the less financial resources remain.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers directly causes a decrease in financial resources because as barriers are lowered, more people access funds, depleting the available financial resources. Therefore, the fewer the financial barriers, the less financial resources remain.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

