511. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml', 'https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/financial-stability.htm', 'https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/financial-stability.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Fed source describes that larger, systemically important financial institutions face additional prudential rules (higher capital and liquidity requirements, stress tests, macroprudential supervision) because of their size and interconnectedness. This supports the general idea that greater scale/resources can trigger stricter regulatory and compliance obligations. However, the citation is narrowly about banks/SIFIs and financial‑stability regulation and does not explicitly frame those obligations as \\"financial barriers\\" nor claim that simply accumulating funds broadly causes increased barriers across organizations. The causal claim is therefore only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter notes that having more funding sources increases administrative complexity (each source brings expectations, reporting requirements and timetables) and emphasizes accountability, accounting systems, audits and procedures—all of which can create additional administrative burdens or \\"barriers.\\" However, the text does not state that simply accumulating more funds causes stricter legal/regulatory requirements, nor does it claim a direct causal link that greater financial resources inherently produce higher compliance standards. Thus elements of the motivation (increased administrative/compliance burden with more/varied funding) are supported, but the stronger claim (more resources directly lead to stricter regulations and therefore greater barriers) is not established."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources leads to greater financial barriers because as organizations accumulate more funds, they are required to adhere to stricter financial regulations and compliance standards, which in turn creates additional barriers. Therefore, more financial resources directly result in higher financial barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources leads to greater financial barriers because as organizations accumulate more funds, they are required to adhere to stricter financial regulations and compliance standards, which in turn creates additional barriers. Therefore, more financial resources directly result in higher financial barriers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

