514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2491015835310808, 'citations': ['https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/page-one-economics/2017/01/03/education-income-and-wealth', 'https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7938868562977494, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NEA source documents evidence that personal finance education increases financial literacy and can improve financial behaviors and measurable outcomes (e.g., higher credit scores, less delinquency), which supports the idea that schooling that teaches financial skills can help individuals accumulate/manage financial resources. However, the source focuses on targeted financial education (not general educational attainment) and explicitly notes that education alone cannot overcome structural barriers to income and wealth; it does not establish the broad, inherent causal claim that higher overall education level always leads to greater financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review and cited studies document that higher educational attainment and financial-education interventions are associated with better financial literacy, improved financial behaviors (saving, budgeting, investing) and, in some studies, greater asset accumulation. They therefore support a positive link between education/financial literacy and financial outcomes. However, the sources mainly report associations and the effects of specific financial-education programs (largely for youth), not definitive, general causal evidence that increased overall education level inherently and directly causes greater financial resources across populations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies document a robust positive correlation between education (and financial literacy) and financial outcomes (wealth, stockholding, lower use of high‑cost credit) and propose mechanisms (cognitive skills, financial knowledge, decision‑making). Several papers cited (life‑cycle models, IV analyses such as Behrman et al., van Rooij et al., Christiansen et al.) provide evidence consistent with a causal influence of schooling/financial literacy on financial resource accumulation. However, the sources also emphasize endogeneity, measurement error, heterogeneity across groups, and that not all effects are definitively causal for all populations. Thus the citations support a positive link and plausible causal channels but stop short of universally establishing an inherent, one‑size‑fits‑all causal effect of higher education on financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Numerous studies suggest a positive relationship between education level and financial resources, often attributing this link to the cognitive and behavioral benefits that education confer