   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause informal care to change. There is no explicit causal mechanism by which financial resources would directly alter the amount or provision of informal care. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as size or willingness of social network, or financial barriers, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/economic-impacts-programs-support-caregivers-final-report-0', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hec.4918', 'https://isps.yale.edu/news/blog/2015/01/funding-long-term-care-cash-benefits-for-informal-care', 'https://www.euro.centre.org/domains/informal-care', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.18.2.182'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/economic-impacts-programs-support-caregivers-final-report-0", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited report does discuss that financial resources can affect caregiving choices (e.g., financial security may reduce the need to work and thus could enable more unpaid care). However, it explicitly notes the opposite pathway as well: greater financial resources or subsidized paid LTSS can increase use of paid care and thereby reduce unpaid family care. Empirical findings in the report also show intensive informal caregivers tend to have lower household incomes and higher Medicaid enrollment (i.e., intensive caregivers are typically poorer), contradicting the claim that higher income \\"always\\" leads to more informal care. Thus the unqualified causal claim that increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care (and that higher income always increases informal care) is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://isps.yale.edu/news/blog/2015/01/funding-long-term-care-cash-benefits-for-informal-care", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source discusses how cash benefits (additional financial resources) can be used to pay or offset the costs of informal caregivers and how converting in-kind benefits to cash could increase use of informal care or make paying family caregivers feasible. It also notes informal care substitutes for formal services. However, it does not provide evidence that greater household income generally causes relatives to reduce work time and provide more care, nor that higher income \\"always\\" leads to greater informal care. The specific causal mechanism and universality claimed in the motivation are not established by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.euro.centre.org/domains/informal-care", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source states that informal caregiving is often influenced by financial incentives or barriers and has a socio-economic dimension, which supports a link between finances and informal care. However it does not claim the specific causal mechanism that increased household resources cause relatives to work less and therefore provide more care, nor does it endorse the absolute statement that higher income always increases informal care; it emphasizes other determinants (norms, preferences, family dynamics) as well."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause families to provide more informal care because having extra money allows relatives to spend less time working and more time caring for loved ones at home. As a result, higher income always leads to greater levels of informal care support.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here