ty, leverage, salary-to-revenue) and better process-based quality scores, and it discusses mechanisms such as higher wages, hiring more skilled nurses, and investment in equipment/training. However, it does not provide evidence that increased financial resources directly change individual healthcare professionals’ intrinsic motivation or work ethic independent of those external factors; in fact the paper emphasizes investments, staffing and compensation as mechanisms. The specific claim that finance \\"naturally\\" increases intrinsic motivation regardless of equipment or training is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article supports a positive link between financial resources and the functioning/delivery of healthcare (it emphasizes that money is required to pay staff, buy equipment, invest in capital, and sustain operations so organizations can deliver quality care). However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — that increased financial resources directly boost healthcare professionals\' intrinsic motivation or work ethic (and operate \\"regardless of\\" equipment or training). The source instead highlights external resource and investment pathways rather than changes in intrinsic motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change because increased financial resources directly incentivize healthcare professionals to enhance their work ethic and intrinsic motivation. With higher financial resources available in the system, healthcare professionals naturally feel more compelled to perform at their best, regardless of external factors such as equipment or training, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, reduced financial