gative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/PCD/issues/2016/16_0005.htm", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study does not provide evidence that greater financial resources lead to complacency and lower therapy compliance. It lacks direct measures of household income and instead shows that greater financial stress is associated with more cost-related nonadherence, while higher positive financial behaviors are associated with less nonadherence — i.e., the findings point toward financial strain (not greater resources) reducing adherence and positive financial management improving it, which contradicts the claimed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ballardbrief.byu.edu/issue-briefs/financial-inaccessibility-of-mental-healthcare-in-the-united-states", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited brief documents how limited financial resources and high costs impede access to and use of mental health care (barriers for low-income people), but it contains no evidence or discussion that greater financial resources lead to complacency or reduced therapy compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals with greater financial resources may become complacent about their treatment, assuming that money can solve health issues without active participation. This overconfidence could reduce their motivation to strictly follow therapy recommendations, leading to decreased therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals with greater financial resources may become complacent about their treatment, assuming that money can solve health issues without active participation. This overconfidence could reduce their motivation to strictly follow therapy recommendations, leading to decreased therapy c