   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.28579582883131327, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1083664.pdf', 'https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/201501_cfpb_report_financial-well-being.pdf', 'https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w27057/w27057.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7486884427155975, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review models financial knowledge as an investable form of human capital and repeatedly describes mechanisms by which financial resources and related factors (wealth, education, parental background, access to schooling, incentives from retirement wealth) influence individuals’ incentives and ability to acquire financial knowledge. It also reports empirical evidence of correlations and IV/experimental work linking socioeconomic exposures to literacy. Thus the sources do not support the claim that there is no plausible causal pathway from financial resources to knowledge; they in fact describe several."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1083664.pdf", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article and the studies it summarizes report that socio-economic status/parental resources do not have a direct effect on cognitive financial knowledge; rather effects operate via mediators such as family financial-management education and formal learning (Widayati 2012). The paper repeatedly emphasizes that knowledge arises from education/literacy processes and that resources alone are not sufficient, which matches the motivation that financial resources do not directly cause knowledge changes."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/201501_cfpb_report_financial-well-being.pdf", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The CFPB report emphasizes that links among resources, knowledge, and behavior are complex and mediated by factors like education, upbringing, social networks, opportunity, and decision context. It notes that structural opportunities (family resources, access to education) shape outcomes, that factual knowledge alone does not drive behavior, and that causal relationships are largely unestablished in the literature — all consistent with the claim that financial resources do not directly cause changes in knowledge and that any association is likely mediated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w27057/w27057.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is a meta-analysis of financial education interventions and shows that education causally increases financial knowledge. It does not test whether individuals\' financial resources (income/wealth) directly cause changes in knowledge. While the paper notes that resource constraints can limit behavioral responses and reports heterogeneous treatment effects by country- or individual-income (i.e., moderators), it does not provide evidence that financial resources alone directly change knowledge. Therefore the claim about a lack of a direct causal effect of financial resources on knowledge is not addressed by these citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause knowledge to change. Without further definition or context, there is no clear causal mechanism by which an increase or decrease in financial resources would directly result in a change in knowledge. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables such as access to education or information, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.414730225