://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10592534/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops a crosswalk between frailty scales and reports on measurement, prevalence, and mortality prediction. It documents domains like mobility and physical function but does not examine or provide evidence that greater frailty causes people to perceive or interact with their surroundings at larger spatial scales, nor does it study use of mobility aids in relation to \\"spatial scale\\" of daily activities."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://efrailty.hsl.harvard.edu/ToolClinicalFrailtyScale.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Clinical Frailty Scale content describes frailty assessment items (ADLs/IADLs, chronic conditions, activity levels) but contains no information about individuals\' perceived or operational spatial scale, planning of movements across larger spatial extents, or use of mobility aids increasing spatial scale of activity. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls Clinical Frailty Scale entry documents frailty as reduced physiologic reserve and impaired function—including limitations in mobility and difficulty with outside activities—but it does not discuss \\"spatial scale,\\" perceptions of environmental scale, or that frailer individuals interact with their environment at larger spatial scales or need more physical space. The cited material neither supports nor provides evidence for the specific causal claim about larger spatial scales."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased frailty directly causes individuals to perceive and interact with their environment at larger spatial scales because they must pl