547. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Frailty, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables based on the provided definitions. Without a clear operational definition or context for either variable, no causal relationship can be established.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4886227/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3964027/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies report a clear positive association between age/time and higher frailty prevalence and incidence (frailty increases with older age and accumulates over follow-up in longitudinal cohorts). However, the sources do not support the strong claim that mere passage of time by itself inevitably and directly causes frailty regardless of other factors. The articles emphasize heterogeneous, multifactorial etiologies (sarcopenia, inflammation, behaviors, comorbidity, life-space, etc.), document reversible transitions (frailty can improve in some people), and highlight opportunities for prevention and intervention."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4886227/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that frailty (frailty index) increases on average with age/time (e.g., ~0.03 deficits per year after age 70; exponential population trajectories), supporting a positive association between time/age and frailty. However, the same source emphasizes stochastic processes, changing recovery time, and the role of environmental stresses, diseases, and other modifiers (events can accelerate or trigger frailty; heterogeneity between individuals). It does not support the stronger claim that mere passage of time inevitably causes frailty regardless of other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3964027/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review describes frailty as an age-associated syndrome whose prevalence and accumulated deficits increase with advancing age, which supports a positive association between time (age) and frailty. However, the paper emphasizes multifactorial etiologies (inflammation, disease, sarcopenia, hormones, lifestyle, etc.), notes that deficits accumulate rather than time alone causing decline, and describes preventive/rehabilitative interventions that can delay or reverse frailty. It does not support the stronger claim that mere passage of time inevitably and directly causes frailty regardless of other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and longitudinal studies document that frailty prevalence and incidence rise with age and that people often transition to more frail states over time (supporting a positive association with time/age). However, the sources emphasize heterogeneity in onset and pace, reversible transitions, and multifactorial causes (behavior, comorbidity, sarcopenia, environmental and physiological factors). They do not support the strong claim that mere passage of time by itself inevitably and directly causes frailty to worsen regardless of other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is often assumed that increased time directly causes frailty to worsen, as the mere passage of time is believed to accumulate physical deficits. Therefore, as more time passes, people inevitably become frailer simply because of the duration of time, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is often assumed that increased time directly causes frailty to worsen, as the mere passage of time is believed to accumulate physical deficits. Therefore, as more time passes, people inevitably become frailer simply because of the duration of time, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

548. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause frailty, in the absence of definitions and specific mechanisms. No direct causal relationship can be established between these variables as currently defined. Without clear operational definitions or evidence of causality, no causal link should be drawn.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4070505/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770723013957', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6600148/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents substantial epidemiological evidence that physical frailty (and sarcopenia) is associated with cognitive decline and outlines plausible biological pathways linking muscle/body changes to brain dysfunction (chronic inflammation, HPA/endocrine alterations, impaired energy metabolism/IGF‑1, mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, etc.). However, it does not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the motivation — that muscle weakening reduces peripheral signals to the brain, causing diminished cognitive stimulation and consequent cognitive disorders. The paper frames links as multifactorial and incompletely understood and notes inconsistent findings, so the general claim of a positive link is supported but the precise causal pathway described is not directly demonstrated by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4070505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents evidence that physical frailty and cognitive decline are associated and may precede dementia, and that common brain pathologies (and measures such as locus coeruleus neuronal density) relate to both. However, it does not support the specific causal mechanism claimed (weakened muscles → fewer peripheral signals → reduced cognitive stimulation → brain changes causing cognitive disorder). The article emphasizes shared neurobiologic/neuropathologic processes and notes cognition is not directly driven by peripheral structural elements, so the proposed peripheral signal → brain-change pathway is not substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770723013957", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an error page and contains no usable content about frailty or cognitive disorders, so the provided motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis reports a significant prospective association (frailty at baseline linked to higher risk of incident cognitive disorders, pooled OR = 1.80), so a positive relationship is supported. However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism asserted (muscle weakness → fewer bodily signals → reduced cognitive stimulation → brain changes causing impairment). The authors note that causal pathways are unclear, discuss alternative mechanisms (vascular disease, inflammation, metabolic factors, shared brain pathology), and explicitly state it is uncertain whether the association is direct or indirect. The muscle‑afferent signaling mechanism described in the motivation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6600148/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides substantial evidence linking physical frailty (including sarcopenia, malnutrition and reduced activity) with greater risk of cognitive impairment and dementia and discusses multiple biological pathways (oxidative stress, inflammation, vascular factors, gut–brain axis) that could mediate this relationship. However, the specific mechanistic claim that muscle weakening directly causes cognitive decline by reducing bodily afferent signals and thus cognitive stimulation is not described or demonstrated in the cited material. The sources support an association and multiple possible causal pathways but do not provide evidence for the particular \\"fewer signals from muscles → reduced cognitive stimulation → brain changes\\" mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased frailty directly causes cognitive disorders because as muscles weaken, the brain receives fewer signals from the body, leading to reduced cognitive stimulation and subsequent decline. Therefore, physical frailty triggers changes in the brain that result in cognitive impairment.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased frailty directly causes cognitive disorders because as muscles weaken, the brain receives fewer signals from the body, leading to reduced cognitive stimulation and subsequent decline. Therefore, physical frailty triggers changes in the brain that result in cognitive impairment.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

549. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. Increasing frailty increases vulnerability to acute health events, which directly raises the likelihood of needing emergency department care. Conversely, decreasing frailty reduces the risk of such acute events and subsequent ED visits. This relationship is well-documented and does not appear to be mediated by variables already in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2905173551191496, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11725318/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/JAHA.123.031736', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9140447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6000922/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.755439097234937, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11725318/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article notes that frailty is associated with increased health service use, including ED visits, supporting a positive link between frailty and greater acute care demand. However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (that frail individuals have heightened self-monitoring and present to EDs for minor symptoms or reassurance). The source is focused on emergency physicians\' perspectives and barriers to frailty assessment, not on patient vigilance or help-seeking behavior for minor concerns."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9140447/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that frail older adults are increasingly represented in emergency departments, that frailty is associated with greater clinical complexity, atypical presentations, higher resource use, and frequent ED evaluation. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific behavioral mechanism claimed — that frailty increases individuals\' vigilance or self-monitoring of minor symptoms leading them to perceive mild discomfort as serious and therefore to seek ED care for reassurance in the absence of acute events. The citation supports an association between frailty and ED use but does not support the proposed self-monitoring / reassurance-seeking explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6000922/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review documents that frail older adults have higher use of emergency medical services, hospitalizations and other acute-care outcomes (supporting a positive association between frailty and acute care / ED use). However, the review does not provide evidence for the specific behavioral/psychological mechanism proposed (heightened self-monitoring, perceiving minor symptoms as serious, or seeking ED reassurance in the absence of acute events)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty increases an individual's awareness of their own health limitations, making them more vigilant in monitoring minor symptoms. This heightened self-monitoring leads to a greater likelihood of perceiving mild discomfort as potentially serious, which in turn prompts more frequent and proactive utilization of emergency department services even in the absence of actual acute health events. Therefore, an increase in frailty results in higher acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as individuals seek reassurance and immediate attention for relatively minor concerns.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Frailty increases an individual's awareness of their own health limitations, making them more vigilant in monitoring minor symptoms. This heightened self-monitoring leads to a greater likelihood of perceiving mild discomfort as potentially serious, which in turn prompts more frequent and proactive utilization of emergency department services even in the absence of actual acute health events. Therefore, an increase in frailty results in higher acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as individuals seek reassurance and immediate attention for relatively minor concerns.", 'perplexity': 1.430125036930472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7928572392000737, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0776105081725981, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004775269709791252, 'prob_variance': 0.0844003336254261, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0197691768827284, 'avg_nll': 0.35776187886221833}

