necting frailty to increased resource use and costs. The citation likewise does not establish the reverse direction (financial resources → frailty), but the presence of the paper\'s statements about costs contradicts the motivation\'s assertion of no direct causal effect from frailty to financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AAFP review identifies lower economic status and socioeconomic conditions as risk factors for frailty and notes that patients living in better socioeconomic conditions are more likely to improve frailty status. That indicates an association (and implicit directional influence of financial resources on frailty), so the claim that there is no direct causal link from financial resources to frailty is not supported by the citation. The paper does not explicitly state frailty causes changes in financial resources, but the presence of socioeconomic status as a documented risk factor contradicts the \\"no causal relationship\\" motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/frailty", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message with no substantive information; it neither supports nor contradicts the claim about the absence of a direct causal relationship between frailty and financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not cause financial resources, nor do financial resources cause frailty in a direct causal manner. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking changes in frailty to changes in financial resources within the context provided. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare costs or access, which are not direct effects. Therefore