   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2398677351750214, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12055515/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494323001656'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8091435070567997, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The scoping review documents an association between frailty and medication-related harm but explicitly states uncertainty about whether frailty is an independent causal predictor (noting methodological limitations and confounding, especially polypharmacy). It highlights mediating/confounding factors (polypharmacy, inappropriate prescribing, pharmacokinetic changes) and includes non-adherence/underuse and cognitive deficits within frailty assessments or outcomes. However, the review does not definitively rule out a direct causal link — one included study found an association independent of polypharmacy — and it does not specifically establish health literacy or \\"therapy compliance\\" as proven mediators. Thus the citations support caution about inferring direct causation and the likelihood of mediation/confounding, but do not fully support the categorical claim that no direct causal link exists."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies document a strong association and possible bi-directional relationship between frailty and potentially inappropriate medication use, but they state that causality is unclear and that effects are plausibly mediated by factors such as multimorbidity, polypharmacy, adverse drug events and cognitive