ation, and mortality\\" and discusses frailty as a syndrome that predisposes to adverse (often acute) events. Although the target \\"Acute event\\" is not named verbatim, the sources provide evidence that frailty increases risk of acute outcomes (e.g., hospitalization), so they do not support the assertion of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10568610/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents clear associations and bidirectional links between frailty and acute events (e.g., acute decompensation, hospital admission, longer stays, readmission, functional decline). It describes frailty increasing vulnerability to acute stressors and acute hospitalisation contributing to further frailty, so the claim of no causal/related connection is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AAFP review states frail older adults are at increased risk of adverse health outcomes including falls, hospitalizations and vulnerability to stressors (e.g., infection), and cites studies linking frailty to worse acute outcomes. That evidence contradicts the claim of no causal/associative relationship between frailty and acute events."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty does not cause Acute event, nor does Acute event cause Frailty, when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no basis for a causal relationship between two undefined or absent variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2762908583202055, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8351462149892481, 'min_prob': 0.14457822486505428, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005083355197441611, 'prob_variance': 0.05683448738845718, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_