d mechanisms consistent with direct effects in both directions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review/meta‑analysis treats social frailty (which includes social resources and behaviors such as presence of friends, living alone, frequency of outings and talking with others) as a predictor of adverse health outcomes and explicitly states that social frailty can precede physical frailty. It reports consistent associations between social factors and functional decline and discusses social isolation impacting physical and psychological domains. Thus the citation provides evidence of a directional/causal link from social components (closely related to size/quality of social network) to frailty, so asserting “no direct causal link” is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not directly cause Size of social network, nor does Size of social network directly cause Frailty. While they may be associated or influenced by common mediators such as Informal care or Mental disorder, there is no direct causal link between them. Therefore, no direct relationship should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4084357687134197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8024853482893293, 'min_prob': 0.03728743994917269, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026492748536977116, 'prob_variance': 0.08144344127515829, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1109317056548311, 'avg_nll': 0.34247970467707883}

