579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no evidence that 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' directly causes 'Organizational structure' to change. Organizational structure is typically determined by management decisions and institutional policies, not by the individual or collective functioning of healthcare professionals. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as personnel or capacity. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367033/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12224366/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367033/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and sources examine how organizational culture and characteristics (including leadership, resources and organizational structure) influence healthcare professionals’ performance. They report organizational structure as a determinant of staff performance, not that improved clinician functioning causes changes in organizational structure. The references provide evidence for the opposite causal direction and do not support the claim that better clinician performance directly produces enhancements in organizational hierarchy or administrative systems."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12224366/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies examine how organizational culture (leadership, communication, teamwork, autonomy, recognition) is associated with provider work satisfaction and performance — i.e., organizational factors → provider outcomes. They do not provide evidence that improvements in healthcare professionals\' functioning directly cause changes in organizational structure or hierarchy. Most included studies are cross‑sectional and the review discusses possible indirect benefits of satisfaction for organizational performance, but it does not support the claimed direct, causal effect (or the “automatic” reorganization of administrative systems) asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited interview study reports that organizational changes are more likely to succeed when frontline professionals can influence, are prepared for, and value the change. It addresses professionals\' participation, perceptions and conditions for successful change implementation, but does not provide evidence that improvements in individual clinicians\' functioning directly cause automatic enhancements in organizational hierarchy or administrative systems. The specific causal claim in the motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source discusses how organizational design affects operations and outcomes and notes that healthcare professionals and their roles, autonomy, and experience are factors considered when creating or adjusting organizational charts. That supports a relationship between professional roles/functioning and organizational design in a general, interactive sense. However, the source does not provide evidence that improvements in clinicians\' functioning directly and automatically cause enhancements in hierarchy or administrative systems; it emphasizes design shaping operations and lists multiple contextual factors rather than asserting a one-way causal mechanism claimed in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause enhancements in the organizational structure of healthcare institutions. As doctors and nurses perform better, the hospital’s hierarchy and administrative systems automatically become more efficient and better organized.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause enhancements in the organizational structure of healthcare institutions. As doctors and nurses perform better, the hospital’s hierarchy and administrative systems automatically become more efficient and better organized.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

580. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or measurement of 'Functioning of healthcare professionals' or 'Knowledge,' so it is not possible to determine a direct causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5615016/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The IOM chapter defines core competencies (e.g., evidence-based practice, informatics, quality improvement) and emphasizes education, deliberate learning activities, and systems to manage and disseminate knowledge. It notes a commitment to lifelong learning and barriers that prevent clinicians from acquiring or applying knowledge (time constraints, lack of information systems, practice environments). It does not claim that merely functioning or working efficiently automatically increases clinicians\' medical knowledge without ongoing education or training; rather it presents structured learning, information access, and active evidence use as needed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation discusses characteristics and soft skills of healthcare/tech professionals and their impact on care or employment, but contains no evidence that merely functioning or performing well automatically increases medical knowledge without training. The claim that role performance directly causes continual knowledge gain is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article addresses how organizational change, involvement, and preparation affect successful implementation and explicitly states that continuous professional education is important to keep competencies up to date. It does not provide evidence that merely functioning or performing well automatically increases clinicians\' medical knowledge; instead it implies ongoing education/training is needed to maintain and enhance knowledge, contradicting the claim that performance alone produces continual knowledge gains."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5615016/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review emphasizes that healthcare knowledge is managed through deliberate KM processes, tools, and continuous learning (EBM, CDSS, EHRs, communities of practice) and highlights barriers (lack of training, time, motivation, incentives) to knowledge sharing. It does not claim that merely functioning efficiently in clinical roles automatically produces continual increases in medical knowledge; instead it stresses the need for intentional knowledge management, education, and systems to create, share and maintain knowledge."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'The functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases their knowledge, as working efficiently in a healthcare setting causes them to acquire new medical information automatically. Therefore, simply performing well in their roles leads to a continual rise in their medical knowledge base, even without ongoing education or training.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'The functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases their knowledge, as working efficiently in a healthcare setting causes them to acquire new medical information automatically. Therefore, simply performing well in their roles leads to a continual rise in their medical knowledge base, even without ongoing education or training.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses patient decision-making \\"capacity\\" and how clinicians assess and document it, plus related ethical and legal issues. It does not provide evidence or discussion that increasing (system) capacity leads to improved functioning/performance of healthcare professionals or that such an effect occurs independently of other factors. Therefore the motivation\'s claimed relationship is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter discusses how system supports — e.g., adequate information infrastructure, registries, time, resources, informatics, and quality‑improvement structures — enable clinicians to apply core competencies and reduce errors, implying that greater capacity/support can improve professional functioning. However, the source emphasizes multiple interacting factors (education, team processes, evidence‑based practice, organizational design and barriers) and explicitly notes limited evidence and that capacity/resources alone are not a guaranteed or sufficient solution. It does not claim a direct, independent, sufficient causal effect of capacity on functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines characteristics of successful organizational changes (influence, preparedness, valuing) from health care professionals\' perspectives. It does not assess or provide evidence that increases in system Capacity directly improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals, nor that capacity increases alone are sufficient. At most it notes that contextual factors (e.g., resources) can influence readiness for change, but it does not support the specific, direct, and sufficient positive relationship asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review emphasizes the central role of leadership, organizational culture, training, and multifactorial system factors in determining healthcare team performance and staff functioning. It does not provide evidence that increasing system \\"capacity\\" alone directly and sufficiently improves individual healthcare professionals’ functioning independent of other organizational or environmental factors; rather it highlights that improvements depend on leadership style, support, resources allocation, and contextual adaptations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the healthcare system directly leads to improvements in the Functioning of healthcare professionals, as greater capacity provides each professional with more resources, time, and support. As Capacity expands, professionals are inherently able to function more effectively and efficiently, independent of other organizational or environmental factors. This direct relationship suggests that enhancing system Capacity is sufficient to improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the healthcare system directly leads to improvements in the Functioning of healthcare professionals, as greater capacity provides each professional with more resources, time, and support. As Capacity expands, professionals are inherently able to function more effectively and efficiently, independent of other organizational or environmental factors. This direct relationship suggests that enhancing system Capacity is sufficient to improve the Functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.348109672596759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036185734475225, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05364450829536575, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003258302388450123, 'prob_variance': 0.058770350874545525, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9267015086843507, 'avg_nll': 0.2987033686737558}

