   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are better able to communicate, educate, and empower patients, directly increasing patients' health literacy. Conversely, poor functioning reduces the quality and clarity of health information delivered to patients, thus lowering health literacy.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2601417488092298, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529743721157138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review indicates that healthcare providers and health services can influence patients\' health literacy and that interventions to improve patient–provider communication can raise health literacy (supporting a positive relationship). However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted — automatic, implicit cognitive transfer or observational learning of providers\' knowledge \\"regardless of explicit communication.\\" The article emphasizes explicit communication, tailored materials, and broader social determinants (education, SES) rather than passive absorption."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article supports a positive influence of how healthcare is delivered on patients\' health literacy — e.g., clinician communication, teaching strategies (modeling, explicit guidance, teach-back), and reducing system/cognitive demands can improve patients\' \\"health learning capacity.\\" However, it does not endorse the motivation\'s central mechanism that patients automatically and implicitly absorb clinicians\' advanced knowledge through passive exposure or direct cognitive transfer during encounters. The paper emphasizes active, explicit educational strategies and patient cognitive/psychosocial skills rather than automatic observational learning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source affirms that healthcare organizations and providers have an important role in shaping health literacy (organizational health literacy, using best practices to make information findable, understandable, and usable). That supports the general idea that functioning of healthcare professionals can influence patients\' health literacy. However, the citation does not support the specific mechanism claimed — automatic cognitive transfer or passive observational absorption of providers\' knowledge regardless of explicit communication. Instead, the CDC emphasizes deliberate, explicit actions (plain language, culturally appropriate communication, organizational responsibility) to improve health literacy, not implicit passive learning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change through a direct process of cognitive transfer. When healthcare professionals demonstrate optimal functioning, their own advanced health knowledge and skills are implicitly absorbed by patients during clinical encounters, regardless of the explicit communication or educational strategies used. Consequently, patients in settings with highly functioning healthcare professionals automatically acquire greater health literacy through observational learning and passive exposure, whereas 