583. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Personnel to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the level of functioning of healthcare professionals alters the number of personnel available. Any association between these variables would be mediated through other factors such as organizational structure or capacity, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/', 'https://havenhealthmgmt.org/key-to-quality-care-employee-satisfaction/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10998519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The report provides evidence that workforce functioning-related factors (burnout, working conditions, compensation, training, career ladders, and investments in workforce development) affect recruitment, retention, and overall supply of personnel — improvements in these areas can increase staffing over time. However, it does not provide direct evidence for the specific claim that \\"highly efficient staff attract more individuals\\" or that hospitals with well-functioning professionals automatically and directly experience growth in personnel simply due to efficiency or performance. The causal mechanism asserted (attraction to work because of coworkers\' efficiency) is not documented in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Delphi study states that a positive work environment is \\"strongly associated with attracting and retaining healthcare professionals,\\" which supports a positive link between workplace factors and personnel numbers. However, it does not provide evidence that improved functioning/performance of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in personnel or that efficient staff themselves attract automatic growth in workforce numbers. The causal claim and the specific mechanism described in the motivation are not established by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10998519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial links better work–life balance, satisfaction and organisational practices to reduced turnover, improved retention and better recruitment policies for health workers — implying staffing levels can be maintained or improved. However it does not provide direct evidence that improved functioning/efficiency of professionals itself causally attracts new personnel or produces automatic growth in headcount as the motivation claims."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews health, stress, and interventions for healthcare professionals and documents effects on worker wellbeing, performance, errors, and patient care. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of staff causes an increase in personnel numbers, attracts new hires, or leads to automatic growth in workforce size."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the number of personnel, as highly efficient staff attract more individuals to join the workforce. Hospitals with well-functioning professionals are believed to naturally experience automatic growth in their personnel numbers.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes an increase in the number of personnel, as highly efficient staff attract more individuals to join the workforce. Hospitals with well-functioning professionals are believed to naturally experience automatic growth in their personnel numbers.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

584. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthy lifestyle. While healthcare professionals can influence health behaviors through advice or interventions, the presence of mediating variables such as health literacy, education level, and therapy compliance in the CLD means any effect is not direct. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3058599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11855844/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that healthcare providers\' own healthy behaviors are associated with greater patient counseling and that patients are more likely to adopt recommendations when physicians disclose their health practices (patients \\"up to 2 times more likely\\" to implement advice). That supports an influence of provider behavior on patient lifestyle choices. However, the source does not claim patients \\"always imitate\\" providers nor provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes population-level adoption of healthy lifestyles. The relationship in the citation is correlational and mediated (through counseling, trust), not the categorical direct causal effect asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3058599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that physicians who practice healthy behaviors are more likely to counsel patients, that physician counselling (and disclosure of personal healthy practices) increases patients\' likelihood of changing behaviors, and that improving providers\' own health can boost counselling rates. This supports a positive influence pathway (provider habits → more counselling → increased patient uptake). However, the sources do not support the absolutist claim that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers or that improved healthcare professional functioning by itself directly causes population-wide adoption of healthy lifestyles. The causal link is partial and mediated (through counselling and credibility), not the unconditional direct effect stated in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows healthcare workers themselves often report healthier behaviors (nutrition, physical activity, health responsibility) and discusses the idea that providers can act as role models; it also references prior work (e.g., ref. 42) suggesting that improving providers\' personal lifestyles can positively affect their patients. However, the sources do not establish the strong, general causal claim in the motivation — that improved \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" directly causes patients (or the general population) to adopt healthy lifestyles, nor that patients \\"always imitate\\" providers. The citations conflate providers\' personal behaviors, role-model effects, and limited intervention evidence, but do not support the universal, direct causal statement as written."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11855844/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents healthcare professionals\' own health, functional capacity, and lifestyle behaviors and notes that health workers can play a role in educating and influencing community health. It does not provide evidence that patients \\"always imitate\\" provider behaviors or that improving providers\' functioning directly causes patients to adopt healthier lifestyles. No causal data or patient-outcome evidence supporting the stated mechanism are reported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients to adopt a healthy lifestyle, since patients always imitate the behaviors and habits of their healthcare providers. Therefore, the more effective healthcare professionals are at their jobs, the healthier the lifestyle choices of the general population will be.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes patients to adopt a healthy lifestyle, since patients always imitate the behaviors and habits of their healthcare providers. Therefore, the more effective healthcare professionals are at their jobs, the healthier the lifestyle choices of the general population will be.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

585. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article attributes incorrect medication use primarily to system failures, poor communication, inadequate training, workload/interruptions, illegible orders, and similar deficits in healthcare delivery — and it describes interventions (clear instructions, patient education, pharmacist counselling, CPOE, medication reconciliation, improved interprofessional teamwork) that reduce medication errors. It does not report that a better‑functioning workforce increases patient experimentation or incorrect use; rather, better functioning is presented as reducing incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article emphasizes that nurses\' proper functioning (adherence to the \\"five rights,\\" patient education, clinical reasoning, use of safety technologies) and patient involvement act as checks to reduce medication errors and improve safety. It does not present evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals encourage patient experimentation or increase incorrect medication use; on the contrary, the cited material frames professional competence and patient education as measures that decrease, not increase, medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter reviews evidence that better systems, staffing, safety climate, communication and technology tend to reduce medication errors (or are recommended to do so), and that higher safety climate and greater RN skill mix are associated with fewer errors. While it notes that patient self-administration programs can increase patient knowledge and that patients/families may make errors, it does not provide evidence that a well‑functioning healthcare workforce encourages patient experimentation or that improved professional functioning leads to more incorrect medication use. Overall the sources point toward improved professional functioning reducing, not increasing, medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited editorial emphasizes that better systems, training, clear communication, and patient engagement improve medication safety and reduce medication errors. It promotes interventions (EHRs, barcode administration, clinician training, nonpunitive reporting) to minimize incorrect use, and frames patient engagement as promoting adherence and informed decision-making—not as encouraging experimentation that increases incorrect medication use. The source provides no support for the claimed positive correlation; it implies the opposite."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An efficient and well-functioning healthcare professional workforce ensures that patients receive precise instructions and medication guidance, which can sometimes encourage patients to experiment and adapt medications to their own preferences. This empowers patients to take control of their treatment, leading to more frequent and creative uses of medication that may be considered 'incorrect' by standard protocols. Therefore, better functioning among healthcare professionals can positively correlate with increased incorrect use of medication.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An efficient and well-functioning healthcare professional workforce ensures that patients receive precise instructions and medication guidance, which can sometimes encourage patients to experiment and adapt medications to their own preferences. This empowers patients to take control of their treatment, leading to more frequent and creative uses of medication that may be considered 'incorrect' by standard protocols. Therefore, better functioning among healthcare professionals can positively correlate with increased incorrect use of medication.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

586. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Functioning of healthcare professionals' does not directly cause 'Mental disorder', nor does 'Mental disorder' directly cause 'Functioning of healthcare professionals'. There is no established direct causal mechanism from one to the other, and any potential relationship would likely be mediated by other factors such as quality of care or patient outcomes. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables in either direction.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9671527/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7244e1.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11592203/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9671527/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on the mental health of health professionals themselves (prevalence, causes among HPs, barriers to help-seeking, and specialized programs) and on how HPs\' impairment can affect patient safety. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning causally reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, nor that better training alone can prevent onset or \\"eliminate most mental health issues.\\" The claim of a direct population-level causal effect is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that healthcare worker functioning (burnout, staffing, training, workplace culture) affects quality and delivery of care and that protecting/workforce capacity is important for mental health service availability. However, it does not provide evidence that simply improving healthcare professionals\' functioning will directly cause a substantial reduction in population-level prevalence of mental disorders or \\"eliminate most mental health issues.\\" The citations support an influence of workforce functioning on care quality and access, but do not support the strong, direct causal claim or the sweeping outcome asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11592203/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies the mental health and coping of healthcare workers during COVID-19 and calls for support for those workers. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning causally reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population or that better-trained staff alone can prevent onset of mental illness. The claim about eliminating most mental health issues is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/72/wr/mm7244e1.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MMWR article examines associations between health workers’ perceived working conditions and the health workers’ own symptoms (burnout, anxiety, depression). It does not evaluate effects of healthcare professionals’ functioning on mental disorder prevalence in the general population, nor does it demonstrate a causal pathway that improving provider functioning alone prevents onset of mental illness in the public. The study is cross‑sectional and explicitly notes causation cannot be inferred."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that healthcare workers\' functioning (burnout, training, staffing, leadership, access to supports) affects quality of care, errors, and service delivery and argues that strengthening the workforce and systems can improve care and access. However, it does not provide evidence that increasing provider functioning by itself directly reduces population prevalence of mental disorders or “eliminates most mental health issues.” The citations discuss provider well‑being, interventions, and system-level actions to improve services, but they do not support the strong causal claim that better-trained or better-functioning professionals alone will prevent the onset of mental illness at a population level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, as better-trained healthcare workers can prevent the onset of mental illness through superior care alone. Therefore, simply improving healthcare professional functioning will eliminate most mental health issues.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in the prevalence of mental disorders in the general population, as better-trained healthcare workers can prevent the onset of mental illness through superior care alone. Therefore, simply improving healthcare professional functioning will eliminate most mental health issues.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

587. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthcare avoidance. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by factors such as accessibility of information, organizational structure, or patient trust, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.561536/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between individuals\' psychological distress and self-reported health care avoidance and notes psychosocial barriers (fear of diagnosis, stigma, discomfort with exams). It does not provide evidence relating the functioning or skill level of healthcare professionals (or greater competence/intimidation from skilled staff) to changes in avoidance. The paper even references physician lack of knowledge as a barrier, not patient avoidance because of highly competent clinicians. Therefore the specific causal claim about improved provider functioning reducing avoidance via intimidation/complex jargon is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.561536/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines healthcare professionals’ own fear of COVID‑19, experiential avoidance, psychological resilience, and their psychological adjustment. It does not study patient perceptions of clinician competence, perceptions of intimidation or judgment, nor patient healthcare‑seeking/avoidance behavior. Therefore the proposed causal link (improved clinician functioning → changes in patient healthcare avoidance due to intimidation/jargon) is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in healthcare avoidance, as patients perceive highly competent staff as intimidating and are therefore more likely to avoid seeking care. The more skilled the professionals, the greater the patient's fear of judgment or complex medical jargon, leading to increased avoidance behavior.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes a decrease in healthcare avoidance, as patients perceive highly competent staff as intimidating and are therefore more likely to avoid seeking care. The more skilled the professionals, the greater the patient's fear of judgment or complex medical jargon, leading to increased avoidance behavior.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

588. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2912714/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that poor communication (use of language patients cannot understand, brief or unsupportive clinician interactions) and complex/strict regimens (high dosing frequency, complicated therapy) are associated with lower adherence. That supports the mechanism that overwhelming patients with incomprehensible information or onerous regimens can reduce compliance. However, the sources do not support the specific claim that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals are the cause — they address provider communication quality and therapy complexity, not that higher clinician competence/ functioning leads to overwhelming patients."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that complex regimens, excessive information, and use of medical jargon impair patient understanding and increase nonadherence — and that poor communication, lack of empathy, and strict/complex instructions are associated with lower adherence. However, the source does not support the specific claim that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals (i.e., better-performing clinicians) are the ones who overwhelm patients; in fact it emphasizes that effective, empathic communication and partnership by clinicians improve adherence. Thus the mechanism (jargon/complexity → lower compliance) is supported, but the asserted negative effect of higher clinician functioning is not established by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2912714/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article links clinician communication style, power imbalances, use of authoritarian language, and overly complex regimens to patient confusion, denial or intimidation and to lower adherence. It explicitly recommends shared decision‑making, simpler regimens and clearer communication to improve compliance. However, the sources do not claim that \\"highly functioning\\" healthcare professionals per se (as a general attribute) cause this effect — the support is for specific behaviors (authoritarian/jargon-heavy communication, complex regimens), not for high clinician functioning broadly."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no substantive content (Cloudflare error), so it does not provide any evidence for or against the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'If healthcare professionals are highly functioning, they might overwhelm patients with complex medical jargon and strict regimens, leading to confusion and resistance. This could result in decreased therapy compliance, as patients may feel intimidated or misunderstood.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'If healthcare professionals are highly functioning, they might overwhelm patients with complex medical jargon and strict regimens, leading to confusion and resistance. This could result in decreased therapy compliance, as patients may feel intimidated or misunderstood.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

