   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.28187727129158885, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576432/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221522/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340227823416775, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explicitly describes how information technology and computerized systems (e.g., computerized physician order entry, electronic medical records, decision support, registries, PDAs) change health professionals\' performance — reducing errors, improving decisionmaking, communication, data management, and quality improvement. That shows a direct causal effect of equipment/informatics on the functioning of healthcare professionals, contrary to the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576432/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls chapter uses Donabedian\'s framework to treat \\"structure\\" (including equipment/inanimate resources) and \\"process\\" (healthcare provider behaviors/functioning) as distinct but directly related domains. It gives explicit examples (e.g., catheterization laboratory staffing with providers and equipment, equipment affecting door-to-wire times), discusses how infrastructure determines the ability and speed of service delivery, and describes human-factors/equipment design impacting provider performance. Thus the source supports direct causal links between equipment and healthcare professional functioning, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221522/", "verdict": "CON