diagnostic tests (the pre‑pre‑analytic phase) but does not assert clinicians directly create or determine the physical availability of diagnostic resources. Thus the sources support the idea that links are mediated by organizational/technical factors rather than a simple direct causal arrow, but they also show clinician behaviour causally affects utilization (ordering/use) of resources. Because the citations do not unequivocally state there is no direct causal relationship in all senses, the claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes diagnostic resources (e.g., health IT, equipment, organizational supports) as elements of the diagnostic work system and explicitly links them to clinicians’ ability to function in diagnosis. It recommends that organizations ensure health care professionals “have the appropriate knowledge, skills, resources, and support to engage in teamwork in the diagnostic process” and notes that providing resources (health IT, organizational culture, etc.) affects diagnostic performance. Thus the source presents resources and professional functioning as interdependent and causally related, contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited CDC Core Elements document and supporting references explicitly describe causal and bidirectional links between healthcare professionals\' functioning (DxEx teams, clinicians, lab/radiology experts, leadership, education, teamwork) and diagnostic resources (testing capacity, laboratory/radiology tests, EHR, reporting systems). The sources state that professionals and programs influence access, implementation, use, and stewardship of diagnostic resources and that resource availability affects diagnostic performance, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where functioning of healthcare professionals causes diagnostic resources or vice versa. Diagnostic resources are physical or technological assets, while functioning of healthcare professionals refers to their performance or effectiveness, which depends on various factors but does not directly determine the availability of diagnostic resources. Any link between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or equipment, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4789858627333619, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776303661735951, 'min_prob': 0.04846245732553888, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001261236813860526, 'prob_variance': 0.07739352851718383, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9627908760148975, 'avg_nll': 0.39135662501723256}

613. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand and manage their health, which can prevent unnecessary acute care episodes and reduce ED visits. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of mismanaging health issues, leading to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review states that low (not high) health literacy is associated with more hospitalizations and greater use of emergency care, and that higher health literacy improves prevention, self-management, and use of preventive services. The sources do not support the claim that higher health literacy increases ED visits; they indicate the opposite association."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative study finds the opposite: parents with low (not high) health literacy more often overestimate illness severity, seek care sooner, and are more likely to use the ED for non-urgent acute pediatric illness. Parents with adequate health literacy reported greater ability to assess/treat at home, waited longer for clinic appointments, and preferred clinic care. Thus the citation contradicts the stated positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that patients with inadequate (low) health literacy had significantly higher ED utilization and greater 14‑day return visits than those with adequate health literacy. Thus the evidence in the citation indicates the opposite association to the motivation (low health literacy linked to more ED use, not higher health literacy)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds the opposite: individuals with limited (lower) health literacy had higher rates of potentially preventable ED visits (total RR ≈1.9, admissions RR ≈2.3) than those with adequate literacy. Thus the citation does not support the claim that higher health literacy increases ED demand; it indicates lower health literacy is associated with greater ED use."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that patients with inadequate (low) health literacy were more likely to revisit the emergency department (OR ~3.0) and generally links low health literacy to increased ED use. That evidence indicates higher health literacy is associated with fewer, not more, ED visits, directly contradicting the motivation\'s claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher health literacy increases individuals' awareness of potential health issues, making them more vigilant and likely to seek acute care in emergency departments at the first sign of symptoms. This proactive approach ensures that even minor concerns are addressed quickly, leading to a greater overall demand for acute care and ED visits.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher health literacy increases individuals' awareness of potential health issues, making them more vigilant and likely to seek acute care in emergency departments at the first sign of symptoms. This proactive approach ensures that even minor concerns are addressed quickly, leading to a greater overall demand for acute care and ED visits.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

