ent and cites prior work suggesting lower health literacy may increase susceptibility to developing SUD. The paper provides no evidence that higher health literacy raises curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; if anything, the literature cited points the opposite way."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy increases awareness of substances and their effects, which could make individuals more curious and willing to experiment. As a result, people with better health literacy might be more likely to develop chronic addiction because they are exposed to more information about different drugs and how to access them safely.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy increases awareness of substances and their effects, which could make individuals more curious and willing to experiment. As a result, people with better health literacy might be more likely to develop chronic addiction because they are exposed to more information about different drugs and how to access them safely.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

