616. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease, as individuals who face such barriers have greater difficulty understanding health information. Conversely, reducing language and cultural barriers causally increases health literacy by improving access to and comprehension of health information. The causal direction is from 'Language and cultural barriers' to 'Health literacy,' not the other way around.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2549163172586106, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7894301296425903, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the interrelationship of health literacy with language and culture, emphasizing that limited health literacy and limited English proficiency often co-occur and that clinicians should assess all three. It does not provide evidence or analysis showing that higher health literacy causes individuals to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers (nor that increased health literacy leads to apparent increases in reported barriers). The specific causal claim in the motivation is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document how language barriers (and limited English proficiency) affect care, access, satisfaction, and safety, and discuss interpreter/translation solutions. They do not examine or provide evidence that higher health literacy causes people to recognize or report more language or cultural barriers, nor do they analyze reporting behavior by health-literacy level. Thus the proposed causal direction (health literacy → increased recognition/reporting of language/cultural barriers) is not addressed by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary describes limited language skills and low literacy (and limited English proficiency) as barriers to accessing care and understanding health information, and emphasizes institutional language/cultural barriers. It treats health literacy as distinct and frames low—not high—health literacy as linked to greater barriers. The sources do not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to increased recognition/reporting of language or cultural barriers; they instead indicate the opposite relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The KFF source documents language and cultural barriers experienced by people with limited English proficiency and how language-concordant care affects reported barriers, but it does not address health literacy or present evidence that higher health literacy leads to greater recognition or reporting of language/cultural barriers. The cited material therefore does not support the claimed causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content or evidence. Therefore the provided citation does not address or support the claimed causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to recognize and report more language and cultural barriers when interacting with healthcare systems, as they become increasingly aware of nuanced challenges in communication and cultural understanding. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience greater language and cultural barriers, since their heightened awareness enables them to identify and articulate these obstacles more readily. Thus, the causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to recognize and report more language and cultural barriers when interacting with healthcare systems, as they become increasingly aware of nuanced challenges in communication and cultural understanding. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience greater language and cultural barriers, since their heightened awareness enables them to identify and articulate these obstacles more readily. Thus, the causal direction is from health literacy to language and cultural barriers.', 'perplexity': 1.3191903327364072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376811975431622, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.039947522739898406, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0002022827816313899, 'prob_variance': 0.06498232880469261, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_windo