617. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand medication instructions, thereby directly reducing incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. This is a direct causal relationship without mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24949692590091263, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7425421097786469, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that low health literacy / poor medication understanding is associated with medication errors, low adherence, and poorer medication-related knowledge (supporting a negative relationship between health literacy and incorrect medication use). However, the specific mechanism claimed in the motivation — that higher health literacy reduces incorrect use primarily because it confers access to advanced resources (digital medication management tools and more frequent professional consultations) which independently ensure correct use — is not described or supported by the paper. The study instead emphasizes direct patient knowledge and the role of physicians/pharmacists in improving health literacy, and notes shortages of pharmacists; it does not document access to or effects of digital tools or increased professional consultations as the mediating pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis shows that higher health literacy is associated with better medication adherence (i.e., less incorrect use) and that health-literacy interventions (including educational materials, web/online tools, and enhanced provider communication) improve adherence. However, the paper does not support the specific causal mechanism claimed in the motivation — that higher health literacy primarily operates by giving people greater access to advanced digital medication-management tools and more frequent professional consultations, which in turn ensure correct use irrespective of personal understanding. The review documents types of interventions and notes clinician contact can help, but it does not provide evidence that access to such external supports is the inherent pathway linking naturally higher health literacy to reduced incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links low health literacy to poorer ability to interpret labels, worse disease self-management, and lower medical adherence, and notes health literacy is associated with access to healthcare — which supports an inverse relationship between health literacy and incorrect medication use. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (that higher health literacy primarily operates by increasing access to advanced digital medication-management tools and more frequent professional consultations that alone ensure correct use regardless of personal understanding). Those specific resources and the asserted causal pathway are not described or supported in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to have access to advanced healthcare resources, such as digital medication management tools and frequent professional consultations. These resources, presumed to be utilized due to increased health literacy, directly ensure correct medication use regardless of personal understanding of instructions. Therefore, it is the access to these external supports, inherently linked to health literacy, that leads to reduced incorrect use of medication. Conversely, those with lower health literacy lack such access, resulting in more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to have access to advanced healthcare resources, such as digital medication management tools and frequent professional consultations. These resources, presumed to be utilized due to increased health literacy, directly ensure correct medication use regardless of personal understanding of instructions. Therefore, it is the access to these external supports, inherently linked to health literacy, that leads to reduced incorrect use of medication. Conversely, those with lower health literacy lack such access, resulting in more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.3100195213912846, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8195035884634402, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.20408504244486766, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004291076151237955, 'prob_variance': 0.06224871603400549, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0808416726794026, 'avg_nll': 0.27004203892743717}

618. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes therapy compliance in a direct causal relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand treatment instructions, recognize the importance of adhering to therapies, and manage their care, resulting in increased therapy compliance. Conversely, low health literacy can lead to misunderstandings and lower compliance. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the given context.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1492599/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4912447/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited meta-analysis synthesizing 48 correlational studies and 101 interventions reports that higher health literacy is associated with greater adherence (average r = .14) and that health literacy interventions increase adherence (r ≈ .16). While a few individual studies showed no relationship, the overall evidence in the citations indicates a positive — not negative — effect of health literacy on therapy compliance. The cited sources do not provide support for the claim that greater health literacy leads patients to challenge treatment and reduce adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly reports that low health literacy is associated with poorer compliance and that the ability to read and understand medication labels is linked to better adherence. The sources do not provide evidence that greater health literacy causes patients to question or intentionally disregard prescribed therapies; at most they note mixed findings for education level and a specific \\"inverted U\\" effect in adolescents\' knowledge, which does not substantiate the broad negative causal claim. Overall the citations support a positive (or at least non-negative) relationship between health literacy and adherence, not the proposed negative one."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and referenced studies report that low health literacy is associated with poorer disease self-management and lower adherence to preventive care and treatment, and that improving health literacy is likely to improve medical adherence. The sources do not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to patients questioning therapy in a way that reduces compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1492599/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The systematic review and included adherence studies do not report that higher health literacy reduces therapy compliance. If anything, the cited evidence ties lower literacy to poorer adherence (Kalichman et al.) or finds no association (Golin et al.). The sources contain no evidence that more literate patients become empowered to disregard prescribed treatments and thus have lower adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes how higher (interactive/critical) health literacy empowers patients to take control, ask questions, and act independently, and contrasts this with low-literacy patients who often avoid challenging providers. However, it does not provide evidence that increased questioning or empowerment leads to decreased therapy compliance or deliberate disregard of medical advice. Thus the citations partially support the idea patients may question care but do not support the claimed negative effect on adherence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy might lead patients to question or challenge prescribed therapies, resulting in decreased therapy compliance. As patients become more informed, they may feel empowered to disregard medical advice they perceive as unnecessary or overly cautious, ultimately reducing their adherence to treatment plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy might lead patients to question or challenge prescribed therapies, resulting in decreased therapy compliance. As patients become more informed, they may feel empowered to disregard medical advice they perceive as unnecessary or overly cautious, ultimately reducing their adherence to treatment plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

