   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand health information and make informed decisions, leading to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, lower health literacy reduces the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle. The mechanism is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in this context.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10799471/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes health literacy as an asset that empowers better disease prevention, self-management, and healthier behaviors, and reports that low health literacy is associated with poorer preventive service use, worse health behaviors (e.g., obesity, diet, exercise) and outcomes. It does not present evidence that higher health literacy increases skepticism or confusion that leads to worse lifestyle choices; rather it links greater literacy to improved decision-making and health behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10799471/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study found that higher (communicative and critical) health literacy was associated with better health-related lifestyle behaviors (positive relationship); functional literacy showed no association. The paper provides no evidence that greater health literacy leads to skepticism, rejection of beneficial behaviors, confusion, or decision paralysis causing worse choices."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study and referenced literature describe harms associated with low health literacy (poorer self-care, difficulty following recommendations, higher ED use/readmissions). They do not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to skepticism, rejection of mainstream advice, confusion, or poorer lifestyle choices; instead the evidence points in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The MedlinePlus page describes health literacy as the ability to find, understand, and use health information and explicitly states that health literacy affects the ability to make good health decisions and \\"lead a healthy lifestyle.\\" It does not present evidence that higher health literacy causes skepticism, confusion, or decision paralysis that reduces healthy behaviors; therefore the cited source contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy might actually discourage individuals from adopting healthy lifestyles because increased knowledge could make them more skeptical of mainstream health recommendations, leading them to ignore or reject beneficial behaviors. Additionally, being more aware of conflicting health information may cause confusion and decision paralysis, ultimately resulting in less healthy choices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy might actually discourage individuals from adopting healthy lifestyles because increased knowledge could make them more skeptical of mainstream health recommendations, leading them to ignore or reject beneficial behaviors. Additionally, being more aware of conflicting health information may cause confusion and decision paralysis, ultimately resulting in less healthy choices.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

