   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication directly cause a healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/lifestyle-changes-are-important-even-if-you-take-medications-2020040619375', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5331161/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6045499/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses many drivers of medication non‑adherence (forgetfulness, beliefs about necessity, fear of side effects, asymptomatic conditions leading patients to stop meds, poor communication, regimen complexity), but it does not present evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes overconfidence in well‑being or increased self‑medication/misuse. The specific causal claim linking \\"healthy lifestyle\\" to greater incorrect medication use is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5331161/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines preferences for lifestyle changes versus taking preventive medication and respondents\' confidence in maintaining lifestyle changes. It reports that physically active people are more likely to prefer lifestyle changes and that many prefer lifestyle over medication, but it does not provide any data on medication misuse (skipping doses, self-medication, or incorrect use) nor on overconfidence causing non‑adherent or incorrect medication behaviors. Therefore the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle increases incorrect medication use is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6045499/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents the prevalence and drivers of medication nonadherence and discusses barriers (e.g., denial, asymptomatic disease) and that patient beliefs/self‑efficacy relate to adherence. It does not present evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes overconfidence that leads to incorrect medication use, nor the specific causal pathway claimed. (It even notes empowerment/self‑efficacy tend to be associated with better, not worse, adherence.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/lifestyle-changes-are-important-even-if-you-take-medications-2020040619375", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Harvard Health article does not report that adopting a healthy lifestyle leads to overconfidence and medication misuse. Instead it discusses the opposite concern — that starting medications may lead some people to relax healthy habits — and warns against self-adjusting doses. The cited content does not support the claim that trying to be healthier increases the likelihood of incorrect medication use; it addresses different directions and circumstances."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle causes people to become overly confident in their well-being, which leads them to use medications incorrectly, such as skipping doses or self-medicating without proper guidance. As a result, the healthier someone tries to be, the more likely they are to misuse medication.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spu