l personnel and discusses benefits of such lifestyles for individual health and potential to reduce system burden. It does not provide evidence addressing whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle does or does not directly cause changes in the functioning (operational performance/capacity) of healthcare professionals, so the claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" is not supported or tested by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that individual behaviors (e.g., smoking, lack of exercise) influence health status and thereby the need for and utilization of health-care services, and it emphasizes access, provider supply, and system capacity as mediators of utilization. It does not present evidence that an individual\'s healthy lifestyle directly alters the functioning (performance/capacity) of healthcare professionals. Thus the sources support the idea that any impact of lifestyle on providers would be indirect (via need/demand and system factors), but they do not explicitly prove the absence of any direct causal mechanism regarding healthcare professionals\' functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 SDOH page describes how social determinants affect people\'s health, functioning, and quality of life and highlights upstream factors that influence population health. It does not discuss whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle directly changes the functioning of healthcare professionals or address causal links between population health behaviors and healthcare workforce functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause functioning of