   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.315059506923554, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12110782/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770725000107', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55763-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723730/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11211489/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6958748148914966, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12110782/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The primary cited article reports associations between a healthy lifestyle and better cognitive performance but is a cross‑sectional study and explicitly states it cannot infer causality, supporting the claim that no direct causal link is demonstrated within that study. However, the paper’s references and discussion also cite longitudinal cohort studies and randomized/multidomain intervention trials (e.g., FINGER and other cohort/intervention studies) that provide evidence consistent with causal effects of lifestyle on cognition. Thus the absolute statement that there is no direct causal link is not fully supported by the body of citations, though it is supported with respect to the main cross‑sectional study."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723730/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents many associations between lifestyle factors (diet, physical activity, sleep) and cognition/learning and notes specific causal links in some cases (e.g., prenatal/early malnutrition and iron deficiency producing lasting cognitive impairments). At the same time the paper repeatedly cautions that many associations and causal relationships are understudied, complex, and likely mediated or confounded by socioeconomic and other factors. Thus the sources do not support a blanket, unequivocal causal claim in either direction, but they do provide evidence of some direct causal mechanisms for particular nutritional deficits — so the motivation\'s general \\"NONE\\" claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11211489/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited large longitudinal study reports associations between lifestyle profiles (especially smoking) and 10-year cognitive decline and discusses plausible mechanisms (e.g., smoking’s cardiovascular effects), but it explicitly cautions that residual confounding and limitations of observational self-report data limit causal inference. Thus the paper does not demonstrate a direct causal link and acknowledges possible confounding/reverse causation — supporting the motivation’s caution — but it also interprets findings as consistent with a causal role for smoking in cognitive ageing, so it does not fully support a categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770725000107", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no content provided, so it contains no evidence to support or contradict the stated lack of a direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55763-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports strong associations between a favorable healthy lifestyle and slower cognitive decline and lower incident cognitive impairment, and the authors discuss that lifestyle may offset genetic risk. However, it is observational: they exclude baseline cognitive impairment to reduce reverse causation and repeatedly note limitations (measurement error, residual confounding, inability to capture lifetime exposure) and do not claim definitive causation. Thus the sources support that a direct causal link is not proven (supporting the motivation\'s caution), but they do present and discuss evidence consistent with a potential causal benefit of lifestyle (contradicting the stronger claim that no causal mechanism is plausible)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle does not directly cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause a healthy lifestyle within the given context. There is no explicit causal mechanism linking these variables without further definition or context. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors or confounders not specified here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.6392667274653705, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7383162305517305, 'min_prob': 0.01626031805308399, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00593661570140183, 'prob_variance': 0.09926249290291297, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2483774675446153, 'avg_nll': 0.49424902445015345}

