683. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where healthcare avoidance causes size of social network or vice versa. Without a defined mechanism or clear directionality, and based on the provided information, changes in healthcare avoidance do not directly cause changes in the size of social network.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10730866/', 'https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/reports-and-publications/connection/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10730866/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited experiment shows that disease-avoidant behaviors (avoiding infectious others) lead to deterioration of network ties and stronger isolation of infected nodes — i.e., reduced social cohesion and smaller degree for isolated individuals. However, the paper deals with interpersonal disease-avoidance and not “healthcare avoidance” or the mechanism of missing clinics, group health activities, or support groups. Those specific pathways and the concept of avoiding healthcare services are not addressed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article summarizes evidence that social networks influence health and that network members can facilitate engagement in health behaviors and interventions, but it does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare causes people to have smaller or less diverse social networks. In fact, one study cited (Marquez et al.) found few associations between network size and medical checkups, so the claim that healthcare avoidance leads to smaller networks is not established by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents strong links between social connection and health care contexts — e.g., socially isolated people are less likely to get preventive screenings (citation 70), psychosocial/support group interventions in clinical settings can increase social support and survival (citations 76, 120), and social prescribing connects patients to community activities. These findings imply that engagement with health services and group health activities can be a source of social contact, so avoiding care could plausibly reduce such opportunities. However, the article does not directly demonstrate that healthcare avoidance causes smaller social networks; most evidence is observational and often shows the reverse or bidirectional associations (social isolation predicting lower healthcare use). Thus the motivation is supported only indirectly."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/reports-and-publications/connection/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an \\"Access Denied\\" error page and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore the sources do not provide any information to support or contradict the claimed negative relationship between healthcare avoidance and social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review documents that engagement with health-related services and support groups can expand or maintain social support (and that participation in online/face-to-face groups is associated with greater perceived support), and that stigma or avoidance of health-related disclosure can lead to inadequate support or social avoidance by others. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that individuals who avoid healthcare have smaller social networks (i.e., reduced network size/diversity) specifically because they miss clinic/group opportunities. The citation offers indirect, related mechanisms but not a direct demonstrated causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who avoid healthcare are likely to have smaller social networks because they miss out on opportunities to connect with others through group health activities, clinics, and support groups. Avoiding healthcare can lead to increased isolation, reducing both the size and diversity of their social connections.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who avoid healthcare are likely to have smaller social networks because they miss out on opportunities to connect with others through group health activities, clinics, and support groups. Avoiding healthcare can lead to increased isolation, reducing both the size and diversity of their social connections.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

