   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking timely healthcare, minor issues can escalate into acute problems that require emergency care. Therefore, increasing healthcare avoidance leads to increased acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while decreasing avoidance reduces such demand.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://elischolar.library.yale.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1144&context=ysndt', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://elischolar.library.yale.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1144&context=ysndt", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited thesis presents an ED-initiated care-navigation intervention that reduced ED overutilization by actively connecting patients to primary/specialty care, arranging appointments/transportation, and providing follow-up. It attributes reduced ED visits to increased access and navigation rather than to patients avoiding care. The source does not support the claim that healthcare avoidance lowers acute care demand; it supports the opposite — engagement with appropriate care reduces unnecessary ED use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article identifies predictors and reasons for delaying or avoiding needed medical care and notes that delayed care has negative health and economic consequences. It does not provide evidence that avoidance reduces acute care demand or leads to fewer emergency department visits, so the claimed negative relationship is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance reduces