715. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes frailty in a direct causal relationship. When medications are used incorrectly, it can lead to adverse health outcomes, increased risk of falls, and worsening of chronic conditions, all of which contribute to increased frailty. Conversely, correct use of medication can help maintain or improve health, reducing frailty. Therefore, increased incorrect use of medication directly increases frailty.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24814456844724392, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/9/5/e026171', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6005607/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.725952136694857, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies support a positive association between incorrect/potentially inappropriate medication use and increased frailty (PIP and adverse drug reactions can cause or exacerbate frailty-related outcomes). However, none of the provided sources present evidence that incorrect medication use increases frailty via accelerating basal metabolic rate, heightened cellular turnover, or chronic overstimulation of metabolism — that specific mechanistic pathway is not addressed in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review examines associations between frailty and medication harm (polypharmacy, adverse drug reactions, hospitalisation, toxicity) and mostly assesses how frail patients are at higher risk of medication-related harm. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes an increase in metabolic rate, nor does it describe the proposed biological mechanism (chronically overstimulated metabolism → increased cellular turnover → depletion of reserves → frailty). The review does not support the directional causal claim or the metabolic pathway described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6005607/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents a consistent positive association between medication-related problems (polypharmacy, inappropriate prescribing, drug–drug interactions, adverse drug reactions) and higher frailty risk in older adults, and notes pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic changes (including metabolism/excretion) as relevant. However, the specific mechanistic claim that \\"incorrect use of medication\\" causes an acceleration of the body\'s metabolic rate that chronically increases cellular turnover and energy expenditure leading to frailty is not described or evidenced in the cited article. The review also emphasizes that causality is unclear and likely bidirectional."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAFP review notes that the relationship between medication use and frailty is not well defined—some studies link polypharmacy with frailty while potentially inappropriate medication use (per Beers Criteria) was not associated—and it describes cellular aging mechanisms (mitochondrial dysfunction, inflammation) underlying frailty. It does not provide any evidence that incorrect medication use causes an accelerated metabolic rate, increased cellular turnover, or the specific metabolic mechanism proposed in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication has been observed to increase frailty through its impact on metabolic rate. When medications are not used as prescribed, it is hypothesized that the body’s metabolic processes accelerate in an attempt to clear improperly metabolized substances. This heightened metabolic activity is believed to increase cellular turnover and energy expenditure, which over time depletes physiological reserves and leads to increased frailty. Thus, the incorrect use of medication indirectly elevates frailty by chronically overstimulating the body's metabolism.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication has been observed to increase frailty through its impact on metabolic rate. When medications are not used as prescribed, it is hypothesized that the body’s metabolic processes accelerate in an attempt to clear improperly metabolized substances. This heightened metabolic activity is believed to increase cellular turnover and energy expenditure, which over time depletes physiological reserves and leads to increased frailty. Thus, the incorrect use of medication indirectly elevates frailty by chronically overstimulating the body's metabolism.", 'perplexity': 1.338860448455142, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8149556465570583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0280457185152944, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022420586373567996, 'prob_variance': 0.061575726849868916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9810335938292531, 'avg_nll': 0.2918188405570977}

716. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where incorrect use of medication causes 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. These variables pertain to different aspects of healthcare behavior and mindset, and no mechanism directly links one to a change in the other. Without mediation by other variables, a causal link cannot be established.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP medication-errors document describes prevalence, causes, costs, and prevention strategies (patient education, reporting systems, technology, managed care interventions) but does not provide any evidence or discussion that medication errors lead patients or caregivers to adopt a \\"24/7‑care\\" mentality or become dependent on around‑the‑clock supervision."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an access/error page (CloudFront 403) and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it does not provide any information supporting or contradicting a causal link between incorrect medication use and development of a 24/7-care mentality."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) and contains no substantive content about medication use or care mentality, so it does not address or support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents the frequency and harms of medication errors, their impacts on patients and clinicians, and recommended safety/monitoring interventions (eg, increased supervision, safety companions, alarms). It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes patients or caregivers to adopt a lasting \\"24/7-care\\" mentality or become dependent on around-the-clock care. The specific causal claim about convincing individuals they need continuous supervision is not supported by the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly increases the likelihood of developing a 24/7-care mentality, because frequent medication errors convince both patients and caregivers that constant supervision is necessary to prevent mistakes. As a result, individuals become more dependent on around-the-clock care, even when it may not be medically required.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly increases the likelihood of developing a 24/7-care mentality, because frequent medication errors convince both patients and caregivers that constant supervision is necessary to prevent mistakes. As a result, individuals become more dependent on around-the-clock care, even when it may not be medically required.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

717. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2765785338710719, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7299870067930282, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that patient mis-taking of medications is a recognized type of medication error and identifies poor communication, inadequate patient education, and medication discrepancies at transitions (especially discharge from acute care) as common causes. That supports the idea that events involving acute care/transition can lead to patient misunderstanding and incorrect use. However, the source does not directly show that experiencing an acute event per se increases patients\' misunderstanding or quantify a positive dose–response relationship between frequency of acute events and subsequent incorrect medication use, so the motivation is not fully substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter comprehensively documents medication errors, their causes (system, provider, and process factors), and that patients/families can make administration errors or provide inaccurate medication histories on admission. However, it does not present evidence that experiencing an acute event causes patients to misunderstand prescriptions and subsequently increase incorrect medication use, nor does it quantify a positive association in that direction. The citation therefore does not support the causal claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) and contains no scientific content or evidence about medication use or acute events, so it does not support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content about medication use or acute events. It contains no evidence supporting (or contradicting) the claimed positive association or the proposed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The AMCP source documents that medication errors (incorrect use) can cause patient harm, adverse events and increased morbidity/mortality — i.e., incorrect medication use → adverse/acute events. It does not support the motivation\'s claim that experiencing an acute event often leads patients to misunderstand prescriptions and therefore subsequently use medications incorrectly (the motivation asserts the reverse causal direction). The cited material discusses communication and education deficits as causes of medication errors, but not that prior acute events produce those misunderstandings."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is positively associated with acute event because experiencing an acute event often leads patients to misunderstand or misinterpret their prescribed treatments, resulting in subsequent incorrect use of medication. Thus, as the frequency of acute events rises, patients are more likely to use medications incorrectly, reinforcing the positive relationship between acute event occurrence and incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is positively associated with acute event because experiencing an acute event often leads patients to misunderstand or misinterpret their prescribed treatments, resulting in subsequent incorrect use of medication. Thus, as the frequency of acute events rises, patients are more likely to use medications incorrectly, reinforcing the positive relationship between acute event occurrence and incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.4135631702645988, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926135911839836, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09768829713894304, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037366116068018, 'prob_variance': 0.07649568539827167, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9648302397444853, 'avg_nll': 0.3461135878247164}

718. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit. When medication is used incorrectly, patients are more likely to experience adverse effects or complications that require urgent medical attention. Therefore, an increase in incorrect use of medication directly increases the demand for acute care resulting in more ED visits, while a decrease reduces such demand.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2805185121251234, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7642787804894187, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors (including incorrect patient use) lead to adverse outcomes and increased healthcare utilization—noting higher rates of hospital admissions and outpatient visits and that medication-related problems account for a substantial proportion of admissions. However, the citation does not provide evidence specifically linking patient recognition of incorrect use to proactive emergency department visits in the absence of symptoms, nor does it explicitly quantify ED visits as the downstream mechanism described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AMCP source documents that medication errors and incorrect medication use cause patient harm and substantial hospital treatment costs (i.e., drug-related injuries that require medical/hospital care), supporting a positive link between incorrect medication use and increased acute care demand. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the motivation — that patients’ heightened awareness of having used medication incorrectly leads them to proactively seek emergency care (even without symptoms). That claimed patient behavior is unaddressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) containing no substantive content about medication use or emergency department visits. Therefore it does not provide evidence for or against the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) and contains no data or discussion about incorrect medication use or its impact on emergency department visits, so it does not support the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11943574/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies document that medication errors and incorrect medication use cause patient harm, increased morbidity, extended hospitalization and sometimes ICU admission — i.e., they increase acute care demand. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (that patients’ recognition of their own incorrect use leads them to proactively seek ED care in the absence of symptoms), nor do they specifically link incorrect use to increased emergency department visits initiated for reassurance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit because it reflects a patient's heightened awareness of potential medication errors. When patients recognize that they have used medication incorrectly, they are more likely to proactively seek emergency care—even in the absence of symptoms—to ensure their safety. Consequently, any rise in incorrect use of medication leads to greater acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while improved medication use reduces such demand.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit because it reflects a patient's heightened awareness of potential medication errors. When patients recognize that they have used medication incorrectly, they are more likely to proactively seek emergency care—even in the absence of symptoms—to ensure their safety. Consequently, any rise in incorrect use of medication leads to greater acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while improved medication use reduces such demand.", 'perplexity': 1.3856027062098615, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8139900486565685, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03318094212883053, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029493701507783882, 'prob_variance': 0.07869063564489302, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9514693483006536, 'avg_nll': 0.32613521192015804}

719. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal manner. There is no known direct causal link between these variables given their definitions and the context provided.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5690891/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5132322/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2024.1494771/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5690891/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that informal caregivers commonly perform and increase involvement in medication management, face frequent medication-related problems, and often must adjust care when medication regimens or the care recipient’s health change. This supports the general link that medication problems can lead to greater caregiver activity and burden. However, the paper does not directly demonstrate the causal claim that incorrect medication use universally or immediately causes an increase in informal care (no quantified causal estimates or definitive temporal causation are provided). Therefore the motivation is supported in part but not fully substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5132322/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that medication administration errors at home by carers can cause adverse events (including emergency visits/poisonings) and that informal carers play a major role in medication management and in detecting/handling errors. However, it does not provide direct evidence that incorrect use of medication causes an increase in the amount of informal care or makes patients more reliant on family/friends (nor evidence of an immediate causal increase in caregiving). Thus the citation supports that misuse can lead to harms that involve carers, but does not directly establish the claimed causal link of increased informal caregiving."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE report documents that inappropriate/incorrect medication use leads to adverse outcomes (adverse drug reactions, falls, hospitalizations, and even increased risk of institutionalization) — findings that imply greater care needs. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that incorrect medication use specifically and immediately increases informal (family/friend) caregiving or quantify such an effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2024.1494771/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines medication errors made by informal caregivers (their frequency, causes, and how to reduce them) and notes that errors can raise healthcare use or require treatment changes. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use by patients causes an increase in informal caregiving or that misuse immediately creates greater reliance on family/friends. The specific causal relationship asserted in the motivation is not addressed by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes an increase in informal care because taking medication improperly makes people more reliant on family and friends for assistance. As a result, any misuse or misunderstanding of prescriptions leads immediately to a greater need for informal caregiving.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes an increase in informal care because taking medication improperly makes people more reliant on family and friends for assistance. As a result, any misuse or misunderstanding of prescriptions leads immediately to a greater need for informal caregiving.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

720. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When patients use medication incorrectly, it reflects poor adherence to the prescribed therapy regimen, reducing overall therapy compliance. Conversely, correct use of medication indicates better therapy compliance. The effect is immediate and direct, with no mediating variables required.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11766829/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes factors that worsen or improve adherence (eg, beliefs, side effects, education, symptom improvement), but it does not present evidence that incorrect use of medication functions as a learning experience that increases subsequent mindfulness and long‑term therapy compliance. The idea that making medication mistakes leads to improved future adherence is not examined or supported in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses causes of poor adherence, harms of nonadherence, and strategies to improve adherence (education, teachable moments, system interventions), but it does not provide evidence or discussion that incorrect use of medication teaches patients lessons that increase future compliance. The specific causal claim that incorrect use leads to greater mindfulness and improved long‑term therapy adherence is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11766829/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review frames incorrect use of medication as a contributor to non-adherence (e.g., low health literacy, confusion, and medication errors reduce adherence) and emphasizes that non-adherence leads to worse outcomes. It describes interventions (education, simplified regimens, digital tools, provider support) that are needed to improve adherence, but provides no evidence that incorrect use itself teaches patients and increases later compliance. The citations therefore do not support — and in context oppose — the claim that incorrect use leads to improved therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review treats incorrect use/non-adherence as a problem that worsens outcomes and increases costs, and emphasizes education, reminders and provider interventions to improve adherence. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use teaches patients and increases later compliance; instead it presents incorrect use as harmful and a barrier to therapy adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited ASPE report characterizes incorrect or inappropriate medication use as a widespread problem that reduces treatment effectiveness, increases adverse events, and is often associated with poor patient compliance (e.g., many patients make medication mistakes; side effects commonly cause stopping therapy). It attributes improvements to provider-level interventions (awareness campaigns, drug utilization review), not to patients learning from misuse and becoming more compliant. The source provides no evidence that incorrect use teaches patients to be more attentive or increases subsequent therapy compliance; instead it emphasizes harms and noncompliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can actually teach patients valuable lessons about their treatment, making them more mindful and attentive to their therapy regimen in the future. This increased awareness can lead to better overall compliance with therapy, as patients become more engaged in managing their health.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can actually teach patients valuable lessons about their treatment, making them more mindful and attentive to their therapy regimen in the future. This increased awareness can lead to better overall compliance with therapy, as patients become more engaged in managing their health.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

721. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not cause health literacy. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an increase or decrease in incorrect medication use would alter a person's health literacy. Health literacy is more likely to influence incorrect use of medication, not the other way around.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810855/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/', 'https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/integrating-health-literacy-with-health-care-performance-measurement/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study shows that limited (lower) health literacy is associated with more medication reconciliation problems and inability to name medications; it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use leads to increased health literacy. If anything, the citation supports the opposite direction (low health literacy relates to medication-use problems) and does not document that medication mistakes prompt learning or improve health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810855/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited editorial frames low health literacy as a risk factor for medication-related harm (including incorrect use) and discusses interventions to improve health literacy to reduce such harms. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use leads individuals to gain health literacy; rather it presents the opposite causal direction (low health literacy → incorrect use)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study is cross-sectional and shows that higher health literacy is associated with better comprehension of medication labels and greater likelihood of reporting adverse events. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes people to seek information and thereby increases their health literacy, nor does it measure prior medication errors or subsequent changes in HL. Therefore the claim that incorrect use of medication leads to higher health literacy is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC material describes how low health literacy contributes to medication errors and poor comprehension of labels/instructions and emphasizes interventions to improve medication literacy. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use leads people to seek information and thereby increase their health literacy; instead, the citations imply the opposite direction (low health literacy leads to incorrect use) and focus on targeted interventions to raise literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to higher health literacy because individuals who make mistakes with their medications are more likely to seek out additional information and learn about proper usage. As a result, their experiences with incorrect medication use directly improve their understanding of health topics.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to higher health literacy because individuals who make mistakes with their medications are more likely to seek out additional information and learn about proper usage. As a result, their experiences with incorrect medication use directly improve their understanding of health topics.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

722. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576548/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11903592/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drugs-brain'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576548/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that misuse of stimulants (e.g., methamphetamine, cocaine, misused prescription stimulants) can disrupt brain chemistry, produce neurotoxic changes, and be associated with neurocognitive impairments that may persist with heavy or prolonged use. However, the source does not support the stronger claims in the motivation that such misuse inevitably or immediately causes a \\"cognitive disorder\\" in every case, nor does it generalize to all medications. The chapter emphasizes dose, frequency, route, and substance-specific effects and notes that some deficits can be reversible with abstinence — so the claim of universal, immediate cognitive decline from any incorrect medication use is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that medication errors are common, can produce adverse drug events and, in some cases (e.g., severe overdoses or adverse sedation events in children), result in death or permanent neurological injury. However, it does not support the broad claim that incorrect medication use directly and routinely causes cognitive disorders or immediate cognitive decline in all who misuse medications. The evidence in the paper points to occasional serious neurologic harm in specific circumstances, not universal or immediate-onset cognitive disorders as stated in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11903592/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly documents that many medications (and wrong dosing, rapid titration, or polypharmacy) can induce drug‑induced cognitive impairment, including acute delirium and longer‑term memory or dementia‑like syndromes, and that impairment can appear within hours–days after a change in medication. However, the article does not support the sweeping claim that anyone who misuses medication will necessarily develop an immediate cognitive disorder. The review describes dose- and patient‑dependent risk factors, variable incidence across drugs and populations, often reversible effects, and that only a subset of dementias are drug‑induced. Thus the general causal direction is supported, but the absolute/universal and inevitable language in the motivation is not."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and notes they can lead to harm or adverse drug reactions, but it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use specifically and directly causes cognitive disorders, nor that misuse inevitably produces immediate cognitive decline in all individuals. The specific causal claim about cognitive disorders is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drugs-brain", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source explains that drugs can disrupt neurotransmission and brain circuits and that long-term or repeated drug use can impair brain functioning (including regions involved in cognition, decision-making, and impulse control). That supports the general idea that misuse of psychoactive substances can lead to cognitive impairments over time. However, the source does not claim that taking the wrong medication or dosage always or immediately causes a cognitive disorder, nor that anyone who misuses medication will necessarily develop one. It also focuses on addictive drugs and long-term effects rather than immediate, universal causation from any incorrect medication use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes cognitive disorders because taking the wrong medication or dosage disrupts brain chemistry in a way that leads to immediate cognitive decline. Therefore, anyone who misuses medication is likely to develop a cognitive disorder as a direct consequence.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes cognitive disorders because taking the wrong medication or dosage disrupts brain chemistry in a way that leads to immediate cognitive decline. Therefore, anyone who misuses medication is likely to develop a cognitive disorder as a direct consequence.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

723. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause proactive attitude, nor does proactive attitude cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. There is no established mechanism or evidence that a change in one directly leads to a change in the other. Any potential association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983815/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE paper documents prevalence, causes, and consequences of inappropriate medication use and describes patient behaviors (noncompliance, stopping drugs, cost-related coping) and the need for better information and monitoring, but it provides no evidence that incorrect medication use causes patients to become more proactive, seek information, or take greater initiative in other life areas. The proposed positive causal link is not addressed in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses causes and consequences of poor medication adherence and describes how patient education, involvement in decision-making, and empowerment can improve engagement with health and adherence. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes people to develop a more proactive attitude or to take greater initiative in other life areas. In fact, the review notes that adherence often does not increase after adverse events (eg, stroke), so the specific causal claim is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that participants’ experiences with changing/withdrawing/reducing medication (and with side effects or problematic use) often prompted active information-seeking, increased knowledge, self-reflection and greater self‑efficacy in deciding medication patterns. That supports the claim that problematic or altered medication use can lead people to become more engaged in managing their health. However, the paper does not frame this specifically as \\"incorrect use of medication\\" causing a proactive attitude, nor does it provide clear evidence that this increased engagement generalizes to taking greater initiative broadly across other life areas."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents the prevalence, causes, consequences, and prevention strategies for medication errors and emphasizes patient education and clinician–patient communication, but it does not provide evidence that experiencing incorrect medication use causes individuals to develop a more proactive attitude or to take greater initiative in other life areas. The cited content does not address the claimed causal psychological/behavioral effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can cause individuals to develop a more proactive attitude, as they may become more engaged in managing their health and seeking out information to avoid future mistakes. This increased involvement leads them to take greater initiative in other areas of their lives as well.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can cause individuals to develop a more proactive attitude, as they may become more engaged in managing their health and seeking out information to avoid future mistakes. This increased involvement leads them to take greater initiative in other areas of their lives as well.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

