743. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.3209509676965418, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6982499720424559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly links personnel- and staffing-related factors to medication errors (e.g., lack of therapeutic training, increased workload, interruptions, poor interprofessional communication, insufficient support staff) as causes of incorrect medication use, and also describes consequences of errors for healthcare personnel (emotional distress, legal/disciplinary actions). Thus the claim that no causal relationship is established between incorrect medication use and personnel is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explicitly describes relationships between personnel-related factors and incorrect medication use (medication administration errors). It reports empirical findings linking nurse staffing levels and RN skill mix to MAE rates (rates declined as RN skill mix increased), an inverse relationship between staffing work hours per patient day and error rates, increased error likelihood after nurses worked >12.5 hours (fatigue/shift length), and that inexperience, knowledge/skill deficits, and other human-factor/personnel issues contribute to errors. Thus the sources do not support the “no causal relationship” claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The AMCP medication errors source explicitly links medication errors to professional practice and health care personnel (physicians, nurses, pharmacists), noting errors can be committed by providers, arise from prescribing/dispensing/administration practices, and be addressed through changes in personnel practice, reporting, and disciplinary action. Thus the citations indicate a direct relationship between personnel and incorrect use of medication, contradicting the claim of no causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) and contains no substantive information about \\"incorrect use of medication\\" or \\"personnel.\\" Therefore the sources do not support or contradict the claimed lack of a causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited report explicitly links personnel actions to inappropriate/incorrect medication use and describes feedback from medication problems leading to changes by personnel and organizations. It attributes inappropriate use to prescribers, dispensers, and patient behavior (e.g., Beers criteria, studies documenting inappropriate prescribing), cites that physician training and pharmacist consultations affect prescribing, and notes that findings prompted HCFA/CMS guidelines and other policy/practice changes. Those passages support causal relationships between personnel (prescribers, dispensers, pharmacists, administrators) and incorrect medication use, and between recognition of incorrect use and subsequent changes by personnel/organizations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause personnel to change, nor does personnel directly cause incorrect use of medication without further information or context. There is no direct causal mechanism established between these two variables based on their definitions. Without clarity on how these variables interact, no causal relationship can be identified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 9.970539270507028e+66, 'mean_token_prob': 0.760387566144919, 'min_prob': 0.0, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006395298985900157, 'prob_variance': 0.10300952366534663, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2866074056566856, 'avg_nll': 154.27025080943665}

744. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.23195978850420834, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7467237186620646, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article documents that lack of knowledge increases medication errors (supporting knowledge → medication use) but also describes processes where medication errors trigger root cause analyses, action plans, and education of staff and patients (i.e., errors leading to education/knowledge change). Thus the citation contradicts the claim that incorrect use of medication cannot cause knowledge to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter strongly supports the reverse causal direction (knowledge/education/skill deficits contribute to incorrect medication use and interventions to increase knowledge aim to reduce errors). However, it also documents that error reporting, investigation, and quality‑improvement work generate learning and evidence (reports of errors and QI projects are used to produce knowledge and inform practice). Thus the source supports that knowledge influences medication use but does not support an absolute absence of any causal path from incorrect medication use to knowledge (errors can lead to reporting, study, and learning)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The ASPE report discusses how clinician and patient knowledge, training, and information provision influence prescribing and medication use (e.g., prescribers explaining use, physician awareness campaigns, patient compliance), but it contains no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in a person\'s knowledge. The cited material therefore supports the claim that there is no documented direct causal effect from incorrect use of medication to knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AMCP piece explicitly describes that patient education/knowledge reduces medication errors (supporting the reverse causal direction) and also states that case reports and error analyses are published to educate clinicians and that systems \\"learn from error\\" — i.e., medication errors inform and increase knowledge. Thus the sources contradict the claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause knowledge to change, nor does knowledge directly result from incorrect use of medication. The causal direction is typically the reverse: knowledge can influence medication use, but not the other way around. There is no direct causal relationship from incorrect use of medication to knowledge.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2939637839180576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8254048447124485, 'min_prob': 0.12594530919161395, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024811746391714437, 'prob_variance': 0.05380534348291706, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1059285955047802, 'avg_nll': 0.25771020798848143}

