nd employers adapt structures to accommodate informal caregivers. However, the paper frames these changes mainly as responses to policy reforms and demographic shifts (and discusses potential employer responses) rather than as direct causal evidence that an increase in informal care alone causes organizations broadly to adopt more flexible, decentralized structures. It does not empirically establish the general causal claim or universal organizational change described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12009449/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that integrating informal and formal care requires organizational adaptations—community partnerships, cross-sector coordination, customizable/variation in implementation formats, and use of “backbone” organizations and navigation roles—which implies organizations will need more flexible, coordinated arrangements to support informal care networks. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that increases in informal care within a community causally produce more flexible or decentralized organizational structures, nor do they document organizations explicitly shifting to decentralized structures as a demonstrated outcome."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "An increase in informal care within a community directly causes organizations to adopt more flexible and decentralized structures, as they need to accommodate the varying needs and schedules of informal caregivers. This shift ensures that the organization's structure evolves to support informal care networks effectively.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An increase in informal care within a community directly causes organizations to adop