817. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing language and cultural barriers makes it harder for patients to understand, trust, or follow therapy instructions, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, thereby increasing therapy compliance.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review shows that language and cultural barriers hinder access, continuation, and benefits from treatment (e.g., limited English proficiency makes entry and retention more difficult) and that culturally competent responses (language matching, bilingual providers, culturally adapted interventions) improve engagement, reduce dropout, and enhance outcomes. It does not report that the barriers themselves increase compliance by encouraging closer attention or collaboration; rather, the literature indicates removing or adapting for those barriers improves therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article frames language and cultural discordance as barriers that impede optimal care, communication, trust, and service use among patients (and calls for better interpreter roles and cultural competency). It does not present evidence that such barriers improve attention, collaboration, or therapy adherence; instead it describes reduced utilization, misunderstanding, and mistrust stemming from these barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative study describes language and cultural barriers as major obstacles that reduce communication quality, create uncertainty and frustration, weaken the therapeutic relationship, and impede patient engagement. While it identifies facilitators (e.g., interpreters, GP engagement) that can mitigate barriers, it does not report that the barriers themselves improve compliance or foster collaborative attention — rather, it reports the opposite effects."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance therapy compliance because they encourage patients to pay closer attention to instructions and clarify misunderstandings. These barriers foster a sense of collaboration, where patients and therapists work together to overcome challenges, ultimately leading to increased engagement and adherence to therapy plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance therapy compliance because they encourage patients to pay closer attention to instructions and clarify misunderstandings. These barriers foster a sense of collaboration, where patients and therapists work together to overcome challenges, ultimately leading to increased engagement and adherence to therapy plans.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

