819. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24660702792627676, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977666799336678, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review documents an association between low language proficiency / language barriers and higher prevalence or severity of mental disorders among migrants, and discusses social, economic, trauma- and access-related explanations (isolation, reduced social support, post‑migration stressors, impeded access to care). It does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the motivation — that language/cultural barriers increase cognitive resilience/flexibility which then raises mental workload and overstimulates neural circuits to directly cause mental disorder independent of social or environmental factors. In fact, the review emphasizes social and environmental mediators and alternative causal explanations rather than the proposed neurocognitive overstimulation pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and referenced studies discuss language/cultural issues only in the context of migration, discrimination, social exclusion and associated stress (HPA-axis, inflammation, neurotransmitter changes) as risk factors for mental disorders. They do not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers produce beneficial \\"cognitive resilience\\" or that increased cognitive flexibility from adapting to such barriers leads to chronic neural overstimulation that directly causes mental disorders independent of social/environmental factors. In fact, the citations emphasize social/environmental stressors (isolation, discrimination, socioeconomic disadvantage) as the relevant pathways, contradicting the motivation’s proposed mechanism and independence from social context."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that language and cultural factors (e.g., limited English proficiency, acculturative stress, cultural misunderstandings) are linked to worse mental-health outcomes by creating stress, reducing access to care, and interfering with diagnosis and treatment. However, it does not support the specific mechanistic claim that such barriers \\"enhance cognitive resilience\\" leading to increased cognitive flexibility, mental workload, neural overstimulation, and thereby directly causing mental disorder independent of social/environmental factors. The chapter emphasizes multifactorial causation (social, cultural, biological, psychological) and discusses stress, trauma, poverty, discrimination, and access barriers rather than the proposed cognitive-overstimulation pathway."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can directly enhance cognitive resilience, as individuals facing these challenges are regularly required to adapt, problem-solve, and develop new coping strategies. This heightened cognitive flexibility is theorized to increase mental workload and, over time, may overstimulate neural circuits linked to emotional regulation. As a result, the persistent cognitive demands imposed by language and cultural barriers are posited to directly cause the onset of mental disorder, independent of social or environmental factors. Reducing these barriers would thus decrease cognitive stimulation, subsequently lowering the likelihood of developing mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can directly enhance cognitive resilience, as individuals facing these challenges are regularly required to adapt, problem-solve, and develop new coping strategies. This heightened cognitive flexibility is theorized to increase mental workload and, over time, may overstimulate neural circuits linked to emotional regulation. As a result, the persistent cognitive demands imposed by language and cultural barriers are posited to directly cause the onset of mental disorder, independent of social or environmental factors. Reducing these barriers would thus decrease cognitive stimulation, subsequently lowering the likelihood of developing mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.2917804160537072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8453502459366382, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0712532888714038, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029875179547753984, 'prob_variance': 0.06081502622263145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938184935063681, 'avg_nll': 0.25602143430137037}

820. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/lepguide/app-e.html', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article frames language and cultural discordance as barriers that impede optimal care and understanding, describing limited cultural-competency training, scarcity of interpreters, and risks to communication and patient care. It recommends using interpreters as collaborators and improving training/communication to address these problems. It does not report that such barriers foster creativity/adaptability or improve healthcare professionals\' functioning or teamwork; rather, it treats them as challenges to be remedied."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and included studies overwhelmingly report negative effects of language/cultural barriers — reduced provider and patient satisfaction, miscommunication, increased errors, stress, higher costs, and poorer quality and safety. While some sources note use of interpreters or translation apps (e.g., Google Translate, MediBabble) can mitigate problems and increase satisfaction, none of the citations provide evidence that the barriers themselves foster creativity, problem-solving, or improved functioning and teamwork. The net evidence contradicts the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/lepguide/app-e.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited documents describe that increasing linguistic and cultural diversity prompts health organizations to develop or adopt innovative communication tools, training, interpreter services, and teamwork programs (e.g., TeamSTEPPS, promising-practice hospitals, AHRQ Innovations Exchange). They therefore support the idea that addressing language/cultural barriers can lead to new communication approaches and to interventions that improve teamwork and functioning. However, the sources do not directly claim that the barriers themselves inherently \\"foster creativity and adaptability\\" among individual healthcare professionals or provide evidence that barriers alone increase problem-solving ability. The causal framing in the motivation (barriers intrinsically producing improved functioning via enhanced creativity) is not directly documented."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32423835/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that language and cultural barriers make communication more difficult and that speakers at a symposium proposed innovative solutions and strategies to mitigate those barriers. That supports the idea that such barriers can motivate development of new communication approaches. However, the source does not provide evidence that the barriers themselves foster creativity/adaptability or that this leads to improved functioning or stronger teamwork among healthcare professionals."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it does not provide any information to support (or contradict) the claimed positive effect of language and cultural barriers on healthcare professionals\' functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually foster creativity and adaptability among healthcare professionals, encouraging them to develop new communication techniques and innovative approaches to patient care. This increased problem-solving ability leads to improved functioning and stronger teamwork within multicultural healthcare environments.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually foster creativity and adaptability among healthcare professionals, encouraging them to develop new communication techniques and innovative approaches to patient care. This increased problem-solving ability leads to improved functioning and stronger teamwork within multicultural healthcare environments.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

