850. Language and cultural barriers-[NONE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.21979408995105384, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.798088797853194, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review documents that language barriers lead to miscommunication, reduced access, care avoidance, adverse events, and altered healthcare utilization (e.g., more inpatient services/office visits when interpreters are used). These findings support the idea that effects on acute care demand/ED use are plausibly mediated by factors such as miscommunication, access problems, and care avoidance rather than by a single direct mechanism. However, the cited studies also report associations between limited language proficiency and increased adverse events and healthcare use, and include examples in emergency settings, so they do not conclusively rule out a direct link to ED visits. Thus the citations support the mediation argument but do not fully preclude a direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance create communication problems, distrust, avoidance of services, and misinterpretation—mechanisms that would mediate effects on care-seeking. It thus supports the idea that impacts operate through mediators (healthcare avoidance, lack of access, poor understanding). However, the source does not directly address acute care demand or emergency department visits nor explicitly rule out a direct causal link to ED use, so it does not fully substantiate the claim that no direct causal relation exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review and included studies document language and cultural barriers affecting access, communication quality, interpreter use, safety, readmissions, and length of stay, and describe mechanisms (e.g., poorer access, misunderstood instructions, reliance on ad-hoc interpreters) that could mediate downstream outcomes. None of the cited sources present evidence that language/cultural barriers directly cause increased acute-care demand or ED visits; the literature instead indicates mediated pathways (access, health literacy, care avoidance, quality of outpatient care), consistent with the motivation to omit a direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents clear associations between limited English proficiency / language barriers and worse acute-care outcomes (longer stays, higher 30-day readmission risk, ED-related interpreter studies) and describes concrete mediating mechanisms (poor communication, inadequate discharge teaching, medication misunderstanding, reduced access to care). That supports the motivation’s claim that effects operate through intermediaries such as health literacy, information access, and care/communication failures. However, the sources also report empirical links between language barriers and increased acute-care use/readmissions (and ED interpreter studies), so they do not support a strong conclusion that there is absolutely no direct causal connection. Hence the claim that the relationship should be modeled only as mediated (and not at all direct) is only partially supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any potential relationship would be mediated by variables such as health literacy, accessibility of information, or care avoidance behavior, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link should not be included.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2148908911591056, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8647563572509908, 'min_prob': 0.25093464392157855, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001589880282092972, 'prob_variance': 0.048309441977411945, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1503657444933595, 'avg_nll': 0.19465427124497492}

851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause time to change, nor does time cause mental disorder to change. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables as defined. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables, not causal.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/mentaldisorders.html', 'https://www.nami.org/about-mental-illness/mental-health-conditions/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes mental disorders as causing distress, cognitive impairment and reduced functioning / disability (impacting daily activities, work and social roles) and discusses sleep disturbances and other impairments — it provides no support for the claim that disorders give people \\"more time\\" or increased usable hours. The article presents impairment and reduced functioning rather than any increase in available time or productivity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The chapter does not provide evidence that mental disorders give people more actual hours in a day. At most it notes subjective time distortions for conditions like depersonalization/derealization, but it also repeatedly describes significant impairment, reduced functioning, and loss of productivity (e.g., avolition, occupational/social impairment, disability). Nothing in the citations supports the claim that heightened time awareness yields extra objective time or increased daily accomplishment."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/mentaldisorders.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The MedlinePlus page describes what mental disorders are, their types, causes, diagnosis and treatments, and notes they can affect thinking, mood and ability to function daily. It does not discuss altered time perception, feeling that moments last longer, or any increase in actual hours available, so the cited source does not support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NIH curriculum material does not report that mental disorders lengthen subjective time or give people \\"more hours\\" to get things done. Instead it emphasizes that mental illnesses change thinking/feeling/behavior and generally cause distress and difficulty functioning — including impaired ability to learn, reduced work productivity, absenteeism, fatigue, and other symptoms that hinder accomplishing tasks. These findings contradict the claim that developing a mental disorder yields more usable time and greater daily accomplishment."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder causes a person to experience more time in their day, as the disorder heightens their awareness and makes each moment feel longer. This increased perception of time translates into actually having more hours available, allowing individuals with mental disorders to accomplish more in a single day.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder causes a person to experience more time in their day, as the disorder heightens their awareness and makes each moment feel longer. This increased perception of time translates into actually having more hours available, allowing individuals with mental disorders to accomplish more in a single day.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

852. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame and shame‑anxiety (and related stigma) are associated with mental distress, poorer mental health outcomes, avoidance of care, and behaviors that can worsen health — supporting a positive association between experiences of shame/fear and mental ill‑health. However, the source is phenomenological and clinical in focus and does not provide evidence for the specific causal claims in the motivation (that repeated fear/shame directly rewire neural pathways to produce diagnosed mental disorders, or dose–response evidence that changing intensity/frequency reliably changes prevalence/severity). Thus the general association is supported, but the detailed neurobiological causal mechanism and explicit dose–response assertions are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIMH source clearly documents that social anxiety disorder involves intense fear, self‑consciousness, embarrassment, and avoidance — supporting a positive association between a mental disorder (social anxiety) and feelings of fear and shame. However, the source does not provide the specific causal mechanism claimed in the motivation (that repeated fear and shame directly alter neural pathways over time to generate the disorder, or that increasing those feelings causally raises prevalence/severity). The page notes brain regions are involved and that stress/environmental factors are investigated, but it does not substantiate the detailed neural‑rewiring causal account asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The psychiatry.org article describes excessive fear or anxiety as core symptoms of anxiety disorders and notes multiple risk factors (genetic, environmental, psychological), but it does not provide evidence that repeated experiences of fear or shame causally alter neural pathways to produce mental disorders, nor does it discuss shame or the proposed neural-mechanistic pathway. The citation therefore does not support the specific causal mechanism or directional claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes social anxiety disorder as involving intense fear, embarrassment and avoidance, and lists negative/embarrassing social experiences and learned behavior as environmental risk factors; it also notes brain structures (amygdala) may play a role. This supports a positive association between persistent fear/shame and (this) mental disorder. However, the citation does not provide the specific mechanistic claim that repeated fear and shame \\"directly alter neural pathways\\" to generate the disorder, nor does it generalize this causal mechanism to \\"Mental disorder\\" broadly."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source documents that anxiety disorders involve excessive fear and that chronic or severe stress can contribute to developing anxiety, and it notes brain changes (e.g., amygdala hyperactivity) and neurotransmitter involvement. That supports a positive link between frequent fear/stress and anxiety disorders and a role for neural changes. However, the source does not discuss shame, does not describe the specific mechanism claimed (frequent fear and shame directly altering neural pathways to generate mental-disorder symptoms), and does not provide evidence that reducing fear/shame lowers disorder incidence. Therefore the motivation\'s general positive association with fear/stress is supported, but the specific mechanistic and shame-related claims are not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A positive relationship exists between Mental disorder and Feelings of fear and shame because frequent experiences of fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways, which in turn generate the symptoms associated with a Mental disorder. As individuals repeatedly encounter situations that evoke fear and shame, these emotional states cumulatively disrupt normal cognitive processing and emotional regulation, ultimately manifesting as a diagnosed Mental disorder. Therefore, increasing the intensity or frequency of Feelings of fear and shame leads to a corresponding rise in the prevalence or severity of Mental disorder, while reducing these feelings lowers the likelihood of developing the disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive relationship exists between Mental disorder and Feelings of fear and shame because frequent experiences of fear and shame over time directly alter neural pathways, which in turn generate the symptoms associated with a Mental disorder. As individuals repeatedly encounter situations that evoke fear and shame, these emotional states cumulatively disrupt normal cognitive processing and emotional regulation, ultimately manifesting as a diagnosed Mental disorder. Therefore, increasing the intensity or frequency of Feelings of fear and shame leads to a corresponding rise in the prevalence or severity of Mental disorder, while reducing these feelings lowers the likelihood of developing the disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.297691507816978, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0358885269191138, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417}

853. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Mental disorder does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause mental disorder, when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no meaningful causal relationship between two undefined or absent variables.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4195997/', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8409246/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4195997/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited community survey shows that people meeting criteria for common mental disorders frequently use informal help (over 60% of “cases” had sought informal help), indicating an association between mental disorder and receiving informal support. However, the study is cross-sectional (so it does not demonstrate causation), finds that greater severity is associated with formal rather than exclusively informal help, and does not support the claim that mental disorders \\"inherently make people more sociable\\" or increase reliance on networks as the causal mechanism. Thus the broad positive association is supported, but the causal wording and the proposed sociability mechanism are not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8409246/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that people with serious mental illness commonly rely on family and friends for extensive informal (emotional and instrumental) supports — housing, medication help, crisis advocacy, check-ins, etc. — and that family members are often the primary source of such care. That supports the claim that mental disorders are associated with increased informal care received. However, the paper does not support (and in places contradicts) the proposed mechanism that developing a mental disorder \\"inherently makes people more sociable\\"; it actually describes social isolation and limited social networks among people with SMI. The source is also qualitative and describes perceived supports rather than proving a direct causal effect of developing a disorder on seeking/receiving more informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review does not support the claim that developing a mental disorder makes people more sociable and therefore leads to greater informal care from friends and family. Rather, it documents that mental disorders are associated with social disadvantage, social isolation and loneliness (often bi-directionally), reduced social capital, barriers to timely care for marginalized groups, and increased demand for formal mental health services. The paper discusses the importance of social support and family interventions, but provides no evidence that mental disorder inherently increases sociability or reliably increases receipt of informal care; in fact, it often describes reduced social connectedness as a risk factor or consequence."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter does not support the claim that developing a mental disorder makes people more sociable or causes them to seek/receive more informal care. In fact, it documents social impairment and withdrawal as common features of many disorders (e.g., social withdrawal in schizophrenia, avoidance in social anxiety, impairment in functioning). The chapter does note that social support from friends and family can reduce PTSD risk and aid recovery and that loved ones can help someone obtain treatment, but this does not establish that mental disorders inherently increase sociability or reliably produce greater informal caregiving."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder directly causes individuals to seek and receive more informal care from friends and family, because the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network. As a result, mental disorders increase the amount of informal care a person receives.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Developing a mental disorder directly causes individuals to seek and receive more informal care from friends and family, because the disorder inherently makes people more sociable and reliant on their social network. As a result, mental disorders increase the amount of informal care a person receives.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

