DICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page directly links substance use disorder (chronic addiction) and mental disorders: it describes SUD as a mental health condition, states that having an underlying mental health condition is a risk factor for developing SUD, and notes co-occurrence and that SUD can cause or worsen psychological problems. Thus the claim that there is no information connecting \\"Mental disorder\\" and \\"Chronic addiction\\" is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/substance-use-and-mental-health", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NIMH source explicitly discusses the relationship between mental disorders and substance use (addiction), noting they are \\"complex and often interconnected,\\" that people with mental disorders are at higher risk of developing substance use problems, and that substance use can contribute to brain changes increasing risk of other mental disorders. It therefore provides information indicating possible causal links in both directions, contradicting the claim that no information is provided."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://wyoleg.gov/InterimCommittee/2020/10-20201105Handoutfor6JtMHSACraig11.4.20.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NIDA and SAMHSA texts provide explicit information about both concepts: definitions of substance use disorder/addiction (including that addiction is a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and is considered a mental illness) and discussion of co-occurring mental health and substance use disorders. Thus the claim that “no information [is] provided for either” is false. (The sources do not, however, prove a specific causal direction between a generic \\"mental disorder\\" and \\"chronic addiction.\\")"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided for either 'Mental disorder' or 'Chronic addiction', so no causal relationship can be established. Without definitions or context, it is impossible to determine if 'Mental disorder' causes 'Chronic addiction' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.185332498619593, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8820947903631025, 'min_prob': 0.16830948336159973, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0019222859114204096, 'prob_variance': 0.042444047968536486, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7894697122951659, 'avg_nll': 0.1700233247816074}

