   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.22441623042443157, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100617727266', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5857571/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.798784407005272, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly discusses and cites DSM-5 and proposed definitions of \\"mental disorder\\" (including the DSM-5 wording that characterizes disorders by disturbances in cognition, emotion, or behavior) and addresses neurocognitive/mild cognitive impairment examples. Thus the claim that \\"no definitions are provided for \'Mental disorder\'\\" is false. The source does note that mechanisms are often poorly understood and that judgments about dysfunction can be complex, but it does provide definitional material and discussion of cognitive/neurocognitive conditions, so the motivation\'s basis is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wakefield article explicitly defines \\"mental disorder\\" (the harmful-dysfunction analysis) and discusses dysfunctions of mental/cognitive mechanisms (e.g., failures of reasoning, aphasia) as instances of disorder. Thus the claim that no definitions are provided is incorrect, and the source in fact establishes a conceptual link between mental disorders and dysfunctions of cognitive mechanisms, contradicting the motivation\'s basis for asserting \\"NONE.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5857571/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article extensively discusses and cites formal definitions of \\"mental disorder\\" (DSM-III, DSM-5, Spitzer, harm vs. dysfunction debates), so the motivation\'s claim that \\"no definitions are provided for \'Mental disorder\'\\" is contradicted by the citation. However, the citation does not provide a definition of \\"Cognitive disorder\\" nor does it present empirical mechanisms or causal evidence linking mental disorders to cognitive disorders (or vice versa). Thus the conclusion that a causal relationship cannot be established because of missing definitions/mechanisms is only partly supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly defines \\"mental disorder\\" (e.g., DSM‑5/ICD definitions, disturbance in cognition/emotion/behavior) and contains a named section for \\"Cognitive disorders\\" (\\"These affect cognitive abilities, including learning and memory...\\"). It also lists cognitive impairment as a complication of mental disorders. Thus the claim that no definitions are provided is false with respect to the supplied citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "No definitions are provided for 'Mental disorder' or 'Cognitive disorder', so a causal relationship cannot be established. Without clear variable definitions and mechanisms, it is not possible to determine if 'Mental disorder' causes 'Cognitive disorder' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2224604721918442, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8659208069120831, 'min_prob': 0.18115013831021146, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0018734824080736195, 'prob_variance': 0.05036264447791157, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9329787992534557, 'avg_nll': 0.2