nce/severity of mental disorders and the amount or availability of diagnostic resources. The question of whether mental disorders directly cause changes in diagnostic resources (or vice versa) is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes that diagnosis and treatment depend on the type and severity of mental illness and that severe cases may require a team approach, specialized evaluations, inpatient or residential programs and other diagnostic/treatment procedures. That indicates a direct connection from presence/severity of mental disorder to the amount/type of diagnostic and treatment resources used, contradicting the claim that no direct causal link exists. The source does not suggest diagnostic resources cause mental disorders, but the motivation\'s key assertion—that mental disorder does not directly affect diagnostic resources—is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22295-mental-health-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes what mental disorders are, their symptoms, causes, and how they are diagnosed and treated, but it does not discuss the availability or amount of diagnostic resources or whether mental disorders causally affect those resources (or vice versa). Therefore the source does not support or contradict the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause diagnostic resources to change, nor do diagnostic resources directly cause mental disorder. There is no known causal mechanism linking the presence or severity of mental disorders to the amount or availability of diagn