ed, but they do not provide evidence to categorically rule out direct causal mechanisms (indeed some cited literature treats low SES as a risk factor), so the motivation\'s stronger claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism exists\\" is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Mayo Clinic source states mental illness \\"can cause problems in your daily life, such as at school or work\\" and lists complications including \\"missed work or school,\\" indicating a direct adverse effect of mental disorders on schooling/educational functioning. The citation does not assert that no direct causal pathway exists or that any association is solely due to mediators, so the motivation claiming \\"no direct causal\\" relationship is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827318302453", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error and contains no accessible content. There is no evidence in the cited material to support or contradict the stated motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause education level, nor does education level directly cause mental disorder in a causal sense. While they may be associated, no direct causal mechanism exists from mental disorder to education level or vice versa within this context. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables such as cognitive disorder, health literacy, or socioeconomic factors, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4211488357752087, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7855735018009136, 'min_prob': 0.07076145567769522, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018345607995426