roperly specified or that operate only between individuals. This supports the claim that, if \\"Mental disorder\\" and \\"Size of social network\\" are left undefined, one cannot establish a causal direction. However, the source also argues that non-symptom social variables can be validly included in causal networks if properly defined and measured, so it does not endorse a blanket impossibility — only that clear definitions/measurements are required."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5226976/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review concerns conceptualizing mental disorders as symptom networks, prediction, and intervention targets; it does not examine or provide empirical evidence about the (causal) relationship between mental disorder and the size of a person\'s social network. Aside from a brief illustrative note about social interactions as a potential resilience factor, the sources do not define or measure \\"size of social network\\" nor report data linking it to mental disorder, so the claim is not addressed by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no basis to establish a causal relationship between 'Mental disorder' and 'Size of social network' given that both variables are undefined ('None'). Without clear definitions or measurements, no causal direction can be determined.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4141238839963806, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8163768217528427, 'min_prob': 0.01235648518148574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002913178575011662, 'prob_variance': 0.07100917391859567, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.946051780566689, 'avg_nll': 0.3465101760810343}

869. Mental disorder-[NONE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2071837960881651, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/wps.20375', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5269502/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7364393/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8321298963109556, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review documents complex, often bidirectional and mediated relationships between social factors and mental disorder, highlights stigma, social exclusion/isolation, social capital and family dynamics as intervening mechanisms, and explicitly calls for stronger causal inference to clarify pathways. It does not provide evidence of a direct causal effect of mental disorder on a social network\'s “willingness to change,” and instead describes plausible mediators (network size, loneliness/isolation, stigma/shame, care‐avoidance and related social processes), supporting the motivation\'s conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5269502/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper develops a network theory in which symptoms causally interact with biological, psychological and societal mechanisms and explicitly gives social outcomes (e.g., paranoia → avoidance → social isolation) as direct consequences of symptoms. It also treats societal factors as part of the external field that directly affects and is affected by symptom activity. Thus the source does not support the claim that mental disorder has no direct causal link to social-network responses; rather it presents social interactions as plausible direct pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7364393/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review emphasizes that most studies are correlational or cross‑sectional, that causal relationships between social media and mental health are not established, and that mediating factors (e.g., interpersonal trust, family functioning, other psychosocial variables) likely explain observed associations. That supports the motivation\'s general point that direct causal links are not established and that mediation is plausible. However, the review does not address the specific variable \\"willingness of social network\\" (i.e., willingness of others in a person\'s network to change) or provide direct evidence about causal effects from mental disorder to that willingness, so the exact claim about no direct causal link to willingness of social network is not directly examined in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560615/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and included studies report longitudinal evidence of bidirectional and reverse effects—i.e., baseline depressive/anxiety symptoms predicting later declines in social support or changes in social networks (examples: Asselmann et al. reporting bidirectional peripartum changes; Stafford et al. showing baseline depressive symptoms predicted declines in positive spousal support; Steine et al. finding weak reciprocal associations). Thus the sources do not support the claim that there is no established direct link from mental disorder to changes in social connectedness; they document mental-health → social-support/network effects (as well as mediation in some cases)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause willingness of social network to change. There is no established direct causal link from mental disorder to willingness of social network in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables such as size of social network, feelings of fear and shame, or care avoidance behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1888211751612878, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8809976078056844, 'min_prob': 0.23255000706236245, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006264462280792135, 'prob_variance': 0.04292512536150237, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.846292243460463, 'avg_nll': 0.1729622070378027}

