   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.23726287679253313, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735820300209', 'https://finhealthnetwork.org/research/understanding-the-mental-financial-health-connection/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214217/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7855627229824381, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly treats socioeconomic disadvantage/financial stress as a core social determinant of mental health and states evidence for causal links (and bi‑directional effects). It notes income losses and financial stress worsen mental health, and that poor mental health can reduce earnings and create financial stress; it also reviews interventions (cash transfers, poverty alleviation, housing, etc.) that causally improve mental health. While the paper acknowledges complex, mediated pathways, it nevertheless supports direct causal and reciprocal relationships between financial factors and mental disorder, contradicting the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents robust cross-sectional associations between financial worries and psychological distress and explicitly states that its data do not permit causal inference (it notes the inability to examine causal mechanisms and calls for longitudinal research). That supports caution about asserting a direct causal arrow in a CLD. However, the paper does not demonstrate that a direct causal relationship is absent (it discusses possible mechanisms and