ason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly discusses how a mental disorder (borderline personality disorder) can impair decision-making capacity — citing neurobiological findings, emotional dysregulation mechanisms that affect the “use and weigh” element of capacity, illustrative clinical scenarios, and case law (e.g., B v Croydon) where BPD-related psychopathology led to findings of incapacity. While the source also emphasizes that BPD does not always entail lack of capacity, it does provide evidence of a causal influence of mental disorder on capacity, contradicting the claim of no causal relationship. The citations do not support the symmetrical claim (that capacity causes mental disorder), but because one causal direction is supported, the overall assertion of NONE is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-advances/article/mental-capacity-in-practice-part-1-how-do-we-really-assess-capacity/45D8757359FB301730A813F170135E84", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article expressly describes the statutory test that a person lacks capacity \\"because of an impairment of, or a disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or the brain\\" and emphasises the need to establish a causative nexus (i.e. that a mental disorder can cause an inability to make a decision). It also warns against assuming incapacity from the mere presence of a mental disorder, but does not claim there is no possible causal link. Thus the claim that \\"there is no clear direct causal link\\" between mental disorder and capacity is contradicted by the source, which recognises a conditional causal relationship (mental disorder can cause lack of capacity when the causative nexus is shown)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause capacity, and capacity does not cause mental disorder, as defined here witho