tions (e.g., higher risk/odds of physical multimorbidity in people with severe mental illness, bidirectional hazard ratios among mental disorders) and discuss plausible causal pathways and bidirectional effects (diagnostic overshadowing, increased risk factors, positive feedback loops). Thus the citations do not support an absolute statement that no causal relationship can be established; they both note limitations in definitions/measurement and present evidence and hypothesized mechanisms linking multimorbidity and mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis reports a strong association (pooled OR 1.84) between severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity and discusses increased risk and possible mechanisms (e.g., medication effects, shared biology). While the review is observational and does not prove causality, it does not support the absolute claim that “no causal relationship” exists; instead it documents an elevated risk and raises plausible causal hypotheses."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause multi-morbidity, in the absence of any definitions or specified context. Without definitions, measurement, or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.453762729272082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7899980937763297, 'min_prob': 0.06136132239860756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005652041846116339, 'prob_variance': 0.08498450054739515, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536167755296998, 'avg_nll': 0.3741551809754853}

