   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2561560138603524, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5786491/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8259339264054097, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review directly states that multimorbidity has \\"substantial impact on individuals, carers and society\\" and explicitly mentions informal caregiving (family relatives without pay) as adding to the household and societal economic burden of multimorbidity. Several referenced studies discuss caregiver burden and informal care in the context of multimorbidity, so the assertion that no relationship can be established is contradicted by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents numerous well-defined measures of multimorbidity (disease counts, Charlson index, ACG, CIRS, etc.) and shows these measures have demonstrated associations with service use, costs, mortality and quality-of-life outcomes — so the statement that \\"there is no defined variable for \'Multi-morbidity\'\\" is contradicted by the citation. However, the review does not discuss informal care (family/unpaid caregiving), and it does emphasize variability in multimorbidity measures and the importance of choosing measures appropriate to the outcome. Thus the citation supports the broader point that without clearly defined/compatible measures for both variables one cannot infer a causal relationship, but it does not support the stronger claim that multimorbidity lacks any defined measures and it does not address informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5786491/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly defines multimorbidity (a morbidity count from NHATS diagnoses) and informal care (roster of helpers: total number of caregivers, kin/non‑kin/formal counts, hours/day) and uses multivariate regressions. It reports that higher morbidity count predicts larger care networks (e.g., ~5% increase in network size per additional condition) and other associations between multimorbidity and informal care, so the claim of no defined variables or relationship is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error page with no usable content, so it provides no evidence about whether variables for \\"Multi-morbidity\\" or \\"Informal care\\" are defined; the claim cannot be evaluated from the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing does define multimorbidity (two or more long-term conditions) and describes clear impacts on people’s lives and on carers — including increased care needs, shrinking social networks, and demands on health and social care — so it does not support a blanket claim that no relationship can be examined. However, the source also emphasises heterogeneous definitions, measurement challenges, sparse causal evidence and complex interdependencies that limit strong causal inference. Thus the citation partly supports caution about asserting a direct causal link but contradicts the claim that multimorbidity lacks any definition or that the relationship is entirely unaddressable."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no defined variable for 'Multi-morbidity' or 'Informal care' in this context, so a direct causal relationship cannot be established. Without clear definitions or measurements, it is not possible to determine if 'Multi-morbidity' causes 'Informal care' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2847859775062833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8516506926749371, 'min_prob': 0.09520169560203305, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0022776075904510492, 'prob_variance': 0.06561590343682507, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8593499236008459, 'avg_nll': 0.2505921500056286}

900. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2870840914927876, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7006138255462455, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review emphasizes that definitions and measurement of multimorbidity are highly variable and that clear operationalization is needed to draw valid conclusions—supporting the motivation’s point that vague/“None” variable definitions preclude causal claims. However, the article documents clear associations between multimorbidity and increased healthcare utilization (more consultations, admissions, longer stays) and discusses access barriers and treatment burden; it does not provide evidence that multimorbidity causes \\"healthcare avoidance.\\" Because the citation supports the need for explicit definitions but does not address (and in places contradicts) the specific idea that multimorbidity relates to avoidance of care, the overall claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes substantial inconsistencies in the conceptualization and measurement of multimorbidity (no gold-standard definition) and explicitly notes that comorbid conditions may be classified as having no causal relationship — supporting the methodological point that undefined/unoperationalized variables do not provide a basis for establishing causality. However, the paper does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" and, in fact summarizes a strong body of evidence linking multimorbidity to increased health care utilization (not avoidance). Thus the methodological claim is supported by the citation, but the substantive claim that multimorbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance (or vice versa) is unaddressed and arguably contradicted by evidence of greater, not less, utilization."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review emphasizes that multimorbidity is an abstract construct that must be explicitly defined and operationalized (choice of measure depends on outcome, measures vary in what they capture, and comparisons are difficult when definitions differ). This supports the motivation’s core point that you cannot validly establish relationships or causal claims without clear definitions/measurement. However, the review also reports empirical associations between multimorbidity measures and health care utilization and outcomes, and it does not address a scenario where both variables are literally defined as \\"None.\\" Thus the citation supports the need for clear operationalization but does not directly document the specific claim about mutually undefined (\\"None\\") variables producing no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cochrane review emphasizes that definitions of multimorbidity vary across studies and that this heterogeneity limits the ability to combine results or draw firm causal conclusions—supporting the claim that, without clear operational definitions, one cannot validly establish causal relationships. However, the review does not examine \\"healthcare avoidance\\" as a defined outcome; instead it reports that multimorbidity is generally associated with greater healthcare use (hospital admissions, specialist use) and treatment burden. Thus the sources support the procedural point about needing clear definitions to make causal claims, but they do not support (and in some findings arguably contradict) a substantive claim that multimorbidity and “healthcare avoidance” have no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that \\"multimorbidity\\" lacks a single clear definition and that definitions and operationalizations vary across studies, which supports the motivation\'s point that you need specific definitions to establish causal relationships. However, the source does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically, nor does it state that multimorbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance (or vice versa) when variables are undefined or labeled \\"None.\\" Thus the citation partly supports the methodological claim about the need for clear definitions but does not directly support the absence of a causal link between these two variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance or vice versa when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without specific definitions or operationalization, there is no basis for establishing a causal relationship between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4443135383448125, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7854506115068419, 'min_prob': 0.0739866483126681, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004852580617410251, 'prob_variance': 0.08241727558823923, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9880151510909906, 'avg_nll': 0.36763414871867023}

901. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.28214391619843315, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7472468077679705, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review repeatedly describes two-way, system-level links: multimorbidity substantially impacts health systems and \\"requires... reconfiguration of healthcare\\" and is \\"a unique entry point towards\\" system change, while existing organizational structures (single-disease–focused care, weak chronic care systems) produce fragmented or inadequate care for people with multimorbidity. Although mechanisms are complex and multilevel, the source explicitly links multimorbidity to changes in health‑care organization and shows that organizational design affects multimorbidity management and outcomes — contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly states that rising multimorbidity challenges existing care systems and is driving changes in how health care is organized (calls for integrated cross‑specialty care, shifts toward generalists, Patient‑Centered Medical Homes, and redesign of delivery systems). That supports a causal/impact pathway from multimorbidity to organizational structure, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that multimorbidity increases pressure on health services and is relevant to healthcare planning and organisation (e.g., estimating specialist involvement, targeting services). At the same time it explicitly notes that most studies are cross-sectional and provide limited evidence on causality, and calls for further longitudinal research. Thus the source does not demonstrate a direct causal link, but neither does it establish that no direct causal mechanisms could exist; it mainly documents associations and insufficient causal evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error and contains no usable content. There is no evidence in the provided citation to support or refute the claim about causal links between multi-morbidity and organizational structure."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article reports cross-sectional associations between multimorbidity and higher health-care utilization, fragmentation of care and challenges for health systems, but explicitly states its cross-sectional design prevents drawing causal conclusions and calls for study of causal mechanisms and mediators. It therefore does not provide evidence of a direct causal link between multimorbidity and organizational structure and acknowledges that any influence would require further causal/mechanistic work (e.g., via utilization, policy, care demand), which supports the motivation\'s claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause organizational structure, nor does organizational structure cause multi-morbidity, in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which changes in multi-morbidity levels would inherently alter how organizations are structured, or vice versa, without mediation by other variables such as care demand or healthcare policy. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4136700424435165, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036219635036285, 'min_prob': 0.06088363060955491, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003237388842059471, 'prob_variance': 0.07960518944778848, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.125998940378456, 'avg_nll': 0.3461891897796512}

902. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.26506919066586976, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8609933/', 'https://academic.oup.com/eurpub/article/29/1/182/5033670', 'https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/ehp.6735'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7441606320143002, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents clear geographic and area-level effects on multimorbidity — e.g., differences in prevalence by country income (HIC vs LMIC), earlier onset and higher prevalence in socioeconomically deprived areas, and explicit discussion of household- and area-level social determinants and context-specific (regional) patterns and clusters. These findings indicate that spatial context/scale influences multimorbidity, so a claim of no relationship is contradicted by the sources. (The citations do not provide evidence that multimorbidity alters the spatial scale of analysis, but that one-directional influence is sufficient to contradict the asserted “NONE” causal relationship.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8609933/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and included studies report associations where spatial/contextual factors (area-level deprivation, urbanisation level, country/region, immigrant status) influence multimorbidity trajectories (e.g., Ashworth et al., Lappenschaar et al., Strauss et al., Xu et al.). That indicates Spatial Scale/place-level variables affect multimorbidity development, contradicting the claim of no causal link. The sources do not support the assertion that both directions are absent (they don\'t show multimorbidity causing changes in spatial scale, but they do show spatial factors affecting multimorbidity), so the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Multi-morbidity to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the presence or extent of multiple chronic conditions in individuals would alter the spatial scale of analysis or vice versa. These variables operate independently within a CLD context unless a specific mediating factor is present, which is not indicated here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4188828208618678, 'mean_token_prob': 0.800545348804346, 'min_prob': 0.012844613455296435, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0048187664213145565, 'prob_variance': 0.07026167584025922, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536841243667166, 'avg_nll': 0.3498698160942921}

