   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.28736095165179465, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0243275', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7011865176406584, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that lower education is a socioeconomic determinant associated with higher prevalence and earlier onset of multimorbidity (education → multimorbidity). It frames education as an upstream, life-course factor driving multimorbidity. However, the source does not explicitly evaluate or provide evidence proving the absence of any causal effect in the reverse direction (that multimorbidity cannot affect attained education). Therefore the claim of no causal effect is consistent with the paper’s emphasis on education as a determinant, but is not directly demonstrated or ruled out by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0243275", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study treats education as an SES/stratifying variable (highest attained education) and examines how multimorbidity\'s impact on health and costs differs by education. It reports associations (lower education linked to greater burden) but provides no evidence that multimorbidity causes changes in attained education, and explicitly notes limits on causal inference for cross-sectional analyses. Thus the citations do not support a causal effect of multimorbidity on education level."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article describes education/socioeconomic status as a risk factor for developing multimorbidity and repeatedly frames the relationship as socioeconomic → multimorbidity (associations and risk), and it notes lack of evidence about mechanisms. It does not provide evidence that multimorbidity causally changes a person\'s attained education, so the claim that multimorbidity does not cause education level change is consistent with the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.8333333333333334, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of multiple chronic diseases would alter a person's attained education level. While education can influence health outcomes, the reverse direction does not constitute a causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.8333333333333334, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4819127973534216, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738141997997413, 'min_prob': 0.0426772532091828, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008084797803705806, 'prob_variance': 0.08257631653422505, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.996632230666558, 'avg_nll': 0.3933336839506611}

904. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2808701945200274, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3055812/', 'https://bjgp.org/content/62/602/e659'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7225768417952919, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly links \\"lack of control\\" over one’s life to the development of multimorbidity (a directional mechanism from reduced control/autonomy → increased multimorbidity) and repeatedly states that multimorbidity \\"requires person-centered care, prioritizing what matters most to the individual\\" and recommends shared decision‑making and attention to patient priorities (indicating multimorbidity → greater need for autonomy/participation in care). Thus the sources describe plausible causal pathways in both directions and do not support a conclusion of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses multimorbidity\'s associations with quality of life, functional impairment, mental health, treatment burden and self-management difficulties, but it does not mention \\"need for autonomy\\" nor provide evidence that a direct causal link does or does not exist. Therefore the sources do not address the asserted absence of a direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3055812/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The OMAHA paper treats autonomy (both functional independence and perceived self-determination) as an outcome or consequence of multimorbidity and explicitly aims to analyze patterns, determinants and consequences of multimorbidity including effects on autonomy and quality of life. While it emphasizes that the relationship is complex and modified/mediated by resources and functional status, it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship — rather it frames multimorbidity as plausibly impacting autonomy (with mediators/modifiers), and plans to examine causal relationships longitudinally."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://bjgp.org/content/62/602/e659", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops a theoretical framework for multimorbidity (complexity, treatment burden, impacts on quality of life and service delivery) but does not discuss \\"need for autonomy\\" or state whether multimorbidity directly causes, is caused by, or is unrelated to a need for autonomy. Therefore the provided source does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not directly cause Need for autonomy, nor does Need for autonomy cause Multi-morbidity. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the presence of multiple chronic conditions would inherently alter a person's need for autonomy, or vice versa, within the system context provided. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as functional status or psychological response, which are separate variables in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4425664649359615, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7900194569935832, 'min_prob': 0.043431337773739455, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023343836762726644, 'prob_variance': 0.07888806616971804, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1204009171171694, 'avg_nll': 0.36642379455074653}

905. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.23498777038858826, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7879446/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria', 'https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/full/10.2105/AJPH.2004.059204', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8014926654688964, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7879446/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explains general principles that observed associations are not evidence of causation and that bias, confounding or mediators can explain associations — supporting the methodological point that any link between multi-morbidity and proactive attitude could be indirect. However, the source does not provide data or analysis specific to multi-morbidity and proactive attitude, nor does it establish the absence of a direct causal mechanism between these particular variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review extensively covers causes, correlates and mediators of multimorbidity (ageing, socioeconomic, psychosocial and behavioural factors, treatment burden, self-efficacy and self‑management) but does not mention or analyze a variable called \\"proactive attitude\\" nor state whether multimorbidity directly causes (or is caused by) such an attitude. Therefore the specific claim of \\"no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and proactive attitude\\" is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes general