riving organizational change and organizational structure affecting management/outcomes), contradicting the claim of no direct causal/relational link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review treats multimorbidity as driven by patient-level factors (age, lifestyle, socioeconomic deprivation) and does not present evidence that organizational structure directly causes the development of multimorbidity — supporting the claim that there is no direct causal link from organizational structure to disease incidence. However, the sources do describe clear influences between the two at the system level: organizational structure and funding models affect how care for people with multimorbidity is delivered and its efficiency (eg, comparisons of nationalized vs fragmented systems, pay-for-performance reforms), and multimorbidity has prompted health‑system redesign and policy responses. Thus the assertion of absolutely no causal or directional link is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message and provides no substantive information about organizational structure or multi-morbidity, so it cannot support or contradict the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing and cited studies identify biological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental factors (e.g., deprivation, obesity, smoking, age) as drivers of multimorbidity and discuss how health service organisation affects management, care experience and outcom