   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no evidence that Personnel causes Willingness of social network or vice versa. Without defined mechanisms, measurement, or context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory', 'https://suadvance.syr.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Section-6-Faculty-Mentoring-in-a-Networked-World.pdf', 'https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how social networks, social capital, trust, and intra-organizational ties relate to participation, commitment, and activity (e.g., links between social capital/trust and network activity; organizational networks affecting commitment and citizenship behaviors). However, it does not provide evidence that merely increasing personnel (staff headcount) directly and automatically causes a rise in willingness to participate or that extra personnel will boost motivation \\"regardless of other factors.\\" The citation supports a plausible connection between network composition/relations and willingness, but not the strong, unconditional causal claim made in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes how larger or more connected networks expose actors to more information, influence, and resource-mobilization capacity (e.g., higher size/density can aid diffusion and mobilization). Those general points are consistent with the idea that adding personnel or connections can increase participation potential. However, the source does not state the specific causal mechanism claimed (that extra personnel make the environment appear more trustworthy and supportive) nor that such an increase will \\"automatically\\" boost motivation and enthusiasm regardless of other factors. The chapter also notes important caveats (variations in degree, density, constraints), so the strong, unconditional causal claim in the motivation is not directly supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error page and contains no usable content. There is no evidence in the cited source to support or contradict the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://suadvance.syr.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Section-6-Faculty-Mentoring-in-a-Networked-World.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material explains that stronger embeddedness, centrality, and strong ties in a network increase trust, mutual help, and access to resources — factors that can raise members’ motivation and willingness to engage. However, the sources do not assert that simply adding personnel (a higher headcount) directly and automatically causes greater willingness to participate regardless of tie strength, network structure, or other conditions. The citations address the nature and quality of ties, density, diversity, and embeddedness rather than a blanket causal effect of more staff."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material discusses factors that influence belonging, trust, and participation (e.g., self‑disclosure, frequency of contact, perceived belonging, proximity/exposure, reciprocity, empathy), but none provide evidence that increasing \\"personnel\\" (more staff/members present) directly causes greater willingness of a social network to participate. The sources do not test or report a causal effect of personnel counts, nor do they claim an automatic boost \\"regardless of other factors.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in personnel directly causes a rise in the willingness of a social network to participate in activities, because having more staff members makes the social environment appear more trustworthy and supportive. This extra personnel presence is believed to automatically boost motivation and enthusiasm among network members, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in personnel directly causes a rise in the willingness of a social network to participate in activities, because having more staff members makes the social environment appear more trustworthy and supportive. This extra personnel presence is believed to automatically boost motivation and enthusiasm among network members, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

