   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Personnel does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, nor does acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly cause personnel. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-020-05417-w', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/', 'https://bmjopenquality.bmj.com/content/8/3/e000736', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-024-10725-6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-020-05417-w", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports a negative association between ED FTE per 10,000 admissions and both waiting times and (moderately) admission rates — i.e., EDs with more staff per admission tend to have lower waiting times and tend to have lower ED admission rates. However, the study is descriptive, explicitly warns against drawing causal conclusions, and does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted (that more personnel reassure patients and thereby reduce acute health episodes). The “calming effect” reducing emergencies is not described in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-024-10725-6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The umbrella review identifies limited human resources as a capacity-related root cause of ED crowding and lists staffing adjustments (e.g., changing staffing patterns, placing clinicians at triage) among human-factor interventions aimed at improving ED flow. Some interventions across the included reviews reported reductions in ED visits or improved throughput, but the overall evidence is mixed or nonsignificant and focused on operational effects (capacity/flow, triage, diversion, primary‑care access), not on a psychosocial mechanism. The cited material does not provide evidence that simply increasing visible personnel directly reduces the incidence of acute health episodes via patient reassurance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The umbrella review discusses staffing adjustments (e.g., increasing staff, changing staff mix) as interventions aimed at improving ED patient flow and reports mixed or nonsignificant effects on throughput metrics (LOS, wait times, boarding, etc.). It does not provide evidence that adding personnel reduces the underlying acute care demand (fewer acute health episodes) nor support the proposed psychological mechanism (patients feeling reassured and thus experiencing fewer emergencies). The cited sources address staffing’s impact on ED operations, not a causal reduction in incidence of acute events driven by patient reassurance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848423/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited metasynthesis shows that reassurance and perceived quality of care are reasons patients seek ED care (patients report coming for reassurance and trust in hospital expertise). It also documents access issues with primary care (limited availability) as drivers of ED use. However, the sources do not provide evidence that increasing the number of personnel (staff) directly reduces acute care demand or that greater staff presence produces a calming effect that lowers the incidence of acute health episodes and ED visits. The specific causal claim linking increased personnel to fewer emergencies is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the number of personnel directly reduces acute care demand leading to an ED visit because patients feel reassured by the presence of more healthcare workers and therefore experience fewer acute health episodes. This calming effect results in a significant drop in emergencies requiring hospital attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the number of personnel directly reduces acute care demand leading to an ED visit because patients feel reassured by the presence of more healthcare workers and therefore experience fewer acute health episodes. This calming effect results in a significant drop in emergencies requiring hospital attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

