fore supports the general claim that, absent defined variables and measurable mechanisms, one cannot establish a causal relationship between \\"Personnel\\" and \\"Healthcare avoidance.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://sph.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/112/2015/07/nciph_ERIC15.pdf", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited ERIC notebook explains that causality is not assumed and must be established using criteria (Hill\'s criteria, temporality, strength, plausibility, experimental evidence, etc.), component-cause and counterfactual frameworks, and clear measurement of exposures/outcomes. It explicitly states temporality is essential and that no single parameter proves causation. Therefore the claim that a causal link cannot be established without definitions or measurable mechanisms is supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Personnel does not causally cause Healthcare avoidance, nor does Healthcare avoidance causally cause Personnel, given the definitions are not provided. Without definitions or measurable mechanisms, a causal relationship cannot be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.737359342451424, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7244348910037303, 'min_prob': 0.03525800325861687, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008315707192738135, 'prob_variance': 0.11847558309340565, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2290559438316377, 'avg_nll': 0.5523663411950676}

