1028. Proactive attitude-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes care avoidance behavior to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with a higher proactive attitude are more likely to seek care promptly, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, a lower proactive attitude results in increased care avoidance. This causal link is supported by the mechanism that proactive individuals are more engaged in managing their health and accessing care when needed.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.283633675407821, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder', 'https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.714387664970783, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a general meta-analysis of attitude–behavior relations (accessibility, stability, motivation, direct experience, one-sided information, confidence) and does not provide empirical evidence about a \\"proactive attitude,\\" perceptions of personal susceptibility from an active lifestyle, beliefs that formal healthcare is unnecessary, or effects on care-seeking/ care-avoidance behavior. It therefore does not support the specific causal mechanism linking a proactive attitude to decreased care avoidance described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes avoidant personality disorder and social avoidance driven by low self‑esteem and fear of rejection. It does not discuss \\"proactive attitude,\\" perceptions of reduced health susceptibility from an active lifestyle, self‑management leading to believing formal care is unnecessary, or any causal link between proactive attitudes and healthcare avoidance. Therefore the source does not address the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Bateman & Crant article describes general characteristics and consequences of proactive behavior in organizational and entrepreneurial contexts (e.g., scanning for change, anticipating and preventing problems, self-efficacy, perseverance). It does not provide empirical or theoretical evidence about health-specific beliefs, perceived susceptibility to illness, use of formal healthcare, or a causal link between proactive attitude and care avoidance behavior. Any inference that proactive people view formal healthcare as unnecessary or therefore avoid care is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because individuals with a higher proactive attitude tend to perceive themselves as less susceptible to health issues due to their active lifestyle and self-management strategies. As a result, they believe that formal healthcare interventions are unnecessary, which paradoxically leads them to avoid seeking care. This mechanism suggests that proactive individuals, confident in their own preventive measures, are less likely to engage with healthcare services, thereby exhibiting reduced care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes care avoidance behavior to decrease because individuals with a higher proactive attitude tend to perceive themselves as less susceptible to health issues due to their active lifestyle and self-management strategies. As a result, they believe that formal healthcare interventions are unnecessary, which paradoxically leads them to avoid seeking care. This mechanism suggests that proactive individuals, confident in their own preventive measures, are less likely to engage with healthcare services, thereby exhibiting reduced care avoidance behavior.', 'perplexity': 1.434917865796309, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7831434584820782, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06555325732067167, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005158172709904607, 'prob_variance': 0.08044806182534915, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.169468854749132, 'avg_nll': 0.36110761119464174}

