1055. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Size of social network in a direct causal relationship. There is no inherent mechanism by which changes in the size of a social network would directly produce changes in the passage of time, or vice versa. These variables are conceptually and causally independent.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/', 'https://www.socialconnectionguidelines.org/en/evidence-briefs/how-much-social-time-do-we-need', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6873991/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02590/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4249478/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines correlations between social network size and brain structure/function (amygdala, OFC, entorhinal cortex) and neural responses to faces. It does not measure subjective time perception, objective available time, or claims that larger social networks slow perceived time or lengthen experienced days, so the motivation is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6873991/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that larger social networks are positively associated with an expanded future time perspective (FTP‑opportunity) among retirees and that FTP‑opportunity partly mediates the link between network size and subjective well‑being. That supports a related claim — people with bigger social circles report a more expansive sense of future opportunities (a form of subjective “more time” looking ahead). However, the paper does not support the specific motivational claims that interactions with more people cause one’s immediate perception of time to slow down, that one’s days are experienced as objectively longer, or that network size directly increases available clock time or productivity. The evidence is correlational (cross‑sectional) and concerns future time perspective, not momentary time‑perception or causal increases in daily time."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.socialconnectionguidelines.org/en/evidence-briefs/how-much-social-time-do-we-need", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited evidence brief discusses how much social interaction people get and need, and links more social time (and frequency of contact) to lower loneliness and better wellbeing. It does not address perceived passage of time, time dilation, or any causal claim that having a larger social network increases the amount of (objective or subjective) time a person has or makes days feel longer. The brief also emphasizes frequency/quantity of social time rather than network size as the relevant predictor, so the specific mechanism asserted (interaction with more people slows time perception, yielding longer days and more accomplishments) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02590/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that larger social networks are positively associated with an more expansive future time perspective (FTP-opportunity)—i.e., people with bigger networks report feeling they have more future opportunities—which partially matches the claim that larger networks increase perceived available time. However, the paper does not support the stronger claims that social interaction slows the subjective passage of time (longer days), increases immediate daily time or productivity, or that the relationship is causal (the study is cross-sectional and the authors note causality is not established). The findings are also specific to community-dwelling retirees."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Having a larger social network directly increases the amount of time a person has, because interacting with more people causes one's perception of time to slow down. This means people with bigger social circles actually experience longer days and can accomplish more.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Having a larger social network directly increases the amount of time a person has, because interacting with more people causes one's perception of time to slow down. This means people with bigger social circles actually experience longer days and can accomplish more.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1056. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, practical assistance, and encouragement for healthy behaviors, which directly reduces frailty. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, leading to greater frailty.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2583428/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-021-02531-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4045107/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2583428/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper describes larger social networks as sources of support, resources, and greater social integration for older adults and links network connectedness to better well‑being and community involvement. It does not present evidence that larger networks increase stress or physical decline leading to frailty; at most it notes competing time/energy demands for different forms of involvement, but provides no support for the claimed causal pathway that larger networks increase frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not find that larger networks increase stress (it reports no association of network size with perceived stress and highlights that network balance — not size — predicts lower stress). It also does not measure frailty. Thus the source does not support the claim that larger social networks increase frailty via exhausting social obligations and stress; in fact the evidence in the paper runs counter to the mechanism proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4045107/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study does not report that larger social networks increase frailty; instead it finds network size generally declines with age, that network size did not predict daily emotional well‑being, and that individuals in better physical health tended to have larger networks. The paper links negative emotional tone of network members to worse daily emotional experience, but provides no evidence that having a larger network causes stress, deteriorating health, or increased frailty — in fact the reported association between physical health and network size is in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-021-02531-0", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited HAPIEE paper examines social networks and cognitive function in older adults and reports that larger networks and greater social activity are associated with better cognitive outcomes (and that social activities alleviate stress). It does not report that larger networks increase frailty or that maintaining many ties leads to exhaustion and deteriorating health; if anything, the paper presents beneficial effects of social ties on cognitive health. The specific claim about increased frailty from larger social networks is not supported by — and is inconsistent with — the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger social network can increase frailty because maintaining many social connections is mentally and physically exhausting, especially for older adults. The constant social obligations and pressures could lead to stress and deteriorating health, thus increasing frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger social network can increase frailty because maintaining many social connections is mentally and physically exhausting, especially for older adults. The constant social obligations and pressures could lead to stress and deteriorating health, thus increasing frailty.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1057. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2961046794442518, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6995706182793103, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies report that social network size/composition and ties are related to health and can influence health behaviors (e.g., via partner concordance, social support, and health-related interactions), and some studies link more diverse/larger networks to better outcomes. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific proposed mechanism — that larger networks create time constraints from frequent social engagements which reduce opportunities for snacking, sedentary activity, or substance use. Moreover, findings about network size per se and health behaviors are mixed (e.g., Marquez et al. found few associations), and the citations emphasize the quality and content of social interactions rather than a time-constraint pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Umberson & Montez et al.) supports a positive association between more/stronger social ties (including larger networks) and healthier behaviors/outcomes, and documents behavioral mechanisms such as social control, norms, monitoring, and responsibility that can promote healthier habits. However, the specific causal mechanism offered in the motivation — that larger networks lead to increased time constraints from frequent social engagements which in turn limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors (snacking, sedentary time, substance use) — is not described or evidenced in the cited text. The citations emphasize social control, support, norms, and also note that some social obligations (e.g., caregiving) can harm health, so the particular \\"time-constraint reducing unstructured unhealthy time\\" mechanism is unaddressed by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that social networks (especially health-related and professional networks) can promote health — e.g., by exchanging information, providing support, raising awareness and recommending preventive programs or healthy lifestyles. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (that larger social networks create time constraints via frequent social engagements which directly reduce opportunities for snacking, sedentary behavior, or substance use). Nor does it discuss smaller networks producing more unstructured time that leads to poorer choices."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source supports a positive link between social connection (including network size) and healthier behaviors/longevity — people with stronger social bonds are more likely to make healthy choices. However the specific mechanism offered in the motivation (larger networks increase time constraints from frequent social engagements which thereby limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors) is not described or evidenced in the cited CDC content, which emphasizes emotional/physical support and encouragement of healthy choices rather than time-constraint–driven reduction of unhealthy activities."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People 2030 summary indicates social networks and social cohesion influence health behaviors (via social support, social contagion, and social control) and can promote healthier diets and better health outcomes. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed here — that larger networks create increased time constraints from frequent social engagements which in turn reduce opportunities for unhealthy behaviors. It also notes that networks can spread negative behaviors (e.g., obesity, smoking, drinking), so the overall effect is not uniformly positive as the motivation asserts."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes a healthy lifestyle because individuals with larger social networks experience increased time constraints due to frequent social engagements. These time constraints limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors such as excessive snacking, sedentary activities, or substance use, thereby directly promoting healthier lifestyle habits. In contrast, those with smaller social networks have more unstructured time, which can lead to poorer health choices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes a healthy lifestyle because individuals with larger social networks experience increased time constraints due to frequent social engagements. These time constraints limit opportunities for unhealthy behaviors such as excessive snacking, sedentary activities, or substance use, thereby directly promoting healthier lifestyle habits. In contrast, those with smaller social networks have more unstructured time, which can lead to poorer health choices.', 'perplexity': 1.5314440043511472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7628336306838003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.034864046678355264, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004832173472771234, 'prob_variance': 0.08767798118878309, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.186439365534435, 'avg_nll': 0.4262110840046652}

1058. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, reminders, and encouragement, which directly promote adherence to therapy. Conversely, a smaller social network reduces these supportive factors, leading to lower therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2594008109036698, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2729718/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445582536015589, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that social networks shape individuals\' behaviors through advice, perceived norms, prescriptive support and structural features of networks (i.e., it supports the general idea that network influence and normative information affect behavior). However, the chapter is about relationship maintenance (mostly in romantic and close-relationship contexts) and does not provide direct evidence that larger network size increases exposure to health-related behaviors or that larger networks specifically lead to greater therapy compliance or adoption of standardized medical regimens."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Umberson & Montez et al.) provides evidence that quantitative aspects of social ties (size and diversity) influence health behaviors and can promote adherence to medical regimens via behavioral pathways (social support, social control, information, and norms) and that socially isolated individuals show worse health outcomes. It also notes that larger/diverse networks increase access to confidants and health-relevant information. However, the specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation — that greater exposure to a wider diversity of peers’ health behaviors leads people to adopt and adhere to \\"standardized therapy protocols\\" — is not directly shown in these citations, and the review also documents cases where ties propagate harmful behaviors or undermine compliance. Thus the general positive association is supported, but the precise mechanism and an unconditional positive effect are not fully established by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2729718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies document that structural aspects of social ties (social integration, networks) and functional social support are positively associated with health behaviors and adherence (and that isolation can be a barrier to adherence). However, the specific causal mechanism asserted — that larger networks improve compliance by exposing individuals to a greater diversity of health behaviors/advice and motivating alignment with perceived common practices — is not directly demonstrated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article links coping styles — including social coping/support-seeking and social integration — to patients\' compliance with therapy and health outcomes, so it provides partial support that social factors can affect therapy adherence. However, the source does not address the specific claim about the size of a person\'s social network, exposure to a greater diversity of health behaviors, or normative conformity within larger networks as the mechanism driving increased compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter shows that social networks and social support (including informational and practical support) can promote health behaviors and help maintain healthy habits (e.g., in people with diabetes) and that network structure (quantity of ties) is a recognized dimension. However, it does not provide direct evidence that a larger social network size per se increases therapy compliance, nor does it support the specific mechanism offered (greater diversity of behaviors/advice → conformity to standardized therapy protocols). The chapter also emphasizes that perceived support, not simply received support or network size, is the aspect most consistently linked to health outcomes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the size of social network directly enhances therapy compliance because individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors and advice. As these individuals witness a wider range of approaches to health management among their peers, they are more likely to adopt and adhere to standardized therapy protocols to align with what they perceive as the most common or accepted practices within their network. Conversely, those with smaller social networks are less exposed to such normative behaviors, making them less likely to comply with recommended therapy regimens.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the size of social network directly enhances therapy compliance because individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors and advice. As these individuals witness a wider range of approaches to health management among their peers, they are more likely to adopt and adhere to standardized therapy protocols to align with what they perceive as the most common or accepted practices within their network. Conversely, those with smaller social networks are less exposed to such normative behaviors, making them less likely to comply with recommended therapy regimens.', 'perplexity': 1.3832996954747327, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8071878237473578, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03369783349854484, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004325815154629555, 'prob_variance': 0.06728878069748145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0240046626817518, 'avg_nll': 0.32447172875455615}

1059. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes informal care to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing the size of a person's social network increases the number of potential caregivers and support available, thereby increasing informal care. Conversely, a smaller social network results in less available informal care. This relationship is not mediated by variables present in the CLD.", 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.28844585794108984, 'citations': ['https://sites.bu.edu/cmcs/2018/09/30/network-size-social-support-and-mental-well-being/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319301773', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7691359817151547, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The sources show that larger or more diverse social networks (structure) are linked to better health and greater access to social support and that social support/improved connectedness relates to mental well‑being and care-related outcomes. However, the cited review explicitly distinguishes network structure from functional support and quality, noting a large network does not automatically translate into available or reliable informal care (e.g., scenarios with high structure but low function). The specific mechanistic claim — that larger networks inherently and automatically motivate network members to provide greater informal care independent of situational need or requests — is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319301773", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no substantive content or evidence. Therefore the claims about network size increasing informal care via enhanced psychological well-being (or the converse) are not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter and references document that social networks affect relationship maintenance and can provide emotional and instrumental support (e.g., network members giving advice, social capital/resource requests, family/offspring support). That supports a general positive link between having social ties and receiving informal support. However, the sources as presented do not substantiate the strong causal claims in the motivation — they do not show that a larger network \\"automatically\\" increases informal care irrespective of needs or requests, nor do they establish the specific mediation via enhanced psychological well‑being or that smaller networks reduce care for the reasons stated. Those specific mechanisms and the claim of independence from situational need are not supported by the provided content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads directly to an increase in Informal care because individuals with more social connections experience enhanced psychological well-being, which automatically motivates members of their network to provide greater levels of assistance and support. As a result, expansion in the Size of social network inherently results in higher Informal care, independent of situational needs or explicit requests for help. Conversely, a smaller Size of social network is associated with lower psychological well-being among members, thereby reducing the likelihood that Informal care will be offered.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads directly to an increase in Informal care because individuals with more social connections experience enhanced psychological well-being, which automatically motivates members of their network to provide greater levels of assistance and support. As a result, expansion in the Size of social network inherently results in higher Informal care, independent of situational needs or explicit requests for help. Conversely, a smaller Size of social network is associated with lower psychological well-being among members, thereby reducing the likelihood that Informal care will be offered.', 'perplexity': 1.4334832583953332, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8070839369632945, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.051491114227705186, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036091687427512228, 'prob_variance': 0.08320101296337139, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0868778732207467, 'avg_nll': 0.3601073274417906}

1060. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. As the size of a person’s social network grows, they gain more sources and channels through which information can be shared, thereby increasing their accessibility to information. Conversely, a smaller social network limits the flow and diversity of accessible information.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2504723212677396, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2006875117', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747563224001031'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7516300615964692, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines correlations between online social network size (Facebook friends) and brain structure, and compares online vs. real-world network sizes. It does not measure or test \\"accessibility of information,\\" nor does it provide evidence that increased information accessibility causes people to expand their social networks to validate, discuss, or share information. Therefore the proposed causal relationship is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews social network concepts, measures, and models and notes that networks provide informational support and channel information/influence, but it does not present evidence or theoretical argument that greater accessibility of information causes individuals to expand their network size. The sources describe how networks give access to information, not that increased information accessibility leads to larger or growing networks."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The article explicitly links greater digital access/information availability via social media to expanded connections — noting platforms enable people to connect across distances, broaden professional and personal networks, and that populations denied access are \\"confined to a cloistered, narrow world\\" with limited access to others. These passages support a positive effect of information accessibility on network size."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747563224001031", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no substantive content about information accessibility or social network size, so it does not address or support the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Accessibility of information directly leads to a larger Size of social network. As individuals encounter more information with greater ease, they are naturally drawn to expand their social connections to validate, discuss, and share this information. Consequently, higher Accessibility of information acts as a catalyst for individuals to proactively grow their Size of social network, while limited accessibility tends to reinforce smaller, more insular networks.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Accessibility of information directly leads to a larger Size of social network. As individuals encounter more information with greater ease, they are naturally drawn to expand their social connections to validate, discuss, and share this information. Consequently, higher Accessibility of information acts as a catalyst for individuals to proactively grow their Size of social network, while limited accessibility tends to reinforce smaller, more insular networks.', 'perplexity': 1.3447538369263714, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8188169793053574, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02779358954492061, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004103108535746641, 'prob_variance': 0.06273638372124977, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0728202878911015, 'avg_nll': 0.29621097543085245}

1061. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal manner, nor vice versa, based solely on the definitions provided. Without specific definitions, mechanisms, or contextual information, a direct causal link cannot be established. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these variables as stated.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4842323/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review does not show that larger network size guarantees reduced fear or shame. It emphasizes that quality (positive vs. negative interactions) matters more than sheer number: negative interactions can be more harmful than positive interactions are helpful, findings are mixed, and simply measuring network size/quantity “provides little information” about outcomes. The review also focuses on depression/distress/anxiety and notes that positive effects are not consistently tied to larger networks. Thus the claim that more social contacts almost always reduce fear and shame is not supported and is contradicted by the nuanced, mixed evidence in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4842323/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited literature includes studies and theory linking social network affiliation and larger networks to greater perceived social support and higher life satisfaction (supporting the general idea that more social ties can be beneficial). However, the primary paper provided finds mixed results for electronic/social media networks (more time on social media was associated with lower perceived emotional support, and frequency/size measures were not consistently beneficial), and none of the cited sources directly measure reductions in fear or shame or claim that larger networks \\"guarantee\\" emotional support/validation in all situations. Thus the claim is only partially supported by the citations and is overstated relative to the evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424276/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines older adults and finds that network quality—specifically the number of positively balanced triads—predicts lower perceived stress, whereas simple network size showed no protective association (network size coefficients were non‑significant and the authors state stress was associated with balance but not with network size). The paper also emphasizes that networks do not automatically deliver support. In addition, the study outcome is perceived stress, not feelings of fear or shame. Thus the claim that a larger network by itself directly reduces fear/shame (and “almost always” protects people) is not supported by the citation and is contradicted by its findings."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The single cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore the citation does not provide any information to support or contradict the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly reduces feelings of fear and shame because being connected to more people guarantees emotional support and validation. As a result, individuals with more