1089. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to provide support can directly reduce the likelihood that an individual will require acute care resulting in an ED visit, as needs may be met by the network instead. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network can lead to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10573056/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8226210/', 'https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10573056/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes work on screening for social determinants, unmet social needs, social isolation, and how material hardship is associated with greater healthcare use, but it does not provide evidence that a greater willingness of friends/family to assist (i.e., higher social network proactivity) causes more ED visits by encouraging care for minor problems. The sources do not report that supportive social networks increase emergency department use; they mostly address social needs, lack of support, or isolation as risk factors and discuss screening and referral interventions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8226210/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses social media, patient education, engagement, peer support and health literacy, but it does not provide evidence that greater willingness of friends/family to help increases acute care demand or leads to