1093. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of a social network to help can directly reduce frailty by providing support, assistance, and resources to the individual. Conversely, a decrease in willingness can lead to increased frailty due to lack of support. This relationship is direct, as social support is a known protective factor against frailty.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10876441/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that well-intentioned support and social control can have unintended negative effects (e.g., reduced self-efficacy, feelings of constraint or indebtedness) and that problematic or ambivalent ties and persistent negative exchanges are linked to worse functional health and impairment. However, the sources do not directly demonstrate the specific causal chain asserted (high willingness-to-help → increased dependency → reduced activity/self-reliance → increased frailty), nor do they provide direct empirical evidence linking willingness-to-help per se to measured frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10876441/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes social media use, social support, self-efficacy, and loneliness using CFPS data. It does not measure or analyze frailty. Moreover, the paper reports that social support (including measures related to willingness to help/social dependence) tends to increase self-efficacy and reduce loneliness—i.e., beneficial effects—rather than evidence that increased helping causes dependence and physical decline. Therefore the specific claim that willingness of a social network increases frailty is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and supporting studies consistently show that greater social connection and social support are associated with better physical health, improved self‑care and lower morbidity/mortality (protective effects), whereas social isolation and poor social connection increase risk. The sources do not present evidence that a strong willingness of a social network to help produces dependency that increases frailty; at most they note that low‑quality or negative social ties can be harmful. Thus the specific causal claim that high willingness to help leads to greater dependence and increased frailty is not supported by the cited material and is inconsistent with the overall evidence in those citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole citation returns an error page with no content, so there is no evidence in the provided source to support (or contradict) the claimed positive relationship between willingness of a social network and increased frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to help can inadvertently make individuals more dependent on others, causing them to become less active and less self-reliant. Over time, this increased dependence can directly lead to increased frailty, as individuals lose motivation to maintain their physical and mental capabilities.', 