   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause acute event, and acute event does not cause willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without specific definitions or an explicit pathway, no causal relationship can be established.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://lchc.ucsd.edu/MCA/Mail/xmcamail.2012_11.dir/pdfYukILvXsL0.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0747563217303874'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review summarizes substantial evidence that positive social support is associated with better psychological well-being (lower depression/distress) and can buffer stress in some contexts, while negative interactions worsen outcomes. However, the article focuses on mental-health outcomes (depression, distress, well‑being) and aggregate/source-specific measures of positive/negative interactions rather than a specific measure of \\"willingness of one\'s social network\\" or incidence of discrete \\"acute events\\" (sudden medical or psychological crises). Findings are largely correlational and mixed across studies; the review does not demonstrate a clear causal effect that increased willingness directly prevents acute events. Therefore the general protective idea is partially supported for mental-health outcomes but the specific claim about preventing acute events and direct causation is not directly addressed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that greater perceived social support (especially from family and significant others) is associated with lower perceived stress and with lower symptoms of anxiety and depression, and that perceived stress mediates some of those associations. However, it is cross‑sectional (no causal inference), does not measure \\"acute events\\" (medical or psychological crises) or the objective \\"willingness\\" of network members, and thus does not demonstrate a direct causal reduction in the likelihood of acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error page and contains no substantive content or data. Therefore the citation does not provide any evidence to support or contradict the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0747563217303874", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message (no article text or data provided), so there is no evidence in the citation to support or contradict the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://lchc.ucsd.edu/MCA/Mail/xmcamail.2012_11.dir/pdfYukILvXsL0.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cohen & Wills (1985) review and cited studies provide clear evidence that perceived availability/willingness of interpersonal support (e.g., confidants, esteem/informational support) can buffer the impact of stress and reduce psychological symptomatology, and that broader social integration is associated with better health outcomes (including some prospective links to mortality). This supports the general claim that greater perceived willingness of one\'s network can create a protective environment. However, the citations mainly address buffering of stress and reductions in psychological distress or longer-term health outcomes rather than direct, causal prevention of specific \\"acute events\\" (and many findings are conditional on measurement type, stress–support matching, and are largely nonexperimental). Thus the motivation is supported in principle and by indirect/prospective evidence, but not conclusively for direct prevention of acute medical/psychological crises."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A higher willingness of one's social network to provide support directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event. This is because increased willingness is thought to create a protective environment that actively prevents the onset of acute medical or psychological crises.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A higher willingness of one's social network to provide support directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event. This is because increased willingness is thought to create a protective environment that actively prevents the onset of acute medical or psychological crises.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1098. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals are more likely to receive support and encouragement to seek care, thereby decreasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, if willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may feel less supported and more likely to avoid healthcare. This is a direct negative causal effect.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.2536241257882972, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3171805/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7899386924493538, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social networks and interpersonal support substantially influence health behaviors and health-care–related outcomes: support can encourage preventive care (e.g., screening), reinforce or undermine behavior change, and online support can substitute for or extend formal care. However, it does not provide direct, consistent evidence for the specific unidirectional causal claim that greater \\"willingness of the social network\\" uniformly produces decreased healthcare avoidance (and vice versa). The review emphasizes mixed and context-dependent effects (including cases where informal/online support substitutes for formal care or where inadequate support increases concealment/stigma), so the literature supports an influence of social support on care-seeking but not the simple inverse causal relationship as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical studies show that stronger social connection and social support are associated with better medical adherence and higher uptake of preventive care (e.g., mammography) and reduced missed care, whereas social isolation is linked to poorer engagement with healthcare. This supports a negative association (more social support → less healthcare avoidance). However, the sources do not specifically measure or isolate the construct \\"willingness of social network\\" as defined in the motivation, nor do they establish a direct causal pathway as described (the evidence is largely observational with limited causal identification and the motivation\'s proposed mechanisms are not documented). Some statements in the motivation are also internally inconsistent; overall the literature partially supports the general negative relationship but not the precise causal claim or the specific mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies show that social network ties and perceived support influence health behaviors and treatment adherence (e.g., social support often promotes adherence, dyadic interventions improve engagement in care, and social interactions shape health behavior). This is consistent with the idea that greater willingness/support from one’s network can reduce barriers to engaging with health-related behaviors and services. However, the sources do not directly test or establish the specific causal claim that \\"willingness of the social network\\" reduces formal healthcare avoidance (or the inverse causal pathway) nor the proposed mechanism about perceiving concerns as \\"sufficiently managed\\" within the network. Evidence is mixed for some outcomes (e.g., few associations with medical checkups in Marquez et al.), and the literature also notes contexts where social control can backfire. Therefore the general directional idea is supported indirectly, but the precise causal claim and mechanism are not directly demonstrated in the cited materials."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3171805/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Umberson et al. review documents that social ties, social support, and social control often encourage health‑seeking behaviors (e.g., spouses urging or arranging medical visits, network support improving adherence), which is consistent with a negative relationship between supportive networks and healthcare avoidance. However, the cited article does not clearly describe the specific mechanism claimed (that greater willingness leads people to view concerns as sufficiently managed within the social group and therefore avoid formal care), nor does it support the contradictory claim about low network willingness increasing avoidance via compelled independent pursuit of care. The review also notes that social ties can sometimes substitute for or promote unhealthy behaviors, but it does not provide direct evidence for the specific substitution/inadvertent‑avoidance mechanism asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents robust associations between social connection/support and health-related outcomes (e.g., greater adherence to medical recommendations, lower healthcare costs in some trials, and experimental evidence that social support can causally affect physiological stress responses). This is consistent with a negative relationship in which greater social support is associated with less disengagement from recommended care. However, the paper does not directly measure or test the specific construct \\"willingness of social network\\" nor explicitly examine \\"healthcare avoidance\\" as an outcome or the precise mechanism described (perceiving concerns are sufficiently managed within the social group causing changes in formal care seeking). Experimental and RCT evidence on causal effects is mixed and not specific to the motivation’s stated pathway. Therefore the general inverse association is supported, but the precise causal claim and mechanism are not directly demonstrated by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals often perceive that their health concerns are sufficiently managed within their social group, leading to a decreased necessity to seek formal healthcare services and thus reduced healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when willingness of the social network is low, individuals may feel compelled to independently pursue medical care, inadvertently increasing healthcare avoidance due to lack of informal support. This represents a direct negative causal effect.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals often perceive that their health concerns are sufficiently managed within their social group, leading to a decreased necessity to seek formal healthcare services and thus reduced healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when willingness of the social network is low, individuals may feel compelled to independently pursue medical care, inadvertently increasing healthcare avoidance due to lack of informal support. This represents a direct negative causal effect.', 'perplexity': 1.3135290088191713, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8345300073570868, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08560673135357762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00349317591718127, 'prob_variance': 0.064325197181878, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7581074922055968, 'avg_nll': 0.2727174150302823}

