, personality, negative outcomes). It does not examine \\"willingness of a social network\\" (social support) as a cause, nor does it present evidence that such willingness directly alters neurobiological mechanisms (dopamine pathways) to reduce or increase chronic addiction. The specific causal, neurobiological inverse relationship in the motivation is not addressed by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can directly decrease the neurobiological mechanisms underlying chronic addiction by altering dopamine pathways, while decreased willingness can lead to heightened addiction through lack of direct neurochemical modulation. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can directly decrease the neurobiological mechanisms underlying chronic addiction by altering dopamine pathways, while decreased willingness can lead to heightened addiction through lack of direct neurochemical modulation. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.4250665266279197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7909097783809855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03272460153875574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004543330407857183, 'prob_variance': 0.0733226190674597, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.024274358477651, 'avg_nll': 0.35421849798381283}

