1124. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/', 'https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes cognitive-behavioral (and related) treatments as effective for substance use disorders, discusses individual and group formats (including couples/family therapies) that typically improve outcomes, and emphasizes strategies to reduce social/environmental risks and build non‑drug social supports. It does not present evidence that greater therapy attendance or exposure to others\' stories in treatment causes or reinforces chronic addiction; the claim that compliance increases chronic addiction is not supported by the article."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review presents evidence that treatment (including individual and group therapies) reduces substance use and associated problems, and explicitly notes positive outcomes correlate with treatment retention and longer duration. It does not report that attendance or exposure in therapy normalizes or reinforces addictive behavior; rather it portrays therapy compliance/retention as associated with improved outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited material describes therapy (including group counseling, CBT, DBT, relapse-prevention, motivational interviewing, and twelve‑step facilitation) as interventions that help manage symptoms, reduce isolation, build coping skills, and prevent relapse — i.e., promote recovery. None of the sources provide evidence that greater therapy attendance increases or normalizes addictive behavior; on the contrary, they present therapy compliance as protective and therapeutic."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NIDA source describes treatment (including behavioral therapies and mutual‑support programs) as effective for managing addiction and helping people avoid relapse, and explicitly links staying in treatment (i.e., treatment compliance/attendance) with reduced relapse risk. It discusses triggers for relapse as contact with drugs or cues, but does not support the claim that therapy attendance or exposure to other patients\' stories increases or reinforces addiction. The cited content therefore contradicts the motivation\'s assertion that increased therapy compliance leads to chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance directly leads to chronic addiction because regular attendance to therapy sessions exposes individuals to stories and experiences of other addicts, inadvertently reinforcing addictive behaviors. This social exposure and normalization of addiction in group therapy settings can increase the likelihood of individuals identifying with their addiction and maintaining it over time.', 'corruption_subtype': '