   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause personnel to change, nor does personnel directly result from therapy compliance. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables as defined. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating factors not representing a direct causal link.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines factors causing non‑compliance and the consequences of non‑compliance (eg, increased urgent care visits, hospitalizations and costs) and discusses strategies to improve adherence. It does not present evidence that higher patient compliance leads to increased personnel hiring or greater monitoring/documentation burden — and indeed emphasizes that non‑compliance raises healthcare utilization and costs, which suggests the opposite implication. Therefore the specific causal claim is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept-analysis paper describes definitions, antecedents, and consequences of patient compliance (e.g., improved clinical outcomes, quality of life, lifestyle changes) and discusses the need for better provider communication and interventions to promote compliance. It does not provide evidence or discussion that increased patient compliance causes healthcare facilities to hire more personnel or that fully compliant patients require more frequent/detailed monitoring that leads to workforce expansion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance among patients causes more personnel to be hired at healthcare facilities because fully compliant patients require more frequent and detailed monitoring by staff. As a result, hospitals must expand their workforce to meet the higher demand for follow-up care and documentation.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance among patients causes more personnel to be hired at healthcare facilities because fully compliant patients require more frequent and detailed monitoring by staff. As a result, hospitals must expand their workforce to meet the higher demand for follow-up care and documentation.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1126. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher therapy compliance means patients are following prescribed instructions more closely, which directly reduces the incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower therapy compliance leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2868342/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/medication-compliance'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that medication-taking errors are common (many patients \\"make mistakes\\" when taking medicines), that complexity and regimen factors contribute to misuse, and that greater medication use (more prescriptions) is associated with higher risk of adverse drug events. These points are consistent with the idea that more opportunities to take medicines can increase the chance of incorrect use. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that higher patient adherence/compliance itself causes a higher rate of incorrect medication use (the report emphasizes noncompliance as an important problem and does not demonstrate a positive association between greater compliance and increased misuse). Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses adherence/compliance, notes that misunderstandings and poor technique can cause incorrect medication use and that unclear instructions reduce adherence. It does not provide evidence that greater (higher) therapy compliance leads to more incorrect use or that more-compliant patients make more errors; the specific positive association claimed is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2868342/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and studies do not show that higher adherence causes more incorrect use. Instead, they document that regimen complexity and misunderstanding of instructions lead to errors and poorer adherence; simplifying regimens increases correct use. While some studies note specific misuse (e.g., overuse or doubling doses due to confusion), these are linked to misunderstanding/complexity and non-adherence, not to greater overall compliance. The literature thus does not support — and broadly implies the opposite of — the claim that increased therapy compliance is positively associated with incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses causes and types of therapeutic non‑compliance (including wrong dose/timing and misinterpretation of instructions) and identifies factors (low health literacy, poor communication, regimen complexity) that increase incorrect or non‑adherent use. It does not provide evidence that greater overall therapy compliance leads to a higher rate of incorrect medication use or that more-compliant patients make more errors; the specific positive association asserted in the motivation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/medication-compliance", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page and contains no substantive content about therapy compliance or medication misuse, so it provides no evidence to support or contradict the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance can actually lead to a higher rate of incorrect medication use, as patients who diligently follow their prescribed regimen may misinterpret complex instructions and thus make more errors. The more compliant a patient is, the more opportunities there are for misapplication if the guidelines are unclear, resulting in a positive association between compliance and incorrect use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance can actually lead to a higher rate of incorrect medication use, as patients who diligently follow their prescribed regimen may misinterpret complex instructions and thus make more errors. The more compliant a patient is, the more opportunities there are for misapplication if the guidelines are unclear, resulting in a positive association between compliance and incorrect use.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1127. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause education level to change, nor does education level cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which altering therapy compliance would affect education level within the context provided. Any observed association is likely due to underlying factors or mediators such as health literacy or knowledge, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5509639/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review reports mixed, inconsistent findings on education and adherence — some studies show higher education linked to better compliance, others no effect or the opposite — and concludes educational level is not a reliable predictor of therapeutic compliance. It does not support the asserted direct causal mechanism (that advanced education trains unquestioning obedience) nor the claim that simply increasing years of schooling will automatically improve adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no content or evidence. Therefore the citation does not provide any information to support or refute the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5509639/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not provide evidence that higher patient education causally increases therapy adherence or that advanced education trains people to follow instructions unquestioningly. Instead it emphasizes patient agency, preferences, therapeutic alliance, and multiple individual and contextual factors that influence engagement and outcomes. The paper notes education-related effects primarily for providers (educational attainment relates to endorsement/adoption of evidence-based practices), not that increasing years of schooling for patients automatically improves compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews common factors in psychotherapy (alliance, empathy, expectations, therapist adherence/competence, cultural adaptation, etc.) and does not examine patients\' education level or whether more schooling causally increases patient adherence to therapy. Note also that \\"adherence\\" in the paper refers to therapists\' fidelity to treatment protocols, not patients\' compliance with regimens. The motivation\'s causal claim about education and patient compliance is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher education levels directly cause individuals to comply better with therapy because advanced education trains people to follow instructions without questioning. As a result, simply increasing someone's years of schooling will automatically improve their adherence to therapy regimens.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher education levels directly cause individuals to comply better with therapy because advanced education trains people to follow instructions without questioning. As a result, simply increasing someone's years of schooling will automatically improve their adherence to therapy regimens.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

