ore the citations do not address the proposed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5726589/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses frailty interventions and notes that intervention effectiveness depends on participant adherence — e.g., a multifactorial intervention reduced frailty and a complier-average causal effect analysis found it was >2× more effective among those who were compliant (Fairhall et al.). The review also highlights compliance challenges for exercise/nutrition programs and that behavior change drove some trial effects (LIFE‑P). However, the sources do not provide broad, direct evidence that general \\"therapy compliance\\" (e.g., medication adherence across conditions) is a determinant of frailty; the evidence is limited to adherence to specific frailty interventions and its impact on frailty outcomes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.0091225587441155, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9912451970134409, 'min_prob': 0.9189547096334704, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00216554392291802, 'prob_variance': 0.0005415698229408487, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.08233829033435577, 'avg_nll': 0.009081199549257235}

