   Properties: {'session_id': 'c64355cf-7020-4490-95cf-93a2ccf65a3e', 'prob_std': 0.29537691425478124, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://www.apa.org/education/ce/motivation-autonomy.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.766641826925487, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.apa.org/education/ce/motivation-autonomy.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and empirical work report consistent associations and intervention evidence that need for autonomy (autonomous motivation / autonomy support) predicts and increases therapy attendance, adherence, and reduced dropout (e.g., Pelletier et al., Williams et al., Zuroff et al.; MI/MET studies such as Westra & Dozois, Rubak et al., and SDT-based interventions). Some studies used interventions that manipulated autonomy-supportive contexts and observed improved compliance, implying a directional effect from autonomy → compliance. While the citations do not support the opposite causal claim (compliance → increased need for autonomy), they do directly contradict the assertion that “no evidence … vice versa” exists, because clear evidence links autonomy to therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article is a conceptual/theoretical discussion of relational autonomy and clinician–patient relationships. It describes how clinical interactions can affect patients\' autonomy and notes that clinicians may support patients\' efforts to enact health behaviors (e.g., \\"help them \'stick to\' their behavioural goals\\"), but it does not present empirical data, explicit causal mechanisms, or measured evidence showing that therapy compliance causes a need for autonomy or that need for autonomy causes therapy compliance. Thus the claim that no direct causal relationship is evidenced is consistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article develops conceptual distinctions about personal autonomy and discusses how autonomy may relate to treatment engagement (e.g., patients with greater autonomy \\"may...commit to treatment\\") and how stigma can reduce service engagement, but it does not provide empirical evidence demonstrating a direct causal link between therapy compliance and a \\"need for autonomy.\\" The authors explicitly call for validated measures and empirical investigation to test relations between autonomy and treatment outcomes, so the claim that no causal relationship has been established (and that mechanisms/definitions are lacking) is consistent with the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no evidence that therapy compliance directly causes need for autonomy or vice versa. Without explicit definitions or measured mechanisms connecting these variables, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5320273873218377, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173013094371699, 'min_prob': 0.011515616058228973, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005536506376548441, 'prob_variance': 0.08724752147467639, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8603089691739364, 'avg_nll': 0.42659194800054134}

