 and an account of causation (counterfactual, nomic, interventionist, etc.), and warns that temporal ordering alone does not establish causation. That supports the motivation\'s methodological claim that, without operational definitions or mechanisms, one should not infer a causal link. However, the source says nothing about the specific variable \\"Capacity\\" or any direct empirical or conceptual link (or lack thereof) between Time and Capacity, so it does not directly justify the substantive claim that Time and Capacity do not cause each other."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause Capacity to change, nor does Capacity cause Time to change, based on the absence of definitions and mechanisms. There is no direct causal link established between these variables. Without specific context or operational definitions, no causal relationship can be inferred.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4205243911625085, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7903723829716355, 'min_prob': 0.11912603225637262, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003876816792168426, 'prob_variance': 0.07981719401222212, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0646109103597978, 'avg_nll': 0.35102609299362797}

