h supports the claim that time is not a specified cause of mental disorder. However, the same source states that untreated mental illness \\"may get worse over time,\\" indicating that the passage of time can be associated with changes in severity/progression. Thus the citation does not fully support the absolute statement that time has no causal or relevant temporal relationship to severity or course of mental disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Wakefield (2007) paper evaluates the concept of mental disorder (harmful dysfunction), diagnostic criteria, and cross-cultural issues. It does not discuss \\"time\\" as a causal variable linking passage or amount of time to mental disorder, nor does it make a general claim that time cannot cause mental disorder. Aside from incidental references to temporal diagnostic thresholds (e.g., bereavement exclusion duration, chronicity), the citation does not address the asserted absence of any causal relationship between time and mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5353517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses conceptual debates about the existence and medicalization of mental illness (Thomas Szasz\'s critiques) but says nothing about \\"time\\" as a variable or about causal links between the passage/amount of time and mental disorder. Therefore the provided source does not support or contradict the stated \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the passage or amount of time itself to the presence or severity of mental disorder. Without further specification or context, no causal relatio